/* CAPE Digital Media Unit 2 (DME2) · Module 2: Web and Digital Publishing. Developed by Kester Small. Built from her DME2 course outline and the CAPE Digital Media syllabus. */
(window.COURSE_MODULES = window.COURSE_MODULES || []).push({"id":"M2","code":"Module 2","title":"Web and Digital Publishing","overview":"<p>Module 2 teaches you to author, design, publish and manage digital content. You will learn the language of digital publishing (ePub, ISBN, metadata, CMS, Kindle Direct Publishing, Google Play Books, Kobo), trace the history of the Internet from ARPANET to Web 3.0, and write for blogs, podcasts, video scripts and social media with a Caribbean voice while respecting copyright and ethical standards. You will plan sites with concept maps, site maps and prototypes, write web-friendly and search-optimised content, produce photo, audio and video for the web, and build working web pages in <b>HTML and CSS</b>. Finally you will evaluate forms of digital publishing and online platforms, describe content management systems including headless CMS, and discuss the trends shaping web publishing. This module follows the ten sections of the DME2 course outline.</p>\n<p>In CAPE Unit 2, <b>Paper 01</b> has 45 multiple-choice items, 15 from each module, worth 20%. <b>Paper 02</b> has three compulsory 15-mark questions, one per module, worth 20% in total and sat without internet access; for Module 2 expect to plan a site structure, correct or write short HTML and CSS, and evaluate publishing platforms. The <b>School-Based Assessment (Paper 031)</b> is worth 60%: in groups of 2 to 3 you design and implement a product or service (preferably local, social or entrepreneurial) and submit a proposal (14 marks), evidence of progress and process (12) and the product (19), including a <b>30-second audio-visual ad</b> (5). The report is limited to 1,500 words. A website, blog or e-zine built in this module is often the core of a strong SBA product.</p>\n<p>Work through the units in order. Keep VS Code (or Notepad) and a browser open while you study the HTML and CSS units, and type every code sample yourself: copying by hand builds the accuracy Paper 02 demands. Use the easier version of an activity if you need a first step, complete each quick check-in honestly and write the short task at the end of each unit. Finish with the Module 2 Test (pass mark 70%) and the Paper 02 practice question.</p>","objectives":["By the end you will be able to explain digital publishing and related Internet terminologies (ePub, ISBN, metadata, CMS, MMOG) and describe the history of the Internet and the digital revolution.","By the end you will be able to generate original content ideas for digital publishing using blog, podcast, video and audio slideshow writing techniques, storyboards and scripts, with a Caribbean perspective and respect for copyright and ethical standards.","By the end you will be able to create low-fidelity and high-fidelity prototypes, concept maps, navigational charts, site maps and hierarchy maps that apply design principles to layout.","By the end you will be able to create web-friendly, audience-appropriate content guided by a creative brief, plain-language writing, licensing, SEO, machine indexability and accessible media.","By the end you will be able to apply appropriate cameras, shooting principles, and open-source, industry-standard and cloud-based tools to develop photo, audio, podcast and video content for the web.","By the end you will be able to create functional websites using HTML and CSS, including hyperlinks, multimedia, the box model, responsive layout and accessible, usable page design.","By the end you will be able to evaluate the various forms of digital publishing using criteria such as accuracy, authority, purpose, usability, accessibility, risk and security.","By the end you will be able to evaluate the usefulness of online publishing platforms, including free and paid hosted options, blogs, Wikipedia, YouTube and live streaming.","By the end you will be able to describe content management tools and techniques, including self-hosted and cloud CMS, open-source and proprietary systems, CMS types and content workflows.","By the end you will be able to discuss the factors that influence development and trends in web publishing, including emerging innovations and research tools."],"glossary":[{"term":"Digital publishing","def":"Creating and distributing content such as books, magazines, news and websites in electronic form."},{"term":"ePub","def":"An open e-book format whose reflowable text adapts to different screen sizes and reader settings."},{"term":"Interactive e-book","def":"An e-book that includes features such as audio, video, quizzes, links or animations."},{"term":"ISBN","def":"The International Standard Book Number: a unique code that identifies one edition and format of a book."},{"term":"Metadata","def":"Data about data, such as title, author, keywords and description, that helps content be found and organised."},{"term":"Kindle Direct Publishing (KDP)","def":"Amazon's self-publishing service for e-books and print-on-demand books."},{"term":"Content management system (CMS)","def":"Software that lets people create, edit, organise and publish web content without writing code for every page."},{"term":"ARPANET","def":"The US Department of Defense-funded network of the late 1960s that was the forerunner of the Internet."},{"term":"World Wide Web","def":"The system of linked web pages invented by Tim Berners-Lee around 1989 to 1991 that runs on the Internet."},{"term":"Digital revolution","def":"The shift from analogue and mechanical technology to digital technology that began in the late twentieth century."},{"term":"MMOG","def":"A massively multiplayer online game in which very large numbers of players share one persistent online world."},{"term":"Conceptualisation","def":"Developing an original content idea from a brief or a real-world problem."},{"term":"Script","def":"A written plan of the words, sounds and actions in a podcast, video or audio slideshow."},{"term":"Audio slideshow","def":"A sequence of still images played over narration or sound to tell a story."},{"term":"Plagiarism","def":"Presenting someone else's words, ideas or work as your own without credit."},{"term":"Attribution","def":"Crediting the creator and source of content you use."},{"term":"Fair use","def":"A limited legal exception that allows small, transformative uses of copyright material, for example for criticism or education; it varies by country."},{"term":"Creative Commons","def":"A set of free licences that let creators grant permission in advance, with conditions such as BY (credit) and NC (non-commercial)."},{"term":"Cybercrime","def":"Crime committed using computers or networks, including large-scale copyright piracy and hacking."},{"term":"Low-fidelity prototype","def":"A quick, rough model of a layout, often on paper, used to test structure early."},{"term":"High-fidelity prototype","def":"A detailed, often clickable digital model that looks and behaves close to the final product."},{"term":"Concept map","def":"A diagram that shows ideas and the relationships between them."},{"term":"Site map","def":"A diagram listing all pages of a website and how they link together."},{"term":"Hierarchy map","def":"A tree diagram showing parent and child pages and the pathways users follow."},{"term":"Navigational chart","def":"A diagram that plans how users move from page to page through menus and links."},{"term":"Creative brief","def":"A short planning document defining the purpose, target group, tone of voice and content checklist for a piece of content."},{"term":"Content scope","def":"The defined purpose, topic, depth, format, platform and intended outcome of a piece of content."},{"term":"Web-friendly writing","def":"Writing for screens using short paragraphs, scannable headings, direct address and plain language."},{"term":"SEO","def":"Search Engine Optimisation: improving content so it ranks higher in search results."},{"term":"Machine indexability","def":"Preparing content so search engines and platforms can find, read and index it."},{"term":"Alt text","def":"A short text description of an image, read by screen readers and used by search engines."},{"term":"Depth of field","def":"The range of distance in a photo that appears sharp; shallow depth of field blurs the background."},{"term":"Noise reduction","def":"An editing process that removes background hiss or hum from an audio recording."},{"term":"Breadcrumbs","def":"A navigation trail such as Home > Tours > Pigeon Island that shows users where they are in a site."},{"term":"HTML","def":"HyperText Markup Language: the code that defines the structure and content of a web page."},{"term":"Hyperlink","def":"A clickable link created with the a element that takes users to another page, file or location."},{"term":"Anchor link","def":"A link that jumps to a specific section of the same page using an id."},{"term":"CSS","def":"Cascading Style Sheets: the language that controls the appearance and layout of HTML."},{"term":"Selector","def":"The part of a CSS rule that chooses which elements are styled, such as p, .price or #header."},{"term":"Box model","def":"The CSS idea that every element is a box made of content, padding, border and margin."},{"term":"Responsive design","def":"Design that adapts the layout to different screen sizes, often using flexible widths and media queries."},{"term":"rem","def":"A CSS unit relative to the root font size, which helps text scale consistently."},{"term":"dpi","def":"Dots per inch: a print resolution measure; screens are measured in pixels instead."},{"term":"WCAG","def":"The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines: international standards for making web content accessible."},{"term":"Wireframe","def":"A simple skeleton layout of a page showing where content and navigation will go, without final styling."},{"term":"Authority","def":"An evaluation criterion that asks who created the content and whether they are qualified and credible."},{"term":"Hosted platform","def":"A publishing service that stores and runs your site for you, such as WordPress.com or Medium."},{"term":"Self-hosted CMS","def":"A CMS installed on hosting you rent and manage yourself, such as WordPress.org software."},{"term":"Platform dependency","def":"The risk of relying on a platform you do not control, whose rules or algorithms can change."},{"term":"Live streaming","def":"Broadcasting video to an online audience in real time, for example on Facebook Live or Twitch."},{"term":"Open-source CMS","def":"A CMS whose code is free to use and modify, such as WordPress, Joomla or Drupal."},{"term":"Proprietary CMS","def":"A CMS owned by a company, usually paid for and closed, such as Wix or Squarespace."},{"term":"Headless CMS","def":"A CMS that stores content separately from its presentation and delivers it to many channels through an API."},{"term":"Content workflow","def":"The stages content passes through: creation, review, approval, publishing and archiving."},{"term":"Progressive web app (PWA)","def":"A website that behaves like an installable app, with features such as offline use and home-screen icons."},{"term":"Voice search","def":"Searching by speaking to a device or assistant, which favours natural, question-style content."},{"term":"Short-form content","def":"Brief content such as reels and short posts designed for quick consumption and algorithm-driven feeds."}],"units":[{"id":"M2U1","title":"Digital publishing and internet terminologies","objectives":["Define digital publishing and explain its purpose for creators, businesses and audiences in the Caribbean.","Describe digital publishing platforms and tools: Amazon Kindle Direct Publishing, Apple Books, Google Play Books, Kobo Writing Life, Barnes &amp; Noble Press (Nook), Wattpad, IngramSpark, Draft2Digital and content management systems.","Explain the eight key features of digital publishing platforms.","Explain interactive e-books, ePub (EPUB 3), PDF, reflowable and fixed layout, ISBN and metadata, and evaluate the advantages and disadvantages of e-books.","Describe the history of the Internet and the digital revolution: ARPANET, TCP/IP, the World Wide Web, Mosaic, Web 1.0, 2.0 and 3.0, broadband and mobile.","Explain accessibility and global adoption, and why massively multiplayer online games (MMOGs) count as a form of digital publishing."],"videos":[{"title":"What is digital publishing?","q":"what is digital publishing explained ebook self publishing platforms"},{"title":"EPUB vs PDF: reflowable and fixed layout","q":"epub vs pdf reflowable vs fixed layout ebook explained"},{"title":"History of the Internet","q":"history of the internet arpanet tcp ip world wide web explained"},{"title":"Web 1.0 vs Web 2.0 vs Web 3.0","q":"web 1.0 vs web 2.0 vs web 3.0 explained simply"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"What is digital publishing?","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Digital publishing</b> (often referred to as online publishing or e-publishing) is the publication on the web or on electronic carriers of magazines, e-books, white papers, reports, or any form of content that is typically long-form. In short, it is the use of online technology to digitise print material and share it digitally.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Web publishing</b> is the process of publishing original content on the Internet. The process includes building and uploading websites, updating the associated webpages, and posting content to these webpages online. Web publishing comprises personal, business and community websites in addition to e-books and blogs.</p>\n<p>Put simply, digital publishing is the creation, formatting and distribution of content (text, images, audio, video and interactive media) in electronic form. Web publishing is the part of it that happens on websites. Traditional publishing prints copies, ships them to shops and waits. Digital publishing releases a file once and delivers it to anyone with a connection, often within minutes. Forms of digital publishing include <b>e-books</b>, <b>online newspapers and magazines</b>, <b>blogs</b>, <b>websites</b>, <b>podcasts</b>, <b>video channels</b>, <b>apps</b>, <b>newsletters</b> and even <b>online games</b>.</p>\n<p><b>The purpose of digital publishing</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Reach</b>: a poet in Roseau can sell to readers in Toronto, London and Kingston on the same day.</li>\n<li><b>Lower cost</b>: no printing, warehousing or shipping, which matters on small islands where imported paper and freight are expensive.</li>\n<li><b>Speed and updating</b>: a national newspaper can post breaking hurricane news online and correct it within seconds; a printed edition cannot be changed once it leaves the press.</li>\n<li><b>Interactivity and multimedia</b>: links, video, audio, quizzes and comments.</li>\n<li><b>Data</b>: publishers see downloads, reading time and clicks, and use these analytics to improve content.</li>\n<li><b>Preservation and access</b>: out-of-print Caribbean books, oral histories and archives can be kept alive digitally.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Traditional publishing</th><th>Digital publishing</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Product</td><td>Printed copies</td><td>Electronic files and streams</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gatekeepers</td><td>Publishers and editors decide what is printed</td><td>Creators can self-publish directly</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Distribution</td><td>Physical shops, shipping</td><td>Online stores, websites, apps</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Updating</td><td>New print run needed</td><td>Upload a revised file</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>In this unit the regional agency <b>Island Pulse Media</b> is helping clients publish digitally. Zara, the creative director, puts it simply: \"Publishing is no longer about owning a printing press. It is about choosing the right platform, the right format and the right data.\"</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When a question says \"explain the purpose\", give the purpose <b>and</b> the reason it matters: \"Digital publishing lowers cost because there is no printing or shipping, which helps small Caribbean authors compete.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Digital publishing means making content such as books, magazines, videos or podcasts and sharing it as electronic files instead of printed copies. Web publishing is the part that happens on websites. Think of a Barbados poet who uploads her book once and sells it to readers in Toronto and Kingston that same afternoon, with no printing or shipping.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A Grade One answer goes beyond \"it is cheaper\". Digital publishing changes <b>who controls publishing</b>: traditional publishers acted as gatekeepers, while self-publishing lets creators reach readers directly. That brings a trade-off, because without an editor, quality and accuracy depend on the creator. A common misconception is that digital publishing only means e-books; blogs, podcasts, apps and online games are publications too. When a question says \"explain the purpose\", link each purpose to a consequence: instant updating matters during a hurricane, when a national newspaper must correct a road-closure report within minutes rather than wait for tomorrow's print run.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"The eight key features of digital publishing platforms","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Digital publishing platforms</b> are online tools or services that enable individuals or organisations to create, publish and distribute digital content in various formats, such as e-books, magazines and other digital publications.</p>\n<p>There are <b>eight key features and aspects</b>. Learn all eight by name: they make a ready-made answer plan for \"describe the features of a digital publishing platform\".</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Content creation</b>: tools for creating and formatting digital content, including text, images, multimedia and interactive elements for a more engaging user experience. Example: Kindle Create or Apple Pages for laying out a book.</li>\n<li><b>Distribution</b>: making content available to a wide audience for download, streaming or online reading through web browsers or dedicated apps.</li>\n<li><b>Monetisation</b>: ways for creators to earn from their content, such as selling digital products, subscriptions or advertising.</li>\n<li><b>Accessibility</b>: users can open publications on various devices, such as e-readers, tablets, smartphones and computers.</li>\n<li><b>Global reach</b>: authors reach a worldwide audience without the limits of physical distribution, which increases visibility.</li>\n<li><b>Interactivity</b>: some platforms support multimedia, hyperlinks and other interactive content that goes beyond traditional print.</li>\n<li><b>Analytics</b>: tools that track engagement, sales and other metrics, so creators understand their audience and improve their content and marketing.</li>\n<li><b>Self-publishing</b>: independent authors and smaller publishers can publish and distribute without a traditional publishing house.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Applied to Dominica.</b> The Roseau poet Ms. Joseph writes a collection about Hurricane Maria. Through one platform she can <b>create</b> the e-book, <b>distribute</b> it to readers in Curaçao and New York (<b>global reach</b>), set a price (<b>monetisation</b>), let readers open it on a phone or a Kindle (<b>accessibility</b>), add audio of her reading each poem (<b>interactivity</b>), see that most sales come from the Netherlands (<b>analytics</b>), and do all of this without a publishing house (<b>self-publishing</b>).</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In a \"describe\" question, name the feature <b>and</b> say what it does for the creator or the reader. \"Analytics\" alone earns little; \"Analytics lets the author track sales and engagement so she can target her marketing at diaspora readers\" earns the full mark.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A digital publishing platform is an online service for making, sharing and selling digital content. Learn its eight features: content creation, distribution, monetisation, accessibility, global reach, interactivity, analytics and self-publishing. For example, a Grenada cook can make a recipe e-book, sell it, and see which countries buy it most, all without a publishing house.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The eight features are not separate boxes; they feed each other. <b>Analytics</b> is only useful because of <b>global reach</b>: an author who sees that most sales come from the Netherlands can then target her marketing there. <b>Interactivity</b> can reduce <b>accessibility</b> if the e-reader cannot play embedded audio, so creators test on several devices. In a \"describe\" question, name the feature, say what it does and add who benefits. For an \"evaluate\" question, show the limits too: self-publishing removes the gatekeeper, but it also removes the professional editing and marketing that a publishing house would provide.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Which key feature is this?","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is explaining digital publishing platforms to a client. Name the key feature, from the list of eight, that each situation shows.</p>","items":[{"text":"A Grenada author sees a dashboard showing how many readers finished chapter 3 and where they live.","options":["Content creation","Self-publishing","Analytics"],"answer":2,"fb":"Tracking engagement and audience data is analytics. Creators use it to improve content and marketing."},{"text":"A Jamaican cookbook writer sets a price of US$4.99 and earns a royalty on every download.","options":["Monetisation","Interactivity","Accessibility"],"answer":0,"fb":"Earning money from content through sales, subscriptions or ads is monetisation."},{"text":"A Dominican student publishes her novel without being accepted by any publishing house.","options":["Self-publishing","Distribution","Global reach"],"answer":0,"fb":"Publishing independently, without a traditional publishing house, is self-publishing."},{"text":"The same e-book opens on a Kindle, an Android phone, an iPad and a laptop.","options":["Analytics","Monetisation","Accessibility"],"answer":2,"fb":"Reading on many kinds of devices is the accessibility feature."},{"text":"A Barbados history e-book includes embedded video of Crop Over and links to museum archives.","options":["Interactivity","Global reach","Self-publishing"],"answer":0,"fb":"Multimedia and hyperlinks that go beyond print are interactivity."},{"text":"A Belize travel guide is bought by readers in Japan, Germany and Canada on its first day, with no shipping.","options":["Global reach","Analytics","Content creation"],"answer":0,"fb":"Reaching a worldwide audience without physical distribution channels is global reach."},{"text":"A platform offers templates to lay out text, add images and build a quiz at the end of each chapter.","options":["Monetisation","Distribution","Content creation"],"answer":2,"fb":"Tools for creating and formatting content are the content creation feature."}],"win":"Excellent. You can identify all of the key features of digital publishing platforms: content creation, distribution, monetisation, accessibility, global reach, interactivity, analytics and self-publishing.","tutor":[{"h":"Money, data or devices?","p":"<p>Ask what the situation is about: earning money, collecting data, or opening on different devices.</p>"},{"h":"Who is the gatekeeper?","p":"<p>If no publishing house was needed, think about who is doing the publishing.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which key feature? (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"An author sees how many people read her book.","options":["Monetisation","Analytics"],"answer":1,"fb":"Tracking readers is analytics."},{"text":"An author sells her e-book for a price.","options":["Interactivity","Monetisation"],"answer":1,"fb":"Earning money is monetisation."},{"text":"An e-book has videos and links inside it.","options":["Interactivity","Self-publishing"],"answer":0,"fb":"Multimedia and links are interactivity."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Each situation shows more than one feature at work. Choose the feature that is <b>MOST</b> directly shown by the detail in bold type.</p>","items":[{"text":"A St. Kitts author notices that 70% of readers stop at chapter 4, so she <b>rewrites that chapter and re-uploads the file</b> after studying the dashboard.","options":["Distribution","Analytics","Interactivity","Content creation"],"answer":1,"fb":"The decision came from engagement data on the dashboard, which is analytics. Re-uploading is a result, not the feature that informed it."},{"text":"A Jamaican comic artist offers chapter 1 free but <b>charges a monthly subscription for new chapters</b>.","options":["Self-publishing","Monetisation","Accessibility","Global reach"],"answer":1,"fb":"Subscriptions are a way of earning from content, so this is monetisation."},{"text":"A Guyanese history e-book can be <b>opened on a Kindle, an Android phone and a library computer</b> with the same account.","options":["Accessibility","Analytics","Distribution","Global reach"],"answer":0,"fb":"Being usable on many kinds of device is the accessibility feature. Distribution is about making it available, not about which devices open it."},{"text":"A Dominica tour guide <b>uploads one file and it becomes available for download, streaming and browser reading</b> the same day.","options":["Interactivity","Content creation","Distribution","Self-publishing"],"answer":2,"fb":"Making content available to readers through downloads, streaming or browsers is distribution."},{"text":"A Guyanese choir adds <b>tap-to-play audio of each song and links to a lyrics archive</b> inside its songbook.","options":["Monetisation","Interactivity","Analytics","Accessibility"],"answer":1,"fb":"Embedded multimedia and hyperlinks that print cannot offer are interactivity."},{"text":"A Belize teenager <b>publishes her novel without any agent or publishing house accepting it</b>, and it is bought in Ghana and Canada.","options":["Self-publishing","Global reach","Distribution","Monetisation"],"answer":0,"fb":"The bold detail is about bypassing the traditional publisher, which is self-publishing. The overseas sales show global reach, but that is not the part in bold."},{"text":"A Trinidad teacher uses <b>built-in templates to lay out text, insert diagrams and build end-of-chapter quizzes</b>.","options":["Self-publishing","Distribution","Interactivity","Content creation"],"answer":3,"fb":"Tools for creating and formatting content are the content creation feature. The quizzes become interactive, but the bold detail is about the tools used to build them."}],"win":"Excellent analysis. You separated the feature the detail shows from the other features happening around it, which is exactly the precision an examiner rewards.","title":"Which key feature is this? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Digital publishing platforms and tools","html":"<p>A <b>digital publishing platform</b> is a service that lets authors upload content and sell or distribute it to readers. There are six popular platforms: <b>Amazon KDP, Apple Books, Google Play Books, Kobo Writing Life, Wattpad and CMS platforms</b> such as WordPress. Learn all of them, along with the others below.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Platform</th><th>What it is</th><th>Key point for the exam</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Amazon Kindle Direct Publishing (KDP)</b></td><td>Amazon's self-publishing service for Kindle e-books and print-on-demand paperbacks</td><td>Authors upload, set prices and reach a global audience; largest e-book store; Kindle e-books do not need an ISBN because Amazon assigns its own ASIN number</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Apple Books</b></td><td>Apple's store and reading app on iPhone, iPad and Mac</td><td>Strong support for EPUB 3 and interactive, fixed-layout books</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Google Play Books</b></td><td>Google's platform for selling and distributing digital books: e-books, audiobooks and comics; uploads go through the Play Books Partner Center</td><td>Reaches Android users, who are the majority of smartphone users in much of the Caribbean</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Kobo Writing Life</b></td><td>Self-publishing portal for Rakuten Kobo's store and e-readers</td><td>Publishes and distributes e-books globally; Kobo is known for its e-reader devices and wide range of e-books</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Wattpad</b></td><td>A platform for sharing and discovering stories, often written chapter by chapter with reader comments</td><td>Has grown a paid programme, Wattpad Books, where selected stories are published in various formats, including e-books</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Barnes &amp; Noble Press (Nook)</b></td><td>The US bookseller's self-publishing service; Nook is its e-reader and app</td><td>E-books and print books for the US market</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>IngramSpark</b></td><td>Print-on-demand and e-book <b>distributor</b> linked to the Ingram network that supplies bookshops and libraries</td><td>Gets books into bookshop and library catalogues</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Draft2Digital</b></td><td>An <b>aggregator</b>: upload once, and it distributes to many stores</td><td>Saves time; takes a share of each sale instead of an upfront fee</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>CMS platforms</b> such as WordPress are also on the list: they are used for creating and managing digital content and publications on the web (see the next learn screen).</p>\n<p><b>Retailer or distributor?</b> KDP, Apple Books, Google Play Books, Kobo and Barnes &amp; Noble are <b>retail platforms</b>: the store where the reader buys. IngramSpark and Draft2Digital are <b>distribution platforms</b>: they deliver one file to many retailers. These are also called <b>digital distribution platforms</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Tools</b> used to prepare content include word processors, Kindle Create, Apple Pages, Calibre (a free e-book converter and manager), Sigil (a free EPUB editor), Canva for covers and Adobe InDesign for professional layout.</p>\n<p>Example: St. Lucian author Mrs. Joseph writes a children's book about a Kwéyòl folk tale. She could upload separately to five stores, or upload once to Draft2Digital and let it distribute. She might also use KDP for print-on-demand paperbacks so that schools can order printed copies.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 01 often tests the difference between a <b>retailer</b> (sells to the reader) and an <b>aggregator or distributor</b> (sends your book to many retailers). Learn which platforms fall into each group.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A publishing platform is where an author uploads a book so readers can buy or read it. Retail platforms such as Amazon KDP, Apple Books, Google Play Books and Kobo are the shops. Distribution platforms such as Draft2Digital and IngramSpark send one file to many shops. A St. Lucian author can upload once and reach five stores.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Choosing a platform is a trade-off. Amazon KDP reaches the largest store, but its KDP Select programme asks for exclusivity, so the e-book cannot be sold on Kobo or Apple Books at the same time. An aggregator such as Draft2Digital saves time but takes a percentage of each sale. Google Play Books matters in the Caribbean because Android phones are so common. A Grade One answer recommends a <b>combination</b> and justifies it against the author's goals: for example, KDP for Amazon readers plus Draft2Digital for every other store, and IngramSpark if schools and libraries must be able to order printed copies.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the publishing platform","intro":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is advising three local authors. Match each platform to its best description.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Amazon KDP","r":"Self-publishing for Kindle e-books and print-on-demand paperbacks; assigns an ASIN"},{"l":"Draft2Digital","r":"An aggregator: upload once and the book goes to many stores"},{"l":"IngramSpark","r":"Distributor that places books in bookshop and library catalogues"},{"l":"Apple Books","r":"Store and reading app on iPhone, iPad and Mac with strong EPUB 3 support"},{"l":"Google Play Books","r":"E-book store reached through the Partner Center, strong on Android devices"},{"l":"Kobo Writing Life","r":"Self-publishing portal for Rakuten Kobo e-readers and store"},{"l":"Barnes &amp; Noble Press","r":"US bookseller service publishing to the Nook e-reader and app"},{"l":"Wattpad","r":"Story-sharing community whose Wattpad Books programme publishes selected stories"}],"win":"Well matched. Wattpad shows how a story-sharing community can become a publisher. You can now distinguish retail platforms (KDP, Apple Books, Google Play Books, Kobo, Barnes &amp; Noble) from distribution platforms (IngramSpark, Draft2Digital).","tutor":[{"h":"Retailer or middleman?","p":"<p>Two of these do not sell directly to readers; they pass your book on to other stores.</p>"},{"h":"Think of the device","p":"<p>Kindle, Nook, Kobo, iPad and Android phones each link to one platform.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the platform (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Amazon KDP","r":"Kindle e-books and paperbacks"},{"l":"Draft2Digital","r":"Upload once, sent to many stores"},{"l":"Apple Books","r":"Reading app on iPhone and iPad"},{"l":"Wattpad","r":"Share and discover stories online"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Malik has eight authors with very specific needs. Match each platform to the author it suits BEST.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Amazon KDP","r":"A Nevis novelist wants the largest single e-book store and is happy with an ASIN instead of buying an ISBN"},{"l":"Draft2Digital","r":"A Guyanese historian has no time to upload to six stores separately"},{"l":"IngramSpark","r":"A Barbados school wants libraries and bookshops to be able to order the printed textbook"},{"l":"Apple Books","r":"An Antigua illustrator needs a fixed-layout EPUB 3 picture book for iPad readers"},{"l":"Google Play Books","r":"A Jamaican author whose readers mostly use Android phones"},{"l":"Kobo Writing Life","r":"A Curaçao writer targeting readers who own Kobo e-readers in the Netherlands and Canada"},{"l":"Barnes &amp; Noble Press","r":"A Virgin Islands author targeting Nook readers in the United States"},{"l":"Wattpad","r":"A Form 6 student wants to post a story chapter by chapter and grow readers through comments first"}],"win":"Well matched. You chose each platform by its audience, device and purpose, the reasoning Paper 02 rewards when you recommend a platform.","title":"Match the publishing platform (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Content management systems (CMS)","html":"<p class=\"passage\">A <b>content management system (CMS)</b> is software that lets users create, edit, organise and publish web content through a dashboard, without writing all the code by hand.</p>\n<p>A CMS separates <b>content</b> (the words, images and videos stored in a database) from <b>design</b> (themes and templates). That means Keisha, the marketing lead at Island Pulse Media, can post a new Carnival schedule from her phone while Maya, the web developer, controls the look of the site.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>CMS</th><th>Type</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Limitations</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>WordPress</b></td><td>Open-source (self-hosted WordPress.org) or hosted (WordPress.com)</td><td>Powers a very large share of all websites; thousands of themes and plugins; blogs to online shops</td><td>Self-hosted sites need updates and security care</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Wix</b></td><td>Proprietary, hosted, drag-and-drop</td><td>Very easy for beginners; templates; hosting included</td><td>Harder to move your site elsewhere; less control</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Squarespace</b></td><td>Proprietary, hosted</td><td>Polished, design-led templates; good for portfolios, restaurants and small shops</td><td>Paid plans only; fewer extensions</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Joomla</b></td><td>Open-source, self-hosted</td><td>Flexible; good for membership sites and multilingual content</td><td>Steeper learning curve than WordPress</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Common CMS features</b>: user roles (administrator, editor, author), a media library, scheduled posts, categories and tags, templates and themes, plugins or apps, built-in SEO settings and revision history.</p>\n<p><b>Why a CMS is a digital publishing tool</b>: a Grenada online newspaper, a Belize tour operator's blog and a Sint Maarten school website can all publish and update daily using a CMS. Each new post is a digital publication.</p>\n<p>You will meet CMS types (enterprise, web, document, mobile and headless CMS) in detail in unit 2.9. For now, know the four named examples and the difference between <b>open-source</b> (free code you can modify and host yourself) and <b>proprietary hosted</b> (a company owns the software and hosts it for a monthly fee).</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> If asked to recommend a CMS, justify it against the client: budget, technical skill, how much control is needed and who will update the site. \"Wix suits the Mango Café because the owner has no coding skills and needs hosting included\" earns more than \"Wix is easy\".</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A content management system (CMS) is software that lets you add and change website content through a simple dashboard, without writing code each time. WordPress, Wix, Squarespace and Joomla are examples. A bakery owner in Grenada could post a new cake photo from her phone, while a web designer looks after how the site looks.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The key idea is the <b>separation of content from design</b>. Because the words and images sit in a database and the look sits in a theme, a whole site can be redesigned without retyping a single post. The difference between open-source and proprietary is often misunderstood: open-source WordPress.org is free to download, but the owner still pays for hosting and must handle updates and security. Proprietary Wix includes hosting and security, but it is harder to move the site elsewhere. An evaluation should weigh cost, control, skills and who will update the site, not simply say one CMS is \"better\".</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Platform or CMS? Make the call","intro":"<p>Clients are calling the Island Pulse Media. Choose the best option for each.</p>","items":[{"text":"An Ocho Rios restaurant owner with no coding skills wants a beautiful menu site, hosting included, set up this week.","options":["IngramSpark","Self-hosted Joomla","Wix or Squarespace"],"answer":2,"fb":"Hosted drag-and-drop builders suit beginners who need speed and included hosting. Joomla needs technical skill, and IngramSpark is for books."},{"text":"A Guyanese historian wants her e-book in Apple Books, Kobo and Barnes &amp; Noble without uploading three times.","options":["Amazon KDP only","Wix","Draft2Digital"],"answer":2,"fb":"Draft2Digital is an aggregator: one upload, many stores. KDP reaches only Amazon."},{"text":"A Jamaican news start-up needs a flexible, open-source site with plugins and several editors with different roles.","options":["Squarespace","Google Play Books","WordPress (self-hosted)"],"answer":2,"fb":"Self-hosted WordPress is open-source, has a huge plugin library and supports user roles such as editor and author."},{"text":"A Barbadian poet wants her printed collection listed in bookshop and library catalogues.","options":["Kobo Writing Life","Wix","IngramSpark"],"answer":2,"fb":"IngramSpark distributes print-on-demand books through the network that supplies bookshops and libraries."},{"text":"A Trinidad author wants the biggest single e-book store and does not want to buy an ISBN for the Kindle edition.","options":["Amazon KDP","Barnes &amp; Noble Press","Joomla"],"answer":0,"fb":"KDP is the largest e-book store and assigns its own ASIN, so a Kindle e-book does not need a purchased ISBN."},{"text":"A Curaçao community group wants a multilingual site (Papiamentu, Dutch, English) and has a volunteer developer.","options":["Draft2Digital","Joomla","Apple Books"],"answer":1,"fb":"Joomla has strong multilingual features and suits a team with some technical skill."}],"win":"Excellent. You matched each platform or CMS to the client's needs, skills and budget, which is how Paper 02 expects you to justify a recommendation.","tutor":[{"h":"Book or website?","p":"<p>Books go to publishing platforms; websites and blogs go to a CMS.</p>"},{"h":"Skill and budget","p":"<p>No skills and little time point to hosted builders. A developer on the team opens up open-source options.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Platform or CMS? (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A beginner wants a hosted, drag-and-drop website.","options":["IngramSpark","Wix"],"answer":1,"fb":"Wix is a hosted, beginner-friendly CMS."},{"text":"An author wants one upload to reach many e-book stores.","options":["Squarespace","Draft2Digital"],"answer":1,"fb":"Draft2Digital is an aggregator."},{"text":"A team wants an open-source CMS with many plugins.","options":["WordPress","Apple Books"],"answer":0,"fb":"WordPress is open-source with a huge plugin library."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>These clients have competing needs. Choose the BEST recommendation for each, considering budget, skills, control and audience.</p>","items":[{"text":"A St. Vincent craft shop wants an online store with hundreds of products, full control of its data and a part-time developer to maintain it.","options":["Draft2Digital","Wattpad","Squarespace basic plan","Self-hosted WordPress with a shop plugin"],"answer":3,"fb":"Self-hosted WordPress gives full control and shop plugins, and the developer can manage updates and security."},{"text":"A Bridgetown photographer with no coding skills wants a polished portfolio this weekend and will pay a monthly fee.","options":["Squarespace","Self-hosted Joomla","IngramSpark","Hand-coded HTML"],"answer":0,"fb":"Squarespace offers design-led templates with hosting included, ideal for a portfolio built quickly without code."},{"text":"A Trinidad author wants her e-book in Amazon and every other major store but has only one evening to upload.","options":["Barnes &amp; Noble Press only","Wix","Kobo Writing Life only","Amazon KDP plus Draft2Digital for the other stores"],"answer":3,"fb":"KDP covers Amazon directly and the aggregator reaches the other stores with one upload. Note: this cannot be combined with KDP Select exclusivity."},{"text":"A Suriname community radio station needs pages in Dutch, Sranan Tongo and English, with volunteer editors given different permissions.","options":["Apple Books","Google Play Books","Joomla","A PDF newsletter"],"answer":2,"fb":"Joomla is strong on multilingual content and user roles, and suits a team with some technical help."},{"text":"A Bahamas poet wants her printed collection to appear in library catalogues and also sell as an e-book in many stores.","options":["IngramSpark for print, plus an e-book distributor or retailers","Wix only","Wattpad only","Squarespace only"],"answer":0,"fb":"IngramSpark places print books in library and bookshop catalogues; an aggregator or direct retailers cover the e-book."},{"text":"A Montserrat heritage group has no budget, no developer and needs a simple site its members can update.","options":["A custom-coded app","IngramSpark","A free hosted plan such as WordPress.com or Wix free","Self-hosted Joomla on a paid server"],"answer":2,"fb":"A free hosted plan costs nothing and needs no technical skill. The trade-offs are adverts, a sub-domain and fewer features."}],"win":"Strong evaluation. You balanced budget, skill, control and audience for each client, which is what \"recommend and justify\" questions require.","title":"Platform or CMS? Make the call (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"ePub, EPUB 3, PDF and interactive e-books","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>ePub (Electronic Publication)</b>: a digital e-book format widely used for e-books. It is an open standard and is compatible with many e-readers and reading apps.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Update:</b> Older sources say ePub is maintained by the International Digital Publishing Forum (IDPF). That was true until 2017, when the IDPF merged into the <b>W3C</b> (World Wide Web Consortium). Today the W3C maintains the EPUB standard.</p>\n<p>An EPUB file is really a ZIP package containing web files: XHTML for text, CSS for styling, images, fonts and a <b>package file</b> listing the contents and metadata.</p>\n<p><b>EPUB 3</b>, the current version, is built on HTML5. It adds embedded <b>audio and video</b>, <b>scripting</b> for interactivity, <b>MathML</b> for equations, better support for right-to-left and vertical languages, <b>fixed-layout</b> pages and stronger <b>accessibility</b> features such as navigation for screen readers and image descriptions.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Reflowable layout</th><th>Fixed layout</th></tr>\n<tr><td>How it behaves</td><td>Text flows to fit any screen; reader can change font size</td><td>Every page looks exactly as designed, like a printed page</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Novels, essays, text-heavy books</td><td>Children's picture books, comics, cookbooks, textbooks with complex layouts</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Accessibility</td><td>Strong: resizable text, screen readers</td><td>Weaker on small phones: readers must zoom</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>PDF vs ePub.</b> PDF (Portable Document Format, created by Adobe in 1993 and now an ISO standard) is a <b>fixed</b> format: it keeps exact layout for printing and forms, but is awkward to read on a phone. ePub is usually <b>reflowable</b> and designed for screens. A Hurricane Irma recovery report from a government office is best as a PDF; a novel set in Gouyave is best as a reflowable ePub.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Interactive e-books</b>: e-books that include interactive elements, such as multimedia, quizzes and other engaging features.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Update:</b> iBooks Author was once a popular creation tool. Apple discontinued iBooks Author in 2020; <b>Apple Pages</b> can now create EPUB books. Other options include Kotobee, Book Creator, Sigil and Adobe InDesign.</p>\n<p>Interactive e-books go beyond text. They may include narration with highlighted words (read-aloud), embedded video, pop-up glossaries, quizzes, animations and hyperlinks. An interactive guide to Bonaire's flamingos might let readers tap a flamingo to hear its call. Interactive features rely on EPUB 3 or on app-based books, and not every e-reader supports them, so publishers test on several devices.</p>\n<p>Kindle devices use Amazon's own formats, but KDP accepts EPUB uploads and converts them.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When asked to \"distinguish between PDF and ePub\", contrast them on the same feature: \"PDF has a fixed layout that looks the same on every screen, whereas a reflowable ePub adjusts its text to fit the reader's screen.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>ePub is an open e-book format that most reading apps can open. Its text usually reflows, which means it rearranges itself to fit any screen. EPUB 3 can also hold audio, video and quizzes. A PDF keeps every page fixed, like paper. A novel set in Gouyave suits ePub; a printed government report suits PDF.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Reflowable versus fixed is a design decision, not just a technical one. Fixed layout protects the designer's spreads but harms accessibility on phones, because readers must pinch and zoom. EPUB 3 supports both, so the question to ask is \"does the meaning depend on exact position?\" A frequent exam error is claiming that PDF \"cannot be read on phones\"; it can, but it does not adapt to the screen. Remember the standards update: the W3C, not the IDPF, now maintains EPUB. A strong answer to \"distinguish\" contrasts both formats on the same feature in one sentence, then recommends one for a named reader and device.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Reflowable or fixed layout?","intro":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is converting a stack of client projects into e-books. Decide the best layout for each.</p>","main":"Should this be published as reflowable or fixed layout?","bins":["Reflowable","Fixed layout"],"items":[{"t":"A comic about a Carnival superhero, panel by panel.","bin":1,"fb":"Comic panels depend on exact placement: fixed layout."},{"t":"A poetry collection where readers with low vision need very large text.","bin":0,"fb":"Reflowable text can be enlarged without breaking the page, which supports accessibility."},{"t":"A children's picture book where the text sits inside the illustrations.","bin":1,"fb":"Text placed on artwork must stay in position, so fixed layout is needed."},{"t":"A 300-page novel about a family in San Fernando.","bin":0,"fb":"Text-heavy novels should reflow so readers can resize text on any screen."},{"t":"A collection of short essays on Caribbean identity.","bin":0,"fb":"Plain text suits reflowable layout and screen readers."},{"t":"A replica of a printed tourism magazine with full-page adverts.","bin":1,"fb":"A magazine replica must look like the print design, so it is fixed layout (or a PDF)."},{"t":"A recipe book for johnny cakes and coconut tarts with photos beside each step.","bin":1,"fb":"Designed spreads with photos beside steps keep their meaning only in fixed layout."},{"t":"A guide to CAPE study skills that students read on phones.","bin":0,"fb":"Small phone screens need text that reflows."}],"win":"Well sorted. Reflowable suits text-heavy books and accessibility; fixed layout suits designed pages such as picture books, comics, cookbooks and magazines.","tutor":[{"h":"Does position matter?","p":"<p>If moving the text would break the meaning of the page, it needs a fixed layout.</p>"},{"h":"Think of the reader","p":"<p>Readers on phones and readers who need bigger text benefit from text that flows.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Reflowable or fixed? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A text-only novel.","bin":0,"fb":"Text flows: reflowable."},{"t":"A comic book.","bin":1,"fb":"Panels stay in place: fixed."},{"t":"A picture book with text on the art.","bin":1,"fb":"Position matters: fixed."},{"t":"Essays read on a phone.","bin":0,"fb":"Small screens need reflowable text."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder cases: some books mix text and design. Decide which layout keeps the book's meaning for its readers, thinking about device and accessibility.</p>","items":[{"t":"A long-read history of the Haitian Revolution with three small maps and an embedded audio interview.","bin":0,"fb":"It is mostly text, and EPUB 3 can embed audio and images in a reflowable book, so reflowable keeps it readable on phones."},{"t":"A CAPE Mathematics workbook where answers must be written into boxes beside each diagram.","bin":1,"fb":"The boxes depend on exact positions beside the diagrams, so fixed layout is needed."},{"t":"A dyslexia-friendly reader where users switch to a special font and wider spacing.","bin":0,"fb":"Changing font and spacing is only possible when text can reflow."},{"t":"A graphic novel about the Arawak and Kalinago peoples, told in illustrated panels.","bin":1,"fb":"Panel art and speech bubbles must stay in place, so fixed layout is required."},{"t":"An illustrated Antiguan cookbook where each photo sits beside its numbered method steps.","bin":1,"fb":"The photo-to-step relationship is part of the meaning, so it needs fixed layout."},{"t":"A collection of calypso lyrics and essays read mostly on phones during commutes.","bin":0,"fb":"Text-heavy content read on small screens should reflow."},{"t":"A digital replica of a 1970s Jamaica Gleaner front page for an archive.","bin":1,"fb":"A replica must look exactly like the original, so it is fixed layout (or PDF)."},{"t":"A travel guide whose readers in Belize often enlarge text to read outdoors in sunlight.","bin":0,"fb":"Readers who enlarge text need reflowable layout so lines rewrap instead of running off the screen."},{"t":"A children's alphabet book where each letter is drawn into the scenery of a Grenada beach.","bin":1,"fb":"The letters are part of the artwork, so their position cannot change."},{"t":"A government policy paper that readers will cite by exact page number in court.","bin":1,"fb":"Stable page numbers are only guaranteed by a fixed format such as PDF or fixed-layout EPUB."}],"win":"Well analysed. The test is always: does the meaning depend on exact position? If yes, fixed layout; if the reader needs to resize or restyle text, reflowable.","title":"Reflowable or fixed layout? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"E-books, their advantages and disadvantages, and e-reader vs tablet","html":"<p>An <b>e-book</b> is a book published in digital form, read on an e-reader, tablet, smartphone or computer. Here are the advantages and disadvantages.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Advantages of e-books</th><th>Disadvantages of e-books</th></tr>\n<tr><td>\n<ul>\n<li>Portable: one device, many books (one e-reader can hold thousands)</li>\n<li>Eco-friendly: no paper, and no physical shipping</li>\n<li>Shareable: content can be shared, liked and discussed</li>\n<li>Relatively cheap</li>\n<li>Accessible everywhere: download almost instantly, from almost anywhere</li>\n<li>Cannot be damaged physically</li>\n<li>Allows digital convergence: text, video and audio together</li>\n<li>Can be accessed on multiple devices</li>\n<li>Can rearrange the flow of text (reflow)</li>\n<li>Easy bookmarking</li>\n<li>Easily updated: authors add up-to-date information without reprinting costs</li>\n<li>Allows the extension of knowledge through hyperlinks and hypermedia</li>\n</ul></td><td>\n<ul>\n<li>Requires some type of power source</li>\n<li>Can cause eye strain</li>\n<li>Demotivates hard-copy readers (not suited to all types of readers)</li>\n<li>Takes up storage</li>\n<li>Cannot be accessed without a device</li>\n<li>Some are device specific: you may lose access if you lose your account</li>\n<li>Can be hacked</li>\n</ul></td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Caribbean context.</b> After Hurricane Irma, many homes in Sint Maarten had no power for weeks, so an e-book library was useless until devices could be charged, while a paperback still worked by candlelight. On the other hand, a student in Belmopan can download a set text in seconds instead of waiting weeks for a book to be shipped to the island.</p>\n<p><b>Why buy an e-reader when I have a smartphone or tablet?</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>E-reader (for example Kindle, Kobo)</th><th>Tablet (for example iPad, Android tablet)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Size and weight</td><td>Usually smaller and lighter, so more portable and easier on the wrists</td><td>Heavier; built for many tasks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Screen</td><td>Non-glare screen, better for reading in bright sunlight (useful on a beach at Grace Bay)</td><td>Backlit, glossy screen that is hard to read in sunlight</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Long reading of text-heavy books</td><td>Interactive, colour and video-rich e-books, apps and browsing</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For \"evaluate\" or \"discuss the advantages and disadvantages\", give at least two points on each side and finish with a judgement for a named reader: \"For a CAPE student in Castries, e-books are better because set texts arrive instantly and cost less, as long as the student has a charged device.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>An e-book is a book you read on a screen. E-books are light, cheap, quick to download and easy to update, but they need power and a device and can strain your eyes. An e-reader such as a Kindle has a non-glare screen, so you can read it in bright sun at Grace Bay, while a glossy tablet is harder to see there.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Advantages and disadvantages depend on <b>who</b> is reading and <b>where</b>. For a Caribbean reader, the cost and delay of shipping printed books makes instant download a powerful advantage, but frequent power cuts in hurricane season turn device dependence into a serious weakness. Some points cut both ways: easy sharing helps word of mouth but also encourages illegal copying that reduces an author's income. A Grade One evaluation weighs at least two points on each side, then reaches a justified judgement for a named reader, such as a CAPE student with a charged phone, rather than declaring e-books simply \"better\" or \"worse\" than print.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Advantage or disadvantage of e-books?","intro":"<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media is building a slide for a school library talk. Sort each statement about e-books.</p>","main":"Is this an advantage or a disadvantage of e-books?","bins":["Advantage","Disadvantage"],"items":[{"t":"Reading on a bright screen for hours gives a student tired eyes.","bin":1,"fb":"Eye strain is one of the listed disadvantages."},{"t":"A reader loses her whole library when she loses access to her account.","bin":1,"fb":"Some e-books are device or account specific, so access can be lost."},{"t":"The device battery dies during a power cut after a storm.","bin":1,"fb":"E-books require a power source, a real problem during outages."},{"t":"A student with low vision makes the text larger and it reflows.","bin":0,"fb":"Rearranging the flow of text supports accessibility."},{"t":"One e-reader can hold thousands of books for a long trip to Guyana.","bin":0,"fb":"Portability (one device, many books) is a key advantage."},{"t":"A history e-book embeds video of the 1848 Emancipation commemoration and audio interviews.","bin":0,"fb":"Digital convergence of text, video and audio is an advantage print cannot offer."},{"t":"Some readers who love hard copies lose motivation to read on a screen.","bin":1,"fb":"E-books are not suited to all types of readers."},{"t":"The author fixes an error and every reader gets the updated edition.","bin":0,"fb":"E-books can be easily updated without reprinting costs."},{"t":"A reader's account is hacked and her purchases are stolen.","bin":1,"fb":"E-books and accounts can be hacked, a security disadvantage."}],"win":"Well sorted. You can weigh the advantages of e-books (portable, cheap, updatable, convergent, reflowable) against the disadvantages (power, eye strain, device dependence, hacking).","tutor":[{"h":"Who benefits?","p":"<p>If the statement makes reading easier, cheaper or richer, it is likely an advantage.</p>"},{"h":"Depend on what?","p":"<p>Anything that depends on power, a device, an account or security points to the other side.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Advantage or disadvantage? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Holds thousands of books.","bin":0,"fb":"Portable: an advantage."},{"t":"Needs a charged battery.","bin":1,"fb":"Needs power: a disadvantage."},{"t":"Can cause eye strain.","bin":1,"fb":"Eye strain is a disadvantage."},{"t":"Easy bookmarking.","bin":0,"fb":"Bookmarking is an advantage."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Each card describes a real situation. Judge whether it shows an advantage or a disadvantage of e-books <b>for the person named</b>.</p>","items":[{"t":"A cruise-ship worker from St. Vincent carries 300 books on her phone for a six-month contract.","bin":0,"fb":"Portability is a clear advantage for someone living away from home with little space."},{"t":"A Dominica author finds free copies of her e-book shared in WhatsApp groups, and her sales fall.","bin":1,"fb":"Easy copying and illegal sharing reduce the author's income, a disadvantage for her."},{"t":"A grandmother in Grenada with low vision has the e-book read aloud by text-to-speech.","bin":0,"fb":"Text-to-speech and resizable text are accessibility advantages."},{"t":"A Form 5 student in rural Guyana cannot download the set text because the mobile signal is weak.","bin":1,"fb":"E-books depend on connectivity and a device, a real barrier in areas with poor coverage."},{"t":"A Belize publisher corrects a wrong date and every buyer's copy updates overnight.","bin":0,"fb":"Updating without reprinting is an advantage for the publisher and the reader."},{"t":"A reader in Anguilla loses a whole library when a store closes its e-book service.","bin":1,"fb":"E-books are often licensed to an account or device, so access can be lost."},{"t":"A Bajan teacher clicks a hyperlink in the e-book to watch a Crop Over video during the lesson.","bin":0,"fb":"Hyperlinks and digital convergence extend learning beyond the page."},{"t":"A student in Antigua reads for three hours on a bright phone and gets headaches.","bin":1,"fb":"Eye strain from long screen reading is a disadvantage."},{"t":"A library in Tobago lends e-books to members without needing extra shelf space.","bin":0,"fb":"E-books take no physical space and cannot be damaged, an advantage for libraries."},{"t":"After a storm, a family in Tortola has no electricity for two weeks to charge their e-readers.","bin":1,"fb":"E-books require a power source, a serious weakness during long outages."}],"win":"Strong judgement. You judged each point from the named person's position, which is how to build a balanced \"evaluate\" answer.","title":"Advantage or disadvantage of e-books? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"ISBN and metadata","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>ISBN (International Standard Book Number)</b>: a unique identifier for books, including digital books. It helps in cataloguing and identifying specific editions of a book. <b>Metadata</b>: information about a digital book that helps with its discovery and categorisation, including title, author, description, genre and keywords. Metadata is crucial for searchability and proper categorisation on digital platforms.</p>\n<p>A modern ISBN is a unique 13-digit number that identifies one specific edition and format of a book. Since 2007 ISBNs have 13 digits; older books carry 10-digit ISBNs.</p>\n<p><b>Key ISBN facts</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Each <b>format</b> needs its own ISBN: the paperback, hardback, EPUB and audiobook of the same title each get a different number.</li>\n<li>A new <b>edition</b> with significant changes needs a new ISBN; fixing a few typing errors does not.</li>\n<li>ISBNs are issued by the national ISBN agency that serves the publisher's country. Check which agency serves your territory before you publish.</li>\n<li>Some platforms provide a free identifier: Amazon assigns an <b>ASIN</b> to Kindle e-books, and some platforms offer free ISBNs, but then the platform may be listed as the publisher.</li>\n<li>The ISBN is printed above a barcode on the back cover of print books and appears in the e-book's metadata.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Metadata</b> is data about data. In publishing it is the information that describes a book so that stores, libraries and search engines can find, sort and sell it.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Metadata field</th><th>Example for a Turks and Caicos title</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Title and subtitle</td><td>Salt and Sea: A History of the Salt Cay Salinas</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Author / contributors</td><td>Author, illustrator, translator</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Description (blurb)</td><td>A clear 150 to 200 word summary with keywords</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Keywords and categories</td><td>Caribbean history, Turks and Caicos, salt industry</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Identifiers</td><td>ISBN or ASIN</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Language, publication date, publisher, price, rights territories</td><td>English, 2026, Pelican Press, worldwide</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Accessibility metadata</td><td>Has image descriptions, screen-reader friendly</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Why metadata matters</b>: good metadata improves <b>discoverability</b>. A reader in Antigua searching \"Caribbean history for teens\" will only find the book if its keywords and categories say so. Poor metadata (a vague title, no keywords, the wrong category) hides even an excellent book. Metadata also links to <b>SEO</b>, which you will study in unit 2.4.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A classic Paper 01 trap: one ISBN does <b>not</b> cover all formats. Paperback, e-book and audiobook each need their own.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>An ISBN is a 13-digit number that identifies one edition in one format of a book, so the paperback and the e-book each need their own number. Metadata is information that describes the book, such as title, author, description and keywords. Good keywords like \"Turks and Caicos history\" help a reader in Aruba find the book online.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Metadata is \"data about data\", and it is what search engines and online stores actually read. A brilliant book with weak metadata is effectively invisible, so publishers treat the description and keywords as marketing, not paperwork. A subtle point: a free ISBN from a platform may list that platform as the publisher, which limits where the author can use it. Another trap: an ASIN identifies a product on Amazon only; it is not an ISBN and will not work in other stores or library catalogues. Accessibility metadata is increasingly important too, because it tells a blind reader, before buying, whether the e-book will work with a screen reader.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the correct ISBN and metadata advice","intro":"<p>Zara asked a new intern at the Island Pulse Media to write advice for a client publishing a book on Antiguan folk tales. Some of the intern's lines are wrong.</p>","q":"Select ALL the lines that give correct advice.","lines":["The paperback and the EPUB edition each need their own ISBN.","One ISBN covers every format of the same book forever.","Keywords such as \"Caribbean folklore\" and \"Antigua\" help readers find the book.","Metadata only matters for printed books in libraries.","Amazon gives Kindle e-books an ASIN, so a purchased ISBN is optional for that edition.","A vague title and no description will not affect sales.","Accessibility metadata tells readers whether the e-book works with screen readers.","A modern ISBN has 13 digits."],"correct":[0,2,4,6,7],"win":"Spot on. Each format needs its own ISBN, modern ISBNs have 13 digits, and accurate metadata (keywords, description, accessibility) drives discoverability.","miss":"Look again. Remember: one ISBN per format, and metadata matters for every digital book because it is how stores and search engines find it.","tutor":[{"h":"Formats","p":"<p>Ask: is a paperback the same product as an e-book?</p>"},{"h":"Discoverability","p":"<p>How does an online store know what a book is about if no one tells it?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"ISBN and metadata (Foundation)","q":"Select the TWO correct lines.","lines":["Each format of a book needs its own ISBN.","Metadata does not affect whether readers find a book.","Good keywords improve discoverability.","An ISBN has 5 digits."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Correct. One ISBN per format, and keywords help readers find the book.","miss":"Not quite. Think about formats and how stores find books."},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Zara has drafted advanced advice for a Jamaican publisher releasing a paperback, an EPUB and an audiobook of a reggae history. Some lines are subtly wrong.</p>","q":"Select ALL the lines that are accurate.","lines":["The paperback, the EPUB and the audiobook each need a separate ISBN.","An Amazon ASIN can be used as the ISBN when the book is sold in other stores.","A second edition with two new chapters needs a new ISBN.","Correcting a few spelling errors in the EPUB requires a new ISBN.","A free ISBN from a platform may list that platform, not the author, as the publisher.","Repeating the keyword \"reggae\" twenty times in the description is the best way to rank higher.","Rights-territory metadata tells stores in which countries the book may be sold.","Accessibility metadata lets a blind reader check screen-reader support before buying.","Metadata matters less for e-books because readers find them by browsing shelves.","Books published before 2007 may carry older 10-digit ISBNs."],"correct":[0,2,4,6,7,9],"win":"Excellent. You spotted the subtle traps: an ASIN is not an ISBN, small corrections do not need a new ISBN, and keyword stuffing harms rather than helps.","miss":"Look again at the traps: an ASIN works only on Amazon, a few typing fixes do not need a new ISBN, keyword stuffing is penalised, and e-books depend on metadata even more than print.","title":"Spot the correct ISBN and metadata advice (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"A brief history of the Internet","html":"<p>The <b>Internet</b> is a global network of networks. The <b>World Wide Web</b> is one service that runs on it: a system of linked pages viewed in a browser. They are not the same thing. Email, streaming and online games also use the Internet.</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 640 120\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Timeline from ARPANET 1969 to smartphones 2007\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" style=\"max-width:640px\">\n<line x1=\"20\" y1=\"60\" x2=\"620\" y2=\"60\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n<g stroke-width=\"2\"><circle cx=\"40\" cy=\"60\" r=\"6\"/><circle cx=\"130\" cy=\"60\" r=\"6\"/><circle cx=\"220\" cy=\"60\" r=\"6\"/><circle cx=\"310\" cy=\"60\" r=\"6\"/><circle cx=\"400\" cy=\"60\" r=\"6\"/><circle cx=\"490\" cy=\"60\" r=\"6\"/><circle cx=\"590\" cy=\"60\" r=\"6\"/></g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" stroke=\"none\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"40\" y=\"40\">1969</text><text x=\"40\" y=\"90\">ARPANET</text>\n<text x=\"130\" y=\"40\">1971</text><text x=\"130\" y=\"90\">Email, e-books</text>\n<text x=\"220\" y=\"40\">1983</text><text x=\"220\" y=\"90\">TCP/IP</text>\n<text x=\"310\" y=\"40\">1989 to 91</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"90\">WWW</text>\n<text x=\"400\" y=\"40\">1993</text><text x=\"400\" y=\"90\">Mosaic</text>\n<text x=\"490\" y=\"40\">2004</text><text x=\"490\" y=\"90\">Web 2.0</text>\n<text x=\"590\" y=\"40\">2007</text><text x=\"590\" y=\"90\">Smartphones</text>\n</g></svg>\n<ul>\n<li><b>1960s, ARPANET</b>: the Internet originated during the 1960s as a means to share information, after the formation of ARPANET (Advanced Research Projects Agency Network). ARPANET was a great success, but membership was limited to academic and research organisations with Defense Department contracts, so other networks were created to share information too. Funded by the US Department of Defense, it linked university computers. The first message was sent from UCLA to the Stanford Research Institute in October 1969. It used <b>packet switching</b>: data is broken into packets that travel separately and are reassembled.</li>\n<li><b>1971</b>: Ray Tomlinson sent the first networked email using the @ sign, and Michael Hart started <b>Project Gutenberg</b>, often called the first e-book project.</li>\n<li><b>1 January 1983, TCP/IP</b>: before this, the various computer networks had no standard way to communicate with each other. ARPANET and the Defense Data Network officially changed to the TCP/IP standard on 1 January 1983, which is considered the official birthday of the Internet. TCP/IP allowed different kinds of computers on different networks to \"talk\" to each other: all networks could now be connected by a universal language.</li>\n<li><b>1989 to 1991, the World Wide Web</b>: Tim Berners-Lee at CERN proposed the Web in 1989 and created HTML, HTTP, URLs, the first browser and the first website (1991). CERN released the Web technology royalty-free in 1993, so anyone could build on it.</li>\n<li><b>1993, Mosaic</b>: the first widely popular graphical browser, showing images alongside text. It led to Netscape Navigator and the browser wars, and made the Web usable for ordinary people.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Update:</b> Some older sources expand TCP/IP as \"Transfer Control Protocol/Internetwork Protocol\". The correct current name is <b>Transmission Control Protocol/Internet Protocol</b>. Use this full form in the exam.</p>\n<p>The Caribbean came online through the 1990s, first through dial-up from national telephone companies, which, with newer providers such as Digicel and Flow, later brought broadband and mobile data.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Do not write \"Tim Berners-Lee invented the Internet.\" He invented the <b>World Wide Web</b>. The Internet grew from ARPANET and TCP/IP. This distinction is a favourite Paper 01 trap.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The Internet is a huge network that links computer networks around the world. It began as ARPANET in 1969. On 1 January 1983 the networks agreed to share one set of rules, TCP/IP. Tim Berners-Lee then created the World Wide Web, pages you open in a browser. Your WhatsApp messages travel on the Internet, but they are not the Web.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The pattern in this history is <b>shared standards unlock growth</b>. ARPANET worked, but only when TCP/IP gave every network a common language could separate networks join into one Internet. Likewise, the Web spread because CERN released it royalty-free in 1993, so anyone could build browsers and sites without paying. Packet switching also made the network resilient: packets can take different routes if one link fails. In an exam, keep the Internet (the infrastructure) separate from the Web (one service running on it), use the correct expansion \"Transmission Control Protocol/Internet Protocol\", and explain why each milestone mattered rather than listing dates alone.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Put the digital revolution in order","intro":"<p>Jerome is building a \"then vs now\" timeline for an Island Pulse Media school workshop. Drag the milestones into the correct order, earliest first.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"ARPANET sends its first message between UCLA and Stanford Research Institute (1969)"},{"t":"Project Gutenberg begins publishing free e-books (1971)"},{"t":"ARPANET switches to TCP/IP (1983)"},{"t":"Tim Berners-Lee proposes the World Wide Web at CERN (1989)"},{"t":"The Mosaic graphical browser makes the Web popular (1993)"},{"t":"The term \"Web 2.0\" spreads as social, user-generated sites grow (2004)"},{"t":"Touchscreen smartphones and app stores bring the Web to pockets (2007 to 2008)"}],"win":"Correct order. The digital revolution moved from a research network (ARPANET) to shared rules (TCP/IP), to the Web, graphical browsers, social Web 2.0 and finally mobile.","tutor":[{"h":"Network before Web","p":"<p>The Web needed an Internet to run on. Which came first?</p>"},{"h":"Browsers","p":"<p>A graphical browser could only appear after the Web existed.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Order the milestones (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Earliest first.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"ARPANET (1969)"},{"t":"TCP/IP adopted (1983)"},{"t":"World Wide Web proposed (1989)"},{"t":"Mosaic browser (1993)"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>The dates have been removed and three milestones added. Put all ten in order, earliest first, using what you know about cause and effect: each development made the next one possible.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"ARPANET sends its first message from UCLA to the Stanford Research Institute"},{"t":"Ray Tomlinson sends the first networked email using the @ sign"},{"t":"Vint Cerf and Bob Kahn publish their design for the TCP protocol"},{"t":"ARPANET officially switches to TCP/IP, the Internet's \"birthday\""},{"t":"Tim Berners-Lee proposes the World Wide Web at CERN"},{"t":"The Mosaic graphical browser makes the Web popular"},{"t":"Wikipedia launches as an encyclopedia anyone can edit"},{"t":"The term \"Web 2.0\" spreads as social, user-generated sites grow"},{"t":"Amazon launches the Kindle e-reader"},{"t":"Apple opens the App Store for iPhone apps"}],"win":"Correct order: ARPANET (1969), email (1971), the TCP design (1974), TCP/IP (1983), the Web proposal (1989), Mosaic (1993), Wikipedia (2001), Web 2.0 (2004), the Kindle (2007) and the App Store (2008). Each step built on the one before.","title":"Put the digital revolution in order (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Web 1.0, 2.0, 3.0, broadband and mobile","html":"<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Web 1.0 (about 1991 to 2004)</th><th>Web 2.0 (about 2004 onward)</th><th>Web 3.0 (emerging)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Nickname</td><td>The \"read-only\" Web</td><td>The \"read-write\" or social Web</td><td>The \"read-write-own\" or intelligent Web</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Users</td><td>Mostly consumers of content</td><td>Creators and sharers (user-generated content)</td><td>Owners of data and digital assets; served by AI</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pages</td><td>Static HTML, few updates</td><td>Dynamic, interactive, constantly updated</td><td>Personalised, machine-readable, decentralised</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>Early company brochure sites, web directories</td><td>Facebook, YouTube, Wikipedia, blogs, Instagram</td><td>Semantic search, AI assistants, blockchain apps, digital wallets</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The term <b>Web 2.0</b> was popularised around 2004. <b>Web 3.0</b> has two meanings you should know: Tim Berners-Lee's <b>Semantic Web</b>, where data is tagged so machines can understand it, and the newer <b>Web3</b> idea of a decentralised web built on blockchain, where users own their data and assets. Web3 also appears as an emerging trend in unit 2.10.</p>\n<p><b>Broadband</b>: always-on, high-capacity connections (DSL, cable, fibre) replaced slow dial-up from the late 1990s and 2000s. Broadband made streaming video, podcasts and large e-book downloads practical. Undersea fibre-optic cables connect Caribbean islands to the global Internet, so a single cable cut can slow a whole island.</p>\n<p><b>Mobile</b>: from 2007 onward, smartphones and app stores moved the Web into people's pockets. In much of the Caribbean, many people's main or only Internet device is a smartphone, often on prepaid data from providers such as Digicel, Flow or TelEm. This is why publishers now design <b>mobile-first</b>: a St. Kitts news site must work on a small screen with limited data before it works on a desktop.</p>\n<p><b>Technological milestones of the digital revolution</b> therefore include the personal computer, the Internet, the Web, broadband, Wi-Fi, smartphones, cloud computing, social media and now AI.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For \"describe the changes from Web 1.0 to Web 2.0\", focus on the user's <b>role</b>: from passive reader to active creator and sharer. Give a Caribbean example of each.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Web 1.0 was the \"read-only\" web of static pages. Web 2.0 is the social \"read-write\" web, where users post, comment and share. Web 3.0 is the emerging intelligent and decentralised web. Broadband and smartphones made streaming and mobile use possible. A Trinidad teen posting a TikTok review of a doubles stand is using Web 2.0.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The stages overlap rather than replace one another: a static Web 1.0 style brochure site still exists alongside Web 2.0 social media. Note that \"Web 3.0\" has two meanings, Tim Berners-Lee's Semantic Web of machine-readable data and the blockchain-based \"Web3\" of user-owned assets, so define which one you mean. Infrastructure shapes publishing in the Caribbean: undersea fibre cables and prepaid mobile data explain why publishers design mobile-first and keep pages light. For \"describe the changes\", centre the answer on the user's role (reader, then creator, then owner) and support each stage with one regional example.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Web 1.0, 2.0 or 3.0?","intro":"<p>Keisha is preparing a social media explainer for Island Pulse Media. Sort each example into the correct stage of the Web.</p>","main":"Which stage of the Web does this example best represent?","bins":["Web 1.0","Web 2.0","Web 3.0"],"items":[{"t":"Visitors post TikTok reviews of a Negril beach bar.","bin":1,"fb":"User-generated content and sharing are the heart of Web 2.0."},{"t":"An early web directory where an editor lists links by hand.","bin":0,"fb":"Hand-built lists for reading only belong to Web 1.0."},{"t":"Students edit a Wikipedia article about Emancipation Day.","bin":1,"fb":"Collaborative, read-write editing is Web 2.0."},{"t":"An AI assistant personalises a news feed using linked data about the user's interests.","bin":2,"fb":"Intelligent, personalised and machine-readable services point to Web 3.0."},{"t":"A search engine understands \"cheap ferry to Anguilla tomorrow\" and shows times and prices from tagged data.","bin":2,"fb":"Machines understanding meaning from structured data is the Semantic Web side of Web 3.0."},{"t":"A Trinidad blogger gets comments and shares on every post.","bin":1,"fb":"Two-way interaction through comments and shares is Web 2.0."},{"t":"A digital wallet lets a Belize artist sell and prove ownership of digital art on a blockchain.","bin":2,"fb":"Decentralised ownership on a blockchain is the Web3 idea of Web 3.0."},{"t":"A 1998 hotel website with one static page of text and a phone number.","bin":0,"fb":"Static, read-only pages with no user interaction are Web 1.0."}],"win":"Well sorted. Web 1.0 is read-only, Web 2.0 is read-write and social, and Web 3.0 is intelligent, machine-readable and decentralised.","tutor":[{"h":"Who creates?","p":"<p>If only the site owner creates content, think 1.0. If users create and share, think 2.0.</p>"},{"h":"Machines and ownership","p":"<p>Machines understanding meaning, or users owning assets on a blockchain, points to the newest stage.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which Web? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A static page you can only read.","bin":0,"fb":"Read-only: Web 1.0."},{"t":"Posting and sharing videos on YouTube.","bin":1,"fb":"User-generated: Web 2.0."},{"t":"Owning digital art on a blockchain.","bin":2,"fb":"Decentralised ownership: Web 3.0."},{"t":"Commenting on a blog.","bin":1,"fb":"Two-way interaction: Web 2.0."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>These examples are harder to place. Look at how users interact with the content and how the data is handled, not at how modern the site looks.</p>","items":[{"t":"A 2026 Barbados restaurant site with a single fixed page, a menu and a phone number, and no way to comment or book.","bin":0,"fb":"Even if it was built recently, a static, read-only page follows the Web 1.0 model."},{"t":"A Jamaica Facebook group where members post hurricane updates and photos for each other.","bin":1,"fb":"Users creating and sharing content for each other is Web 2.0."},{"t":"A search engine reads structured data tags on a Barbados events page and shows the concert date directly in its results.","bin":2,"fb":"Machines understanding tagged, machine-readable data is the Semantic Web side of Web 3.0."},{"t":"A 1990s government directory listing ministries' phone numbers, updated by one webmaster.","bin":0,"fb":"A one-way, editor-built list is classic Web 1.0."},{"t":"A Belize musician sells a limited digital album whose ownership is recorded on a blockchain.","bin":2,"fb":"User-owned digital assets on a blockchain belong to the Web3 idea."},{"t":"Volunteers in Guyana add photos and corrections to an online map of local businesses.","bin":1,"fb":"Collaborative, user-generated editing is Web 2.0."},{"t":"A YouTube channel where a St. Lucian chef uploads videos and replies to viewers' comments.","bin":1,"fb":"Uploading and two-way comment conversations are Web 2.0."},{"t":"A decentralised social app where users keep their own data in a digital wallet and move it between services.","bin":2,"fb":"Users owning and controlling their data across services is the decentralised Web3 vision."},{"t":"An online brochure for a Dominica hotel from 1999, with photos but no forms or comments.","bin":0,"fb":"Static pages for reading only are Web 1.0."}],"win":"Well analysed. The stage depends on the user's role and how data is handled: read-only (1.0), read-write and social (2.0), or machine-readable and user-owned (3.0), not on the date the site was built.","title":"Web 1.0, 2.0 or 3.0? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Accessibility, global adoption and MMOGs","html":"<p><b>Accessibility</b> has two meanings in the syllabus, and you should use both.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Access to the Internet</b>: whether people can connect at all, which depends on infrastructure, cost of data and devices, and digital skills. The gap between those who can and cannot is the <b>digital divide</b>. The ITU estimated that roughly two thirds of the world's population used the Internet in 2023 (check the latest ITU figure). Within the Caribbean, rural Guyana or the interior of Belize may have far weaker coverage than Port of Spain or Bridgetown.</li>\n<li><b>Accessibility for people with disabilities</b>: designing content so that everyone can use it, including people who are blind, deaf, have low vision, limited mobility or learning differences. Examples: alt text on images, captions on video, transcripts for podcasts, resizable reflowable text, good colour contrast and screen-reader support. The <b>WCAG</b> (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines) from the W3C set the standard, and EPUB 3 includes accessibility features.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Global adoption</b>: the digital revolution spread through falling device prices, cheaper data, mobile networks and free platforms. Hurricane Irma in 2017 showed both sides: Sint Maarten residents relied on WhatsApp and social media to find family, yet many lost connectivity for days when infrastructure was damaged.</p>\n<p><b>MMOGs as digital publishing</b></p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>MMOGs</b> are online video games that support large numbers of players interacting in a persistent virtual world. These games often involve an open-world environment, where players can explore, complete quests and interact with each other in real time.</p>\n<p>\"Persistent\" means the world keeps running even when you log off. Examples include RuneScape (2001), World of Warcraft (2004), Fortnite (2017) and Roblox.</p>\n<p>Why do MMOGs count as digital publishing?</p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are <b>distributed digitally</b> through online stores and app stores, not on discs.</li>\n<li>They are <b>continuously published</b>: developers release patches, seasons, new maps and story content, like issues of a magazine.</li>\n<li>They contain <b>narrative, art, music and text</b>, the same content types as other publications.</li>\n<li>Many publish <b>user-generated content</b>: Roblox players build and publish their own games for others.</li>\n<li>They are <b>monetised</b> like digital media, through subscriptions, in-game purchases and advertising.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A team of young Jamaican developers could publish a Caribbean-themed world on Roblox and reach players globally without a traditional games publisher.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Explain why an MMOG is a form of digital publishing\" needs two or three linked reasons. Mention digital distribution, ongoing updates and user-generated content.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Accessibility means two things: whether people can get online at all, and whether people with disabilities can use the content. Captions, alt text and readable colours help everyone. An MMOG is a huge online game where thousands play in one world that keeps running. Roblox is digital publishing because new content is released online all the time.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The two meanings of accessibility connect: a site that is accessible to screen-reader users but needs 20 MB of data per page is still inaccessible to a prepaid-data user in rural Belize. A Grade One answer recognises both barriers. For MMOGs, avoid the weak reasoning \"it is online, so it is published\". The strong argument is that MMOGs are <b>continuously published</b> products: seasons, patches and story chapters are released like magazine issues, user-generated worlds are published to other players, and the whole product is distributed and monetised digitally. Link each reason to a named game and say what it shows.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"MMOGs and digital publishing","q":"The Kingston studio Yaad Games releases a free online game in which thousands of players explore a shared Caribbean island at the same time. Every month the studio publishes a new story chapter, and players can build and share their own levels. Which statement BEST explains why this is an example of digital publishing?","options":[{"t":"It uses graphics, and anything with graphics is digital publishing.","fb":"Graphics alone do not make a product a publication. The answer must link to how content is distributed and released."},{"t":"Thousands of people play it, so it is a website.","fb":"An MMOG is not the same as a website. Player numbers make it \"massively multiplayer\", but that does not explain the publishing link."},{"t":"Content is distributed online, published in ongoing updates and includes user-generated levels shared with other players.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Digital distribution, continuous publishing of new content and user-generated content are the key links between MMOGs and digital publishing."},{"t":"It is free, so it must be a form of publishing.","fb":"Price has nothing to do with whether something is published. Many paid products are publications too."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Look for publishing verbs","p":"<p>Which option talks about distributing, releasing and sharing content?</p>"},{"h":"Avoid weak reasons","p":"<p>Price and graphics are features, not reasons something counts as published.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"MMOGs and digital publishing (Foundation)","q":"Why is an MMOG a form of digital publishing?","options":[{"t":"It has colourful graphics.","fb":"Graphics alone do not make it a publication."},{"t":"It is free to play.","fb":"Price does not decide whether something is published."},{"t":"Its content is released and updated online for many players.","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. It is distributed and updated digitally."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A Trinidad studio publishes \"Island Quest\", an MMOG with monthly story seasons, in-game purchases and a level editor whose creations other players can download. A critic argues it is \"just a game, not digital publishing\". Which response is the STRONGEST rebuttal?","options":[{"t":"It is digital publishing because it is downloaded from an app store, which is the only feature that matters.","fb":"Digital distribution is relevant, but calling it the only feature makes the argument weaker, not stronger. The best rebuttal combines several reasons."},{"t":"It is digital publishing because thousands of people play it at the same time.","fb":"Player numbers explain \"massively multiplayer\", but they do not show any publishing process."},{"t":"It is digital publishing because it contains text, images and music.","fb":"Content types alone are not enough; a printed board game contains text and images too. The answer must explain how content is released and distributed."},{"t":"It is digital publishing because the studio continuously releases new narrative content online, publishes players' levels to others and earns through digital sales, just as a magazine publishes issues.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. This links three publishing features (continuous release, user-generated publication and digital monetisation) to the game, and draws an apt comparison."}],"title":"MMOGs and digital publishing (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Activity: a digital presentation on the history of the Internet","html":"<p>This is the class activity for this unit. It pulls together everything on the history of the Internet, the digital revolution, accessibility and MMOGs, and it practises the planning and digital production skills tested in Paper 02 and the SBA.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Practical task:</b> Design a digital presentation on the brief history of the Internet and the digital revolution. Identify key milestones in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Development</b>: for example ARPANET in the 1960s, the switch to TCP/IP on 1 January 1983, the World Wide Web (1989 to 1991).</li>\n<li><b>Technology</b>: for example packet switching, the Mosaic browser (1993), broadband, Wi-Fi, smartphones and app stores, cloud computing.</li>\n<li><b>Accessibility</b>: for example falling device and data prices, mobile networks reaching rural areas, the digital divide, WCAG and EPUB 3 accessibility features.</li>\n<li><b>Massively multiplayer online games (MMOGs)</b>: for example RuneScape (2001), World of Warcraft (2004), Fortnite (2017), Roblox and user-generated worlds.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Tips for a strong presentation</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Start with a timeline slide.</b> Show the whole story at a glance (1960s to today) before you zoom in. Colour-code the four strands: development, technology, accessibility and MMOGs.</li>\n<li><b>One milestone per slide.</b> Give each slide a date, a short heading, one image and two or three bullet points. Do not paste paragraphs.</li>\n<li><b>Add a Caribbean slide.</b> When did dial-up, broadband and mobile data reach your territory and the wider region? Who were the providers? Check your facts with a reliable source.</li>\n<li><b>Cite your sources.</b> Put a short source line on each slide and a full references slide at the end. Use reliable sites and avoid copying text word for word.</li>\n<li><b>Design for your audience.</b> Large readable fonts, strong colour contrast and alt text on images make the presentation accessible.</li>\n<li><b>Export carefully.</b> Save the editable file (PowerPoint, Google Slides or Canva) and export a PDF copy, using a clear file name such as <code>Surname_InternetHistory.pdf</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Further reading</b> (find these sites through a search engine):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>State of Digital Publishing: its feature on trends and developments in digital publishing.</li>\n<li>Colorado State University WAC Clearinghouse: its writing guide on digital publishing, for comparing digital and print publishing.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Malik at the Island Pulse Media has one rule for timeline decks: if the audience cannot read the date from the back of the room, the slide has failed. Keep text short, and use the speaker notes for the detail you will say aloud.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>In this activity you design a digital slideshow about how the Internet grew. Show milestones in four strands: development, technology, accessibility and MMOGs. Put one milestone on each slide, with a date, a picture and short points. Add a slide on when broadband reached your island, and cite every source.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A strong presentation tells a story of cause and effect, not a list of dates. For each milestone, add one line on <b>why it mattered</b>, for example \"TCP/IP let separate networks join, so the Internet could grow\". Check every date against two reliable sources, because popular websites often repeat errors such as calling TCP \"Transfer Control Protocol\". Apply your accessibility learning to your own slides: alt text, high contrast and fonts that can be read from the back of the room. Finish with a judgement slide that answers a question such as \"Which milestone did most to bring the Internet to Caribbean homes?\", and justify your choice.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Publishing a Caribbean e-book","intro":"<p>You are working with Zara at the Island Pulse Media. A retired teacher from Spanish Town has written an illustrated history of Emancipation Day in Jamaica and wants it read across the region and the diaspora. Make the best publishing decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The book has about 80 pages of text and 20 photographs with captions. Most of her readers will use phones. Which format do you recommend first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A scanned PDF of the printed pages.","to":"bad1","fb":"A scanned PDF has fixed pages that are hard to read on phones, and screen readers cannot read scanned text."},{"t":"A reflowable EPUB 3 with alt text for every photograph.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Reflowable text suits phones, and alt text supports readers who use screen readers."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>She wants the book in Apple Books, Kobo, Google Play Books and Barnes &amp; Noble, but has little time. How should she distribute it?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Post the full book free on her Facebook page.","to":"bad2","fb":"Social media is useful for promotion, but it is not a publishing platform with stores, metadata and sales."},{"t":"Upload once through an aggregator such as Draft2Digital, and use KDP for Amazon.","to":"n3","fb":"Efficient. The aggregator reaches several retailers with one upload, while KDP covers the largest store."},{"t":"Email the file to readers who ask for it.","to":"bad2","fb":"This gives up discoverability, sales tracking and protection. Readers cannot find the book in stores."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Before upload, she asks about ISBNs and metadata. What do you advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Get separate ISBNs for the e-book and any print edition, and write a keyword-rich description with categories such as Caribbean history.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. One ISBN per format, and strong metadata makes the book discoverable."},{"t":"Use one ISBN for every format and leave the description blank so readers are curious.","to":"bad3","fb":"One ISBN cannot cover several formats, and a blank description hides the book from search."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Published.</b> The book appears in several stores, readers in New York and London find it by searching \"Jamaican history\", and blind readers can follow it with a screen reader. A Grade One answer would justify each decision: format suits the device, distribution suits the time available, and ISBN and metadata support discoverability.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Hard to read.</b> Phone readers have to pinch and zoom on every page, and screen-reader users cannot access it. Try again and choose a format designed for screens.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Hidden book.</b> Without proper platforms the book has no store listing, no sales data and very little reach. Try again and use retail or distribution platforms.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Rejected and invisible.</b> The stores flag the ISBN problem and searches never show the book. Try again: each format needs its own ISBN, and metadata drives discoverability.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Format first","p":"<p>Think about where readers will read and whether everyone can access it.</p>"},{"h":"Reach with little time","p":"<p>Which type of platform sends one upload to many stores?</p>"},{"h":"Being found","p":"<p>Stores and search engines rely on identifiers and descriptions.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Publishing a Caribbean e-book (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A short version: a local author wants her text-heavy history book read on phones.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Which format do you recommend?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A scanned PDF.","to":"bad","fb":"Fixed scanned pages are hard to read on phones."},{"t":"A reflowable EPUB with alt text.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. It fits phone screens and supports accessibility."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You matched the format to the device and the reader.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Readers struggle on small screens. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A harder brief. A Dominica cultural foundation wants to publish an illustrated, bilingual (English and Kwéyòl) book of Jing Ping songs with audio recordings. Readers include elders with low vision, students on Android phones and libraries overseas. Every decision has a trade-off.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The book mixes song lyrics, photographs and audio of each song. Which format plan do you recommend?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A PDF with links to audio files on a website.","to":"bad1","fb":"A PDF does not reflow on phones, and the audio breaks if the website goes down."},{"t":"A fixed-layout EPUB 3 so the photos and lyrics stay exactly as designed.","to":"ok1","fb":"It keeps the design, but elders and phone readers will have to zoom on every page. The lyrics do not depend on exact position."},{"t":"A reflowable EPUB 3 with embedded audio, alt text on every photo and language tags for English and Kwéyòl.","to":"n2","fb":"Strong. Reflowable text suits phones and low vision, EPUB 3 carries audio, and language tags help screen readers pronounce each language."}]},"ok1":{"text":"<p>The fixed-layout version looks beautiful on an iPad, but test readers on phones complain. The foundation asks you to fix it before release. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Keep it and tell readers to buy tablets.","to":"bad1","fb":"This shuts out the very readers the foundation wants to reach."},{"t":"Rebuild it as a reflowable EPUB 3, keeping fixed pages only for the two photo spreads that need them.","to":"n2","fb":"Good recovery. EPUB 3 allows mixed approaches; keep fixed layout only where meaning depends on position."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The foundation wants the book in Amazon, Apple Books, Google Play Books and in library catalogues overseas, with a small budget. How should it be distributed?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Enrol in KDP Select for the extra promotion and also upload to Apple Books and Google Play Books.","to":"bad2","fb":"KDP Select requires the e-book to be exclusive to Amazon, so selling it elsewhere at the same time breaks the agreement."},{"t":"Sell it only through the foundation's Facebook page.","to":"bad2","fb":"Social media can promote the book, but it gives no store listing, library access or sales data."},{"t":"KDP for Amazon (without KDP Select exclusivity), Draft2Digital for the other stores, and a distributor that serves libraries.","to":"n3","fb":"Excellent. Direct upload for the largest store, one upload for the rest, and a library channel, while avoiding an exclusivity deal that would block the other stores."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Last step: identifiers and metadata. What do you advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Use the free platform identifiers only, and write the description in English to keep it simple.","to":"ok2","fb":"It works, but a platform identifier may list the platform as publisher, may not work in library catalogues, and Kwéyòl speakers searching in their language may miss the book."},{"t":"Separate ISBNs for the EPUB and any print edition; a description and keywords in both languages; categories such as Caribbean music; and accessibility metadata.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Each format has its own ISBN, and bilingual, accessible metadata makes the book discoverable to all three audiences."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Published and found.</b> Elders enlarge the lyrics and play each song, students read on their phones, and libraries in London and Montreal add the book to their catalogues. A Grade One answer justifies every choice against the readers, the budget and discoverability, and explains the trade-off it avoided.</p>","end":"good"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Published, but limited.</b> The book sells in some stores, yet libraries struggle to catalogue it and Kwéyòl speakers rarely find it. Try again and plan identifiers and metadata for every audience.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Hard to use.</b> Phone readers and elders with low vision give up, and the audio is fragile. Try again and match the format to the readers and the content.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Blocked or hidden.</b> Either the exclusivity agreement is breached or the book has no proper store listing. Try again: combine platforms without signing an exclusivity deal.</p>","end":"bad"}},"title":"Publishing a Caribbean e-book (Challenge)"}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Explain digital publishing to a client","genre":"Structured response: explaining digital publishing terms and history","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Regent Street Readers Collective in Georgetown wants to publish a collection of short stories by young Guyanese writers, both as an e-book and as an interactive version with audio readings. The members know little about digital publishing and have asked Island Pulse Media for a briefing.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a briefing for the collective that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>defines</b> digital publishing and explains <b>two</b> purposes it would serve for the collective;</li><li><b>distinguishes</b> between a retail platform and a distribution platform, naming one example of each;</li><li><b>explains</b> what an ISBN and metadata are and why each matters;</li><li><b>distinguishes</b> between ePub and PDF, recommending one for the interactive version;</li><li>briefly <b>describes</b> how the change from Web 1.0 to Web 2.0 makes this project possible.</li></ol>","min":220,"max":450,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Digital publishing is ... One purpose is ... because ... A second purpose is ...<br><b>(b)</b> A retail platform such as ... whereas a distribution platform such as ...<br><b>(c)</b> An ISBN is ... Each format ... Metadata is ... It matters because ...<br><b>(d)</b> PDF has ... whereas ePub ... For the interactive version I recommend EPUB 3 because ...<br><b>(e)</b> Web 1.0 was ... whereas Web 2.0 ... This means the collective can ...</p>","checklist":["I defined digital publishing in one clear sentence.","I named one retail platform and one distribution platform and contrasted them.","I said that each format needs its own ISBN.","I contrasted ePub and PDF on the same feature using \"whereas\".","I linked Web 2.0 to user-generated content and sharing.","I applied every part to the collective and Guyana."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Knowledge of publishing terms","marks":5,"look":["electronic|digital form|online","retail|store|sell","aggregat|distribut|draft2digital|ingram","isbn|13.digit|identif","metadata|keyword|descri","reflow|fixed|layout"],"min":4,"strong":"Your definitions of platforms, ISBN, metadata and formats are accurate.","weak":"Check your definitions: an ISBN identifies one format of one edition; metadata describes the book so it can be found; ePub reflows, PDF is fixed."},{"criterion":"History and the Web","marks":3,"look":["web 1|read.only|static","web 2|read.write|user.generated|social","share|comment|interact|creat"],"min":2,"strong":"You explained the shift from passive reading to active creating and sharing.","weak":"Contrast Web 1.0 (static, read-only) with Web 2.0 (interactive, users create and share) and link it to the project."},{"criterion":"Application to the scenario","marks":4,"look":["collective|writer|stor","guyan|georgetown|regent street|caribbean","audio|interactiv|epub ?3","reader|audience|diaspora"],"min":3,"strong":"Your advice is clearly tailored to the collective and its readers.","weak":"Link each point to the collective, for example: \"EPUB 3 allows the audio readings by the young writers to be embedded in each story.\""}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Digital publishing is the creation and distribution of content such as e-books, audio and video in electronic form so that readers can access it on phones, tablets and computers. One purpose for the collective is reach: the stories can be bought by readers in the Caribbean diaspora in New York and the Netherlands without printing or shipping costs. A second purpose is interactivity: the young writers can add audio readings, which a printed book cannot include.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> A retail platform, such as Amazon Kindle Direct Publishing, is the store where the reader buys and downloads the book, whereas a distribution platform, such as Draft2Digital, takes one upload and sends the book to many retailers like Apple Books and Kobo. The collective could use both to save time and reach more readers.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> An ISBN is a unique 13-digit number that identifies one edition in one format, so the e-book and any printed edition each need their own ISBN. Metadata is data that describes the book: title, authors, description, keywords and categories. It matters because stores and search engines use it to show the book to readers, so keywords such as \"Guyanese short stories\" make the collection discoverable.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> PDF has a fixed layout that looks identical on every screen and suits printing, whereas ePub is usually reflowable, adjusting text to fit any screen. For the interactive version I recommend EPUB 3, because it is built on HTML5 and can embed audio of each writer reading, while also supporting screen readers.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> Web 1.0 was a static, read-only web where only site owners published, whereas Web 2.0 is interactive and lets ordinary users create and share content. This means young writers in Georgetown can publish, promote their stories on social media and receive comments from readers, without needing a traditional publisher.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Multi-part structured questions reward precision. Label each part, keep definitions to one sentence, and always add one applied sentence about the client. In \"distinguish\" parts, write both sides in one sentence with \"whereas\".</p>",
 "easy": {"bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: digital publishing terms (8 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part in one or two sentences. Use the sentence starters to help you.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Define</b> digital publishing. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE way digital publishing would help the collective, and say why. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>State</b> what an ISBN is and how many the collective needs for an e-book and a printed book. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Say</b> which format, ePub or PDF, is better for reading on a phone, and why. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":80,"max":200,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Digital publishing is ...<br><b>(b)</b> It would help the collective because ...<br><b>(c)</b> An ISBN is ... The collective needs ... ISBNs because ...<br><b>(d)</b> ... is better for phones because ...</p>","checklist":["I defined digital publishing using the word \"electronic\" or \"digital\".","I gave one benefit and a reason.","I said each format needs its own ISBN, so two are needed.","I chose ePub and said that its text fits the screen."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Definition","marks":2,"look":["electronic|digital|online","content|book|stor|text|audio|video|share|distribut"],"min":2,"strong":"Clear definition.","weak":"Say that digital publishing is creating and sharing content such as e-books in electronic form."},{"criterion":"Benefit for the collective","marks":2,"look":["reach|cheap|cost|audio|update|world|diaspora|reader","because|so|which"],"min":2,"strong":"A benefit with a reason.","weak":"Give one benefit, such as reaching readers overseas, and add \"because ...\" to explain it."},{"criterion":"ISBN","marks":2,"look":["number|identif|13","two|2|each format|separate|own"],"min":2,"strong":"Correct ISBN facts.","weak":"An ISBN is a 13-digit number that identifies a book; the e-book and the printed book each need their own, so two."},{"criterion":"ePub or PDF","marks":2,"look":["epub","reflow|fit|screen|resiz|adjust"],"min":2,"strong":"Correct choice and reason.","weak":"Choose ePub, because its text reflows to fit a small phone screen."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Digital publishing is creating and sharing content, such as e-books, audio and video, in electronic form so people can read it on phones and computers.</p><p><b>(b)</b> It would help the collective reach readers in the Caribbean diaspora, because the stories can be downloaded anywhere without printing or shipping.</p><p><b>(c)</b> An ISBN is a 13-digit number that identifies one format of a book. The collective needs two ISBNs, because the e-book and the printed book each need their own.</p><p><b>(d)</b> ePub is better for phones because its text reflows to fit the screen, while a PDF page stays fixed.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Answer every part, even with one sentence. Use the starter, then add \"because\" to give the reason: most marks in short parts come from the reason, not the name.</p>","title":"Explain digital publishing to a client (Foundation)"},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Structured response: digital publishing briefing, Grade One extension (20 marks)","prompt":"<p>Write a full briefing for the collective that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>defines</b> digital publishing and <b>explains</b> THREE purposes it would serve for the collective. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>compares</b> retail and distribution platforms and <b>recommends</b> a combination for the collective, with reasons. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>explains</b> how the collective should handle ISBNs and metadata for the e-book, the interactive version and a print edition. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>evaluates</b> EPUB 3 against PDF for the interactive audio version, considering phone readers and readers with disabilities. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>discusses</b> how Web 2.0 and mobile Internet access create both opportunities and risks for the young writers. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":350,"max":650,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Digital publishing is ... First, ... Second, ... Third, ...<br><b>(b)</b> Retail platforms such as ... whereas distribution platforms such as ... I recommend ... because ...<br><b>(c)</b> Each format ... Metadata should include ... because ...<br><b>(d)</b> EPUB 3 ... whereas PDF ... For screen-reader users ... Therefore ...<br><b>(e)</b> Web 2.0 allows ... Mobile access means ... However, the risks are ... On balance ...</p>","checklist":["I explained three purposes, each linked to the collective.","I compared retail and distribution platforms and recommended a justified combination.","I covered ISBNs for every format and named specific metadata fields.","My evaluation of EPUB 3 and PDF considered phones and accessibility, and ended with a judgement.","I discussed opportunities and risks of Web 2.0 and mobile access, with a balanced conclusion.","I wrote in Standard English with labelled parts."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Definition and purposes","marks":4,"look":["electronic|digital form|online","reach|diaspora|global|world","cost|cheap|no print|shipping","audio|interactiv|update|analytic|data"],"min":3,"strong":"Accurate definition and three applied purposes.","weak":"Define digital publishing, then explain three purposes (e.g. reach, low cost, interactivity), each linked to the collective."},{"criterion":"Platforms compared and recommended","marks":4,"look":["retail|store","distribut|aggregat|draft2digital|ingram","kdp|kindle|apple|google play|kobo","recommend|combination|both|because"],"min":3,"strong":"Clear comparison and a justified combination.","weak":"Contrast retail (where readers buy) with distribution (one upload to many stores) and recommend a justified mix such as KDP plus Draft2Digital."},{"criterion":"ISBN and metadata","marks":4,"look":["isbn","each format|separate|own|three","keyword|descri|categor","accessib|language|discover"],"min":3,"strong":"Correct ISBN plan and specific metadata advice.","weak":"Give each format its own ISBN and list metadata fields (description, keywords, categories, accessibility) with why they help discovery."},{"criterion":"Evaluation of EPUB 3 and PDF","marks":4,"look":["epub ?3","pdf","reflow|fixed|phone|screen","screen reader|alt text|accessib|disab","therefore|recommend|on balance|overall"],"min":4,"strong":"A balanced evaluation ending in a judgement.","weak":"Weigh both formats for phone readers and screen-reader users, then make a clear recommendation."},{"criterion":"Web 2.0 and mobile: opportunities and risks","marks":4,"look":["web 2|user.generated|social|share|comment","mobile|smartphone|phone|data","risk|however|piracy|copy|negative|abuse|privacy","on balance|overall|therefore|conclude"],"min":3,"strong":"A balanced discussion with a conclusion.","weak":"Explain opportunities (sharing, comments, promotion on phones) and risks (piracy, negative comments, data costs), then conclude."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Digital publishing is the creation, formatting and distribution of content such as e-books, audio and video in electronic form. First, it gives the collective global reach: readers in the diaspora in New York and Rotterdam can buy the stories on the day of release. Second, it lowers cost, because there is no printing, warehousing or shipping, which are expensive on a small island. Third, it allows interactivity: the young writers can add audio readings, and analytics show which stories readers finish, so future collections can be improved.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> Retail platforms such as Amazon KDP, Apple Books and Google Play Books are the stores where readers buy, whereas distribution platforms such as Draft2Digital take one upload and send the book to many stores. I recommend a combination: KDP for Amazon, because it is the largest store, and Draft2Digital for Apple Books, Kobo and others, because one upload saves the volunteers time. The collective should avoid KDP Select, because its exclusivity would block the other stores.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Each format needs its own ISBN, so the reflowable e-book, the interactive edition and the print edition need three separate numbers. The metadata should include a keyword-rich description, keywords such as \"Guyanese short stories\" and \"Caribbean young writers\", categories such as Caribbean fiction, and accessibility metadata stating that the book supports screen readers. This matters because stores and search engines use metadata to decide which readers see the book, so it drives discoverability.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> EPUB 3 is built on HTML5, so it can embed each writer's audio reading, reflow text to fit any phone and carry alt text and navigation for screen readers. PDF, on the other hand, keeps a fixed layout that is ideal for printing but forces phone readers to zoom, and it handles audio poorly. A PDF is easier to create, but the collective's readers mainly use phones and some are visually impaired. Therefore, on balance, I recommend EPUB 3 for the interactive version.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> Web 2.0 lets the writers publish, share their stories on social media and receive comments, and mobile access means most readers can find them on a smartphone. However, the risks are real: e-books can be copied and shared illegally, young writers may receive hurtful comments, and readers on prepaid data may avoid large audio files. On balance, the opportunities outweigh the risks if the collective moderates comments, keeps files compressed and protects its copyright.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Command words decide the marks here. \"Compare\" needs both sides in the same sentence; \"recommend\" needs a choice plus a reason; \"evaluate\" and \"discuss\" need strengths, weaknesses and a final judgement. Finish parts (d) and (e) with \"Therefore\" or \"On balance\" so the examiner can see your judgement.</p>","title":"Explain digital publishing to a client (Challenge)"}}]},{"id":"M2U2","title":"Authoring digital content in a Caribbean context","objectives":["Apply digital media writing techniques for blogs, websites, podcasts, video scripts, audio slideshows and social media.","Generate original content ideas through conceptualisation and creative planning from a brief or real-world problem.","Use storyboards, scripts, mock-ups and wireframes to plan content before production.","Develop creative content that reflects authentic Caribbean stories, voices and cultural context.","Explain copyright law, the Berne Convention and Caribbean intellectual property offices, and apply ethical standards: plagiarism, attribution, fair use and fair dealing, and Creative Commons licences.","Explain cybercrime in relation to digital content and the legal consequences of copyright infringement.","Apply the key techniques: the definition, uses and three-part format of a podcast, the seven video script writing tips and on-camera presentation, and voice tone, pacing and sequencing for audio slideshows."],"videos":[{"title":"Writing for the web","q":"how to write for the web scannable content tips"},{"title":"How to write a two-column video script","q":"how to write an AV two column video script"},{"title":"Storyboards and wireframes explained","q":"storyboard vs wireframe vs mockup explained"},{"title":"Creative Commons licences explained","q":"creative commons licenses explained BY SA NC ND"},{"title":"Video script writing tips for beginners","q":"video script writing tips for beginners write like you speak"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Writing for digital media: the core techniques","html":"<p>People do not read screens the way they read a printed book. Eye-tracking research by usability experts such as the Nielsen Norman Group shows that most web users <b>scan</b> pages, often in an F-shaped pattern, picking out headings, bold words and the first words of lines. Digital media writing is shaped around that behaviour.</p>\n<p><b>Core techniques for all digital writing</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Know the audience and purpose</b> before writing a word: who is reading, on what device, and what do you want them to do?</li>\n<li><b>Front-load</b> the most important information (the <b>inverted pyramid</b> used in news): key point first, details after.</li>\n<li><b>Short paragraphs and sentences</b>: two to four sentences per paragraph on the web; one idea each.</li>\n<li><b>Scannable structure</b>: clear headings and subheadings, bullet lists, bold key terms.</li>\n<li><b>Plain language and direct address</b>: write \"you\", use active verbs, avoid jargon.</li>\n<li><b>A clear call to action (CTA)</b>: \"Book your table\", \"Subscribe\", \"Share your story\".</li>\n<li><b>Write for search</b>: use the words your audience would type, in headings and the first paragraph.</li>\n<li><b>Write for listening and watching</b> when the content is audio or video: conversational, short sentences that are easy to say aloud.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Blogs</b> are regularly updated, personal or expert posts. A good blog post has a specific, searchable headline (\"5 Fry-Jack Spots in Belize You Must Try This September\"), a hook in the first two lines, subheadings, images with captions, a conversational voice and a closing CTA or question to invite comments.</p>\n<p><b>Websites</b> contain more permanent content (Home, About, Services, Contact). Website copy is concise and task-focused: visitors want to find opening hours or book a tour quickly. Each page should answer one main question, and link text should describe the destination (\"View the ferry timetable\", not \"click here\").</p>\n<p>Example: Keisha at the Island Pulse Media rewrote the Home page of the Placencia Kayak Tours. The old page opened with three paragraphs about the owner's childhood. Her version opens with \"Paddle the Placencia Lagoon at sunrise. Tours daily from 7 a.m. Book online in two minutes.\"</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In Paper 02, if asked to \"rewrite this content for the web\", show the techniques visibly: a headline, a short lead sentence, bullets, bold key words and a call to action. Examiners award marks for techniques they can see.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>People skim screens instead of reading every word. So digital writing puts the main point first, uses short paragraphs, clear headings, bullet points and bold key words, and ends with a call to action such as \"Book now\". A kayak tour site in Placencia should open with the tour times and a booking link, not the owner's life story.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>These techniques work because they reduce the effort a reader must spend, which matters on a small phone screen in bright sunlight. The inverted pyramid also protects meaning: if a reader leaves after one line, they still leave with the key message. A common misconception is that \"short\" means \"shallow\". Good web writing can carry detail, but it layers it, with the headline and lead for scanners and the depth lower down for readers who want it. In a Paper 02 rewrite, make each technique visible and briefly name it, so the examiner can award every technique you used.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Digital writing techniques: podcasts, video, audio slideshows and social media","html":"<p>There are three headings: <b>Digital Writing Techniques for Podcasts</b>, <b>for Video</b> and <b>for Audio Slideshow</b>. This overview follows them; the next screens unpack each one.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Format</th><th>Writing techniques</th><th>Caribbean example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Podcast</b></td><td>Use the three-part <b>format</b>: a catchy introduction (podcast name and episode theme), focused main content, and a conclusion that summarises, gives a call to action and previews the next episode. Write for the ear: conversational tone, short sentences, signposting (\"First... next... finally\"), show notes with timestamps and links</td><td>A weekly podcast on Bahamian history interviewing elders from Over-the-Hill in Nassau</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Video script</b></td><td>Builds on podcast writing, but viewers now <b>see</b> you. Establish the purpose in a brief; keep it short and catchy; write like you speak; plan the visual elements; read it aloud. Use a two-column (audio-visual) script and captions</td><td>A 60-second Reel on how sorrel drink is made</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Audio slideshow</b></td><td>Pictures (and sometimes short video inserts) put together with narration to tell a story, give a message or present. Keep it simple: the right voice tone, a monitored pace, images sequenced on a storyboard before you write, one idea per image</td><td>Photos of the rebuilding of a Roseau church after Hurricane Maria, with the voices of the volunteers</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Social media</b></td><td>Short, punchy hook; platform-specific length and tone; hashtags used sparingly; emojis only if they suit the brand; alt text on images; CTA</td><td>An Instagram carousel announcing the Crop Over schedule</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Platform awareness.</b> The same message needs different writing on each platform. A newspaper story may run to 600 words online, become a 25-word Facebook post with a link, a 9:16 TikTok with on-screen captions and a WhatsApp broadcast of two lines. This is called <b>repurposing</b> content, and you should practise turning one idea into several outputs.</p>\n<p><b>Audio slideshows</b> are an often-forgotten format in exams. They are cheaper than video (mainly stills and sound are needed), work on low bandwidth and are powerful for documentary stories. Tools include Adobe Express, Canva video, WeVideo and the free Shotcut or OpenShot.</p>\n<p><b>Writing for accessibility</b> applies to every format: transcripts for podcasts, captions for video, alt text for images and plain, readable language.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When a question asks you to \"describe two techniques for writing a podcast script\", name the technique and say <b>why</b> it works for listeners: \"Signposting phrases such as 'first' and 'finally' help listeners follow the structure because they cannot scroll back up the page.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Each digital format needs its own writing style. Podcasts need a catchy intro, focused content and a clear ending. Video scripts plan what viewers hear and see. Audio slideshows match narration to photos. Social media needs a short hook. One Carnival story could become a blog, a TikTok and a two-line WhatsApp message.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The deeper skill is <b>repurposing</b>: keeping one core message while changing length, tone and structure for each platform's audience and behaviour. A good test is to ask how the audience receives the content. Listeners cannot scroll back, so podcasts need signposting; viewers watch with the sound off, so videos need captions; scrollers decide in a second, so social posts need a hook. A common mistake is pasting the same text everywhere. In an exam, justify each technique by linking it to how that audience reads, listens or watches, and remember that accessibility (captions, transcripts, alt text) applies to every format.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the writing technique to the format","intro":"<p>Malik and Keisha at the Island Pulse Media are training interns. Match each technique to the format it suits best.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Signposting phrases and show notes with timestamps","r":"Podcast"},{"l":"A two-column layout pairing narration with shots","r":"Video script"},{"l":"Narration written to a sequence of still photographs","r":"Audio slideshow"},{"l":"A searchable headline, subheadings and a closing question for comments","r":"Blog post"},{"l":"Concise, task-focused copy and descriptive link text on a Contact page","r":"Website"},{"l":"A short hook, few hashtags and alt text on an image post","r":"Social media"}],"win":"Well matched. Each digital format has its own writing techniques, chosen to fit how the audience reads, listens or watches.","tutor":[{"h":"Ear or eye?","p":"<p>Formats that are heard need help with structure because listeners cannot skim back.</p>"},{"h":"Stills or moving?","p":"<p>One format uses photographs instead of moving footage.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the technique (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Show notes with timestamps","r":"Podcast"},{"l":"Narration beside shot descriptions","r":"Video script"},{"l":"Photos with narration","r":"Audio slideshow"},{"l":"Short hook and hashtags","r":"Social media"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Each draft below has a problem. Match each problem to the technique that would fix it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Podcast listeners say they lost track of which of the three tips they were on","r":"Add signposting phrases such as \"first\", \"next\" and \"finally\""},{"l":"A tourism video's narration describes a fort that never appears on screen","r":"Use a two-column script pairing each line with its shot"},{"l":"An audio slideshow about a church rebuild jumps backwards and forwards in time","r":"Sequence the photos on a storyboard before writing narration"},{"l":"A blog post titled \"Some thoughts\" gets almost no search traffic","r":"Write a specific, searchable headline using the reader's keywords"},{"l":"A ferry company's Contact page buries the phone number in paragraph three","r":"Use concise, task-focused copy with key details first"},{"l":"An Instagram post has eighteen hashtags and the image has no description","r":"Keep a short hook, use few hashtags and add alt text"}],"win":"Well diagnosed. Matching a problem to its fix shows you understand why each technique exists, not just its name.","title":"Match the writing technique to the format (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Writing for podcasts","html":"<p>This screen covers <b>Digital Writing Techniques for Podcasts</b>.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>What is a podcast?</b> A podcast is a digital media file (audio or video) distributed over the Internet that listeners can download or stream. The name blends <b>\"iPod\"</b> and <b>\"broadcast\"</b>. Podcasts can be <b>downloaded automatically</b> using <b>RSS</b> or <b>Atom</b> feeds. Video podcasts are called <b>vodcasts</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Technical aspects of podcasting</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Feed formats:</b> RSS or Atom are standardised feed formats that allow automatic distribution. In practice today, the big directories (Apple Podcasts, Spotify) work with <b>RSS</b> feeds, so RSS is the one to remember.</li>\n<li><b>Aggregators:</b> feed readers or aggregators (today, podcast apps such as Apple Podcasts, Spotify or Pocket Casts) automatically check the feed for new episodes and fetch them.</li>\n<li><b>Key distinction:</b> unlike an ordinary media file posted on a website, a podcast can be <b>subscribed to</b>, so new episodes arrive without the listener searching. In three words: <b>distribution</b> (downloadable or streamable), <b>subscription</b> (RSS/Atom updates) and <b>consumption</b> (on demand).</li>\n<li><b>Content structure:</b> episodes may be <b>standalone</b> or part of a <b>series</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Uses of a podcast</b>: <b>talk shows</b>, <b>music shows</b>, <b>interviews</b>, <b>storytelling</b> and <b>tutorials</b>. A Caribbean set might be a talk show on Jamaican politics, a soca and calypso music show ahead of Carnival, interviews with Oistins fish-fry vendors, folk stories from Dominica, and a tutorial series on making pepper sauce.</p>\n<p><b>Format of a podcast</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Introduction:</b> begin with a catchy intro. State your podcast name and the episode theme clearly.</li>\n<li><b>Main content:</b> deliver your core material. Keep segments focused and engaging.</li>\n<li><b>Conclusion:</b> summarise the key points, include a call to action and preview upcoming episodes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Example: the <i>Rock Talk TT</i> opens with \"Welcome to Rock Talk TT, I'm Keisha, and today: how three Chaguanas teens turned bake and saltfish into a business.\" It closes with \"Follow us for episode four, when we head to the Port of Spain fish market.\"</p>\n<p>Practical activity: write a <b>10 to 15 minute podcast script</b> on a topic of interest, individually or in a team, then record, share and gather peer feedback. Production steps are in unit 2.5.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> If asked to \"define a podcast\", give three parts for full marks: digital audio or video, distributed over the Internet, and subscribed to through a feed (RSS) so new episodes arrive automatically. The feed is what separates a podcast from an ordinary MP3 on a website.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A podcast is an audio or video show shared online that people can subscribe to. An RSS feed tells podcast apps when a new episode is ready, so it downloads by itself. A good episode has a catchy introduction, focused main content and a conclusion with a call to action. A Grenada podcast could interview cocoa farmers each week.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The feed is the defining technology. An MP3 on a website is only a file; it becomes a podcast when an RSS feed lists each episode with its title, date and description, and directories such as Apple Podcasts read that feed. That is why show notes matter: they become feed metadata that helps listeners find the episode in search. Writing for the ear also differs from writing for the eye. Repeat key points, avoid long lists of numbers, and write the way the host naturally speaks. For a \"define\" question, give all three parts: digital media, distributed online, and subscribed to through a feed.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Podcast quick-fire","intro":"<p>Jerome is quizzing interns at the Island Pulse Media before they start the <i>Caribbean Roots</i> podcast.</p>","items":[{"text":"The word \"podcast\" blends which two words?","options":["pod and cast list","portable and broadcast","iPod and forecast","iPod and broadcast"],"answer":3,"fb":"Podcast blends \"iPod\" and \"broadcast\", from the era when episodes were loaded onto iPods."},{"text":"What lets podcast episodes download automatically?","options":["A QR code","A USB cable","Email attachments","RSS or Atom feeds"],"answer":3,"fb":"RSS (or Atom) feeds tell apps that a new episode exists so they can fetch it automatically."},{"text":"A video podcast is called a...","options":["livestream","webinar","vodcast","vlog feed"],"answer":2,"fb":"Video podcasts are also called vodcasts."},{"text":"What software checks feeds for new episodes and downloads them?","options":["A word processor","A video editor","An aggregator (feed reader or podcast app)","An antivirus program"],"answer":2,"fb":"Aggregators, today usually podcast apps, check subscribed feeds for new episodes."},{"text":"Weekly interviews with Grenadian cocoa farmers fit which use of a podcast?","options":["Tutorial","Interviews","Music show","Talk show with no guests"],"answer":1,"fb":"In-depth conversations with guests and experts are the interview use."},{"text":"Where should a podcast preview the next episode?","options":["In the first line of the introduction","Halfway through the main content","In the conclusion","Nowhere"],"answer":2,"fb":"The conclusion summarises key points, gives a call to action and previews upcoming episodes."},{"text":"What makes a podcast different from an ordinary audio file on a website?","options":["It cannot be streamed","It is always longer","It can be subscribed to and updates automatically","It must be in video"],"answer":2,"fb":"Automatic delivery through a subscription feed is the key distinction."}],"win":"Well done. You know what a podcast is, how feeds and aggregators deliver it, its uses and its three-part format.","tutor":[{"h":"Feeds","p":"<p>Think about what an app needs to know that a new episode is ready.</p>"},{"h":"Format","p":"<p>Introduction, main content and conclusion each have a job. Which one looks ahead?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Podcast quick-fire (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"\"Podcast\" blends iPod and...","options":["forecast","podium","broadcast"],"answer":2,"fb":"iPod plus broadcast."},{"text":"Episodes download automatically through...","options":["an RSS feed","a printer","a flash drive"],"answer":0,"fb":"RSS feeds deliver new episodes."},{"text":"A podcast intro should state...","options":["only the music credits","the podcast name and episode theme","the next episode's guest"],"answer":1,"fb":"Name and theme come first."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder, applied questions about the <i>Caribbean Roots</i> podcast. Choose the BEST answer.</p>","items":[{"text":"Listeners in Antigua complain that they must visit the host's website every week to check for new MP3 files. What should the team set up?","options":["A bigger MP3 file","An RSS feed submitted to podcast directories","A longer introduction","A PDF of the episode list"],"answer":1,"fb":"An RSS feed listed in directories lets podcast apps fetch new episodes automatically, which is what turns files into a subscribable podcast."},{"text":"Episode 3 opens with four minutes of the hosts chatting about their week before the topic is named. Which part of the format is weak?","options":["The conclusion","The show notes","The introduction","The feed"],"answer":2,"fb":"The introduction should be catchy and state the podcast name and episode theme straight away."},{"text":"The team wants listeners to jump straight to the interview with a Carriacou boat builder. What should they add?","options":["A longer conclusion","A second RSS feed","More background music","Timestamps in the show notes"],"answer":3,"fb":"Timestamps in the show notes let listeners skip to the segment they want."},{"text":"A deaf student wants to follow the episode on Jamaican Maroon history. What should the team publish?","options":["A shorter episode","A full transcript","An Atom feed","A louder version"],"answer":1,"fb":"A transcript makes the audio content accessible to deaf and hard-of-hearing users, and also helps search engines index it."},{"text":"Which closing line BEST follows the podcast conclusion format?","options":["\"So today we heard three ways Grenada's cocoa is changing. Subscribe, and next week we visit the Belmont Estate.\"","\"Our sponsor is a cocoa company.\"","\"Okay, bye.\"","\"Today we will discuss cocoa farming.\""],"answer":0,"fb":"It summarises, gives a call to action and previews the next episode, the three jobs of a conclusion."},{"text":"The team films the same interview and releases it with the audio. The video version is best described as a...","options":["live stream","webinar","audio slideshow","vodcast"],"answer":3,"fb":"A video podcast is a vodcast. It is still delivered as a podcast through a feed, unlike a live stream or webinar."}],"win":"Excellent. You applied podcast terms to real production problems: feeds for subscription, a strong three-part format, timestamps and transcripts.","title":"Podcast quick-fire (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Seven video script writing tips","html":"<p>Preparing content for a vodcast or video is similar to writing a podcast, but now <b>people are seeing you</b> as you produce content, so you must take great care to keep the audience interested. Visual cues, body language and setting all affect interest, and the script must account for what viewers will see. Here are <b>7 Video Script Writing Tips</b> (adapted from Track Five):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Establish the purpose of the video.</b> Create a <b>brief</b> for every video stating the purpose, the audience, the keyword targeted and the platform that will host it (YouTube or Facebook, for example). Is it promoting a social campaign or an instructional piece?</li>\n<li><b>Keep it short and catchy.</b> You have only a few seconds to tell people what the video is about. The intro should tell viewers explicitly why they should watch.</li>\n<li><b>Write like you speak.</b> Perfect written grammar can sound stiff aloud. Keep the script conversational; viewers are drawn to authentic, personal content.</li>\n<li><b>Consider the visual elements.</b> Where will the host stand? What background, hand motions and added graphics will help? Where will captions fall? Note camera angles and b-roll in the script.</li>\n<li><b>Promote your other content when possible.</b> Find creative ways to plug your other videos or services.</li>\n<li><b>Read it out loud and get feedback.</b> You will change wording once you hear it.</li>\n<li><b>Accept that you might still make changes during filming.</b> You wrote it the way <i>you</i> speak, not necessarily the way your host speaks. If possible, have the host read it aloud before filming.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Example.</b> Malik at the Island Pulse Media scripts a 60-second instructional video for a youth volunteer group in Grenada on preparing a hurricane kit. His brief: purpose, teens aged 13 to 18, keyword \"hurricane kit checklist\", hosted on Instagram Reels. His opening line: \"Hurricane season is here. In one minute, you'll know exactly what goes in your kit.\" At the read-through, the host stumbles on \"nonperishable provisions\", so Malik changes it to \"food that won't spoil\".</p>\n<p>A gentle update: the often-quoted \"eight-second attention span\" is a popular claim that researchers have questioned. The lesson still stands: the first few seconds decide whether people keep watching.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> The tips can be condensed to five (purpose, concise, conversational, visual elements, read aloud and revise). In Paper 02, name a tip and apply it to the scenario: \"I would write like I speak, using 'you' and short phrases, because viewers drop off when narration sounds scripted.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Video scripts need extra care because viewers see you. The seven tips are: know the purpose, keep it short and catchy, write like you speak, plan the visuals, promote your other content, read it aloud, and accept changes while filming. A hurricane-kit video for Red Cross teens should say in the first line what viewers will learn.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The tips form a loop, not a list. The brief (purpose) decides how long the video can be and what visuals are needed; reading aloud tests whether \"write like you speak\" really worked; and accepting changes on set means the brief still guides any rewrite. The \"eight-second attention span\" claim is weak evidence, so avoid quoting it as fact; the stronger point is that platform data shows many viewers leave in the opening seconds. A Grade One answer applies a tip to the scenario and explains its effect on the audience, for example how conversational wording keeps a teenage viewer watching.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the video script tip","intro":"<p>Zara is reviewing scripts for a Vincy Mas promo by the Island Pulse Media. Match each action to its tip.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Writing a brief: audience, keyword and YouTube as the host platform","r":"Establish the purpose"},{"l":"Opening with \"In 30 seconds, here is everything you need for J'ouvert\"","r":"Keep it short and catchy"},{"l":"Changing \"attendees are advised\" to \"you'll want to bring\"","r":"Write like you speak"},{"l":"Noting where the host stands and where captions fall","r":"Consider the visual elements"},{"l":"Ending with \"Watch our Carnival food guide next\"","r":"Promote your other content"},{"l":"Reading the script to Keisha and asking what sounded awkward","r":"Read it out loud and get feedback"},{"l":"Rewording a line the host keeps tripping over on set","r":"Accept changes during filming"}],"win":"Well matched. You can apply all seven video script writing tips.","tutor":[{"h":"Before, during, after","p":"<p>Some tips happen before writing, some while writing and one only on set.</p>"},{"h":"Plugging","p":"<p>Pointing viewers to another video is a form of promotion.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the tip (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Writing a brief first","r":"Establish the purpose"},{"l":"Using everyday spoken words","r":"Write like you speak"},{"l":"Planning background and graphics","r":"Consider the visual elements"},{"l":"Hearing the script before filming","r":"Read it out loud"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Zara's feedback on seven weak scripts is below. Match each problem to the tip that was ignored.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"The script never says who the video is for or which platform will host it","r":"Establish the purpose"},{"l":"The first 15 seconds describe the company's history before saying what the video is about","r":"Keep it short and catchy"},{"l":"The host must say: \"It is imperative that attendees ensure adequate hydration\"","r":"Write like you speak"},{"l":"The narration describes a route map that is never planned to appear on screen","r":"Consider the visual elements"},{"l":"The video ends abruptly with no link to the channel's other Carnival guides","r":"Promote your other content"},{"l":"Nobody heard the script before filming, and three lines turned out to be tongue-twisters","r":"Read it out loud and get feedback"},{"l":"The writer refuses to let the host reword a line she keeps stumbling over on set","r":"Accept changes during filming"}],"win":"Well diagnosed. You recognised each tip from the problem it prevents, which is how you will apply them when reviewing your own scripts.","title":"Match the video script tip (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Presenting on camera and writing for audio slideshows","html":"<p><b>Presenting on camera.</b> Because viewers see the actual delivery of information, <b>dressing, voice, speech and deportment</b> (how you carry yourself) must all be considered.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Dressing and background:</b> when a presenter wears a coloured top, make sure the background balances it. A bright red shirt against a red wall at an old courthouse blends in; against a plain light wall it stands out. Avoid tiny stripes and checks, which can shimmer on camera.</li>\n<li><b>Microphones:</b> make sure they do not block the speaker's face or affect delivery. A clip-on lavalier mic is clipped to the shirt, not held in front of the mouth.</li>\n<li><b>Cue cards:</b> where scripts are used, keep cue cards in order and slide each one away discreetly. (The handout spells them \"que cards\"; the usual spelling is <b>cue cards</b>.)</li>\n<li><b>Voice, speech and deportment:</b> clear, unhurried speech, open posture, eye contact with the lens.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Digital writing techniques for audio slideshow.</b> As with video: <b>keep it simple!</b> An audio slideshow puts pictures (and sometimes short video inserts) together with sound to communicate a story, a message or a presentation. There are four techniques:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Voice tone selection:</b> match the narration style to the emotional tone of the content. A Hurricane Irma remembrance needs a calm, respectful voice; a Carnival preview can be bright and energetic.</li>\n<li><b>Pacing control:</b> monitor how fast you speak, leaving room for delays in sound and for each image to register.</li>\n<li><b>Visual sequencing:</b> place the photographs and video inserts in the correct sequence to create the <b>storyboard before you start writing</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Flexible production:</b> some people find it easier to record the voice first, then lay out the storyboard and use editing skills to finish. (Unit 2.5 shows both workflows.)</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Example.</b> The Palmetto Point Secondary Heritage Club in Antigua makes an audio slideshow on Emancipation Day. They sequence twelve photos on a storyboard (the 1834 emancipation in Antigua, the Emancipation Day march, a family preparing johnny cakes), then write one or two calm sentences for each frame, pausing between slides.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> If asked to \"explain two techniques for writing an audio slideshow\", link each to the audience: \"Pacing control matters because viewers need time to look at each photograph while they listen.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>On camera, viewers notice your clothes, your background, your microphone and how you stand and speak. In an audio slideshow, photos tell the story while a voice narrates. Choose the right tone, speak at a steady pace and put the photos in order first. An Emancipation Day slideshow needs a calm voice and time to look at each picture.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Both forms are about managing <b>attention</b>. On camera, anything distracting (a striped shirt that shimmers, a microphone in front of the mouth, a cue card in shot) pulls attention away from the message. In an audio slideshow, the danger is a mismatch between voice and image: narration that runs ahead of the photos, or a cheerful tone over images of loss. Audio slideshows are also a smart choice for low-budget and low-bandwidth projects because they need only stills and sound. When asked to explain a technique, link it to its effect on the viewer, such as giving time for each photograph to register.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"On camera or audio slideshow?","intro":"<p>Keisha has a pile of production notes for two projects by Island Pulse Media: a filmed host video and an audio slideshow. Sort each note into the right project.</p>","main":"Which project does this note belong to?","bins":["On-camera video","Audio slideshow"],"items":[{"t":"Arrange the 15 photos on a storyboard before writing narration.","bin":1,"fb":"Visual sequencing of photos before writing is an audio slideshow technique."},{"t":"Clip the mic to the shirt so it does not block the face.","bin":0,"fb":"Microphones must not block the speaker's face on camera."},{"t":"Work on posture, eye contact and deportment.","bin":0,"fb":"Deportment is visible only when the presenter is on screen."},{"t":"Keep cue cards in order and slide each one away discreetly.","bin":0,"fb":"Cue cards support an on-camera presenter."},{"t":"Slow the narration so each photo has time to register.","bin":1,"fb":"Pacing control lets images and sound work together."},{"t":"Check the host's yellow top against the background at Fort Charles in Port Royal.","bin":0,"fb":"Dressing and background balance matter when viewers see the presenter."},{"t":"Record the voice first, then lay out the images in the editor.","bin":1,"fb":"Voice-first is a flexible workflow for audio slideshows."},{"t":"Choose a calm voice tone for a hurricane remembrance story.","bin":1,"fb":"Voice tone selection matches narration to the emotional tone of the images."}],"win":"Well sorted. On camera, the presenter's dressing, mic and deportment matter; in an audio slideshow, tone, pacing and image sequence carry the story.","tutor":[{"h":"Is anyone seen?","p":"<p>If the note is about how a person looks, the audience must be seeing them.</p>"},{"h":"Photos and voice","p":"<p>Sequencing stills and timing narration belong to one format.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"On camera or audio slideshow? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Match the host's top to the background.","bin":0,"fb":"On camera."},{"t":"Put the photos in story order.","bin":1,"fb":"Audio slideshow."},{"t":"Keep the mic away from the face.","bin":0,"fb":"On camera."},{"t":"Speak slowly so each photo registers.","bin":1,"fb":"Audio slideshow."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","main":"Which project does this problem belong to?","intro":"<p>These are problems reported after screenings. Decide which project each problem belongs to.</p>","items":[{"t":"Viewers say the presenter's thin-striped shirt flickers on screen.","bin":0,"fb":"Fine stripes shimmer on camera, a dressing problem for an on-screen presenter."},{"t":"The narration finishes before the last three photos have appeared.","bin":1,"fb":"This is a pacing problem: the voice and images are out of step in the slideshow."},{"t":"The clip-on mic rustles against the host's necklace.","bin":0,"fb":"Microphone placement on the presenter is an on-camera issue."},{"t":"The story of the church rebuild jumps from the finished roof back to the storm damage.","bin":1,"fb":"The images are in the wrong order, a visual sequencing problem."},{"t":"An upbeat, jokey voice is used over photos of hurricane destruction.","bin":1,"fb":"The voice tone does not match the emotional tone of the images."},{"t":"The host's cue card is visible at the edge of the shot.","bin":0,"fb":"Cue cards must be handled discreetly by an on-camera presenter."},{"t":"The host slouches and keeps looking away from the lens.","bin":0,"fb":"Posture and eye contact are deportment issues seen only on camera."},{"t":"A short video clip of the steel band sits awkwardly between two still photos with no narration.","bin":1,"fb":"Short video inserts belong in the slideshow's sequence and need narration or sound planned for them."},{"t":"A red top disappears against a red wall at the courthouse.","bin":0,"fb":"Clothing must be balanced against the background when the presenter is on screen."},{"t":"The team recorded the voice first but never trimmed the photos to fit each sentence.","bin":1,"fb":"Voice-first is a valid slideshow workflow, but the editing step that fits images to narration was skipped."}],"win":"Well diagnosed. On-camera problems come from what the presenter shows; audio slideshow problems come from how voice, pace and image order work together.","title":"On camera or audio slideshow? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Conceptualisation and creative planning","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Conceptualisation</b> is the process of generating, developing and refining an original idea (a concept) in response to a brief or a real-world problem, before any production begins.</p>\n<p>A <b>creative brief</b> is the starting point. It states the client, the problem or goal, the target audience, key message, tone, platform, budget, deadline and deliverables. You will write full briefs in unit 2.4; here you use them to spark ideas.</p>\n<p><b>Stages of creative planning</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Understand the brief or problem</b>: what is really being asked? Who is it for?</li>\n<li><b>Research</b>: the audience, competitors, cultural context and facts.</li>\n<li><b>Generate ideas (divergent thinking)</b>: brainstorming, mind maps, mood boards, \"How might we...?\" questions, SCAMPER (Substitute, Combine, Adapt, Modify, Put to another use, Eliminate, Reverse).</li>\n<li><b>Select and refine (convergent thinking)</b>: judge ideas against the brief, audience and budget; pick the strongest.</li>\n<li><b>Develop the concept</b>: a title, a one-line <b>logline</b> or big idea, the format and platform, and the tone.</li>\n<li><b>Plan production</b>: storyboards, scripts, mock-ups and wireframes (next screen), plus a schedule.</li>\n<li><b>Pitch and get feedback</b>: present the concept to the client or class and revise.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Example from a real-world problem.</b> Zara is given this brief by the Bahamas Youth Environmental Club: \"Teenagers leave litter on Cable Beach after events.\" Brainstorming produces twenty ideas. The team selects one: \"Leave Only Footprints\", a series of 15-second TikToks in which local athletes race to clean a stretch of beach, ending with a challenge hashtag. The concept is original, suits a teen audience, fits a small budget and uses a platform the audience already uses.</p>\n<p>A good concept is <b>original</b>, <b>relevant to the audience</b>, <b>achievable</b> with the resources available and <b>measurable</b> (you can tell whether it worked).</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Your SBA proposal (14 marks) is marked on how well you justify your concept. Keep your brainstorm notes and mind maps: they are evidence of process for the Progress and Process criterion.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Conceptualisation means coming up with a strong idea before you start making anything. Start with the brief, research the audience, brainstorm many ideas, then choose the best one. Next, plan it with storyboards or scripts and show it to the client. A beach clean-up campaign in The Bahamas might become fun TikToks of athletes racing to collect litter.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The key distinction is between <b>divergent</b> thinking (many ideas, no judging) and <b>convergent</b> thinking (judging ideas against the brief, audience, budget and time). Students often judge too early and lose their best ideas. A strong concept is also <b>measurable</b>: \"reduce litter on Cable Beach after events\" is a goal, but \"300 entries to the clean-up hashtag in a month\" can actually be checked. Techniques such as SCAMPER force fresh angles: what if the beach clean-up were combined with a sports challenge? In a planning answer, show the stages in order and give one specific action for each stage.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Order the creative planning process","intro":"<p>The Grenada Chocolate Collective has asked Island Pulse Media for a podcast series. Put Zara's planning steps in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Read the brief and clarify the goal and target audience"},{"t":"Research listeners, competing podcasts and the history of Grenadian cocoa"},{"t":"Brainstorm and mind-map many possible episode ideas"},{"t":"Select the strongest concept against the brief and budget"},{"t":"Write the logline, episode outline and scripts"},{"t":"Pitch the concept to the client and revise from feedback"},{"t":"Begin recording and production"}],"win":"Correct. Creative planning moves from understanding and research, through divergent and convergent thinking, to planning documents and feedback before production.","tutor":[{"h":"Wide, then narrow","p":"<p>You must have many ideas before you can select the best one.</p>"},{"h":"Production is last","p":"<p>Recording before the client agrees wastes time and money.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Order the planning steps (Foundation)","intro":"<p>First step at the top.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Understand the brief"},{"t":"Brainstorm ideas"},{"t":"Write the script"},{"t":"Record and produce"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>A fuller process with nine steps. Put Zara's plan for the Grenada Chocolate Collective podcast in order, from brief to review.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Read the brief and clarify the goal and target audience with the client"},{"t":"Research listeners, competing podcasts and the history of Grenadian cocoa"},{"t":"Brainstorm freely with mind maps and SCAMPER, without judging ideas yet"},{"t":"Judge the ideas against the brief, budget and timeline, and select the strongest"},{"t":"Develop the concept: series title, one-line logline, format and tone"},{"t":"Plan production: episode outlines, scripts and a recording schedule"},{"t":"Pitch the concept to the client and revise it from feedback"},{"t":"Record, edit and publish the episodes"},{"t":"Measure downloads and listener feedback against the goals in the brief"}],"win":"Correct. Divergent thinking comes before convergent judging, the concept is developed before production is planned, the pitch comes before recording, and results are measured against the brief at the end.","title":"Order the creative planning process (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Planning tools: storyboards, scripts, mock-ups and wireframes","html":"<p>Planning tools let a team agree on content and flow before spending money on production. The four main tools are storyboards, scripts, <b>mock-ups</b> and <b>wireframes</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it is</th><th>Used for</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Storyboard</b></td><td>A sequence of sketched frames showing each shot, with notes on action, camera angle, duration, dialogue and sound</td><td>Video, animation, audio slideshows, ads (including your 30-second SBA ad)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Script</b></td><td>The written words: dialogue, narration and directions. Video uses a two-column (audio-visual) script; podcasts use a running script or segment outline</td><td>Video, podcasts, radio, presentations</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Wireframe</b></td><td>A low-fidelity, black-and-white layout of a page or screen showing structure: boxes for header, menu, images, text and buttons; no final colour or images</td><td>Websites, apps, blogs</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Mock-up</b></td><td>A high-fidelity static design showing the final look: colours, fonts, real images and logo, but not working</td><td>Websites, apps, social posts, e-book covers</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>A two-column script</b> for a 30-second ad for a Gros Islet bakery:</p>\n<pre><code>VIDEO                              | AUDIO\n-----------------------------------|----------------------------------\n1. CU: hands kneading dough, 5s    | SFX: soft island music begins\n2. MS: baker pulls johnny cakes    | VO: \"Every morning since 1985...\"\n   from the oil, 6s                |\n3. WS: queue outside the shop, 5s  | VO: \"...Gros Islet wakes up hungry.\"\n4. Logo on screen + address, 4s    | VO: \"Fresh. Local. Ours.\"\n</code></pre>\n<p>(CU = close-up, MS = medium shot, WS = wide shot, VO = voice-over, SFX = sound effects.)</p>\n<p><b>Fidelity</b>: wireframes are low fidelity and fast to change; mock-ups are high fidelity and show the client the final look; a <b>prototype</b> (unit 2.3) adds clickable interaction. Tools include paper and pencil, Figma, Canva, Balsamiq and storyboard templates in Google Slides or StoryboardThat.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In Paper 02 you may be asked to sketch a storyboard with pencil and paper. Number each frame, show the shot type, and write duration, audio and transitions under each box. Stick figures are fine: examiners mark planning, not drawing talent.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Planning tools help a team agree before spending money. A storyboard sketches each shot. A script holds the words to be spoken. A wireframe is a plain black-and-white layout showing where things go on a page. A mock-up shows the final colours, fonts and pictures. A bakery advert in Gros Islet would start with a storyboard and a script.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Choose the tool by asking what decision the team needs to make next. If the question is \"what will viewers see, and in what order?\", storyboard. If it is \"what will be said?\", script. If it is \"where does each element go?\", wireframe; and if it is \"will the client like the look?\", mock-up. Fidelity is a trade-off: low-fidelity tools are cheap to change, so use them early; high-fidelity tools are persuasive but slow to change. A common error is showing a client a polished mock-up too early, so feedback focuses on colours instead of structure. Prototypes then add clickable interaction.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Which planning tool?","intro":"<p>Maya and Malik are planning several projects for the Island Pulse Media. Decide which tool each task needs.</p>","main":"Which planning tool fits this task best?","bins":["Storyboard","Script","Wireframe","Mock-up"],"items":[{"t":"Sketching the eight shots of a 30-second ad for the Heineken Regatta, with camera angles.","bin":0,"fb":"Shot-by-shot visual planning with angles is a storyboard."},{"t":"Writing the host's words and interview questions for a podcast episode.","bin":1,"fb":"The words to be spoken are planned in a script."},{"t":"Narration lines beside shot descriptions for a tourism video.","bin":1,"fb":"A two-column audio-visual script pairs narration with shots."},{"t":"Showing a client exactly how an Instagram post will look with brand colours and fonts.","bin":3,"fb":"Showing the final visual design is a mock-up."},{"t":"A quick black-and-white layout of a blog post page drawn in Balsamiq.","bin":2,"fb":"Balsamiq is designed for quick, low-fidelity wireframes."},{"t":"Frames showing the order of photos in an audio slideshow about Emancipation Day.","bin":0,"fb":"Sequencing images visually is storyboarding."},{"t":"A full-colour design of the app home screen with the real logo and photos, for client approval.","bin":3,"fb":"A high-fidelity static design that shows the final look is a mock-up."},{"t":"Grey boxes showing where the menu, hero image and booking button go on a hotel home page.","bin":2,"fb":"A low-fidelity layout of structure only is a wireframe."}],"win":"Well sorted. Storyboards plan shots, scripts plan words, wireframes plan structure and mock-ups show the final look.","tutor":[{"h":"Pictures or words?","p":"<p>Sketched frames over time are one tool; spoken words are another.</p>"},{"h":"Fidelity","p":"<p>Grey boxes and structure are low fidelity. Final colours and images are high fidelity.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which planning tool? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Sketches of each shot in a video.","bin":0,"fb":"Storyboard."},{"t":"The words the narrator will say.","bin":1,"fb":"Script."},{"t":"Grey boxes showing page layout.","bin":2,"fb":"Wireframe."},{"t":"A full-colour design of the final page.","bin":3,"fb":"Mock-up."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Five tools now, including the prototype (a clickable model). Decide which tool each task needs.</p>","bins":["Storyboard","Script","Wireframe","Mock-up","Prototype"],"items":[{"t":"Frames showing the order of archive photos in an audio slideshow about the island's old salt ponds.","bin":0,"fb":"Sequencing images visually is storyboarding."},{"t":"The narration lines in the audio column beside each shot of a tourism video.","bin":1,"fb":"The audio column of a two-column script holds the spoken words."},{"t":"A brand-coloured design showing exactly how a sponsored Instagram post will look.","bin":3,"fb":"Showing the final visual design, without interaction, is a mock-up."},{"t":"Grey boxes showing where the menu, gallery and booking button sit on a guesthouse page.","bin":2,"fb":"A low-fidelity layout of structure only is a wireframe."},{"t":"A clickable model where testers tap \"Book\" and move to a booking screen.","bin":4,"fb":"Clickable interaction makes it a prototype."},{"t":"Testing whether users can find opening hours by clicking through linked screens in Figma.","bin":4,"fb":"Testing navigation through linked, clickable screens is prototyping."},{"t":"A quick pencil layout of a blog post page to agree where headings and images go.","bin":2,"fb":"A quick structural sketch with no final styling is a wireframe."},{"t":"Writing the voice-over and interview questions for a Jing Ping documentary.","bin":1,"fb":"The words to be spoken are planned in a script."},{"t":"A full-colour static design of a festival app home screen with real photos, for client sign-off.","bin":3,"fb":"A high-fidelity static design showing the final look is a mock-up."},{"t":"Planning each camera angle and duration for a 30-second ferry advert.","bin":0,"fb":"Shot-by-shot visual planning with angles and timings is a storyboard."}],"win":"Well sorted. Storyboards plan shots, scripts plan words, wireframes plan structure, mock-ups show the final look, and prototypes test interaction.","title":"Which planning tool? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Web authoring tools","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Web authoring tools</b> are software programs used to create and edit web pages and websites. They let users design, code and manage content and layout without extensive coding knowledge, through user-friendly interfaces that simplify web development.</p>\n<p><b>Three types of web authoring tool</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>How it works</th><th>Examples</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Text editor</b></td><td>Basic tools for writing and editing HTML, CSS and JavaScript directly, line by line</td><td>Notepad++, Sublime Text, Visual Studio Code</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>WYSIWYG editor</b> (\"what you see is what you get\")</td><td>Visual, drag-and-drop design; the tool writes the code for you</td><td>Adobe Dreamweaver, WordPress Gutenberg (the block editor)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Content management system (CMS)</b></td><td>A platform with pre-designed templates, themes and plugins for creating and managing a whole site's content easily</td><td>WordPress, Joomla, Drupal</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Update:</b> Older lists also include <b>Microsoft Expression Web</b>. Microsoft discontinued it in 2012, so it no longer receives updates. If a question asks for a WYSIWYG example, give Dreamweaver or a block editor such as Gutenberg instead.</p>\n<p><b>Popular web authoring tools</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Adobe Dreamweaver</b>: code editing, a visual design view and website management in one program.</li>\n<li><b>WordPress</b>: a popular CMS used by millions of websites, with a friendly interface and extensive customisation.</li>\n<li><b>Wix</b>: a cloud-based platform with a drag-and-drop editor, templates and e-commerce features.</li>\n<li><b>Shopify</b>: an all-in-one e-commerce platform with professional templates and simple product management.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Benefits</b>: <b>streamlined development</b> (quick builds and updates without deep technical knowledge); <b>enhanced compatibility</b> (responsive features that optimise sites for different devices and screen sizes); <b>improved user experience</b> (drag-and-drop, themes and templates for attractive sites); <b>advanced functionality</b> (social media integration, e-commerce, SEO and analytics).</p>\n<p><b>Key features to consider when choosing</b>: <b>user interface</b> (intuitive and friendly), <b>compatibility</b> (works across browsers and platforms), <b>customisation</b> (flexible design options), <b>code editing</b> (can you get into the HTML and CSS?) and <b>responsive design</b> (mobile-friendly).</p>\n<p><b>Caribbean example</b>: <b>Bay Street Batik</b>, a small clothing shop in Nassau, wants to sell online to cruise visitors after they sail home. Maya at the Island Pulse Media compares options. Shopify wins on e-commerce and product management; Wix is quicker for a simple brochure site; WordPress with WooCommerce gives the most customisation but needs more upkeep. Because most customers browse on phones on the pier, responsive design is non-negotiable. She also checks payment options that work for a Dutch Caribbean business before deciding.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In a \"justify\" question, link each feature to the client's need: \"Shopify is appropriate because its product management lets the owner update stock herself, and its responsive templates suit visitors on mobile phones.\" You will hand-code with a text editor in unit 2.6 and study CMS types in depth in unit 2.9.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Web authoring tools help people build websites. A text editor such as VS Code lets you type HTML and CSS yourself. A WYSIWYG editor such as Dreamweaver lets you drag things onto a page while it writes the code. A CMS such as WordPress manages a whole site with templates. A Bay Street clothing shop might choose Shopify to sell online.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Each type trades <b>control</b> against <b>convenience</b>. A text editor gives complete control and clean code, but needs coding skill and time. A WYSIWYG editor is faster for beginners, but may produce bloated code that is harder to maintain. A CMS makes updating easy for non-technical owners, but adds plugins, updates and security tasks. The categories also blur: WordPress is a CMS whose Gutenberg editor works in a WYSIWYG way. A justified recommendation names the type and a product, then links it to the client's skills, budget, update frequency and need for features such as e-commerce or responsive templates.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Text editor, WYSIWYG or CMS?","intro":"<p>Maya is helping several clients across the region choose a web authoring tool. Sort each tool or description into its type.</p>","main":"Which type of web authoring tool is this?","bins":["Text editor","WYSIWYG editor","Content management system (CMS)"],"items":[{"t":"Notepad++: fixing a broken link tag by hand.","bin":0,"fb":"Notepad++ is a basic text editor for editing code directly."},{"t":"Adobe Dreamweaver design view: dragging an image into place and seeing the page update.","bin":1,"fb":"Dreamweaver offers a visual WYSIWYG view that writes the code for you."},{"t":"Visual Studio Code: typing HTML and CSS for a Speightstown restaurant menu page.","bin":0,"fb":"VS Code is a text (code) editor: you write the HTML and CSS yourself."},{"t":"WordPress with a theme and plugins: a Bequia guesthouse posting weekly news.","bin":2,"fb":"WordPress as a whole is a CMS: themes, plugins and content management."},{"t":"Joomla: a heritage foundation site with user accounts, menus and many articles.","bin":2,"fb":"Joomla is a CMS for managing a whole site's content."},{"t":"Sublime Text: editing a JavaScript file for a photo gallery.","bin":0,"fb":"Sublime Text is a text editor for writing code line by line."},{"t":"WordPress Gutenberg: adding a heading block and an image block to a blog post.","bin":1,"fb":"Gutenberg is a visual block editor: what you see is what you get."},{"t":"Drupal: a large, structured site for a Caribbean university with many editors.","bin":2,"fb":"Drupal is a powerful CMS suited to complex sites."}],"win":"Well sorted. Text editors mean writing code yourself, WYSIWYG editors let you design visually, and a CMS manages a whole site with templates, themes and plugins.","tutor":[{"h":"Who writes the code?","p":"<p>If you type every tag yourself, think about the most basic type of tool.</p>"},{"h":"Page or whole site?","p":"<p>Editing what one page looks like is different from managing many pages, users and plugins.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Text editor, WYSIWYG or CMS? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Notepad++","bin":0,"fb":"A text editor for writing code directly."},{"t":"Visual Studio Code","bin":0,"fb":"A text (code) editor."},{"t":"Adobe Dreamweaver design view","bin":1,"fb":"A WYSIWYG visual editor."},{"t":"Joomla","bin":2,"fb":"A content management system."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","main":"Which type of web authoring tool should this person use?","intro":"<p>Now recommend: which type of tool BEST suits each person's situation?</p>","items":[{"t":"A CAPE student practising hand-written HTML for Paper 02 who must see and type every tag.","bin":0,"fb":"Practising code accuracy needs a text editor such as VS Code or Notepad++."},{"t":"A developer optimising a single fast-loading landing page and wanting full control of every line of code.","bin":0,"fb":"Full control of lean code comes from writing it in a text editor."},{"t":"A designer who wants to drag images and text boxes onto one page and preview it, with the option to view the code.","bin":1,"fb":"A WYSIWYG editor such as Dreamweaver gives a visual design view with access to the code."},{"t":"A teacher building a single event page visually, with no plans for regular updates or multiple users.","bin":1,"fb":"For one visually built page with no ongoing content workflow, a WYSIWYG editor is enough."},{"t":"A Jamaican newsroom where ten writers publish stories daily with editor approval.","bin":2,"fb":"Many authors, roles and daily publishing need a CMS."},{"t":"A Bequia guesthouse owner with no coding skills who posts news and photos every week.","bin":2,"fb":"Regular updates by a non-technical owner are what a CMS is designed for."},{"t":"A university with thousands of structured pages and departmental editors.","bin":2,"fb":"Large, structured, multi-editor sites need a CMS such as Drupal."},{"t":"A student checking exactly why a closing tag is missing in a page built by hand.","bin":0,"fb":"Finding and fixing code errors line by line is done in a text editor."}],"win":"Strong recommendations. You matched control, skill and update needs to the right type of tool, which is the reasoning a \"justify\" question rewards.","title":"Text editor, WYSIWYG or CMS? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Creativity within a Caribbean perspective","html":"<p>You are expected to create content <b>within a Caribbean perspective</b>. This means more than adding a palm tree. It means telling stories from inside the culture, in authentic voices, for audiences who will recognise themselves.</p>\n<p><b>Sources of Caribbean content</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Local stories and history</b>: emancipation (1834 to 1838 in the British colonies, 1848 in the French and 1863 in the Dutch), the salt industry of Turks and Caicos and Bonaire, Junkanoo in The Bahamas, hurricane recovery, the Garifuna in Belize, Indo-Caribbean heritage in Trinidad and Guyana.</li>\n<li><b>Voices and language</b>: Sint Maarten English, Papiamentu, Kwéyòl, Jamaican Patois, Spanish and Dutch. Using the audience's language builds trust, but subtitles or a glossary can widen reach.</li>\n<li><b>Food, music and festivals</b>: guavaberry, johnny cakes, roti, the Oistins fish fry, soca, calypso, dancehall, steelpan, kaiso, Crop Over, Junkanoo.</li>\n<li><b>Real problems</b>: climate change, coral reef loss, sargassum, the cost of imported food, youth unemployment, migration and the diaspora.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Authenticity versus stereotype.</b> A <b>stereotype</b> reduces a culture to clichés: \"the Caribbean is only beaches, rum and parties\". Authentic content shows complexity: entrepreneurs, scientists, teachers, rural life and city life. Avoid using sacred or community symbols carelessly, and ask permission before recording people or using their stories.</p>\n<p><b>Techniques to bring in a Caribbean perspective</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Interview local experts and elders (with consent).</li>\n<li>Use local music licensed from Caribbean artists, or original music.</li>\n<li>Film real locations in your own territory: a market, a waterfront, a fort, a village street.</li>\n<li>Design with local visual references: the colours of the national flag, gingerbread architecture, local crafts.</li>\n<li>Solve a local problem: an app or campaign that helps residents, not only tourists.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your SBA is \"preferably local social or entrepreneurial\", so this section feeds straight into your proposal mark.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When asked to \"suggest content ideas within a Caribbean context\", make each idea specific: name a place, a group, a tradition or a problem. \"A podcast about Caribbean food\" is weak; \"a podcast where Oistins fish-fry vendors share family recipes and the history behind them\" is strong.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A Caribbean perspective means telling real local stories in real local voices, not just adding palm trees. Use local history, languages, food, music and real problems, and always ask permission before recording people. A podcast where Oistins fish-fry vendors share family recipes is authentic; a video of only beaches and cocktails is a stereotype.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Authenticity is also an ethical question of <b>who tells the story</b>. Content made with a community, using its own voices, is usually more accurate and more respected than content made about it from outside. Language choices carry this too: using Papiamentu or Kwéyòl builds trust, while subtitles widen reach, so creators balance both. Beware of \"positive stereotypes\" as well; showing every Caribbean person as cheerful and carefree still flattens real lives. In an exam, make every idea specific: name a place, a group, a tradition or a local problem, and explain why that choice speaks to the target audience.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Authentic or stereotyped?","intro":"<p>Interns at the Island Pulse Media pitched ideas for the Island Pulse Youth Tourism Challenge. Zara wants concepts that show an authentic Caribbean perspective.</p>","q":"Select ALL the ideas that show an authentic, specific Caribbean perspective.","lines":["An audio slideshow of salt-pond workers' descendants telling family stories, recorded with their consent.","A video showing only a beach, a cocktail and a reggae track downloaded from YouTube.","A blog series in which young chefs in Belize City rework their grandmothers' johnny cake recipes.","An ad where actors put on fake accents to sound \"Caribbean\".","A podcast in Papiamentu with English show notes, interviewing Carnival troupe leaders.","A stock photo collage of generic tropical islands with the caption \"Paradise\".","A TikTok series on how local fishermen in Grenville, Grenada, are adapting to sargassum seaweed.","An interactive map of Willemstad's heritage buildings with stories from a local museum (used with permission)."],"correct":[0,2,4,6,7],"win":"Excellent. Authentic content is specific, uses real voices with consent, reflects real issues and treats culture with respect.","miss":"Look again. Generic beaches, fake accents and unlicensed music are stereotypes or ethical problems, not an authentic Caribbean perspective.","tutor":[{"h":"Specific or generic?","p":"<p>Does the idea name real people, places, traditions or problems?</p>"},{"h":"Respect and rights","p":"<p>Is anyone being mocked, or is someone else's work used without permission?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Authentic or stereotyped? (Foundation)","q":"Select the TWO authentic ideas.","lines":["Local fishermen explain how sargassum affects their work.","A generic beach with the word \"Paradise\".","Elders share Emancipation Day memories, with consent.","Actors using fake accents."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Correct. Real voices and real issues make content authentic.","miss":"Not quite. Choose the ideas with real people and specific stories."},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A regional youth media competition has received these entries. Some look Caribbean but are stereotyped or unethical.</p>","q":"Select ALL the entries that show an authentic AND ethical Caribbean perspective.","lines":["A Junkanoo documentary filmed with a Bahamian crew and narrated by a costume builder from her own workshop.","A Crop Over video with a licensed Bajan soca track and interviews with costume designers.","A food blog that calls every dish from every island \"island food\" and uses the same beach photo for each.","A heritage video that films a Kalinago ceremony in Dominica without asking the community.","A campaign showing young coders in Belize City building an app that helps local farmers sell produce.","A tourism ad that uses Jamaican Patois only as a joke punchline.","A podcast in Kwéyòl with English show notes, interviewing Dominican elders who gave written consent.","A vlog claiming the cruise pier and duty-free shops are \"the real Barbados\".","A photo essay of Indo-Trinidadian Divali celebrations with captions written together with the families pictured.","A festival poster that adds random \"tribal\" patterns to a Garifuna event without any research."],"correct":[0,1,4,6,8],"win":"Excellent judgement. Authentic content is specific and made with the community; ethical content has consent and licences. You rejected both clichés and disrespectful use of culture.","miss":"Look again. Some entries are stereotypes (one \"island food\", language as a joke, tourist zones as \"the real\" island), and some are unethical (filming a ceremony without consent, random patterns added to a culture without research).","title":"Authentic or stereotyped? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Copyright law in digital content creation","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Copyright</b> is a legal right that gives the creator of an original work exclusive control over how it is copied, distributed, adapted, performed and communicated to the public, for a limited time.</p>\n<p><b>What is protected?</b> Original works fixed in a tangible or digital form: writing (books, blogs, scripts, song lyrics), music and sound recordings, photographs, films and videos, artwork and graphics, software code, broadcasts, and the layout of published editions. Copyright arises <b>automatically</b> when the work is created and fixed: there is no need to register it or use the © symbol, although the symbol is a useful reminder.</p>\n<p><b>What is NOT protected?</b> Ideas, facts, titles, short phrases, names and slogans (these may be protected instead by <b>trademarks</b>), and works whose copyright has expired and entered the <b>public domain</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Economic and moral rights.</b> Economic rights let the owner earn money (licences, sales, royalties). Moral rights protect the creator's reputation: the right to be named as author and to object to distortion of the work.</p>\n<p><b>Duration.</b> The <b>Berne Convention for the Protection of Literary and Artistic Works</b> (1886, administered by WIPO, the World Intellectual Property Organization) sets a minimum term of the author's life plus 50 years. Many countries, including the Netherlands and the United States, use life plus 70 years. Jamaica extended its term to life plus 95 years in 2015. Terms vary by territory and type of work, so always check the local law.</p>\n<p><b>The Berne Convention</b> also means a work protected in one member country is protected in all member countries, without registration. Almost every Caribbean state is a member, so a song recorded in Trinidad is protected in Jamaica, Canada and Japan.</p>\n<p><b>Caribbean intellectual property offices</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sint Maarten: Bureau for Intellectual Property Sint Maarten (BIPSM)</li>\n<li>Jamaica: Jamaica Intellectual Property Office (JIPO)</li>\n<li>Trinidad and Tobago: Intellectual Property Office of Trinidad and Tobago</li>\n<li>Barbados: Corporate Affairs and Intellectual Property Office (CAIPO)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These offices register trademarks and patents, educate the public and, in some cases, keep voluntary records of copyright works. Collecting societies such as COTT (Copyright Music Organisation of Trinidad and Tobago) and JACAP (Jamaica Association of Composers, Authors and Publishers) license music and collect royalties for artists.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A common Paper 01 trap: \"You must register your work to get copyright.\" False. Copyright is automatic once the work is fixed. Registration and the © symbol only help you prove ownership.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Copyright gives creators control over copying and sharing their original work. It starts automatically as soon as the work is written, recorded or saved, so no registration is needed. It protects songs, photos, videos, writing and code, but not ideas, facts or names. A soca song recorded in Trinidad is also protected in Jamaica.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Copyright protects <b>expression, not ideas</b>: anyone may make a podcast about Carnival history, but not copy someone else's script or recording. Remember the two families of rights: economic rights let the owner earn through licences and royalties, while moral rights protect the creator's name and the integrity of the work, so cropping out a photographer's credit can breach moral rights even when a fee has been paid. Duration varies: Berne sets a minimum of life plus 50 years, and many countries go further. A common error is thinking that buying a copy of a song gives you the right to use it in your video; it does not.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Copyright quick-fire","intro":"<p>Jerome is building an app for Antigua Sailing Club and keeps asking copyright questions. Help him.</p>","items":[{"text":"Jerome writes original code for the app tonight. When is it protected by copyright?","options":["Only after he registers it with an IP office","Automatically, as soon as it is written and saved","Only after he adds the © symbol"],"answer":1,"fb":"Copyright is automatic once an original work is fixed in a form, such as a saved file. Registration and © are optional."},{"text":"He wants to name the app \"Sail Away\". Can copyright protect the name?","options":["No, titles and short names are not protected by copyright; a trademark may protect them","Yes, all names are copyright","Yes, but only for 10 years"],"answer":0,"fb":"Copyright does not protect titles or short phrases. Brand names are protected by trademark registration."},{"text":"Which international agreement means his app is protected in Canada and the UK without registering there?","options":["The CARICOM Treaty","The Kyoto Protocol","The Berne Convention"],"answer":2,"fb":"The Berne Convention gives automatic protection in all member countries."},{"text":"The Berne Convention minimum term of copyright for most works is...","options":["until the author stops selling the work","exactly 20 years from publication","life of the author plus 50 years"],"answer":2,"fb":"Berne sets a minimum of life plus 50 years. Many countries go further, such as life plus 70."},{"text":"He finds a 19th-century engraving of English Harbour whose artist died over 150 years ago. It is most likely...","options":["in the public domain","a trademark","still in copyright"],"answer":0,"fb":"With the creator long dead, copyright has expired and the work is in the public domain. A modern photograph of it could still carry its own copyright, so check the source."},{"text":"The club wants a soca track in the app trailer. Who can usually license it?","options":["The rights holder or a collecting society such as COTT","Any YouTube user who uploaded it","Nobody: music cannot be licensed"],"answer":0,"fb":"Rights holders, often through collecting societies, license music. An uploader on YouTube rarely owns the rights."}],"win":"Excellent. You know copyright is automatic, protects expression but not names or ideas, lasts at least life plus 50 years under Berne, and is licensed by rights holders.","tutor":[{"h":"Automatic protection","p":"<p>Ask: does the law need any paperwork for copyright to exist?</p>"},{"h":"Copyright or trademark?","p":"<p>Names, logos and slogans used in trade belong to a different type of IP.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Copyright quick-fire (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Copyright protection begins...","options":["automatically when the work is fixed","only after registration"],"answer":0,"fb":"Copyright is automatic."},{"text":"Can copyright protect an app name?","options":["No, that is for trademarks","Yes, always"],"answer":0,"fb":"Names are protected by trademarks, not copyright."},{"text":"The international copyright agreement is the...","options":["Paris Climate Agreement","Berne Convention"],"answer":1,"fb":"Berne protects works across member countries."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder copyright cases from the Antigua Sailing Club project. Choose the BEST answer.</p>","items":[{"text":"Jerome copies the gameplay idea of a popular sailing game but writes all-new code and draws all-new art. Has he infringed the game's copyright?","options":["No, because apps cannot be copyrighted","Yes, unless he registers his app first","No: copyright protects expression such as code and art, not the underlying idea","Yes: game ideas are always protected"],"answer":2,"fb":"Ideas are not protected; the copying of actual code, art or text would be. Note that software code itself is protected."},{"text":"A Jamaican composer died in 1990. Using Jamaica's term of life plus 95 years, when does copyright in her songs end?","options":["It never ends","At the end of 2085","At the end of 2060","At the end of 2040"],"answer":1,"fb":"1990 + 95 = 2085. Terms usually run to the end of that calendar year."},{"text":"A hotel licenses a photographer's image of English Harbour, then crops out her name and alters it to look like a different island. Which right is most at issue?","options":["No rights, because the hotel paid","Her economic rights only","Her moral rights: to be named and to object to distortion","Trademark rights"],"answer":2,"fb":"Paying a licence fee does not remove moral rights. Removing her credit and distorting the work affect attribution and integrity."},{"text":"Jerome registers his code with an IP office. What does registration add?","options":["Evidence that helps prove ownership; protection already existed","A trademark on the app name","Copyright protection for the first time","Protection of his app idea"],"answer":0,"fb":"Copyright is automatic. Registration or a voluntary record only helps to prove ownership and the date of creation."},{"text":"Jerome bought a soca track online for personal listening. Can he use 10 seconds of it in the club's promotional trailer?","options":["No: buying a copy does not include a licence to use it in a video","Yes, because he paid for it","Yes: 10 seconds is always allowed","Yes, if he credits the artist"],"answer":0,"fb":"A purchase gives personal use only. Using music in a video needs a licence from the rights holders; there is no automatic \"10-second rule\"."},{"text":"The club wants to stop rival businesses using its slogan \"Sail the 365\". What protects a slogan?","options":["A Creative Commons licence","Copyright, automatically","The Berne Convention","Trademark registration"],"answer":3,"fb":"Short phrases and slogans are not protected by copyright. Registering a trademark protects a brand name or slogan used in trade."}],"win":"Excellent. You applied the key principles: ideas are free but expression is protected, moral rights survive payment, registration only proves ownership, and slogans need trademarks.","title":"Copyright quick-fire (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Ethical standards: plagiarism, attribution, fair use and Creative Commons","html":"<p><b>Plagiarism</b> is presenting someone else's words, ideas, images, code or media as your own. It is an <b>ethical</b> and academic offence (CXC can cancel SBA results for it) even when no law is broken. Copyright infringement is a <b>legal</b> offence: using protected work without permission. You can plagiarise a public domain text (unethical) and you can infringe copyright while crediting the creator (illegal). Attribution does not equal permission.</p>\n<p><b>Attribution</b> means crediting the source properly: creator, title, source or link, and licence. A good attribution: \"Photo: Carlisle Bay at dusk by Ana Richardson, CC BY 4.0, via a named photo site\".</p>\n<p><b>Fair use and fair dealing</b> are exceptions that allow limited use of copyright work without permission.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Fair use</b> (United States) is a flexible test weighing four factors: the purpose (for example, educational or transformative), the nature of the work, the amount used and the effect on the market.</li>\n<li><b>Fair dealing</b> (the UK and Commonwealth Caribbean, including Jamaica, Trinidad and Tobago and Barbados) is narrower: use must fit a listed purpose such as research, private study, criticism, review or news reporting, and must be fair, usually with acknowledgement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Neither is a free pass: using a whole song in a promotional video is not fair use or fair dealing just because you are a student.</p>\n<p><b>Creative Commons (CC)</b> licences let creators give permission in advance under set conditions.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Element</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>BY</b> (Attribution)</td><td>You must credit the creator. Every CC licence includes BY.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>SA</b> (ShareAlike)</td><td>If you adapt it, you must share your version under the same licence.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>NC</b> (NonCommercial)</td><td>No commercial use, such as ads or products for sale.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>ND</b> (NoDerivatives)</td><td>You may share it unchanged, but you may not edit, remix or adapt it.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The six licences combine these: CC BY, CC BY-SA, CC BY-NC, CC BY-ND, CC BY-NC-SA and CC BY-NC-ND (the most restrictive). <b>CC0</b> lets a creator give up their rights and place work in the public domain. Search for CC media on Openverse, Wikimedia Commons and Flickr's licence filter.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For each CC question, ask three things: Must I credit? Can I use it commercially? Can I change it? BY answers the first, NC the second, ND the third.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Plagiarism means passing off someone else's work as your own. Copyright infringement means using protected work without permission. Credit is good manners but not permission. Fair use and fair dealing allow small uses for reasons like review or news. Creative Commons licences give permission in advance. A CC BY photo of Carlisle Bay can be used if you credit the photographer.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Plagiarism and infringement overlap but are not the same, and examiners like students who can separate them: copying a public domain poem without credit is plagiarism but not infringement, while crediting a photographer whose image you used without a licence is infringement without plagiarism. Fair dealing in the Commonwealth Caribbean is narrower than US fair use, because the use must fit a listed purpose before fairness is even considered. With Creative Commons, read every element: NC blocks client and advertising work, ND blocks cropping and remixing, and SA controls the licence of your new version. Keep a record of each licence you rely on.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Can I use it? Match the Creative Commons licence","intro":"<p>Keisha is choosing media for client campaigns at the Island Pulse Media. Match each licence to what it allows.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"CC BY","r":"Use, edit and use commercially, as long as you credit the creator"},{"l":"CC BY-SA","r":"Edit and use commercially, but share your adaptation under the same licence"},{"l":"CC BY-NC","r":"Edit and share with credit, but not for commercial purposes"},{"l":"CC BY-ND","r":"Share unchanged with credit, even commercially, but no edits or remixes"},{"l":"CC BY-NC-ND","r":"Share unchanged with credit, non-commercially only: the most restrictive"},{"l":"CC0","r":"No conditions: the creator has placed the work in the public domain"}],"win":"Well matched. BY means credit, SA means share alike, NC means no commercial use, ND means no changes, and CC0 means no conditions.","tutor":[{"h":"Read each letter","p":"<p>Each added letter adds one condition. Count the conditions.</p>"},{"h":"The odd one","p":"<p>One licence has no BY at all. What does that mean?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the CC element (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"BY","r":"You must credit the creator"},{"l":"NC","r":"No commercial use"},{"l":"ND","r":"No changes allowed"},{"l":"SA","r":"Share your version under the same licence"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Read what each creator allows, then match it to the correct licence.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"You may remix it into a poster for a paid concert and license your poster however you like, as long as you credit me","r":"CC BY"},{"l":"You may remix it for a paid campaign, but your remix must carry this same licence","r":"CC BY-SA"},{"l":"You may remix it for a free school project with credit, but never to make money","r":"CC BY-NC"},{"l":"You may use it in a paid advert with credit, but you must not crop, filter or edit it","r":"CC BY-ND"},{"l":"You may remix it for a charity newsletter with credit, and must share your version under the same terms","r":"CC BY-NC-SA"},{"l":"You may share it unchanged with credit in a non-profit newsletter only","r":"CC BY-NC-ND"},{"l":"You may do anything with it, even without naming me","r":"CC0"}],"win":"Well matched. Reading permissions back into licence codes is the skill you need when a client asks \"can we use this?\"","title":"Can I use it? Match the Creative Commons licence (Challenge)"}},{"type":"mcq","title":"Fair dealing or infringement?","q":"A CAPE student in Port of Spain is producing a promotional video for the Maracas Bay food truck. She uses a full three-minute hit soca song as the soundtrack, credits the artist in the description and says it is \"fair use because I am a student\". Which statement BEST evaluates her position?","options":[{"t":"It is likely infringement: Trinidad and Tobago applies fair dealing for listed purposes, and using a whole song in a commercial promotion fits none of them, even with credit.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Fair dealing covers purposes such as research, criticism, review and news reporting. A whole song in a promotional video for a business is a commercial use, and credit is not permission."},{"t":"It is fair use because she credited the artist.","fb":"Attribution is good practice, but it does not give permission. Crediting the artist does not make the use legal."},{"t":"It is legal because the song is on YouTube, so it is public.","fb":"Being publicly available online does not mean a work is in the public domain. The song is still protected by copyright."},{"t":"It is legal because students are exempt from copyright law.","fb":"There is no general student exemption. Educational purposes can help in some fair dealing cases, but this video promotes a business."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Purpose","p":"<p>Is the video for study or review, or does it promote a business?</p>"},{"h":"Amount","p":"<p>How much of the song was used? Does that seem \"fair\"?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Fair dealing or infringement? (Foundation)","q":"A student uses a whole hit song in a business advert and credits the artist. Is this allowed?","options":[{"t":"Yes, because it is on YouTube.","fb":"Online does not mean public domain."},{"t":"Yes, because she gave credit.","fb":"Attribution does not replace permission."},{"t":"No. Credit is not permission, and a commercial advert is not fair dealing.","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. She needs a licence or royalty-free music."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","q":"Four Barbadian students use parts of a new calypso album. In Barbados, a fair dealing country, which use is MOST likely to count as fair dealing?","options":[{"t":"Using the 30-second chorus in an advert for a cousin's rum shop, with the artist credited.","fb":"An advert is a commercial use that fits no fair dealing purpose, and credit is not permission."},{"t":"Uploading the whole album to a video site with a written review in the description.","fb":"Copying the whole album goes far beyond what is needed for a review and would replace sales, so it is not fair."},{"t":"A music blog review that quotes two short clips, with acknowledgement, to criticise the arrangement and the lyrics.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Criticism and review is a listed fair dealing purpose, the clips are short and used to support the critique, and the source is acknowledged."},{"t":"Playing the album as background music in a travel vlog with the caption \"no copyright infringement intended\".","fb":"Such disclaimers have no legal effect. Background music in a vlog is not criticism, review or news reporting."}],"title":"Fair dealing or infringement? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Cybercrime and the consequences of infringement","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Cybercrime</b> is criminal activity carried out using computers, networks or the Internet, or aimed at them.</p>\n<p><b>Cybercrimes linked to digital content</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Digital piracy</b>: illegally copying, sharing or selling films, music, software, e-books or games, including running illegal streaming sites and selling pirated USBs.</li>\n<li><b>Circumventing DRM</b>: breaking digital rights management locks to copy protected content.</li>\n<li><b>Hacking and unauthorised access</b>: breaking into a publisher's or creator's accounts to steal or alter content.</li>\n<li><b>Identity theft and impersonation</b>: fake accounts posing as a brand or creator to scam followers.</li>\n<li><b>Cyberbullying, harassment and non-consensual sharing of images</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Online defamation</b>: publishing false statements that damage someone's reputation.</li>\n<li><b>Deepfakes and manipulated media</b> used to deceive.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Caribbean laws.</b> Several territories have specific legislation, for example Jamaica's Cybercrimes Act (2015), Trinidad and Tobago's Computer Misuse Act (2000) and Barbados's Computer Misuse Act (2005), alongside each country's Copyright Act. Sint Maarten's Criminal Code includes computer crime offences. Check the current versions, as laws are updated.</p>\n<p><b>Consequences of copyright infringement</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>Consequences</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Civil</b></td><td>Being sued by the rights holder; paying damages and legal costs; court orders (injunctions) to stop; seizure of copies</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Criminal</b> (usually for commercial-scale piracy)</td><td>Fines and possible imprisonment</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Platform</b></td><td>Takedown notices, copyright strikes on YouTube, muted audio, demonetisation, account bans</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Academic and professional</b></td><td>Cancelled SBA or exam results, lost clients, damaged reputation</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Example: a Port of Spain café posts a Reel using a famous international pop song. Instagram mutes the audio and the brand's account receives a warning. A local photographer finds her drone shot of Pigeon Point in a hotel brochure without permission and asks for a licence fee; the hotel pays and apologises.</p>\n<p><b>How to stay safe</b>: create original content, use licensed or royalty-free libraries (YouTube Audio Library, Pixabay, Openverse), keep records of licences, get written permission and model releases, and attribute properly.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When asked for \"legal consequences\", separate <b>civil</b> (the creator sues for money) from <b>criminal</b> (the state prosecutes). Naming both types shows depth.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Cybercrime is crime carried out using computers or the Internet, such as piracy, hacking, fake accounts and online bullying. Breaking copyright can lead to being sued, fines, takedowns or account bans. A Port of Spain café that posts a Reel with a famous pop song may have the sound muted and get a warning. Use original or licensed media.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Separate the three routes of enforcement. <b>Civil</b> action is brought by the rights holder for damages or an injunction; <b>criminal</b> prosecution is brought by the state, usually for commercial-scale piracy; and <b>platform</b> enforcement (muted audio, copyright strikes, demonetisation) can happen in minutes without any court. For a small Caribbean brand, the platform route is often the most damaging, because a banned account loses its whole audience. Also note that cyber laws differ by territory and are updated, so name the law as an example and avoid claiming exact penalties unless you have checked the current Act.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"The Carnival campaign deadline","intro":"<p>You are a junior creator at Island Pulse Media. The Spicemas Youth Troupe in Grenada needs a 30-second promotional video and a blog post in three days. Make the right authoring decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Zara asks how you will start. The client's brief says: \"Show young people that Carnival is about culture and creativity, not just partying.\" What do you do first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Brainstorm concepts against the brief, then storyboard the 30-second video and outline the blog post.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Concept development and planning tools save time and keep the content on brief."},{"t":"Start filming random party footage at a Spicemas fete tonight and decide later.","to":"bad1","fb":"Without a concept or storyboard, footage may not match the brief, which asks you to move away from a party image."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Your concept shows troupe members designing costumes, practising steelpan and talking about their grandparents' Carnival memories. You need music. Malik suggests a famous international chart hit. What do you choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Ask the troupe's steelpan players to record an original piece, or license a local soca track from the rights holder.","to":"n3","fb":"Excellent. Original or licensed local music is legal and adds an authentic Caribbean voice."},{"t":"Use the chart hit and credit the artist in the caption.","to":"bad2","fb":"Credit is not permission. The platform will likely mute or remove the video, and the client could face a claim."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>For the blog post you want to include a beautiful photograph of last year's parade that you found online. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Use it because it is on Google Images, so it is free.","to":"bad3","fb":"Search engines show images; they do not license them. Most images online are still protected by copyright."},{"t":"Copy it and write the caption as if you took it.","to":"bad3","fb":"That is both plagiarism (claiming it as yours) and copyright infringement (using it without permission)."},{"t":"Check the licence. If it is CC BY, use it with full attribution; if not, contact the photographer for permission or use your own photo.","to":"good","fb":"Correct. You checked the licence, attributed properly and respected the creator's rights."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Campaign live.</b> The video, with original steelpan music, is shared widely, the blog credits every source, and the troupe receives praise for an authentic Caribbean message. A Grade One answer would explain each decision: plan with a concept and storyboard, use original or licensed media, and check licences and attribute sources.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Off brief.</b> The footage shows only partying, which is the opposite of the client's message, and there is no time to reshoot. Try again: conceptualise and plan before production.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Muted and flagged.</b> Instagram mutes the audio within an hour and the client receives a copyright warning. Try again: attribution is not permission.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Takedown notice.</b> The photographer recognises her image and sends a takedown notice and an invoice. Try again: always check the licence and attribute or get permission.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Plan first","p":"<p>Which planning tools turn a brief into a shot list?</p>"},{"h":"Music rights","p":"<p>Does naming the artist give you permission to use the song?</p>"},{"h":"Images online","p":"<p>Where would you find out whether an image may be reused?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"The Carnival campaign (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A short version: you need music for a promotional video.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Which music do you use?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A famous chart hit, credited in the caption.","to":"bad","fb":"Credit is not permission."},{"t":"Original music by local players, or a properly licensed track.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. It is legal and authentic."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You respected copyright and added a Caribbean voice.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> The video is muted for copyright. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A harder version. The Spicemas Youth Troupe in Grenada needs a 30-second video, a blog post and an Instagram carousel in three days. Several troupe members are under 18, and the brief is: \"Show that Carnival is about culture and creativity.\" Each choice has legal and ethical consequences.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>How do you begin?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Film tonight at a Spicemas fete and decide the story later.","to":"bad1","fb":"Unplanned party footage may contradict the brief and include people who never agreed to be filmed."},{"t":"Storyboard the first idea that comes to mind to save time.","to":"ok1","fb":"Planning is good, but skipping brainstorming may lock you into a weak concept."},{"t":"Clarify the brief and audience with the client, brainstorm concepts, then storyboard the video and outline the blog.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Clarifying the brief first keeps every later decision on message."}]},"ok1":{"text":"<p>Your first idea is a montage of dancers. Zara says it looks like every party advert. What now?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Keep it, but add the word \"culture\" to the caption.","to":"bad1","fb":"A caption cannot fix a concept that contradicts the brief."},{"t":"Return to brainstorming and test ideas against the brief: costume design, pan practice and elders' memories.","to":"n2","fb":"Good recovery. Divergent thinking before convergent selection produces a stronger, on-brief concept."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>You will film three troupe members aged 15 to 17 designing costumes. What permissions do you need?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Signed release forms from each member's parent or guardian, plus the troupe leader's agreement for the location.","to":"n3","fb":"Correct. Minors need a parent or guardian's consent, and the location needs permission too."},{"t":"Verbal agreement from the teenagers is enough because they are in the troupe.","to":"bad2","fb":"Under-18s cannot give full consent alone, and verbal agreement leaves no record."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>For the carousel, you find a stunning parade photo on Flickr marked CC BY-NC. The troupe sells costume packages through the campaign. Can you use it?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Yes, as long as you credit the photographer.","to":"bad3","fb":"Credit satisfies BY, but NC still forbids commercial use. Attribution is not permission for a use the licence excludes."},{"t":"No. The campaign is commercial, so NC rules it out. Ask the photographer for a paid licence or use your own photos.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent reading of the licence. NC means no commercial use, and selling costume packages is commercial."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Malik wants a famous international chart hit for the video. The troupe's pan side offers to record a piece. What do you choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Record the pan side's original piece, with written agreement on how the troupe may use the recording.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Original local music is authentic, and a written agreement settles who may use the recording and where."},{"t":"Use the chart hit but keep only 15 seconds.","to":"bad3","fb":"There is no safe number of seconds. Without a licence, the platform will likely mute the video."},{"t":"Use a royalty-free library track that sounds generic.","to":"ok2","fb":"It is legal, but a generic track weakens the authentic Caribbean message in the brief."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Campaign live and legal.</b> The video, with the pan side's original music, is shared widely, every photo is owned or licensed, and every minor has a signed release. A Grade One answer explains each decision as a legal or ethical problem plus its solution.</p>","end":"good"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Legal but flat.</b> Nothing is taken down, but viewers say the video could be from anywhere. Try again and choose media that carries the Caribbean voice the brief asked for.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Off brief.</b> The footage shows only partying, and some people in it never agreed to be filmed. Try again: conceptualise and plan before production.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Consent problem.</b> A parent objects to her daughter appearing online, and the video must be pulled. Try again: minors need written consent from a parent or guardian.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Licence breached.</b> The rights holder files a complaint and the post is removed. Try again: read every licence element and remember that credit does not equal permission.</p>","end":"bad"}},"title":"The Carnival campaign deadline (Challenge)"}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Plan ethical Caribbean content","genre":"Structured response: generating and planning digital content ethically","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Sint Maarten Heritage Foundation wants young people to learn about the history of the Great Salt Pond in Philipsburg. It has asked your group to create digital content for publication on its website and social media. The Foundation has old photographs from its archive, and an elder who worked near the salt pond as a child is willing to be interviewed.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a response that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>proposes</b> one original content idea for a chosen format (blog, podcast, video or audio slideshow) that reflects a Caribbean perspective;</li><li><b>describes</b> two writing techniques suitable for your chosen format;</li><li><b>explains</b> which planning tool(s) you would use and why;</li><li><b>explains</b> two copyright or ethical issues the group must address, and how it will address them, referring to attribution and Creative Commons or permission;</li><li><b>states</b> one possible legal consequence of ignoring copyright.</li></ol>","min":220,"max":450,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Our idea is ... in the format of ... It reflects a Caribbean perspective because ...<br><b>(b)</b> One technique is ... because ... A second technique is ...<br><b>(c)</b> We would use a storyboard / script ... because ...<br><b>(d)</b> First, ... We will address this by ... Second, ...<br><b>(e)</b> If we ignored copyright, ...</p>","checklist":["My idea is specific to Sint Maarten and the salt pond, not generic.","I named two writing techniques for my chosen format (for example voice tone, pacing or sequencing) and explained why they work.","I explained why my planning tool suits the format.","I addressed consent for the interview and permission or licences for photos and music.","I distinguished plagiarism from copyright infringement or explained attribution.","I gave a civil, criminal or platform consequence."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Content idea and writing techniques","marks":4,"look":["salt pond|salt|philipsburg|sint maarten|st\\. maarten","blog|podcast|video|slideshow","hook|headline|signpost|conversational|short sentence|caption|call to action|subheading","elder|interview|voice|stor","tone|pac|sequenc|storyboard|brief|purpose|like you speak|read.{0,10}aloud|out loud|introduction|conclusion"],"min":3,"strong":"Your idea is specific and authentic, and your techniques suit the format.","weak":"Make the idea specific to the salt pond and name techniques such as a strong hook, signposting, writing like you speak, voice tone, pacing or sequencing the images on a storyboard, with a reason for each."},{"criterion":"Planning tools","marks":3,"look":["storyboard","script","wireframe|mock.?up|frame|shot|sequence"],"min":2,"strong":"You chose suitable planning tools and justified them.","weak":"Name a planning tool and say why it fits, e.g. \"a storyboard to plan the order of the archive photos and the narration under each\"."},{"criterion":"Copyright, ethics and consequences","marks":5,"look":["copyright","permission|licen","attribut|credit","consent|release","creative commons|cc by|public domain","sue|damage|fine|takedown|strike|remov|court"],"min":4,"strong":"You explained the legal and ethical issues clearly and gave realistic solutions and consequences.","weak":"Address permission for archive photos, consent from the elder, credit for sources and a consequence such as being sued for damages or a takedown notice."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Our idea is \"Salt in Our Blood\", a five-minute audio slideshow in which archive photographs of the Great Salt Pond are shown while the elder describes what working near the pond was like as a child. It reflects a Caribbean perspective because it tells Sint Maarten's history in a local voice, in Sint Maarten English, and connects young people with an elder's real memories instead of a textbook summary.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> One technique is visual sequencing: we will place the photographs in order on a storyboard before writing, then write narration to the image order so each photograph is introduced with one clear idea, in a calm, respectful voice tone and at a slow pace so viewers have time to look. A second technique is using short, conversational sentences and signposting such as \"Before the salt was raked...\", because audio is heard once and listeners cannot scroll back.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> We would use a storyboard to plan the sequence of photographs, the duration of each slide and the narration or sound under each frame. We would also use a script with interview questions and our linking narration, so the recording session is focused and the final piece fits five minutes.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> First, the archive photographs may be protected by copyright. We will ask the Foundation for written permission, check who took each photo and attribute each one, for example \"Photo: Sint Maarten Heritage Foundation archive, used with permission\". If we add extra images, we will use only CC BY or public domain images and credit them. Second, we must get the elder's informed consent, in writing, to record and publish her voice and story online, and let her review the final piece. We will also use original or royalty-free music, because crediting a song is not the same as having permission, and presenting others' work as ours would be plagiarism.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> If we ignored copyright, the photographer or rights holder could sue the Foundation for damages in a civil claim, and the platform could remove the content with a takedown notice, damaging the Foundation's reputation.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Paper 02 Module 2 questions often combine creative and legal parts. Keep your idea specific and Caribbean, then treat each legal point as problem plus solution: \"The photos may be protected by copyright, so we will get written permission and attribute them.\" That structure earns both the \"identify\" and the \"explain\" marks.</p>",
 "easy": {"bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: planning content ethically (8 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part. Use the sentence starters to help you.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Choose</b> a format (blog, podcast, video or audio slideshow) and <b>give</b> one idea about the Great Salt Pond. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE writing technique for your format and say why it helps. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> ONE planning tool you would use. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> what the group must do before using the old photographs and the elder's interview. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":80,"max":200,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> We will make a ... about ...<br><b>(b)</b> One technique is ... It helps because ...<br><b>(c)</b> We will use a ...<br><b>(d)</b> Before using the photos, we must ... We must also credit ... Before recording the elder, we must ...</p>","checklist":["My idea names the Great Salt Pond.","I named one writing technique and gave a reason.","I named a planning tool such as a storyboard or script.","I said we need permission for the photos.","I said we will credit the source.","I said the elder must agree (consent)."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Format and idea","marks":2,"look":["blog|podcast|video|slideshow","salt pond|salt"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear format and a specific idea.","weak":"Name a format and link your idea to the Great Salt Pond."},{"criterion":"Writing technique","marks":2,"look":["hook|headline|signpost|conversational|short sentence|tone|pac|sequenc|subheading|call to action|like you speak","because|helps|so that|so "],"min":2,"strong":"A technique with a reason.","weak":"Name a technique such as a calm voice tone or short sentences, then say why it helps the audience."},{"criterion":"Planning tool","marks":1,"look":["storyboard|script|wireframe|mock.?up"],"min":1,"strong":"Suitable tool named.","weak":"Name a planning tool, such as a storyboard."},{"criterion":"Permission, credit and consent","marks":3,"look":["permission|licen|ask","credit|attribut|name the","consent|agree|release"],"min":2,"strong":"You covered permission, credit and consent.","weak":"Ask permission to use the photos, credit the source, and get the elder's consent before recording."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> We will make an audio slideshow about what life was like near the Great Salt Pond, using old photos and the elder's memories.</p><p><b>(b)</b> One technique is pacing control. It helps because viewers need time to look at each photo while they listen.</p><p><b>(c)</b> We will use a storyboard to put the photos in order.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Before using the photos, we must ask the Foundation for permission. We must also credit the archive under each photo. Before recording the elder, we must explain the project and get her written consent.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>For part (d), write three short actions: ask permission, give credit, get consent. Each one is a separate mark, so do not join them into one vague sentence.</p>","title":"Plan ethical Caribbean content (Foundation)"},
 "up": {"bloom":"Create","genre":"Structured response: an ethical content plan, Grade One extension (20 marks)","prompt":"<p>The Foundation now wants a <b>multi-platform</b> campaign: one main piece (blog, podcast, video or audio slideshow) repurposed for two social media platforms. Write a plan that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>proposes</b> an original main piece and <b>justifies</b> how it reflects an authentic Caribbean perspective for young people. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>explains</b> how the content will be repurposed for TWO named social platforms, with one writing technique for each. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>justifies</b> the choice of TWO planning tools for the campaign. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>evaluates</b> the copyright and ethical risks of using the archive photographs, the elder's interview and music, and <b>explains</b> how each will be managed. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>distinguishes</b> between civil, criminal and platform consequences of infringement. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":380,"max":650,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Our main piece is ... It is authentic because ... Young people will connect with it because ...<br><b>(b)</b> On ... we will ... using the technique of ... On ... we will ...<br><b>(c)</b> A storyboard is needed because ... A script is needed because ...<br><b>(d)</b> The first risk is ... We will manage it by ... The second risk is ... The music ... Overall, the greatest risk is ... because ...<br><b>(e)</b> A civil consequence is ... whereas a criminal consequence ... On a platform, ...</p>","checklist":["My main piece is specific to the Salt Pond and justified as authentic, not just Caribbean-looking.","I named two platforms and changed length, tone or format for each, with a technique.","I justified two planning tools by what each helps the team decide.","I evaluated photos, interview and music separately and gave a solution for each.","I judged which risk is greatest.","I distinguished civil, criminal and platform consequences."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Main piece and Caribbean perspective","marks":4,"look":["salt pond|salt","blog|podcast|video|slideshow","elder|voice|memor|stor","authentic|local|sint maarten english|community|stereotyp"],"min":3,"strong":"A specific main piece with a convincing justification of authenticity.","weak":"Make the piece specific to the Salt Pond and explain why its local voices make it authentic for young people."},{"criterion":"Repurposing for two platforms","marks":4,"look":["instagram|tiktok|facebook|whatsapp|youtube|x\\b|twitter","repurpos|shorter|clip|carousel|caption|vertical|9:16","hook|hashtag|alt text|caption|call to action|short"],"min":3,"strong":"Two platforms with clear changes and techniques.","weak":"Name two platforms and say how the length, format and writing change on each, e.g. a 30-second captioned TikTok with a hook."},{"criterion":"Planning tools justified","marks":3,"look":["storyboard","script|wireframe|mock.?up","because|so that|helps|decide"],"min":3,"strong":"Two tools justified by what they plan.","weak":"Justify each tool by what it helps decide: the storyboard plans the image order; the script plans the words."},{"criterion":"Evaluation of copyright and ethical risks","marks":6,"look":["photo|archive","permission|licen","attribut|credit","consent|release","music|soundtrack|song","greatest|most serious|biggest|main risk|overall","creative commons|cc by|public domain|original|royalty"],"min":5,"strong":"A thorough evaluation with a solution for each risk and a judgement.","weak":"Treat photos, interview and music separately, giving the risk and the solution for each, and say which risk is greatest and why."},{"criterion":"Civil, criminal and platform consequences","marks":3,"look":["civil|sue|damages","criminal|prosecut|fine|prison","takedown|strike|mute|remov|ban|platform"],"min":3,"strong":"All three routes distinguished.","weak":"Contrast civil (the rights holder sues), criminal (the state prosecutes) and platform (takedowns or strikes) consequences."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Our main piece is \"Salt in Our Blood\", a six-minute audio slideshow in which archive photographs of the Great Salt Pond appear while the elder describes raking salt as a child. It is authentic because the story is told by someone who lived it, in Sint Maarten English, rather than by an outsider summarising a textbook. Young people will connect with it because it links a place they pass every day in Philipsburg to their own grandparents' generation, avoiding the stereotype that Caribbean heritage is only beaches and festivals.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> We will repurpose the slideshow for TikTok as three 30-second vertical clips, each with one striking memory. The technique is a strong hook in the first two seconds, such as \"At eight years old, she raked salt barefoot\", with burned-in captions because many viewers watch without sound. On Instagram we will post a carousel of five archive photos with short captions, a call to action (\"Watch the full story at the link in our bio\") and alt text on every image.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> A storyboard is needed because it helps the team decide the order of the photographs, the duration of each slide and where each clip will be cut for TikTok. A script is needed because it plans the interview questions and our linking narration, so the recording session is focused and the narration fits the image order.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> The first risk is the archive photographs: the Foundation may hold the prints but not the copyright, because some were taken by private photographers. We will check the source of each image, get written permission and attribute each one, and replace any image we cannot clear with a public domain or CC BY alternative. The second risk is ethical: the elder must give informed, written consent to publication online, including on social media, and she should review the final cut, because publishing personal memories without care could cause harm. The music is a further risk; crediting a commercial song is not permission, so we will use original music or a royalty-free track. Overall, the greatest risk is the photographs, because their ownership is the least certain and the campaign reuses them on several platforms.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> A civil consequence is that the rights holder could sue the Foundation for damages or an injunction, whereas a criminal consequence, usually for commercial-scale piracy, means the state prosecutes and can impose a fine or prison. On a platform, the post can be removed by a takedown notice or the account can receive a copyright strike, which happens quickly and can damage the campaign even before any court case.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>\"Evaluate\" needs a judgement, so name the greatest risk and say why. Treat each risk as problem, solution and reason. In part (e), use \"whereas\" to keep civil and criminal consequences in one comparative sentence, then add the platform route.</p>","title":"Plan ethical Caribbean content (Challenge)"}}]},{"id":"M2U3","title":"Prototypes using design principles","objectives":["Define a prototype as an incomplete version of a product taken into user testing, and distinguish low-, mid- and high-fidelity prototypes and wireframes, mock-ups and interactive prototypes.","Follow the design journey from wireframe to mockup to prototype, justify when to use each artifact and which tool (Balsamiq, Figma, Sketch, Adobe XD, Axure RP, Moqups), explain why teams prototype early (new perspective, fresh eyes, stakeholder buy-in) and identify the four types of prototype (feasibility, low-fidelity user, high-fidelity user, live-data).","Explain and create a concept map (\"brainstorming with a purpose\", with linking words), a navigational chart, a site map (HTML sitemap and XML sitemap) and a hierarchy map, and trace user pathways.","Distinguish a sitemap from navigation, and justify the five types of website navigation: horizontal bar, dropdown, hamburger, vertical sidebar and footer.","Apply design principles to layouts: rule of thirds, composition, balance, alignment, hierarchy, contrast, spacing and visual flow.","Compare cloud-based prototyping and authoring tools such as Figma, Canva, Adobe Express, Adobe XD, Balsamiq and Marvel, and choose cloud-based tools for each map (Lucidchart, Miro, Canva whiteboards, FigJam, draw.io, Coggle, MindMeister, Octopus.do, Slickplan, XML-Sitemaps.com).","Build a simple clickable wireframe in Figma using frames, auto layout, components and prototype links."],"videos":[{"title":"Low-fidelity vs high-fidelity prototypes","q":"low fidelity vs high fidelity prototype explained UX"},{"title":"How to make a concept map in Lucidchart","q":"How to Make a Concept Map in Lucidchart"},{"title":"What is a website site map?","q":"What Is: Website Site Map"},{"title":"Figma wireframe tutorial for beginners","q":"figma tutorial for beginners wireframe auto layout components prototype"},{"title":"Design principles for layouts","q":"graphic design principles balance alignment contrast hierarchy explained"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"The design journey: from concept to interactive experience","html":"<p>Start with the <b>design journey</b>. Creating successful digital products requires a structured approach that moves through distinct phases, each serving a unique purpose in the development lifecycle.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Stage</th><th>What it does</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1. Wireframes</b></td><td>Define <b>structure and layout</b> with low-fidelity blueprints.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2. Mockups</b></td><td>Visualise <b>aesthetics</b> with high-fidelity designs.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3. Prototypes</b></td><td>Test <b>interactions</b> with clickable simulations.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Each stage builds upon the previous one, progressively adding detail and functionality. By choosing the right tool for each phase, teams <b>save time, reduce development costs</b> and <b>minimise the risk</b> of building features that do not meet user needs. You must be able to create prototypes using design principles to visually indicate layout solutions, and this journey is how professionals get there.</p>\n<p>You will also see the word <b>fidelity</b>: how closely a design resembles the finished product.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Low-fidelity</b>: rough and simple, often on paper or a whiteboard, showing layout and structure but not final colours, fonts or images.<br><b>Mid-fidelity</b>: a digital, clickable version in greyscale, with real layout and flows but no final styling.<br><b>High-fidelity</b>: detailed and digital, looking (and in a prototype, behaving) almost like the finished product.</p>\n<p>Here is a clear example of each level:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Low-fidelity (lo-fi)</th><th>Mid-fidelity</th><th>High-fidelity (hi-fi)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Handout example</td><td>Paper drawings: paper wireframes of app screens</td><td>A click-through of a few pieces of content: clickable greyscale wireframes with real layout and flows</td><td>A fully functioning site: pixel-polished app screens with live-style content</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tools</td><td>Paper, pencil, sticky notes, black marker, Balsamiq</td><td>Balsamiq, Figma or Moqups in greyscale, Marvel with linked sketches</td><td>Figma, Adobe XD, Sketch, Canva, Marvel</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Detail</td><td>Grey boxes, \"X\" for images, lines for text</td><td>Real layout and spacing, placeholder text and images</td><td>Real content, brand colours, fonts, icons</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Interactivity</td><td>None, or a person \"plays computer\" by swapping paper screens</td><td>Key buttons and links work for one or two flows</td><td>Clickable buttons, transitions, scrolling</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cost and speed</td><td>Very cheap, minutes to make</td><td>Hours, needs basic software skill</td><td>Slower, needs software and skill</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Early ideas, structure, quick team feedback</td><td>Testing navigation and flow with users</td><td>Client sign-off, usability testing, developer handover</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>At the Island Pulse Media, Zara follows the journey on every job: paper wireframes go up on the studio wall, Maya turns the approved ones into Figma mockups, and then links them into a clickable prototype for the client to test.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In Paper 02 you will have paper, drawing pencils and black markers. A neat, labelled lo-fi wireframe drawn with a ruler earns marks. Label every element (\"logo\", \"nav bar\", \"hero image\", \"call-to-action button\").</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Designers build a digital product in three steps. A wireframe plans the layout with simple grey boxes. A mockup shows the final colours, fonts and pictures. A prototype lets people click through and test it. Planning a café website on Bay Street, you would sketch the page on paper first, then add colour, then make the buttons work.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Fidelity is a <b>cost and feedback</b> decision. Low-fidelity work is cheap to change, and people give honest feedback on structure because it clearly is not finished. High-fidelity work persuades clients but invites comments on colour instead of flow, and every change costs more. Mid-fidelity, clickable greyscale screens, is the sweet spot for testing navigation. A common misconception is that the journey is strictly one-way; in practice teams loop back, for example returning to a wireframe when a prototype test shows users cannot find the booking button. In Paper 02, a ruled, fully labelled lo-fi wireframe often scores better than a messy attempt at a polished design.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Low-fidelity or high-fidelity?","intro":"<p>The Island Pulse Media is designing a website for the Mango Lane Café in Scarborough, Tobago. Sort each item from the project folder.</p>","main":"Is this a low-fidelity or a high-fidelity prototype?","bins":["Low-fidelity","High-fidelity"],"items":[{"t":"A pencil sketch of the home page on printer paper, with boxes marked \"photo\" and \"menu\".","bin":0,"fb":"Paper, pencil and placeholder boxes are the signs of a low-fidelity prototype."},{"t":"Paper screens that Zara swaps by hand while a customer \"taps\" them with a finger.","bin":0,"fb":"This is paper prototyping. A person acts as the computer, which is still low-fidelity."},{"t":"A Figma file where clicking \"Order now\" slides to a checkout screen with the café's real logo.","bin":1,"fb":"Real branding plus working interactions make this high-fidelity."},{"t":"A black-marker layout drawn in a Paper 02 exam with labelled boxes.","bin":0,"fb":"Hand-drawn layouts with labels are low-fidelity, and they are exactly what Paper 02 often asks for."},{"t":"A prototype with smart-animate transitions that the developer will inspect for exact spacing values.","bin":1,"fb":"Detailed spacing, transitions and developer handover belong to high-fidelity work."},{"t":"A clickable mobile prototype using the café's brand colours, photos of johnny cakes and the final fonts.","bin":1,"fb":"Final colours, images and fonts with clickable links are high-fidelity."},{"t":"Sticky notes on a whiteboard, each one a page, moved around during a team meeting.","bin":0,"fb":"Sticky notes are quick, cheap and rough, which is low-fidelity."},{"t":"A grey-box Balsamiq wireframe with hand-drawn style lines and no colour.","bin":0,"fb":"Balsamiq deliberately looks sketchy so people focus on structure, not style. That is low-fidelity."}],"win":"Well sorted. Low-fidelity is rough, cheap and focused on structure; high-fidelity is detailed, branded and interactive.","tutor":[{"h":"Look at the tools","p":"<p>Paper, sticky notes and sketchy wireframes point one way. Real brand assets and clickable transitions point the other.</p>"},{"h":"Purpose","p":"<p>Is it for testing an early idea quickly, or for getting final sign-off and handing over to a developer?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Lo-fi or hi-fi? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A pencil sketch with grey boxes.","bin":0,"fb":"Rough and on paper: low-fidelity."},{"t":"A clickable design with the real logo and photos.","bin":1,"fb":"Detailed and interactive: high-fidelity."},{"t":"Sticky notes on a whiteboard.","bin":0,"fb":"Quick and cheap: low-fidelity."},{"t":"A Figma screen with final colours and fonts.","bin":1,"fb":"Final styling: high-fidelity."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Three levels now: low, mid and high fidelity. Sort each item from the Mango Lane Café project folder.</p>","main":"What level of fidelity is this?","bins":["Low-fidelity","Mid-fidelity","High-fidelity"],"items":[{"t":"Sticky notes for each page arranged on the studio wall.","bin":0,"fb":"Quick, rough and physical is low-fidelity."},{"t":"Greyscale Figma screens with real layout and spacing, where tapping \"Menu\" opens the Menu screen.","bin":1,"fb":"Digital, clickable and greyscale with no final styling is mid-fidelity."},{"t":"Polished screens in the café's brand colours with real photos of johnny cakes and smooth transitions.","bin":2,"fb":"Real content, brand colours and transitions are high-fidelity."},{"t":"Placeholder text and grey image boxes in a click-through that tests the ordering flow for one task.","bin":1,"fb":"Working links for one or two flows with placeholder content is mid-fidelity."},{"t":"A pencil sketch of the home page with an \"X\" for each photo.","bin":0,"fb":"Paper, pencil and placeholder crosses are low-fidelity."},{"t":"Screens the developer inspects for exact fonts, colours and spacing values before coding.","bin":2,"fb":"Pixel-polished detail for developer handover is high-fidelity."},{"t":"A Figma prototype with the final logo, icons and live-style content, used for client sign-off.","bin":2,"fb":"Final branding used for sign-off is high-fidelity."},{"t":"Paper screens that a designer swaps by hand while a customer \"taps\" them.","bin":0,"fb":"A person playing computer with paper screens is still low-fidelity."},{"t":"A greyscale Moqups file linking Home, Menu and Order with real page layout but no logo or brand fonts.","bin":1,"fb":"Real layout and links without final styling is mid-fidelity."}],"win":"Well sorted. Low-fidelity shows rough structure, mid-fidelity adds real layout and working flows in greyscale, and high-fidelity adds final content, branding and polish.","title":"Low-fidelity or high-fidelity? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Wireframes and mockups","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Wireframe: the skeleton of your design.</b> Wireframes are the foundational blueprints of digital design: simplified, <b>low-fidelity</b> representations that focus exclusively on <b>layout, structure and user flow</b>. Think of them as architectural floor plans for your digital product.</p>\n<p>They are intentionally minimalist: basic shapes, greyscale palettes and placeholder content strip away visual distractions, so teams concentrate on <b>functionality and user experience</b>. They are quick to make on paper, whiteboards, or in Balsamiq, Figma or Sketch, so teams can explore several concepts and align stakeholders before detailed design work.</p>\n<p><b>Wireframe benefits and best practices</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Clarifies information architecture</b>: sets the content hierarchy and navigation paths so users can find what they need, and reveals usability issues early.</li>\n<li><b>Accelerates feedback cycles</b>: low-fidelity designs speed up ideation and invite honest feedback, because stakeholders discuss functionality rather than colours or fonts.</li>\n<li><b>Keep it simple</b>: resist adding detail; over-designed wireframes defeat their purpose. Use boxes for images, lines for text and basic shapes for buttons. Label your deliverables <b>\"Low-Fidelity Wireframe\"</b> to set clear expectations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Mockup: the visual design preview.</b> Mockups are <b>high-fidelity, static</b> representations that bring wireframes to life with full visual detail: the actual <b>colours, typography, icons, imagery and spacing</b> of the final product. They focus on look and feel and need no coding or interactivity.</p>\n<p><b>Why use mockups?</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Refine visual consistency</b>: perfect UI elements, establish design systems and keep every screen consistent before code is written.</li>\n<li><b>Secure stakeholder buy-in</b>: polished, realistic designs make abstract ideas tangible for non-technical clients.</li>\n<li><b>Rapid visual iteration</b>: changing a mockup is far faster and cheaper than changing code.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Strategic decision point</b>: teams often create <b>multiple mockup variations</b> of key screens, such as the home page, so stakeholders can compare options and choose a visual direction before development. Industry-standard mockup tools are <b>Adobe XD, Sketch and Figma</b>.</p>\n<p>Example: for the Mango Lane Café in Scarborough, Maya draws a grey wireframe labelled \"Low-Fidelity Wireframe\", then makes two mockups, one in sea blue and one in mango orange, for the owner to compare.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> To <b>distinguish</b> a wireframe from a mockup, compare on the same features: fidelity (low versus high), focus (structure versus look and feel) and content (placeholders versus real colours and images). Both are static.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A wireframe is like a floor plan for a web page. It uses grey boxes and lines to show where things go, without colour. A mockup is a finished-looking picture of the page with real colours, fonts and photos, but nothing can be clicked yet. A designer for a Barbados bakery might show the owner two mockups in different colours.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The two artifacts answer different questions, so they invite different feedback. A wireframe asks \"is this the right structure?\", while a mockup asks \"is this the right look?\" Showing a client a mockup too early is a common error, because discussion turns to colours while a buried booking form goes unnoticed. Mockups add value at the <b>strategic decision point</b>: offering two or three visual directions for the key screen lets stakeholders choose before any code exists. Remember that both are static. When you distinguish them, compare on fidelity, focus and content in the same sentence, using \"whereas\" to show the contrast.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"What is a prototype?","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Prototype: an incomplete version of a physical or digital product, taken into user testing.</b> Prototypes are the <b>interactive experience</b>: clickable simulations that show how users will interact with your product. Unlike static mockups, they bring designs to life with navigation, transitions and user workflows.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>A prototype includes</th><th>A prototype leaves out</th></tr>\n<tr><td>All fundamental features and functions</td><td>The final design elements that create a polished aesthetic</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>So a prototype is <b>a draft that tests your ideas before you build</b>: it explores ideas and shows the intention behind a feature or overall design concept <b>before investing time and money in development</b>. It can be built at any of the three fidelity levels from the first screen: paper drawings, a click-through of a few pieces of content, or a fully functioning site.</p>\n<p>Three numbered purposes from the earlier handout:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Simulate real interactions</b>: button clicks, page transitions, form submissions and navigation flows.</li>\n<li><b>Validate user experience</b>: testing with real users finds usability issues and confusing workflows before development.</li>\n<li><b>De-risk development</b>: prototypes act as detailed specifications, so developers and designers share one vision.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Change it early, when it is cheap.</b> Changing a product early is much cheaper than changing it after the site is developed. So build prototypes <b>early in the process</b>, and gather user feedback while you are still planning and designing.</p>\n<p><b>How prototyping helps product teams</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>New perspective</b>: a good idea in principle may not seem so relevant once it is in hand.</li>\n<li><b>Fresh eyes</b>: testers flag even minor functional flaws or inconveniences as they perform core tasks, uncovering issues before launch.</li>\n<li><b>Stakeholder buy-in</b>: something intangible becomes something clients and investors can interact with, creating \"aha moments\".</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Example</b>: Jerome at the Island Pulse Media built a clickable sign-up flow (landing screen, account creation, first login) for the Bus Pass Barbados app. Students at the Fairchild Street bus terminal in Bridgetown tested it, and the fresh eyes spotted that the \"Top up\" button was hidden. Moving it took ten minutes in Figma; after coding it would have taken days.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Learn the definition word for word: \"an incomplete version of a product, taken into user testing\". The word that separates a prototype from a mockup is <b>interactive</b>; the word that separates it from the finished product is <b>incomplete</b>.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A prototype is an early, incomplete version of a product that real people test. It can be clicked, so testers can tap buttons and move between screens. Testing early finds problems while they are still cheap to fix. Students testing a bus app at the Fairchild Street terminal found a hidden \"Top up\" button, and fixing it took only minutes.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The value of a prototype lies in what it lets you <b>learn before you build</b>. Each round of testing reduces risk: technical risk (can it work?), usability risk (can people use it?) and market risk (will people want it?). The two key words in the definition matter: \"incomplete\" separates a prototype from the finished product, and \"user testing\" separates it from a mockup. A common misconception is that prototypes must look polished; a paper prototype with a person swapping screens is still a prototype, because it is tested interactively. In an evaluation, link prototyping to cost: a fix found in testing is far cheaper than one found after launch.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Wireframe, mockup or prototype?","intro":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media has mixed up the files for the Punda Market Online project. Sort each file using the definitions.</p>","main":"Is this a wireframe, a mockup or a prototype?","bins":["Wireframe","Mockup","Prototype"],"items":[{"t":"A Sketch file used to perfect button styles and spacing across every screen.","bin":1,"fb":"Refining visual consistency is a reason to use mockups."},{"t":"A Figma file where tapping \"Sign up\" moves through account creation to first login.","bin":2,"fb":"A clickable sign-up flow simulating real interactions is a prototype."},{"t":"A clickable demo tested with five vendors to find confusing steps before coding.","bin":2,"fb":"Validating user experience with real users is a purpose of prototypes."},{"t":"Two versions of the home page, one in teal and one in coral, for the client to compare.","bin":1,"fb":"Multiple variations of a key screen to choose a visual direction is the mockup \"strategic decision point\"."},{"t":"A whiteboard sketch mapping where the search bar, product list and menu go.","bin":0,"fb":"It shows only layout and structure, so it is a wireframe."},{"t":"An Axure RP file where a form shows different screens depending on the answer chosen.","bin":2,"fb":"Conditional logic and dynamic content are advanced prototyping features."},{"t":"A greyscale layout labelled \"Low-Fidelity Wireframe\", with boxes for images and lines for text.","bin":0,"fb":"Greyscale, placeholders and the label all mark a wireframe: the skeleton of the design."},{"t":"A full-colour home page with the market's logo, fonts and product photos, but nothing can be clicked.","bin":1,"fb":"High-fidelity but static: that is a mockup."}],"win":"Well sorted. Wireframes define structure, mockups show the look and feel, and prototypes simulate interaction: the design journey.","tutor":[{"h":"Colour?","p":"<p>Greyscale and placeholders point to the first stage of the journey.</p>"},{"h":"Can you click it?","p":"<p>If it looks finished but does nothing, it is static. If it responds to taps, it is the third stage.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Wireframe, mockup or prototype? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A grey layout with boxes and lines.","bin":0,"fb":"Structure only: wireframe."},{"t":"A full-colour design you cannot click.","bin":1,"fb":"Static and detailed: mockup."},{"t":"Linked screens you can tap through.","bin":2,"fb":"Interactive: prototype."},{"t":"Two colour versions of the home page to compare.","bin":1,"fb":"Mockup variations help choose the look."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder files from the Punda Market Online project. Remember: interaction makes something a prototype, whatever its fidelity.</p>","items":[{"t":"Greyscale boxes with \"X\" image placeholders and lines for text, which cannot be clicked.","bin":0,"fb":"Static and low-fidelity, focused on structure: a wireframe."},{"t":"Greyscale screens linked so testers can tap from \"Products\" to \"Basket\".","bin":2,"fb":"Even in greyscale, clickable screens tested with users form a prototype (a low-fidelity user prototype)."},{"t":"A pixel-perfect static image of the product page used for developer handoff.","bin":1,"fb":"High fidelity but not interactive: a mockup."},{"t":"Paper screens swapped by a designer while a vendor \"taps\" through the sign-up steps.","bin":2,"fb":"Testing interaction with paper screens is paper prototyping."},{"t":"A whiteboard sketch agreeing where the search bar and categories go.","bin":0,"fb":"Layout and structure only: a wireframe."},{"t":"Three colour versions of the home page for the client to choose a visual direction.","bin":1,"fb":"Visual variations for stakeholders are the mockup strategic decision point."},{"t":"A branded Figma file where the \"Pay\" button triggers a smart-animate transition to a receipt screen.","bin":2,"fb":"Simulated interaction with transitions is a prototype."},{"t":"A Sketch file refining button styles and spacing across all screens, with no links.","bin":1,"fb":"Refining visual consistency without interaction is mockup work."},{"t":"A labelled \"Low-Fidelity Wireframe\" page drawn with a ruler in Paper 02.","bin":0,"fb":"A labelled, static, hand-drawn layout is a wireframe."}],"win":"Well analysed. Fidelity tells you how detailed something is; interactivity tells you whether it is a prototype. A clickable grey screen is a prototype, while a polished static screen is a mockup.","title":"Wireframe, mockup or prototype? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Four types of prototype","html":"<p>Not every prototype is a set of clickable screens. There are <b>four types</b>, each built by a different person to answer a different question.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>What it is</th><th>Who builds it</th><th>Question it answers</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1. Feasibility</b></td><td>Just enough code to test new technology, such as an updated algorithm</td><td>An engineer</td><td>Can it work? It helps the team understand <b>technical risk</b>, often performance.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2. Low-fidelity user</b></td><td>An interactive wireframe that does not look real</td><td>Designers</td><td>Does the <b>workflow</b> make sense? It finds usability issues early.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3. High-fidelity user</b></td><td>A realistic-looking, working simulation</td><td>Designers</td><td>Will people want it? It is good for <b>communicating a proposed product to stakeholders</b>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>4. Live-data</b></td><td>A very limited implementation that has access to <b>real data and live traffic</b></td><td>Developers</td><td>Does it really work in the world? It proves the idea works.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>One Caribbean project, four prototypes.</b> The Island Pulse Media is making a ferry app for the Bay Hopper service between Basseterre and Charlestown.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Feasibility</b>: before anything is designed, Jerome writes a quick script to check that the boat's GPS position can update the map every few seconds on a cheap phone over a weak signal. That is a performance risk, so it is tested first.</li>\n<li><b>Low-fidelity user</b>: Zara links grey Balsamiq screens (Timetable, Choose trip, Pay) and asks five passengers at the Basseterre ferry terminal to \"buy a ticket\". Two cannot find the return-trip option, so the workflow is changed.</li>\n<li><b>High-fidelity user</b>: Maya builds a polished Figma version in the ferry's colours with real photos of the boats, and Keisha uses it to show the owners and the tourism office what the finished app will feel like.</li>\n<li><b>Live-data</b>: a bare-bones version is released to 50 regular commuters, using real timetables and real bookings, to prove that the system holds up with live traffic.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Notice how the types follow \"change it early, when it is cheap\": the risky technical question and the workflow are tested before money is spent on polish or code for everyone.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> The two \"user\" types are the easiest to mix up. Low-fidelity user prototypes test the <b>workflow</b> and \"do not look real\"; high-fidelity user prototypes look realistic and are used to <b>communicate with stakeholders</b>. If a question mentions engineers, algorithms or performance, think feasibility; if it mentions real data or live traffic, think live-data.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>There are four types of prototype. A feasibility prototype checks if the technology can work. A low-fidelity user prototype checks if the steps make sense. A high-fidelity user prototype shows clients what it will look and feel like. A live-data prototype tests a small real version with real users, like a ferry app tried by 50 commuters.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The four types are a <b>risk ladder</b>: test the riskiest unknown first, at the lowest cost. If the core technology might fail, as with GPS updates on cheap phones over a weak signal, build a feasibility prototype before any design work. If the technology is ordinary but the workflow is new, start with a low-fidelity user prototype. A common confusion is between high-fidelity user and live-data: a polished simulation can look completely real yet still use sample data, whereas a live-data prototype may look rough but runs on real data and real traffic. In an exam, identify the type from the clue: who built it, and what question it answers.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Which type of prototype?","intro":"<p>The Island Pulse Media is working on several projects across the islands. Sort each prototype into one of the four types.</p>","main":"Which of the four types of prototype is this?","bins":["Feasibility","Low-fidelity user","High-fidelity user","Live-data"],"items":[{"t":"Zara watches three students try to register for a Carnival event using clickable sketches, noting where they hesitate.","bin":1,"fb":"Finding usability issues early in the workflow with rough screens is the job of a low-fidelity user prototype."},{"t":"A rough test checking whether a photo-recognition feature can identify Caribbean reef fish quickly enough to be useful for Belize Reef Divers.","bin":0,"fb":"It tests technical risk before any design work, which is feasibility."},{"t":"A bare-bones booking page goes online for one weekend to see whether real customers actually book Placencia kayak tours through it.","bin":3,"fb":"Real traffic proving that it works in the world is the mark of a live-data prototype."},{"t":"Linked grey boxes in Balsamiq that let testers tap from \"Menu\" to \"Order\" for a fish-fry stand at Oistins.","bin":1,"fb":"An interactive wireframe that does not look real, used to test the workflow, is a low-fidelity user prototype."},{"t":"A polished Figma simulation in full brand colours that Keisha presents to the board of an Antigua hotel to win approval.","bin":2,"fb":"Realistic-looking and used to communicate a proposed product to stakeholders: high-fidelity user."},{"t":"Jerome writes a few lines of code to see whether a new route-finding algorithm for the Bus Pass Barbados app is fast enough on older phones.","bin":0,"fb":"An engineer testing new technology and performance with just enough code is a feasibility prototype."},{"t":"Developers release a very limited version of a market-price app to 30 vendors in Georgetown, pulling real prices each morning.","bin":3,"fb":"A very limited implementation with real data and live traffic is a live-data prototype."},{"t":"A realistic, working simulation of a Curaçao art trail app, with real photos and smooth transitions, shown to investors.","bin":2,"fb":"It looks real and works as a simulation, but it does not use live data. That makes it high-fidelity user."}],"win":"Well sorted. Feasibility tests technical risk, low-fidelity user tests the workflow, high-fidelity user communicates with stakeholders, and live-data proves it works with real data and traffic.","tutor":[{"h":"Who builds it?","p":"<p>An engineer testing an algorithm, designers testing screens, or developers releasing something small to real users? The builder is a strong clue.</p>"},{"h":"Does it look real?","p":"<p>Both user types are interactive. One looks rough, the other looks finished. Ask what each is for: testing the flow, or impressing stakeholders?</p>"},{"h":"Real data?","p":"<p>Only one type touches real data and live traffic.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which type of prototype? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"An engineer writes a little code to test whether a new algorithm is fast enough.","bin":0,"fb":"Testing technical risk: feasibility."},{"t":"A clickable wireframe that does not look real, used to test the workflow.","bin":1,"fb":"Rough and interactive: low-fidelity user."},{"t":"A realistic simulation shown to the client to win approval.","bin":2,"fb":"Looks real, for stakeholders: high-fidelity user."},{"t":"A tiny version that uses real data and real visitors.","bin":3,"fb":"Real data and live traffic: live-data."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","main":"Which type of prototype should the team build next?","intro":"<p>For each situation, choose the type of prototype the team should build NEXT to answer its biggest question.</p>","items":[{"t":"A Grenada spice app depends on a new image-recognition library, and nobody knows if it runs fast enough on budget Android phones.","bin":0,"fb":"The biggest unknown is technical performance, so a feasibility prototype comes first."},{"t":"The tech is proven, but the team is unsure whether shoppers understand a new three-step checkout.","bin":1,"fb":"Testing whether a workflow makes sense, cheaply, is the job of a low-fidelity user prototype."},{"t":"The workflow tested well, and now the tourism board must be persuaded to fund the full app.","bin":2,"fb":"Communicating the proposed product to stakeholders needs a realistic high-fidelity user prototype."},{"t":"Everything is designed, but the team needs proof that the booking system holds up with real customers and payments.","bin":3,"fb":"Proving it works in the world with real data and traffic is a live-data prototype."},{"t":"An engineer must check whether a Barbados bus-tracking algorithm can update positions every five seconds.","bin":0,"fb":"Engineer plus algorithm plus performance means feasibility."},{"t":"Five commuters at the Basseterre ferry terminal are asked to \"buy a ticket\" using linked grey screens.","bin":1,"fb":"Linked grey screens that do not look real, testing the workflow: low-fidelity user."},{"t":"Investors want to feel the finished experience, with the real brand, photos and transitions.","bin":2,"fb":"A realistic simulation for investors is a high-fidelity user prototype."},{"t":"A bare-bones price board goes live for 30 vendors for one week, pulling real market prices.","bin":3,"fb":"A limited real release with live data is live-data."}],"win":"Strong reasoning. You chose each prototype by the biggest unanswered question: can it work, does the flow make sense, will people want it, or does it really work in the world?","title":"Which type of prototype? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Concept maps: brainstorming with a purpose","html":"<p>Before you draw a single screen, you must organise the <b>ideas</b> and the <b>structure</b>. For <b>Prototypes using design principles</b> (Module 2, Objective 3a), you should be able to <b>explain and create</b> four maps: a <b>concept map</b>, a <b>navigational chart</b>, a <b>site map</b> and a <b>hierarchy map</b>. We start with the concept map.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Concept map</b>: a visual organisation and representation of knowledge. It shows concepts and ideas and the relationships among them.</p>\n<p>Key phrase: <b>concept mapping is brainstorming with a purpose</b>. You start with an <b>overarching concept</b> and break it down into its smaller parts, using <b>arrows</b> and <b>linking words</b> to show how the ideas are connected. Concept maps are helpful in any field because they drive <b>creative and visual thinking</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Linking words</b> are the short verbs or phrases written on each arrow, such as \"includes\", \"needs\", \"leads to\" or \"is sold at\". They turn every pair of boxes into a sentence you can read aloud: \"Carnival <i>includes</i> J'ouvert\". Without linking words you only have a list of bubbles.</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 560 190\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Concept map: Carnival includes J'ouvert and the Grand Parade; Carnival needs safety tips; Carnival features local food, which is sold at roadside stalls.\" style=\"max-width:560px\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\">\n<rect x=\"220\" y=\"10\" width=\"120\" height=\"36\" rx=\"18\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"130\" width=\"120\" height=\"36\" rx=\"6\"/>\n<rect x=\"150\" y=\"130\" width=\"120\" height=\"36\" rx=\"6\"/>\n<rect x=\"290\" y=\"130\" width=\"120\" height=\"36\" rx=\"6\"/>\n<rect x=\"430\" y=\"130\" width=\"120\" height=\"36\" rx=\"6\"/>\n<path d=\"M240 46 L80 130 M270 46 L210 130 M300 46 L350 130 M320 46 L490 130\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n<text x=\"280\" y=\"33\" font-weight=\"bold\" font-size=\"13\">Carnival</text>\n<text x=\"70\" y=\"153\">J'ouvert</text>\n<text x=\"210\" y=\"153\">Grand Parade</text>\n<text x=\"350\" y=\"153\">Safety tips</text>\n<text x=\"490\" y=\"153\">Local food</text>\n<text x=\"135\" y=\"85\" font-style=\"italic\">includes</text>\n<text x=\"222\" y=\"100\" font-style=\"italic\">includes</text>\n<text x=\"350\" y=\"100\" font-style=\"italic\">needs</text>\n<text x=\"440\" y=\"85\" font-style=\"italic\">features</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media builds this map before planning a Trinidad and Tobago Carnival website. The groups she finds (events, safety, food) later become the main pages of the site. A concept map is for <b>thinking</b>, not for navigation.</p>\n<p><b>Making one in Lucidchart</b> (see the video in this unit): open a blank document or a concept map template, drag a shape onto the canvas for the main concept, add shapes for the smaller ideas, draw lines between them, then click a line and type the linking word on it. Share the link so your group can edit together.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A <b>mind map</b> branches out from one centre with no labels on the lines. A <b>concept map</b> puts <b>linking words</b> on every arrow and can link ideas across branches. If an exam answer has no linking words, it is not yet a concept map.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A concept map shows ideas and how they connect. Start with one main idea in the middle, add smaller ideas around it, and write a linking word on every arrow, such as \"includes\" or \"needs\". For a Carnival website you might write \"Carnival includes J'ouvert\". These groups of ideas later become the main pages of the site.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The linking words are what make a concept map an analytical tool rather than a decorative one. Each arrow forms a proposition you can test: \"Food stalls need permits\" may reveal a page you had forgotten. Concept maps can also link across branches, showing that \"Safety\" connects to both \"J'ouvert\" and \"Parking\", which a mind map cannot show. A typical error is drawing a concept map that looks like a site map; remember the concept map organises ideas, while the site map lists pages. In Paper 02, draw boxes, label every arrow, include at least one cross-link, and say how the clusters will become sections of the site.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the planning tool","intro":"<p>Malik and Maya are planning a website for the Morne Diablotin Hiking Club in Dominica. Match each planning tool to the job it does.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Concept map","r":"Brainstorming with a purpose: links ideas such as \"trails\", \"safety\" and \"membership\" with arrows and linking words"},{"l":"Navigational chart","r":"A flowchart that organises how visitors will be guided from Home to Trails to a trail page and back"},{"l":"Site map","r":"A table of contents of every page, for both users and search engines"},{"l":"Hierarchy map","r":"A tree-like diagram with Home at the top, breaking into categories and then sub-categories"},{"l":"User pathway","r":"Traces the exact steps a visitor takes to complete one task, such as joining a guided hike"},{"l":"Wireframe","r":"A grey-box layout of one page showing where the logo, menu and photos go"},{"l":"Mock-up","r":"A static, full-colour image of the final page, not yet clickable"}],"win":"Very good. You can name the right tool for each planning job: ideas, page list, flow, levels, layout or final look.","tutor":[{"h":"Ideas, pages or flow?","p":"<p>Linking words between ideas, a table of contents, or arrows that guide visitors? Each is a different map.</p>"},{"h":"Tree shape","p":"<p>Which map is described as tree-like, with the home page at the top?</p>"},{"h":"One page or many?","p":"<p>Wireframes and mock-ups show a single screen. Maps and charts show the whole site.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the planning tool (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Concept map","r":"Links ideas with arrows and linking words"},{"l":"Site map","r":"A table of contents of all the pages"},{"l":"Navigational chart","r":"Shows how visitors are guided between pages"},{"l":"Hierarchy map","r":"A tree with Home at the top"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>The Pic Paradis Hiking Club team keeps asking questions. Match each question to the planning tool that answers it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Which ideas belong together, and how are they related?","r":"Concept map"},{"l":"Can a visitor get back to Home from every page, and in how many clicks?","r":"Navigational chart"},{"l":"Which pages exist, and which should search engines index?","r":"Site map"},{"l":"Which pages sit at level 2 and which sit beneath Trails at level 3?","r":"Hierarchy map"},{"l":"What exact steps does a hiker take to book a guided hike?","r":"User pathway"},{"l":"Where on the Trails page do the logo, menu and photos go?","r":"Wireframe"},{"l":"Will the club approve the final colours, fonts and photos?","r":"Mock-up"}],"win":"Well matched. Choosing a tool by the question it answers is exactly how professionals plan, and it is how to justify a tool in Paper 02.","title":"Match the planning tool (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Site maps: HTML sitemaps and XML sitemaps","html":"<p>Here are two sides of the same idea.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Site map</b>: a file where you provide information about the pages, videos and other files on your site, and the relationships between them. Search engines like Google read this file to crawl your site more efficiently. A sitemap tells Google which pages and files you think are important in your site.</p>\n<p>A site map also provides valuable information about these files, for example <b>when the page was last updated</b> and any <b>alternate language versions</b> of the page. It is <b>a table of contents for both users and search engines</b> that helps them differentiate between your pages, better understand your site hierarchy, and easily navigate through your website.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>HTML sitemap</th><th>XML sitemap</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Built for</b></td><td>Human <b>visitors</b></td><td>Search engine <b>bots</b> (such as Googlebot) only</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Looks like</b></td><td>A simple, bulleted outline of the site navigation, with links that use <b>page titles as anchor text</b></td><td>A list of URLs written in a specific format: XML or text files marked up with tags</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Purpose</b></td><td>Lets users find all the information they are looking for by following its links</td><td>Guides bots to all the pages you want to be <b>indexed</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Example</b></td><td>The HTML sitemap on Apple.com</td><td>The XML sitemap on the Costco website</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Antigua example.</b> The Seaglass &amp; Silk boutique on Redcliffe Quay in St. John's has a \"Sitemap\" link in its footer. The HTML version a shopper sees:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Home</li>\n<li>Shop: Dresses, Swimwear, Jewellery, Sale</li>\n<li>Lookbook: Carnival Collection, Resort Wear</li>\n<li>About us, Store hours and directions, Returns policy, Contact</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The XML version that Maya submits to Google Search Console looks like this:</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;url&gt;\n  &lt;loc&gt;https://seaglass-silk.example/shop/swimwear&lt;/loc&gt;\n  &lt;lastmod&gt;2026-10-01&lt;/lastmod&gt;\n&lt;/url&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>The <b>&lt;loc&gt;</b> tag holds the page address and <b>&lt;lastmod&gt;</b> tells the bot when it was last updated. A shopper would never read this file; it exists so the swimwear page shows up when a cruise visitor at Heritage Quay searches \"swimwear St. John's\".</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 01 loves this trap: an XML sitemap is <b>not</b> for visitors. If the stem says \"bulleted links using page titles\" or \"helps visitors\", choose HTML. If it says \"search engine bots\", \"indexed\", \"Googlebot\" or \"tags\", choose XML. In a planning question, the site map you draw is a diagram of every page, usually as a hierarchy.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A site map lists the pages on a website. An HTML sitemap is a page of links that helps visitors find things, often linked in the footer. An XML sitemap is a coded file that helps search engines like Google find and index every page. A Redcliffe Quay boutique has both: one for shoppers, one for Googlebot.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>An XML sitemap does not force Google to rank a page; it only helps the crawler <b>discover</b> pages and see when they changed, through tags such as &lt;loc&gt; and &lt;lastmod&gt;. It is most valuable for new sites, large sites, and pages that few other pages link to. An HTML sitemap mainly serves people, but it also creates internal links that crawlers can follow. A common error is calling the XML file \"the site map visitors use\". In planning questions, note that the site map you <b>draw</b> is a diagram of every page, usually a hierarchy, which later guides both the navigation design and the XML file.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"HTML sitemap or XML sitemap?","intro":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is finishing the website for the Seaglass &amp; Silk boutique on Redcliffe Quay. Sort each item.</p>","main":"Does this describe an HTML sitemap or an XML sitemap?","bins":["HTML sitemap","XML sitemap"],"items":[{"t":"Like the sitemap page on Apple.com, styled to match the rest of the site.","bin":0,"fb":"The sitemap on Apple.com is a classic HTML sitemap: a readable page for visitors."},{"t":"A shopper who cannot find the Returns policy clicks \"Sitemap\" in the footer and finds it in the list.","bin":0,"fb":"Helping visitors navigate by following links is the job of an HTML sitemap."},{"t":"A file at /sitemap.xml full of url and loc tags.","bin":1,"fb":"A list of URLs marked up with tags is an XML sitemap."},{"t":"Maya submits it in Google Search Console so the new Carnival Collection pages are crawled.","bin":1,"fb":"Submitting to a search engine so pages are crawled and indexed is what an XML sitemap is for."},{"t":"Designed so users can find all the information they are looking for on the website.","bin":0,"fb":"Guiding visitors to everything on the site is the purpose of an HTML sitemap."},{"t":"Built exclusively for search engine bots, to guide them to every page you want indexed.","bin":1,"fb":"XML sitemaps are for bots, not people."},{"t":"A simple bulleted outline of every page, with links that use page titles as the anchor text.","bin":0,"fb":"A bulleted outline of linked page titles is exactly what an HTML sitemap is."},{"t":"Includes a lastmod tag so Googlebot knows the Sale page changed yesterday.","bin":1,"fb":"Last-updated information in tags makes the site more readable for Googlebot: XML."}],"win":"Well sorted. An HTML sitemap is a bulleted page of links for visitors; an XML sitemap is a tagged list of URLs for search engine bots.","tutor":[{"h":"Who reads it?","p":"<p>Is a person clicking links, or is a search engine bot reading tags?</p>"},{"h":"Clue words","p":"<p>\"Anchor text\", \"visitors\" and \"bulleted\" point one way. \"Tags\", \"indexed\" and \"Googlebot\" point the other.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"HTML or XML sitemap? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A bulleted list of page links for visitors.","bin":0,"fb":"For people: HTML."},{"t":"A tagged list of URLs for search engine bots.","bin":1,"fb":"For bots: XML."},{"t":"A \"Sitemap\" page a shopper clicks in the footer.","bin":0,"fb":"Visitors use it: HTML."},{"t":"A file submitted to Google so pages are indexed.","bin":1,"fb":"For indexing: XML."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder clues. Decide whether each describes an HTML sitemap or an XML sitemap.</p>","items":[{"t":"Contains &lt;loc&gt; and &lt;lastmod&gt; tags for each URL.","bin":1,"fb":"Tagged URLs with last-modified dates are XML."},{"t":"Helps a screen-reader user find the Returns policy by following plain text links.","bin":0,"fb":"A readable page of links for people is an HTML sitemap."},{"t":"Is referenced from the site's robots.txt file so crawlers can locate it.","bin":1,"fb":"Pointing crawlers to the sitemap file is an XML sitemap task."},{"t":"Is linked from the footer of every page and styled like the rest of the site.","bin":0,"fb":"A styled, linked page for visitors is HTML."},{"t":"Lists alternate language versions of each page so search engines can match them.","bin":1,"fb":"Language-version information for bots is carried in an XML sitemap."},{"t":"Uses page titles such as \"Swimwear\" and \"Carnival Collection\" as anchor text.","bin":0,"fb":"Page titles as clickable anchor text form an HTML sitemap."},{"t":"Is most useful for a large new site whose pages have few links pointing to them.","bin":1,"fb":"XML sitemaps help crawlers discover pages they might otherwise miss."},{"t":"Is uploaded to Google Search Console after the Carnival Collection launch.","bin":1,"fb":"Submitting to a search console is an XML sitemap task."},{"t":"Gives shoppers a bulleted outline of every section of the store.","bin":0,"fb":"A bulleted outline for shoppers is an HTML sitemap."}],"win":"Well analysed. Anything written for crawlers (tags, robots.txt, Search Console, language versions) is XML; anything written for people to read and click is HTML.","title":"HTML sitemap or XML sitemap? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Navigation charts: guiding visitors through your site","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Navigation chart</b> (navigation flowchart): organises how you are going to guide visitors through your material.</p>\n<p>To understand the chart, you need the definition of what it plans. <b>Website navigation</b> is a collection of user interface components that helps visitors find content and features on a site. These components take the form of <b>copy, link text, buttons and menus</b>. On a website, a <b>navigation menu</b> is an organised list of links to other web pages, usually internal pages. Navigation menus appear most commonly in <b>page headers or sidebars</b> across a website, allowing visitors to quickly access the most useful pages.</p>\n<p>A navigation chart shows <b>flow</b>, so arrows matter. It answers questions such as: Can a visitor get back to Home from every page? Is \"Reserve a table\" reachable in two clicks? Does the menu page link to the booking page? Navigation structures you should know:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Linear</b>: one page after another, like an online quiz or a checkout (Cart → Details → Payment → Confirmation).</li>\n<li><b>Hierarchical (tree)</b>: a home page branches into sections and sub-pages, like most business websites.</li>\n<li><b>Network (web)</b>: pages link freely to many others, like Wikipedia.</li>\n<li><b>Hybrid</b>: a hierarchy with some linear sequences inside it, which is what most real sites use.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Difference between a sitemap and a navigation chart</b>:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Sitemap</th><th>Navigation</th></tr>\n<tr><td>An <b>outline of all the pages and links</b> on your site</td><td>The <b>series of menus</b> users take to browse through your site and its content</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Comes first: it <b>informs how you structure</b> your navigation</td><td>Built from the sitemap: decides which pages go in the header, dropdowns, sidebar or footer</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Example: for the Lagoon Grill in Jolly Harbour, Maya's sitemap lists 14 pages. Her navigation chart shows that only Home, Menu, Reservations, Events and Contact sit in the main menu, with arrows from every menu page to Reservations, and the rest reached from the footer.</p>\n<p>A useful group activity is to design lo-fi and hi-fi prototypes and then rotate to critique each other's designs. A clear navigation chart makes that critique much easier because everyone can see the intended flow.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Two classic \"distinguish\" answers. Concept map versus navigation chart: \"A concept map organises <b>ideas</b> and their relationships, whereas a navigation chart shows how <b>visitors are guided</b> between pages.\" Sitemap versus navigation: \"A sitemap is an outline of <b>all the pages</b>, whereas navigation is the <b>series of menus</b> users take to browse them.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A navigation chart is a flowchart that shows how visitors move from page to page, so the arrows matter. It checks things like \"Can I get back to Home?\" and \"Can I book in two clicks?\" A site map comes first and lists every page; the navigation chart then decides which pages go in the menu, as for a Jolly Harbour restaurant.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Navigation structures reflect the <b>task</b>. Linear structures suit tasks with a fixed order, such as checkout, where skipping steps causes errors. Hierarchies suit browsing by category; networks suit exploring, as on Wikipedia. Most real sites are hybrids: a hierarchical menu with linear sequences inside. A navigation chart is valuable because it reveals dead ends and long routes before design begins, for example a Reservations page reachable only through Events. When you distinguish it from a site map, focus on purpose: the site map is an inventory of pages, while the navigation chart is a plan of movement. Draw arrows with labels such as \"Book\" so the flow is clear.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Five types of website navigation","html":"<p>There are five types of website navigation menu. Each suits a different amount of content and a different screen size.</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 125\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Five thumbnail wireframes: a horizontal bar across the top, a dropdown hanging below the bar, a phone with a three-line hamburger icon, a vertical sidebar of stacked links, and a footer band of links at the bottom.\" style=\"max-width:600px\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"10\" width=\"106\" height=\"80\" rx=\"4\"/><path d=\"M10 28 H116 M22 19 H38 M46 19 H62 M70 19 H86 M94 19 H108\"/>\n<rect x=\"128\" y=\"10\" width=\"106\" height=\"80\" rx=\"4\"/><path d=\"M128 28 H234 M140 19 H156 M164 19 H180 M188 19 H204\"/><rect x=\"160\" y=\"28\" width=\"40\" height=\"40\"/><path d=\"M166 38 H194 M166 48 H194 M166 58 H194\"/>\n<rect x=\"268\" y=\"10\" width=\"44\" height=\"80\" rx=\"6\"/><path d=\"M294 18 H306 M294 23 H306 M294 28 H306\"/>\n<rect x=\"346\" y=\"10\" width=\"106\" height=\"80\" rx=\"4\"/><path d=\"M376 10 V90 M354 22 H370 M354 32 H370 M354 42 H370 M354 52 H370\"/>\n<rect x=\"464\" y=\"10\" width=\"106\" height=\"80\" rx=\"4\"/><path d=\"M464 28 H570 M464 70 H570 M474 80 H490 M498 80 H514 M522 80 H538 M546 80 H562\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n<text x=\"63\" y=\"108\">1. Horizontal</text><text x=\"181\" y=\"108\">2. Dropdown</text><text x=\"290\" y=\"108\">3. Hamburger</text><text x=\"399\" y=\"108\">4. Sidebar</text><text x=\"517\" y=\"108\">5. Footer</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>What it is</th><th>When to use it</th><th>Caribbean example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1. Horizontal navigation bar</b></td><td>The most common type: the major pages listed side by side in the website header</td><td>A small site with about five to seven main pages</td><td>The Seaglass &amp; Silk boutique on Redcliffe Quay: Home, Shop, Lookbook, About, Contact</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2. Dropdown menu</b></td><td>The most important or general items sit in the top bar; the rest drop down beneath them</td><td>Content-rich sites with a complex information architecture (IA), where listing every link side by side would look cluttered or not fit</td><td>A Barbados Visitor Guide: \"Beaches\" drops down to Crane Beach, Carlisle Bay, Bathsheba</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3. Hamburger menu</b></td><td>Links collapse behind a three-line icon; tapping it opens a vertical drop-down or horizontal pop-out</td><td>Mobile web design and apps, where screen space (real estate) is limited</td><td>The Lagoon Grill in Jolly Harbour: on phones, Menu, Reservations and Events hide behind the icon</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>4. Vertical sidebar</b></td><td>Items stacked on top of each other in a sidebar</td><td>When you want longer link text and more top-level options; eye-catching, so it suits agencies and creative businesses</td><td>The Island Pulse Media portfolio: \"Branding for Caribbean start-ups\", \"Carnival campaigns\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>5. Footer menu</b></td><td>Links at the bottom of the page, usually paired with and expanding on a horizontal bar</td><td>Extra links visitors may still need after scrolling: policies, hours, careers, social media</td><td>Lagoon Grill's footer: Opening hours, Private events, WhatsApp us, Privacy policy</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Real sites usually <b>combine</b> types. The Lagoon Grill site shows a horizontal bar on a laptop, collapses it into a hamburger on a phone, and repeats key links in the footer.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When asked to \"justify\" a navigation type, link it to the content and the device: \"A hamburger menu, because most of the grill's visitors book on their phones from the beach and screen space is limited.\" Naming the type alone earns little.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>There are five kinds of website menu. A horizontal bar lists main pages across the top. A dropdown hides extra links under a heading. A hamburger menu folds links behind a three-line icon on phones. A vertical sidebar stacks links down the side. A footer menu sits at the bottom. A Jolly Harbour restaurant site might use a hamburger on phones.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Each type has a usability cost. Hamburger menus save space but hide options, so important actions such as \"Reserve\" should stay visible as a separate button. Dropdowns can be awkward on touch screens and hard for keyboard users if they open only on hover. Horizontal bars break down beyond about seven items. That is why responsive sites <b>combine</b> types: a horizontal bar on desktop, a hamburger on mobile, and a footer repeating key links. A Grade One justification links the type to three things: the amount of content, the device most visitors use, and the most important task, such as booking a table from a phone on the beach.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Which navigation type?","intro":"<p>Island Pulse Media has five website jobs on Zara's board. Match each type of website navigation to the job it suits best.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Horizontal navigation bar","r":"The Seaglass & Silk boutique on Redcliffe Quay has five main pages and wants them side by side in the header"},{"l":"Dropdown menu","r":"A Barbados Visitor Guide has 40 pages grouped under Beaches, Food, Shopping and Events"},{"l":"Hamburger menu","r":"On phones, the Lagoon Grill in Jolly Harbour must hide its menu links behind a three-line icon to save space"},{"l":"Vertical sidebar menu","r":"A creative agency wants an eye-catching menu with long link labels and many top-level options"},{"l":"Footer menu","r":"Opening hours, Privacy policy, Careers and a WhatsApp link at the bottom of every page, expanding on the header links"}],"win":"Well matched. You chose each navigation type by the amount of content, the screen size and where visitors look.","tutor":[{"h":"How many links?","p":"<p>A handful of pages fit side by side. Dozens of pages need a way to tuck the extra links under a few main items.</p>"},{"h":"Which device?","p":"<p>Limited screen space on a phone calls for one particular icon.</p>"},{"h":"Where on the page?","p":"<p>Header, side or bottom? One type lives where visitors end up after scrolling.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which navigation type? (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Horizontal bar","r":"A few main pages side by side in the header"},{"l":"Dropdown","r":"Many pages tucked under a few top-level items"},{"l":"Hamburger","r":"A three-line icon that saves space on phones"},{"l":"Footer","r":"Extra links at the bottom of the page"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Seven harder jobs, including two navigation structures. Match each type to the job it suits BEST.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Horizontal navigation bar","r":"A Nevis guesthouse with exactly five main pages that visitors mostly view on laptops when planning trips"},{"l":"Dropdown menu","r":"A Jamaica tourism portal with 60 pages grouped under Parishes, Attractions, Food and Events"},{"l":"Hamburger menu","r":"A Bonaire dive shop whose analytics show 85% of visitors arrive on phones, so header space is scarce"},{"l":"Vertical sidebar menu","r":"An online CAPE revision site needing many long topic labels visible at once"},{"l":"Footer menu","r":"Careers, Privacy policy, Accessibility statement and WhatsApp links that visitors look for after scrolling"},{"l":"Linear structure","r":"A visa application where users must go Details → Documents → Payment → Confirmation in order"},{"l":"Network structure","r":"A Caribbean heritage wiki where every article links freely to many related articles"}],"win":"Excellent. You matched each menu type and structure to the amount of content, the device and the task, which is the reasoning examiners reward.","title":"Which navigation type? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Hierarchy maps and user pathways","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Hierarchy map</b>: hierarchy diagrams are commonly used for website building, as they classify information by starting with <b>broad categories</b> and narrowing down to <b>more detailed levels</b>. A hierarchical navigation structure is <b>tree-like</b> in nature. The <b>home page is at the top</b>. Underneath, it breaks into categories, which can be further broken into sub-categories. There will usually be <b>a link back to the home page on every page</b> below it.</p>\n<p><b>Creating hierarchy maps: two steps</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Plan and structure</b>: decide what pages you will use, what features will be on each one, and how you want your audience directed through your website.</li>\n<li><b>Organise the pages</b>: think about the number of pages in your website package and what you would use each of them for, then place them in levels.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Here is a hierarchy map for Jolly Harbour Eco Tours.</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 640 250\" width=\"100%\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Hierarchy map: Home links to About, Tours, Gallery, Book Now and Contact. Tours links to Lagoon Kayak, Sunset Cruise and Shirley Heights Hike.\" style=\"max-width:640px\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\">\n<rect x=\"270\" y=\"10\" width=\"100\" height=\"36\" rx=\"6\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"100\" width=\"100\" height=\"36\" rx=\"6\"/>\n<rect x=\"140\" y=\"100\" width=\"100\" height=\"36\" rx=\"6\"/>\n<rect x=\"270\" y=\"100\" width=\"100\" height=\"36\" rx=\"6\"/>\n<rect x=\"400\" y=\"100\" width=\"100\" height=\"36\" rx=\"6\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 3\" stroke-width=\"3\"/>\n<rect x=\"530\" y=\"100\" width=\"100\" height=\"36\" rx=\"6\"/>\n<rect x=\"30\" y=\"195\" width=\"100\" height=\"36\" rx=\"6\"/>\n<rect x=\"140\" y=\"195\" width=\"100\" height=\"36\" rx=\"6\"/>\n<rect x=\"250\" y=\"195\" width=\"100\" height=\"36\" rx=\"6\"/>\n<path d=\"M320 46 V73 M60 73 H580 M60 73 V100 M190 73 V100 M320 73 V100 M450 73 V100 M580 73 V100\"/>\n<path d=\"M190 136 V165 M80 165 H300 M80 165 V195 M190 165 V195 M300 165 V195\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"13\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n<text x=\"320\" y=\"33\" font-weight=\"bold\">Home</text>\n<text x=\"60\" y=\"123\">About</text>\n<text x=\"190\" y=\"123\">Tours</text>\n<text x=\"320\" y=\"123\">Gallery</text>\n<text x=\"450\" y=\"123\" font-weight=\"bold\">Book Now</text>\n<text x=\"580\" y=\"123\">Contact</text>\n<text x=\"80\" y=\"218\">Lagoon Kayak</text>\n<text x=\"190\" y=\"218\">Sunset Cruise</text>\n<text x=\"300\" y=\"218\">Hike</text>\n<text x=\"600\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"11\">Level 1</text>\n<text x=\"600\" y=\"160\" font-size=\"11\">Level 2</text>\n<text x=\"420\" y=\"218\" font-size=\"11\">Level 3</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<p>Reading the map: Home is the <b>parent</b> of five level 2 categories; Tours is the parent of three <b>child</b> pages. The dashed Book Now box is the site's main goal. Every page carries the logo linking back to Home. Keep sites <b>shallow and wide</b>: most users should reach any page in three clicks or fewer.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>User pathway</b> (user flow): the sequence of pages and actions a particular user takes to complete one goal.</p>\n<p>A cruise passenger at the St. John's cruise pier with four hours to spare might follow: Google search → Home → Tours → Lagoon Kayak → Book Now → choose a time → pay → confirmation with a WhatsApp contact. Mapping this shows where to add shortcuts, such as a \"Book this tour\" button on every tour page.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In Paper 02, draw a hierarchy map with boxes for pages, straight lines for parent and child links, Home at the top and the levels labelled. Add a key if you use arrows or dashed boxes for a user pathway.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A hierarchy map is a tree diagram of a website. Home is at the top, main sections are underneath, and smaller pages sit below those. A user pathway shows the steps one visitor takes to finish a task. A cruise visitor might go Home → Tours → Lagoon Kayak → Book Now → pay → confirmation.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Good hierarchies are <b>shallow and wide</b>: most pages should be reachable within three clicks, because every extra level loses visitors. But too wide is also a problem, since a level with twenty items becomes hard to scan; grouping into five to seven categories is a common balance. User pathways test the hierarchy against real goals. If the most common task, booking, needs five steps, add shortcuts such as a \"Book this tour\" button on every tour page. A frequent exam error is drawing a hierarchy with no labelled levels or mixing pathway arrows into it without a key. Label levels, and draw the pathway separately or in a different line style.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Put the user pathway in order","intro":"<p>A visitor from Antigua wants to buy a ticket to an Emancipation Day concert on the Barbados Live Events website. Put the pathway in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Finds the site through a Google search for \"Emancipation Day concert Barbados\"."},{"t":"Lands on the Home page and sees the featured event banner."},{"t":"Taps the banner to open the Event Details page (date, venue, line-up)."},{"t":"Taps \"Buy tickets\" and chooses the number of tickets."},{"t":"Enters name and payment details on the Checkout page."},{"t":"Sees the Confirmation page with an e-ticket and QR code."}],"win":"Correct. A user pathway follows one goal from entry point to completion, and every step is a page or action you must design.","tutor":[{"h":"Entry point","p":"<p>Where does a user begin before they even reach the site?</p>"},{"h":"Goal at the end","p":"<p>The pathway ends when the goal is complete, not when the user pays.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"User pathway (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the ticket-buying steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Lands on the Home page."},{"t":"Opens the Event Details page."},{"t":"Pays on the Checkout page."},{"t":"Sees the Confirmation page."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>A longer pathway with more decisions. A visitor from Antigua buys two concert tickets on the Barbados Live Events site. Put all nine steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Searches Google for \"Emancipation Day concert Barbados\" and clicks the result."},{"t":"Lands on the Home page and taps \"Events\" in the hamburger menu."},{"t":"Filters the Events list by date to show only the concert weekend."},{"t":"Opens the Event Details page to check the venue and line-up."},{"t":"Taps \"Buy tickets\" and selects two tickets."},{"t":"Chooses \"Continue as guest\" instead of creating an account."},{"t":"Enters name, email and payment details on the Checkout page."},{"t":"Sees the Confirmation page with the order number."},{"t":"Receives an email with two e-tickets and QR codes."}],"win":"Correct. Each step depends on the one before. Mapping the full pathway also shows design choices, such as offering guest checkout to keep the path short.","title":"Put the user pathway in order (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Design principles 1: rule of thirds, composition, balance, alignment","html":"<p>A prototype is not only about where things go; it must also look clear and attractive. Apply the <b>rule of thirds</b> and <b>composition</b>, together with <b>balance, alignment, hierarchy, contrast, spacing and visual flow</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Rule of thirds</b>: divide a frame into a 3 by 3 grid with two horizontal and two vertical lines. Place key elements (a face, a headline, a call-to-action button) on the lines or at the four intersections (\"power points\"), not dead centre. A hero image of Grand Anse Beach works better with the sailing boat on the upper-left intersection and the headline on the right third.</li>\n<li><b>Composition</b>: the overall arrangement of elements in the layout so they work together and guide the eye. Good composition has a clear focal point, groups related items and leaves out clutter.</li>\n<li><b>Balance</b>: the distribution of visual weight. <b>Symmetrical</b> balance mirrors elements on both sides (formal, calm, good for a bank such as a credit union). <b>Asymmetrical</b> balance uses different elements of equal weight (a large photo on the left balanced by a block of text and a bright button on the right), which feels modern and dynamic.</li>\n<li><b>Alignment</b>: lining elements up along common edges or a centre line so the page looks organised. Web designers use a <b>grid</b> (often 12 columns on desktop, 4 on mobile). Mixing left, centre and right alignment on one page looks messy.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Principle</th><th>Common mistake</th><th>Fix</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Rule of thirds</td><td>Every element centred, making a static page</td><td>Move the focal point to an intersection</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Balance</td><td>All heavy images on the left, empty right side</td><td>Add a text block or button with matching weight</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Alignment</td><td>Captions slightly off the edge of their photos</td><td>Snap all elements to the same grid column</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> If a question says \"apply design principles\", do not just list them. Name the principle, state <b>where</b> it is used on your layout and <b>why</b>: \"The Book Now button sits on the lower-right thirds intersection, where the eye ends its scan, so users notice it.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Design principles make a page clear and attractive. The rule of thirds places key things on a 3 by 3 grid instead of dead centre. Composition is how everything is arranged. Balance spreads the visual weight evenly. Alignment lines things up neatly on a grid. A Grand Anse Beach photo looks better with the boat on a grid line than in the middle.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>These principles work because of how the eye behaves: people look first at focal points, then follow edges and lines. The rule of thirds creates tension and movement that centred layouts lack, though centring still suits a single, formal message such as a memorial notice. Balance is about <b>visual weight</b>, not equal numbers of items: one bright button can balance a large grey photo. Alignment is the cheapest improvement, since snapping elements to a 12-column desktop or 4-column mobile grid instantly looks professional. In exam answers, apply each principle to a named element and give the user benefit, rather than defining the principle in the abstract.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Which design principle?","intro":"<p>Zara is reviewing student interns' layouts for the Gouyave Seafood Festival. Identify the principle each comment is about.</p>","items":[{"text":"\"Your headline, photo and button are all stacked in the exact centre. Try putting the chef's face on one of the grid intersections.\"","options":["contrast","rule of thirds","spacing"],"answer":1,"fb":"Placing the focal point on a grid intersection, instead of the centre, is the rule of thirds."},{"text":"\"The left column has three big photos and the right is empty. The page feels as if it will tip over.\"","options":["hierarchy","alignment","balance"],"answer":2,"fb":"Uneven visual weight is a balance problem. Add weight to the right or redistribute the photos."},{"text":"\"Your captions start at slightly different points from the edges of the images.\"","options":["visual flow","alignment","contrast"],"answer":1,"fb":"Elements that do not share a common edge are misaligned. Snap them to a grid."},{"text":"\"Pale yellow text on a white background is almost impossible to read in the sun.\"","options":["balance","composition","contrast"],"answer":2,"fb":"Readability depends on contrast between text and background, especially on phones outdoors."},{"text":"\"The ticket price is the same size as the small print. Make the most important information biggest.\"","options":["rule of thirds","hierarchy","balance"],"answer":1,"fb":"Visual hierarchy uses size, weight and position to show what matters most."},{"text":"\"Everything is jammed together. Give each section some breathing room.\"","options":["contrast","spacing","alignment"],"answer":1,"fb":"White space (spacing) separates groups and makes a page easier to scan."}],"win":"Excellent. You diagnosed layout problems by naming the exact design principle, which is the vocabulary examiners reward.","tutor":[{"h":"Position words","p":"<p>\"Intersection\" or \"grid lines\" point to one principle. \"Edges lining up\" point to another.</p>"},{"h":"Weight and importance","p":"<p>Weight spread across the page is balance. Importance shown by size is hierarchy.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which design principle? (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Placing the main photo on a grid intersection uses...","options":["the rule of thirds","contrast"],"answer":0,"fb":"Grid intersections are the rule of thirds."},{"text":"Light grey text on white is hard to read. This is a problem of...","options":["balance","contrast"],"answer":1,"fb":"Text needs strong contrast with its background."},{"text":"Making the headline biggest shows...","options":["hierarchy","alignment"],"answer":0,"fb":"Size shows importance: visual hierarchy."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder layout reviews for the Gouyave Seafood Festival. Choose the principle that is MOST at fault, or the BEST fix.</p>","items":[{"text":"\"The chef in the hero photo faces off the left edge of the screen, and visitors' eyes follow her gaze away from the Buy Tickets button on the right.\" Which principle is broken?","options":["Spacing","Alignment","Visual flow","Balance"],"answer":2,"fb":"The direction people face in photos leads the eye. Here it leads away from the goal, so visual flow is broken."},{"text":"\"The page has a big photo on the left and a block of text and a bright coral button on the right, and it feels stable.\" Which principle is being applied well?","options":["The F-pattern","Asymmetrical balance","Symmetrical balance","Contrast only"],"answer":1,"fb":"Different elements of equal visual weight on each side is asymmetrical balance."},{"text":"\"The vendor list is a long, text-heavy page, but you designed it for a Z-pattern scan.\" What is the BEST fix?","options":["Add more photos at random","Centre every line","Remove all headings","Put key headings down the left side for an F-pattern scan"],"answer":3,"fb":"Text-heavy pages are scanned in an F-pattern, so key headings and first words should sit down the left side."},{"text":"\"Your spacing values are 7 px, 13 px, 22 px and 31 px.\" What is the BEST fix?","options":["Use consistent multiples of 8 px such as 8, 16, 24 and 32","Make all gaps 1 px","Use a different value for every section","Remove all spacing"],"answer":0,"fb":"A consistent spacing scale, often multiples of 8 px, makes groups clear and the page feel ordered."},{"text":"\"Grey #999999 body text on white looks stylish.\" Why is this a problem for festival visitors?","options":["It breaks the rule of thirds","Grey is never allowed on websites","It is too dark","Its contrast is below the 4.5:1 WCAG guidance for body text, so it is hard to read in sunlight"],"answer":3,"fb":"Mid-grey on white falls below the 4.5:1 contrast ratio recommended for normal text, which matters for phones read outdoors."},{"text":"\"The festival logo, the date and the ticket price are all the same size and weight.\" Which principle should be applied first?","options":["Alignment","Hierarchy","Spacing","Balance"],"answer":1,"fb":"When nothing stands out, visual hierarchy is needed: make the most important information, the date and the price, larger or bolder."}],"win":"Excellent. You diagnosed subtle problems and chose fixes, which is the \"apply and justify\" skill Paper 02 rewards.","title":"Which design principle? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Design principles 2: hierarchy, contrast, spacing, visual flow","html":"<ul>\n<li><b>Hierarchy</b>: showing what is most important first through size, weight, colour and position. A typical web page has a clear H1 headline, smaller subheadings, then body text. On an electricity company's outage notice, the affected areas and expected restore time should be the largest text, not the company slogan.</li>\n<li><b>Contrast</b>: difference between elements (light and dark, large and small, bold and regular, warm and cool colours). Contrast creates emphasis and makes text readable. Accessibility guidance (WCAG) recommends a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1 for normal body text. Caribbean users often read phones outdoors in bright sun, so strong contrast matters even more.</li>\n<li><b>Spacing (white space)</b>: the empty space around and between elements. It is not wasted space. It groups related items (the <b>proximity</b> idea), separates sections and makes a page feel calm and premium. Use consistent spacing values, such as multiples of 8 pixels (8, 16, 24, 32).</li>\n<li><b>Visual flow</b>: the path the eye follows across a layout. Designers use size, lines, arrows, the direction people face in photos, and colour to lead the eye towards the goal. Two common scanning patterns: the <b>Z-pattern</b> for simple pages with little text (logo top-left → menu top-right → diagonal across the hero → call to action bottom-right) and the <b>F-pattern</b> for text-heavy pages such as a newspaper's news article (users scan across the top, then down the left side reading headings).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These principles work together. On the Island Pulse Media home page, Maya makes the headline the largest element (hierarchy), places it on the top-left third (rule of thirds), uses white text on a deep teal band (contrast), leaves 32 pixels of space between sections (spacing), and puts a bright coral \"Start a project\" button where the Z-pattern ends (visual flow).</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Question to ask</th><th>Principle</th></tr>\n<tr><td>What does the user see first, second, third?</td><td>Hierarchy, visual flow</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Can I read it on a phone at the beach?</td><td>Contrast</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Can I tell which items belong together?</td><td>Spacing, alignment</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Does the page feel stable?</td><td>Balance</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In the SBA, annotate your Figma or paper prototype with small callouts naming the principles. It is direct evidence for the \"Product\" criteria and shows the examiner your design was deliberate, not accidental.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Hierarchy makes the most important thing biggest. Contrast makes text easy to read, such as dark text on a light background. Spacing, or white space, gives each part room to breathe. Visual flow guides the eye towards a goal like a \"Book now\" button. An electricity outage notice should show the affected areas in the largest text.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>These principles depend on each other. Hierarchy needs contrast, because size alone fails if the large headline is pale. Spacing creates grouping, so it supports hierarchy by showing which details belong to which heading. Visual flow then strings the elements together in the order the user needs them. Remember the WCAG contrast guidance: at least 4.5:1 for normal text and 3:1 for large text. Outdoor phone use in the Caribbean makes this practical, not theoretical. The Z-pattern suits simple landing pages, while the F-pattern suits text-heavy pages. A Grade One answer explains how two or more principles work together on one element, not just one at a time.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the layout problems","intro":"<p>Jerome drafted a home page wireframe for the Roseau Market Crafts shop. Read his design notes.</p>","q":"Select every note that BREAKS a design principle.","lines":["The logo, headline, photo, prices and button are all centred and the same size.","The hero photo of a craft stall is placed so the vendor's face sits on the upper-right thirds intersection.","Body text is light grey (#CCCCCC) on a white background.","All product cards snap to a 4-column grid with equal 24 px gaps.","There is no space between the product grid, the reviews and the footer.","The \"Shop now\" button is placed where the eye finishes its Z-pattern scan.","Captions are left-aligned on some cards, centred on others and right-aligned on the rest.","The headline is the largest text on the page."],"correct":[0,2,4,6],"win":"Well spotted. Same-size centred items break hierarchy, pale grey on white breaks contrast, no gaps break spacing, and mixed text alignment breaks alignment.","miss":"Not quite. Check each note against hierarchy, contrast, spacing and alignment. The notes about thirds, the grid, the Z-pattern and the headline describe good practice.","tutor":[{"h":"Four suspects","p":"<p>Look for problems with importance (size), readability (colour), breathing room and consistent edges.</p>"},{"h":"Good notes exist too","p":"<p>Half of these notes describe good practice. Do not select those.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the layout problems (Foundation)","q":"Select the notes that break a design principle.","lines":["Light grey text on white.","The headline is the biggest text.","No space between sections.","Cards line up on a grid."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Low contrast and no spacing are both problems.","miss":"Look for low contrast and missing white space."},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Jerome's revised notes for the Roseau Market Crafts home page are trickier. Some describe good practice.</p>","q":"Select every note that BREAKS a design principle.","lines":["The headline is the same size and weight as the body text.","Body text is very dark grey (#222222) on white.","Product cards snap to a 12-column grid on desktop and a 4-column grid on mobile.","The vendor in the hero photo faces off the edge of the page, away from the \"Shop now\" button.","Gaps between sections are 8, 16, 24 and 32 px, used consistently.","The logo sits at the bottom right and the \"Shop now\" button at the top left, the reverse of the Z-pattern.","Pale yellow captions sit on a white background.","A large photo on the left is balanced by a text block and a bright button on the right.","Related prices sit far from their product photos, closer to the next product.","On the long \"Our vendors\" article, key headings run down the left side for F-pattern scanning."],"correct":[0,3,5,6,8],"win":"Well judged. Equal sizes break hierarchy, a gaze away from the goal and reversed Z-pattern placement break visual flow, pale yellow on white breaks contrast, and prices placed far from their products break spacing (proximity).","miss":"Look again. The dark grey text, the consistent grids, the 8-pixel spacing scale, the asymmetrical balance and the F-pattern headings are all good practice. Problems involve hierarchy, flow, contrast and proximity.","title":"Spot the layout problems (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Cloud-based prototyping and authoring tools","html":"<p>Cloud-based tools run in a web browser (or a light app), save work online and let several people edit or comment at the same time. Key cloud-based authoring tools include Figma, Canva, Adobe Express, Google Workspace, WeVideo, CapCut, Adobe XD, Balsamiq and Marvel. Widely used wireframing tools include <b>Balsamiq, Figma, Sketch, Adobe XD, Axure RP, InVision and Moqups</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Tool</th><th>Best for</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Limitations</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Figma</b></td><td>Wireframes, mockups and clickable prototypes: collaborative design and prototyping in one platform</td><td>Real-time collaboration, components, auto layout, free education plan for verified students and teachers</td><td>Needs a stable connection; many features to learn</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Canva</b></td><td>Quick mock-ups, social posts, simple web pages</td><td>Templates, drag-and-drop, brand kits</td><td>Limited true interactivity; designs can look template-like</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Adobe Express</b></td><td>Fast branded graphics, simple pages, short videos</td><td>Adobe fonts and stock, easy to use</td><td>Not built for detailed app prototyping</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Adobe XD</b></td><td>UI design and prototypes</td><td>Familiar to Adobe users, repeat grids</td><td>Adobe has stopped active development, so most studios have moved to Figma; check availability</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Balsamiq</b></td><td>Low-fidelity wireframes</td><td>Sketch style keeps focus on structure; very quick</td><td>Not for hi-fi visuals</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Sketch</b></td><td>Wireframes and hi-fi mockups</td><td>Strong design systems and component libraries, named in the handout as an industry-standard mockup tool</td><td>macOS only; paid licence</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Axure RP</b></td><td>Advanced prototypes</td><td>Conditional logic and dynamic content (for example, a form that changes based on answers)</td><td>Steeper learning curve; paid</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>InVision</b></td><td>User testing and stakeholder collaboration on prototypes</td><td>Comments and review on clickable screens</td><td>InVision closed its design collaboration services at the end of 2024, so you may meet it in older resources but not use it now</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Moqups</b></td><td>All-in-one wireframing, mockups and prototyping in the browser</td><td>One tool for all three stages; simple to learn</td><td>Free plan is limited in size</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Marvel</b></td><td>Turning sketches or screens into clickable prototypes, user testing</td><td>Simple linking, can upload photos of paper sketches</td><td>Fewer design features than Figma</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Google Workspace</b></td><td>Shared planning: Slides for storyboards, Drawings for site maps, Docs for briefs</td><td>Free, familiar, easy sharing and comments</td><td>Not a dedicated design tool</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>WeVideo, CapCut</b></td><td>Video prototypes, animatics and short promos</td><td>Browser or phone editing, templates</td><td>Free tiers add limits or watermarks</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Activity: cloud-based tools for each map.</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Map</th><th>Cloud-based tools to try</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Concept map</td><td>Lucidchart (concept map templates, see the video in this unit), Coggle, MindMeister, Miro, Canva whiteboards</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Navigational chart</td><td>draw.io (diagrams.net, free and usable without an account), Lucidchart, Miro, FigJam</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Site map</td><td>Octopus.do and Slickplan (visual sitemap builders); the XML-Sitemaps.com generator creates an XML sitemap from a live site</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hierarchy map</td><td>Lucidchart or draw.io org-chart shapes, FigJam, Slickplan, Octopus.do</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>When choosing a tool, consider the <b>fidelity</b> you need, the <b>team</b> (do people need to edit together?), <b>cost</b>, <b>device</b> (Chromebook, laptop, phone) and <b>Internet reliability</b>. After Hurricane Irma in 2017, many St. Maarten businesses learned that cloud files survived when local hard drives did not; but cloud tools also stop working when the connection drops, so download backup copies.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For \"justify your choice of tool\" questions, link a tool feature to a project need: \"Figma, because our group of three can edit the same file at once and link screens into a clickable prototype for user testing.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Cloud-based tools run in a web browser and save work online, so a group can edit together from different homes. Figma is used for wireframes, mockups and clickable prototypes; Canva and Adobe Express for quick designs; Balsamiq for rough wireframes. A group in Dominica could build a site map in draw.io and a prototype in Figma.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Tool choice is a set of trade-offs: fidelity needed, collaboration, cost, device and connectivity. Sketch is industry-standard but runs only on macOS, so it fails a team using Chromebooks. Adobe XD is no longer actively developed and InVision has closed its design services, so recommending them now shows outdated knowledge. Cloud tools carry a Caribbean-specific risk: they protect files from local damage, as many businesses learned after Hurricane Irma, but stop working when the connection drops. A strong answer recognises both sides and suggests a mitigation, such as exporting backup copies, and justifies one tool by linking a specific feature to a specific project need.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Choose a cloud tool for each map","intro":"<p>This activity asks you to list cloud-based tools for a concept map, a navigational chart, a site map and a hierarchy map. Help the Island Pulse Media team pick the best tool for each job.</p>","items":[{"text":"Keisha wants to build a concept map for a Carnival website, with linking words on every arrow, starting from a ready-made concept map template.","options":["XML-Sitemaps.com generator","Lucidchart","Octopus.do"],"answer":1,"fb":"Lucidchart has concept map templates and lets you type linking words on each line, as in the video in this unit."},{"text":"Jerome needs a free flowchart of the checkout navigation for the Lagoon Grill in Jolly Harbour, on a school computer where he cannot create an account.","options":["draw.io (diagrams.net)","Slickplan","MindMeister"],"answer":0,"fb":"draw.io is free, has flowchart shapes and arrows, and can be used without signing up."},{"text":"Maya wants to lay out every page of the Seaglass & Silk boutique as a visual site map of page blocks before any design starts.","options":["Coggle","CapCut","Octopus.do"],"answer":2,"fb":"Octopus.do is a visual sitemap builder made for planning pages as blocks."},{"text":"The boutique site is live and Maya now needs an XML sitemap to submit to Google Search Console.","options":["FigJam","Canva whiteboards","XML-Sitemaps.com generator"],"answer":2,"fb":"The XML-Sitemaps.com generator crawls a live site and produces the XML file for search engines."},{"text":"The team is already designing in Figma and wants a shared whiteboard to drag sticky notes into a hierarchy map, Home at the top.","options":["XML-Sitemaps.com generator","Audacity","FigJam"],"answer":2,"fb":"FigJam is Figma's whiteboard, so the hierarchy map sits right beside the design files."},{"text":"A CAPE group already makes its SBA posters in Canva and wants a free infinite board to brainstorm ideas with a purpose.","options":["Slickplan","Google Search Console","Canva whiteboards"],"answer":2,"fb":"Canva whiteboards suit brainstorming and concept maps, and the group already knows Canva."}],"win":"Excellent. You matched cloud tools to each map: Lucidchart, Coggle and Canva whiteboards for concept maps, draw.io for navigation charts, Octopus.do and Slickplan for site maps, and FigJam for hierarchy maps.","tutor":[{"h":"What is being made?","p":"<p>Ideas with linking words, a flowchart, a page plan, a file for search engines, or a tree? Each points to a different kind of tool.</p>"},{"h":"Read the constraints","p":"<p>No account, already using Figma, already using Canva: these clues decide between tools that could all draw boxes.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Choose a cloud tool (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Making a concept map from a template:","options":["XML-Sitemaps.com generator","Lucidchart"],"answer":1,"fb":"Lucidchart has concept map templates."},{"text":"Creating an XML sitemap for Google:","options":["Coggle","XML-Sitemaps.com generator"],"answer":1,"fb":"It generates the XML file from a live site."},{"text":"Drawing a free navigation flowchart:","options":["CapCut","draw.io (diagrams.net)"],"answer":1,"fb":"draw.io has free flowchart shapes."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Now the constraints are tighter. Choose the BEST tool for each situation and be ready to justify it.</p>","items":[{"text":"A group uses school Chromebooks and must co-edit a clickable app prototype in real time.","options":["Sketch","Adobe XD","Figma","Audacity"],"answer":2,"fb":"Figma runs in the browser with real-time co-editing and prototyping. Sketch is macOS only, and Adobe XD is no longer actively developed."},{"text":"A designer needs a prototype whose form shows different screens depending on the answer chosen, using conditional logic.","options":["Canva","Coggle","XML-Sitemaps.com generator","Axure RP"],"answer":3,"fb":"Axure RP is known for conditional logic and dynamic content in advanced prototypes."},{"text":"A teacher finds a 2019 tutorial recommending InVision for prototype reviews. What should the class use instead?","options":["Marvel or Figma, because InVision closed its design services at the end of 2024","InVision, as in the tutorial","An XML sitemap","Notepad"],"answer":0,"fb":"InVision's design collaboration services closed, so a current tool such as Marvel or Figma is the right choice."},{"text":"A team must plan the shots and timing of a 20-second animated app promo before filming.","options":["Slickplan","WeVideo or CapCut, to make a quick animatic","Google Search Console","Lucidchart concept map"],"answer":1,"fb":"Video tools such as WeVideo or CapCut can build a rough animatic to test timing and shots."},{"text":"A small business on a weak connection wants a visual sitemap now and a backup in case the Internet goes down.","options":["Octopus.do or Slickplan, and export a PDF or image copy regularly","CapCut","A cloud tool only, with no export","An XML file written by hand only"],"answer":0,"fb":"Visual sitemap builders do the job, and regular exports protect the work when the connection fails."},{"text":"A designer wants deliberately rough, hand-drawn-style wireframes so the client focuses on structure, not looks.","options":["Adobe Express","Canva brand kit","Balsamiq","Sketch hi-fi mockups"],"answer":2,"fb":"Balsamiq's sketch style keeps attention on layout and structure."}],"win":"Strong evaluation. You weighed devices, features, current availability and connectivity, which is how to justify a tool choice for marks.","title":"Choose a cloud tool for each map (Challenge)"}},{"type":"mcq","title":"Choosing a prototyping tool","q":"A group of three CAPE students at Mahoe Grove High School must produce a clickable mobile app prototype for their SBA. They work from different homes, want to edit the same file at the same time, and need reusable buttons that update everywhere. Which tool BEST meets all of these needs?","options":[{"t":"Balsamiq","fb":"Balsamiq is excellent for quick low-fidelity wireframes, but the group needs a detailed, clickable prototype with reusable components."},{"t":"Figma","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Figma offers real-time multi-user editing in the cloud, components that update every instance, and prototype links for clickable testing."},{"t":"CapCut","fb":"CapCut is a video editor. It is not designed for building clickable app screens with reusable components."},{"t":"Paper and black markers","fb":"Paper prototypes are great for early ideas but cannot be edited by three people in different homes at once."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Three needs","p":"<p>Collaboration from different places, reusable elements, clickable screens. Which tool has all three?</p>"},{"h":"Fidelity","p":"<p>Some options are only for lo-fi work or for a different media type.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Choosing a prototyping tool (Foundation)","q":"Which tool lets a group edit the same clickable app prototype together online?","options":[{"t":"A paper sketch","fb":"Paper cannot be edited by people in different places."},{"t":"Audacity","fb":"Audacity is an audio editor."},{"t":"Figma","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Figma is cloud-based, collaborative and supports prototype links."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A CAPE group of four must build a clickable prototype. Two members use Chromebooks, one uses a Windows laptop and one a Mac. They need real-time co-editing, reusable components and free access. One member argues for Sketch \"because it is an industry-standard mockup tool\". Which response BEST evaluates this argument?","options":[{"t":"Agree: industry-standard tools are always the best choice for any team.","fb":"Industry use is only one factor. A tool that half the team cannot run fails the project's needs."},{"t":"Sketch is industry-standard, but it is macOS only and paid, so three members could not edit; Figma runs in any browser, supports real-time co-editing and components, and has a free education plan.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. This acknowledges the argument, tests it against every requirement and recommends the tool that meets them all."},{"t":"Choose Adobe XD instead, because it has repeat grids.","fb":"Adobe XD is no longer actively developed, does not run on Chromebooks, and repeat grids do not solve the collaboration need."},{"t":"Use Balsamiq, because rough wireframes are always better than detailed prototypes.","fb":"Balsamiq suits early lo-fi work, but the brief asks for a clickable prototype with reusable components."}],"title":"Choosing a prototyping tool (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Step by step: a clickable wireframe in Figma","html":"<p>Maya builds a two-screen mobile wireframe for the Kingston Bus Times app: a <b>Home</b> screen and a <b>Route</b> screen. Follow the same steps for your SBA.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Create a file.</b> Sign in at figma.com, choose <b>New design file</b> and rename it with a clear name, e.g. <i>BusTimes_Wireframe_v1</i>.</li>\n<li><b>Add frames.</b> Press <b>F</b> (Frame tool) and pick a preset from the right panel, such as an iPhone or Android size. Name it \"Home\". Duplicate it (Ctrl+D or Cmd+D) and rename the copy \"Route\". A <b>frame</b> is a screen or container.</li>\n<li><b>Block out the layout.</b> Use <b>R</b> (rectangle) and <b>T</b> (text) to add grey boxes for the header, a search bar, a list of routes and a bottom navigation bar. Keep it greyscale: this is a wireframe.</li>\n<li><b>Use auto layout.</b> Select a group of items, for example one route row with an icon, the route name and a time, and press <b>Shift+A</b>. Set direction (horizontal or vertical), <b>gap</b> (space between items, e.g. 16) and <b>padding</b>. Now items reflow automatically when text gets longer, which keeps spacing consistent.</li>\n<li><b>Make components.</b> Select the finished route row and choose <b>Create component</b> (Ctrl+Alt+K or Cmd+Option+K). Copies are <b>instances</b>. Edit the main component and every instance updates. Do the same for buttons and the navigation bar.</li>\n<li><b>Add prototype links.</b> Open the <b>Prototype</b> tab. Select the \"Route 1\" row on Home, drag the small circle (connection) onto the Route frame and set <b>On tap → Navigate to → Route</b>, with an animation such as <b>Instant</b> or <b>Smart animate</b>. Add a back arrow on Route that navigates back to Home. Set Home as the <b>starting point</b> (flow).</li>\n<li><b>Test.</b> Click <b>Present</b> (the play button) and tap through as a user would. Fix dead ends.</li>\n<li><b>Share and gather feedback.</b> Click <b>Share</b>, set the link to \"can view\" or allow comments, and send it to testers. Export key frames as PNG for your SBA report.</li>\n</ol>\n<pre><code>File name:   BusTimes_Wireframe_v1\nFrames:      Home, Route\nComponents:  RouteRow, NavBar, PrimaryButton\nLinks:       RouteRow (On tap) &gt; Route\n             BackArrow (On tap) &gt; Home</code></pre>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 02 has no Internet, so Figma itself will not be available in the exam. You may be asked to <b>describe</b> these steps or draw the wireframe on paper. Learn the vocabulary: frame, auto layout, component, instance, prototype link, starting point.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>In Figma you make a clickable wireframe step by step. Create a file, add phone-sized frames for each screen, draw grey boxes, use auto layout to keep spacing tidy, and turn repeated parts into components. Then link screens in the Prototype tab, press Present to test, and share the link. A bus-times app for Kingston needs only two linked screens.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The order of steps reflects good practice: structure, then consistency, then interaction, then testing. Components and auto layout save more time than they first appear to: change the main route row once and every instance updates, which keeps a ten-screen prototype consistent. A common mistake is linking screens before the layout is settled, so links break when frames are rebuilt. Another is forgetting the starting point, which makes Present open on the wrong screen. Because Paper 02 is sat without Internet access, learn the vocabulary (frame, auto layout, component, instance, prototype link, starting point) and be ready to describe the workflow or draw the wireframe on paper.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Order the Figma workflow","intro":"<p>Jerome is building a wireframe for the Dominica Trail Guide app. Put his Figma steps in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Create a new design file and name it clearly."},{"t":"Add phone-sized frames for each screen and name them."},{"t":"Block out grey boxes and text for the layout."},{"t":"Apply auto layout to rows and set gap and padding."},{"t":"Turn repeated rows and buttons into components."},{"t":"Add prototype links between frames in the Prototype tab."},{"t":"Present the prototype and click through to test it."},{"t":"Share the link with testers and collect comments."}],"win":"Correct. Structure first (frames and layout), then consistency (auto layout and components), then interaction (links), then testing and feedback.","tutor":[{"h":"Containers first","p":"<p>You cannot place anything until the screens (frames) exist.</p>"},{"h":"Links come late","p":"<p>You can only link screens once the buttons you will tap exist.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Order the Figma workflow (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the main stages in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Add frames for each screen."},{"t":"Block out the layout."},{"t":"Add prototype links."},{"t":"Present and test."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Eleven detailed steps for the Dominica Trail Guide wireframe. Put them in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Create a new design file and rename it TrailGuide_Wireframe_v1."},{"t":"Press F, choose a phone preset and name the frame \"Home\"; duplicate it and name the copy \"Trail\"."},{"t":"Use R and T to block out grey boxes for the header, search bar and trail list."},{"t":"Select one trail row and press Shift+A to apply auto layout, setting a 16 px gap."},{"t":"Turn the finished trail row into a component so every copy becomes an instance."},{"t":"In the Prototype tab, drag a connection from the trail row to the Trail frame: On tap → Navigate to."},{"t":"Add a back arrow on the Trail frame that navigates back to Home."},{"t":"Set Home as the starting point of the flow."},{"t":"Click Present and tap through as a user would, fixing any dead ends."},{"t":"Share the link with testers, allowing comments."},{"t":"Export the key frames as PNG images for the project report."}],"win":"Correct. File and frames, then layout, auto layout and components, then links and the starting point, then testing, sharing and exporting evidence.","title":"Order the Figma workflow (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"When to use each, and designing with purpose","html":"<p>In summary: strategic selection of design deliverables optimises your team's efficiency and project outcomes. <b>Not every project requires all three artifacts</b>: choose based on your goals, timeline and resources.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Wireframes</th><th>Mockups</th><th>Prototypes</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>When</b></td><td>Early-stage concept validation and structural alignment</td><td>Visual design refinement and stakeholder approval</td><td>Interactive testing and user validation before development</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Purpose</b></td><td>Establish information architecture, content hierarchy and basic user flows without visual distractions</td><td>Define the look and feel with complete visual fidelity while remaining static</td><td>Simulate real user interactions to identify UX issues and validate workflows</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Best for</b></td><td>Quick ideation, stakeholder alignment, exploring many concepts rapidly</td><td>Brand consistency, client presentations, developer handoff specifications</td><td>Usability testing, investor demos, complex interaction patterns</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Designing with purpose: build what users truly need</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Start simple, iterate fast</b>: begin with low-fidelity wireframes to validate concepts quickly. Add detail only when you have confirmed you are solving the right problem. Speed early prevents expensive mistakes later.</li>\n<li><b>Involve users early and often</b>: feedback at every stage, from wireframe testing to prototype validation, means you build for real needs, not assumptions. The earlier you learn, the less it costs to adapt.</li>\n<li><b>Choose tools strategically</b>: match deliverables to project goals and resources. A simple website refresh might only need mockups, while a complex application benefits from full prototyping. Work smarter, not harder.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Two Caribbean examples. Treasure Beach Guest House only wants new colours and photos on its existing site, so Zara skips wireframes and presents two <b>mockups</b>. The power-company outage reporting app has many screens, maps and forms, so Jerome goes through all three stages and runs a <b>prototype</b> test with residents before Maya codes anything.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Justify\" questions on this topic want the <b>when</b> plus the <b>purpose</b> for the scenario: \"A mockup, because the client must approve the brand colours before developer handoff.\" Quote a clue from the stimulus each time.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Not every project needs all three design steps. Use wireframes early to agree the structure, mockups to agree the look, and prototypes to test how people use it. A guesthouse that only wants new colours and photos may need just mockups, while a big outage-reporting app needs all three. Start simple, involve users and choose tools wisely.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Choosing deliverables is a <b>value-for-effort</b> judgement. Skipping wireframes is sensible when the structure already works and only the look changes; skipping prototypes is risky whenever there is a new workflow, many screens or forms, because untested flows are expensive to fix after coding. \"Involve users early\" also means choosing the right users: testing a booking app only with the design team misses problems that real residents or cruise visitors would find. In \"justify\" questions, quote a clue from the scenario (\"only wants new colours\", \"many forms\") and connect it to the purpose of the artifact you choose.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"When to use each design artifact","intro":"<p>Island Pulse Media has several jobs this month. Choose the best artifact for each stage or need.</p>","items":[{"text":"Curaçao Art Trail team has just agreed the idea and needs to settle which pages exist and how they connect.","options":["Mockups","Wireframes","Prototypes"],"answer":1,"fb":"Early-stage concept validation and structural alignment is when to use wireframes."},{"text":"A hotel client must approve the brand colours and fonts before the developer receives handoff specifications.","options":["Prototypes","Mockups","Wireframes"],"answer":1,"fb":"Visual design refinement, stakeholder approval and developer handoff specifications are mockup jobs."},{"text":"An investor wants to tap through a ferry-booking app on a phone before funding it.","options":["Mockups","Prototypes","Wireframes"],"answer":1,"fb":"Investor demos and interactive testing are what prototypes are best for."},{"text":"The team wants to explore five different layout ideas in one afternoon.","options":["Prototypes","Wireframes","Mockups"],"answer":1,"fb":"Exploring multiple concepts rapidly is a wireframe strength: they are quick and cheap."},{"text":"A small restaurant only wants a fresh look for its existing website. Following \"choose tools strategically\", the team should mainly produce...","options":["A full interactive prototype","Nothing until coding starts","Mockups"],"answer":2,"fb":"A simple refresh might only need mockups. A complex app benefits from full prototyping."},{"text":"Which principle says you should add detail only after confirming you are solving the right problem?","options":["Engage stakeholders deeply","Choose tools strategically","Start simple, iterate fast"],"answer":2,"fb":"Start simple with low-fidelity wireframes and iterate fast before adding detail."}],"win":"Excellent. You can choose wireframes, mockups or prototypes by stage and purpose, and explain the three ideas of designing with purpose.","tutor":[{"h":"Stage clue","p":"<p>Early structure, visual approval, or interactive testing? Each points to one artifact.</p>"},{"h":"Not every project needs all three","p":"<p>Match the deliverable to the size of the job.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"When to use each (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Agreeing the page structure early:","options":["Wireframes","Prototypes"],"answer":0,"fb":"Structure first: wireframes."},{"text":"Client approval of colours and fonts:","options":["Wireframes","Mockups"],"answer":1,"fb":"Look and feel: mockups."},{"text":"Users tap through to test the flow:","options":["Mockups","Prototypes"],"answer":1,"fb":"Interactive testing: prototypes."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder judgements. Choose the BEST deliverable or decision for each situation.</p>","items":[{"text":"A Tobago guesthouse's site works well, but the owner wants a new colour scheme and fresh photos. What should the team mainly produce?","options":["A feasibility prototype","A full set of wireframes, mockups and prototypes","An XML sitemap only","Two or three mockup variations of the key pages"],"answer":3,"fb":"The structure is not changing, so mockups showing visual options are enough. Building everything would waste time."},{"text":"A new government permit app has 14 screens, forms and maps. Which plan BEST manages risk?","options":["Wireframes, then mockups, then a prototype tested with residents before coding","Go straight to coding to save time","A single paper sketch","Mockups only"],"answer":0,"fb":"Complex, form-heavy apps benefit from every stage, and testing with real users before coding prevents costly rework."},{"text":"The team has three different layout ideas for a festival site and one afternoon to choose. What should they make?","options":["Three live-data prototypes","Quick wireframes of each idea","Three polished mockups","A finished website"],"answer":1,"fb":"Wireframes are fast and cheap, ideal for exploring several concepts quickly."},{"text":"A client approved the mockups, but nobody has checked whether visitors can complete a booking. What is missing?","options":["A prototype tested with real users","More colour options","A concept map","A footer menu"],"answer":0,"fb":"Mockups are static. Only an interactive prototype in user testing shows whether the booking flow works."},{"text":"A designer wants to test the prototype only with colleagues at the agency. What is the main weakness?","options":["Colleagues are too slow","It ignores \"involve users early and often\": colleagues know the design and miss problems real users would find","Prototypes should never be tested","Colleagues cannot use Figma"],"answer":1,"fb":"Testing with real users from the target audience, with fresh eyes, is the point of user validation."},{"text":"Which statement BEST explains \"start simple, iterate fast\"?","options":["Never change a design once it is made","Always make the most detailed design first","Validate the concept cheaply with low-fidelity work and add detail only once you know you are solving the right problem","Skip planning and start coding"],"answer":2,"fb":"Early low-fidelity validation prevents expensive mistakes later."}],"win":"Excellent judgement. You chose deliverables by stage, risk and purpose, and recognised when testing was missing or flawed.","title":"When to use each design artifact (Challenge)"}},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Prototyping for a real client","intro":"<p>You are a junior designer at Island Pulse Media. Belize Reef Divers company wants a booking website. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The owner sends a long list of ideas: dive packages, a blog, photo galleries, safety rules, staff bios, prices and a booking form. Zara asks you where to begin.</p>","choices":[{"t":"Open Figma straight away and design a beautiful full-colour home page.","to":"bad1","fb":"Jumping to hi-fi before agreeing on structure wastes time when the content changes."},{"t":"Make a concept map of the ideas, then group them into a site map with clear levels.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. You organise ideas first, then structure the pages, before any visual design."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The site map is approved. Next you must show the owner the layout of the home and booking pages. What do you produce?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A final-colour mock-up of every page so the owner can pick colours first.","to":"bad2","fb":"Colour decisions distract from structure. Fix layout and flow first."},{"t":"Quick low-fidelity wireframes on paper or in Balsamiq, then test them with two real customers.","to":"n3","fb":"Yes. Cheap lo-fi testing catches layout problems early."},{"t":"Skip testing; the owner already likes the site map.","to":"bad3","fb":"A site map is not a layout. Users may still get lost."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Testers could not find the booking button on the home page. You move to a high-fidelity Figma prototype. How do you fix the problem?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Give the \"Book a dive\" button strong contrast, place it where the Z-pattern ends, and add it to every package page as a component.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Contrast, visual flow and components solve the problem consistently."},{"t":"Make the button the same colour as the background so the design looks calm.","to":"bad4","fb":"Low contrast hides the main call to action. Calm is fine, invisible is not."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> The owner approves the prototype and bookings rise after launch. A Grade One answer would explain the process: concept map and site map for structure, lo-fi testing for layout, then hi-fi with design principles (contrast, visual flow) and components for consistency.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Wasted effort.</b> The owner changes the page list the next day and your beautiful page no longer fits. Try again: plan the structure before the visuals.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Stuck on colours.</b> Two meetings are spent choosing shades of blue while nobody notices the booking form is buried. Try again: test layout with lo-fi first.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Users get lost.</b> At launch, visitors cannot find prices. A short lo-fi test would have shown this. Try again and test early.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Hidden button.</b> Bookings stay low because the call to action disappears. Try again and use contrast and hierarchy for the main goal.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Structure before style","p":"<p>Ideas → pages → layout → visuals. Which step comes first?</p>"},{"h":"Test cheaply","p":"<p>Low-fidelity testing finds problems when they are cheapest to fix.</p>"},{"h":"Make the goal obvious","p":"<p>The main action needs contrast and a place in the visual flow.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Prototyping for a client (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: a dive company wants a booking website.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Where do you start?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Plan the pages with a site map, then sketch lo-fi wireframes.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. Structure and layout before visuals."},{"t":"Design a full-colour home page immediately.","to":"bad","fb":"Too early. The structure is not agreed yet."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You planned structure first and tested cheaply.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Hi-fi work is wasted if the structure changes. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A harder client. Belize Reef Divers wants a booking website and app in six weeks with a small budget. Its customers are mostly overseas divers on phones, and the booking system must check boat capacity in real time. Plan the design process.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The owner sends a long list of ideas. Zara asks how you will structure them.</p>","choices":[{"t":"Make only a site map, because a navigation chart repeats the same information.","to":"ok1","fb":"A site map lists pages, but it does not show how visitors move. The booking flow still needs planning."},{"t":"Make a concept map of the ideas, then a hierarchy-style site map with labelled levels and a navigation chart for the booking flow.","to":"n2","fb":"Excellent. Ideas, pages and movement are each planned with the right tool before any layout."},{"t":"Design a full-colour home page in Figma first to impress the owner.","to":"bad1","fb":"Polishing before the structure is agreed wastes time when the page list changes."}]},"ok1":{"text":"<p>The owner asks: \"Can a diver book from a package page in two taps?\" Your site map cannot answer. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Tell the owner it will be fine.","to":"bad3","fb":"Assumptions are what testing is meant to replace."},{"t":"Add a navigation chart and a user pathway for booking, then shorten the route.","to":"n2","fb":"Good recovery. The navigation chart and pathway show the movement the site map cannot."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The real-time capacity check uses a new booking service, and nobody knows if it responds fast enough on weak mobile signals at sea. What is the next step?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Ask Jerome to build a feasibility prototype of the capacity check before detailed design.","to":"n3","fb":"Correct. The biggest risk is technical, so test it first, cheaply."},{"t":"Build the polished high-fidelity prototype first and worry about the technology later.","to":"bad2","fb":"If the technology fails, all that polish must be redone. Test the riskiest unknown first."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The service works. Now the booking layout must be agreed. What do you produce?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Lo-fi wireframes linked into a low-fidelity user prototype, tested with five real divers on phones.","to":"n4","fb":"Yes. Cheap, interactive testing with the real audience finds workflow problems early."},{"t":"Final mockups of every page so the owner can choose colours.","to":"bad3","fb":"Colour choices distract from the workflow, and static mockups cannot show whether booking works."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Testers could not find the \"Book a dive\" button on phones, and two missed the price. How do you fix this in the high-fidelity version?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Add more photos of reefs above the button.","to":"ok2","fb":"Photos may attract divers, but pushing the button lower makes it even harder to find."},{"t":"Make \"Book a dive\" a high-contrast component on every package page where the scan ends, and raise the price in the visual hierarchy.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Contrast, visual flow, hierarchy and components fix the problem consistently across pages."},{"t":"Hide the button in the hamburger menu to keep the design clean.","to":"bad4","fb":"Hiding the main call to action makes the problem worse."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Launched on time.</b> Bookings from overseas divers rise, and the capacity check works at sea. A Grade One answer explains why each tool and prototype type was chosen: plan structure and movement first, test the riskiest technology early, test the workflow cheaply with real users, then apply design principles consistently.</p>","end":"good"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Pretty but slow.</b> The site looks stunning, but bookings are below target because the button is still hard to find. Try again and use hierarchy and contrast for the main goal.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Wasted effort.</b> The owner changes the page list and the polished page must be rebuilt. Try again: structure before visuals.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Costly rework.</b> The booking service times out at sea, and weeks of polished screens must change. Try again: test technical risk first with a feasibility prototype.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Problems found too late.</b> At launch, divers cannot complete bookings on their phones. Try again: test the workflow early with real users.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Hidden goal.</b> Bookings fall further. Try again: the main call to action must stay visible.</p>","end":"bad"}},"title":"Prototyping for a real client (Challenge)"}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Plan a prototype using design principles","genre":"Structured response: prototyping and design principles (Paper 02 style)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Front Street Heritage Walk is a non-profit in Philipsburg that offers free walking tours of historic buildings such as the Courthouse. It wants a small mobile-friendly website with pages for Home, About, Tour Stops (with three stop pages), Events and Contact, plus a way to register for a tour.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a response for the board of the Front Street Heritage Walk:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Distinguish</b> between a wireframe, a mockup and a prototype, and say which you would make first and why. (4 marks)</li><li><b>Describe</b> a hierarchy map for the website, naming the levels and one user pathway for registering for a tour, and <b>justify</b> one type of website navigation for phone users. (4 marks)</li><li><b>Explain</b> how you would apply THREE design principles to the home page layout. (6 marks)</li></ol>","min":220,"max":420,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> A wireframe is ... A mockup ... whereas a prototype ... I would make the ... first because ...<br><b>(b)</b> Level 1 is ... Level 2 contains ... Level 3 ... A user who wants to register would ... → ... → ... On phones I would use a ... menu because ...<br><b>(c)</b> First, I would apply ... by placing ... so that ... Second, ... Third, ...</p>","checklist":["I distinguished wireframe, mockup and prototype on the same features (fidelity, focus, interactivity).","I defined a prototype as an incomplete version taken into user testing, and justified which artifact comes first (change it early, when it is cheap).","I named the levels of the site map and placed the three stop pages correctly.","I described a user pathway step by step with arrows or sequence words.","I justified one navigation type (horizontal bar, dropdown, hamburger, sidebar or footer) for phone users.","I named three design principles and explained where and why each is used.","I linked my answer to the heritage walk and its users."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Wireframes, mockups and prototypes","marks":4,"look":["wirefram","mock.?up","prototyp","low.?fidelity|lo-fi|paper|sketch|rough","grey|placeholder|box|structure|layout","clickab|interactiv|link|simulat","colour|color|font|brand|static|look and feel","first|early|cheap|quick|iterat","incomplete|user test|testing|tester","feedback|fresh eyes|buy-in|stakeholder"],"min":3,"strong":"You distinguished the three artifacts clearly, used the definition of a prototype and justified starting with a wireframe. Next step: name a tool for each.","weak":"Use the design journey: a wireframe is a low-fidelity skeleton of layout and structure; a mockup is a high-fidelity but static design showing colours, fonts and images; a prototype is an incomplete, clickable version taken into user testing. Say you start simple with a wireframe to test structure cheaply."},{"criterion":"Hierarchy map, user pathway and navigation","marks":4,"look":["level|parent|child|hierarch|tree","home","tour stop|stop","register|regist|sign up|book","pathway|flow|→|then|next","hamburger|dropdown|drop-down|horizontal|sidebar|footer"],"min":3,"strong":"Your hierarchy map, pathway and navigation choice are clear and correctly structured.","weak":"Draw it as a tree with Home at the top: put Home at level 1, the main pages (About, Tour Stops, Events, Register, Contact) at level 2 and the three stop pages at level 3 under Tour Stops. Then trace a pathway such as Home → Events → Tour details → Register → Confirmation. Then justify a navigation type, such as a hamburger menu because phone screens have limited space."},{"criterion":"Applying design principles","marks":6,"look":["third","balance|symmetr","align|grid","hierarch|size|largest","contrast","spacing|white space|flow|z-pattern|f-pattern"],"min":3,"strong":"You applied principles to specific elements with reasons. That is what earns the higher marks.","weak":"For each principle, say what element, where it goes, and why it helps the user: \"The Register button uses high contrast so visitors see it in bright sunlight.\""}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> A <b>wireframe</b> is the skeleton of the design: a low-fidelity blueprint, sketched on paper or made in Balsamiq, that uses grey boxes and placeholders to show layout, structure and user flow without real colours or fonts. A <b>mockup</b> is a high-fidelity but static design, made in Figma or Sketch, that shows the final colours, typography, icons and real photos of the Courthouse so the board can approve the look and feel. A <b>prototype</b> is an incomplete version of the product taken into user testing: it includes the fundamental features, such as a working Register button, but leaves out the final polish, so testers can tap through the booking flow and their fresh eyes reveal problems. I would make the wireframe first, labelled \"Low-Fidelity Wireframe\", because it is quick and cheap to change: following \"start simple, iterate fast\", the board can agree on structure and the position of the Register button before time is spent on colours and interaction. Changing the design early, when it is cheap, costs far less than changing the finished site.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> The hierarchy map is tree-like, with the Home page at the top as Level 1. Level 2 contains the five main pages: About, Tour Stops, Events, Register and Contact. Level 3 contains the three individual stop pages (for example the Courthouse, Wathey Square and the Old Street area), which are children of Tour Stops. One user pathway is: a cruise visitor lands on Home → taps Events → opens the next tour date → taps Register → fills in name and group size → sees a Confirmation page with the meeting point. A \"Register\" button on every stop page keeps this pathway short, and every page links back to Home through the logo. For phone users I would use a <b>hamburger menu</b>, because most visitors will open the site on their phones while walking and screen space is limited; a <b>footer menu</b> would repeat Contact and Register at the bottom of each page.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> First, I would apply the <b>rule of thirds</b> to the hero photo of the Courthouse, placing the cupola on the upper-right intersection and the headline on the left third so the layout feels dynamic rather than static. Second, I would use <b>visual hierarchy</b>: the headline \"Walk Philipsburg's history\" would be the largest text, the tour time would be medium, and details smaller, so visitors understand the offer in seconds. Third, I would use strong <b>contrast</b> for the Register button, white text on a deep red, placed where the Z-pattern scan ends at the bottom right, so it stands out even when visitors read their phones outdoors in bright sun. Consistent 24-pixel spacing between sections would also group related information and keep the small screen uncluttered.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Part (c) carries the most marks. Each principle should earn two marks: one for naming and placing it, one for explaining the benefit to the user. Avoid simply listing \"balance, contrast, alignment\" with no element or reason.</p>",
 "easy": {"bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: prototyping and design principles (8 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part. Use the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>State</b> what a wireframe is and what a prototype is. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>List</b> the pages that go at Level 1, Level 2 and Level 3 of the website. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> a menu type for phone users and give one reason. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li><b>Choose</b> ONE design principle and say how you would use it on the Register button. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":70,"max":200,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> A wireframe is ... A prototype is ...<br><b>(b)</b> Level 1: ... Level 2: ... Level 3: ...<br><b>(c)</b> I would use a ... menu because ...<br><b>(d)</b> I would use ... by making the Register button ... so that ...</p>","checklist":["I said a wireframe is a simple layout with grey boxes.","I said a prototype can be clicked and tested.","I put Home at Level 1.","I put the three stop pages at Level 3 under Tour Stops.","I named a menu type with a reason.","I applied one principle to the Register button."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Wireframe and prototype","marks":2,"look":["wirefram.{0,80}(layout|box|grey|gray|structure|plan)","prototyp.{0,80}(click|test|interactiv|tap)"],"min":2,"strong":"Both terms explained.","weak":"A wireframe is a simple grey-box layout; a prototype is a version people can click and test."},{"criterion":"Levels of the site","marks":3,"look":["level 1|home","about|events|contact|tour stops","stop|courthouse|level 3"],"min":2,"strong":"Levels correctly listed.","weak":"Level 1: Home. Level 2: About, Tour Stops, Events, Contact, Register. Level 3: the three stop pages."},{"criterion":"Menu for phones","marks":1,"look":["hamburger|dropdown|footer|horizontal|sidebar"],"min":1,"strong":"Menu type named.","weak":"Name a menu type, such as a hamburger menu, which saves space on small screens."},{"criterion":"Design principle applied","marks":2,"look":["contrast|hierarch|third|spacing|balance|align|flow","register|button"],"min":2,"strong":"A principle applied to the button.","weak":"Name a principle and apply it to the button, e.g. high contrast so visitors see it in the sun."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> A wireframe is a simple plan of a page layout that uses grey boxes instead of real pictures. A prototype is an early version of the site that people can click and test.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Level 1: Home. Level 2: About, Tour Stops, Events, Register and Contact. Level 3: the three stop pages, such as the Courthouse, under Tour Stops.</p><p><b>(c)</b> I would use a hamburger menu because phone screens are small.</p><p><b>(d)</b> I would use contrast by making the Register button deep red with white text so that visitors can see it clearly in bright sunlight.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>For part (b), write the levels as a list or draw a small tree. Put each stop page under Tour Stops. For part (d), always name the element (the Register button) and the reason, not just the principle.</p>","title":"Plan a prototype using design principles (Foundation)"},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Structured response: prototyping and design principles, Grade One extension (20 marks)","prompt":"<p>The board now also wants volunteers to log in and update tour dates. Write a response that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Evaluates</b> the role of wireframes, mockups and prototypes in this project, recommending which stages to use and in what order. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li><b>Designs</b> a hierarchy map with labelled levels (including the volunteer area) and traces TWO user pathways: a visitor registering for a tour and a volunteer updating a tour date. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li><b>Justifies</b> a combination of navigation types for phone and desktop users. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explains</b> how FOUR design principles would work together on the home page, linking each to the heritage walk's users. <i>(7 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":380,"max":650,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Wireframes ... whereas mockups ... Prototypes ... For this project I recommend ... first because ... However, ...<br><b>(b)</b> Level 1: ... Level 2: ... Level 3: ... Pathway 1: ... → ... Pathway 2: ... → ...<br><b>(c)</b> On phones, ... because ... On desktop, ... A footer ...<br><b>(d)</b> First, ... Second, ... These work together because ... Third, ... Fourth, ...</p>","checklist":["I evaluated all three artifacts, recommended an order and gave a reason linked to the project.","My hierarchy map labels three levels and includes a volunteer area.","I traced two complete pathways with arrows.","I justified a combination of navigation types for phones and desktop.","I explained four principles on specific elements and showed how they work together.","I linked every point to the heritage walk's visitors or volunteers."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Evaluation of design stages","marks":5,"look":["wirefram","mock.?up","prototyp","first|order|then|before","cheap|early|test|risk|cost","however|but|although|whereas"],"min":5,"strong":"A balanced evaluation with a justified sequence.","weak":"Explain what each stage is for, recommend an order (wireframe, mockup, prototype) and justify it with cost and risk."},{"criterion":"Hierarchy map and two pathways","marks":5,"look":["level 1|level one","level 2|level two","level 3|level three","volunteer|log ?in|admin|dashboard","register","→|->|then"],"min":5,"strong":"Clear levels and two complete pathways.","weak":"Label Levels 1 to 3, add a volunteer login area and trace two pathways step by step."},{"criterion":"Navigation combination","marks":3,"look":["hamburger","horizontal|dropdown|drop-down","footer","phone|mobile|small screen|desktop|laptop"],"min":3,"strong":"A justified combination for each device.","weak":"Combine a hamburger menu on phones, a horizontal bar on desktop and a footer for secondary links, with reasons."},{"criterion":"Four principles working together","marks":7,"look":["hierarch","contrast","third","spacing|white space","flow|z-pattern|f-pattern","balance|align","together|combin|support|work with"],"min":5,"strong":"Four principles applied to elements, with their combined effect explained.","weak":"For each principle, name the element, where it goes and why; then show how two or more principles support each other."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Wireframes define structure cheaply: grey boxes let the board agree where the Register button and tour stops go without being distracted by colour. Mockups add high-fidelity colours, fonts and real photos of the Courthouse, which helps the board approve the look, whereas they are static and cannot show whether visitors can complete a registration. Prototypes are incomplete, clickable versions taken into user testing, so they reveal workflow problems such as a hidden button. For this project I recommend all three, in that order: wireframes first because changes are cheapest early, then mockups for sign-off, then a prototype tested with real visitors and volunteers before coding. However, the volunteer area is a new workflow, so it should be prototyped at low fidelity early rather than waiting until the end, because it carries the most risk.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> Level 1: Home. Level 2: About, Tour Stops, Events, Register, Contact and Volunteer Login. Level 3: the three stop pages (Courthouse, Wathey Square and Old Street) under Tour Stops, and a Volunteer Dashboard with an Edit Tour Dates page under Volunteer Login. Pathway 1: a visitor searches Google → Home → Events → next tour → Register → enters name and group size → Confirmation. Pathway 2: a volunteer taps the footer link → Volunteer Login → Dashboard → Edit Tour Dates → changes the date → Save → the Events page updates.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> On phones I would use a hamburger menu, because screen space is limited and most visitors browse while walking, but I would keep a visible Register button outside it because registration is the main goal. On desktop, a horizontal navigation bar can show the six main pages side by side, with a dropdown under Tour Stops for the three stops. A footer menu would hold Volunteer Login, Contact and the privacy policy, which visitors rarely need in the main menu.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> First, visual hierarchy: the headline \"Walk Philipsburg's history\" is the largest text, the next tour date is medium and details are smaller, so visitors grasp the offer at once. Second, contrast: the Register button uses white text on deep red, giving a high contrast ratio that remains readable outdoors. These work together because a large headline still fails if it is pale, so hierarchy depends on contrast. Third, the rule of thirds places the Courthouse cupola on the upper-right intersection, with the headline on the left third, creating a dynamic focal point. Fourth, spacing: consistent 24-pixel gaps separate the tour, events and volunteer sections, grouping related information, while visual flow leads the eye along a Z-pattern from the logo to the headline and down to the Register button where the scan ends.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>For part (d), the extra marks come from showing how principles interact. After explaining two principles, add a sentence such as \"These work together because ...\" For part (b), check that each pathway starts where a real user would start and ends with a confirmation.</p>","title":"Plan a prototype using design principles (Challenge)"}}]},{"id":"M2U4","title":"Content development for online publishing","objectives":["Explain what makes digital content effective (useful, usable and purposeful) and describe the seven-stage content cycle from planning to measuring and maintaining.","Define and research content scope (topic, purpose, audience, depth, format, channels and constraints) using audience research, keyword research, competitor review and a content audit, with accurate, relevant and credible sources.","Prepare a creative brief that includes the target group, a persona, a content checklist and tone of voice.","Apply criteria for web-friendly content: writing for scanners, the inverted pyramid, front-loading, short paragraphs, scannable headings, direct address and plain language, avoiding needless repetition and jargon.","Explain content licensing (proprietary, royalty-free, Creative Commons, public domain, open access) and TASL attribution, and optimise content for search: crawling, indexing, ranking, keywords, metadata and machine indexability.","Prepare images and multimedia for the web (format, size, alt text) and apply UX and accessibility considerations, including WCAG's POUR principles, before reviewing content against a checklist."],"videos":[{"title":"How to write for the web","q":"how to write for the web F pattern inverted pyramid scannable content"},{"title":"SEO basics for beginners","q":"SEO basics for beginners crawling indexing ranking title tag meta description"},{"title":"Writing a creative brief","q":"how to write a creative brief content marketing example"},{"title":"Optimising images for the web","q":"optimize images for web WebP compression resize tutorial"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Case study: Taste of Castries","html":"<p>This unit follows one real-feeling project from start to finish. Keep it in mind on every screen: it is the model for your own blog post at the end.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>The client:</b> a group of St. Lucian street-food vendors. <b>The project:</b> a \"Taste of Castries\" street-food trail for the cruise season. <b>The goal:</b> bring cruise visitors and residents to the vendors.</p>\n<p>The vendors hire the regional agency <b>Island Pulse Media</b>. For this case study the team takes on these roles:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Team member</th><th>Role on Taste of Castries</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Zara</b></td><td>Director: owns the brief and signs off the final content</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Malik</b></td><td>Writer: drafts the guide and the scripts for the clips</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Maya</b></td><td>Images: sources, shoots and optimises every photo</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Jerome</b></td><td>SEO: keyword research, metadata and search performance</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Keisha</b></td><td>Review: checks facts, names, accessibility and the brief</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Good content is useful, usable and purposeful</h4>\n<p>Before the team writes anything, Zara reminds them what \"good\" means. Effective digital content passes three tests:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Useful</b>: it answers a real need. A cruise visitor wants to know where to eat, what it costs and how far it is from the pier.</li>\n<li><b>Usable</b>: it is easy to find, read and navigate. The guide must appear in search, load fast on a phone and be easy to scan while walking along Jeremie Street.</li>\n<li><b>Purposeful</b>: it moves readers to act. Every section should push the reader one step closer to buying a plate of johnny cakes or lobster at a vendor's stall.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Content that is only one or two of these fails. A beautiful page nobody can find is not usable. A page full of facts with no call to action is not purposeful. A page about the history of Castries architecture, however well written, is not useful to a hungry visitor with five hours ashore.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Useful, usable, purposeful\" is a neat three-part framework for any \"evaluate this web content\" question. Take each word in turn, give one strength or weakness with evidence, and you have a structured, well-balanced answer.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Good digital content passes three tests. It is useful, because it answers a real need. It is usable, because it is easy to find, read and navigate. It is purposeful, because it moves readers to do something. A street-food guide for cruise visitors in Castries should give prices and walking times, load fast on a phone, and end with a \"Get directions\" button.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The three tests depend on each other, which is why content that passes only one or two still fails. Usefulness is judged from the <b>reader's</b> need, not the writer's interest: a beautiful history of Castries architecture is useful to a heritage tourist but not to a hungry visitor with five hours ashore. Usability includes being findable, so SEO is part of usability, not an extra. Purposefulness links content to the client's goal; without a clear next step, readers enjoy the page and leave. In an \"evaluate\" answer, take each test in turn, give evidence from the content, and finish with a judgement on which weakness matters most.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Useful, usable or purposeful?","intro":"<p>Keisha is reviewing early notes for the Taste of Castries guide. Each note describes one quality of the content. Sort it into the test it helps the content pass.</p>","main":"Which quality does this feature mainly show?","bins":["Useful","Usable","Purposeful"],"items":[{"t":"The guide appears when someone searches \"street food Castries\".","bin":1,"fb":"Being easy to find is part of usability. Content that cannot be found cannot be used."},{"t":"Every entry ends with a \"Get directions\" button.","bin":2,"fb":"A clear button moves the reader to act, so this is purposeful."},{"t":"A short line invites readers to share the trail with friends on the ship.","bin":2,"fb":"It prompts an action that spreads the content, so it is purposeful."},{"t":"Walking times from the cruise pier are given for every stop.","bin":0,"fb":"Visitors need to know they can get there and back in time. That is a real need answered."},{"t":"Descriptive headings let readers jump straight to the vendor they want.","bin":1,"fb":"Headings make content easy to scan and navigate, which is usability."},{"t":"A note explains which vendors take cards and which take cash only.","bin":0,"fb":"Payment is a real need for visitors, so this detail is useful."},{"t":"The page loads in under three seconds on a phone with roaming data.","bin":1,"fb":"Speed is about how easy the content is to use, so this is usability."},{"t":"Each vendor entry lists prices in US dollars and opening hours.","bin":0,"fb":"Prices and hours answer the real questions a visitor has, so this makes the content useful."}],"win":"Well sorted. Effective content is useful (answers a need), usable (easy to find, read and navigate) and purposeful (moves readers to act).","tutor":[{"h":"Ask the question","p":"<p>Does it give information people need, make the content easier to use, or push them to do something?</p>"},{"h":"Finding counts","p":"<p>Being easy to find in search is part of one of the three tests.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Useful, usable or purposeful? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Vendor prices and opening hours.","bin":0,"fb":"Answers a real need: useful."},{"t":"Fast loading on a phone.","bin":1,"fb":"Easy to use: usable."},{"t":"A \"Get directions\" button.","bin":2,"fb":"Moves readers to act: purposeful."},{"t":"Clear headings for each vendor.","bin":1,"fb":"Easy to scan and navigate: usable."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Now Keisha is reviewing a weak guide from another agency. Each note describes a weakness. Sort it by the test the content FAILS.</p>","main":"Which test does this weakness mainly fail?","items":[{"t":"The guide never says what any dish costs.","bin":0,"fb":"Visitors need prices to decide, so missing prices fail the useful test."},{"t":"The page takes 14 seconds to load on mobile data.","bin":1,"fb":"Slow loading makes content hard to use, a usability failure."},{"t":"There is no button or link to directions, booking or the vendors.","bin":2,"fb":"Without a next step, readers cannot act, so it fails the purposeful test."},{"t":"Most of the page describes the history of Morne Fortune, not food.","bin":0,"fb":"Content that does not answer the reader's real need is not useful to this audience."},{"t":"The guide cannot be found when visitors search \"street food Castries\".","bin":1,"fb":"Content that cannot be found cannot be used, so findability is part of usability."},{"t":"Grey text on a beige background is unreadable in bright sun.","bin":1,"fb":"Poor contrast makes content hard to read, a usability failure."},{"t":"The page ends without inviting readers to visit, share or save the trail.","bin":2,"fb":"Ending without a call to action fails the purposeful test."},{"t":"Opening hours are missing, so visitors cannot plan around their ship's departure.","bin":0,"fb":"Hours answer a real need for time-limited visitors, so their absence fails usefulness."},{"t":"The vendor list is one long paragraph with no headings.","bin":1,"fb":"Without headings, readers cannot scan or navigate, a usability problem."}],"win":"Strong evaluation. You traced each weakness to the test it fails, which gives you the structure for a balanced \"evaluate this content\" answer.","title":"Useful, usable or purposeful? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"The seven-stage content cycle","html":"<p>The course outline describes content development as \"plan, research, create, edit, publish, maintain\". In practice, content moves through a <b>seven-stage cycle</b>. It is a cycle, not a straight line, because what you learn at the end feeds the next piece of content.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Stage</th><th>What happens</th><th>Taste of Castries</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1. Plan</b></td><td>Set the scope and goals.</td><td>Zara agrees the Taste of Castries scope with the vendors.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2. Research</b></td><td>Study the audience and keywords.</td><td>Jerome researches what cruise visitors search for.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3. Create</b></td><td>Write, shoot, record, design.</td><td>Malik writes the guide; Maya shoots photos and clips.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>4. Edit and review</b></td><td>Check facts, quality and fit with the brief.</td><td>Keisha checks vendor names, prices and the brief.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>5. Publish</b></td><td>Put the content live on the chosen channels.</td><td>Live by 1 December, before the busy cruise weeks.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>6. Promote</b></td><td>Share it so the audience actually sees it.</td><td>Clips on Instagram and TikTok, flyers at the pier.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>7. Measure and maintain</b></td><td>Track analytics; update or retire content.</td><td>Check page views and searches; update prices each month.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 300 120\" width=\"300\" height=\"120\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Seven stages arranged in a loop, with an arrow from stage 7 back to stage 1\">\n<g fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"1.5\">\n<ellipse cx=\"150\" cy=\"60\" rx=\"120\" ry=\"42\"/>\n<path d=\"M60 92 l-8 -4 l2 9 z\"/>\n</g>\n<g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\">\n<text x=\"150\" y=\"14\">1 Plan</text><text x=\"252\" y=\"30\">2 Research</text><text x=\"282\" y=\"64\">3 Create</text>\n<text x=\"250\" y=\"110\">4 Edit</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"117\">5 Publish</text><text x=\"52\" y=\"110\">6 Promote</text>\n<text x=\"40\" y=\"30\">7 Measure</text><text x=\"150\" y=\"64\">Analytics feed the next piece</text>\n</g>\n</svg>\n<p><b>Analytics feed the next piece of content.</b> If the measure stage shows that many visitors searched for \"vegetarian food Castries\", the next blog post can answer that need. This is why \"maintain\" matters: outdated prices or a vendor that has closed make the whole guide less trustworthy.</p>\n<p>Many students skip stages 4, 6 and 7. They write something, post it, and stop. Professional content teams never stop at \"publish\".</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Your SBA Progress and Process marks reward evidence of each stage. Keep dated screenshots of your plan, research notes, drafts with edits, the published product and any feedback or analytics. Label each one with the stage it shows.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Content goes through seven stages: plan, research, create, edit and review, publish, promote, then measure and maintain. It is a cycle, because what you learn at the end helps plan the next piece. A street-food guide for St. Lucia must be checked again each month so that prices and opening hours stay correct.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The stages students most often skip, review, promote and measure, are the ones that decide whether content succeeds. Publishing without promotion assumes an audience will simply find the page; measuring without acting wastes the data. The cycle also explains why content can be <b>retired</b>: a page with outdated prices damages trust in everything else on the site. Analytics close the loop. Searches for \"vegetarian food Castries\" reveal an unmet need, which becomes the next plan. In exam answers, describe the cycle with one concrete action per stage, and show the feedback arrow from measure back to plan.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Order the seven-stage content cycle","intro":"<p>Malik has the Taste of Castries task cards in a muddle. Put the seven stages in the correct order, starting with the first.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Plan: agree the Taste of Castries scope and goals with the vendors"},{"t":"Research: study cruise visitors and the keywords they search for"},{"t":"Create: write the guide, shoot the photos and the three clips"},{"t":"Edit and review: check facts, names and fit with the brief"},{"t":"Publish: put the guide live by 1 December"},{"t":"Promote: share the clips and flyers so visitors see the guide"},{"t":"Measure and maintain: track analytics and update prices"}],"win":"Excellent. Plan, research, create, edit and review, publish, promote, measure and maintain; and the analytics feed the next piece of content.","tutor":[{"h":"Before you make it","p":"<p>Two stages happen before anyone writes a word.</p>"},{"h":"After it goes live","p":"<p>Going live is not the end. People must see it, then you check how it performed.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Order the content cycle (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put these four stages in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Plan the scope"},{"t":"Create the guide"},{"t":"Publish it"},{"t":"Measure and maintain"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>The stage names are gone. Put these nine Taste of Castries tasks in the order they happen in the content cycle.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Agree the six vendors, the goals and the 1 December deadline with the client."},{"t":"Interview visitors on the waterfront and check what they search for in Google Trends."},{"t":"Draft the 800-word guide and shoot the vendor photos and clips."},{"t":"Phone every vendor to confirm prices and hours, and check the draft against the brief."},{"t":"Zara signs off the reviewed guide."},{"t":"Put the guide live on the agency blog."},{"t":"Post the clips on Instagram and TikTok, and hand out QR flyers at the pier."},{"t":"Check page views and search terms after one month."},{"t":"Update the prices and plan a vegetarian guide based on the search data."}],"win":"Correct. Plan, research, create, review and sign-off, publish, promote, measure, then maintain, which feeds the next plan.","title":"Order the seven-stage content cycle (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Scope sets the boundaries before you write","html":"<p>Before you write a word, you must decide <b>what</b> you are creating, <b>for whom</b> and <b>why</b>. This is called \"defining and researching content scope\". Here is the definition:</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Content scope</b>: defining the purpose, topic, audience, platform, format and intended outcome of the content.</p>\n<p>This unit uses seven headings. They cover the same ideas: <b>channels</b> is the platform, the <b>purpose</b> includes the intended outcome, and two more are added: <b>depth</b> and <b>constraints</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Scope element</th><th>Question to answer</th><th>Taste of Castries</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Topic</b></td><td>What exactly is it about?</td><td>Taste of Castries: six street-food vendors near the cruise port</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Purpose</b></td><td>Why does it exist? What should readers do?</td><td>Bring cruise visitors and residents to the vendors</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Audience</b></td><td>Who will use it, and on what device?</td><td>Cruise visitors (mainly on phones) and residents</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Depth</b></td><td>How much detail?</td><td>About 800 words: enough for six vendors, not a history book</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Format</b></td><td>Text, video, infographic, podcast?</td><td>A blog guide plus three 30-second clips</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Channels</b></td><td>Where will it be published and shared?</td><td>Agency blog, Instagram, TikTok, QR flyers at the pier</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Constraints</b></td><td>Deadlines, budget, rules?</td><td>Live by 1 December; no paid stock photos; mobile-first</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Writing the scope down stops <b>scope creep</b>, where a project keeps growing until it misses its deadline. When one vendor asked the team to add a full section on Rodney Bay restaurants, Zara pointed to the agreed scope: the trail is about six vendors near the Castries port. A Rodney Bay guide could be a separate project.</p>\n<p>Constraints are easy to forget but shape everything. \"No paid stock photos\" meant Maya had to shoot or find free, properly licensed images. \"Mobile-first\" meant every decision had to work on a small screen with roaming data.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Your SBA Proposal must define scope. Use clear headings so examiners can tick each element quickly, and make the purpose measurable (\"150 QR scans in December\") rather than vague (\"raise awareness\").</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Content scope sets the limits before you write. Decide the topic, purpose, audience, depth, format, channels and constraints such as deadlines and budget. Writing it down stops the project from growing too big. When a vendor asked to add Rodney Bay restaurants to a Castries street-food guide, the team pointed to the agreed scope.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Scope is a tool for <b>saying no well</b>. Scope creep rarely arrives as one big change; it comes as many small, reasonable requests that together break the deadline. A written scope turns each request into a choice: change the scope and adjust time and budget, or keep it for a later project. Constraints are especially powerful because they force creative decisions: \"no paid stock photos\" led the team to shoot authentic vendor photos, which improved the guide. The weakest element in most student proposals is purpose. Make it measurable, such as \"150 QR scans in December\", so success can actually be judged.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the scope element","intro":"<p>Zara is reading the Taste of Castries scope statement aloud. Match each scope element to the team's decision.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Topic","r":"Six street-food vendors near the Castries cruise port"},{"l":"Purpose","r":"Bring cruise visitors and residents to the vendors"},{"l":"Audience","r":"Cruise visitors on phones, plus local residents"},{"l":"Depth","r":"About 800 words, one short section per vendor"},{"l":"Format","r":"A blog guide plus three 30-second clips"},{"l":"Channels","r":"The agency blog, Instagram, TikTok and QR flyers"},{"l":"Constraints","r":"Live by 1 December, no paid stock photos, mobile-first"}],"win":"Very good. You can break any content project into topic, purpose, audience, depth, format, channels and constraints.","tutor":[{"h":"Why vs what","p":"<p>The reason for the content is different from its subject matter.</p>"},{"h":"Where vs how","p":"<p>Channels are where it lives. Format is the shape the content takes. Depth is how much detail.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the scope element (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Audience","r":"Cruise visitors on phones"},{"l":"Format","r":"A blog guide plus three clips"},{"l":"Channels","r":"Blog, Instagram and TikTok"},{"l":"Constraints","r":"Live by 1 December, no paid stock photos"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Each problem happened because one scope element was never agreed. Match the missing element to the problem it caused.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Topic","r":"Writers argue about whether the guide should also cover Rodney Bay restaurants"},{"l":"Purpose","r":"Nobody can say what readers should do after reading, so the page has no call to action"},{"l":"Audience","r":"Prices are given only in EC dollars, although most readers are American cruise visitors"},{"l":"Depth","r":"The draft grows to 4,000 words of island history"},{"l":"Format","r":"Half the team writes a blog post while the other half scripts a podcast"},{"l":"Channels","r":"The guide goes live, but nobody planned where it would be shared"},{"l":"Constraints","r":"The team buys stock photos and then learns the budget forbids paid images"}],"win":"Well diagnosed. Each scope element prevents a specific kind of failure, which is why a complete scope protects the deadline and the budget.","title":"Match the scope element (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Research: find the gap, check the facts","html":"<p><b>Research</b> is part of content scope: \"gathering accurate, relevant and credible information to support content development\". Good research tells you what the audience needs and what already exists.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Research area</th><th>Methods</th><th>Taste of Castries</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Audience</b></td><td>Surveys (Google Forms), interviews, website and social analytics</td><td>Short interviews with visitors on the waterfront; analytics showed most visitors used phones</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Keywords</b></td><td>Google Trends, Google's \"People also ask\" box, search suggestions</td><td>Jerome saw searches like \"where to eat near cruise port St Lucia\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Competitors</b></td><td>Competitor review: what do other guides offer, and what do they miss?</td><td>Existing lists were old, scattered or about sit-down restaurants</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Your own content</b></td><td>Content audit: list what you already have, what is out of date, what can be reused</td><td>Old agency posts had useful vendor photos but wrong prices</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The result was clear: <b>there was no single, up-to-date guide</b> to street food near the port. That gap is the reason the content is useful.</p>\n<h4>Check every source</h4>\n<p><b>Primary research</b> is information you collect yourself (interviews, surveys, photographs). <b>Secondary research</b> uses existing sources: government sites, national newspapers, reports from the Caribbean Tourism Organization or the World Bank. Test each source with the CRAAP checklist:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Currency</b>: Is it recent? Prices and opening hours change.</li>\n<li><b>Relevance</b>: Does it answer your topic and suit your audience?</li>\n<li><b>Authority</b>: Who wrote it? A named, knowledgeable source beats an anonymous post.</li>\n<li><b>Accuracy</b>: Can you confirm it in another independent source?</li>\n<li><b>Purpose</b>: Is it informing, or selling? Watch for bias.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Keep a <b>source log</b>: title, author or organisation, URL, date accessed and what you used. Be careful with chatbots and search summaries: they can state wrong facts confidently. Verify every fact, and give statistics with a year and source type (\"ITU figures for 2023\").</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In an \"evaluate a source\" question, name the criterion and apply it: \"This page lacks currency because the prices were last updated in 2019.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Research tells you what your audience needs and what already exists. Use interviews, surveys, keyword tools and a look at other guides to find the gap. Check every source: is it recent, relevant, written by someone who knows, accurate and honest about its purpose? A 2014 blog post with opening hours is too old to trust.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Research has two jobs: to find the <b>gap</b> (what the audience needs that nobody provides) and to <b>verify</b> every fact you publish. The CRAAP test is a thinking tool rather than a tick-box: an official government page can still fail currency, and a vendor's own post is authoritative about today's opening hours but not about being \"the best on the island\". Primary research is powerful in small Caribbean markets, where published data is scarce. Be especially careful with automated search summaries and chatbots, which can state wrong figures confidently; trace every statistic to its original source and give the year and source type.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Credible or questionable?","intro":"<p>Keisha is checking sources before the Taste of Castries guide goes live. Sort each source.</p>","main":"Is this source credible enough to use, or questionable?","bins":["Credible","Questionable"],"items":[{"t":"A chatbot answer giving a precise number of food trucks on the island, with no source.","bin":1,"fb":"Such tools can invent figures. Without a verifiable source, do not publish it."},{"t":"An anonymous TikTok claiming accra was \"first made in 2010\".","bin":1,"fb":"No authority, no evidence and it cannot be confirmed. Questionable."},{"t":"The official St. Lucia tourism website listing this year's cruise season events.","bin":0,"fb":"Official, current and relevant. Still double-check dates close to publishing."},{"t":"A university research paper on Caribbean food heritage with references.","bin":0,"fb":"Academic, referenced work has strong authority and accuracy."},{"t":"A recorded interview with a johnny cake vendor about her recipe and prices this season.","bin":0,"fb":"Primary research from the person who knows best is credible, as long as it is quoted accurately with permission."},{"t":"A 2014 blog post giving vendor opening hours.","bin":1,"fb":"It fails currency. Opening hours change; confirm directly with the vendor."},{"t":"A national newspaper report quoting named food vendors.","bin":0,"fb":"An established news outlet with named sources has authority and accountability."},{"t":"A vendor's own flyer saying it has \"the best ribs on the island\".","bin":1,"fb":"The purpose is to sell, so it is biased. You may quote it as their claim, not as fact."}],"win":"Well sorted. Credible sources are current, relevant, authoritative, accurate and honest about their purpose.","tutor":[{"h":"Who said it?","p":"<p>Can you identify a named, knowledgeable author or organisation?</p>"},{"h":"When and why?","p":"<p>Old information and sales talk need extra checking.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Credible or questionable? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"An official government page updated this year.","bin":0,"fb":"Official and current: credible."},{"t":"An anonymous social media post with no evidence.","bin":1,"fb":"No authority: questionable."},{"t":"A news report quoting named people.","bin":0,"fb":"Accountable outlet with named sources: credible."},{"t":"A 10-year-old blog post about prices.","bin":1,"fb":"Out of date: questionable."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder sources. Think about which CRAAP criterion each one passes or fails for the fact the team wants to use.</p>","items":[{"t":"A 2025 Caribbean Tourism Organization report on cruise arrivals, used for a visitor statistic.","bin":0,"fb":"Current, relevant and from an authoritative regional body."},{"t":"An official government page, last updated in 2016, listing vendor licence fees.","bin":1,"fb":"Official does not mean current. Fees may have changed, so it fails currency; check with the department."},{"t":"A polished site with no author or date that sells food tours and calls one stall \"the most visited\".","bin":1,"fb":"No authority, no date, and a selling purpose: questionable for a factual claim."},{"t":"A national newspaper article quoting the government statistics office, with the year of the figure given.","bin":0,"fb":"A named official source and year make it verifiable."},{"t":"An automated search summary giving a vendor's opening hours, with no source link.","bin":1,"fb":"With no traceable source, the hours cannot be verified; confirm with the vendor."},{"t":"A vendor's own Instagram post from this morning showing today's menu and hours.","bin":0,"fb":"The vendor is the authority on her own hours and the post is current. It would not be credible for a claim such as \"the best on the island\"."},{"t":"A single anonymous review saying a stall has \"closed forever\".","bin":1,"fb":"One anonymous claim lacks authority and must be confirmed before publishing."},{"t":"A 2023 peer-reviewed article on food tourism in small island states.","bin":0,"fb":"Peer review and references give strong authority and accuracy."},{"t":"A cruise line press release announcing \"record numbers\" with no figures.","bin":1,"fb":"Its purpose is promotion and it gives no data, so the claim cannot be checked."}],"win":"Strong evaluation. Credibility depends on the claim as well as the source: a vendor is credible about her hours, an official page can be out of date, and promotional sources need checking.","title":"Credible or questionable? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"The creative brief: the contract for the content","html":"<p>A <b>creative brief</b> is a short document that tells the team what to make, for whom, and how it should feel. Think of it as <b>the contract for the content</b>: the client and the team agree it <b>before</b> production, and every later decision is checked against it. It must include the <b>target group</b>, the <b>content checklist</b> and the <b>tone of voice</b>.</p>\n<p>A brief has three parts:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Direction</th><th>Message</th><th>Delivery</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Overview</b>: the client, the problem and the project<br><b>Measurable objectives</b>: what success looks like in numbers<br><b>Target group</b>: who the content is for, often with a persona</td><td><b>Key message</b> in one sentence: the one thing readers must remember<br><b>Tone of voice</b>: how the content should sound</td><td><b>Deliverables checklist</b>: every item to be produced<br><b>Channels, timeline and approvals</b>: where, when, and who signs off</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>For Taste of Castries, the key message was: <i>\"Real St. Lucian street food is a short walk from your ship, and you will be back on board in time.\"</i> One sentence, and every part of the guide supports it.</p>\n<p>The <b>deliverables checklist</b> tracks every item:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Deliverable</th><th>Format</th><th>Owner</th><th>Due</th><th>Done?</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Trail guide (about 800 words)</td><td>Blog post</td><td>Malik</td><td>15 Nov</td><td>[ ]</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vendor photos (six)</td><td>WebP, under 200 KB each</td><td>Maya</td><td>18 Nov</td><td>[ ]</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Three 30-second clips</td><td>MP4, vertical, captioned</td><td>Maya and Malik</td><td>22 Nov</td><td>[ ]</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Title tag, meta description, slug</td><td>Text</td><td>Jerome</td><td>22 Nov</td><td>[ ]</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Final review and sign-off</td><td>Checklist</td><td>Keisha, then Zara</td><td>28 Nov</td><td>[ ]</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The brief protects everyone. If a vendor later asks \"Why is there no Rodney Bay section?\", the team can point to the agreed brief and discuss a change in scope, time and cost.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Your SBA product includes a 30-second audio-visual ad. Write a mini creative brief for it under Direction, Message and Delivery; it gives you clear evidence for Proposal and Process marks.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A creative brief is a short plan that the client and the team agree before making content. It covers direction (overview, goals, target group), message (one key message and the tone) and delivery (a checklist of items, channels, timeline and who approves). If a client asks for something extra later, the brief shows what was agreed.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The brief works as a contract because every later decision can be <b>tested against it</b>: does this photo, headline or clip support the key message for this target group? That is why the key message must be one sentence; if it needs a paragraph, the team does not yet agree on what matters. The deliverables checklist turns the brief into a schedule with owners and dates, which makes delays visible early. A common weakness is a vague objective. \"Raise awareness\" cannot be checked, whereas \"200 checklist downloads in June\" can. Strong briefs also name who signs off, preventing endless rounds of changes.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Case study: write for Denise, not for everyone","html":"<p>\"Cruise visitors\" is too broad to write for. So the team created a <b>persona</b>: an imagined but realistic profile that represents the target group. Writing for one clear person makes the content sharper.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Denise</b>: 42, from Atlanta, five hours ashore in Castries.<br><b>Wants:</b> real local food, and to be back on the ship on time.<br><b>Has:</b> a phone with roaming data, so pages must load fast.</p>\n<p>Every decision can now be tested against Denise:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>She has <b>five hours</b>, so the guide gives walking times from the pier and opening hours.</li>\n<li>She wants <b>real local food</b>, so the guide explains bakes, saltfish, accra and cocoa tea, not burgers she can get at home.</li>\n<li>She is on <b>roaming data</b>, so images must be small and the page mobile-first.</li>\n<li>She is a <b>visitor</b>, so prices are in US dollars and directions use landmarks such as the waterfront and the cathedral on Derek Walcott Square.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A second persona could represent residents, for example <b>Shanice</b>, 19, a Sandbox Tree College student in Castries who uses Instagram and TikTok on a mid-range Android phone and buys street food after class.</p>\n<h4>Tone of voice in pairs</h4>\n<p>Tone describes how content sounds. A useful tip is to write tone as <b>pairs</b>: what you want, and the extreme you must avoid.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>We are...</th><th>but not...</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Friendly</b></td><td>childish</td><td>\"You'll love this\" rather than \"OMG yummy!!!\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Confident</b></td><td>boastful</td><td>\"A local favourite since 2009\" rather than \"The best food in the Caribbean\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Local</b></td><td>stereotyped</td><td>Real vendor names and dishes rather than clichés about \"island time\"</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Tone must match audience and purpose. A hurricane shelter update needs calm, clear authority; a Carnival fete promo needs energy. Denise needs warmth and trust.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When a question asks you to \"describe the target group\", go beyond age. Give location, needs, device and situation, as the Denise persona does. Specific detail earns the marks.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A persona is a profile of one typical reader, such as Denise, a cruise visitor with five hours ashore and roaming data. Writing for one clear person makes content sharper. Tone of voice is how the writing sounds. Write it as pairs, such as \"friendly, not childish\", so writers know what to aim for and what to avoid.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A persona turns vague audience descriptions into <b>testable decisions</b>. \"Cruise visitors\" cannot settle an argument; \"Denise has five hours and roaming data\" decides that walking times and small images matter more than a long history section. Good personas are built from research, not imagination: interviews on the waterfront and analytics showing phone use. Tone pairs work because tone usually fails at an extreme; \"friendly\" can slide into childish, and \"confident\" into boastful. Tone must also shift with purpose for the same brand: a storm-shelter notice and a Carnival promo need different voices. Describe a target group by situation, need and device, not by age alone.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Tone of voice and target group","intro":"<p>Keisha is checking the Taste of Castries brief and some briefs for other clients. Choose the best answer each time.</p>","items":[{"text":"Denise has five hours ashore and roaming data. Which feature matters MOST for her?","options":["a gallery of 60 full-size photos","a long history of Castries","walking times from the pier and fast-loading pages"],"answer":2,"fb":"The persona tells you her real constraints: time and data. The content must serve those first."},{"text":"Which line is \"friendly, not childish\"?","options":["\"You'll love the crispy johnny cakes at this stall.\"","\"OMG these are SOOO yummy!!!\"","\"Patrons are advised that johnny cakes are available.\""],"answer":0,"fb":"It is warm and addresses the reader, without childish slang or cold formality."},{"text":"Which line is \"confident, not boastful\"?","options":["\"The greatest food on Earth.\"","\"We think it might be quite nice.\"","\"A Jeremie Street favourite with locals for over ten years.\""],"answer":2,"fb":"Confidence comes from specific, believable evidence, not empty superlatives or weak hedging."},{"text":"A brief for a storm shelter update in St. Lucia. The best tone is...","options":["playful, with emojis","formal legal language","calm, clear and authoritative"],"answer":2,"fb":"In emergencies people need calm, clear, trustworthy instructions."},{"text":"\"Real St. Lucian street food is a short walk from your ship\" is the brief's...","options":["deliverables checklist","key message","measurable objective"],"answer":1,"fb":"The key message is the one sentence readers must remember."},{"text":"\"150 QR code scans from the pier flyers in December\" is a...","options":["measurable objective","persona","tone of voice"],"answer":0,"fb":"It has a number and a time, so success can be measured."}],"win":"Excellent. You can use a persona to make decisions, write tone as pairs, and name the parts of a creative brief.","tutor":[{"h":"Think like Denise","p":"<p>What would worry a visitor with limited time and data?</p>"},{"h":"Tone pairs","p":"<p>The right answer sits between two extremes.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Tone and target group (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A storm update should sound...","options":["jokey","calm and clear"],"answer":1,"fb":"People need clear, trustworthy information."},{"text":"\"Denise, 42, from Atlanta, five hours ashore\" is a...","options":["deliverables checklist","persona"],"answer":1,"fb":"It is a profile of the target group."},{"text":"Which is \"friendly, not childish\"?","options":["\"OMG yummy!!!\"","\"You'll love this stall.\""],"answer":1,"fb":"Warm and direct, without childish slang."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder brief decisions for Taste of Castries and two other clients. Choose the BEST answer.</p>","items":[{"text":"Shanice, 19, a Sandbox Tree College student on a mid-range Android phone, is the resident persona. Which content decision suits her BEST?","options":["An email newsletter only","A formal PDF report","A 3,000-word printed brochure","A 30-second TikTok clip of the vendors after class hours, with captions"],"answer":3,"fb":"Her persona names TikTok, a mid-range phone and after-class buying, so short captioned clips fit her best."},{"text":"Which rewrite is \"local, not stereotyped\"?","options":["\"Food is available.\"","\"Relax on island time with a rum punch!\"","\"Exotic island treats await you!\"","\"Miss Lyn has fried bakes on Jeremie Street since 2009. Try them with saltfish.\""],"answer":3,"fb":"Real names, real dishes and a real place avoid clichés such as \"island time\" and \"exotic\"."},{"text":"Which objective is MOST measurable?","options":["150 QR scans from the pier flyers and 2,000 page views in December","Raise awareness of street food","Make the vendors more popular","Have a great cruise season"],"answer":0,"fb":"It has numbers and a time period, so success can be checked."},{"text":"A Grenada spice festival brief and a St. Lucia storm-shelter notice come from the same agency. What should change between them?","options":["Nothing, the agency always uses one tone","Only the font","The tone: energetic and inviting for the festival, calm and authoritative for the shelter notice","Only the length"],"answer":2,"fb":"Tone must match audience and purpose, even for the same writers."},{"text":"A vendor asks halfway through for a Rodney Bay section. What is the BEST response using the brief?","options":["Add it quietly to keep the vendor happy","Point to the agreed scope, and discuss it as a change that affects time and cost, or as a separate project","Refuse and stop the project","Replace two Castries vendors with Rodney Bay ones"],"answer":1,"fb":"The brief is the contract. Changes are possible, but they must be agreed with their effect on time and cost."},{"text":"The key message draft is three sentences long. What does this suggest?","options":["The persona is too detailed","The tone is wrong","The team has not yet agreed the one thing readers must remember","The brief is very thorough"],"answer":2,"fb":"A key message should be one sentence. If it needs three, the team has not decided what matters most."}],"win":"Excellent. You used the persona, tone pairs, measurable objectives and the brief itself to make and defend decisions.","title":"Tone of voice and target group (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Readers scan in an F-shape, so write for scanners","html":"<p>People rarely read web pages word by word. Eye-tracking studies show many readers scan in an <b>F-shape</b>: across the top, across a little lower, then down the left side. Words at the start of headings and lines get seen; the rest is often skipped. The criteria for web-friendly content all help scanners.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Technique</th><th>What it means</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Key message first</b></td><td>Lead with the point. Denise should know the main idea in the first two lines.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Inverted pyramid</b></td><td>Most important information at the top, then key details, then background. If readers stop early, they still have what matters.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Front-load words</b></td><td>Put key words at the start of headings and sentences: \"Bakes and saltfish near the pier\", not \"If you want something tasty, try johnny cakes\".</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Lists and plain language</b></td><td>Bullet points for steps and options; \"use\" not \"utilise\", \"buy\" not \"procure\". Plain language also helps readers whose first language is Dutch, Spanish, Papiamentu or Haitian Creole.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Keep it short</b></td><td>Short sentences (about 15 to 20 words), short paragraphs (two to four sentences), one idea each.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Descriptive headings</b></td><td>Headings that say what follows, so readers can jump.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Make action clear</b></td><td>Address users directly (\"you\"), use meaningful link text and end with a clear <b>call to action</b>.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>Cut the repetition and the jargon</h4>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr>\n<tr><td>\"At this point in time, we would like to take the opportunity to inform each and every visitor that the vendors are open.\"</td><td>\"All vendors are open today.\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td>\"Click here for more information.\"</td><td>\"See prices and opening hours for every vendor.\"</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The first \"after\" removes filler (\"at this point in time\", \"each and every\") and says it once. The second replaces vague link text with words that describe the destination, which helps scanners, screen-reader users and search engines.</p>\n<p>Avoid <b>jargon</b>: technical or insider terms confuse general audiences. If a term is needed, explain it the first time.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 02 may give you a badly written paragraph to rewrite for the web. Show several criteria at once: add a front-loaded heading, put the key message first, shorten sentences, use \"you\", cut jargon and repetition, and end with a descriptive call to action.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Most people scan web pages in an F-shape. They read the top line, part of the next, then glance down the left side. So put the key message first, start headings with the important words, keep sentences short, use lists and plain words, and end with a clear action. \"All vendors are open today\" beats a long, wordy sentence.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Web-friendly writing is really about <b>respecting the reader's effort</b>. Front-loading works because scanners read only the first few words of each line, so \"Bakes and saltfish near the pier\" survives a scan while \"If you want something tasty...\" does not. Plain language is also an accessibility and inclusion choice: in St. Lucia many readers speak Kwéyòl as a first language, many visitors speak Spanish or French, and simple wording translates better. A subtle error is cutting so much that meaning is lost; short is good only if the essential facts remain. When rewriting for Paper 02, make several techniques visible in the same short paragraph.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the weak copy","intro":"<p>Malik's first draft of the Taste of Castries guide has some strong lines and some weak ones. Keisha needs your help with the edit.</p>","q":"Select every line that is WEAK web copy and needs an edit.","lines":["Six local street-food stops, all within a 10-minute walk of the cruise pier.","At this point in time, we would like to take the opportunity to inform each and every visitor that the vendors are open.","Try crispy johnny cakes with saltfish for under 8 US dollars.","Click here for more information.","Our curated gastronomic proposition leverages authentic culinary touchpoints.","See prices and opening hours for every vendor.","The vendors are open daily. Every day of the week, the vendors are open.","Back on board by 4 p.m.? Start at stop 1 and work your way along Jeremie Street."],"correct":[1,3,4,6],"win":"Well spotted. Filler phrases, vague \"click here\" links, jargon and needless repetition all slow scanners down. The strong lines are front-loaded, specific and direct.","miss":"Not quite. Look for filler that says nothing, vague link text, jargon and repeated information. Short, specific lines that speak to \"you\" are strong copy.","tutor":[{"h":"Read it aloud","p":"<p>If a sentence sounds like a report or says the same thing twice, it needs an edit.</p>"},{"h":"Links","p":"<p>Does the link text tell the reader where it goes?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the weak copy (Foundation)","q":"Select the lines that are weak web copy.","lines":["Click here for more information.","All vendors are open today.","Each and every visitor is hereby informed that the vendors are open.","See prices and opening hours for every vendor."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Vague link text and wordy filler are weak copy.","miss":"Look for a vague link and for a wordy, repetitive sentence."},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Malik's second draft is better, but some weaknesses are harder to spot. Edit with Keisha.</p>","q":"Select every line that is WEAK web copy and needs an edit.","lines":["Bakes, saltfish and accra: six stops within a 10-minute walk of the pier.","It should be noted that prices may be subject to change at the discretion of the vendors.","Learn more.","Miss Lyn's stall opens at 10 a.m.; arrive early, because the johnny cakes sell out by noon.","Utilising locally procured ingredients, the vendors facilitate an authentic gastronomic experience.","Visitors who are interested in history, culture, music, food and shopping, and who may only have a few hours before returning to their ship, will find that there is a lot to enjoy in Castries.","Back on board by 4 p.m.? Start at stop 1 and walk along Jeremie Street.","Delicious food. Very delicious food that is delicious.","See prices, hours and walking times for all six vendors.","The best street food in the whole Caribbean, guaranteed!"],"correct":[1,2,4,5,7,9],"win":"Sharp editing. You caught wordy filler, vague link text, jargon, an overlong sentence that buries the point, repetition and a boastful claim that breaks the \"confident, not boastful\" tone.","miss":"Look again for subtler weaknesses: wordy filler (\"it should be noted\"), vague links (\"Learn more\"), jargon, a 40-word sentence with no clear point, repetition, and an unprovable boast.","title":"Spot the weak copy (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Check the licence before you publish anything","html":"<p>\"Licensing\" is one of the criteria for content development. Every image, song, video clip and font you publish has an owner and a licence. You met copyright and ethics in Unit 2.2; here you apply it when publishing.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Licence type</th><th>What it means</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Proprietary (all rights reserved)</b></td><td>The owner keeps all rights. You need permission or a paid licence.</td><td>A news agency photo; a chart hit</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Royalty-free</b></td><td>You pay once (or nothing) and can reuse it under the licence terms. Not the same as \"copyright-free\".</td><td>Stock images and music libraries</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Creative Commons (CC)</b></td><td>The creator allows some uses in advance, with conditions.</td><td>Many images on Wikimedia Commons and Flickr</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Public domain</b></td><td>No copyright applies: it has expired, or the creator gave it away (for example CC0).</td><td>Very old artworks; CC0 photos</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Open access</b></td><td>Research or educational material free to read online, often under a CC licence.</td><td>Open-access journal articles</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<h4>The four CC conditions</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>BY</b> (attribution): credit the creator.</li>\n<li><b>SA</b> (share-alike): your new work must use the same licence.</li>\n<li><b>NC</b> (non-commercial): no money-making use.</li>\n<li><b>ND</b> (no derivatives): no changes, such as cropping, remixing or adding text.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So <b>CC BY-NC</b> means \"credit me and do not use it commercially\".</p>\n<h4>Attribute with TASL</h4>\n<p>A good credit gives the <b>T</b>itle, <b>A</b>uthor, <b>S</b>ource (link) and <b>L</b>icence. For example: \"Sunset over Castries Harbour\" by J. Richardson, via Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Taste of Castries:</b> Maya found a lovely CC BY-NC beach photo. But the guide promotes paying businesses, which is <b>commercial use</b>, so NC ruled it out. The constraint \"no paid stock photos\" ruled out buying one. The team shot its own photos and got <b>signed releases</b> from the vendors and anyone recognisable.</p>\n<p>You also choose a licence for <b>your own</b> work. Island Pulse Media keeps client work proprietary but shares some training photos under CC BY so local schools can reuse them.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In your SBA report, include an \"Assets and licences\" table listing every third-party image, music track and font with its TASL details. It protects you and shows professional practice.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Every image, song, clip and font has an owner and a licence. Proprietary means you need permission or must pay. Royalty-free means you pay once or nothing under set terms. Creative Commons gives some permissions in advance. Public domain means anyone can use it. Credit with TASL: title, author, source and licence. A promotional food guide cannot use a CC BY-NC photo.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>\"Royalty-free\" is the most misunderstood label: it describes how you pay, not whether copyright exists, and its licence terms still apply. \"Commercial use\" is also wider than students expect. Content that promotes a paying business is commercial even if readers pay nothing, which is why NC images were ruled out for Taste of Castries. Licensing applies to people as well as works: recognisable faces in promotional photos need signed releases. Professionals keep an <b>assets log</b> recording each item's source, licence and permission, so any challenge can be answered quickly. Choosing a licence for your own work is part of the same skill.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Sort the Creative Commons conditions","intro":"<p>Maya is teaching an intern the four CC conditions. Each card describes a rule or a use. Sort it under the condition it describes.</p>","main":"Which CC condition does this describe?","bins":["BY","SA","NC","ND"],"items":[{"t":"You must use the video clip exactly as it is, with no edits.","bin":3,"fb":"ND means you may not change the work."},{"t":"The credit line follows TASL: title, author, source, licence.","bin":0,"fb":"TASL is how you meet the BY (attribution) condition properly."},{"t":"You remix a CC song, so your remix must be shared under the same licence.","bin":1,"fb":"SA (share-alike) means new work must carry the same licence."},{"t":"You translate a CC article into Papiamentu and must release it under the same CC licence.","bin":1,"fb":"A translation is a new work; SA requires the same licence."},{"t":"You may share the image, but you cannot crop it or add text.","bin":3,"fb":"ND (no derivatives) means no changes at all."},{"t":"You cannot use the photo on a page that promotes paying vendors.","bin":2,"fb":"NC (non-commercial) forbids money-making uses, including promoting businesses."},{"t":"A student uses the photo free in a school project, but a hotel cannot use it in an advert.","bin":2,"fb":"Non-commercial use is allowed; commercial use is not. That is NC."},{"t":"You must credit the photographer by name.","bin":0,"fb":"BY means attribution: always credit the creator."}],"win":"Well done. BY means credit, SA means same licence, NC means no commercial use, and ND means no changes.","tutor":[{"h":"Decode the letters","p":"<p>BY = by whom? SA = share alike. NC = non-commercial. ND = no derivatives.</p>"},{"h":"Changing vs sharing","p":"<p>One condition stops changes; another controls the licence on the changed work.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Sort the CC conditions (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Credit the creator.","bin":0,"fb":"BY means attribution."},{"t":"Share your new version under the same licence.","bin":1,"fb":"SA means share-alike."},{"t":"No money-making use.","bin":2,"fb":"NC means non-commercial."},{"t":"No changes allowed.","bin":3,"fb":"ND means no derivatives."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Each use breaks exactly ONE condition of its licence. Identify which condition is broken.</p>","main":"Which CC condition does this use break?","items":[{"t":"Posting a CC BY photo on the blog without naming the photographer.","bin":0,"fb":"Leaving out the credit breaks BY (attribution)."},{"t":"Releasing a remix of a CC BY-SA song as \"all rights reserved\".","bin":1,"fb":"A remix of SA work must use the same licence, so this breaks SA."},{"t":"Using an unedited, credited CC BY-NC photo in a paid hotel advert.","bin":2,"fb":"Credit is given and there are no edits, but a paid advert is commercial, so NC is broken."},{"t":"Adding a caption onto a credited CC BY-ND photo in a free school newsletter.","bin":3,"fb":"The use is non-commercial and credited, but adding text changes the work, breaking ND."},{"t":"Translating a credited CC BY-NC-SA article for a charity, then releasing it under plain CC BY.","bin":1,"fb":"The use is non-commercial and credited, but the translation must carry the same BY-NC-SA licence, so SA is broken."},{"t":"Cropping a credited CC BY-NC-ND photo for a free charity flyer.","bin":3,"fb":"The charity use is non-commercial and credited, but cropping is a change, so ND is broken."},{"t":"Selling T-shirts printed with an unedited, credited CC BY-NC-SA drawing.","bin":2,"fb":"Selling T-shirts is commercial use, which NC forbids."},{"t":"Sharing an unchanged CC BY-ND video in a commercial newsletter with no credit.","bin":0,"fb":"Commercial use and no changes are allowed by BY-ND, but leaving out the credit breaks BY."}],"win":"Excellent analysis. You checked each condition in turn and found the one that failed, which is how to answer \"can we use this?\" with confidence.","title":"Sort the Creative Commons conditions (Challenge)"}},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Maya's photo decision","intro":"<p>You are Maya, responsible for images on the Taste of Castries guide. The deadline is close and the brief says \"no paid stock photos\". Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>You find a stunning photo of Vigie Beach on Flickr. The licence label says <b>CC BY-NC 2.0</b>. What do you do first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Use it with a credit line; credit is all CC asks for.","to":"bad1","fb":"BY asks for credit, but this licence also has NC. You must meet every condition."},{"t":"Read what each condition means for this project before deciding.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Check the licence terms against your use before you publish anything."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>BY means credit the creator. NC means non-commercial. Is the Taste of Castries guide a commercial use?</p>","choices":[{"t":"No. It is a free blog post, so it is non-commercial.","to":"bad2","fb":"Free to read does not mean non-commercial. The purpose is to earn money for the vendors."},{"t":"Yes. The vendors paid for it and it promotes their businesses, so it is commercial.","to":"n3","fb":"Correct. Promoting paying businesses is commercial use, even on a blog."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>So the CC BY-NC photo is ruled out, and paid stock is not allowed. What now?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Take a similar photo from a Google image search; it is online, so it is free.","to":"bad3","fb":"Online does not mean free. Most images are proprietary by default."},{"t":"Shoot your own photos of the vendors and their food.","to":"n4","fb":"Yes. Your own photos solve the licence problem and show the real vendors."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Your photos show vendors and some customers whose faces are clear. The images will promote businesses. What do you do before publishing?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Publish them; the photos were taken in a public street.","to":"bad4","fb":"For commercial promotion, the professional standard is written permission from recognisable people."},{"t":"Get signed releases from the vendors and anyone recognisable, and log them with the photos.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Releases give written permission to use people's images commercially."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> The guide has original, properly licensed photos with signed releases on file. This is exactly what the Taste of Castries team did. A Grade One answer would explain each step: check the conditions, identify commercial use, create your own assets and obtain releases.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Licence breach.</b> You credited the photographer but ignored NC. The photographer complains and the agency must remove the image and apologise. Try again and check every condition.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong judgement.</b> The guide exists to bring customers to paying vendors, so it is commercial. Using an NC image breaks the licence. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Copyright problem.</b> The image belongs to a photographer who never gave permission. The agency receives a takedown notice. Try again and use assets you have the right to use.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Permission problem.</b> A customer sees her face in an advert-style guide and asks for it to be removed. Without a release, the team must re-edit everything. Try again and get releases first.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Every letter counts","p":"<p>A CC licence can carry more than one condition. Check them all.</p>"},{"h":"Who benefits?","p":"<p>If content helps a business make money, think carefully about NC.</p>"},{"h":"People in photos","p":"<p>What written document gives permission to use someone's image?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Maya's photo decision (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: you are choosing a photo for a guide that promotes paying vendors.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The photo you like is CC BY-NC. The guide promotes businesses. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Use it with a credit line.","to":"bad","fb":"NC forbids commercial use, even with credit."},{"t":"Do not use it; shoot your own photos instead.","to":"n2","fb":"Yes. Promoting businesses is commercial use, so NC rules it out."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Your photos show vendors' faces. What do you need?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Signed releases from the vendors.","to":"good","fb":"Correct. Releases give written permission."},{"t":"Nothing; it is a public street.","to":"bad","fb":"For commercial use, get written permission."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> Your photos are original and properly cleared.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Check the licence and permissions. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A harder version. You are Maya, and the Taste of Castries guide also needs a trail map, background music for the clips and a photo of the vendors at night. The brief says \"no paid stock\" and the launch is in four days.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>You find a night photo of Jeremie Street on Flickr marked <b>CC BY-SA 4.0</b>. You want to add the trail logo on top. Can you?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Yes, and the agency can then claim full copyright in the edited version.","to":"bad1","fb":"SA requires the adapted image to carry the same licence, so the agency cannot lock it up."},{"t":"Yes, with a TASL credit, as long as the edited photo is also released under CC BY-SA.","to":"n2","fb":"Correct. BY-SA allows commercial use and changes, with credit and the same licence on your version."},{"t":"No, because SA forbids any changes.","to":"ok1","fb":"That describes ND, not SA. SA allows changes but controls the licence of the result."}]},"ok1":{"text":"<p>Zara asks you to check again. What do the letters SA mean?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Single Author: only one person may use it.","to":"bad1","fb":"That is not a CC condition. Misreading licences leads to breaches."},{"t":"ShareAlike: you may adapt it, but your version must use the same licence.","to":"n2","fb":"Right. Now you can use the photo correctly."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>For the clips, Malik suggests a royalty-free track from a music library. What do you check first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"The library licence terms: whether commercial promotion and social media use are allowed, and what credit is required.","to":"n3","fb":"Good. Royalty-free describes payment, not permission for every use; the terms decide."},{"t":"Nothing. Royalty-free means copyright-free.","to":"bad2","fb":"Royalty-free tracks are still copyright works with licence terms."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The trail map uses streets from an online map service. Its terms require a visible credit and forbid removing it. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Crop the credit off because it spoils the design.","to":"bad3","fb":"Removing a required credit breaches the terms."},{"t":"Keep the required credit on the map and log the terms in the assets table.","to":"n4","fb":"Correct. Map data has licence terms too, and the credit is part of them."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Finally, your own photos show the vendors and a group of customers. One customer is a child. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Ask the child if it is okay, then publish.","to":"bad4","fb":"A child cannot give full consent; a parent or guardian must sign."},{"t":"Get signed releases from the vendors and adults, a parent's release for the child, or crop out anyone without a release.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Promotional use needs written permission, and a child needs a parent or guardian to sign."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Every asset cleared.</b> The guide launches with an adapted BY-SA photo released under the same licence, licensed music, a correctly credited map and signed releases on file. A Grade One answer explains the licence condition behind each decision.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Licence breach.</b> The photographer points out that the edited image must carry the same licence. The agency must re-license or remove it. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Claim received.</b> The library's terms excluded advertising use, and the clips are flagged. Try again and read the terms.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Terms broken.</b> The map service asks for the map to be taken down. Try again and keep required credits.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Consent problem.</b> The child's parent objects, and every clip with the child must be re-edited. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}},"title":"Maya's photo decision (Challenge)"}},{"type":"mcq","title":"Choosing the right licence","q":"Antigua Sailing Week Fan Club runs a commercial website that sells T-shirts. Keisha wants to use a photo from Wikimedia Commons on the shop page and crop it into a banner. Which licence on the photo would allow this use, as long as she credits the photographer?","options":[{"t":"CC BY 4.0","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. CC BY allows any use, including commercial use and changes such as cropping, provided the creator is credited."},{"t":"All rights reserved","fb":"All rights reserved means she would need direct permission or a paid licence from the owner."},{"t":"CC BY-NC 4.0","fb":"NC means non-commercial. A website that sells T-shirts is a commercial use, so this licence does not allow it."},{"t":"CC BY-ND 4.0","fb":"ND means no derivatives. Cropping the photo into a banner is a change, which ND does not allow."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Two conditions","p":"<p>The use is commercial AND the photo is changed. Which licence allows both?</p>"},{"h":"Decode the letters","p":"<p>NC = no commercial use. ND = no changes. BY = credit the creator.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Choosing the right licence (Foundation)","q":"Which Creative Commons element means you MUST credit the creator?","options":[{"t":"NC","fb":"NC means non-commercial."},{"t":"BY","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. BY means attribution."},{"t":"ND","fb":"ND means no derivatives (no changes)."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","q":"Keisha wants to crop a Wikimedia Commons photo into a banner for the fan club's T-shirt shop, credit the photographer, and keep the finished banner under the club's own all-rights-reserved terms. Which licence on the photo allows ALL of this?","options":[{"t":"CC BY-ND 4.0","fb":"Cropping is a change, which ND forbids."},{"t":"CC BY-SA 4.0","fb":"BY-SA allows commercial use and cropping, but the banner would have to be released under BY-SA too, not kept all rights reserved."},{"t":"CC BY 4.0","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. CC BY allows commercial use and changes with credit, and does not control the licence of the new work."},{"t":"CC BY-NC 4.0","fb":"A shop that sells T-shirts is commercial, which NC forbids."}],"title":"Choosing the right licence (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"SEO helps the right people find your content","html":"<p><b>SEO (search engine optimisation)</b> is improving content so the right people find it in search. Learn this key idea:</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Machine indexability</b>: preparing content so that it can be found, read and indexed by search engines and digital platforms.</p>\n<p>Search works in three steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Crawling</b>: automated programs (crawlers or bots) visit pages and follow links.</li>\n<li><b>Indexing</b>: the search engine stores what it read in a huge database, the index.</li>\n<li><b>Ranking</b>: when someone searches, the engine orders indexed pages by relevance and quality.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>If a crawler cannot read your content, it will not be indexed or ranked, however good it is.</p>\n<h4>Keywords</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Short-tail</b>: one or two broad words, such as \"St Lucia food\". Huge competition.</li>\n<li><b>Long-tail</b>: longer, specific phrases, such as \"where to eat near cruise port St Lucia\". Fewer searches, but the searcher knows exactly what they want.</li>\n<li><b>Local</b>: phrases with a place, such as \"street food Castries\".</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Use keywords naturally in headings and text. Never <b>keyword stuff</b> (repeating a phrase unnaturally); search engines penalise it and readers hate it.</p>\n<h4>Page essentials</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Title tag</b>: 50 to 60 characters, keyword near the front.</li>\n<li><b>Meta description</b>: 150 to 160 characters; it persuades people to click.</li>\n<li><b>Short URL slug</b>: <i>/taste-of-castries</i>, not <i>/page?id=4471</i>.</li>\n<li><b>One H1</b>, then H2 and H3 in order.</li>\n<li><b>Alt text</b> on every meaningful image.</li>\n<li><b>Internal links</b> to related pages, so crawlers and readers find more.</li>\n<li><b>Real text</b>, not words trapped inside images or scanned PDFs.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>&lt;title&gt;Taste of Castries: Street Food Near Castries Cruise Port&lt;/title&gt;\n&lt;meta name=\"description\" content=\"...\"&gt;\n&lt;h1&gt;Taste of Castries: six street-food stops near the pier&lt;/h1&gt;</code></pre>\n<p><b>Result:</b> the Taste of Castries guide reached first-page rankings for several long-tail keywords.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Do not confuse the <b>XML sitemap</b> (a file for search engines) with the <b>site map</b> you draw when planning (Unit 2.3). Examiners like this trap.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>SEO helps the right people find your content in search. Search engines crawl pages, store them in an index, then rank them. Use the words people really search for, such as \"street food Castries\", in your headings. Write a clear title tag and meta description, use a short web address, and add alt text to images.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>SEO is not a trick applied after writing; it is part of understanding the audience. Long-tail keywords convert better because the searcher's intent is clear: \"where to eat near cruise port St Lucia\" signals someone ready to act. Remember the limits of each element: a good title tag improves click-through, but a page that does not satisfy the searcher will lose ranking over time. Machine indexability is the foundation, since text trapped inside images or scanned PDFs cannot be read, however relevant. Avoid keyword stuffing, which search engines penalise and readers dislike. In answers, explain why each element helps crawling, indexing or ranking.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the SEO element to its rule","intro":"<p>Jerome is checking the Taste of Castries page before launch. Match each SEO element to its limit or rule.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Title tag","r":"50 to 60 characters, keyword near the front"},{"l":"Meta description","r":"150 to 160 characters that persuade people to click"},{"l":"URL slug","r":"Short, lower case, words joined by hyphens"},{"l":"H1","r":"Only one per page: the main heading"},{"l":"Alt text","r":"A short description of an image for screen readers and crawlers"},{"l":"Internal links","r":"Links to related pages on the same site, so crawlers find them"},{"l":"Long-tail keyword","r":"\"where to eat near cruise port St Lucia\""},{"l":"Keyword stuffing","r":"Never: repeating a phrase unnaturally, which search engines penalise"}],"win":"Very good. You know the limits for title tags (50 to 60) and meta descriptions (150 to 160), and the page essentials for machine indexability.","tutor":[{"h":"Shorter and longer","p":"<p>The headline in results is much shorter than the summary under it.</p>"},{"h":"Only one","p":"<p>Which heading level should appear just once on a page?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the SEO element (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Title tag","r":"50 to 60 characters"},{"l":"Meta description","r":"150 to 160 characters"},{"l":"H1","r":"One per page"},{"l":"Alt text","r":"Describes an image"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Jerome has spotted problems on a client page. Match each problem to the fix.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"The search result shows a cut-off headline ending in \"...\"","r":"Shorten the title tag to 50 to 60 characters with the keyword near the front"},{"l":"The snippet under the result is random text from the page footer","r":"Write a persuasive meta description of 150 to 160 characters"},{"l":"The address is /page?id=4471&ref=xx","r":"Use a short, lower-case, hyphenated slug such as /taste-of-castries"},{"l":"The page has four headings all marked as H1","r":"Keep one H1, then H2 and H3 in order"},{"l":"A screen reader announces the photos only as \"image\"","r":"Add short, meaningful alt text to each meaningful image"},{"l":"A useful vendor page has no other pages linking to it","r":"Add internal links from related pages"},{"l":"The opening paragraph repeats \"street food Castries\" eight times","r":"Remove keyword stuffing and use the phrase naturally"},{"l":"The opening hours exist only as words inside a photo of a sign","r":"Publish the hours as real text so crawlers can read them"}],"win":"Well matched. You can diagnose SEO and indexability problems and prescribe the exact fix, the skill Paper 02 tests with code and metadata questions.","title":"Match the SEO element to its rule (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Optimise images: right format, size and alt text","html":"<p>You must show \"appropriate preparation of images and multimedia content\". Large files make pages slow, use up visitors' mobile data (remember Denise on roaming) and hurt search ranking.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Factor</th><th>Good practice for the web</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Format</b></td><td><b>WebP or JPEG for photos</b> (WebP is usually smaller); <b>PNG for graphics</b> with sharp edges, text or transparency; <b>SVG for logos</b> and icons; MP4 (H.264) for video; MP3 or AAC for audio.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Dimensions</b></td><td><b>Resize to display size.</b> If the column is 800 px wide, export at about 800 px (or 1600 px for sharp high-density screens). A 6000 px phone photo is wasteful.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Resolution</b></td><td>Screens use pixel dimensions; the 300 ppi print setting does not matter on the web. Think pixels, not dpi.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>File size</b></td><td><b>Keep most images under 200 KB.</b> Export JPEG or WebP at roughly 70 to 80% quality and check visually.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>File names</b></td><td>Lower case, hyphens, descriptive: <i>johnny-cakes-front-street.webp</i>, not <i>IMG_4492.JPG</i>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Alt text</b></td><td>Describe content and purpose briefly; use empty alt (alt=\"\") for purely decorative images.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Video and audio</b></td><td>Add captions and transcripts; host long videos on YouTube or Vimeo and embed them.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Maya's gallery:</b> her first upload of vendor photos straight from the camera came to <b>38 MB</b>. After resizing each photo to display size, exporting as WebP and renaming the files, the whole gallery came to <b>under 1 MB</b>. Same photos, same quality on a phone, a fraction of the data.</p>\n<p><b>Writing good alt text</b>: say what matters in context. Weak: \"image1\" or \"photo of food\". Strong: \"Vendor frying golden bakes at a Jeremie Street stall\". Do not start with \"Image of\"; screen readers already announce it as an image.</p>\n<p><b>In GIMP</b>: Image, Scale Image (set the width in px), then File, Export As, choose a .webp or .jpg name, and set quality to about 75. Check the file size before uploading.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For Paper 02 export questions, give three facts and a reason: \"Export as WebP, 800 px wide, under 200 KB, named johnny-cakes-front-street.webp, so the page loads quickly on mobile data while staying sharp.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Big image files make pages slow and use up mobile data. Use WebP or JPEG for photos, PNG for graphics with text, and SVG for logos. Resize photos to the size they will be shown, keep most under 200 KB, and give them clear names such as johnny-cakes-front-street.webp. Add alt text that says what the picture shows.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Optimisation is a balance between <b>quality and weight</b>. For high-density phone screens, exporting at about twice the display width keeps images sharp, but beyond that extra pixels are invisible and waste data. Ppi or dpi settings do not affect how an image looks on a web page; pixel dimensions do. Alt text is context-dependent: the same photo might need \"Vendor frying bakes\" in a food guide but \"Jeremie Street stall on a busy cruise day\" in a tourism report, and purely decorative images should have empty alt so screen readers skip them. Explain format choices by the image's content: photographic, flat graphic with text, or vector logo.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Format and size choices","intro":"<p>Maya is preparing assets for the Taste of Castries page. Choose the best option each time.</p>","items":[{"text":"A colour photo of a plate of saltfish and johnny cakes.","options":["BMP","SVG","WebP or JPEG"],"answer":2,"fb":"Photos with many colours compress best as WebP or JPEG."},{"text":"The Taste of Castries logo, which must stay sharp at any size.","options":["SVG","JPEG","GIF"],"answer":0,"fb":"SVG is vector, so logos stay crisp at every size and the files are tiny."},{"text":"A trail map graphic with text labels and flat colours.","options":["MP4","JPEG at 40% quality","PNG"],"answer":2,"fb":"PNG keeps text and sharp edges clean. Low-quality JPEG would blur the labels."},{"text":"For most images on the page, aim to keep each file...","options":["at 300 ppi","around 5 MB","under 200 KB"],"answer":2,"fb":"Under 200 KB keeps pages fast on mobile data. Ppi is a print setting."},{"text":"The best file name for a photo of bakes at the Castries Market is...","options":["IMG_7781.JPG","bakes-castries-market.webp","Bakes Photo FINAL (2).webp"],"answer":1,"fb":"Lower case, hyphens and descriptive words help both SEO and organisation."},{"text":"A 6000 px wide camera photo will be shown in an 800 px column. You should...","options":["resize it to display size first","save it as PNG","upload it as it is"],"answer":0,"fb":"Resize to display size. The extra pixels are invisible but cost data and time."}],"win":"Excellent. WebP or JPEG for photos, PNG for graphics, SVG for logos, resized to display size and mostly under 200 KB.","tutor":[{"h":"Photo or graphic?","p":"<p>Many colours and soft detail, or flat colours and sharp edges?</p>"},{"h":"Think of Denise","p":"<p>What size of file suits a phone on roaming data?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Format and size (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A food photo should be saved as...","options":["SVG","WebP or JPEG"],"answer":1,"fb":"Photos compress best as WebP or JPEG."},{"text":"A logo should be saved as...","options":["JPEG","SVG"],"answer":1,"fb":"SVG stays sharp at any size."},{"text":"Most web images should be...","options":["over 5 MB","under 200 KB"],"answer":1,"fb":"Small files load fast on phones."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder asset decisions for the Taste of Castries page. Choose the BEST option.</p>","items":[{"text":"The hero photo will display at 800 px wide, and many readers use high-density phone screens. What width should Maya export?","options":["About 1600 px, then compress it","6000 px, the camera size","200 px","It does not matter"],"answer":0,"fb":"About twice the display width keeps images sharp on high-density screens without wasting data."},{"text":"A logo must sit on both light and dark backgrounds and stay sharp at every size.","options":["SVG with a transparent background","JPEG","MP3","BMP"],"answer":0,"fb":"SVG scales without blurring and can have a transparent background."},{"text":"A screenshot of the trail app with small text labels.","options":["JPEG at 30% quality","SVG","PNG","MP4"],"answer":2,"fb":"PNG keeps text and sharp edges clean; heavy JPEG compression would blur the labels."},{"text":"A decorative wave line between sections carries no information.","options":["alt=\"decorative wave\"","alt=\"\" (empty)","alt=\"image\"","No alt attribute at all"],"answer":1,"fb":"Empty alt tells screen readers to skip a purely decorative image. Missing alt can cause the file name to be read aloud."},{"text":"A 4-second looping clip of johnny cakes frying. Which format is usually far smaller for the same quality?","options":["A PNG","A BMP sequence","A short MP4 (H.264) video set to loop","An animated GIF"],"answer":2,"fb":"Modern video compression makes a short looping MP4 much smaller than a GIF of the same clip."},{"text":"A 12-minute vendor interview video for the guide.","options":["Email it to readers","Upload the raw file directly to the page","Host it on YouTube or Vimeo, embed it, and add captions","Convert it to a GIF"],"answer":2,"fb":"Hosting long video on a video platform and embedding it saves bandwidth, and captions make it accessible."}],"win":"Excellent. You chose formats, sizes and alt text by the content and the reader's device, which is how to justify media decisions.","title":"Format and size choices (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Great UX is readable, fast and accessible","html":"<p>The course outline lists three <b>user experience (UX)</b> considerations: readability, navigation and accessibility. Add one more word: <b>fast</b>.</p>\n<h4>Readable</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Body text at least <b>16 px</b>, with comfortable line spacing and a line length of roughly 50 to 75 characters.</li>\n<li>Contrast of at least <b>4.5:1</b> between text and background for normal text. Pale grey on white, or yellow on a sunny beach photo, fails.</li>\n<li>Short paragraphs, headings and lists (the web-writing rules you already know).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Fast and mobile-first</h4>\n<p>Design for the phone first, then scale up to laptop. Most Taste of Castries visitors used phones, so the team tested every page on a mid-range Android phone over mobile data, not only on office Wi-Fi.</p>\n<h4>Navigable</h4>\n<p>Clear menus in a consistent place, descriptive link text, buttons big enough to tap, and a visible way back to the trail map.</p>\n<h4>Accessible: WCAG's POUR</h4>\n<p>The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) group their rules under four principles:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Principle</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Perceivable</b></td><td>Users can see or hear the content in some form.</td><td>Alt text on photos; captions on the 30-second clips</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Operable</b></td><td>Users can use every control.</td><td>Works with a keyboard; large tap targets</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Understandable</b></td><td>Content and controls make sense.</td><td>Plain language; consistent menus; clear error messages</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Robust</b></td><td>It works across browsers and assistive technology.</td><td>Proper HTML headings and labels, so screen readers can follow</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Accessible content widens the audience.</b> St. Lucia welcomes visitors and residents who speak Kwéyòl, French and Spanish as well as English. Plain language, clear icons and captions help them, and a page built with real text can be run through a browser's translate feature. Accessibility also helps older users, people with visual or hearing impairments, and anyone reading in bright sunshine on the waterfront.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In an exam, do not just say \"make it accessible\". Name a principle and a technique: \"Perceivable: add captions to the clips so deaf visitors and people watching with the sound off get the message.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Good user experience means content is readable, fast, easy to navigate and accessible. Use text of at least 16 px with strong contrast, design for phones first, and make buttons big enough to tap. WCAG accessibility rules follow POUR: perceivable, operable, understandable and robust. Captions on a video help deaf visitors and people watching with the sound off.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Accessibility is often presented as a cost, but it usually <b>widens the audience</b>. Captions serve deaf users, viewers on a noisy ferry and second-language speakers; strong contrast serves people with low vision and anyone reading in bright Caribbean sun. POUR also helps you diagnose problems systematically: an image with no alt text fails Perceivable, a menu that needs a mouse fails Operable, unclear error messages fail Understandable, and headings faked with bold text fail Robust because screen readers cannot follow them. In exam answers, pair each principle with a specific technique and a named user who benefits.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Case study: review against the checklist","html":"<p>Stage 4 of the cycle is <b>edit and review</b>. Before Taste of Castries went live, Keisha reviewed it against a checklist in three groups. Zara signed off only when every box was ticked.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Content</th><th>Experience</th><th>Final review</th></tr>\n<tr><td>\n<b>Scope</b>: still six vendors near the port?<br>\n<b>Brief</b>: key message and tone match?<br>\n<b>Writing</b>: key message first, inverted pyramid, front-loaded headings, no jargon or repetition, clear call to action?<br>\n<b>Licensing</b>: every asset owned or licensed, TASL credits, releases on file?\n</td><td>\n<b>SEO</b>: title tag 50 to 60, meta description 150 to 160, short slug, one H1, internal links?<br>\n<b>Media</b>: right formats, resized, most under 200 KB, descriptive names, alt text?<br>\n<b>UX and accessibility</b>: 16 px text, 4.5:1 contrast, mobile-first, captions, POUR?\n</td><td>\n<b>Facts</b>: prices, hours, walking times confirmed with vendors?<br>\n<b>Names</b>: every vendor and dish spelled correctly and consistently?<br>\n<b>Proofreading</b>: spelling, grammar, links all work?<br>\n<b>The brief</b>: does it deliver what was agreed?\n</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The final review matters most for trust. If the guide says a stall opens at 10 a.m. and Denise arrives to find it closed, she will not believe anything else on the page. Keisha phoned every vendor to confirm hours the week before launch.</p>\n<h4>The result</h4>\n<p>The guide went live before 1 December. It reached <b>first-page rankings for several long-tail keywords</b>, and the measure-and-maintain stage began: Jerome tracked searches and page views, and the team updated prices each month. The analytics also showed which vendor pages were most popular, which fed the plan for the next piece of content.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\">As the team put it: \"We did not just write a post. We developed content.\"</p>\n<p>That sentence sums up the unit. Writing is one stage. Content development is the whole cycle: plan, research, create, edit and review, publish, promote, measure and maintain.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Attach a completed review checklist to your SBA product evidence. It shows the \"edit and review\" stage clearly and often catches small errors that cost marks.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Before publishing, check the content against a checklist: scope, brief, writing, licences, SEO, images, accessibility, facts, names and spelling. One wrong opening time can make readers distrust the whole page. That is why the reviewer phoned every vendor to confirm hours. After launch, keep measuring and updating the content.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A checklist works because review is where <b>trust</b> is won or lost. Small errors carry large costs: an inconsistent vendor name looks careless, and a closed stall at 10 a.m. makes the reader doubt every other fact. Effective review separates roles; the writer is the worst person to proofread their own work, so a second person checks. Review against the brief, not personal taste, so feedback stays objective. The case study's closing line captures the unit: \"We did not just write a post. We developed content.\" Writing is one stage, while development includes research, review, promotion and maintenance.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Keisha's final review","intro":"<p>It is the day before launch. Keisha is running the review checklist on the Taste of Castries page. Here are her notes.</p>","q":"Select every note that shows a problem that must be fixed before publishing.","lines":["Title tag: \"Taste of Castries: Street Food Near Castries Cruise Port\" (56 characters).","Meta description: 212 characters long.","Hero photo: 3.4 MB PNG straight from the camera.","Body text 16 px; contrast ratio 7:1.","Alt text on the bakes photo: \"image1\".","All six vendor photos shot by the team; signed releases on file.","One vendor is spelled \"Miss Lyn's\" in one section and \"Ms. Lynn's\" in another.","Only one H1 on the page; H2s for each vendor.","The last line says \"Click here\"."],"correct":[1,2,4,6,8],"win":"Well reviewed. The meta description is over 160 characters, the hero photo is the wrong format and far too large, \"image1\" is useless alt text, the vendor name is inconsistent, and \"Click here\" is vague link text.","miss":"Not quite. Check each note against the limits and rules: 50 to 60 and 150 to 160 characters, photos as WebP or JPEG under 200 KB, meaningful alt text, consistent names and descriptive links.","tutor":[{"h":"Check the numbers","p":"<p>Compare each number with the limits you learned for metadata, file size and contrast.</p>"},{"h":"Final review","p":"<p>Names must be spelled the same way every time.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Keisha's final review (Foundation)","q":"Select the notes that show a problem.","lines":["Alt text: \"image1\".","Body text 16 px.","Hero photo: 3.4 MB from the camera.","One H1 on the page."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Yes. Meaningless alt text and a huge image must be fixed.","miss":"Look for weak alt text and an oversized file."},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Keisha's review notes for a second client page, the Placencia Lagoon Kayak guide. Some problems are subtle.</p>","q":"Select every note that shows a problem that must be fixed before publishing.","lines":["Title tag: \"Placencia Lagoon Kayak Tours | Sunrise and Sunset Trips\" (55 characters).","Title tag on the booking page is identical to the home page title tag.","Meta description: 158 characters, with the main keyword and a call to action.","Hero photo: 1600 px wide WebP, 180 KB.","Decorative wave graphic has alt=\"decorative wave graphic image\".","The tour price says US$45 in the intro and US$55 in the booking section.","Captions are provided for the 30-second clip.","The heading \"Our tours\" is styled bold text inside a paragraph, not an H2.","Body text is 14 px light grey on white.","The guide photos show two guests; signed releases are on file."],"correct":[1,4,5,7,8],"win":"Thorough review. Duplicate title tags confuse search results, decorative images need empty alt, inconsistent prices destroy trust, fake headings fail screen readers, and small pale text fails readability and contrast.","miss":"Look again. The 57-character title, the 158-character description, the 180 KB WebP, the captions and the releases are all correct. The problems are subtler: duplication, alt text on a decorative image, a price conflict, a fake heading and weak text styling.","title":"Keisha's final review (Challenge)"}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Plan a blog post on a local topic","genre":"Content development plan for a 600 to 800 word blog post (Paper 02 and SBA style)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">Your task mirrors the Taste of Castries project. Choose a topic from your own territory (for example hurricane preparation, a beach or hike, a festival, a local dish, a community event). Develop the content plan for a <b>600 to 800 word blog post</b> that is useful, usable and purposeful.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write the content development plan for your blog post. Include:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>A <b>creative brief</b>: overview, measurable objective, target group, key message in one sentence, tone of voice (as pairs), deliverables, channels and timeline. Include a short <b>persona</b>. (5 marks)</li><li>The <b>structure</b>: an H1, an opening paragraph written as an inverted pyramid, at least three <b>H2</b> headings, one <b>list</b>, and a clear <b>call to action</b>. (5 marks)</li><li><b>Three optimised images</b> (file name, format, size, alt text) and <b>SEO metadata</b>: keywords, title tag (50 to 60 characters), meta description (150 to 160 characters) and URL slug. Note any licensing. (5 marks)</li></ol>","min":350,"max":750,"frame":"<p><b>(a) Creative brief</b><br>Overview: ... Objective: ... by ... Target group: ... Persona: [name], [age], [place], wants ..., has ...<br>Key message: ... Tone: ..., not ...; ..., not ... Deliverables: ... Channels and timeline: ...</p><p><b>(b) Structure</b><br>H1: ...<br>Opening (inverted pyramid): ...<br>H2: ... H2: ... H2: ...<br>List: ...<br>Call to action: ...</p><p><b>(c) Images and SEO</b><br>Image 1: file name ..., WebP, ... px, under 200 KB, alt text \"...\"<br>Keywords: short-tail ..., long-tail ..., local ...<br>Title tag: ... Meta description: ... Slug: /...</p>","checklist":["My brief has an overview, a measurable objective, a target group and a one-sentence key message.","I wrote a persona and tone of voice as pairs (\"friendly, not childish\").","My structure has one H1, an inverted-pyramid opening, at least three H2s, a list and a call to action.","I planned three images with descriptive file names, format, size and alt text.","I wrote a title tag (50 to 60 characters), a meta description (150 to 160 characters) and a short slug.","I mentioned licensing or releases for my images."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Creative brief and persona","marks":5,"look":["brief|overview","objective|goal|measur","target|audience","persona|\\d\\d,|years old","key message|message","tone|not childish|not boastful|not "],"min":4,"strong":"Your brief works as a contract: it is specific, measurable and clearly aimed at your persona.","weak":"Include an overview, a measurable objective (a number and a date), a target group with a named persona, a one-sentence key message, and tone written as pairs such as \"friendly, not childish\"."},{"criterion":"Structure and web writing","marks":5,"look":["h1","h2","inverted pyramid|key message first|most important","list|bullet","call to action|cta|book|visit|share|download|sign up","short|plain|front-load|scan"],"min":4,"strong":"Your structure is scannable and leads readers from the key message to a clear action.","weak":"Plan one H1, an opening that puts the most important information first (inverted pyramid), at least three front-loaded H2s, one list and a specific call to action."},{"criterion":"Images, SEO and licensing","marks":5,"look":["alt","webp|jpe?g|png|svg","kb|px|resiz","title tag|title","meta","slug|url|/[a-z]+-[a-z]","keyword|long-tail|local","licen|release|own photo|cc |creative commons|tasl"],"min":5,"strong":"Your images and metadata are optimised with precise, correct detail.","weak":"For each image give a descriptive file name, WebP or JPEG, a width in px, a size under 200 KB and meaningful alt text. Write a title tag of 50 to 60 characters, a meta description of 150 to 160, a short slug, and note who owns or licenses each image."}],"model":"<p><b>Topic:</b> Hurricane season preparation for families in Sint Maarten.</p>\n<p><b>(a) Creative brief</b></p>\n<p><b>Overview:</b> A blog post for the community site \"SXM Ready\" helping families prepare before the Atlantic hurricane season (1 June to 30 November). Many residents still remember Hurricane Irma in 2017, but existing advice is scattered across several sites and social media posts. <b>Measurable objective:</b> 1,000 page views and 200 checklist downloads between 1 and 30 June. <b>Target group:</b> parents and caregivers in Sint Maarten, aged about 25 to 55, mostly reading on phones.</p>\n<p><b>Persona:</b> Andre, 38, lives in Dutch Quarter with two children. <i>Wants:</i> a simple, trustworthy list he can act on this weekend. <i>Has:</i> a phone, limited free time and a tight budget.</p>\n<p><b>Key message:</b> \"A few simple steps this month will keep your family safer when a storm comes.\" <b>Tone:</b> calm, not alarming; clear, not childish; local, not preachy. <b>Deliverables:</b> one 700-word post, three photos, a one-page PDF checklist. <b>Channels:</b> the SXM Ready blog, shared on Facebook and WhatsApp groups. <b>Timeline:</b> draft 15 May, review 22 May, live 29 May, update monthly until November.</p>\n<p><b>(b) Structure</b></p>\n<p><b>H1:</b> Hurricane prep for Sint Maarten families: start this month</p>\n<p><b>Opening (inverted pyramid):</b> \"Hurricane season runs from 1 June to 30 November. Prepare now, while shops are calm and prices are normal: build a kit, make a family plan and check your home. This guide shows you how in one weekend.\"</p>\n<p><b>H2:</b> Build your emergency kit<br><b>List:</b> water (about four litres per person per day for at least three days), canned food and a manual opener, torch and spare batteries, first-aid kit and medicines, copies of ID documents in a waterproof bag, phone power bank, cash.<br><b>H2:</b> Make a family plan<br><b>H2:</b> Secure your home and yard<br><b>H2:</b> Where to get official updates (the Meteorological Department of Sint Maarten and the government's official channels)</p>\n<p><b>Call to action:</b> \"Download the one-page family checklist and share it with your neighbours on WhatsApp.\"</p>\n<p>The writing will use short paragraphs, \"you\", plain language and front-loaded headings, with no jargon such as \"storm surge inundation\" unless explained.</p>\n<p><b>(c) Images and SEO</b></p>\n<p><b>Image 1:</b> <i>hurricane-kit-water-torch.webp</i>, WebP, 1200 px wide, about 150 KB. Alt text: \"Emergency kit with bottled water, torch, batteries and first-aid box on a kitchen table\".<br><b>Image 2:</b> <i>family-plan-dutch-quarter.webp</i>, WebP, 800 px, about 110 KB. Alt text: \"Parent and two children writing a family emergency plan together\".<br><b>Image 3:</b> <i>hurricane-shutters-philipsburg-home.webp</i>, WebP, 800 px, about 120 KB. Alt text: \"Man fitting metal hurricane shutters to a house window in Philipsburg\".<br>All three photos will be shot by me with signed releases from the people shown, so no licence issues arise; the checklist icons will be SVG from a CC0 source, credited with TASL.</p>\n<p><b>Keywords:</b> short-tail \"hurricane prep\"; long-tail \"hurricane checklist for families St Maarten\"; local \"hurricane season Sint Maarten\".<br><b>Title tag (52 characters):</b> Hurricane Season Checklist for Sint Maarten Families<br><b>Meta description:</b> about 155 characters, for example: \"Hurricane season starts 1 June. Build your kit, make a family plan and secure your home in one weekend with this simple Sint Maarten checklist for parents.\"<br><b>Slug:</b> /hurricane-checklist-sxm-families<br>I will add internal links to the PDF checklist and to an earlier post on generators, and a final review will check every fact against official sources before publishing.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Examiners reward specific, applied detail. \"Add some pictures\" earns little; \"hurricane-kit-water-torch.webp, 1200 px, about 150 KB, with alt text describing the kit\" earns full marks. Count your title tag and meta description characters, and check every choice against your persona.</p>",
 "easy": {"bloom":"Apply","genre":"Content development plan: a short blog post plan (9 marks)","prompt":"<p>Choose a topic from your own territory and plan a blog post. Use the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Write</b> a short persona: name, age, where they live, what they want and what device they use. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Write</b> the key message in ONE sentence and the tone as ONE pair (\"..., not ...\"). <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Write</b> an H1 heading and TWO H2 headings. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Plan</b> ONE image: file name, format and alt text. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Write</b> a call to action. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol>","min":80,"max":250,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> My persona is [name], [age], from ... She/He wants ... and uses a ...<br><b>(b)</b> Key message: ... Tone: ..., not ...<br><b>(c)</b> H1: ... H2: ... H2: ...<br><b>(d)</b> Image: file name ..., format ..., alt text \"...\"<br><b>(e)</b> Call to action: ...</p>","checklist":["My persona has a name, age, place, need and device.","My key message is one sentence.","My tone is written as a pair.","I wrote one H1 and two H2s.","My image has a hyphenated file name, a web format and alt text.","My call to action tells readers what to do."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Persona","marks":2,"look":["\\d\\d|years old|aged","phone|android|iphone|laptop|tablet|device|want"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear persona.","weak":"Give your persona a name, an age, a place, a need and a device."},{"criterion":"Key message and tone","marks":2,"look":["key message|message","not "],"min":2,"strong":"Message and tone pair given.","weak":"Write one key message sentence and a tone pair such as \"calm, not alarming\"."},{"criterion":"Headings","marks":2,"look":["h1","h2"],"min":2,"strong":"Headings planned.","weak":"Write one H1 and two H2 headings that start with key words."},{"criterion":"Image","marks":2,"look":["\\.webp|\\.jpe?g|\\.png|webp|jpeg","alt"],"min":2,"strong":"Image planned.","weak":"Give a file name like beach-clean-up-sxm.webp and alt text that says what the photo shows."},{"criterion":"Call to action","marks":1,"look":["download|share|visit|book|sign up|join|call|register|come"],"min":1,"strong":"Clear call to action.","weak":"Tell readers exactly what to do next, e.g. \"Download the checklist\"."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> My persona is Andre, 38, from Dutch Quarter. He wants a simple hurricane checklist for his family and uses an Android phone.</p><p><b>(b)</b> Key message: A few simple steps this month will keep your family safer in a storm. Tone: calm, not alarming.</p><p><b>(c)</b> H1: Hurricane prep for Sint Maarten families. H2: Build your emergency kit. H2: Make a family plan.</p><p><b>(d)</b> Image: file name hurricane-kit-water-torch.webp, format WebP, alt text \"Emergency kit with bottled water, a torch and a first-aid box\".</p><p><b>(e)</b> Call to action: Download the family checklist and share it with your neighbours.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Keep every answer specific. Name a real place, give the persona a real need, and start headings with the key words. One clear sentence per part is enough at this level.</p>","title":"Plan a blog post on a local topic (Foundation)"},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Content development plan with evaluation and maintenance, Grade One extension (20 marks)","prompt":"<p>Develop the full plan for your 600 to 800 word local blog post, then show how you would judge and maintain it. Include:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>A <b>creative brief</b> with TWO personas (a visitor and a resident), a measurable objective, a one-sentence key message and tone pairs, and <b>explain</b> one way the second persona changes your content. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>The <b>structure</b>: an H1, an inverted-pyramid opening written in full, at least three front-loaded H2s, one list and a call to action. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Three optimised images</b> with licensing, and <b>SEO metadata</b>: keywords (short-tail, long-tail, local), title tag with its character count, meta description and slug. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>An <b>evaluation</b> of your plan using the useful, usable and purposeful tests, naming its greatest weakness. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>A <b>measure and maintain</b> plan: what you will track, when, and one change the data might trigger. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":450,"max":900,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Objective: ... Persona 1: ... Persona 2: ... Key message: ... Tone: ..., not ...; ..., not ... Because of persona 2, I will also ...<br><b>(b)</b> H1: ... Opening: ... H2: ... H2: ... H2: ... List: ... Call to action: ...<br><b>(c)</b> Image 1: ... Keywords: ... Title tag (.. characters): ... Meta description: ... Slug: /...<br><b>(d)</b> Useful: ... Usable: ... Purposeful: ... The greatest weakness is ... because ...<br><b>(e)</b> I will track ... every ... If the data shows ..., I will ...</p>","checklist":["My brief has two personas, a measurable objective, a one-sentence key message and tone pairs.","I explained how the second persona changes the content.","My opening is written in full as an inverted pyramid.","Each image has a file name, format, size, alt text and licensing note.","I gave three keyword types, a counted title tag, a meta description and a slug.","I evaluated the plan with all three tests and named its greatest weakness.","My maintenance plan says what, when and what change the data might trigger."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Brief with two personas","marks":5,"look":["objective|goal","persona","visitor|tourist|cruise","resident|local|parent|student","key message","not "],"min":5,"strong":"A complete brief with two personas and a clear effect on the content.","weak":"Include a measurable objective, a visitor persona and a resident persona, a one-sentence key message and tone pairs, then explain one change the second persona causes."},{"criterion":"Structure","marks":4,"look":["h1","h2","list|bullet","call to action|download|book|share|visit"],"min":4,"strong":"A scannable structure with a full inverted-pyramid opening.","weak":"Plan an H1, a full opening paragraph with the key point first, three front-loaded H2s, a list and a call to action."},{"criterion":"Images, licensing and SEO","marks":5,"look":["webp|jpe?g","alt","kb","licen|release|cc0|cc by|own photo","long-tail","title tag","meta description","slug|/[a-z]+-"],"min":6,"strong":"Precise, correct image and SEO detail with licensing.","weak":"Give file names, formats, sizes, alt text and licensing for each image, plus three keyword types, a counted title tag, a meta description and a slug."},{"criterion":"Evaluation with the three tests","marks":3,"look":["useful","usable","purposeful","weakness|weakest|risk|limitation"],"min":4,"strong":"A balanced evaluation that names the greatest weakness.","weak":"Judge the plan against useful, usable and purposeful, and name the weakness that matters most, with a reason."},{"criterion":"Measure and maintain","marks":3,"look":["track|measure|analytics|page views|downloads|clicks","week|month|every|after","update|change|add|rewrite|retire"],"min":3,"strong":"A specific, data-driven maintenance plan.","weak":"Say what you will track, how often, and one change the data might trigger."}],"model":"<p><b>Topic:</b> The Little Bay to Fort Amsterdam walk, Philipsburg.</p>\n<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Objective:</b> 1,500 page views and 120 clicks on the \"Get directions\" button between 1 December and 31 January. <b>Persona 1 (visitor):</b> Denise, 42, from Atlanta, five hours ashore, roaming data on an iPhone; wants a short, safe walk with a view and to be back on the ship on time. <b>Persona 2 (resident):</b> Shanice, 19, a Sandbox Tree College student in Philipsburg on a mid-range Android phone; wants a free weekend activity with friends and great photos for Instagram. <b>Key message:</b> \"A 40-minute walk from Philipsburg gives you Sint Maarten's best harbour view and its colonial history.\" <b>Tone:</b> friendly, not childish; confident, not boastful; local, not stereotyped. Because of Shanice, I will also add a section on the best sunset photo spots and the time of the last light, which visitors rarely need but residents value.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>H1:</b> Fort Amsterdam walk: Philipsburg's best harbour view in 40 minutes.<br><b>Opening:</b> \"Fort Amsterdam is about a 40-minute walk from Philipsburg, with sweeping views of Great Bay and the cruise pier. It is free to visit and the walk suits most fitness levels. Wear good shoes, bring water, and allow 90 minutes there and back.\"<br><b>H2:</b> Route from the Boardwalk, step by step. <b>H2:</b> What to see at the fort. <b>H2:</b> Best photo spots and sunset times. <b>H2:</b> Safety, shade and what to bring.<br><b>List:</b> water, sun hat, sunscreen, closed shoes, a charged phone.<br><b>Call to action:</b> \"Tap Get directions to start the walk from the Boardwalk.\"</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> <b>Image 1:</b> fort-amsterdam-cannons-great-bay.webp, WebP, 1600 px, about 170 KB, alt text \"Old cannons at Fort Amsterdam overlooking Great Bay and the cruise pier\". <b>Image 2:</b> little-bay-path-start.webp, WebP, 800 px, about 110 KB, alt text \"Paved path start near Little Bay with a sign to Fort Amsterdam\". <b>Image 3:</b> sunset-fort-amsterdam-friends.webp, WebP, 800 px, about 120 KB, alt text \"Three friends photographing the sunset from the fort walls\". I will shoot all three myself and get signed releases from the three friends; the map icon is CC0 and logged with TASL details. <b>Keywords:</b> short-tail \"Fort Amsterdam\"; long-tail \"walk from Philipsburg cruise port to Fort Amsterdam\"; local \"hike Philipsburg St Maarten\". <b>Title tag (52 characters):</b> Fort Amsterdam Walk from Philipsburg: Views and Tips. <b>Meta description (157 characters):</b> \"Walk from Philipsburg to Fort Amsterdam in 40 minutes. See Great Bay views, the old cannons and the best sunset spots, with a free route map and safety tips.\" <b>Slug:</b> /fort-amsterdam-walk-philipsburg</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> <b>Useful:</b> the plan answers both personas' real needs, timing for Denise and photo spots for Shanice. <b>Usable:</b> front-loaded H2s, small WebP images and a mobile-first layout suit phones on roaming data. <b>Purposeful:</b> the \"Get directions\" button turns reading into walking. The greatest weakness is safety information: walking times and path conditions can change after heavy rain, so an out-of-date warning could put a visitor at risk and damage trust, which is why the facts must be checked before launch.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> I will track page views, the \"Get directions\" clicks and search terms every two weeks in the analytics dashboard, and re-walk the route once a month to confirm times and conditions. If the data shows many searches for \"Fort Amsterdam opening hours\", I will add a clear hours box near the top; if clicks are low, I will move the call to action higher and test a new button label.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>The extension marks are in parts (d) and (e). \"Evaluate\" means judging your own plan honestly and naming a real weakness, not only praising it. For maintenance, be specific about what you will measure, how often, and the decision the data could change.</p>","title":"Plan a blog post on a local topic (Challenge)"}}]},{"id":"M2U5","title":"Tools and techniques for digital content development","objectives":["Distinguish tools (the hardware and software you use to create) from technique (the methods you apply to capture, edit and tell the story), and make every deliverable sized, formatted, accessible and fit for its platform.","Select cameras and capture devices (mobile phones, handheld, tablet and mounted devices, plus microphones) for web content.","Apply shooting principles for the web: rule of thirds, composition, framing, depth of field, camera angles and lighting.","Compare open-source (GIMP, Inkscape, Audacity, Kdenlive, OBS, Blender), industry-standard (Adobe suite, Final Cut Pro, DaVinci Resolve) and cloud-based tools (Canva, CapCut, Figma, WeVideo, Google Workspace), and plan for disruption by editing locally and backing up to the cloud.","Apply best practice in photo editing (protect, crop and correct, prepare and export; adding text, changing backgrounds, colour correction) and audio editing (clean recording, multiple tracks, noise reduction, mastering), including creating a podcast step by step.","Apply the production techniques: the four stages of launching a podcast (concept, equipment, record and edit, publish and promote) and producing an audio slideshow with voice-first or storyboard-first workflows.","Edit video for the web (hook, purposeful cuts, J-cuts and L-cuts), prepare it with captions and accessibility features, apply navigation such as breadcrumbs and YouTube chapters, and export in suitable web formats."],"videos":[{"title":"Rule of thirds and camera angles","q":"rule of thirds camera angles explained phone photography beginners"},{"title":"GIMP: remove background and add text","q":"GIMP tutorial remove background add text beginners"},{"title":"Audacity noise reduction and multitrack","q":"Audacity noise reduction tutorial podcast multitrack music fade"},{"title":"How to start a podcast on Spotify for Creators","q":"how to start a podcast spotify for creators upload episode"},{"title":"J-cuts and L-cuts explained","q":"J cut L cut video editing explained beginners"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Great content needs tools and technique","html":"<p>The message is simple: <b>choose the tools, shape the story, prepare it for the screen.</b> This unit covers four areas of practical production: <b>photography, audio, video and web publishing</b>. In each one you need two things.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Tools</th><th>Technique</th></tr>\n<tr><td>The <b>hardware and software</b> you use to create: a phone, a mirrorless camera, a USB microphone, GIMP, Audacity, Kdenlive, Canva.</td><td>The <b>methods</b> you apply to capture, edit and tell the story: depth of field, camera angles, room tone, ducking, J-cuts, captions.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Answers the question: <i>What am I using?</i></td><td>Answers the question: <i>How am I using it, and why?</i></td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>An expensive camera does not fix a badly framed shot, and a free tool such as GIMP can produce a professional result in skilled hands. Examiners reward students who explain the <b>technique</b> and its <b>effect</b>, not only the name of the tool.</p>\n<p><b>Following Island Pulse Media.</b> Throughout this unit you follow Island Pulse Media, a regional digital agency, on two jobs:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Antigua Sailing Week</b> in English Harbour: Malik shoots fast-moving yachts, crews and the evening parties, and edits vertical promos for Instagram and TikTok.</li>\n<li><b>The Chez Corail restaurant in English Harbour</b>: Zara and Keisha produce food photos, a short podcast with the chef and a menu promo for the restaurant's website.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Four checks.</b> Every deliverable must be:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Sized</b>: the right pixel dimensions and a small file size, so it loads quickly on mobile data.</li>\n<li><b>Formatted</b>: the right file type (JPEG, WebP, PNG, MP3, MP4) and aspect ratio (9:16, 16:9, 4:5, 1:1).</li>\n<li><b>Accessible</b>: captions, transcripts, alt text and strong contrast, so everyone can use it.</li>\n<li><b>Fit for its platform</b>: a website hero image, an Instagram post and a YouTube video each need different settings.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The closing rule for the whole topic: <b>match the tool, technique and export to the task, budget and platform.</b></p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> If Paper 02 asks you to \"distinguish between\" a tool and a technique, give a definition of each and a paired example: \"GIMP is a tool; colour correction using Levels is a technique applied in that tool.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Making good content needs tools and technique. Tools are what you use, such as a phone, a microphone or GIMP. Technique is how you use them, such as framing a shot or cutting out background noise. Every finished file must be the right size, the right format, accessible and fit for its platform, like a vertical clip for a Regatta Reel.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The tool-versus-technique distinction matters because examiners reward <b>reasoning</b>, not shopping lists. Saying \"use Photoshop\" earns little; saying \"use Levels to brighten the shadows so the dish looks appetising\" shows understanding that works in any software. It also has a practical side for Caribbean creators, because skilled technique in free tools such as GIMP, Audacity and Kdenlive can match expensive software, which keeps costs low for small clients. The four checks (sized, formatted, accessible, fit for platform) are a ready-made evaluation frame. Use them to judge any deliverable and to explain why one export setting is better than another.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Cameras and capture devices for web content","html":"<p>There are four groups of cameras: <b>mobile phones, handheld, tablets and mounted devices</b>. Digital capture and production tools also include <b>microphones and recording equipment</b>. The best camera is the one that suits the content, the audience's platform and your budget.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Device</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Limitations</th><th>Good for</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Mobile phone</b></td><td>Always with you, good sensors, 4K video, portrait mode, instant upload</td><td>Small sensor struggles in low light; digital zoom loses quality; storage and battery limits</td><td>Reels, TikToks, quick event coverage, vlogs</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Handheld</b> (DSLR, mirrorless, compact, action camera)</td><td>Larger sensors, interchangeable lenses, optical zoom, manual control</td><td>Cost, weight, more skill needed</td><td>Product photos, portraits, short films, Carnival parade coverage</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Tablet</b></td><td>Large preview screen, easy editing on the same device</td><td>Awkward to hold steady; cameras often weaker than phones</td><td>Classroom recording, quick interviews, scanning documents</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Mounted</b> (tripod, gimbal, webcam, helmet or car mount, drone)</td><td>Steady shots, hands-free, repeatable framing</td><td>Setup time; drones need permission</td><td>Livestreams, interviews, time-lapses, podcasts on video</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Audio matters as much as image.</b> Viewers forgive slightly soft video but click away from bad sound. A cheap <b>lavalier (clip-on) microphone</b> or a <b>USB microphone</b> greatly improves voice quality. Record somewhere quiet and soft (a room with curtains or a closet of clothes), away from air conditioners, traffic on the Bush Road and wind on the beach. Use a foam windscreen outdoors.</p>\n<p><b>Shooting for the web</b>: decide the <b>aspect ratio</b> before you press record (9:16 vertical for Reels, TikTok and Shorts; 16:9 for YouTube and websites; 1:1 or 4:5 for feed posts). Shoot in good light, keep clips short, and record a few seconds before and after each action to give the editor room.</p>\n<p>Malik at Island Pulse Media keeps a \"phone kit\" for quick jobs: phone, small tripod with a clamp, lavalier mic and a pocket LED light. For the St. John's cruise promo he adds a mirrorless camera and a gimbal. If you plan to fly a drone, check local aviation rules first, especially near V. C. Bird International Airport.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 02 may give a scenario and ask you to \"justify\" a device. Link a feature to a need: \"A tripod-mounted phone, because the interview is long and the framing must stay steady.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>There are four groups of cameras: mobile phones, handheld cameras such as mirrorless or DSLR, tablets, and mounted devices such as tripods, gimbals and drones. Pick the one that suits the job. Good sound matters too, so a cheap clip-on microphone helps a lot. A phone on a tripod with a clip-on mic suits a long Instagram Live interview.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Device choice is a trade-off between <b>image quality, speed and practicality</b>. Larger sensors handle low light and give natural background blur, which matters for restaurant interiors; phones win on speed, vertical video and instant upload. The deciding factor is often not the camera but support and sound: a steady mount and a lavalier mic improve a video more than a camera upgrade. Plan the aspect ratio before recording, because cropping 16:9 footage into 9:16 later throws away most of the frame. Drones add legal duties, especially near V. C. Bird International Airport, so permission is part of choosing the device.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Choose the capture device","intro":"<p>Malik and Keisha are planning shoots for clients across the region. Choose the best option each time.</p>","items":[{"text":"A 40-minute Instagram Live interview with a Trinidad soca artist. Which set-up?","options":["phone on a tripod with a lavalier mic","tablet held by a friend","handheld phone, no stand"],"answer":0,"fb":"Long recordings need steady, hands-free framing and clear voice. A tripod and clip-on mic solve both."},{"text":"Close-up product photos of handmade jewellery for an online shop in St. John's, with a blurred background.","options":["drone","mirrorless camera with a wide-aperture lens","webcam"],"answer":1,"fb":"A wide aperture gives shallow depth of field, blurring the background so the product stands out."},{"text":"A smooth walking tour of Redcliffe Quay filmed while moving.","options":["tablet held at arm's length","phone on a gimbal","fixed webcam"],"answer":1,"fb":"A gimbal stabilises movement so walking shots do not shake."},{"text":"The client wants a quick TikTok from a Dickenson Bay beach fete tonight.","options":["mobile phone shooting vertical 9:16","mounted CCTV camera","DSLR on a heavy tripod"],"answer":0,"fb":"Phones are fast, light and already set up for vertical short video and instant upload."},{"text":"Recording a podcast voice track at home. The biggest quality gain comes from...","options":["a USB or lavalier microphone in a quiet room","a brighter light","a 4K camera"],"answer":0,"fb":"For audio-only content, the microphone and the room matter most."}],"win":"Excellent. You matched devices to the task, the platform and the shooting conditions, which is what \"justify\" questions reward.","tutor":[{"h":"Steady or moving?","p":"<p>Tripods keep still shots steady; gimbals smooth moving shots.</p>"},{"h":"Sound first","p":"<p>For speech and podcasts, think microphone before camera.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Choose the device (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A smooth walking video needs...","options":["a webcam","a gimbal"],"answer":1,"fb":"A gimbal stabilises movement."},{"text":"A quick vertical TikTok tonight needs...","options":["a heavy DSLR rig","a mobile phone"],"answer":1,"fb":"Phones are fast and shoot vertical easily."},{"text":"Clear podcast voice needs...","options":["a 4K camera","a good microphone"],"answer":1,"fb":"Audio quality comes from the mic and the room."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder shoots with competing constraints. Choose the BEST set-up or action.</p>","items":[{"text":"A candlelit dinner at an English Harbour restaurant. The client also wants the photos for a large printed menu later.","options":["A phone using digital zoom","A webcam","A mirrorless camera with a wide-aperture lens on a small tripod","A tablet held at arm's length"],"answer":2,"fb":"A larger sensor and wide aperture handle low light, and the tripod keeps slow exposures sharp, giving quality for print as well as web."},{"text":"Interviewing a fisherman on a windy beach at Half Moon Bay for a podcast clip.","options":["The phone's built-in mic from two metres away","A lavalier mic with a foam windscreen, close to his chest","A drone","A louder voice"],"answer":1,"fb":"A close clip-on mic with wind protection captures clear speech outdoors."},{"text":"Malik wants a drone shot of the fleet near V. C. Bird International Airport. What must happen FIRST?","options":["Shoot at noon","Use portrait mode","Fly low so nobody notices","Check aviation rules and obtain permission before flying"],"answer":3,"fb":"Airspace near an airport is controlled, so rules and permission come before any flight."},{"text":"A one-hour time-lapse of the fleet crossing Falmouth Harbour, with identical framing throughout.","options":["A gimbal while walking","A camera mounted on a sturdy tripod","A tablet on a table","A phone held by hand"],"answer":1,"fb":"A time-lapse needs a fixed, repeatable frame, so a sturdy tripod mount is essential."},{"text":"A reporter must shoot, edit and post a vertical clip from the pier within 15 minutes.","options":["A film camera","A mobile phone shooting 9:16 and a phone editing app","A DSLR and a laptop editing suite","A mounted CCTV camera"],"answer":1,"fb":"Speed and vertical output make the phone the best tool for fast social coverage."},{"text":"Filming from a speedboat bouncing across the waves during the Regatta.","options":["A stabilised action camera or a phone on a gimbal, securely mounted","A tripod on the deck","A heavy studio camera","A tablet held overhead"],"answer":0,"fb":"Stabilisation and a secure mount cope with motion and spray. A tripod on a moving boat does not stop the shake."}],"win":"Strong decisions. You weighed light, sound, motion, speed and legal duties, not just picture quality.","title":"Choose the capture device (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Depth of field and angles shape the feeling","html":"<p>The shooting principles are <b>rule of thirds, composition, depth and angles</b>, plus <b>framing and lighting</b>. To sum it up: <b>depth of field and angles shape the feeling</b>. You met the rule of thirds for layouts in Unit 2.3; here you apply it through the lens.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Shallow focus: f/1.8</th><th>Deep focus: f/11</th></tr>\n<tr><td>A <b>wide aperture</b>, a <b>closer subject</b> and a <b>longer lens</b> isolate detail. The background melts into soft, round blur called <b>bokeh</b>.</td><td>A <b>narrow aperture</b> keeps more of the scene sharp, from the foreground to the horizon.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Use it for: a plate of grilled snapper at Chez Corail in English Harbour; a Sailing Week skipper's face with the crowd blurred.</td><td>Use it for: an <b>establishing shot</b> or landscape, such as the whole Sailing Week fleet off English Harbour.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Angles and feeling</b> (three rules): <b>low angle → powerful</b>; <b>high angle → vulnerable</b>; <b>eye level → honest</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Rule of thirds</b>: turn on the grid in your phone's camera settings. Place the subject on a line or intersection; put the horizon on the top or bottom line, not through the middle. Leave <b>lead room</b> (space in front of a person looking or walking).</li>\n<li><b>Composition and framing</b>: decide what is in and out of the frame. Use <b>natural frames</b> (a doorway at Nelson's Dockyard, palm fronds), <b>leading lines</b> (a pier, a road, the boardwalk) that draw the eye to the subject, and a clean background. Check the edges for clutter.</li>\n<li><b>Depth of field</b>: how much of the image is in sharp focus. On a phone, <b>portrait mode</b> imitates shallow focus.</li>\n<li><b>Lighting</b>: <b>golden hour</b> (shortly after sunrise or before sunset) gives soft, warm light. <b>Avoid harsh midday sun</b>: it gives hard shadows under eyes and blown-out white sails. If you must shoot at noon, move into open shade or use a reflector.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Shot or angle</th><th>Effect</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Extreme wide / establishing shot</b></td><td>Sets the scene: the whole of English Harbour seen from Shirley Heights</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Medium shot</b></td><td>Waist up; natural for interviews and vlogs</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Close-up</b></td><td>Shows emotion or detail: hands shaping johnny cakes</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Eye level</b></td><td>Honest, neutral, relatable: the chef talking to camera</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Low angle</b> (camera looks up)</td><td>Powerful: a winning crew lifting the trophy</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>High angle</b> (camera looks down)</td><td>Vulnerable or small: a lone dinghy in rough water; also good for flat-lay food shots</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Over-the-shoulder</b></td><td>Places the viewer in a conversation</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Bird's-eye view</b></td><td>Directly overhead, often from a drone: patterns of boats in Falmouth Harbour</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When describing a shot plan, always pair the technique with its <b>effect</b>: \"a low-angle shot to make the winning skipper look powerful\" or \"f/1.8 so the dish is sharp and the dining room becomes soft bokeh\". Naming a technique without its purpose earns only half the marks.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Camera choices change how people feel about a picture. Shallow focus blurs the background so the subject stands out; deep focus keeps everything sharp. A low angle makes someone look powerful, a high angle makes them look small, and eye level feels honest. Golden hour light near sunrise or sunset is soft and warm, perfect for sails off English Harbour.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Every technique is a <b>decision about attention and meaning</b>. Shallow focus tells the viewer what matters by removing everything else, while deep focus says \"the whole scene matters\", as in an establishing shot. Angles carry social meaning: a low angle on a winning crew celebrates them, but the same angle on a politician can feel like propaganda. Rules can be broken deliberately: a centred, symmetrical frame suits a formal portrait or a memorial. Light is the factor beginners neglect most; harsh noon sun blows out white sails and darkens eyes, so plan shoots around the light or use open shade and reflectors. Always explain the effect.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the technique to the feeling","intro":"<p>Malik is shooting Antigua Sailing Week and Zara is shooting dishes at Chez Corail in English Harbour. Match each technique to the feeling or effect it creates.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Low angle","r":"The winning crew looks powerful as they lift the trophy"},{"l":"High angle","r":"A small dinghy in choppy water looks vulnerable"},{"l":"Eye level","r":"The chef's interview feels honest and relatable"},{"l":"Shallow focus at f/1.8","r":"The plate is sharp and the dining room melts into bokeh"},{"l":"Deep focus at f/11","r":"The whole fleet off English Harbour stays sharp in an establishing shot"},{"l":"Golden hour light","r":"Soft, warm light on the sails just before sunset"},{"l":"Rule of thirds","r":"The skipper sits on the left grid line, with space on the right for text"}],"win":"Very good. You linked each technique to the feeling it creates: low is powerful, high is vulnerable, eye level is honest, and aperture controls how much is sharp.","tutor":[{"h":"Up or down?","p":"<p>Looking up at someone makes them seem bigger. Looking down makes them seem smaller.</p>"},{"h":"f-numbers","p":"<p>A small f-number means a wide opening and a blurred background. A big f-number keeps more in focus.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the angle to the feeling (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Low angle","r":"Powerful"},{"l":"High angle","r":"Vulnerable"},{"l":"Eye level","r":"Honest"},{"l":"Shallow focus at f/1.8","r":"Blurred background (bokeh)"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Malik and Zara know the effect they want. Match each goal to the technique that achieves it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Make the winning crew look powerful as they lift the trophy","r":"Low angle"},{"l":"Show the patterns of moored boats in Falmouth Harbour from directly above","r":"Bird's-eye view"},{"l":"Place the viewer inside a conversation between skipper and crew","r":"Over-the-shoulder shot"},{"l":"Keep the snapper dish sharp while the dining room melts away","r":"Shallow focus at f/1.8"},{"l":"Keep the fleet and the whole coastline sharp","r":"Deep focus at f/11"},{"l":"Draw the eye along the dockyard path to a vendor's stall","r":"Leading lines"},{"l":"Frame the chef through the restaurant's arched doorway","r":"A natural frame"},{"l":"Give a walking jogger space to move into on the right of the frame","r":"Lead room"},{"l":"Avoid hard shadows under the skipper's eyes","r":"Golden hour light or open shade"}],"win":"Well matched. You started from the effect and chose the technique, which is how a professional plans a shot list.","title":"Match the technique to the feeling (Challenge)"}},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Shooting Antigua Sailing Week","intro":"<p>You are assisting Malik from the Island Pulse Media at Antigua Sailing Week in English Harbour. The brief: three hero photos for a website gallery and Instagram. Make the best camera decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Shot 1 is a portrait of a young local skipper on the dock. Behind her are crowds, ropes and parked cars. Which setting do you choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Deep focus at f/11 so every rope and car is sharp.","to":"bad1","fb":"Deep focus keeps the clutter sharp, so it competes with the skipper."},{"t":"Shallow focus: about f/1.8, step closer and zoom in a little so the background turns to bokeh.","to":"n2","fb":"Right. A wide aperture, a closer subject and a longer lens isolate her from the cluttered dock."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Shot 2 is an establishing shot of the whole fleet crossing the bay from Shirley Heights above English Harbour. What do you choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Deep focus around f/11 so the boats and the coastline are all sharp.","to":"n3","fb":"Yes. A narrow aperture keeps more of a landscape or establishing shot in focus."},{"t":"f/1.8 so one boat is sharp and the rest are blurred.","to":"bad2","fb":"An establishing shot must show the whole scene clearly; shallow focus hides it."},{"t":"Portrait mode on the phone.","to":"bad2","fb":"Portrait mode blurs backgrounds, which is the opposite of what a wide fleet shot needs."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Shot 3 celebrates the winning crew. It is 12 noon with harsh, overhead sun. The prize-giving is at 5.30 p.m. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Wait for the prize-giving in the golden hour and shoot from a low angle as they lift the trophy.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Golden hour gives soft light and a low angle makes the crew look powerful."},{"t":"Shoot now from a high angle, looking down at the crew.","to":"bad3","fb":"Midday sun is harsh, and a high angle makes winners look small and vulnerable."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Gallery approved.</b> The skipper stands out against soft bokeh, the fleet shot sets the scene, and the winning crew looks powerful in warm light. A Grade One answer justifies each setting by its effect.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Cluttered portrait.</b> Cars and ropes pull the eye away from the skipper. Try again: open the aperture and get closer.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>No sense of place.</b> Viewers cannot see the fleet or the bay. Try again: an establishing shot needs deep focus.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong feeling.</b> Hard shadows hide their faces and the high angle shrinks the champions. Try again with better light and a stronger angle.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Portrait or landscape?","p":"<p>Do you want to isolate one person, or show everything?</p>"},{"h":"Time of day","p":"<p>Which part of the day gives soft, warm light?</p>"},{"h":"Winners","p":"<p>Which angle makes a subject look powerful?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Shooting the Regatta (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: two quick decisions at the Regatta.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>You want a skipper's face sharp and the busy dock blurred. Which setting?</p>","choices":[{"t":"f/1.8 (shallow focus)","to":"n2","fb":"Yes. A wide aperture blurs the background."},{"t":"f/11 (deep focus)","to":"bad","fb":"That keeps the busy dock sharp."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>When should you shoot the winners for soft light?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Midday sun","to":"bad","fb":"Midday sun is harsh."},{"t":"Golden hour, near sunset","to":"good","fb":"Yes. Golden hour light is soft and warm."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> Your settings match the feeling you wanted.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Think about how much should be sharp and what light flatters people. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A harder Regatta brief: a 16:9 website hero image with space for a headline, an Instagram portrait post, and a crew photo, all in one day with changing light.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The website hero must show a yacht and leave room for the headline \"Race the island\". It is 10 a.m. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Centre the yacht in a square frame and crop it later.","to":"ok1","fb":"Cropping a square into 16:9 loses the top and bottom and may leave no space for text."},{"t":"Shoot 16:9 with the grid on, place the yacht on the left third with lead room to the right for the headline.","to":"n2","fb":"Excellent. The rule of thirds plus lead room creates space for text and suggests movement."},{"t":"Zoom in digitally on the phone for a tight shot.","to":"bad1","fb":"Digital zoom reduces quality and leaves no space for the headline."}]},"ok1":{"text":"<p>The crop leaves the yacht cramped with no room for the headline. What now?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Reshoot in 16:9 with the yacht on a third and space for text.","to":"n2","fb":"Good recovery. Plan the aspect ratio and the text space before shooting."},{"t":"Put the headline over the yacht's sail anyway.","to":"bad1","fb":"Text over a busy subject is hard to read and hides the subject."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>At noon the client wants the skipper portrait now. The sun is overhead and harsh. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Shoot in direct sun at f/11.","to":"bad2","fb":"Hard shadows under her eyes, and deep focus keeps the cluttered dock sharp."},{"t":"Move her into the open shade of the clubhouse, or use a reflector, and shoot at a wide aperture for soft background blur.","to":"n3","fb":"Correct. Open shade or a reflector softens hard light, and shallow focus isolates her."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The prize-giving is at 5.30 p.m. You want the crew to look like champions. Which shot do you plan?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A low angle in golden-hour light as they lift the trophy, with the bay behind them.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. A low angle gives power and golden-hour light gives warmth."},{"t":"An eye-level shot from far back with the whole crowd included.","to":"ok2","fb":"It records the moment honestly, but the crew is small in the frame and loses impact."},{"t":"A high angle from the stage looking down on them.","to":"bad3","fb":"A high angle makes the winners look small, the opposite of the brief."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>All three approved.</b> The hero image has space for the headline, the portrait is soft and flattering despite midday sun, and the crew looks powerful at sunset. A Grade One answer justifies each choice by its effect and by the platform.</p>","end":"good"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Usable, but flat.</b> The client uses the shot but wanted the crew to dominate the frame. Try again with an angle that adds power.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Unusable hero.</b> There is no room for the headline and the quality is poor. Try again: plan the aspect ratio and the text space.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Unflattering portrait.</b> Harsh shadows and a cluttered background. Try again: soften the light and isolate the subject.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong feeling.</b> The champions look small. Try again with a low angle.</p>","end":"bad"}},"title":"Shooting the Heineken Regatta (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Open-source, industry-standard or cloud: match software to the task","html":"<p>You can use open-source or industry-standard software, plus cloud-based software, to <b>capture, create, edit, store and publish</b> content. The rule: <b>match software to the task</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Open-source</b>: free tools for image, audio, video and 3D work, with public source code anyone can study and improve. Examples: <b>GIMP</b> (photo editing), <b>Inkscape</b> (vector), <b>Audacity</b> (audio), <b>Kdenlive</b> (video editing), <b>OBS</b> (OBS Studio, for screen recording and livestreaming) and <b>Blender</b> (3D and animation).</li>\n<li><b>Industry-standard</b>: what most professional studios use, usually proprietary and paid. Examples: the <b>Adobe suite</b> (Photoshop, Illustrator, Audition, Premiere Pro, After Effects), <b>Final Cut Pro</b> and <b>DaVinci Resolve</b>. Consider <b>support, file compatibility and licensing</b> before choosing.</li>\n<li><b>Cloud</b>: tools that run in a browser or app and store work online: <b>Canva</b>, <b>CapCut</b>, <b>Figma</b> and <b>WeVideo</b>, plus <b>Google Workspace</b> for scripts and shared folders. They allow <b>real-time teamwork</b>, but online collaboration needs a connection.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Open-source</th><th>Industry-standard</th><th>Cloud-based</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cost</td><td>Free</td><td>Subscription (education discounts exist)</td><td>Free tiers; paid plans remove limits or watermarks</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Power</td><td>Strong, some gaps</td><td>Most advanced features</td><td>Simpler, template-based</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Learning curve</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Steep</td><td>Easy</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Internet needed?</td><td>No, installed locally</td><td>Mostly offline after install</td><td>Yes, for most features</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Collaboration</td><td>Share files manually</td><td>Some cloud sharing</td><td>Real-time co-editing and comments</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Job market</td><td>Growing</td><td>Expected in many studios</td><td>Common for social media roles</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Island Pulse Media uses all three: Zara designs in Adobe Illustrator, Malik edits the Regatta promos in DaVinci Resolve, Keisha makes quick social posts in Canva and CapCut, and interns learn on GIMP and Audacity.</p>\n<p><b>Edit locally, back up to the cloud.</b> It is best to edit on the computer's own drive (faster, and it works when the Internet drops) and back up to a cloud drive such as Google Drive or OneDrive. <b>Hurricane Irma</b> in 2017 is a reminder to plan for disruption: power and Internet were cut across St. Maarten for long periods, and work stored only on one laptop, or only online, was at risk. A copy in two places protects the project.</p>\n<p>Remember that Paper 02 has <b>no Internet</b>, and the equipment list names GIMP, Paint.NET or Photoshop and Inkscape or Illustrator, so practise offline tools.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Evaluate\" questions want both sides and a judgement: \"Cloud tools such as WeVideo allow a group to co-edit from home, but they depend on a reliable connection; for a small school team, a cloud tool is best for collaboration, with offline Kdenlive as backup.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>There are three kinds of software. Open-source tools such as GIMP, Audacity and Kdenlive are free, and their code is public. Industry-standard tools such as Photoshop and Premiere Pro are what most studios use, and they usually cost money. Cloud tools such as Canva and CapCut run online and let teams work together. Pick the tool that fits the task.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A common misconception is that \"free\" means \"open-source\". DaVinci Resolve has a free version and CapCut is free to use, but neither publishes its source code, so they are not open-source. Each category carries a different risk: industry tools cost money and take time to learn, cloud tools depend on connectivity and on the company keeping the service, and open-source tools may lack niche features. The strongest workflow combines them: edit locally, then back up to the cloud, a lesson many St. Maarten businesses learned after Hurricane Irma. Remember that Paper 02 is offline, so practise with installed tools.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Open-source, industry-standard or cloud?","intro":"<p>Jerome is listing the software on the Island Pulse Media intern laptops. Sort each tool.</p>","main":"Which category does this tool belong to?","bins":["Open-source","Industry-standard","Cloud-based"],"items":[{"t":"Canva","bin":2,"fb":"Canva runs in the browser and stores designs online."},{"t":"Final Cut Pro","bin":1,"fb":"Final Cut Pro is Apple's professional, paid video editor."},{"t":"Audacity","bin":0,"fb":"Audacity is a free, open-source audio editor."},{"t":"WeVideo","bin":2,"fb":"WeVideo is a browser-based video editor with cloud storage."},{"t":"DaVinci Resolve","bin":1,"fb":"DaVinci Resolve is a professional editing and colour grading suite used in film and TV studios."},{"t":"CapCut","bin":2,"fb":"CapCut is a cloud-connected editor popular for short vertical video."},{"t":"Adobe Photoshop","bin":1,"fb":"Photoshop is the proprietary industry standard for photo editing, sold by subscription."},{"t":"Kdenlive","bin":0,"fb":"Kdenlive is a free, open-source non-linear video editor."},{"t":"GIMP","bin":0,"fb":"GIMP is free, open-source raster image software."},{"t":"OBS","bin":0,"fb":"OBS Studio is free and open-source software for screen recording and livestreaming."}],"win":"Well sorted. Open-source tools are free with public code, industry-standard tools are professional and usually paid, and cloud tools run online with real-time teamwork but need a connection.","tutor":[{"h":"Studios","p":"<p>The Adobe desktop apps, Final Cut Pro and DaVinci Resolve are what professional studios expect.</p>"},{"h":"Browser test","p":"<p>If you mainly use it in a web browser and it saves online, it is cloud-based.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Sort the software (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"GIMP","bin":0,"fb":"Free and open-source."},{"t":"Adobe Photoshop","bin":1,"fb":"Industry standard."},{"t":"Canva","bin":2,"fb":"Runs in the browser: cloud."},{"t":"Audacity","bin":0,"fb":"Free and open-source."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder: some of these tools are free but NOT open-source. Sort each by category, not by price.</p>","items":[{"t":"Inkscape","bin":0,"fb":"Inkscape is free, open-source vector software."},{"t":"Blender","bin":0,"fb":"Blender is free, open-source 3D and animation software."},{"t":"DaVinci Resolve (free version)","bin":1,"fb":"Free to download but proprietary: an industry-standard editing and colour suite, not open-source."},{"t":"Adobe Illustrator","bin":1,"fb":"Illustrator is the paid industry standard for vector design."},{"t":"Adobe Audition","bin":1,"fb":"Audition is Adobe's professional audio editor."},{"t":"Figma","bin":2,"fb":"Figma runs in the browser with real-time co-editing."},{"t":"Google Workspace","bin":2,"fb":"Docs, Slides and Drive run online for shared scripts and folders."},{"t":"OBS Studio","bin":0,"fb":"OBS Studio is free and open-source for recording and livestreaming."},{"t":"Adobe After Effects","bin":1,"fb":"After Effects is the industry standard for motion graphics."},{"t":"Adobe Express","bin":2,"fb":"Adobe Express is a browser-based, template-driven design tool."}],"win":"Well analysed. Category depends on licence and how the tool runs, not on price: a free download can still be proprietary.","title":"Open-source, industry-standard or cloud? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Photo editing: protect the original, then make it web-ready","html":"<p>There are three photo techniques for web products: <b>adding text to images</b>, <b>changing backgrounds</b> and <b>colour correction</b>. The whole job has three steps. The menu names below are from GIMP.</p>\n<p><b>01 Protect</b>: use layers and masks; keep the original.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Work on a <b>copy</b> of the photo. Duplicate the layer (<b>Layer → Duplicate Layer</b>) and hide the original.</li>\n<li>Use a <b>layer mask</b> (paint black to hide, white to reveal) instead of deleting pixels. This is how you <b>change a background</b> non-destructively: select the subject, invert the selection and mask the background out, then add a new background layer underneath.</li>\n<li>Save the working file as <b>.xcf</b>, which keeps every layer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>02 Crop and correct</b>: 1200 to 2000 px wide, well under 500 KB.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Crop</b> with the Crop tool, using the rule of thirds and a fixed aspect ratio for the platform. Then <b>Image → Scale Image</b> to a web width of <b>1200 to 2000 px</b> (1080 px for most social posts).</li>\n<li><b>White balance</b>: <b>Colors → Auto → White Balance</b> removes colour casts, such as the orange of restaurant bulbs or the blue of shade.</li>\n<li><b>Exposure</b>: <b>Colors → Exposure</b> or <b>Colors → Levels</b> (drag the black and white sliders to the edges of the histogram) fixes photos that are too dark or too bright.</li>\n<li><b>Contrast</b>: a gentle S-shape in <b>Colors → Curves</b>. Raise <b>Hue-Saturation</b> only slightly; food and skin tones must look natural.</li>\n<li><b>Sharpen last</b>: <b>Filters → Enhance → Sharpen (Unsharp Mask)</b>, after resizing, because scaling softens the image.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>03 Prepare and export</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Add <b>high-contrast text</b> with the <b>Text tool (T)</b> on its own layer: white text with a subtle shadow, or a semi-transparent dark bar behind it. One or two fonts, a short message.</li>\n<li>Write <b>alt text</b> for the web page; text inside an image is invisible to screen readers and search engines.</li>\n<li><b>File → Export As</b>: <b>JPEG or WebP for photos</b> (quality about 75 to 85); <b>PNG for transparency</b>, such as a logo with no background.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>Working file:  corail-dish_v2.xcf     (layers kept)\nExport:        corail-dish.webp       (1600 px wide, quality 80, about 250 KB)\nAlt text:      Grilled red snapper with plantain on a white plate</code></pre>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In Paper 02, examiners look for file evidence. Name files exactly as instructed, keep the layered working file, and export the format requested. A correct technique saved in the wrong format can lose marks.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Photo editing has three steps. First, protect the original by working on a copy and using layers. Next, crop and correct the colour and brightness. Last, add text and export for the web, as JPEG or WebP for photos and PNG for logos. A restaurant photo that looks orange from indoor bulbs can be fixed with white balance.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The key professional idea is <b>non-destructive editing</b>. Layer masks hide pixels rather than deleting them, so a background change can be refined or undone at any time, and the layered .xcf file is evidence of process. Order also matters: correct colour before judging contrast, and sharpen last, because resizing softens detail. Restraint is a skill too; over-saturated food looks artificial, and skin tones must stay natural across all complexions. Text on images needs contrast and must be repeated as real page text or alt text, because words inside pixels are invisible to screen readers and search engines. Exams reward named menus and settings.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Practical task 1, \"Taste of English Harbour\"","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Practical task 1 (photo).</b> Crop a restaurant photo to <b>1080 x 1350</b>, correct it, add the text <b>\"Taste of English Harbour\"</b> and export it <b>under 400 KB</b>.</p>\n<p>Imagine Zara at the Island Pulse Media has a photo of a lobster dish from the Chez Corail restaurant in English Harbour. It is 4000 px wide, a little orange from the indoor lights and slightly dark. The restaurant wants an Instagram portrait post (4:5). Follow these steps in GIMP.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Protect.</b> Open the photo, <b>Layer → Duplicate Layer</b>, hide the original and save straight away as <i>taste-grand-case_v1.xcf</i>.</li>\n<li><b>Crop to 4:5.</b> Choose the Crop tool, tick <b>Fixed: Aspect ratio</b> and type <b>4:5</b>. Place the dish on a third, leave calm space at the top for the text, then press Enter.</li>\n<li><b>Scale.</b> <b>Image → Scale Image</b>: width <b>1080</b> px; with the chain linked, the height becomes <b>1350</b> px.</li>\n<li><b>Correct.</b> White Balance to remove the orange cast, Levels or Exposure to brighten, a gentle Curves S-shape for contrast.</li>\n<li><b>Add text.</b> Text tool: \"Taste of English Harbour\" in a bold, readable font, white with a soft drop shadow (<b>Filters → Light and Shadow → Drop Shadow</b>) so it contrasts with the food.</li>\n<li><b>Sharpen last.</b> A light Unsharp Mask on the photo layer.</li>\n<li><b>Export.</b> <b>File → Export As</b> <i>taste-grand-case.jpg</i>. In the JPEG dialog, tick <b>Show preview in image window</b>: GIMP shows the file size. Start at quality 85 and lower it until the file is <b>under 400 KB</b>, checking that the text edges stay clean.</li>\n<li><b>Check.</b> Open the exported JPEG at 100%: right size, right file size, readable text. Write alt text: \"Grilled lobster with lime and rice under the words Taste of English Harbour\".</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Paper 02 tips: file naming and export</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use the exact file name the question gives. If none is given, use lower case, hyphens, no spaces, and your candidate number if requested: <i>taste-grand-case.jpg</i>, not <i>IMG_4471 final FINAL.jpg</i>.</li>\n<li>Submit <b>both</b> files when asked: the layered .xcf (process) and the exported JPEG (product).</li>\n<li>Check the brief's numbers: if it says 1080 x 1350 and under 400 KB, an image of 1080 x 1080 or 600 KB does not meet it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Examiners often give marks for <b>evidence of each step</b>. Keep the original, the .xcf and the export side by side in one clearly named folder.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>This practice task turns a big restaurant photo into an Instagram post. In GIMP you copy the layer, crop to 4:5, scale to 1080 by 1350 px, fix the colour, add the words \"Taste of English Harbour\", sharpen, and export a JPEG under 400 KB. Use the exact file name and size the task asks for, and keep the layered file.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Practical tasks are marked on <b>evidence and precision</b>. If the brief says 1080 by 1350 px and under 400 KB, a beautiful 1080 by 1080 image still misses the specification. Crop before scaling: cropping to 4:5 first and then scaling gives exact dimensions, while scaling first wastes detail you later cut away. Lower JPEG quality step by step while watching the preview, because text edges show compression artefacts first. Keep three files side by side: the original, the layered .xcf and the export. In a written answer, give the menu path, setting and reason for each step, for example \"Colors → Auto → White Balance to remove the orange cast from the bulbs\".</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Change the background in GIMP","intro":"<p>Keisha needs a product photo of a bottle of pepper sauce for the Antigua Pepper Co. web shop, placed on a clean white background. Put her GIMP steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Open the photo and duplicate the layer, hiding the original."},{"t":"Add an alpha channel to the duplicate layer."},{"t":"Select the bottle with the Paths or Foreground Select tool."},{"t":"Invert the selection and feather it by 1 to 2 px."},{"t":"Delete the background or hide it with a layer mask."},{"t":"Add a white layer underneath and check the edges at 200% zoom."},{"t":"Save the working .xcf file, then export as WebP for the web shop."}],"win":"Correct. Protect the original, enable transparency, select, invert, remove, replace, then save and export.","tutor":[{"h":"Protect first","p":"<p>What do you do before changing anything?</p>"},{"h":"Transparency","p":"<p>A layer cannot become see-through until it has an alpha channel.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Change the background (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the main steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Duplicate the layer."},{"t":"Select the subject."},{"t":"Remove the background."},{"t":"Export the image."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>A non-destructive version using a layer mask. Put Keisha's nine GIMP steps for the pepper sauce bottle in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Open the photo, duplicate the layer and hide the original."},{"t":"Save straight away as pepper-sauce-bottle_v1.xcf."},{"t":"Draw around the bottle with the Paths tool and convert the path to a selection."},{"t":"Feather the selection by 1 to 2 px so the edges are soft."},{"t":"Add a layer mask to the duplicate, initialised from the selection, so the background is hidden, not deleted."},{"t":"Add a new white layer and move it below the bottle layer."},{"t":"Zoom to 200% and paint on the mask, black to hide and white to reveal, to tidy the edges."},{"t":"Scale the image to the web shop's product width."},{"t":"Save the .xcf again, then export as WebP."}],"win":"Correct. Protect and save, select and feather, mask instead of deleting, add the new background, refine the edges, then scale and export.","title":"Change the background in GIMP (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Audio: get it right at the source, then mix in layers","html":"<p>Audio skills include <b>working with multiple audio tracks</b>, <b>sound recording, adjusting audio and layering tracks</b>, and <b>noise reduction</b>. Audio work has three stages. Audacity (free, open-source) is used for the menu names.</p>\n<p><b>1. Record cleanly</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Record in <b>WAV</b> (uncompressed, full quality); you make the smaller MP3 only at the end.</li>\n<li>Watch the meter: voice <b>peaks around -12 to -6 dB</b> (minus 12 to minus 6). Never touch 0 dB, which causes clipping (harsh distortion that cannot be fixed).</li>\n<li>Capture <b>ten seconds of room tone</b>: everyone stays silent while you record the room. It gives a noise profile and filler for gaps.</li>\n<li>Choose a quiet, soft room away from air conditioners, roosters and jets landing at a nearby airport. Noise reduction cannot remove a sudden car horn on a busy street; re-record the line.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>2. Mix in layers</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep <b>host, guest, music and effects on separate tracks</b>. <b>File → Import → Audio</b> puts each file on its own track; use <b>Edit → Split</b> and Delete to trim mistakes and long pauses.</li>\n<li>Balance each track's <b>gain</b> so voices are clear. <b>Ducking</b> lowers the music automatically when someone speaks: <b>Effect → Volume and Compression → Auto Duck</b>.</li>\n<li>Add <b>fades</b> (Effect → Fading → Fade In / Fade Out) so music never starts or stops abruptly.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>3. Polish and publish</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Noise reduction</b>: select the room tone, <b>Effect → Noise Removal and Repair → Noise Reduction → Get Noise Profile</b>; select the voice track, open it again, and apply gently (about 6 to 12 dB). Too much sounds robotic.</li>\n<li><b>EQ</b> (Effect → EQ and Filters): a gentle low cut removes rumble; a small boost in the middle frequencies helps speech clarity.</li>\n<li><b>Compression</b> (Effect → Volume and Compression → Compressor) evens out loud and quiet words.</li>\n<li><b>Master near -16 LUFS</b> (minus 16) with <b>Loudness Normalization</b>, a common target for podcasts.</li>\n<li><b>Publish MP3 with metadata tags</b>: File → Export Audio → MP3, then fill in title, artist, album (show name), track number (episode) and year.</li>\n</ol>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Practical task 2 (audio).</b> Record a <b>two-minute mini-podcast</b> on a local event (Carnival, Emancipation Day, a regatta or festival) using <b>three tracks</b>: host voice, guest voice and music. Duck the music, add fades, master near -16 LUFS and export a tagged MP3 named <i>event-minipod-ep01.mp3</i>.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Save the Audacity <b>project</b> (.aup3) to keep separate tracks, and <b>export</b> a mixed file for publishing. In Paper 02, state your levels and settings: \"peaks around -12 to -6 dB, ten seconds of room tone, mastered near -16 LUFS\".</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Good audio starts with a clean recording. Record in a quiet, soft room, keep the level below the top of the meter, and record ten seconds of silence called room tone. Then edit in layers: voice, music and effects on separate tracks. Lower the music when people speak, add fades, and export an MP3 with its details filled in.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Audio follows a strict rule: <b>you cannot fix what you did not capture</b>. Clipping at 0 dB destroys the waveform permanently, and noise reduction works on steady sounds such as an air conditioner but not on a sudden car horn or a rooster. That is why monitoring with headphones during recording matters more than any plug-in. Processing order also matters: reduce noise before compression, because compression raises quiet sounds, including noise. Loudness targets such as about -16 LUFS keep episodes consistent with other shows, so listeners do not adjust the volume. Separate tracks keep decisions reversible, which is why the .aup3 project should be kept alongside the MP3.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Fixing a noisy recording","q":"Malik recorded an interview with a Dominica cocoa farmer for Caribbean Roots podcast. There is a steady air-conditioner hum throughout. Following good practice, he recorded ten seconds of room tone at the start. What should he do FIRST in Audacity?","options":[{"t":"Apply Noise Reduction at the maximum setting to the whole track.","fb":"Without a noise profile, Audacity does not know what to remove, and maximum settings make voices sound robotic."},{"t":"Normalize the track to make it louder.","fb":"Normalising raises the hum along with the voice. Remove the noise first."},{"t":"Select the ten seconds of room tone and use Noise Reduction to Get Noise Profile.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Audacity needs a sample of the noise alone before it can remove that noise from the whole track."},{"t":"Add intro music on a new track to cover the hum.","fb":"Music would only hide the hum during the intro. The interview itself would still be noisy."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Why record silence?","p":"<p>The silent part contains only the noise. What can the software learn from it?</p>"},{"h":"Order matters","p":"<p>Cleaning comes before making things louder.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Fixing a noisy recording (Foundation)","q":"Before removing a steady hum in Audacity, you should first...","options":[{"t":"delete the whole track","fb":"You would lose the interview."},{"t":"make the whole track louder","fb":"That makes the hum louder too."},{"t":"select a silent part and get a noise profile","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. The software learns what the noise sounds like."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","q":"Malik's interview with the Dominica cocoa farmer has a steady air-conditioner hum throughout, AND a loud car horn over one key sentence at 3:12. He recorded room tone at the start. Which plan is BEST?","options":[{"t":"Use the room tone as a noise profile and apply gentle Noise Reduction for the hum; for 3:12, re-record that question with the farmer or cut the sentence if it is not essential.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Noise profiles suit steady noise; sudden sounds need re-recording or editing out."},{"t":"Apply maximum Noise Reduction to the whole file to remove both problems.","fb":"Maximum settings make the voice robotic, and noise reduction cannot remove a sudden horn."},{"t":"Cut the sentence at 3:12 and leave the hum, since listeners will not notice.","fb":"A steady hum through a whole interview is distracting and is easy to reduce with a profile."},{"t":"Normalize the file first, then add music to cover the horn.","fb":"Normalising raises the hum too, and music under a key sentence hides the speech."}],"title":"Fixing a noisy recording (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Creating a podcast step by step","html":"<p>A <b>podcast</b> is a digital audio (or video, a <b>vodcast</b>) file distributed over the Internet that listeners download or stream, usually delivered through an <b>RSS feed</b> that apps such as Spotify and Apple Podcasts read (unit 2.2 covers the definition).</p>\n<p>There are four stages for <b>how to launch a podcast</b>: <b>(1) Develop concept</b> (niche, format, target audience), <b>(2) Gather equipment</b> (microphone, headphones, recording software), <b>(3) Record and edit</b> (create content, edit for quality, add music) and <b>(4) Publish and promote</b> (upload to a hosting platform and market the podcast). The detailed steps below fit inside those four stages.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Plan (develop concept).</b> Define scope (Unit 2.4): purpose, audience, format (solo, interview, panel), episode length and schedule. Example: the <i>Rock Talk TT</i>, a 15-minute fortnightly show where Bayleaf Hill Secondary students in Trinidad interview young Caribbean entrepreneurs.</li>\n<li><b>Script or outline.</b> Write a full script or a running order: intro (who, what, why listen), segments, questions for the guest, call to action, outro. Write for the ear: short sentences and spoken-style words.</li>\n<li><b>Gather equipment and record.</b> A USB microphone (or a phone with a clip-on mic), closed-back headphones and recording software such as Audacity. Quiet, soft room; mic about a hand's width from the mouth, slightly to the side to reduce \"p\" and \"b\" pops; record WAV and ten seconds of room tone; do a test recording and check levels peak around -12 to -6 dB, never touching 0 dB (clipping). Get written consent from every guest.</li>\n<li><b>Edit.</b> In Audacity: trim mistakes and long pauses, reduce noise, place music on its own track with ducking and fades, apply EQ and compression, and master near -16 LUFS.</li>\n<li><b>Intro and outro music: licensing.</b> Never use a commercial song without a licence. Use royalty-free libraries whose terms allow podcast use, Creative Commons tracks (check for BY, NC, ND conditions), or music you or a local musician created with written permission. Credit the track in the episode notes (title, artist, source, licence).</li>\n<li><b>Export.</b> File → Export Audio → MP3, around 128 kbps (mono is fine for speech) or higher for music-heavy shows. Name clearly: <i>rocktalk-ep03-pepper-sauce-startup.mp3</i>. Add ID3 metadata (title, episode number, artist).</li>\n<li><b>Cover art and show notes.</b> Square artwork (3000 by 3000 px is widely recommended), readable at thumbnail size. Write a short, keyword-rich episode description with guest name, topics and links.</li>\n<li><b>Publish and promote.</b> Upload to a hosting platform such as <b>Spotify for Creators</b>. The host stores the files and generates an <b>RSS feed</b> that can be submitted to Apple Podcasts and other directories, so one upload reaches many apps. Share on Instagram with a short audiogram clip.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The practical activity asks you to record your 10 to 15 minute script, publish it on a hosting site and gather peer feedback to improve the next episode. Feedback is part of promotion: ask listeners what to keep and what to cut.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> If Paper 02 asks you to \"outline the steps in producing a podcast\", use the four stages as headings, or the detailed steps in order and add one technical detail per step (format, bitrate, licence type, platform). Detail turns a list into a high-scoring answer.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Making a podcast has four stages: develop the concept, gather equipment, record and edit, then publish and promote. Plan who it is for and how long it is, write a script, record in a quiet room, edit in Audacity, and use only licensed music. Upload it to a host that creates an RSS feed, so one upload reaches many podcast apps.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The stages that separate a professional podcast from a recording are the ones around the microphone. Consent forms protect the guest and the producer. Licensing decides whether the episode can stay online: a commercial song in the intro can lead to removal. Metadata and show notes decide discoverability, because directories search titles and descriptions, not the audio itself. Bitrate is a deliberate trade-off: about 128 kbps mono is clear for speech and kind to listeners on mobile data, while music-heavy shows need more. Promotion and feedback close the loop, since listener comments shape the next episode. In exam answers, give one technical detail per step.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Produce a podcast episode","intro":"<p>Keisha is producing episode 1 of the <i>Island Eats</i> podcast about street food from the fish fry in Oistins to doubles in Trinidad. Put her workflow in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Plan the scope: audience, format, length and schedule."},{"t":"Write the script and guest questions."},{"t":"Record in a quiet room with a test of levels and room tone."},{"t":"Edit in Audacity: trim, reduce noise and balance tracks."},{"t":"Add licensed intro and outro music with fades."},{"t":"Export as a named MP3 with metadata."},{"t":"Upload to Spotify for Creators with cover art and show notes."},{"t":"Share the RSS-distributed episode and promote it on social media."}],"win":"Correct. Plan, script, record, edit, add licensed music, export, publish and promote: the full podcast workflow.","tutor":[{"h":"Pre-production","p":"<p>Planning and scripting happen before anything is recorded.</p>"},{"h":"Post-production","p":"<p>Music and export come after the voice edit; publishing comes last.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Produce a podcast (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the main stages in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Plan and script."},{"t":"Record."},{"t":"Edit and export."},{"t":"Publish."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Ten detailed steps for episode 1 of <i>Island Eats</i>. Put them in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Develop the concept: niche, format, audience, episode length and schedule."},{"t":"Write the running order: intro, segments, guest questions, call to action and outro."},{"t":"Set up the USB mic and headphones in a quiet room, test levels and collect the guest's signed consent form."},{"t":"Record in WAV with ten seconds of room tone, keeping peaks around -12 to -6 dB."},{"t":"Trim mistakes and long pauses, then apply gentle Noise Reduction using the room-tone profile."},{"t":"Add the licensed intro music on its own track with Auto Duck and fades."},{"t":"Apply gentle EQ and compression, then master near -16 LUFS."},{"t":"Export a 128 kbps MP3 with ID3 tags such as title and episode number."},{"t":"Upload to the host with square cover art and keyword-rich show notes."},{"t":"Submit the RSS feed to directories, promote with an audiogram and collect listener feedback."}],"win":"Correct. Concept and script, set-up and consent, a clean recording, clean-up before music, polish and loudness, export with tags, then publish, distribute and gather feedback.","title":"Produce a podcast episode (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Producing an audio slideshow","html":"<p>An <b>audio slideshow</b> is videos and pictures put together with sound to communicate a story, a message or a presentation, and the rule is: <b>keep it simple!</b> This screen turns the four techniques (voice tone, pacing, visual sequencing and flexible production) into production steps you can follow in free tools such as Audacity with Shotcut or OpenShot, or in the cloud with Canva or WeVideo.</p>\n<p><b>Two workflows (\"flexible production\")</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Storyboard first</th><th>Voice first</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Sequence the photos and video inserts on a storyboard, write narration for each frame, record it, then set each image's duration to match the voice.</td><td>Record the narration or interview first, edit it in Audacity, then lay the images out along the audio track in the video editor, cutting on natural pauses.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Best when you control the images (an archive, a photo shoot).</td><td>Best for interviews and oral history, where the voice leads the story.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Steps (storyboard first)</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Gather and select</b> 10 to 20 strong images, with permission and credits. Crop to one aspect ratio (16:9 for YouTube, 9:16 for Reels).</li>\n<li><b>Storyboard the sequence</b>: number each frame, note its duration and the line of narration under it.</li>\n<li><b>Choose the voice tone</b> to match the story, then <b>record the narration</b> in Audacity, a sentence or two per image, leaving short pauses.</li>\n<li><b>Edit the audio</b>: trim, reduce noise and export a WAV for editing.</li>\n<li><b>Import into the editor</b>: audio on A1, images on V1 in storyboard order, music or ambient sound low on A2.</li>\n<li><b>Control the pacing</b>: about 4 to 8 seconds per image, longer for detailed photos; allow for delay in sound, so check the voice and the image change line up. A slow zoom (the \"Ken Burns\" effect) adds gentle movement.</li>\n<li><b>Add captions and titles</b>, then export MP4 (H.264/AAC).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Example.</b> Malik at the Island Pulse Media produces an audio slideshow for the Grenville Fishermen's Co-op in Grenada on sargassum. He records the fishermen first (voice first), then cuts each photo of the boats and the seaweed-covered shore to the natural pauses in their speech.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For a Paper 02 practical, name your workflow and justify it: \"I recorded the interview first because the speaker's voice leads the story, then timed each image to his pauses.\" Save the editor project file as well as the exported MP4.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>An audio slideshow puts photos and short clips together with a voice to tell a story. You can storyboard first, then write and record the narration, or record the voice first and fit the pictures to it. Show each image for about four to eight seconds, add gentle zooms, captions and titles, then export an MP4. An interview with fishermen suits voice first.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Choosing the workflow is a judgement about <b>what leads the story</b>. When you control the images, as with an archive, storyboard first gives tight control over sequence. When a real voice leads, as with an oral history, voice first respects the speaker's rhythm, and cutting on natural pauses makes the piece feel unhurried and authentic. Pacing is the hardest skill: too fast and viewers cannot read detailed photos; too slow and attention drifts. Slow zooms add movement, but using them on every image becomes repetitive. Permission and credits apply to every photo and to every person recorded, and captions keep the story accessible.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Build an audio slideshow, storyboard first","intro":"<p>Keisha is producing an Emancipation Day audio slideshow for the Palmetto Point Secondary Heritage Club using the storyboard-first workflow. Put her steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Select and crop the photos, with permission and credits."},{"t":"Sequence the photos on a numbered storyboard."},{"t":"Write one or two lines of narration under each frame."},{"t":"Record the narration in a calm, respectful voice tone."},{"t":"Edit the voice in Audacity and export a WAV."},{"t":"Place images on V1 and the voice on A1 in storyboard order."},{"t":"Adjust each image's duration so the pace matches the voice."},{"t":"Add captions and export an MP4."}],"win":"Correct. Storyboard first: select, sequence, write, record, edit, assemble, pace, then caption and export.","tutor":[{"h":"Sequence before writing","p":"<p>The images go in order on the storyboard before you start writing.</p>"},{"h":"Pacing comes late","p":"<p>You can only match images to the voice once both are in the editor.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Build an audio slideshow (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the main stages in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Sequence the photos on a storyboard."},{"t":"Write and record the narration."},{"t":"Put images and voice together in the editor."},{"t":"Check the pacing and export."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>This time Malik uses the VOICE-FIRST workflow for the Grenville Fishermen's Co-op piece on sargassum. Put his steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Explain the project to the fishermen and collect their signed consent."},{"t":"Record the interviews in WAV, with room tone, at the harbour office."},{"t":"Edit the voices in Audacity: trim, reduce noise and export a WAV."},{"t":"Choose and crop photos that match each topic in the edited interview."},{"t":"Import the edited voice track onto A1 in the video editor."},{"t":"Lay the photos on V1 along the audio, cutting on the natural pauses in speech."},{"t":"Add slow zooms and a low ambient sea track on A2."},{"t":"Add captions and titles, then export an MP4 (H.264/AAC)."}],"win":"Correct. In voice-first, the edited voice sets the timing, and the images are chosen and cut to fit it.","title":"Build an audio slideshow, storyboard first (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Video: a hook, purposeful cuts and navigation cues","html":"<p>Key video techniques are <b>trimming clips, adjusting audio, adding text, layering tracks and combining media elements</b>, together with <b>breadcrumbs, transitions, captions and accessibility features</b>. You can use Kdenlive (open-source), DaVinci Resolve or Premiere Pro (industry-standard), or CapCut and WeVideo (cloud).</p>\n<p><b>The editing workflow</b></p>\n<p class=\"passage\">Ingest → rough cut → fine cut → audio → colour → graphics → export</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Ingest</b>: copy the clips from the camera into a named project folder (and a backup), then import them into the editor.</li>\n<li><b>Rough cut</b>: put the best takes on the timeline in story order.</li>\n<li><b>Fine cut</b>: trim each clip tightly, frame by frame, and remove dead air.</li>\n<li><b>Audio</b>: balance voice and music, add ducking and fades.</li>\n<li><b>Colour</b>: correct white balance and exposure so every clip matches.</li>\n<li><b>Graphics</b>: titles, captions, logo and call to action, inside the safe area.</li>\n<li><b>Export</b> in the right format for the platform.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Hook immediately.</b> Win attention in <b>three seconds</b>: open with the most striking shot (a yacht heeling over at the Regatta start, not a logo). Make the story <b>understandable with sound off</b>, using captions and on-screen text, because many people scroll on mute.</p>\n<p><b>Cut with purpose.</b> Use <b>mostly hard cuts</b> (one clip straight to the next); save a cross-dissolve to show time passing and avoid flashy wipes. <b>J-cuts and L-cuts</b> let interview audio bridge picture changes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>J-cut</b>: the <b>audio of the next clip starts first</b>, before its picture. We hear the Chez Corail chef say \"Every morning I go to the fishermen...\" while still seeing the beach, then cut to her face.</li>\n<li><b>L-cut</b>: the <b>picture changes but the audio carries on</b> from the previous clip. The skipper keeps talking while we cut to shots of her crew hauling the sail.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Give directions.</b> Add <b>breadcrumbs</b> that help viewers navigate: <b>YouTube chapters</b> and <b>section labels</b> (\"1. The race\", \"2. The crews\", \"3. The party\") so they know where they are and can jump ahead.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Practical task 3 (video).</b> Edit a <b>45 to 60 second vertical promo</b> (1080 x 1920) for a local business or event, with <b>captions</b> and a <b>call to action</b> (\"Book your table in English Harbour\", \"Follow us for Sailing Week highlights\"). Export <i>regatta-promo-vertical.mp4</i>.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Your SBA ad is exactly <b>30 seconds</b>, so practise the same hook, cut and CTA thinking on a shorter clip. In Paper 02, name the cut: \"an L-cut so the interview continues over the B-roll\".</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Video editing follows steps: copy and back up the clips, make a rough cut, trim it finely, fix the audio, fix the colour, add titles and captions, then export. Hook viewers in the first three seconds, use mostly simple cuts, and add captions because many people watch with the sound off. A Regatta promo should open with a yacht, not a logo.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The workflow runs from <b>structure to polish</b> for a reason: colour-grading a clip you later cut is wasted effort. J-cuts and L-cuts are signs of a skilled editor because they let sound lead or follow the picture, making interviews feel smooth instead of choppy. Transitions should carry meaning; a dissolve says time has passed, so using it between every clip confuses viewers. Designing for silent viewing changes the edit itself: the story must work through pictures and on-screen text first. Chapters and section labels act as breadcrumbs inside long videos. In Paper 02, name the technique and its effect, such as an L-cut that keeps the skipper's voice running over crew shots.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Order the video workflow","intro":"<p>Malik is editing the Regatta promo for Island Pulse Media. Put the video workflow in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Ingest: copy and back up the clips, then import them."},{"t":"Rough cut: place the best takes in story order."},{"t":"Fine cut: trim each clip tightly and add J-cuts and L-cuts."},{"t":"Audio: balance the voice, duck the music, add fades."},{"t":"Colour: correct white balance and exposure so clips match."},{"t":"Graphics: add captions, titles and the call to action."},{"t":"Export: MP4, H.264, AAC at 1080 x 1920."}],"win":"Correct. Ingest, rough cut, fine cut, audio, colour, graphics, export: structure first, polish later.","tutor":[{"h":"Big to small","p":"<p>You arrange the story roughly before you trim individual frames.</p>"},{"h":"Polish last","p":"<p>Why colour or caption a clip you might still cut out?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Order the video workflow (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the main stages in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Ingest the clips."},{"t":"Rough cut, then fine cut."},{"t":"Audio, colour and graphics."},{"t":"Export."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>A fuller workflow with ten steps for the Regatta Reel. Put them in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Copy the clips into a named project folder and back them up to a second drive."},{"t":"Create a new project set to 1080 x 1920 vertical and import the clips."},{"t":"Rough cut: place the best takes on the timeline in story order."},{"t":"Fine cut: trim each clip tightly and add J-cuts and L-cuts."},{"t":"Audio: balance the skipper's voice, duck the music and add fades."},{"t":"Colour: match white balance and exposure across all clips."},{"t":"Graphics: add titles, captions and the call to action inside the safe area."},{"t":"Proofread every caption, checking names such as Shirley Heights and Falmouth Harbour."},{"t":"Export an MP4 using H.264 video and AAC audio."},{"t":"Watch the exported file on a phone with the sound off to check it still works."}],"win":"Correct. Safe ingest and the right project settings first, then structure, sound, colour and graphics, and finally proofreading, export and a real-device check.","title":"Order the video workflow (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Web-ready means accessible and correctly exported","html":"<p>Finish with two checks that every web video or podcast must pass before it is published: <b>make it accessible</b> and <b>check the export</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Make it accessible</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Proofread auto-captions.</b> Captions show speech and important sounds as text, for muted viewers and for Deaf and hard-of-hearing viewers. Auto-captions in CapCut, YouTube or Kdenlive save time but often misspell Caribbean names and places (Chaguanas, Soufrière, Kreyòl and Papiamentu words). <b>Closed captions</b> can be turned on or off (an <b>SRT</b> or <b>VTT</b> file); <b>open captions</b> are burned into the picture.</li>\n<li><b>Provide transcripts and audio description.</b> A transcript serves podcast listeners who cannot hear and helps search engines; audio description narrates key visual information for blind and low-vision viewers.</li>\n<li><b>Use strong text contrast</b>: large, light text on a dark bar, or dark on light. Do not rely on colour alone to show meaning.</li>\n<li><b>No more than three flashes per second.</b> Rapid flashing (strobe lights at a Carnival fete, fast cuts of fireworks) can trigger seizures.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>1\n00:00:01,000 --&gt; 00:00:03,500\nWelcome to the Heineken Regatta!\n\n2\n00:00:03,600 --&gt; 00:00:06,000\n[crowd cheering] The fleet is off the start line.</code></pre>\n<p><b>Check the export</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Setting</th><th>Web standard</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Video container</td><td><b>MP4</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Video codec</td><td><b>H.264</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Audio codec</td><td><b>AAC</b></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Size</td><td><b>1920 x 1080</b> landscape (16:9, YouTube, websites) or <b>1080 x 1920</b> vertical (9:16, Reels, TikTok, Shorts)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Photos / logos</td><td>JPEG or WebP / PNG with transparency, or SVG</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Podcast audio</td><td>MP3 (about 128 kbps for speech) with metadata tags</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Captions / documents</td><td>SRT or VTT / PDF</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>In Kdenlive use <b>Project → Render</b> and choose an MP4 (H.264/AAC) preset; in GIMP use <b>File → Export As</b>; in Audacity use <b>File → Export Audio</b>.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Learn the difference between a <b>container</b> (MP4, the \"box\") and a <b>codec</b> (H.264 for picture, AAC for sound, the way the contents are compressed). Paper 01 often tests it.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Before publishing a video or podcast, make it accessible and check the export. Proofread captions, add transcripts, use strong text contrast, and avoid more than three flashes per second. Export web video as MP4 with H.264 video and AAC audio: 1920 by 1080 for websites, 1080 by 1920 for Reels. Auto-captions often misspell names like Chaguanas.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The container and codec distinction is a classic exam point: MP4 is the box, while H.264 and AAC describe how the picture and the sound inside are compressed. A file can have the right extension yet use an unsupported codec and fail to play. Accessibility choices also have trade-offs. Open captions are always visible, which suits muted social feeds, but cannot be turned off or translated; closed captions (SRT or VTT) give viewers control and are readable by search engines. Audio description serves blind viewers, a group often forgotten. The flash limit is a safety rule, not a style preference, so fast fireworks montages must be slowed or edited.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Check the export settings","intro":"<p>Keisha is publishing the Chez Corail promo and the Regatta highlights for the Island Pulse Media. Choose the correct setting each time.</p>","items":[{"text":"Which video container is the web standard?","options":["XCF","MP4","AUP3","AVI"],"answer":1,"fb":"MP4 plays in almost every browser and app. XCF and AUP3 are project files, not videos."},{"text":"Which video codec should go inside it?","options":["H.264","AAC","WAV","PNG"],"answer":0,"fb":"H.264 compresses the picture efficiently and is supported almost everywhere. AAC is for audio."},{"text":"Which audio codec pairs with H.264 in an MP4?","options":["SVG","AAC","H.264"],"answer":1,"fb":"AAC is the standard compressed audio codec in web MP4 files."},{"text":"The Regatta promo is for Instagram Reels. Which size?","options":["1080 x 1920","1920 x 1080","1080 x 1080"],"answer":0,"fb":"Reels are vertical 9:16, which is 1080 x 1920."},{"text":"The Chez Corail video is for the restaurant website banner. Which size?","options":["1080 x 1920","1920 x 1080","400 x 400"],"answer":1,"fb":"A website banner is landscape 16:9, which is 1920 x 1080."},{"text":"The video has fast fireworks cuts. What is the safe limit?","options":["Any number if there are captions","No more than three flashes per second","Ten flashes per second"],"answer":1,"fb":"More than three flashes per second can trigger seizures; captions do not change that."}],"win":"Well done. MP4 container, H.264 video, AAC audio, 1920 x 1080 landscape or 1080 x 1920 vertical, and no more than three flashes per second.","tutor":[{"h":"Box and contents","p":"<p>The container is the box; codecs compress the picture and the sound inside it.</p>"},{"h":"Width first","p":"<p>Sizes are written width x height. Which is taller than it is wide?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Check the export (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Web video container:","options":["XCF","MP4"],"answer":1,"fb":"MP4 is the web standard; XCF is a GIMP project file."},{"text":"Vertical video size:","options":["1080 x 1920","1920 x 1080"],"answer":0,"fb":"Vertical is taller than it is wide."},{"text":"Audio codec in a web MP4:","options":["H.264","AAC"],"answer":1,"fb":"AAC is for sound; H.264 is for picture."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder export and accessibility checks. Choose the BEST answer.</p>","items":[{"text":"A file is named promo.mp4 but will not play in some browsers. What is the most likely cause?","options":["It has captions","MP4 is not a video format","It uses a video codec inside the MP4 that those browsers do not support","The file name has lower-case letters"],"answer":2,"fb":"MP4 is only the container. If the codec inside is unsupported, the file will not play even with the right extension."},{"text":"The client wants viewers to switch captions on or off and to translate them. Which captions?","options":["No captions","Closed captions in an SRT or VTT file","Open captions burned into the picture","Captions only in the description"],"answer":1,"fb":"Closed captions are separate text files that viewers can turn on or off and that can be translated."},{"text":"An Instagram portrait feed post in a 4:5 ratio. Which size?","options":["1080 x 1920","1080 x 1350","400 x 500","1920 x 1080"],"answer":1,"fb":"4:5 at 1080 px wide is 1080 x 1350."},{"text":"Keisha uploads chez-corail.xcf to the website and nothing appears. Why?","options":["The name has a hyphen","Websites block all images","The file is too small","XCF is GIMP's layered project file, which browsers cannot display; she must export JPEG or WebP"],"answer":3,"fb":"Project files keep layers for editing; the web needs an exported format."},{"text":"A podcast episode is mostly speech. Which export is sensible for listeners on mobile data?","options":["320 kbps stereo for speech only","Uncompressed WAV","About 128 kbps MP3, mono, with ID3 tags","An MP4 video file"],"answer":2,"fb":"About 128 kbps mono is clear for speech and keeps file sizes small."},{"text":"A blind viewer needs to know what happens on screen when nobody is speaking. What helps most?","options":["Audio description","Captions","Faster cuts","A louder soundtrack"],"answer":0,"fb":"Audio description narrates key visual information, which captions cannot provide for blind viewers."}],"win":"Excellent. You distinguished container from codec, open from closed captions, project files from exports, and captions from audio description.","title":"Check the export settings (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Breadcrumbs for web pages and long videos","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Breadcrumbs</b>: a secondary navigation trail that shows where the user is within a website's hierarchy, with each level clickable, e.g. <b>Home &gt; Tours &gt; Lagoon Kayak</b>.</p>\n<p>The name comes from the fairy tale in which children drop breadcrumbs to find their way home. Breadcrumbs are one of the simple navigation features for web content. They:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>show users their location, which is helpful when they arrive from Google straight onto a deep page;</li>\n<li>let users jump back up one or more levels in one click;</li>\n<li>reduce bounce rate and help search engines understand the site structure;</li>\n<li>match the site map from Unit 2.3: each breadcrumb level is a level in the hierarchy.</li>\n</ul>\n<pre><code>&lt;nav aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"&gt;\n  &lt;a href=\"/\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt; &amp;gt;\n  &lt;a href=\"/tours/\"&gt;Tours&lt;/a&gt; &amp;gt;\n  &lt;span aria-current=\"page\"&gt;Lagoon Kayak&lt;/span&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;</code></pre>\n<p><b>Breadcrumbs in video.</b> The same \"where am I\" idea applies to media. On YouTube, adding <b>chapters</b> (timestamps in the description, starting at 0:00) splits a long video into named sections that viewers can jump to. <b>Section labels</b> on screen do the same job inside the video.</p>\n<pre><code>0:00 Race start off English Harbour\n1:15 Meet the crews\n3:40 Racing round the island\n6:10 Prize-giving and party</code></pre>\n<p>Chapters and labels help viewers who return to find one part, help search engines show sections in results, and support viewers with attention or memory difficulties. Name your exported files in lower case with hyphens and no spaces, so the page links never break.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A common Paper 01 trap: \"Save\" in GIMP or Audacity creates a project file (.xcf, .aup3) that browsers cannot display. For the web, you must <b>Export</b>.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Breadcrumbs are a trail of links that shows where you are on a website, such as Home &gt; Tours &gt; Lagoon Kayak. Each part can be clicked to go back up a level. Long videos use chapters in the same way, with timestamps such as 0:00 for the Regatta start. Remember: for the web you must export files, not just save them.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Breadcrumbs matter most when visitors <b>arrive deep</b> in a site from a search result, because they have never seen the home page or menu. They reflect the hierarchy from the site map, so a confusing breadcrumb trail usually reveals a confusing structure. Accessible breadcrumbs use a nav element with an aria-label and mark the current page with aria-current, so screen-reader users hear where they are. Chapters do the same job for time: they let returning viewers jump to one section and can appear as segments in search results. A common error is treating breadcrumbs as the main navigation; they are secondary, supporting the menu rather than replacing it.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Finishing a web video for a client","intro":"<p>You are working with Malik on a 30-second web promo for Curaçao Jazz Nights. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The edit is finished. The client wants it on their website and on Instagram Reels. How do you export?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Two MP4 (H.264/AAC) versions: 1920 by 1080 for the website and 1080 by 1920 for Reels.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. MP4 H.264 plays almost everywhere, and each platform gets the right aspect ratio."},{"t":"One uncompressed master file of several gigabytes for both.","to":"bad1","fb":"Uncompressed files are far too large for web pages and many platforms reject them."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The client says, \"Most people watch Reels with the sound off.\" What do you add?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Proofread captions: burned-in open captions for Reels and an SRT file for the website player.","to":"n3","fb":"Yes. Captions serve muted viewers and Deaf or hard-of-hearing viewers."},{"t":"Nothing. The music is the best part.","to":"bad2","fb":"Muted viewers miss the key message, and the video is less accessible."},{"t":"Unchecked auto-captions.","to":"bad3","fb":"Auto-captions often get names and places wrong. Always proofread."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The video will sit on the page <i>Home &gt; Events &gt; Jazz Nights</i>. Visitors arrive from Google straight onto that page. How do you help them find other events?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Add a breadcrumb trail at the top so they can click back to Events or Home.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Breadcrumbs show location and give one-click routes back up the hierarchy."},{"t":"Hope they use the browser back button.","to":"bad4","fb":"The back button takes them to Google, not to your other events."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> The promo loads quickly, works on mute and keeps visitors exploring the site. A Grade One answer would explain each choice: suitable export formats and aspect ratios, captions for accessibility, and breadcrumbs for navigation.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Upload failed.</b> The file is too big to embed and Instagram rejects it. Try again and export in a web format.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Message lost.</b> Viewers scroll past without knowing the date or venue. Try again and add captions.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Embarrassing errors.</b> The captions misspell the venue and the headline act. Try again and proofread.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Visitors leave.</b> With no way up the hierarchy, they return to Google. Try again with a navigation feature.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"One file or two?","p":"<p>Different platforms prefer different shapes of video.</p>"},{"h":"Sound off","p":"<p>How does a muted viewer get the message?</p>"},{"h":"Where am I?","p":"<p>Which navigation feature shows the path from Home to the current page?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Finishing a web video (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: you are exporting a promo for a website.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Which export do you choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"An uncompressed master file.","to":"bad","fb":"Far too large for the web."},{"t":"MP4 with H.264 video and AAC audio.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. It plays on almost every browser and platform."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> Your video loads and plays smoothly.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> The file is too big. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A bigger delivery for Curaçao Jazz Nights: a 30-second promo for the website and Reels, plus an eight-minute festival recap for YouTube. The promo includes fast firework shots.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>How do you export the 30-second promo?</p>","choices":[{"t":"One 1920 x 1080 MP4 for both, letting Instagram crop it.","to":"ok1","fb":"Instagram will crop or letterbox it, and key details may fall outside the vertical frame."},{"t":"One uncompressed master file for both.","to":"bad1","fb":"Uncompressed files are far too large for the web and are often rejected."},{"t":"Two MP4 (H.264/AAC) files: 1920 x 1080 for the website and 1080 x 1920 for Reels, each reframed so nothing important is cut off.","to":"n2","fb":"Excellent. Right container and codecs, right ratio for each platform, and reframing rather than simple cropping."}]},"ok1":{"text":"<p>On Reels, the singer's face is cut off at the edge. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Leave it, as the music still plays.","to":"bad1","fb":"A cropped performer looks careless and weakens the promo."},{"t":"Re-edit a separate 1080 x 1920 version, reframing each shot.","to":"n2","fb":"Good recovery. Each platform gets its own frame."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The firework montage flashes about six times per second. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Keep it, but add a warning in the caption.","to":"bad2","fb":"A warning does not make the content safe for viewers who are already watching."},{"t":"Slow the cuts and reduce the flashing to no more than three flashes per second.","to":"n3","fb":"Correct. More than three flashes per second can trigger seizures."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>For captions on Reels and the website, what do you provide?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Unchecked auto-captions everywhere.","to":"bad3","fb":"Auto-captions often misspell names and places. Always proofread."},{"t":"Proofread open captions burned into the Reel, and a closed SRT file for the website player.","to":"n4","fb":"Yes. Open captions suit muted feeds; closed captions give website viewers control and help search."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The eight-minute recap will sit on <i>Home &gt; Events &gt; Jazz Nights</i>. How do you help viewers and visitors find their way?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Add YouTube chapters starting at 0:00, on-screen section labels, and a breadcrumb trail on the web page.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Chapters and labels navigate the video; breadcrumbs navigate the site."},{"t":"Add chapters but no 0:00 timestamp.","to":"ok2","fb":"YouTube chapters need the first timestamp at 0:00, so the chapters may not appear."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Delivered.</b> Each platform gets the right frame, the promo is safe and captioned, and viewers can navigate both the long video and the site. A Grade One answer justifies every setting by platform, accessibility and navigation.</p>","end":"good"},"ok2":{"text":"<p><b>Nearly there.</b> The recap uploads, but the chapters do not show. Try again and start them at 0:00.</p>","end":"ok"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Poor delivery.</b> The upload fails or the performer is cut off. Try again with platform-specific exports.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Safety risk.</b> The flashing breaks accessibility guidance. Try again and reduce the flash rate.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Embarrassing errors.</b> The captions misspell the venue and the headline act. Try again and proofread.</p>","end":"bad"}},"title":"Finishing a web video for a client (Challenge)"}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Apply tools and techniques for web content","genre":"Structured response: digital content production techniques (Paper 02 style)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Jolly Harbour Youth Sailing Club in Antigua wants web content to recruit new members aged 12 to 18. It needs (1) a hero photo of a young sailor for its website, with the text \"Learn to sail this summer\" on the image, and (2) a short podcast episode in which two coaches explain what a first lesson is like. The club has a mid-range smartphone, a laptop with GIMP and Audacity, and a USB microphone.</p>","prompt":"<p>Write a response for the club's committee:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Describe</b> how you would shoot the hero photo, applying TWO shooting principles and explaining the effect of each. (4 marks)</li><li><b>Outline</b> the steps in GIMP to colour-correct the photo, add the text and prepare it for the web. (5 marks)</li><li><b>Outline</b> how you would record, edit and publish the podcast episode, including how you would deal with background noise and intro music. (5 marks)</li></ol>","min":230,"max":440,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> I would use the rule of thirds by ... so that ... I would also use ... angle / depth of field / golden hour light ... which ...<br><b>(b)</b> First, Colors → ... Next, I would use the Text tool ... placed ... with contrast ... Then I would save the .xcf and export ... at ... px as ...<br><b>(c)</b> I would plan and script ... record in ... with room tone ... In Audacity, I would ... Noise Reduction ... put the music on a separate track ... licensed ... export as MP3 ... upload to ...</p>","checklist":["I named two shooting principles and explained the effect of each.","I gave GIMP menu names for colour correction and the Text tool.","I placed the text for contrast and readability, and mentioned alt text or page text.","I gave an export format, size and file name for the photo.","I described noise reduction with a noise profile and multiple tracks.","I addressed music licensing and named a publishing platform."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Shooting principles","marks":4,"look":["third|grid|intersection","angle|low|eye level","depth of field|blur|aperture|portrait mode","light|golden hour|sun|shade","effect|so that|makes|draws"],"min":3,"strong":"You applied two shooting principles with clear effects.","weak":"Name the principle, how you apply it and its effect: \"A low angle makes the sailor look confident\"; \"the sailor sits on the left third, leaving the right side for text\"."},{"criterion":"Photo editing and web preparation","marks":5,"look":["level|curve|white balance|colou?r","text tool|text layer","contrast|shadow|readab","xcf|layer","export|webp|jpe?g","px|pixel|resiz|alt"],"min":4,"strong":"Your GIMP steps are accurate and the photo is properly prepared for the web.","weak":"Include Colors → White Balance or Levels, the Text tool on its own layer with strong contrast, saving the .xcf, then exporting as WebP at about 1600 px with a descriptive name and alt text."},{"criterion":"Podcast production","marks":5,"look":["script|plan|outline|concept","room tone|silence|noise profile","noise reduction","track","licen|royalty|creative commons|permission","mp3|export","spotify|rss|upload|publish|host","promot|feedback|headphone|equipment"],"min":4,"strong":"You covered the full workflow with sound technical detail.","weak":"Cover plan and script, recording with room tone, Noise Reduction using a noise profile, music on a separate track with a licence and fades, export as MP3 and upload to a host such as Spotify for Creators."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> I would shoot the hero photo with the phone's grid turned on and apply the <b>rule of thirds</b>, placing the young sailor and sail on the left vertical third and leaving open sky and water on the right, so there is space for the headline and the eye is drawn into the scene. I would also shoot from a slightly <b>low angle</b>, crouching at the water's edge, which makes the sailor look confident and capable, an inspiring image for teenagers. To avoid harsh midday shadows I would shoot in the <b>golden hour</b> late in the afternoon over Jolly Harbour, giving warm, soft light that flatters skin and makes the sea glow.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> In GIMP I would open a copy of the photo. First, <b>Colors → Auto → White Balance</b> to remove any colour cast, then <b>Colors → Levels</b> to stretch the histogram so the image is not dull, and a small increase in <b>Hue-Saturation</b> so the water looks vivid but skin stays natural. Next, I would use the <b>Text tool</b> to type \"Learn to sail this summer\" on its own layer in a bold sans-serif font, placed in the open sky on the right third. For readability I would use white text with a subtle drop shadow so it has strong contrast. I would save the layered file as <i>sailing-hero_v1.xcf</i>, then <b>File → Export As</b> <i>sailing-club-hero.webp</i>, resized to 1600 px wide at quality 80 so it is under about 300 KB. On the website, the headline would also appear as real text and the image would have alt text such as \"Teenage sailor steering a dinghy in Jolly Harbour at sunset\".</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> I would plan the episode's scope (10 minutes, teens and parents, interview format) and write a short script with an intro and questions for the coaches. I would record in a quiet, soft room with the USB microphone a hand's width from each speaker, recording it as WAV with ten seconds of room tone first and checking levels peak around -12 to -6 dB. In Audacity, I would select the room tone and use <b>Effect → Noise Removal and Repair → Noise Reduction → Get Noise Profile</b>, then apply it gently to the whole voice track, and trim mistakes and long pauses. I would place intro and outro music on a <b>separate track</b>, using a royalty-free or CC BY track whose licence allows podcast use, credited in the show notes, with fade in and fade out and Auto Duck so the music dips under speech. Then I would apply gentle EQ and compression, master the episode near -16 LUFS, export it as <i>sailing-club-ep01.mp3</i> at 128 kbps with metadata tags, and upload it with square cover art and a keyword-rich description to <b>Spotify for Creators</b>, whose RSS feed distributes it to other podcast apps.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Practical \"outline the steps\" questions reward specific menu names, settings and file formats. Write steps in sequence with words such as \"first\", \"next\" and \"then\", and give one technical detail per step. Always mention licensing whenever music or third-party images appear.</p>",
 "easy": {"bloom":"Apply","genre":"Structured response: production techniques (9 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part. Use the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Name</b> ONE shooting principle for the hero photo and say what effect it has. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> TWO steps in GIMP to fix the photo and add the text. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> the file format you would export the photo in for the website. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> how you would remove background noise from the podcast in Audacity. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Say</b> what kind of intro music the club is allowed to use. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":70,"max":220,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> I would use ... This makes ...<br><b>(b)</b> First, I would ... Then I would use the Text tool to ...<br><b>(c)</b> I would export it as ...<br><b>(d)</b> I would record room tone, then ...<br><b>(e)</b> The club can use music that is ... because ...</p>","checklist":["I named one shooting principle and its effect.","I gave a colour-correction step and the Text tool.","I chose WebP or JPEG.","I mentioned room tone and Noise Reduction.","I said the music must be licensed, royalty-free or original."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Shooting principle and effect","marks":2,"look":["third|angle|golden hour|depth of field|light","makes|so that|effect|look"],"min":2,"strong":"Principle and effect given.","weak":"Name a principle, such as a low angle, and say its effect, such as making the sailor look confident."},{"criterion":"GIMP steps","marks":2,"look":["level|white balance|colou?r|bright|exposure","text tool|text"],"min":2,"strong":"Two correct steps.","weak":"Give one colour fix (Colors → Levels or White Balance) and say you would add the words with the Text tool."},{"criterion":"Web format","marks":1,"look":["webp|jpe?g"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct format.","weak":"Export the photo as WebP or JPEG."},{"criterion":"Noise reduction","marks":2,"look":["room tone|noise profile|silence","noise reduction"],"min":2,"strong":"Correct method.","weak":"Record room tone, select it to Get Noise Profile, then apply Noise Reduction gently."},{"criterion":"Music licensing","marks":2,"look":["licen|royalty|creative commons|permission|original","because|so|otherwise|copyright"],"min":2,"strong":"Licensing explained.","weak":"Say the music must be royalty-free, CC licensed or original, because commercial songs are protected by copyright."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> I would use a low angle. This makes the young sailor look confident and strong.</p><p><b>(b)</b> First, I would use Colors → Levels to make the photo brighter. Then I would use the Text tool to add \"Learn to sail this summer\" in white letters over the sky.</p><p><b>(c)</b> I would export it as a WebP file.</p><p><b>(d)</b> I would record room tone, then select it and choose Noise Reduction → Get Noise Profile, and apply Noise Reduction to the voice track.</p><p><b>(e)</b> The club can use music that is royalty-free, Creative Commons licensed or original, because a famous song is protected by copyright.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Use real menu names when you can, such as \"Colors → Levels\". For the music part, give the type of music and the reason. \"Because of copyright\" turns a 1-mark answer into a 2-mark answer.</p>","title":"Apply tools and techniques for web content (Foundation)"},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Structured response: production techniques and evaluation, Grade One extension (20 marks)","prompt":"<p>The club now also wants a 30-second vertical promo video for Instagram Reels from the same shoot. Write a response for the committee that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Justifies</b> a shot plan of THREE shots for the photo and video, naming the shot or angle, the depth of field and the lighting for each, and their effects. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li><b>Outlines</b> a non-destructive GIMP workflow for the hero photo, with menu names, and its web export settings and alt text. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Outlines</b> the podcast workflow, explaining how you would deal with steady noise AND a sudden noise, and the loudness and export settings. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explains</b> the editing and export of the Reel, including a J-cut or L-cut, captions and export settings. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluates</b> whether the club should use only its smartphone, or borrow a mirrorless camera, for this shoot. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":420,"max":750,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Shot 1: ... at ... in ... light, which ... Shot 2: ... Shot 3: ...<br><b>(b)</b> First, I would duplicate the layer ... Then Colors → ... I would use a layer mask ... Export ... Alt text: \"...\"<br><b>(c)</b> Steady noise such as ... I would ... A sudden noise such as ... I would ... Master near ... Export ...<br><b>(d)</b> I would use an L-cut so ... Captions ... Export ...<br><b>(e)</b> The smartphone ... However, a mirrorless camera ... On balance ...</p>","checklist":["My three shots each name an angle or shot type, depth of field and lighting, with effects.","My GIMP workflow is non-destructive and uses menu names.","I gave export settings and meaningful alt text for the photo.","I treated steady and sudden noise differently.","I gave loudness and MP3 export settings.","I explained a J-cut or L-cut, captions and MP4 H.264/AAC 1080 x 1920 export.","My evaluation weighs phone against mirrorless camera and reaches a judgement."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Justified shot plan","marks":5,"look":["low angle|high angle|eye level|close-up|wide|establishing|medium","f/\\d|aperture|depth of field|shallow|deep|bokeh","golden hour|shade|sun|light","third","effect|makes|so that|feel"],"min":4,"strong":"Three shots fully specified and justified.","weak":"For each shot give the angle or shot type, the depth of field and the light, and explain the effect on teenage viewers."},{"criterion":"Non-destructive photo workflow","marks":4,"look":["duplicate|copy","layer mask|mask|layer","level|curve|white balance","webp|jpe?g","alt"],"min":4,"strong":"An accurate non-destructive workflow with export and alt text.","weak":"Duplicate the layer, correct with Levels or Curves, keep text and masks on layers, export as WebP at a web width, and write alt text."},{"criterion":"Podcast noise, loudness and export","marks":5,"look":["room tone|noise profile","noise reduction","re-record|cut|edit out|sudden","lufs","mp3","licen|royalty|creative commons|original"],"min":5,"strong":"Steady and sudden noise handled correctly, with precise settings.","weak":"Use a noise profile for steady noise, re-record or cut sudden noises, master near -16 LUFS and export a tagged MP3 with licensed music."},{"criterion":"Reel editing and export","marks":3,"look":["j-cut|l-cut","caption","h\\.264|1080 ?x ?1920|mp4"],"min":3,"strong":"Edit technique, captions and export all correct.","weak":"Explain a J-cut or L-cut, add proofread captions and export MP4 (H.264/AAC) at 1080 x 1920."},{"criterion":"Evaluation of capture device","marks":3,"look":["phone|smartphone","mirrorless|camera|sensor|lens","however|but|whereas|on the other hand","on balance|therefore|overall|recommend"],"min":4,"strong":"A balanced evaluation with a justified judgement.","weak":"Give a strength of each device for this shoot, then make and justify a recommendation."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Shot 1 is the hero photo: a low-angle medium shot of a young sailor at the tiller, framed with the rule of thirds so she sits on the left third with open sky on the right for the headline. I would use a moderate aperture of about f/5.6 so she and the sail are sharp while the far shore softens slightly, in golden-hour light, which makes her look confident and the scene warm and inviting to teenagers. Shot 2 is a close-up of hands pulling the mainsheet at f/1.8, giving shallow depth of field and soft bokeh; the effect is energy and detail, showing what it feels like to sail. Shot 3 is an establishing wide shot of six dinghies crossing Jolly Harbour at f/11 for deep focus, so the whole fleet and coastline are sharp, which shows new members the scale of the club.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> In GIMP I would open the photo, use Layer → Duplicate Layer, hide the original and save as <i>sailing-hero_v1.xcf</i>. Next, Colors → Auto → White Balance and a gentle S-curve in Colors → Curves for contrast. If the background needs simplifying, I would use a layer mask rather than deleting pixels. The headline would go on its own text layer in white with a soft drop shadow. I would then Image → Scale Image to 1600 px wide, sharpen lightly, and File → Export As <i>sailing-club-hero.webp</i> at quality 80, under about 300 KB. Alt text: \"Teenage sailor steering a dinghy across Jolly Harbour at sunset\".</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> I would script the two coaches' conversation, record WAV in a quiet room with the USB mic and ten seconds of room tone, keeping peaks around -12 to -6 dB. For steady noise such as an air conditioner, I would select the room tone, Get Noise Profile and apply Noise Reduction gently at about 6 to 12 dB. A sudden noise such as a boat horn cannot be removed this way, so I would re-record that line or cut it if it is not essential. Licensed royalty-free or original music would go on a separate track with Auto Duck and fades. Then I would master near -16 LUFS with Loudness Normalization and export <i>sailing-club-ep01.mp3</i> at 128 kbps with ID3 tags.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> For the Reel I would use an L-cut, so a coach's voice saying \"Your first lesson starts on the beach\" continues while the picture cuts to the dinghies launching, which keeps the story flowing. I would add proofread open captions inside the safe area, because many viewers scroll on mute, and export an MP4 with H.264 video and AAC audio at 1080 x 1920.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> The smartphone is quick, shoots vertical video natively and allows instant upload, and the club already owns it. However, a mirrorless camera's larger sensor and interchangeable lenses give genuine shallow depth of field and better results in golden-hour low light, which matters most for the hero photo. On balance, I recommend borrowing the mirrorless camera for the hero photo and close-ups, and using the smartphone on a gimbal for the vertical Reel footage, so each device does the job it does best.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Treat each technical choice as setting plus effect. In part (c), examiners look for the difference between steady and sudden noise, a common source of lost marks. In part (e), \"evaluate\" means weighing both devices for this specific shoot and ending with a recommendation.</p>","title":"Apply tools and techniques for web content (Challenge)"}}]},{"id":"M2U6","title":"Website building with HTML and CSS","objectives":["Distinguish the roles of HTML (structure), CSS (presentation) and JavaScript (behaviour), then write a valid, tidily nested HTML document using doctype, head and body, headings, paragraphs, lists, tables, images with alt text and semantic tags (header, nav, main, section, footer).","Create internal, external and anchor hyperlinks, and open external links safely in a new tab with target=\"_blank\" and rel=\"noopener\".","Style pages with CSS selectors, properties and values, applying the box model, typography, colour (hex and rgb), backgrounds, borders, spacing and layout with flexbox and a simple grid; distinguish external, internal and inline CSS.","Embed images, video, audio and external widgets such as YouTube videos and Google Maps, and explain print versus screen resolution (dpi, px, rem) and responsive media with media queries.","Plan a multi-page site architecture from wireframes and mock-ups, and apply page design principles (contrast, grid systems, legibility, link styling) with UI and UX, responsiveness, WCAG accessibility and usability.","Build and test a mobile-first, one-page business site for Front Street, Philipsburg, with semantic HTML, an external style sheet, a Flexbox row and a media query, checked in browser developer tools (F12) at phone, tablet and desktop widths."],"videos":[{"title":"HTML in one lesson for beginners","q":"HTML tutorial for beginners full structure headings links images"},{"title":"CSS box model explained","q":"CSS box model explained margin border padding content beginners"},{"title":"Flexbox and CSS grid basics","q":"flexbox vs css grid basics tutorial beginners"},{"title":"Responsive design with media queries","q":"responsive web design media queries tutorial beginners"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Frame, walls, paint and electricity","html":"<p>Picture a building site. Every website you visit, from a national newspaper's news pages to a guesthouse booking page in Cane Garden Bay, runs on <b>three languages</b>. Each one has a different job, just as different trades work on a new house.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Language</th><th>Its job</th><th>In the house</th><th>What it handles on a page</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>HTML</b> (HyperText Markup Language)</td><td>Builds the <b>structure</b></td><td>The <b>frame and walls</b></td><td>Headings, paragraphs, images, links, lists</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>CSS</b> (Cascading Style Sheets)</td><td>Controls the <b>presentation</b></td><td>The <b>paint and landscaping</b></td><td>Colours, fonts, spacing and layout</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>JavaScript</b></td><td>Adds <b>behaviour</b></td><td>The <b>electricity</b></td><td>Actions and responses: a menu that opens when tapped, a photo slider, a form that checks your entry before it is sent</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>HTML always comes first.</b> You cannot paint a wall that has not been built, and you cannot wire a light switch into thin air. In the same way, CSS needs HTML elements to style, and JavaScript needs elements to react to. A page with HTML only is plain but still usable; beautiful CSS on broken HTML is a mess. This unit follows the same order: structure first, then style, then a layout that responds to every screen.</p>\n<p>Picture Maya, web developer at Island Pulse Media, building a page for a Road Town client. Her HTML creates a heading that reads \"Main Street Specials\". Her CSS turns that heading turquoise and centres it. A few lines of JavaScript make a \"Show more\" button reveal extra offers when a shopper taps it.</p>\n<p><b>Why keep the three apart?</b> Separation makes a site easier to maintain: when Zara, the creative director, asks for a new brand colour, Maya edits the CSS only and never touches the content. It also helps accessibility, because screen readers rely on clean HTML structure, not on how the page looks.</p>\n<p>This unit concentrates on HTML and CSS. You still need to know where JavaScript fits, because \"distinguish\" questions often ask you to separate the three roles.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A Paper 01 trap: HTML is a <b>markup</b> language, not a programming language; it describes content but makes no decisions. For a \"distinguish\" question, give both sides in one sentence with \"whereas\": \"HTML defines the structure and meaning of the content, whereas CSS controls how that content is presented.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Websites use three languages. HTML builds the structure, like the frame and walls of a house. CSS adds the look, like paint. JavaScript adds actions, like electricity. HTML always comes first. On a guesthouse page in Cane Garden Bay, HTML makes the heading, CSS colours it, and JavaScript makes a \"Show more\" button work.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Separation of concerns is a maintenance and accessibility principle, not just tidiness. Because content sits in HTML and presentation in CSS, a rebrand means editing one style sheet, and assistive technology can read meaning from clean structure regardless of how the page looks. The boundaries are not perfectly sharp: CSS can handle hover effects, transitions and even dark mode, and HTML attributes such as <b>required</b> can block an empty form without any JavaScript. A Grade One answer recognises this and chooses the simplest layer that does the job. Remember also that HTML is a markup language, not a programming language, because it makes no decisions.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"HTML, CSS or JavaScript?","intro":"<p>Maya is planning a page for the Sea Breeze Guesthouse in Cane Garden Bay, Tortola. Using the house analogy, decide which language handles each job: the frame and walls, the paint and landscaping, or the electricity.</p>","main":"Which language does this job?","bins":["HTML (structure)","CSS (presentation)","JavaScript (behaviour)"],"items":[{"t":"A list of three menu links: Home, Rooms, Contact","bin":0,"fb":"Lists and links are structure, built with ul, li and a."},{"t":"Using the same font on every page of the site","bin":1,"fb":"Fonts are presentation, best set once in an external style sheet."},{"t":"A thank-you message that pops up when a guest presses \"Book\"","bin":2,"fb":"Responding to a click is behaviour: the electricity."},{"t":"A main heading that says \"Sea Breeze Guesthouse\"","bin":0,"fb":"The heading itself is content and structure: part of the frame and walls."},{"t":"Placing a photo of the beach on the page","bin":0,"fb":"The img element adds the image to the structure. CSS may size it later, but HTML puts it there."},{"t":"A form that warns \"Check your phone number\" as soon as a guest types one digit too few","bin":2,"fb":"Checking input and responding instantly is an action and response: JavaScript."},{"t":"A slideshow that changes photo every five seconds","bin":2,"fb":"Changing the page over time is behaviour, handled by JavaScript."},{"t":"Stacking the room boxes in one column on a phone","bin":1,"fb":"Layout is presentation. CSS with a media query rearranges the same HTML."},{"t":"Making that heading purple and centred","bin":1,"fb":"Colour and alignment are presentation: the paint."}],"win":"Well sorted. HTML builds the structure (frame and walls), CSS controls the presentation (paint and landscaping) and JavaScript adds behaviour (electricity).","tutor":[{"h":"Is it there, does it look, or does it do?","p":"<p>If the job is about what exists on the page, think frame. If it is about how it looks, think paint. If something happens in response to the user, think wiring.</p>"},{"h":"Layout is a look","p":"<p>Moving boxes around on different screens changes appearance, not content.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"HTML, CSS or JavaScript? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A paragraph of text about the rooms","bin":0,"fb":"Content and structure: HTML."},{"t":"Turning the text blue","bin":1,"fb":"Colour is presentation: CSS."},{"t":"A menu that opens when tapped","bin":2,"fb":"An action and response: JavaScript."},{"t":"A bigger font size for headings","bin":1,"fb":"Size and style are presentation: CSS."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Trickier jobs: some sound like behaviour but are handled by HTML or CSS. Choose the language that normally does each job.</p>","items":[{"t":"A form field marked so the browser refuses to submit it empty","bin":0,"fb":"The HTML required attribute does this with no JavaScript."},{"t":"Links change colour when the mouse hovers over them","bin":1,"fb":"The :hover pseudo-class in CSS handles this; it is presentation, not scripting."},{"t":"A button's colour fades smoothly over 0.3 seconds when hovered","bin":1,"fb":"A CSS transition animates the change."},{"t":"The page switches to dark colours when the phone is set to dark mode","bin":1,"fb":"The CSS media feature prefers-color-scheme detects the setting."},{"t":"The lang attribute telling screen readers the page is in English","bin":0,"fb":"lang is an HTML attribute on the html element."},{"t":"Live ferry departure times update on the page without reloading","bin":2,"fb":"Fetching new data and updating the page while it is open is behaviour: JavaScript."},{"t":"The total price recalculates when a guest changes the number of nights","bin":2,"fb":"Calculating and responding to input is JavaScript."},{"t":"The sidebar is hidden on screens narrower than 600 px","bin":1,"fb":"A CSS media query changes the layout by screen width."},{"t":"A hamburger icon that opens and closes the menu each time it is tapped","bin":2,"fb":"Toggling the menu in response to taps is usually handled with JavaScript."},{"t":"Alt text describing the beach photo","bin":0,"fb":"The alt attribute is part of the HTML image element."}],"win":"Well analysed. Not every interactive-looking effect needs JavaScript: HTML attributes and CSS handle many of them, and the simplest layer is usually best.","title":"HTML, CSS or JavaScript? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"How an HTML document is built","html":"<p>Now look closely at the frame and walls. <b>HTML</b> gives a web page its <b>structure</b>: what is a heading, what is a paragraph, what is a link. <b>CSS</b> controls how it <b>looks</b>. A browser such as Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari reads both and draws the page.</p>\n<p>HTML is written with <b>elements</b>. Most elements have an <b>opening tag</b>, content and a <b>closing tag</b>: <code>&lt;p&gt;Welcome&lt;/p&gt;</code>. Some are <b>empty</b> (void) elements with no closing tag, such as <code>&lt;img&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;br&gt;</code>. Extra information goes in <b>attributes</b> inside the opening tag: <code>&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;</code>.</p>\n<p>Every page you build for Paper 02 or your SBA should start from this skeleton:</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;\n    &lt;meta charset=\"UTF-8\"&gt;\n    &lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;Guavaberry Grove | Home&lt;/title&gt;\n    &lt;link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"css/style.css\"&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Guavaberry Grove Guest House&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Quiet rooms ten minutes from Road Town.&lt;/p&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><b>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</b> tells the browser this is modern HTML5. Without it, browsers may fall back to \"quirks mode\" and display the page unpredictably.</li>\n<li><b>&lt;html&gt;</b> wraps everything. The <b>lang</b> attribute helps screen readers pronounce the text correctly.</li>\n<li><b>&lt;head&gt;</b> holds information <b>about</b> the page that visitors do not see in the window: the character set, the viewport setting for phones, the <b>&lt;title&gt;</b> shown on the browser tab and in search results, and links to CSS files.</li>\n<li><b>&lt;body&gt;</b> holds everything visitors <b>see</b>: text, images, links, video.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Tools.</b> Any plain-text editor will do. The next screen walks through the Notepad method, and once that feels natural you can move up to a code editor such as VS Code. Save the home page as <b>index.html</b>: web servers open this file automatically when someone visits a folder.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In Paper 02 you will not have the Internet, so you cannot copy a template. Practise typing the skeleton from memory until it takes you under two minutes. Use lowercase file names with no spaces (about.html, not About Us.html), because many web servers treat capital letters as different files.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>An HTML page is made of elements. Most have an opening tag, some content and a closing tag, like &lt;p&gt;Welcome&lt;/p&gt;. Every page starts with a skeleton: the doctype, then html, then head for page information and body for what visitors see. Save the home page as index.html, as you would for a Tortola guest house site.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Each skeleton line prevents a specific failure. Without the doctype, browsers may enter quirks mode and lay the page out unpredictably. Without the charset, accented names such as Kwéyòl or Curaçao may display as strange symbols. Without the viewport tag, phones shrink the page to a tiny desktop view. The <b>title</b> is easily underrated: it appears in the browser tab, in bookmarks and as the headline in search results, so it carries SEO weight. Attributes add information to elements, and their values belong in quotation marks. In Paper 02, a skeleton typed accurately from memory earns marks before you write any content.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Try it in Notepad","html":"<p>Before you open any special software, make sure you can build a page with nothing more than <b>Notepad</b> (on Windows) and a <b>browser</b>. If you can do it this way, you understand every line, and no exam room or school computer can stop you.</p>\n<p><b>An annotated skeleton.</b> The notes after each line are HTML comments (<code>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</code>), which the browser ignores, so you can type them in as reminders.</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;                &lt;!-- use modern HTML --&gt;\n&lt;html&gt;                         &lt;!-- the whole page --&gt;\n  &lt;head&gt;                       &lt;!-- page information --&gt;\n    &lt;title&gt;My page&lt;/title&gt;     &lt;!-- title on the browser tab --&gt;\n  &lt;/head&gt;\n  &lt;body&gt;                       &lt;!-- visible page content --&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Hello!&lt;/h1&gt;\n  &lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;                        &lt;!-- end of the page --&gt;</code></pre>\n<p><b>Try it in Notepad</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Type the skeleton.</b> Open Notepad and type it exactly. Change the title and heading to your own, for example \"Sea Breeze Guesthouse\" (a guesthouse in Cane Garden Bay).</li>\n<li><b>Choose Save As</b> from the File menu.</li>\n<li><b>Name it index.html and select All Files</b> in the \"Save as type\" box. Leave Encoding on UTF-8. If you forget All Files, Notepad saves <i>index.html.txt</i> and the browser shows your code as plain text instead of a page.</li>\n<li><b>Open it in a browser.</b> Double-click the file, or drag it into Chrome, Edge or Firefox. Your heading appears, and \"My page\" shows on the tab.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>The edit loop: edit, save, F5.</b> Change something in Notepad, save with Ctrl+S, switch to the browser and press <b>F5</b> to refresh. If nothing changes, you almost certainly forgot to save. Keep both windows open side by side and repeat the loop after every small change, so that when something breaks you know exactly which edit caused it.</p>\n<p>On a Mac, TextEdit works too, but first choose <b>Format, Make Plain Text</b>, or it will save formatted text instead of code.</p>\n<p><b>Moving up.</b> VS Code (free) follows the same loop but adds colour-coded syntax, auto-closing tags and the Live Server extension, which refreshes the browser for you each time you save.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 02 practical tip: turn on file extensions in File Explorer (View, then File name extensions) so you can see at a glance whether you saved <i>index.html</i> or <i>index.html.txt</i>. Keep the page and its images folder together in one project folder, and submit the folder, not just the HTML file.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>You can build a web page with only Notepad and a browser. Type the skeleton, choose Save As, name it index.html and pick All Files, so it is not saved as a text file. Open it in Chrome or Edge to see it. Then repeat: edit, save, press F5 to refresh. If nothing changes, you probably forgot to save.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The Notepad method builds a skill that tools can hide: knowing exactly which line produces which result. The edit-save-refresh loop is also a debugging method, because making one small change at a time tells you precisely what broke the page. Most beginner failures are file problems rather than code problems: index.html.txt saved by accident, file extensions hidden in File Explorer, or an image left outside the project folder so its path breaks. Code editors such as VS Code add colour coding and auto-closing tags, which reduce errors, but they cannot fix a missing file. In practical exams, submit the whole folder.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Build the page skeleton","intro":"<p>Maya at Island Pulse Media (a regional agency) is starting the home page for the Guavaberry Grove guest house. Put the lines of her HTML skeleton in the correct order, top to bottom.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;head&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;title&gt;Guavaberry Grove | Home&lt;/title&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;/head&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;body&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;h1&gt;Welcome to Guavaberry Grove&lt;/h1&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;"}],"win":"Correct structure. The doctype comes first, the head holds information about the page such as the title, and the body holds everything the visitor sees.","tutor":[{"h":"What comes before everything?","p":"<p>One line tells the browser which version of HTML to use. It sits above even the html tag.</p>"},{"h":"Head or body?","p":"<p>The text on the browser tab belongs to information about the page. Visible headings belong where the visitor looks.</p>"},{"h":"Closing order","p":"<p>The last tag opened is the first one closed.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Build the page skeleton (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put these four parts of an HTML page in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;head&gt; ... &lt;/head&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;body&gt; ... &lt;/body&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;/html&gt;"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>A fuller skeleton with semantic regions. Put all twelve lines in order, top to bottom. (The charset line must be the first thing inside the head.)</p>","blocks":[{"t":"&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;head&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;meta charset=\"UTF-8\"&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;title&gt;Guavaberry Grove | Home&lt;/title&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;/head&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;body&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;header&gt;&lt;h1&gt;Guavaberry Grove&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/header&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;main&gt;&lt;p&gt;Quiet rooms ten minutes from Road Town.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/main&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;footer&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sea Cows Bay, Tortola&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/footer&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;/body&gt;"},{"t":"&lt;/html&gt;"}],"win":"Correct. Doctype, html, a head with the charset first and the title, then a body with header, main and footer, each closed in reverse order.","title":"Build the page skeleton (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Tags, tidy nesting and a toolkit of tags","html":"<p><b>Tags make elements.</b> As a formula: <b>opening tag + content + closing tag = one element</b>. In <code>&lt;h1&gt;Sea Breeze Guesthouse&lt;/h1&gt;</code> (Cane Garden Bay guesthouse), &lt;h1&gt; opens, the words are the content and &lt;/h1&gt;, with its forward slash, closes.</p>\n<p><b>Nesting must be tidy.</b> In <code>&lt;p&gt;Stay in &lt;strong&gt;Cane Garden Bay&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</code> the strong element is the <b>inner element</b>, so it must close before the paragraph does. The rule: <b>close tags in reverse order, like putting lids back on nested boxes.</b> The last box opened gets its lid first. <code>&lt;p&gt;Stay in &lt;strong&gt;Cane Garden Bay&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;</code> is wrong: the lids are crossed. <b>Empty tags</b> such as &lt;br&gt; (line break) and &lt;img&gt; have no content and no closing tag.</p>\n<p><b>This toolkit</b> covers most pages:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Build with</th><th>Tags to know</th><th>What they do</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Headings and text</td><td>h1 to h6, p</td><td>Organise ideas and paragraphs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Links and lists</td><td>a, ul, ol, li</td><td>Connect pages; group items</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Images</td><td>img</td><td>src gives the file; alt describes it</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Meaningful emphasis</td><td>strong, em</td><td>Prefer these over b and i</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Semantic layout</td><td>header, nav, main, footer</td><td>Name each part of the page</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Headings</b> run from <code>&lt;h1&gt;</code> (most important) to <code>&lt;h6&gt;</code>. Use <b>one h1</b> per page for the main title, then h2 for sections and h3 for sub-sections. Search engines and screen readers use headings to understand the page, so never pick a heading level just because you like its size: change size with CSS instead.</p>\n<p><b>Paragraphs</b> use <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>. <code>&lt;strong&gt;</code> marks important text and <code>&lt;em&gt;</code> adds emphasis. Use them rather than the older <code>&lt;b&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;i&gt;</code>: b and i only change the look, whereas strong and em carry meaning that screen readers can convey to the listener.</p>\n<p><b>Lists</b>: <code>&lt;ul&gt;</code> is an unordered (bulleted) list, <code>&lt;ol&gt;</code> is an ordered (numbered) list, and each item is an <code>&lt;li&gt;</code>. Room features suit a ul; numbered driving directions from the airport suit an ol. Navigation menus are usually built as a ul of links.</p>\n<p><b>Tables</b> are for <b>data</b>, such as room rates, never for page layout. <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> holds rows (<code>&lt;tr&gt;</code>); header cells are <code>&lt;th&gt;</code> and data cells are <code>&lt;td&gt;</code>.</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;table&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Room&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Per night&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n  &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Garden&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;US$95&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n&lt;/table&gt;</code></pre>\n<p><b>Semantic tags</b> describe the <b>meaning</b> of each region, not its look:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Tag</th><th>Purpose</th></tr>\n<tr><td>&lt;header&gt;</td><td>Top of the page or a section: logo, site name</td></tr>\n<tr><td>&lt;nav&gt;</td><td>The main navigation links</td></tr>\n<tr><td>&lt;main&gt;</td><td>The unique main content of this page (one per page)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>&lt;section&gt;</td><td>A themed group of content with its own heading, such as \"Rooms\" or \"Reviews\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td>&lt;footer&gt;</td><td>Bottom: contact details, copyright, social links</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Older sites used <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> for everything. A div still works for styling, but semantic tags help screen readers jump straight to the navigation or main content, and help search engines index the page. That links accessibility and SEO, two areas examiners value.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> If a Paper 02 question asks you to \"explain\" why you used semantic tags, give a reason plus a benefit: \"I used &lt;nav&gt; so that screen-reader users can jump directly to the menu, making the site more accessible.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Tags make elements, and they must close in reverse order, like putting lids back on boxes inside boxes. Use h1 for the main title, h2 and h3 for sections, p for paragraphs, ul and ol for lists, and table only for data. Semantic tags such as header, nav, main and footer name each part of the page, which helps screen readers.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Semantic HTML is about <b>meaning</b>, which is why heading levels must follow the content's structure rather than the size you like. A screen-reader user can jump from heading to heading or straight to the nav or main region, so skipped or decorative headings break that navigation. Content rules also apply: a p cannot contain a heading, and strong cannot wrap a paragraph, because strong is phrasing content. Browsers silently \"repair\" such mistakes, often producing a structure you did not intend. The choice between strong and b is not about appearance: strong marks importance, while b is purely stylistic. Valid, semantic markup also helps search engines.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the nesting error","intro":"<p>Malik (video and animation, Island Pulse Media) typed some lines for the Sea Breeze Guesthouse page while Maya was at a client meeting. Use the nested-boxes rule: the last box opened gets its lid first, and empty tags have no lid at all.</p>","q":"Select every line with a nesting or closing-tag error.","lines":["&lt;p&gt;Stay in &lt;strong&gt;Cane Garden Bay&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;","&lt;p&gt;Book &lt;em&gt;today&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/em&gt;","&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;Sea view&lt;/li&gt;&lt;li&gt;Free Wi-Fi&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;","&lt;h1&gt;Sea Breeze &lt;strong&gt;Guesthouse&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;","&lt;a href=\"rooms.html\"&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Our rooms&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"contact.html\"&gt;Contact&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;p&gt;Call us&lt;br&gt;+1 284 555 0123&lt;/p&gt;","&lt;img src=\"pool.jpg\" alt=\"Pool at the guesthouse\"&gt;&lt;/img&gt;","&lt;footer&gt;&lt;p&gt;Cane Garden Bay, Tortola&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/footer&gt;"],"correct":[1,3,5,7],"win":"Sharp eyes. Three lines had crossed lids (em, strong and a closed after their outer element) and one gave the empty img tag a closing tag it must not have. Tidy nesting means closing tags in reverse order.","miss":"Not all found. For each line, read the opening tags left to right, then check that the closing tags appear in the reverse order. Remember that br and img are empty tags.","tutor":[{"h":"Read the lids backwards","p":"<p>Write down the opening tags in order. The closing tags must be that list read backwards.</p>"},{"h":"Empty boxes","p":"<p>Two tags in this list never hold content. Does either of them have a closing tag?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the nesting error (Foundation)","q":"Select the two lines with crossed tags.","lines":["&lt;p&gt;Stay in &lt;strong&gt;Cane Garden Bay&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;","&lt;p&gt;Book &lt;em&gt;now&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/em&gt;","&lt;h2&gt;Our rooms&lt;/h2&gt;","&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"contact.html\"&gt;Contact&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/a&gt;"],"correct":[1,3],"win":"Yes. In both lines the inner element closed after the outer one: the lids were crossed.","miss":"Check which tag opened last in each line. It must close first."},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder errors. Some lines look odd but are valid, and some break HTML content rules (for example, a paragraph cannot contain a heading).</p>","q":"Select every line that contains a nesting, closing-tag or content-rule error.","lines":["&lt;p&gt;Our &lt;em&gt;best &lt;strong&gt;rooms&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;","&lt;a href=\"rooms.html\"&gt;&lt;p&gt;See all rooms&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;p&gt;&lt;h2&gt;Rates&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;/p&gt;","&lt;p&gt;Call us&lt;br&gt;&lt;/br&gt;+1 284 555 0123&lt;/p&gt;","&lt;img src=\"pool.jpg\" alt=\"Pool at the guesthouse\"&gt;","&lt;h1&gt;Sea Breeze Guesthouse&lt;/h2&gt;","&lt;nav&gt;&lt;ul&gt;&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"index.html\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;&lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;","&lt;strong&gt;&lt;p&gt;Book now&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;","&lt;table&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Room&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Rate&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;","&lt;footer&gt;&lt;p&gt;Cane Garden Bay, Tortola&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/footer&gt;"],"correct":[0,2,3,5,7],"win":"Excellent. You found crossed lids, a heading inside a paragraph, a closing tag on the empty br, mismatched heading tags, and a paragraph wrapped in strong. A link wrapping a paragraph is valid in HTML5.","miss":"Look again. Check the reverse-order rule, empty tags (br has no closing tag), matching opening and closing names, and content rules: p cannot contain headings, and strong cannot contain a paragraph. A link around a paragraph is allowed.","title":"Spot the nesting error (Challenge)"}},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the tag to its job","intro":"<p>Maya is marking up the Guavaberry Grove home page. Match each HTML element to the content it should hold.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"&lt;nav&gt;","r":"The links Home, Rooms, Gallery and Contact"},{"l":"&lt;footer&gt;","r":"Phone number, address in Sea Cows Bay and copyright line"},{"l":"&lt;h1&gt;","r":"The single main title: Guavaberry Grove Guest House"},{"l":"&lt;ol&gt;","r":"Numbered driving directions from the airport on Beef Island"},{"l":"&lt;table&gt;","r":"Room types and their nightly rates"},{"l":"&lt;section&gt;","r":"A themed block headed \"Guest reviews\""},{"l":"&lt;main&gt;","r":"The unique content of this page, used once"},{"l":"&lt;title&gt;","r":"Text shown on the browser tab and in search results"}],"win":"Well matched. You chose elements by meaning, not by appearance, which is the core idea of semantic HTML.","tutor":[{"h":"Meaning, not looks","p":"<p>Ask \"what is this content?\" not \"how big should it be?\"</p>"},{"h":"Order matters?","p":"<p>If the order of items matters, use the numbered list. If you compare values in rows and columns, use the data element.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the tag (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"&lt;nav&gt;","r":"The main menu links"},{"l":"&lt;footer&gt;","r":"Contact details and copyright"},{"l":"&lt;ul&gt;","r":"A bulleted list of room features"},{"l":"&lt;h1&gt;","r":"The main page title"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Closer choices. Match each element to the content it should hold on the Guavaberry Grove Rooms page.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"&lt;strong&gt;","r":"Important text: \"Check-out is at 11 a.m.\""},{"l":"&lt;em&gt;","r":"Stress that changes meaning: \"We are not a party hotel\" with stress on \"not\""},{"l":"&lt;ol&gt;","r":"Steps from the Road Town ferry terminal to the guest house, in order"},{"l":"&lt;ul&gt;","r":"Room features in no particular order"},{"l":"&lt;th&gt;","r":"The \"Room\" and \"Per night\" header cells of the rates table"},{"l":"&lt;section&gt;","r":"A themed block with its own heading: \"Things to do in Tortola\""},{"l":"&lt;main&gt;","r":"The content unique to this page, used only once"},{"l":"&lt;header&gt;","r":"The logo and site name at the top of every page"},{"l":"&lt;title&gt;","r":"Text on the browser tab and the headline in search results"},{"l":"&lt;meta name=\"description\"&gt;","r":"A summary that search engines may show under the result"}],"win":"Well matched. You chose by meaning in every case, including the subtle pairs: importance (strong) versus stress (em), order (ol) versus no order (ul), and tab title versus search summary.","title":"Match the tag to its job (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Hyperlinks and images","html":"<p>A <b>hyperlink</b> uses the anchor element <code>&lt;a&gt;</code> with an <b>href</b> (hypertext reference) attribute. The course outline asks you to know four kinds.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Link type</th><th>What it does</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Internal</b></td><td>Goes to another page on the same site, using a <b>relative path</b></td><td><code>&lt;a href=\"rooms.html\"&gt;Rooms&lt;/a&gt;</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>External</b></td><td>Goes to a different website, using an <b>absolute URL</b> starting https://</td><td><code>&lt;a href=\"https://www.thedailyherald.sx\"&gt;Local news&lt;/a&gt;</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Anchor</b> (in-page)</td><td>Jumps to an element with a matching <b>id</b> on the same page</td><td><code>&lt;a href=\"#rates\"&gt;See rates&lt;/a&gt;</code> ... <code>&lt;section id=\"rates\"&gt;</code></td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>New tab</b></td><td>Opens the link in a new browser tab</td><td><code>&lt;a href=\"https://...\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"&gt;</code></td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Why rel=\"noopener\"?</b> A page opened with target=\"_blank\" can, in older browsers, use a script to change the page that opened it, for example swapping your booking page for a fake one (a phishing trick). <b>rel=\"noopener\"</b> cuts that connection. Use new tabs only for external links, and warn users (\"opens in a new tab\"), because unexpected tabs confuse screen-reader users.</p>\n<p>Other useful hrefs: <code>mailto:info@guavaberrygrove.example</code> opens an email and <code>tel:+12845550000</code> dials on a phone.</p>\n<p><b>Relative paths</b> depend on your folders. If rooms.html is in the same folder, write <code>rooms.html</code>; if an image is in a subfolder, write <code>images/pool.jpg</code>; to go up one folder, write <code>../index.html</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Images</b> use the empty element <code>&lt;img&gt;</code>:</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;img src=\"images/garden-room.jpg\"\n     alt=\"Garden room with a double bed and a view of the hills\"\n     width=\"800\" height=\"533\"&gt;</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><b>src</b>: the path to the file.</li>\n<li><b>alt</b>: a short description read aloud by screen readers and shown if the image fails to load. <b>Good alt text describes what the image shows</b>, for example <code>alt=\"Sea Breeze Guesthouse in Cane Garden Bay\"</code>, not <code>alt=\"image1\"</code> or <code>alt=\"photo\"</code>. A purely decorative image gets an empty alt (<code>alt=\"\"</code>).</li>\n<li><b>width</b> and <b>height</b> reserve space so the page does not jump while loading.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> The most common Paper 02 coding errors are a missing closing <code>&lt;/a&gt;</code>, a misspelled file name in src or href, and missing alt text. Before you finish, click every link and check every image.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Links use the a tag with an href. Internal links go to another page on your site, external links start with https://, anchor links jump to a part of the same page, and new-tab links use target=\"_blank\" with rel=\"noopener\". Images use img with src for the file and alt to describe it, such as \"Sea Breeze Guesthouse in Cane Garden Bay\".</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Relative paths are where most link errors hide. A path is read from the location of the current page, so a page in a blog subfolder must use ../index.html to reach the home page. Ids used for anchor links are case-sensitive, so #rates will not find id=\"Rates\". Alt text depends on function: a logo that links to the home page needs alt text naming its destination, such as \"Guavaberry Grove home\", while a decorative divider needs an empty alt. Link text should describe the destination, because screen-reader users often browse a list of links out of context, where \"click here\" means nothing.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the link and image errors","intro":"<p>Malik tried to help Maya by adding links and images to the Guavaberry Grove pages, but some lines contain errors. All the files are in the same folder except images, which are in an <b>images</b> subfolder.</p>","q":"Select every line that contains an error.","lines":["&lt;a href=\"rooms.html\"&gt;Our rooms&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;a href=\"gallery.html\"&gt;Gallery","&lt;img src=\"images/pool.jpg\" alt=\"Pool surrounded by palm trees\"&gt;","&lt;img src=\"images/beach.jpg\"&gt;","&lt;a href=\"#contact\"&gt;Contact us&lt;/a&gt; ... &lt;section id=\"contact\"&gt;","&lt;a href=\"www.google.com/maps\" target=\"_blank\"&gt;Map&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;a href=\"https://www.youtube.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"&gt;Our video&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;img src=\"Images/Garden Room.JPG\" alt=\"Garden room\"&gt;"],"correct":[1,3,5,7],"win":"Sharp eyes. You found the missing closing tag, the missing alt text, the external link without https:// or rel=\"noopener\", and the file path with capitals and a space.","miss":"Look again. Check each line for a closing &lt;/a&gt;, an alt attribute on every image, https:// on external links with rel=\"noopener\", and lowercase file names with no spaces.","tutor":[{"h":"Four checks","p":"<p>Closing tags, alt text, full URLs for external sites, and file names that match exactly.</p>"},{"h":"Exact names","p":"<p>Many web servers treat Images and images as different folders. Spaces in file names also cause broken links.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the errors (Foundation)","q":"Select the two lines with errors.","lines":["&lt;a href=\"rooms.html\"&gt;Rooms&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;img src=\"images/pool.jpg\"&gt;","&lt;a href=\"about.html\"&gt;About","&lt;img src=\"images/bar.jpg\" alt=\"Beach bar at sunset\"&gt;"],"correct":[1,2],"win":"Yes: one image has no alt text and one link has no closing tag.","miss":"Check for a missing alt attribute and a missing &lt;/a&gt;."},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder checks. All pages are in the main folder except <b>blog/hurricane-tips.html</b>, which is in a <b>blog</b> subfolder. Images are in <b>images/</b> in the main folder.</p>","q":"Select every line that is wrong or poor practice.","lines":["On index.html: &lt;a href=\"rooms.html\"&gt;Our rooms&lt;/a&gt;","On blog/hurricane-tips.html: &lt;a href=\"index.html\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;","On blog/hurricane-tips.html: &lt;img src=\"../images/kit.jpg\" alt=\"Hurricane kit with bottled water and a torch\"&gt;","&lt;a href=\"#rates\"&gt;See rates&lt;/a&gt; ... &lt;section id=\"Rates\"&gt;","&lt;a href=\"mailto:info@guavaberrygrove.example\"&gt;Email us&lt;/a&gt;","The site logo, which links to the home page: &lt;img src=\"images/logo.png\" alt=\"\"&gt;","A purely decorative divider: &lt;img src=\"images/divider.png\" alt=\"\"&gt;","&lt;a href=\"https://www.thedailyherald.sx\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"&gt;Local news (opens in a new tab)&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;a href=\"rooms.html\"&gt;Click here&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;img src=\"images/pool.jpg\" alt=\"image of pool\"&gt;"],"correct":[1,3,5,8,9],"win":"Sharp checking. The blog page needs ../index.html, ids are case-sensitive, a linked logo needs alt text, \"Click here\" says nothing about the destination, and alt text should describe the scene, not begin with \"image of\".","miss":"Look again. Think about where each page sits in the folders, whether the id matches exactly, whether an image does a job (a linked logo) or is decoration, and whether link and alt text are meaningful.","title":"Spot the link and image errors (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"First CSS: the style attribute, rules, selectors and the cascade","html":"<p><b>A first taste: the style attribute.</b> CSS can start inside an HTML tag. Write <b>property:value;</b> pairs in a <code>style</code> attribute on the opening tag:</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;p style=\"color:purple; text-align:center;\"&gt;\n  Welcome to Tortola!\n&lt;/p&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>Starter properties: colour (<code>color</code>, <code>background-color</code>), type (<code>font-family</code>, <code>font-size</code>) and alignment (<code>text-align</code>).</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Paper 02 tip:</b> This course is written in British English, but CSS only understands <b>American spelling</b>: <code>color</code>, not colour, and <code>center</code>, not centre. The browser silently ignores <code>colour: purple;</code>, so the text stays black and no error appears. Check every color and center before you submit.</p>\n<p><b>Obsolete relics.</b> Old pages may use <code>&lt;font&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;marquee&gt;</code> (scrolling text) and the <code>bgcolor</code> attribute. Recognise them, but avoid them: use CSS instead.</p>\n<p><b>Anatomy of a CSS rule</b>: <code>p { color:pink; font-weight:bold; }</code></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Part</th><th>Here</th><th>Job</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Selector</b></td><td>p</td><td>Chooses every paragraph</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Property</b></td><td>color, font-weight</td><td>Names what changes</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Value</b></td><td>pink, bold</td><td>Sets the new appearance</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Inside the curly braces, each property and value pair is a <b>declaration</b>; end each one with a semicolon. A line to remember: <b>HTML is the foundation; CSS decides gingerbread cottage or glass villa.</b></p>\n<p><b>Three basic selectors</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Selector</th><th>Syntax</th><th>Targets</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Element</b> (type)</td><td><code>p { }</code></td><td>Every p element on the page</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Class</b></td><td><code>.btn { }</code> with <code>class=\"btn\"</code></td><td>Any element with that class; can be reused many times</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Id</b></td><td><code>#booking { }</code> with <code>id=\"booking\"</code></td><td>One unique element; each id is used only once per page</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>You can also group selectors (<code>h1, h2 { }</code>), select descendants (<code>nav a { }</code> for links inside the nav) and style states with pseudo-classes such as <code>a:hover</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Three places to put CSS</b> (one external style sheet can style the whole site)</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>External</b>: a separate .css file, linked in the head of each page with <code>&lt;link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\"&gt;</code>. Best for consistent styling across the whole site: change the brand colour once and every page updates.</li>\n<li><b>Internal</b>: a <code>&lt;style&gt;</code> element inside the head. Useful for one unique page.</li>\n<li><b>Inline</b>: a style attribute on one HTML element. Changes just that element; hard to maintain.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>The cascade.</b> When rules clash, inline styles beat internal and external ones for normal declarations. Next, specificity: id beats class, class beats element. When rules have equal priority and specificity, <b>the last equal rule wins</b>.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Distinguish between external and inline CSS\" needs both sides on the same feature: \"External CSS is stored in a separate .css file that can style every page of a site, whereas inline CSS is written inside one element's style attribute and affects only that element.\" Then add a consequence for maintenance.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>CSS rules have a selector, then properties and values inside curly braces, such as p { color: purple; }. Selectors can pick elements, classes (with a dot) or one id (with a hash). CSS can be external in a .css file, internal in a style tag, or inline on one tag. CSS uses American spelling: color and center, never colour.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The cascade resolves conflicts in a fixed order: importance (inline beats style sheets for normal declarations), then specificity (id beats class, class beats element), then source order (the last equal rule wins). A common misconception is that \"internal beats external\"; neither wins by type, so position in the code decides. Specificity explains many \"my CSS is not working\" problems: a rule on #intro will beat a later rule on p, however far down it appears. Good practice keeps specificity low, using classes rather than ids for styling, so that rules stay easy to override. Silent failures, such as colour: or a missing semicolon, are the other common cause.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"External, internal or inline?","intro":"<p>Maya is tidying the CSS for the Sea Breeze Guesthouse site before Zara reviews it. Decide where each piece of CSS lives, or which method each description fits.</p>","main":"Is this external, internal or inline CSS?","bins":["External","Internal","Inline"],"items":[{"t":"A &lt;style&gt; element inside the head of one page","bin":1,"fb":"CSS written in a style element in the head is internal (embedded) CSS."},{"t":"One file called style.css shared by all five pages","bin":0,"fb":"A shared separate file is the whole point of external CSS."},{"t":"&lt;h1 style=\"text-align:center;\"&gt;Sea Breeze&lt;/h1&gt;","bin":2,"fb":"Another style attribute inside an opening tag: inline."},{"t":"Best for consistent styling across the whole site","bin":0,"fb":"Change one file and every linked page updates."},{"t":"&lt;p style=\"color:purple;\"&gt;","bin":2,"fb":"A style attribute on one element is inline CSS."},{"t":"Useful for one unique page, such as a one-off Carnival countdown page","bin":1,"fb":"Internal CSS suits a single page that needs its own styles."},{"t":"&lt;link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\"&gt; in the head","bin":0,"fb":"The link tag connects the page to a separate .css file: external CSS."},{"t":"Changes just that one element","bin":2,"fb":"Inline CSS affects only the element it is written on."},{"t":"&lt;style&gt; body { font-family: Arial; } &lt;/style&gt;","bin":1,"fb":"Rules inside a style element are internal CSS."}],"win":"Well sorted. External CSS is a separate linked .css file for the whole site, internal CSS is a style element in one page's head, and inline CSS is a style attribute on one element.","tutor":[{"h":"Where does it live?","p":"<p>In a separate file, in the head of one page, or inside one opening tag?</p>"},{"h":"How far does it reach?","p":"<p>The whole site, one page, or one element?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"External, internal or inline? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A separate style.css file","bin":0,"fb":"A separate linked file: external."},{"t":"A &lt;style&gt; element in the head","bin":1,"fb":"Inside one page's head: internal."},{"t":"style=\"color:red;\" on one paragraph","bin":2,"fb":"On one element: inline."},{"t":"Styles every page of the site at once","bin":0,"fb":"Only an external sheet reaches every page."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","main":"Where should the CSS go?","intro":"<p>Now recommend: where SHOULD the CSS go in each situation?</p>","items":[{"t":"The brand colour and fonts for all 12 pages of the site","bin":0,"fb":"One external file keeps every page consistent and changes in one place."},{"t":"The team wants browsers to download the styles once and reuse them on every page, so later pages load faster","bin":0,"fb":"An external style sheet is cached by the browser and reused across pages."},{"t":"A one-off Carnival countdown page with styles used nowhere else on the site","bin":1,"fb":"Internal CSS in that page's head keeps unique styles with the page that needs them."},{"t":"A quick test of one paragraph's colour while debugging, to be removed afterwards","bin":2,"fb":"A temporary inline style is a quick way to test one element."},{"t":"An HTML email newsletter, because many email programs ignore linked style sheets","bin":2,"fb":"Email newsletters often rely on inline styles because many email clients strip linked and embedded CSS."},{"t":"A shared header style that every group member's page must use in a collaborative site build","bin":0,"fb":"A shared external sheet is what makes a group site look like one site."},{"t":"A single landing page whose few styles should arrive with the HTML in one file","bin":1,"fb":"Internal CSS travels inside the page, so no extra file is needed."},{"t":"Changing the size of one special heading on one page only, as a temporary fix","bin":2,"fb":"Inline CSS affects only that element, though a class in the style sheet is better long-term."}],"win":"Strong recommendations. External CSS suits whole-site consistency and caching, internal suits one unique page, and inline suits one element or special cases such as email.","title":"External, internal or inline? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"mcq","title":"Which rule wins the cascade?","q":"The Sea Breeze Guesthouse page links style.css, which contains p { color: blue; }. Further down the head, after the link, an internal style element contains p { color: green; }. One paragraph is written as &lt;p style=\"color:red;\"&gt;. No rule uses !important. What colours appear?","options":[{"t":"That paragraph is red; every other paragraph is blue, because external beats internal.","fb":"Half right. Neither external nor internal wins by type; with equal specificity, the rule that comes last wins, and the internal rule comes after the link."},{"t":"That paragraph is red; every other paragraph is green.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Inline beats the others for normal declarations, so the styled paragraph is red. The external and internal rules have equal priority and specificity (both select p), so the last one in the code, the internal green rule, wins."},{"t":"Every paragraph is green, because internal CSS beats inline CSS.","fb":"Inline styles beat internal and external rules for normal declarations, so the styled paragraph stays red."},{"t":"Every paragraph is blue, because the external style sheet always wins.","fb":"External CSS does not automatically win. It is the easiest to maintain, not the strongest in the cascade."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Step one: inline","p":"<p>Deal with the paragraph that has its own style attribute first. One method always beats the others.</p>"},{"h":"Step two: a tie","p":"<p>Both remaining rules select p. When priority and specificity are equal, what decides?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which rule wins? (Foundation)","q":"A style sheet says p { color: blue; }, but one paragraph has style=\"color:red;\". What colour is that paragraph?","options":[{"t":"Blue","fb":"The style sheet loses to the inline style on that element."},{"t":"Red","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Inline styles beat style-sheet rules for normal declarations."},{"t":"Purple, a mix of the two","fb":"CSS never mixes clashing values; one rule wins."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","q":"style.css (linked first) contains #intro { color: navy; }. A later internal style element contains p { color: green; } and .note { color: orange; }. The page has &lt;p id=\"intro\" class=\"note\"&gt; and a second paragraph &lt;p class=\"note\"&gt;. No inline styles or !important are used. What colours appear?","options":[{"t":"The intro paragraph is navy; the second paragraph is orange.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. The id rule has the highest specificity for the first paragraph, even though it appears earlier. The second paragraph matches the class and element rules, and the class wins."},{"t":"Both paragraphs are orange, because class rules are read last.","fb":"The intro paragraph also matches an id rule, and an id beats a class, wherever it appears."},{"t":"Both paragraphs are green, because the internal rules come last.","fb":"Source order only decides between rules of equal specificity. Id and class selectors beat the element selector."},{"t":"The intro paragraph is navy; the second paragraph is green.","fb":"Half right. For the second paragraph, the class .note is more specific than the element selector p."}],"title":"Which rule wins the cascade? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Element, class or id selector?","intro":"<p>Maya is writing the style sheet for Guavaberry Grove. Decide which kind of selector each rule uses.</p>","main":"Which type of selector is this?","bins":["Element selector","Class selector","Id selector"],"items":[{"t":"#hero { height: 60vh; }","bin":2,"fb":"The single large banner at the top of the home page is unique, so an id fits."},{"t":".highlight { color: #b8860b; }","bin":1,"fb":"A class you can add to any text you want to highlight."},{"t":"p { line-height: 1.6; }","bin":0,"fb":"No dot or hash: it targets every p element."},{"t":"h2 { color: #7a1f3d; }","bin":0,"fb":"All h2 headings are styled; this is an element selector."},{"t":"footer { text-align: center; }","bin":0,"fb":"footer is an HTML element name, so this is an element selector."},{"t":".room-card { border: 1px solid #ccc; }","bin":1,"fb":"The dot shows a class. Every room card can share it."},{"t":".btn { padding: 12px 24px; }","bin":1,"fb":"A reusable button style is a class: many buttons can use it."},{"t":"#booking-form { background: #fff7e6; }","bin":2,"fb":"The hash shows an id. There is only one booking form on the page."}],"win":"Correct. Element selectors use the tag name, class selectors start with a dot and can be reused, and id selectors start with a hash and target one unique element.","tutor":[{"h":"Look at the first character","p":"<p>A dot, a hash, or neither?</p>"},{"h":"Reuse test","p":"<p>Would you want this style on many things, or just one? That tells you which selector a good developer would choose.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Element, class or id? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"p { }","bin":0,"fb":"Tag name only: element."},{"t":".btn { }","bin":1,"fb":"Dot: class."},{"t":"#hero { }","bin":2,"fb":"Hash: id."},{"t":"nav { }","bin":0,"fb":"Tag name only: element."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>A fourth bin now: combined selectors (groups, descendants and pseudo-classes). Sort each rule by its selector type.</p>","bins":["Element selector","Class selector","Id selector","Combined selector"],"items":[{"t":"a:hover { text-decoration: none; }","bin":3,"fb":"A pseudo-class selects links in their hover state."},{"t":"nav a { color: #ffffff; }","bin":3,"fb":"A descendant selector: links inside nav."},{"t":"li { margin-bottom: 8px; }","bin":0,"fb":"Every li element."},{"t":".price { font-weight: 700; }","bin":1,"fb":"The dot marks a class."},{"t":"#hero { min-height: 60vh; }","bin":2,"fb":"The hash marks an id."},{"t":"h1, h2 { font-family: Georgia, serif; }","bin":3,"fb":"A group selector: one rule for two element types."},{"t":".room-card img { border-radius: 8px; }","bin":3,"fb":"A descendant selector: images inside room cards."},{"t":".btn { padding: 12px 24px; }","bin":1,"fb":"A reusable class for buttons."},{"t":"#booking-form { max-width: 480px; }","bin":2,"fb":"One unique form, selected by id."},{"t":"section { padding: 24px; }","bin":0,"fb":"A single tag name: an element selector."}],"win":"Well sorted. Single selectors target one kind of thing; combined selectors group, nest or add a state, which lets you style precisely without extra classes.","title":"Element, class or id selector? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"The box model, typography and colour","html":"<p>Every HTML element is drawn as a rectangular <b>box</b>. The <b>box model</b> has four layers, from inside out:</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 180\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:360px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Box model: margin outside border, border outside padding, padding around content\">\n<rect x=\"5\" y=\"5\" width=\"310\" height=\"170\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 4\"/>\n<rect x=\"35\" y=\"30\" width=\"250\" height=\"120\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"4\"/>\n<rect x=\"70\" y=\"55\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<text x=\"12\" y=\"22\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"currentColor\">margin</text>\n<text x=\"42\" y=\"47\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"currentColor\">border</text>\n<text x=\"76\" y=\"72\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"currentColor\">padding</text>\n<text x=\"132\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"currentColor\">content</text>\n</svg>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Content</b>: the text or image, sized with width and height.</li>\n<li><b>Padding</b>: space <b>inside</b> the border, between content and edge. It takes the element's background colour.</li>\n<li><b>Border</b>: a line around the padding: <code>border: 2px solid #7a1f3d;</code> Add <code>border-radius: 8px;</code> for rounded corners.</li>\n<li><b>Margin</b>: space <b>outside</b> the border, pushing other elements away. It is always transparent.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>By default, width sets only the content, so padding and border make a box wider than you planned. Most developers add <code>* { box-sizing: border-box; }</code> so width includes padding and border.</p>\n<pre><code>.room-card {\n  width: 300px;\n  padding: 16px;\n  margin: 0 auto 24px;  /* top, left/right, bottom */\n  border: 1px solid #d9c7b0;\n  background-color: #fffaf3;\n}</code></pre>\n<p><b>Typography.</b> <code>font-family</code> sets the typeface with fallbacks (<code>font-family: \"Open Sans\", Arial, sans-serif;</code>); <code>font-size</code>, <code>font-weight</code> (400 normal, 700 bold), <code>line-height</code> (1.5 to 1.7 is comfortable for body text), <code>text-align</code> and <code>letter-spacing</code> refine it. Use no more than two typefaces: one for headings, one for body.</p>\n<p><b>Colour.</b> CSS accepts colour names (<code>red</code>), <b>hex</b> codes (<code>#7a1f3d</code>: two digits each for red, green and blue, from 00 to ff) and <b>rgb()</b> (<code>rgb(122, 31, 61)</code>, each channel 0 to 255). <code>rgba()</code> or <code>rgb(122 31 61 / 50%)</code> adds transparency. <code>color</code> sets text colour; <code>background-color</code> sets the fill.</p>\n<p><b>Backgrounds.</b> <code>background-image: url(\"../images/beach.jpg\");</code> with <code>background-size: cover;</code> and <code>background-position: center;</code> creates a full-width hero image. Always set a background colour too, in case the image fails, and check that text on top stays readable.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Remember the box model from the inside out with \"<b>C</b>ats <b>P</b>refer <b>B</b>eing <b>M</b>assaged\": content, padding, border, margin. Paper 01 often asks which layer is transparent (margin) or which takes the background colour (padding).</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Every HTML element is a box with four layers: content in the middle, then padding, then a border, then margin on the outside. Padding takes the background colour; margin is always see-through. CSS also sets fonts, sizes, line spacing and colours, using names, hex codes such as #7a1f3d, or rgb(). Use no more than two typefaces.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The box model explains a classic layout surprise: with the default content-box sizing, a card set to width: 300px with 16px padding and a 1px border actually takes 334px, which can push a three-card row onto two lines. Setting box-sizing: border-box makes the declared width include padding and border, which is why most developers apply it to every element. Hex codes are rgb values in base 16, so rgb(255, 204, 0) is #ffcc00. For legibility, use relative font sizes such as rem and a line-height of about 1.5 to 1.7. Always give a background-image a fallback background colour, so text stays readable if the image fails to load.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"CSS quick-fire","intro":"<p>Keisha (marketing, Island Pulse Media) keeps asking Maya for small changes to the Guavaberry Grove site. Choose the CSS that does each job.</p>","items":[{"text":"Keisha wants more space between the text of a room card and its border, inside the card.","options":["margin","border-spacing","padding"],"answer":2,"fb":"Padding is the space inside the border. Margin would push the whole card away from its neighbours."},{"text":"She wants the cards spaced further apart from each other.","options":["margin","padding","line-height"],"answer":0,"fb":"Margin sits outside the border and separates one box from the next."},{"text":"The brand red is rgb(122, 31, 61). Which hex code is the same colour?","options":["#122313","#7a1f3d","#3d1f7a"],"answer":1,"fb":"122 is 7a, 31 is 1f and 61 is 3d in hexadecimal, in the order red, green, blue."},{"text":"Body text feels cramped. Which property adds space between lines?","options":["font-weight","letter-spacing","line-height"],"answer":2,"fb":"line-height controls the vertical space between lines; 1.5 to 1.7 suits body text."},{"text":"Which declaration gives the booking button rounded corners?","options":["corner: 8px;","border-style: round;","border-radius: 8px;"],"answer":2,"fb":"border-radius rounds the corners of the box. The other two are not valid CSS."},{"text":"Which rule makes every link inside the nav white?","options":["#nav.a { colour: #ffffff; }","nav a { color: #ffffff; }","a nav { color: #ffffff; }"],"answer":1,"fb":"nav a is a descendant selector: links inside nav. Note that CSS uses the American spelling color."}],"win":"Excellent. You used the box model, colour codes and typography properties to solve real client requests.","tutor":[{"h":"Inside or outside?","p":"<p>Inside the border is one property; outside the border is another.</p>"},{"h":"Hex pairs","p":"<p>A hex code is three pairs: red, green, blue. Convert each rgb number separately.</p>"},{"h":"Spelling trap","p":"<p>Your essays use British spelling, but CSS only understands color.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"CSS quick-fire (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Space inside the border is...","options":["padding","margin"],"answer":0,"fb":"Padding is inside; margin is outside."},{"text":"Which sets the text colour?","options":["color","background-color"],"answer":0,"fb":"color sets text; background-color sets the fill."},{"text":"#ffffff is...","options":["black","white"],"answer":1,"fb":"All three channels at maximum (ff) make white."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder requests from Keisha. Some need a quick calculation.</p>","items":[{"text":"A card has width: 300px; padding: 16px; border: 1px solid; and the default box-sizing. How wide is it on screen?","options":["334 px","300 px","316 px","332 px"],"answer":0,"fb":"Content 300 + padding 16 + 16 + border 1 + 1 = 334 px. With box-sizing: border-box it would stay at 300 px."},{"text":"Which declaration keeps that card at exactly 300 px wide including padding and border?","options":["display: block;","padding-box: on;","margin: 0;","box-sizing: border-box;"],"answer":3,"fb":"border-box makes width include padding and border."},{"text":"The festival yellow is rgb(255, 204, 0). Which hex code matches?","options":["#00ccff","#cc00ff","#ffcc00","#ff00cc"],"answer":2,"fb":"255 is ff, 204 is cc and 0 is 00, in the order red, green, blue."},{"text":"The root font size is 16 px. How big is a heading set to 1.5rem?","options":["24 px","15 px","32 px","16 px"],"answer":0,"fb":"1.5 × 16 = 24 px. If the visitor enlarges browser text, rem sizes grow too."},{"text":"Which rule centres a 900 px wide main block horizontally on a wide screen?","options":["main { padding: auto; }","main { width: 900px; margin: 0 auto; }","main { text-align: center; }","main { align: centre; }"],"answer":1,"fb":"Auto left and right margins share the leftover space equally, centring a block with a set width."},{"text":"Which font-family declaration is the most robust?","options":["font-family: Open Sans;","font-family: \"Open Sans\" \"Arial\";","font-family: \"Open Sans\", Arial, sans-serif;","font: Open Sans only;"],"answer":2,"fb":"It quotes the name with a space, gives a common fallback and ends with a generic family, separated by commas."}],"win":"Excellent. You calculated box sizes, converted colours and units, and wrote robust CSS, the precision that Paper 02 code questions reward.","title":"CSS quick-fire (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Layout with flexbox and a simple grid","html":"<p>CSS layout decides where boxes sit. Two modern tools do most of the work.</p>\n<p><b>Flexbox</b> arranges items in <b>one direction</b>, a row or a column. It is ideal for navigation bars, rows of buttons and centring things.</p>\n<pre><code>nav ul {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: space-between; /* spread along the row */\n  align-items: center;            /* centre vertically */\n  gap: 16px;\n  list-style: none;\n}</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><code>display: flex;</code> goes on the <b>parent</b> (the flex container); its children become flex items.</li>\n<li><code>flex-direction</code>: row (default) or column.</li>\n<li><code>justify-content</code>: alignment along the main axis (flex-start, center, space-between).</li>\n<li><code>align-items</code>: alignment across the axis.</li>\n<li><code>flex-wrap: wrap;</code> lets items drop to a new line on small screens.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>CSS Grid</b> arranges items in <b>two directions</b>, rows and columns, like a table for layout. It suits galleries and card layouts.</p>\n<pre><code>.rooms {\n  display: grid;\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); /* three equal columns */\n  gap: 24px;\n}</code></pre>\n<p>The <b>fr</b> unit means \"one fraction of the free space\", so <code>1fr 2fr</code> makes the second column twice as wide. Grid also connects to the design idea of a <b>grid system</b>: a consistent set of columns (often 12) that keeps every page aligned.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Flexbox</th><th>Grid</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Dimensions</td><td>One (row or column)</td><td>Two (rows and columns)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Nav bars, button groups, centring</td><td>Galleries, card grids, whole-page layout</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Guavaberry Grove use</td><td>The menu across the header</td><td>The three room cards and the photo gallery</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>Avoid old methods such as tables or floats for layout: they break on phones and confuse screen readers.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> If asked to justify a layout choice, link the tool to the content: \"I used CSS Grid for the gallery because the photos need to line up in both rows and columns, and flexbox for the navigation because the links sit in a single row.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>CSS layout decides where boxes go. Flexbox lines items up in one direction, a row or a column, so it suits menus and rows of buttons. CSS Grid places items in rows and columns at the same time, so it suits photo galleries and card layouts. Use flexbox for the menu across a Tortola guest house header, and grid for its room gallery.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The choice depends on whether the layout is <b>content-out</b> or <b>layout-in</b>. Flexbox lets items size themselves along one line and wrap when space runs out, which suits menus with links of different lengths. Grid defines a two-dimensional structure first and places items into it, which suits galleries where rows and columns must align. Grid can also be responsive without media queries: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr)) creates as many columns of at least 200 px as will fit. The two work together: a grid of room cards, each card using flexbox inside. Avoid tables and floats for layout, because they break on phones.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Flexbox or grid?","q":"Maya needs the Guavaberry Grove photo gallery to show twelve photos in neat rows of four columns on a laptop, with equal gaps between rows and columns. Which CSS is the MOST suitable starting point?","options":[{"t":".gallery img { margin: 16px; padding: 16px; }","fb":"Spacing alone does not create columns. The images would simply flow one after another."},{"t":"&lt;table&gt; with four &lt;td&gt; cells in each row","fb":"Tables are for data, not layout. They are hard to make responsive and confuse screen readers."},{"t":".gallery { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr); gap: 16px; }","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. The gallery is two-dimensional (rows and columns), so grid is the right tool, and repeat(4, 1fr) creates four equal columns."},{"t":".gallery { display: flex; justify-content: center; }","fb":"Flexbox works in one direction. Without wrapping and set widths, the twelve photos would squeeze into one long row."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Count the directions","p":"<p>Does the layout need rows only, or rows and columns?</p>"},{"h":"Tables trap","p":"<p>Tables are meant for one kind of content only. A gallery is not it.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Flexbox or grid? (Foundation)","q":"Photos must line up in rows AND columns. Which layout tool fits best?","options":[{"t":"A data table","fb":"Tables are for data, not layout."},{"t":"Inline CSS colour","fb":"Colour does not create layout."},{"t":"CSS Grid","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Grid handles two dimensions."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","q":"Maya wants the Guavaberry Grove gallery to show as many columns as fit on any screen, each at least 200 px wide, with equal gaps, and WITHOUT writing any media queries. Which CSS is BEST?","options":[{"t":".gallery { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr)); gap: 16px; }","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. auto-fit with minmax creates as many columns of at least 200 px as fit, stretching them to fill the row, on any screen."},{"t":".gallery { display: flex; justify-content: center; }","fb":"Without wrapping and widths, the items squeeze into one row; flexbox also does not align rows and columns together."},{"t":".gallery { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 200px); gap: 16px; }","fb":"Four fixed 200 px columns need 848 px including gaps, so they overflow a phone screen."},{"t":".gallery img { float: left; width: 25%; }","fb":"Floats are an old layout method; they do not adapt the column count and are hard to align."}],"title":"Flexbox or grid? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Embedding images, video, audio and widgets","html":"<p>You must be able to embed <b>multimedia components</b> and <b>external widgets</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Video</b> and <b>audio</b> use HTML5 elements. The <b>controls</b> attribute shows play, pause and volume. Several <code>&lt;source&gt;</code> lines let the browser pick a format it supports.</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;video controls width=\"640\" poster=\"images/tour-poster.jpg\"&gt;\n  &lt;source src=\"media/tour.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\"&gt;\n  &lt;track src=\"media/tour-en.vtt\" kind=\"captions\" srclang=\"en\" label=\"English\"&gt;\n  Your browser does not support video.\n&lt;/video&gt;\n\n&lt;audio controls&gt;\n  &lt;source src=\"media/welcome.mp3\" type=\"audio/mpeg\"&gt;\n&lt;/audio&gt;</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li>Use <b>MP4 (H.264)</b> for video and <b>MP3</b> for audio for the widest support. WebM and OGG are open alternatives.</li>\n<li><b>poster</b> shows an image before the video plays. The <b>track</b> element adds WebVTT captions, which deaf and hard-of-hearing visitors need.</li>\n<li>Avoid <b>autoplay</b> with sound. If you must autoplay a background loop, add <b>muted</b>; browsers block unmuted autoplay anyway.</li>\n<li>Large video files slow the page and use visitors' data. A 3-minute tour is better hosted on YouTube and embedded.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>External widgets</b> use an <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code>, a window that shows another site inside yours. YouTube and Google Maps both give you the code: on YouTube choose <b>Share &gt; Embed</b>; on Google Maps choose <b>Share &gt; Embed a map</b>, then copy the iframe.</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/VIDEO_ID\"\n  title=\"Guavaberry Grove video tour\"\n  width=\"560\" height=\"315\" allowfullscreen&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;\n\n&lt;iframe src=\"https://www.google.com/maps/embed?pb=...\"\n  title=\"Map to Guavaberry Grove in Sea Cows Bay\"\n  width=\"600\" height=\"400\" loading=\"lazy\"&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>Give every iframe a <b>title</b> so screen readers announce what it is, and add <code>loading=\"lazy\"</code> so the map loads only when the visitor scrolls to it. Other widgets you may meet: Instagram feeds, booking engines, weather boxes and WhatsApp chat buttons.</p>\n<p><b>Images</b> should be the right <b>format</b> (JPEG for photos, PNG for logos with transparency, SVG for icons, WebP for small modern photos) and the right <b>size</b>: export a hero photo at about 1920 px wide, not the 6000 px straight from the camera.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In Paper 02 you will be offline, so you cannot test a live YouTube or Maps embed. Write the iframe correctly, add a title attribute, and state in a comment where the embed code comes from, for example <code>&lt;!-- embed code from Google Maps Share menu --&gt;</code>.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Use the video and audio tags to add media, with the controls attribute so visitors can play and pause. Add a track file for captions. Avoid autoplay with sound. Use an iframe to embed a YouTube video or a Google Map, and give it a title. Keep images the right size: about 1920 px wide for a hero photo, not 6000 px.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Embedding is a trade-off between <b>control and cost</b>. Hosting video yourself gives full control but costs bandwidth and server space, and visitors on prepaid data pay for every megabyte; embedding from YouTube shifts that load and adds adaptive streaming, but brings the platform's branding and tracking. Multiple source elements let the browser choose the first format it supports. The track element needs a WebVTT (.vtt) file; an SRT file must be converted. iframes are pages inside pages, so a title is essential for screen readers, and loading=\"lazy\" stops a map from slowing the first view. Offline exams test whether you can write this code accurately.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the multimedia mistakes","intro":"<p>Jerome (mobile developer at Island Pulse Media) drafted the media section for the Guavaberry Grove \"Experience\" page. Media files are in a <b>media</b> folder.</p>","q":"Select every line that is wrong or poor practice.","lines":["&lt;video controls&gt;&lt;source src=\"media/tour.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\"&gt;&lt;/video&gt;","&lt;video autoplay src=\"media/tour.mov\"&gt;&lt;/video&gt;","&lt;audio controls&gt;&lt;source src=\"media/steelpan.mp3\" type=\"audio/mpeg\"&gt;&lt;/audio&gt;","&lt;iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/abc123\"&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;","&lt;iframe src=\"https://www.google.com/maps/embed?pb=...\" title=\"Map to the guest house\" loading=\"lazy\"&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;","&lt;img src=\"media/sunset-6000px.jpg\" alt=\"Sunset over Cane Garden Bay\"&gt;","&lt;track src=\"media/tour-en.vtt\" kind=\"captions\" srclang=\"en\" label=\"English\"&gt;","&lt;audio src=\"media/welcome.mp3\"&gt;&lt;/audio&gt;"],"correct":[1,3,5,7],"win":"Well spotted. You caught autoplay without controls in a poorly supported MOV file, an iframe with no title, an oversized 6000 px image, and audio with no controls so it cannot be played.","miss":"Not all found. Check for controls on video and audio, title attributes on iframes, supported formats (MP4, MP3) and sensible image sizes.","tutor":[{"h":"Can the visitor control it?","p":"<p>A media element without controls gives the visitor no way to play or pause it.</p>"},{"h":"Think about phones and data","p":"<p>Would a visitor on a mobile data plan in Road Town want to download a camera-sized photo?</p>"},{"h":"Screen readers","p":"<p>Every iframe needs something that tells a screen-reader user what it is.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the multimedia mistakes (Foundation)","q":"Select the two lines with problems.","lines":["&lt;video controls&gt;&lt;source src=\"media/tour.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\"&gt;&lt;/video&gt;","&lt;audio src=\"media/welcome.mp3\"&gt;&lt;/audio&gt;","&lt;iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/abc123\"&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;"],"correct":[1,2],"win":"Yes: the audio has no controls and the iframe has no title.","miss":"Check for controls on audio and a title on the iframe."},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Jerome's second draft of the media section. Some lines are good practice that look unusual.</p>","q":"Select every line that is wrong or poor practice.","lines":["A silent background loop: &lt;video autoplay muted loop playsinline poster=\"images/poster.jpg\"&gt;&lt;source src=\"media/loop.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\"&gt;&lt;/video&gt;","&lt;video controls&gt;&lt;source src=\"media/tour.webm\" type=\"video/webm\"&gt;&lt;source src=\"media/tour.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\"&gt;&lt;/video&gt;","A narrated three-minute tour with no captions: &lt;video controls src=\"media/tour.mp4\"&gt;&lt;/video&gt;","&lt;iframe src=\"https://www.youtube.com/embed/abc123\" title=\"Video tour of Guavaberry Grove\" allowfullscreen&gt;&lt;/iframe&gt;","&lt;audio controls autoplay&gt;&lt;source src=\"media/steelpan.mp3\" type=\"audio/mpeg\"&gt;&lt;/audio&gt;","&lt;track src=\"media/tour-en.srt\" kind=\"captions\" srclang=\"en\" label=\"English\"&gt;","&lt;img src=\"images/hero-1920.webp\" alt=\"Garden at sunrise\" width=\"1920\" height=\"1080\"&gt;","&lt;video controls src=\"media/tour-4k-raw.mov\"&gt;&lt;/video&gt;","&lt;a href=\"media/menu.pdf\"&gt;Download the menu (PDF, 240 KB)&lt;/a&gt;"],"correct":[2,4,5,7],"win":"Well judged. Narrated video needs captions, audio should not autoplay with sound, the track element needs WebVTT (.vtt), and a raw 4K MOV is too large and poorly supported. A muted background loop and multiple sources are fine.","miss":"Look again. A muted, looping background video is acceptable, and offering WebM and MP4 sources is good practice. Problems are missing captions, autoplaying sound, the wrong caption format and an unsuitable video file.","title":"Spot the multimedia mistakes (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Print vs screen resolution, units and responsive media","html":"<p>You must be able to compare <b>print</b> and <b>screen</b> resolution.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Print</th><th>Screen</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Unit</td><td><b>dpi</b> (dots per inch) of ink</td><td><b>px</b> (pixels); ppi describes a screen's density</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Typical standard</td><td>300 dpi for brochures and flyers</td><td>Image dimensions in px (for example 1920 by 1080); the old \"72 dpi for web\" setting does not change how big an image appears</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Colour model</td><td>CMYK (cyan, magenta, yellow, black ink)</td><td>RGB (red, green, blue light), sRGB profile</td></tr>\n<tr><td>File size priority</td><td>Quality first; large files are fine</td><td>Speed first; compress to keep pages fast</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>So a photo of Guavaberry Grove's garden printed on a 6 by 4 inch postcard needs about 1800 by 1200 pixels (6 by 300, 4 by 300). The same photo on the website can be exported at 1200 px wide and compressed to about 200 KB. A 300 dpi CMYK print file is the wrong file to upload to a website.</p>\n<p><b>CSS units</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>px</b>: an absolute unit; good for borders and small fixed details.</li>\n<li><b>rem</b>: relative to the root (html) font size, usually 16 px, so 1.5rem is 24 px. If a visitor increases the browser's text size, rem text grows too, which helps accessibility. <b>em</b> is relative to the parent's font size.</li>\n<li><b>%</b> and <b>vw/vh</b>: a percentage of the parent or of the viewport width or height; useful for fluid layouts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Units and responsive media.</b> Relative units such as rem and % are the first step towards pages that fit every screen, because they scale instead of staying fixed. The responsive design screen, after the next activity, shows the full mobile-first method: the viewport tag, Flexbox, media queries and images with max-width:100%.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A classic Paper 01 trap: \"A web image should be saved at 300 dpi for best quality.\" False. For screens, pixel dimensions and file size matter; dpi is a print setting.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Print and screens work differently. Print uses about 300 dots per inch and CMYK ink colours. Screens use pixels and RGB light colours, and web images must be small files. A 6 by 4 inch postcard needs about 1800 by 1200 pixels, but the same photo can go on a website at 1200 px wide. CSS units such as rem and % help pages fit every screen.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The dpi myth is worth unpicking. A web image's on-screen size depends on its pixel dimensions and the CSS, so a \"72 dpi\" or \"300 dpi\" setting changes nothing in a browser; only for print does dpi convert pixels into physical size. The print calculation is simply inches × dpi, so a 5 by 7 inch menu photo needs 1500 by 2100 pixels. Colour also needs care: a CMYK file uploaded to the web can look dull or wrong, so export web images in sRGB. Unit choice is an accessibility decision too: rem respects the visitor's browser text setting, whereas px text may not grow.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Print or screen?","intro":"<p>Guavaberry Grove needs both a printed brochure for the airport tourism desk and a website. Sort each setting by where it belongs.</p>","main":"Does this belong to print production or screen (web) production?","bins":["Print","Screen"],"items":[{"t":"Colours written as hex codes such as #7a1f3d","bin":1,"fb":"Hex codes describe RGB light colours used on screens and in CSS."},{"t":"CMYK colour model","bin":0,"fb":"Printers mix cyan, magenta, yellow and black inks."},{"t":"Media queries for phones and tablets","bin":1,"fb":"Media queries adapt a web layout to different screen sizes."},{"t":"Font sizes in rem so text scales when the user zooms","bin":1,"fb":"rem is a CSS unit that respects the user's browser text settings."},{"t":"Saved as a high-quality PDF for the print shop","bin":0,"fb":"Print shops usually want a print-ready PDF."},{"t":"Hero photo exported at 1920 px wide and compressed to under 300 KB","bin":1,"fb":"Web images are measured in pixels and kept small so pages load quickly on mobile data."},{"t":"Images set to 300 dpi","bin":0,"fb":"300 dpi is the usual standard for sharp printed material."},{"t":"A 3 mm bleed added around the page edge","bin":0,"fb":"Bleed stops white edges after a printed page is trimmed."}],"win":"Well sorted. Print uses dpi, CMYK, bleed and print-ready PDFs; screens use pixels, RGB or hex colour, rem units, compression and media queries.","tutor":[{"h":"Ink or light?","p":"<p>Ink on paper and light from a screen use different colour models and different measures of detail.</p>"},{"h":"Speed","p":"<p>If the setting is about loading quickly or adapting to devices, think about the medium that loads.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Print or screen? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"300 dpi images","bin":0,"fb":"A print standard."},{"t":"CMYK colours","bin":0,"fb":"Ink colours for print."},{"t":"Hex colour codes","bin":1,"fb":"Screen colours in CSS."},{"t":"Media queries","bin":1,"fb":"Responsive web design."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder settings, including some calculations. Decide where each belongs.</p>","items":[{"t":"A 5 by 7 inch menu photo prepared at 1500 by 2100 pixels","bin":0,"fb":"5 × 300 = 1500 and 7 × 300 = 2100: a 300 dpi print calculation."},{"t":"Exporting the photo in the sRGB colour profile","bin":1,"fb":"sRGB is the standard colour space for screens and browsers."},{"t":"Converting the brochure to CMYK for the print shop","bin":0,"fb":"Printers mix CMYK inks."},{"t":"Resizing to 1200 px wide and compressing to about 200 KB","bin":1,"fb":"Pixel width and small file size matter on the web."},{"t":"A banner set to width: 100vw","bin":1,"fb":"vw is a CSS unit: a percentage of the viewport width."},{"t":"A 3 mm bleed around a flyer","bin":0,"fb":"Bleed prevents white edges after trimming paper."},{"t":"Pantone spot colours for a logo printed on staff T-shirts","bin":0,"fb":"Spot colours are a print and merchandise standard."},{"t":"A brand colour written as #7a1f3d","bin":1,"fb":"Hex codes describe RGB light for screens and CSS."},{"t":"Body text sized at 1rem so it grows with browser settings","bin":1,"fb":"rem is a screen (CSS) unit that respects user settings."}],"win":"Well sorted. Print is measured in physical size, dpi and ink (CMYK, bleed, spot colours); screen is measured in pixels, light (sRGB, hex) and flexible CSS units.","title":"Print or screen? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Mobile-first responsive design","html":"<p><b>Responsive design makes one page fit every screen.</b> The reason comes first: <b>in the British Virgin Islands the phone is the main browsing device.</b> Cruise passengers stepping off in Road Town, students checking Google Classroom on the bus and shoppers looking up a Main Street store's opening hours are mostly on smartphones, often on mobile data. (For the exact share of mobile users in the Caribbean, check the latest ITU or GSMA figure.) So design <b>mobile-first</b>: write the phone layout first, then add rules for wider screens.</p>\n<p><b>Example: a Carnival schedule.</b> Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is building an events page with three boxes: Event A, Event B and Event C. On a phone they <b>stack</b> in one column. From <b>768 px</b> wide (a tablet), the same boxes sit <b>three across</b>. The HTML never changes; CSS changes the layout.</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 320 120\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:360px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"On a phone three event boxes stack vertically; on a tablet they sit side by side in one row\">\n<rect x=\"10\" y=\"5\" width=\"70\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"15\" width=\"50\" height=\"25\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"32\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">A</text>\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"47\" width=\"50\" height=\"25\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"64\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">B</text>\n<rect x=\"20\" y=\"79\" width=\"50\" height=\"25\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">C</text>\n<rect x=\"110\" y=\"20\" width=\"200\" height=\"80\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n<rect x=\"122\" y=\"45\" width=\"52\" height=\"30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/><text x=\"143\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">A</text>\n<rect x=\"184\" y=\"45\" width=\"52\" height=\"30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/><text x=\"205\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">B</text>\n<rect x=\"246\" y=\"45\" width=\"52\" height=\"30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/><text x=\"267\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">C</text></svg>\n<p><b>Four tools</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>The viewport meta tag</b> in the head: <code>&lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"&gt;</code>. Without it, a phone pretends to be a wide desktop screen and shrinks the whole page to unreadable size.</li>\n<li><b>Flexible layouts with Flexbox</b>: <code>display: flex;</code> on the parent box.</li>\n<li><b>Media queries at breakpoints</b>: extra CSS that applies only when the screen reaches a width such as 768 px.</li>\n<li><b>Images with max-width:100%</b>, so a photo shrinks to fit its box instead of spilling off the screen.</li>\n</ol>\n<pre><code>&lt;div class=\"events\"&gt;\n  &lt;section&gt;J'ouvert&lt;/section&gt;\n  &lt;section&gt;Grand Parade&lt;/section&gt;\n  &lt;section&gt;Emancipation Festival concert&lt;/section&gt;\n&lt;/div&gt;\n\n/* style.css: phone first */\n.events { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 16px; }\nimg { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }\n\n@media (min-width:768px) {\n    .events { flex-direction: row; }   /* tablet and up: three across */\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Read the media query aloud as \"if the screen is at least 768 px wide, apply these rules\". Because <b>min-width</b> adds rules as screens grow, it fits mobile-first. <b>Testing:</b> press <b>F12</b> in Chrome or Edge to open developer tools, then the device toolbar (Ctrl+Shift+M) to preview phone, tablet and desktop widths.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In Paper 02, a missing closing brace on an @media block silently breaks every rule after it. Count your braces: the media query needs one pair for itself and one pair for each rule inside it.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Responsive design makes one page fit every screen. Design for phones first, because most people in the BVI browse on phones. Use four tools: the viewport meta tag, flexible layouts with flexbox, media queries that change the layout at a breakpoint such as 768 px, and images with max-width: 100%. A Carnival schedule can stack on phones and sit three across on tablets.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Mobile-first is a strategy as well as a coding order. Starting with the smallest screen forces you to decide what really matters, which usually improves the desktop design too. With min-width media queries, the base CSS serves phones, and each breakpoint only <b>adds</b> rules, so phones download and process less. Breakpoints should be set where the content starts to look cramped, not by copying a list of device sizes. Responsive design also covers touch targets and performance, since a layout that fits but loads a 5 MB image still fails visitors on mobile data. A missing brace in an @media block breaks every rule after it.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the responsive tools","intro":"<p>Keisha is checking her Carnival schedule page against the responsive toolkit. Match each tool or term to what it does.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Viewport meta tag","r":"Stops a phone shrinking the page to a tiny desktop-sized view"},{"l":"Media query","r":"Applies CSS only when a condition, such as min-width:768px, is true"},{"l":"Breakpoint","r":"The screen width where the layout changes, such as 768 px"},{"l":"display: flex","r":"Turns a parent box into a flexible row or column of child boxes"},{"l":"flex-direction: column","r":"Stacks the three events one above another on a phone"},{"l":"max-width: 100%","r":"Lets an image shrink to fit its container without overflowing"},{"l":"Mobile-first","r":"Writing the phone layout first, then adding rules for wider screens"},{"l":"F12 device toolbar","r":"Previews the page at phone, tablet and desktop widths"}],"win":"Well matched. The viewport tag, Flexbox, media queries at breakpoints and max-width:100% images together make one mobile-first page fit every screen.","tutor":[{"h":"Condition or width?","p":"<p>One term is the rule that checks the screen; another is just the number where things change.</p>"},{"h":"Row or stack?","p":"<p>Which value of flex-direction puts boxes one above another?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the responsive tools (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Viewport meta tag","r":"Makes the phone use its real screen width"},{"l":"Media query","r":"Extra CSS for screens of a certain width"},{"l":"max-width: 100%","r":"Keeps images inside their box"},{"l":"Mobile-first","r":"Design the phone layout first"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Keisha's test report lists problems. Match each problem to the fix.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"On a phone the whole page appears zoomed out and the text is tiny","r":"Add the viewport meta tag to the head"},{"l":"The hero photo spills off the right edge of a phone screen","r":"img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }"},{"l":"The three event boxes are squashed side by side on a phone","r":"Stack them by default and set flex-direction: row only inside @media (min-width: 768px)"},{"l":"Every rule written after the media query has stopped working","r":"Add the missing closing brace of the @media block"},{"l":"The navigation links run off the screen on narrow phones","r":"Add flex-wrap: wrap to the nav list"},{"l":"A visitor enlarges browser text but the page text stays the same size","r":"Set font sizes in rem instead of px"},{"l":"The team has no tablet to test on","r":"Use the F12 device toolbar to preview tablet widths"}],"win":"Well matched. You diagnosed each responsive failure and chose the precise fix, which is the \"identify the error and correct it\" skill Paper 02 tests.","title":"Match the responsive tools (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Multi-page site architecture","html":"<p>A professional site is <b>planned before it is coded</b>. Module 2's prototyping unit introduced the tools; here you turn them into files and folders.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Site map</b> (hierarchy): Home at the top, then the main pages, then any sub-pages. Guavaberry Grove has Home, Rooms, Gallery, Experience (things to do in Tortola) and Contact.</li>\n<li><b>Wireframe</b>: a grey-box sketch of each page layout (header, nav, hero, content blocks, footer) with no colour or images. It fixes the structure.</li>\n<li><b>Mock-up</b>: a high-fidelity visual in Figma or Canva with real colours, fonts and photos. The client (here, the guest house owner) approves it before coding starts.</li>\n<li><b>Code</b> the shared template first, then copy it for each page.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Folder structure</b> keeps links working:</p>\n<pre><code>guavaberry-grove/\n  index.html\n  rooms.html\n  gallery.html\n  experience.html\n  contact.html\n  css/style.css\n  images/   (hero.jpg, garden-room.jpg ...)\n  media/    (tour.mp4, welcome.mp3)</code></pre>\n<p><b>Consistent navigation.</b> Every page shares the same header, nav and footer, in the same place, linking to every main page. Mark the current page so users know where they are:</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;nav&gt;\n  &lt;ul&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"index.html\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"rooms.html\" aria-current=\"page\"&gt;Rooms&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"contact.html\"&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;/ul&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;</code></pre>\n<p>Two more navigation ideas matter: <b>breadcrumbs</b> (a trail such as Home &gt; Rooms &gt; Garden Room that shows the user's position) and <b>user pathways</b>: the routes visitors take to complete a goal. For a guest house, the key pathway is Home to Rooms to Contact or Book. A good rule is that any important page should be reachable in about <b>three clicks</b>.</p>\n<p>A multi-page site needs at least <b>home, contact us and content pages</b>, appropriately linked, with navigation buttons. A good class activity is a collaborative build where each student contributes one page (home, about, gallery, contact) to one cohesive site. A shared external style sheet is what makes that possible.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For your SBA, keep your site map, wireframes and mock-ups with dates. They are evidence for the <b>Progress and Process</b> marks, not just the final Product.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Plan a website before you code it. Draw a site map, sketch wireframes and get a mock-up approved. Then set up folders for css, images and media, and code one shared template with the same header, menu and footer on every page. A guest house site might have Home, Rooms, Gallery, Experience and Contact pages, all linked together.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Folder structure and a shared template are what make a multi-page site <b>maintainable</b>. Relative links depend on stable locations, so moving one file later can break dozens of links; deciding the structure first prevents this. Copying a finished template guarantees consistent navigation, but it also copies mistakes, so test the template thoroughly before duplicating it. Marking the current page with aria-current helps both sighted users and screen readers know where they are, and breadcrumbs extend that idea on deeper sites. For a group build, agree file names, class names and the shared style sheet before anyone codes, or the pages will not fit together.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"From brief to live multi-page site","intro":"<p>Put Maya's workflow for the Guavaberry Grove site in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Meet the owner and agree the purpose, audience and pages in a brief"},{"t":"Draw a site map showing Home, Rooms, Gallery, Experience and Contact"},{"t":"Sketch grey-box wireframes for each page layout"},{"t":"Create a colour mock-up in Figma and get the owner's approval"},{"t":"Set up folders (css, images, media) and code index.html with the shared header, nav and footer"},{"t":"Copy the template to the other pages, add content and link the external style sheet"},{"t":"Test every link, image and media file on a phone and a laptop, and check accessibility"},{"t":"Upload the site to a web host and share the link"}],"win":"Correct sequence. Plan (brief, site map, wireframe, mock-up), then build (template, pages), then test, then publish.","tutor":[{"h":"Plan, build, test, publish","p":"<p>Group the steps into those four stages first.</p>"},{"h":"Low before high","p":"<p>Low-fidelity sketches come before high-fidelity mock-ups.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"From brief to live site (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put the four stages in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Agree the brief with the client"},{"t":"Draw the site map and wireframes"},{"t":"Code and test the pages"},{"t":"Publish the site"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Ten steps now, from brief to an updated live site. Put Maya's workflow in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Agree the purpose, audience and pages with the owner in a brief"},{"t":"Draw the site map with Home, Rooms, Gallery, Experience and Contact"},{"t":"Sketch grey-box wireframes for each page layout"},{"t":"Create a colour mock-up and get the owner's approval"},{"t":"Set up the css, images and media folders and code the shared template in index.html"},{"t":"Copy the template to the other pages, add their content and link the external style sheet"},{"t":"Run the HTML and CSS through a validator and fix the errors it reports"},{"t":"Test links, images, media, accessibility and layout on a phone and a laptop"},{"t":"Upload the site to a web host and share the link"},{"t":"Review the site each month and update prices and photos"}],"win":"Correct. Plan, build the template and pages, validate, test, publish, then maintain: a site is never \"finished\" on launch day.","title":"From brief to live multi-page site (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Page design, UI and UX, accessibility and usability","html":"<p>A site that works but is hard to read or use still fails. You are assessed on <b>page design and layout</b> and <b>user interface design</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Page design principles</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Contrast</b>: text must stand out from its background. Dark grey on white or white on a deep guavaberry red reads well; light yellow on white does not. Contrast also creates hierarchy: the booking button should be the most noticeable item.</li>\n<li><b>Grid systems</b>: align content to consistent columns so pages look organised and alike.</li>\n<li><b>Legibility</b>: body text at least 16 px (1rem), line length of about 50 to 75 characters, line-height around 1.5, left-aligned, no long blocks of capitals or italics.</li>\n<li><b>Link styling</b>: links must look clickable (underlined or clearly coloured) and show a change on <code>:hover</code> and <code>:focus</code> for keyboard users. Do not underline text that is not a link.</li>\n<li><b>Graphics</b>: compressed, relevant images with alt text; consistent icon style.</li>\n<li><b>White space</b> (spacing) and <b>consistency</b>: the same colours, fonts and button styles on every page.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>UI and UX.</b> <b>UI</b> (user interface) is what users see and touch: buttons, menus, forms. <b>UX</b> (user experience) is how the whole visit feels: is it quick, clear and satisfying? A beautiful UI with a confusing booking process gives poor UX.</p>\n<p><b>Responsiveness</b>: the layout adapts to phones, tablets and laptops. Touch targets (buttons, links) should be at least about 44 by 44 px.</p>\n<p><b>Accessibility (WCAG).</b> The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines, published by the W3C, group requirements under four principles, <b>POUR</b>:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Principle</th><th>Example on Guavaberry Grove</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Perceivable</b></td><td>Alt text on photos, captions on the video tour, a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1 for body text (WCAG level AA)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Operable</b></td><td>Every link and form works with a keyboard; visible focus outlines</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Understandable</b></td><td>Plain language, consistent menus, clear form labels and error messages</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Robust</b></td><td>Valid, semantic HTML that works in different browsers and screen readers</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Usability</b> is how easily users complete tasks. Steve Krug's book <i>Don't Make Me Think</i> (on the syllabus resource list) argues that every page should be obvious at a glance. Test by watching a real person try a task, such as \"find the price of the garden room\", without helping them.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When you \"evaluate\" a website in Paper 02, use these headings as criteria: contrast, legibility, navigation, responsiveness, accessibility. Give one strength, one weakness and one specific improvement for each.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A website must be easy to read and use, not just working. Use strong contrast, a tidy grid, readable text of at least 16 px, links that look clickable and plenty of white space. UI is what users see and tap; UX is how the whole visit feels. Follow the WCAG POUR rules, and test by watching a real person try to find a room price.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>UI and UX can conflict: a striking design with tiny grey text may win a design award and still lose bookings. Usability testing exposes this, because watching one real person struggle reveals problems the team cannot see. Many accessibility failures are hidden by habit: removing focus outlines for looks makes the site unusable by keyboard; placeholder text instead of labels disappears when users type; colour-only error messages fail colour-blind users. Each fix usually improves the experience for everyone, which is the strongest argument for accessibility. When you evaluate a site, pair each criterion with evidence, a consequence for users and a specific improvement.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Maya builds the Guavaberry Grove site","intro":"<p>Guavaberry Grove is a six-room guest house in Sea Cows Bay, Tortola, in the British Virgin Islands. The owner, Mrs. Richardson, wants a website that brings in direct bookings instead of relying only on travel sites. Help Maya make the right decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Mrs. Richardson is eager and says: \"Just start coding today. I want it to look like a luxury resort site I saw.\" What should Maya do first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Start coding straight away and copy the luxury site's design and photos.","to":"bad1","fb":"Copying another site's design and photos risks copyright infringement, and skipping planning leads to rework."},{"t":"Agree a short brief (audience, purpose, pages), then draw a site map and wireframes for approval.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Planning first prevents costly rewrites and keeps the site focused on direct bookings."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The wireframes are approved. Maya now sets up the code. How should she handle the styling for five pages?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Inline style attributes on each element of each page.","to":"bad2","fb":"Inline CSS must be repeated everywhere, so one change means editing hundreds of lines."},{"t":"One external style sheet (css/style.css) linked from every page, with semantic header, nav, main and footer.","to":"n3","fb":"Correct. One external file keeps all pages consistent and easy to update."},{"t":"A data table to position the header, menu and content.","to":"bad2","fb":"Tables are for data. Table layouts are not responsive and confuse screen readers."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Testing shows that on a phone, the three room cards are squashed side by side and the 5 MB hero photo takes a long time to load on mobile data. What does Maya fix?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Add a mobile-first media query (one column on phones, three from 768 px), set img max-width: 100%, and export the hero at 1920 px compressed to about 250 KB.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. That fixes both responsiveness and page speed for mobile visitors."},{"t":"Tell visitors in the footer to use a laptop for the best experience.","to":"bad3","fb":"Most Caribbean visitors and tourists browse on phones. Asking them to switch devices loses bookings."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Keisha wants a pale yellow booking button with white text, a YouTube tour that opens in a new tab, and a Google Map. What does Maya do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Use the deep guavaberry red with white text for strong contrast, embed the YouTube tour and map with titled iframes, and give any new-tab external link rel=\"noopener\".","to":"good","fb":"Strong choices for accessibility, usability and security."},{"t":"Use the pale yellow button because it matches the brand, and leave out iframe titles to keep the code short.","to":"bad4","fb":"White on pale yellow fails WCAG contrast, and untitled iframes are unclear to screen-reader users."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Launch success.</b> The site is consistent, responsive, fast and accessible, and direct booking enquiries start arriving. A Grade One answer would explain each decision: planning before coding, one external style sheet, mobile-first media queries with compressed images, and WCAG contrast with titled embeds.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Problem.</b> The copied design and photos breach copyright, and with no site map the pages keep changing. Try again and plan with a brief, site map and wireframes.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Maintenance trouble.</b> When the owner asks for a new brand colour, Maya has to edit every page by hand, and the layout breaks on phones. Try again with one external style sheet and semantic layout.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Lost bookings.</b> Phone visitors leave before the page loads. Try again: design mobile-first and optimise images for the web.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Accessibility failure.</b> Many visitors cannot read the button text, and screen-reader users hear only \"frame\". Try again and check contrast and iframe titles.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Plan, then build","p":"<p>What evidence does a professional collect before writing code?</p>"},{"h":"Change once, update everywhere","p":"<p>Which way of adding CSS lets one edit change five pages?</p>"},{"h":"Who is visiting?","p":"<p>Think about phone screens, mobile data and visitors with disabilities.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Maya builds the site (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: Maya is coding the Guavaberry Grove guest house site.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The site has five pages. How should Maya add the CSS?</p>","choices":[{"t":"One external style sheet linked from every page.","to":"n2","fb":"Yes. One file keeps every page consistent."},{"t":"Inline styles on every element.","to":"bad","fb":"That is very hard to maintain."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The room cards squash together on phones. What should she add?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A note asking visitors to use a laptop.","to":"bad","fb":"Most visitors use phones."},{"t":"A media query that shows one column on small screens.","to":"good","fb":"Correct. That makes the layout responsive."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> The site is consistent and responsive.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Think about maintenance and phone users. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A harder build. Mrs. Richardson wants direct bookings, a gallery, an English and Spanish version for visitors from the Dominican Republic and South America, and the site live in three weeks. Help Maya make the decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Mrs. Richardson sends a link to a luxury resort site and says: \"Copy this look exactly.\" What does Maya do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Copy the resort's HTML and photos and change the name.","to":"bad1","fb":"That copies protected code and images, a copyright problem."},{"t":"Use it as inspiration for the brief, then plan an original design with a site map and wireframes that include the Spanish pages.","to":"n2","fb":"Excellent. Inspiration is fine; copying code, design or photos is not, and the bilingual structure must be planned from the start."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>How should Maya organise the English and Spanish pages?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Separate folders (/en/ and /es/) sharing one external style sheet, each page with the correct lang attribute and a language link in the header.","to":"n3","fb":"Good. Shared CSS keeps both versions consistent, and the lang attribute helps screen readers and search engines."},{"t":"Put English and Spanish text side by side in every paragraph.","to":"ok1","fb":"It works, but pages become twice as long and screen readers mispronounce one language."}]},"ok1":{"text":"<p>Testers find the mixed-language pages long and confusing. What now?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Remove the Spanish text.","to":"bad2","fb":"This loses the audience the owner specifically wanted."},{"t":"Split them into /en/ and /es/ versions with lang attributes and a language switch.","to":"n3","fb":"Good recovery."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The gallery must show room photos neatly on phones and laptops. Which approach?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A CSS grid using repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr)), images with max-width: 100%, and photos exported at web sizes.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. The grid adapts to any width, and optimised images keep it fast."},{"t":"A table with four cells per row.","to":"bad3","fb":"Tables are for data; this layout will break on phones."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Testing with a keyboard-only user shows she cannot see where she is on the page, because Keisha removed focus outlines \"to look cleaner\". What should Maya do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Restore visible focus styles, for example a clear outline on a:focus and button:focus, matched to the brand.","to":"good","fb":"Correct. Visible focus is essential for keyboard users and can still look on-brand."},{"t":"Leave it; most visitors use a mouse.","to":"bad4","fb":"Removing focus indicators makes the site unusable for keyboard users, failing WCAG."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Launched on time.</b> The site is original, bilingual, responsive and accessible, and direct bookings come in from Santo Domingo and Bogotá as well as the US. A Grade One answer justifies every decision against the brief, the users and WCAG.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Copyright problem.</b> The resort's agency spots its design and photos and sends a takedown notice. Try again with an original design.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Audience lost.</b> Spanish-speaking visitors leave for other guest houses. Try again and keep both languages, organised properly.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Broken on phones.</b> The table forces sideways scrolling. Try again with a responsive grid.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Accessibility failure.</b> Keyboard users cannot navigate the booking form. Try again and restore visible focus.</p>","end":"bad"}},"title":"Maya builds the Guavaberry Grove site (Challenge)"}},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Accessible or a barrier?","intro":"<p>Zara is reviewing Guavaberry Grove and two other Caribbean sites against WCAG basics. Sort each feature.</p>","main":"Does this feature support accessibility and usability, or create a barrier?","bins":["Supports accessibility","Creates a barrier"],"items":[{"t":"The video tour has captions","bin":0,"fb":"Captions help deaf visitors and anyone watching with the sound off."},{"t":"The booking form can be completed using only the keyboard","bin":0,"fb":"Keyboard access makes the form operable for people who cannot use a mouse."},{"t":"Links are shown only by a slightly different colour, with no underline or hover change","bin":1,"fb":"Users, especially those with colour blindness, may not see that the text is a link."},{"t":"Light grey text on a white background","bin":1,"fb":"Low contrast fails WCAG and is hard to read in bright Caribbean sunlight."},{"t":"Text is set in px and cannot grow when the user zooms","bin":1,"fb":"Users who need larger text are blocked. rem units and zoom-friendly layouts help."},{"t":"Every photo has meaningful alt text","bin":0,"fb":"Alt text makes images perceivable for screen-reader users."},{"t":"Music autoplays loudly when the page opens","bin":1,"fb":"Autoplay audio interferes with screen readers and startles users."},{"t":"Headings follow h1, h2, h3 in order","bin":0,"fb":"A logical heading structure lets screen-reader users navigate the page."}],"win":"Well judged. You applied the POUR principles: perceivable, operable, understandable and robust.","tutor":[{"h":"Imagine the user","p":"<p>Picture someone who is blind, deaf, colour-blind or cannot use a mouse. Can they still use the feature?</p>"},{"h":"POUR","p":"<p>Perceivable, operable, understandable, robust.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Accessible or a barrier? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Alt text on images","bin":0,"fb":"Helps screen-reader users."},{"t":"Captions on videos","bin":0,"fb":"Helps deaf users."},{"t":"Light grey text on white","bin":1,"fb":"Poor contrast."},{"t":"Loud autoplay music","bin":1,"fb":"Disrupts screen readers."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Subtler features. Decide whether each supports accessibility and usability or creates a barrier.</p>","items":[{"t":"outline: none on all links and buttons, with no replacement focus style","bin":1,"fb":"Keyboard users can no longer see where they are on the page."},{"t":"A \"Skip to main content\" link as the first item on the page","bin":0,"fb":"It lets keyboard and screen-reader users bypass the menu on every page."},{"t":"Form fields with placeholder text only and no label elements","bin":1,"fb":"Placeholders vanish when typing and are not reliable labels for screen readers."},{"t":"An error message shown only by turning the field border red","bin":1,"fb":"Colour alone fails colour-blind users and gives no explanation."},{"t":"aria-current=\"page\" on the current link in the menu","bin":0,"fb":"It tells screen-reader users which page they are on."},{"t":"Captions on the video tour plus a text transcript","bin":0,"fb":"Both make the video perceivable without sound."},{"t":"Room prices shown only inside a photograph of a price list","bin":1,"fb":"Text inside images cannot be read by screen readers, enlarged cleanly or indexed by search engines."},{"t":"lang=\"es\" on the Spanish version of the site","bin":0,"fb":"Screen readers switch to Spanish pronunciation, and search engines know the language."},{"t":"A photo carousel that auto-advances every two seconds with no pause button","bin":1,"fb":"Users cannot read or control moving content, which fails WCAG."},{"t":"Buttons at least 44 by 44 px with space between them","bin":0,"fb":"Large, separated touch targets are easier to tap accurately."}],"win":"Strong judgement. Many barriers are small design choices, and every fix you identified helps far more users than the group it was designed for.","title":"Accessible or a barrier? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Practical task: a main-street business page","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Practical task: a main-street business page.</b> Build a one-page site for a <b>jewellery store, souvenir shop or café in your own town</b>. Include <b>semantic HTML and external CSS</b>, <b>alt text</b> for images, a <b>Flexbox row of three feature boxes</b> and <b>one media query</b>. Test before you submit: use <b>F12</b> to check the page at phone, tablet and desktop widths. <b>Marked out of 25.</b></p>\n<p>Suppose you choose Coral Crown Jewellers on Bay Street in Nassau. Work through it in this order.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Set up the folder</b>: <i>main-street/</i> holding <i>index.html</i>, <i>style.css</i> and an <i>images</i> folder. Lowercase names, no spaces.</li>\n<li><b>Write the semantic HTML</b>: a header (shop name), a nav of in-page links (#about, #features, #contact), a main with one h1, an intro paragraph and a photo with real alt text, then a footer with the address, opening hours and phone number.</li>\n<li><b>Link the external CSS</b> in the head with <code>&lt;link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\"&gt;</code>, and add the viewport meta tag.</li>\n<li><b>Add three feature boxes</b> inside one parent, for example \"Duty-free prices\", \"Caribbean gold\" and \"Cruise-day hours\".</li>\n<li><b>Style mobile-first</b>: boxes stacked with <code>flex-direction: column</code>, images with <code>max-width: 100%</code>.</li>\n<li><b>Add one media query</b>: <code>@media (min-width:768px) { .features { flex-direction: row; } }</code> puts the boxes three across.</li>\n<li><b>Test with F12</b> at about 375 px (phone), 768 px (tablet) and 1280 px (desktop). Click every link, check every image, and look for colour or centre spelt the British way in your CSS.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Marks</th><th>What earns full marks</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Functionality</b></td><td>5</td><td>The page opens from index.html, the style sheet is linked, every image loads and every link works</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Code quality</b></td><td>5</td><td>Valid, tidily nested and indented HTML; all styling in the external file, not inline; sensible file names</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Design</b></td><td>5</td><td>Consistent colours and fonts, clear hierarchy and readable contrast that suit the business</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Responsiveness</b></td><td>5</td><td>Viewport tag present; boxes stack on a phone and sit three across from 768 px; images fit the screen</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Accessibility</b></td><td>5</td><td>header, nav, main and footer; meaningful alt text; one h1 and headings in order; strong and em, not b and i</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Total</b></td><td><b>25</b></td><td></td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Take screenshots at the three widths and save them with your files. They prove responsiveness to whoever marks the task, and the same habit gives you evidence for the <b>Progress and Process</b> marks in your Unit 2 SBA.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>In this practical task you build a one-page site for a main-street business in your own town. Use semantic HTML, an external style sheet, real alt text, three feature boxes in a flexbox row and one media query. Test it at phone, tablet and desktop widths with F12, click every link, and check for British spellings in your CSS.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The task's 25 marks reward five separate qualities, so a beautiful page with broken links or inline styles still loses marks. Work in the order of the criteria: get functionality right first (files, links and images), then clean code, then design, responsiveness and accessibility. Test responsiveness by resizing gradually from about 320 px upward and watching for the exact point where the layout breaks; that is where a breakpoint belongs. Typical lost marks come from tiny details: colour instead of color, a missing viewport tag, two h1 elements or alt text such as \"photo\". Screenshots at three widths give clear evidence of testing.</p>"},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Code and justify a web page","genre":"Paper 02 structured response: HTML and CSS coding with justification","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Guavaberry Grove guest house in Sea Cows Bay, Tortola, has asked Island Pulse Media for a responsive \"Rooms\" page. It must have a header with the site name, a navigation menu linking to Home, Rooms and Contact, a main section with a heading, a short paragraph, one image of the garden room and a table of two room rates, and a footer with the phone number. The page must look good on phones and laptops and be accessible.</p>","prompt":"<p>(a) Write the HTML for the Rooms page, using a correct document structure and semantic tags. <b>(6 marks)</b></p>\n<p>(b) Write CSS for an external style sheet that styles the navigation as a horizontal row using flexbox, uses a brand colour as a hex code, and includes one media query or rule that makes the image responsive. <b>(4 marks)</b></p>\n<p>(c) Justify TWO design or accessibility decisions you made. <b>(4 marks)</b></p>","min":150,"max":400,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> &lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt; ... &lt;head&gt; with title, viewport and link to style.css ... &lt;body&gt; with &lt;header&gt;, &lt;nav&gt;, &lt;main&gt;, &lt;footer&gt; ...<br><b>(b)</b> nav ul { display: flex; ... } ... a colour such as #...; img { max-width: 100%; ... } or @media (...) { ... }<br><b>(c)</b> I used ... because ... This helps ... I also ... so that ...</p>","checklist":["My HTML starts with the doctype and has a head (with title and viewport) and a body.","I used header, nav, main and footer, and every tag is closed and correctly nested.","My image has a meaningful alt attribute and my table has th header cells.","My CSS uses display: flex for the nav, a hex colour and a responsive image rule or media query.","I justified two decisions with a reason and a benefit to users."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"HTML structure and semantics","marks":6,"look":["doctype","<head|head>","<title|title>","viewport","<nav|nav>","<main|main>","<footer|footer>","alt=","<th|<table|table>","href="],"min":6,"strong":"Your HTML structure is complete and semantic. Next step: double-check that every tag closes in the right order.","weak":"Include the doctype, a head with a title and viewport meta tag, and a body with header, nav, main and footer. Add alt text to the image and th cells to the table."},{"criterion":"CSS accuracy","marks":4,"look":["display:\\s*flex","#[0-9a-f]{3,6}","max-width|@media","padding|margin|gap","style\\.css|stylesheet"],"min":3,"strong":"Your CSS is accurate and responsive.","weak":"Use display: flex on the nav list, a hex colour such as #7a1f3d, and img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; } or a media query. Link the file with &lt;link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"style.css\"&gt;."},{"criterion":"Justification of decisions","marks":4,"look":["because|so that|this means|which","access|screen reader|wcag","contrast|legib|read","phone|mobile|responsive|device","consisten|maint|external"],"min":3,"strong":"Your justifications link each decision to a clear user benefit.","weak":"For each decision, give a reason and a benefit: \"I added alt text because screen-reader users need it, which makes the site accessible.\""}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b></p>\n<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=\"UTF-8\"&gt;\n  &lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;Rooms | Guavaberry Grove&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"css/style.css\"&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;header&gt;&lt;p class=\"logo\"&gt;Guavaberry Grove&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;nav&gt;&lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"index.html\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"rooms.html\" aria-current=\"page\"&gt;Rooms&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"contact.html\"&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;\n  &lt;/header&gt;\n  &lt;main&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Our rooms&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Six quiet rooms ten minutes from Road Town and Cane Garden Bay.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;img src=\"images/garden-room.jpg\" alt=\"Garden room with a double bed and doors opening onto the garden\"&gt;\n    &lt;table&gt;\n      &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Room&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Per night&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n      &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Garden room&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;US$95&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n      &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Sea-view room&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;US$130&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n    &lt;/table&gt;\n  &lt;/main&gt;\n  &lt;footer&gt;&lt;p&gt;Call us: &lt;a href=\"tel:+12845550000\"&gt;+1 284 555 0000&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/footer&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n<p><b>(b)</b></p>\n<pre><code>body { font-family: \"Open Sans\", Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.6; color: #222222; }\nheader { background-color: #7a1f3d; padding: 16px; }\nnav ul { display: flex; gap: 24px; list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }\nnav a { color: #ffffff; text-decoration: underline; }\nnav a:hover, nav a:focus { text-decoration: none; outline: 2px solid #ffffff; }\nimg { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }\n@media (max-width: 600px) { nav ul { flex-direction: column; gap: 8px; } }</code></pre>\n<p><b>(c)</b> First, I used semantic tags (header, nav, main, footer) and meaningful alt text on the garden-room photo because screen-reader users can then jump straight to the menu or main content and still understand what the image shows. This meets the WCAG principle of making content perceivable and operable.</p>\n<p>Second, I made the page responsive with img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; } and a media query that stacks the menu on screens narrower than 600 px. Most guests will look up the guest house on a phone, so the image never overflows the screen and the links stay large enough to tap. I also chose white text on the deep guavaberry red (#7a1f3d), which gives strong contrast and keeps the menu legible, even in bright sunlight.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Code questions are marked line by line: structure, closing tags, attributes and correct property names all earn marks, so write neatly and indent nested elements. In the justification, examiners want a <b>decision + reason + user benefit</b>. \"I used alt text\" scores little; \"I used alt text so that blind guests using screen readers know what the room looks like, which meets WCAG\" scores fully.</p>",
 "easy": {"bloom":"Apply","genre":"Structured response: HTML and CSS basics (9 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part. Copy and complete the code.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Write</b> the opening line that every HTML5 page starts with. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li><b>Write</b> a nav containing a list of THREE links: Home (index.html), Rooms (rooms.html) and Contact (contact.html). <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Write</b> an img tag for images/garden-room.jpg with suitable alt text. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Write</b> ONE CSS rule that makes the nav list a row, using display: flex. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE reason for using alt text. <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol>","min":30,"max":200,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> &lt;!DOCTYPE ...&gt;<br><b>(b)</b> &lt;nav&gt;&lt;ul&gt; &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"...\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; ... &lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;<br><b>(c)</b> &lt;img src=\"...\" alt=\"...\"&gt;<br><b>(d)</b> nav ul { display: ...; }<br><b>(e)</b> Alt text helps ... because ...</p>","checklist":["I wrote &lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;.","My nav has a ul with three li items, each with a link.","Every a tag has an href and a closing &lt;/a&gt;.","My img has src and meaningful alt text.","My CSS rule uses display: flex and ends with a semicolon.","I gave a reason for alt text."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Doctype","marks":1,"look":["doctype html"],"min":1,"strong":"Correct.","weak":"Start with &lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;."},{"criterion":"Navigation list","marks":3,"look":["<nav|nav>","<ul|<li|li>","href=.?index\\.html","href=.?rooms\\.html","href=.?contact\\.html"],"min":4,"strong":"Correct navigation code.","weak":"Put a ul inside nav, with three li items, each holding an a tag with the right href."},{"criterion":"Image tag","marks":2,"look":["src=.?images/garden-room\\.jpg","alt=.?[a-z]"],"min":2,"strong":"Correct image code.","weak":"Write &lt;img src=\"images/garden-room.jpg\" alt=\"Garden room with a double bed\"&gt;."},{"criterion":"Flexbox rule","marks":2,"look":["nav|ul","display:\\s*flex"],"min":2,"strong":"Correct CSS rule.","weak":"Write nav ul { display: flex; }."},{"criterion":"Reason for alt text","marks":1,"look":["screen reader|blind|visual|load|search|access"],"min":1,"strong":"Good reason.","weak":"Alt text is read aloud by screen readers for blind users."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code></p><p><b>(b)</b></p><pre><code>&lt;nav&gt;\n  &lt;ul&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"index.html\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"rooms.html\"&gt;Rooms&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"contact.html\"&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n  &lt;/ul&gt;\n&lt;/nav&gt;</code></pre><p><b>(c)</b> <code>&lt;img src=\"images/garden-room.jpg\" alt=\"Garden room with a double bed and doors onto the garden\"&gt;</code></p><p><b>(d)</b> <code>nav ul { display: flex; }</code></p><p><b>(e)</b> Alt text helps blind visitors because a screen reader reads it aloud, so they know what the photo shows.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Check every tag twice: each opening tag needs its closing tag (except img), and each CSS declaration ends with a semicolon. Remember the American spelling: color, not colour.</p>","title":"Code and justify a web page (Foundation)"},
 "up": {"bloom":"Create","genre":"Paper 02 structured response: responsive, accessible HTML and CSS with evaluation, Grade One extension (20 marks)","prompt":"<p>The owner now wants the Rooms page to be fully responsive and accessible.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Write</b> the complete HTML for the Rooms page with a correct document structure, semantic regions, a navigation menu that marks the current page, a skip link, the image with alt text, and a rates table with header cells. <i>(7 marks)</i></li><li><b>Write</b> an external style sheet that applies border-box sizing, uses rem units and a hex brand colour, styles the nav with flexbox, gives links a visible focus style, and uses a mobile-first media query at 768 px to place the image and the table side by side. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explain</b> how the cascade and specificity would decide the colour of the current-page link if both nav a and a class rule set its colour. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluate</b> your page against TWO of the WCAG POUR principles, identifying one remaining limitation. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":200,"max":650,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> &lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt; ... &lt;a class=\"skip\" href=\"#main\"&gt;Skip to main content&lt;/a&gt; ... &lt;nav&gt; ... aria-current=\"page\" ... &lt;main id=\"main\"&gt; ... &lt;table&gt; with &lt;th&gt; ...<br><b>(b)</b> * { box-sizing: border-box; } ... nav ul { display: flex; ... } a:focus { outline: ... } .rooms { display: flex; flex-direction: column; } @media (min-width: 768px) { ... }<br><b>(c)</b> Both rules ... but the class rule has higher specificity, so ... If they were equal, ...<br><b>(d)</b> Perceivable: ... Operable: ... One limitation is ...</p>","checklist":["My HTML has the doctype, head with charset, viewport, title and stylesheet link.","I used header, nav, main and footer, a skip link and aria-current on the Rooms link.","My table uses th for headers and my image has meaningful alt text.","My CSS uses border-box, rem, a hex colour, flexbox, a visible focus style and a min-width media query at 768 px.","I explained specificity and source order correctly.","I evaluated two POUR principles with evidence and named a real limitation."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Complete semantic HTML","marks":7,"look":["doctype","charset","viewport","stylesheet","<nav|nav>","<main|main>","aria-current","skip","alt=","<th|th>"],"min":8,"strong":"Complete, valid and semantic HTML with accessibility features.","weak":"Include the full head, semantic regions, a skip link, aria-current on the Rooms link, alt text and th header cells."},{"criterion":"Responsive, accessible CSS","marks":6,"look":["box-sizing:\\s*border-box","rem","#[0-9a-f]{6}","display:\\s*flex",":focus","@media\\s*\\(min-width:\\s*768px\\)"],"min":6,"strong":"Accurate mobile-first CSS meeting every requirement.","weak":"Apply border-box, rem sizes, a hex colour, flexbox on the nav, a visible :focus style and a min-width: 768px media query."},{"criterion":"Cascade and specificity","marks":3,"look":["specificity|specific","class","order|last|later|after"],"min":3,"strong":"Correct explanation of specificity and source order.","weak":"Explain that a class beats an element selector whatever the order, and that order decides only between equal specificity."},{"criterion":"Evaluation against POUR","marks":4,"look":["perceivable","operable|understandable|robust","limitation|however|still|weakness","alt|contrast|keyboard|focus|caption|skip"],"min":4,"strong":"A balanced evaluation with evidence and a genuine limitation.","weak":"Judge the page against two POUR principles using specific features, then name something that still needs improving."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b></p>\n<pre><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;\n&lt;html lang=\"en\"&gt;\n&lt;head&gt;\n  &lt;meta charset=\"UTF-8\"&gt;\n  &lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"&gt;\n  &lt;title&gt;Rooms | Guavaberry Grove, Tortola&lt;/title&gt;\n  &lt;link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"css/style.css\"&gt;\n&lt;/head&gt;\n&lt;body&gt;\n  &lt;a class=\"skip\" href=\"#main\"&gt;Skip to main content&lt;/a&gt;\n  &lt;header&gt;\n    &lt;p class=\"logo\"&gt;Guavaberry Grove&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;nav&gt;&lt;ul&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"index.html\"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"rooms.html\" class=\"current\" aria-current=\"page\"&gt;Rooms&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n      &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href=\"contact.html\"&gt;Contact&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;\n    &lt;/ul&gt;&lt;/nav&gt;\n  &lt;/header&gt;\n  &lt;main id=\"main\"&gt;\n    &lt;h1&gt;Our rooms&lt;/h1&gt;\n    &lt;p&gt;Six quiet rooms ten minutes from Road Town.&lt;/p&gt;\n    &lt;div class=\"rooms\"&gt;\n      &lt;img src=\"images/garden-room.jpg\" alt=\"Garden room with a double bed and doors opening onto the garden\"&gt;\n      &lt;table&gt;\n        &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Room&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Per night&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n        &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Garden room&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;US$95&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n        &lt;tr&gt;&lt;td&gt;Sea-view room&lt;/td&gt;&lt;td&gt;US$130&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;\n      &lt;/table&gt;\n    &lt;/div&gt;\n  &lt;/main&gt;\n  &lt;footer&gt;&lt;p&gt;Call &lt;a href=\"tel:+12845550000\"&gt;+1 284 555 0000&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/footer&gt;\n&lt;/body&gt;\n&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre>\n<p><b>(b)</b></p>\n<pre><code>* { box-sizing: border-box; }\nbody { font-family: \"Open Sans\", Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 1rem; line-height: 1.6; color: #222222; margin: 0; }\nheader { background-color: #7a1f3d; padding: 1rem; }\nnav ul { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1.5rem; list-style: none; margin: 0; padding: 0; }\nnav a { color: #ffffff; }\nnav a.current { color: #ffd166; font-weight: 700; }\na:focus, button:focus { outline: 3px solid #ffd166; outline-offset: 2px; }\n.skip { position: absolute; left: -999px; }\n.skip:focus { left: 1rem; top: 1rem; background: #ffffff; color: #222222; padding: 0.5rem; }\n.rooms { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1rem; }\nimg { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }\n@media (min-width: 768px) {\n  .rooms { flex-direction: row; align-items: flex-start; }\n}</code></pre>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Both nav a and nav a.current match the Rooms link, so they conflict over its colour. Neither is inline, so the browser compares specificity: nav a.current contains a class, which gives it higher specificity than nav a, which uses only element selectors. The current link is therefore #ffd166 whatever the order of the rules. Source order would decide only if the two selectors had equal specificity, in which case the later rule would win.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> <b>Perceivable:</b> the image has meaningful alt text, the rates are real table text rather than a picture, and white text on #7a1f3d gives strong contrast, so the content can be perceived by screen-reader users and in bright sunlight. <b>Operable:</b> the skip link, visible focus outlines and normal links mean the whole page can be used with a keyboard, and the wrapping flexbox menu keeps links tappable on phones. However, one limitation remains: the table has no caption element and the page offers no Spanish version, so some screen-reader users lack a table summary and Spanish-speaking visitors may struggle. Adding a caption and a translated page would improve the page further.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>At this level, examiners check that each feature you claim in part (d) actually appears in your code in parts (a) and (b). Write the code first, then evaluate honestly. In part (c), name both factors: specificity first, then source order for a tie.</p>","title":"Code and justify a web page (Challenge)"}}]},{"id":"M2U7","title":"Website infrastructure, data handling and security","objectives":["Explain how domain names, URLs and DNS work, including Caribbean country-code top-level domains such as .sx, .tt and .jm.","Compare hosting types (shared, VPS, cloud and free static hosts such as GitHub Pages and Netlify) and explain the roles of back-end systems, databases and content management tools.","Explain how websites collect and store data through forms, and apply privacy, consent, data storage and permission principles.","Describe basic website security threats (phishing, malware, weak passwords, outdated plugins) and protections such as strong passwords, 2FA, HTTPS/SSL, updates and backups.","Repeat a hand-coded build in a website builder (Weebly, Google Sites, Wix) and evaluate the two approaches."],"videos":[{"title":"How DNS works","q":"how DNS works explained simply domain name system"},{"title":"Types of web hosting","q":"shared vs VPS vs cloud hosting explained beginners"},{"title":"HTTPS and SSL certificates","q":"what is HTTPS SSL TLS certificate explained simply"},{"title":"Publish a site free with GitHub Pages or Netlify","q":"publish html website free github pages netlify tutorial"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Domains, URLs and DNS","html":"<p>In Unit 6 you built the Guavaberry Grove site on your own computer. To put it on the Internet you need two things: a <b>domain name</b> (the address) and <b>hosting</b> (the place the files live).</p>\n<p><b>The parts of a URL</b></p>\n<p class=\"passage\">https://www.guavaberrygrove.vg/rooms.html#rates</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Part</th><th>Example</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Protocol</td><td>https://</td><td>The rules for transferring the page; the S means secure (encrypted)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Subdomain</td><td>www</td><td>A section of the domain; others include shop. or blog.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Second-level domain</td><td>guavaberrygrove</td><td>The name you register</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Top-level domain (TLD)</td><td>.vg</td><td>The ending</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Path</td><td>/rooms.html</td><td>The file or page on the server</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Fragment</td><td>#rates</td><td>An anchor on that page</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Types of TLD.</b> <b>Generic</b> TLDs (gTLDs) include .com, .org, .net and newer ones such as .shop. <b>Country-code</b> TLDs (ccTLDs) have two letters: <b>.sx</b> (Sint Maarten), <b>.tt</b> (Trinidad and Tobago), <b>.jm</b> (Jamaica), <b>.bb</b> (Barbados), <b>.gy</b> (Guyana), <b>.lc</b> (St. Lucia), <b>.cw</b> (Curaçao), <b>.bs</b> (Bahamas). The French side of the island (Saint-Martin) has a reserved code, <b>.mf</b>, but it has never been put into use, so French-side sites use .fr or .gp. A ccTLD signals a local business and can help local search results; rules for who may register one differ by country.</p>\n<p><b>Registering a domain.</b> You rent a domain, usually yearly, from a <b>registrar</b>. If you forget to renew it, someone else can buy it. The owner's contact details are stored in the <b>WHOIS</b> record; many registrars offer privacy protection to hide them.</p>\n<p><b>DNS</b> (the Domain Name System) is the Internet's phone book. Computers find each other using numeric <b>IP addresses</b>; DNS translates a name like guavaberrygrove.vg into the IP address of the server that hosts it. When you connect a domain to a host, you edit <b>DNS records</b>: an <b>A record</b> points the name to an IP address, a <b>CNAME</b> points one name to another name, and an <b>MX record</b> directs email. Changes can take up to a day or two to <b>propagate</b> around the world.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Do not confuse the domain with hosting. \"The domain is the address on the envelope; hosting is the house where the files live.\" Examiners reward this distinction in \"explain\" questions.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>To put a website online you need a domain name, which is the address, and hosting, which is where the files live. A URL has parts: https://, then a name such as guavaberrygrove, then an ending such as .vg for the British Virgin Islands, then a page. DNS works like a phone book: it turns the name into the number of the server.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The domain-versus-hosting distinction has practical consequences: they are often bought from different companies, so a business can change host without changing its address, simply by updating DNS records. That flexibility is also a risk, because whoever controls the registrar account controls where the domain points; a domain registered in a contractor's name, or allowed to expire, can be lost. A ccTLD such as .sx or .jm signals local identity, but some customers trust .com more, so businesses often register both. DNS changes are not instant: propagation can take up to a day or two, which matters when planning a launch. Explain each part by its job.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"What happens when you type a URL?","intro":"<p>A visitor at the Beef Island airport types guavaberrygrove.vg into her phone. Put the steps in the order they happen.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"The visitor types guavaberrygrove.vg into the browser and presses Go"},{"t":"The browser asks a DNS resolver for the IP address of that domain"},{"t":"DNS servers look up the domain's records and return the IP address of the web host"},{"t":"The browser connects to the server at that IP address and sets up a secure HTTPS connection"},{"t":"The server sends back index.html, the CSS file and the images"},{"t":"The browser reads the HTML and CSS and displays the home page"}],"win":"Correct. DNS turns the name into an IP address first; only then can the browser fetch and display the files from the host.","tutor":[{"h":"Name before number","p":"<p>The browser cannot connect to a name. What must it find out first?</p>"},{"h":"Secure first","p":"<p>The encrypted connection is set up before any page files are sent.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"What happens when you type a URL? (Foundation)","intro":"<p>Put these four steps in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"The user types the domain name"},{"t":"DNS finds the server's IP address"},{"t":"The server sends the page files"},{"t":"The browser displays the page"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>A more detailed version. Put the nine steps in order, from typing the address to seeing the page.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"The visitor types guavaberrygrove.vg and presses Go"},{"t":"The browser checks whether it already has the IP address stored in its cache"},{"t":"It is not cached, so the DNS resolver asks a root server where .sx domains are handled"},{"t":"The .sx top-level-domain server points to the domain's authoritative name server"},{"t":"The authoritative name server returns the A record: the host's IP address"},{"t":"The browser connects to that IP address and completes a TLS handshake for HTTPS"},{"t":"The browser sends an HTTP request for the home page"},{"t":"The server responds with index.html"},{"t":"The browser reads the HTML, requests the CSS and images, and displays the page"}],"win":"Correct. The cache is checked first, then DNS works down from the root to the .sx servers to the authoritative server; only with the IP address can the browser connect securely, request the page and fetch its files.","title":"What happens when you type a URL? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Infrastructure terms","intro":"<p>Maya at Island Pulse Media (a regional agency) is explaining website infrastructure to the owner of the Guavaberry Grove guest house. Match each term to its meaning.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Domain name","r":"The human-friendly address, such as guavaberrygrove.vg"},{"l":"DNS","r":"The system that translates domain names into IP addresses"},{"l":"Registrar","r":"A company that rents out domain names, usually yearly"},{"l":"ccTLD","r":"A two-letter country ending such as .jm or .tt"},{"l":"Web host","r":"A company whose servers store the site files and send them to visitors"},{"l":"A record","r":"A DNS entry that points a domain to a server's IP address"},{"l":"MX record","r":"A DNS entry that tells the Internet where to deliver email for the domain"}],"win":"Well matched. You can now explain the chain from domain name to DNS to host.","tutor":[{"h":"Address vs house","p":"<p>Which terms describe the address, and which describe where the files live?</p>"},{"h":"DNS records","p":"<p>One record is for the website, another is for email (think \"mail exchange\").</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Infrastructure terms (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Domain name","r":"The address, such as guavaberrygrove.vg"},{"l":"DNS","r":"Turns names into IP addresses"},{"l":"Web host","r":"Stores the site files on a server"},{"l":"ccTLD","r":"A country ending such as .sx"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Ten more precise terms. Match each to its meaning for the Guavaberry Grove site.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Protocol","r":"https:// at the start of the address"},{"l":"Subdomain","r":"blog. in blog.guavaberrygrove.vg"},{"l":"Second-level domain","r":"guavaberrygrove: the name actually registered"},{"l":"Path","r":"/rooms.html in the address"},{"l":"Fragment","r":"#rates at the end of the address"},{"l":"CNAME record","r":"Points shop.guavaberrygrove.vg to another name, such as an online store platform's address"},{"l":"MX record","r":"Tells other mail servers where to deliver email for the domain"},{"l":"WHOIS","r":"The public record of who registered a domain, which privacy protection can hide"},{"l":"DNS propagation","r":"The delay of up to a day or two before DNS changes are seen everywhere"},{"l":"Auto-renew","r":"Stops the domain expiring and being bought by someone else"}],"win":"Well matched. You can now read every part of a URL and explain the DNS records and settings behind it.","title":"Infrastructure terms (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Types of web hosting","html":"<p>A <b>web host</b> rents space on a <b>server</b>, a computer that is always on and connected to the Internet. Hosts differ in cost, control, performance and the skills they demand.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>How it works</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Limitations</th><th>Suits</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Shared hosting</b></td><td>Many sites share one server's resources</td><td>Cheapest paid option; host manages the server; often includes email and one-click WordPress</td><td>Slower if a neighbouring site gets heavy traffic; limited control</td><td>A small guest house, church or school club</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>VPS</b> (virtual private server)</td><td>One server is divided into private virtual machines with guaranteed resources</td><td>More power and control; you can install your own software</td><td>Costs more; you (or a technician) manage updates and security</td><td>A growing online shop or busy news site</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Cloud hosting</b></td><td>The site runs across a network of servers (for example AWS, Google Cloud, Microsoft Azure)</td><td>Scales up for traffic spikes, such as Carnival ticket sales; high reliability</td><td>Pricing can be complex; needs technical skill</td><td>Apps, large organisations, sites with unpredictable traffic</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Dedicated server</b></td><td>One whole physical server for one client</td><td>Full control and performance</td><td>Expensive; needs expert management</td><td>Banks, large telecoms</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Free static hosts</b> (GitHub Pages, Netlify)</td><td>Serve HTML, CSS, JavaScript and images, with no server-side code</td><td>Free; HTTPS included; ideal for a hand-coded portfolio</td><td>No built-in database or server-side forms; usage limits apply</td><td>Your SBA prototype, an e-portfolio, a class project</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Publishing a hand-coded site free.</b> On <b>Netlify</b>, you can drag and drop your project folder onto the dashboard and receive a link such as yoursite.netlify.app. On <b>GitHub Pages</b>, you upload your files to a repository, turn on Pages in Settings and receive a link such as username.github.io. Both allow you to connect your own domain later through DNS.</p>\n<p>Traditional hosts often use <b>FTP</b> or SFTP to upload files; the syllabus lists <b>FileZilla</b>, a free FTP client. A <b>control panel</b> such as cPanel lets owners manage files, email accounts and databases.</p>\n<p><b>Choosing a host:</b> consider cost (monthly and renewal), <b>uptime</b> (how often the site is available), speed and server location, storage and bandwidth limits, security features (free SSL, backups), support, and whether it supports a CMS.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> The SBA product criteria include costs such as website hosting. In your proposal, name a real hosting option, state whether it is free or paid, and justify it for your audience and budget.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A web host rents space on a server that is always on. Shared hosting is cheap because many sites share one server. A VPS gives more power and control. Cloud hosting grows when traffic jumps, such as Carnival ticket sales. A dedicated server is one whole machine. Free hosts such as Netlify suit a simple hand-coded site.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Choosing a host means matching <b>what the site does</b> to what the host provides. A static portfolio needs no database, so free static hosting is ideal; a booking form or WordPress blog needs server-side processing, which a static host cannot provide without add-on services. Watch hidden costs: many hosts advertise a low first-year price that rises sharply at renewal. Server location affects speed, and backups and free SSL affect security. Scaling is a trade-off too: shared hosting is cheapest but suffers when a neighbouring site gets heavy traffic, while cloud hosting handles spikes but has complex pricing. Always justify a host against the site's needs and budget.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Choose the right hosting","intro":"<p>Island Pulse Media has five clients across the Caribbean. Choose the most suitable hosting for each.</p>","items":[{"text":"A CAPE student wants to publish her hand-coded HTML and CSS e-portfolio for free with HTTPS.","options":["Netlify or GitHub Pages","VPS","Dedicated server"],"answer":0,"fb":"A static site needs no server-side code, and free static hosts provide HTTPS at no cost."},{"text":"Grenada Spice Tours needs a small WordPress site and a few email addresses on a tight budget.","options":["GitHub Pages","Shared hosting","Cloud cluster"],"answer":1,"fb":"Shared hosting is cheap, managed by the host and usually includes email and one-click WordPress. GitHub Pages cannot run WordPress."},{"text":"The Port of Spain Carnival Tix site gets a huge traffic spike for two weeks each year.","options":["Cloud hosting","Shared hosting","A laptop at the office"],"answer":0,"fb":"Cloud hosting can scale up resources during the spike and scale down afterwards."},{"text":"A growing online craft shop in Kingston needs custom software and more guaranteed resources, with a technician to manage it.","options":["Shared hosting","VPS","Free static host"],"answer":1,"fb":"A VPS gives guaranteed resources and control to install software, at a moderate cost."},{"text":"Which host type would NOT be able to process a booking form and save it to a database without extra services?","options":["A free static host","A VPS","Shared hosting with PHP and MySQL"],"answer":0,"fb":"Static hosts serve files only. They need a third-party form service or other add-ons to collect data."}],"win":"Good decisions. You matched hosting type to cost, traffic, control and the features each site needs.","tutor":[{"h":"Static or dynamic?","p":"<p>Does the site only show files, or does it run software such as WordPress or a database?</p>"},{"h":"Traffic pattern","p":"<p>Steady and small, steady and growing, or sudden spikes?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Choose the right hosting (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Free hosting for a hand-coded portfolio:","options":["GitHub Pages","Dedicated server"],"answer":0,"fb":"Free static hosting suits HTML and CSS sites."},{"text":"A cheap small business site with email:","options":["Shared hosting","Cloud cluster"],"answer":0,"fb":"Shared hosting is low-cost and managed for you."},{"text":"A site with a big yearly traffic spike:","options":["Cloud hosting","Shared hosting"],"answer":0,"fb":"Cloud hosting scales up for spikes."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder hosting decisions with trade-offs. Choose the BEST option.</p>","items":[{"text":"A Jamaican news site's traffic jumps twentyfold whenever a hurricane approaches.","options":["A laptop in the newsroom","Shared hosting","Cloud hosting with a content delivery network (CDN)","A free static host"],"answer":2,"fb":"Cloud resources scale for spikes, and a CDN spreads the load and helps resist floods of traffic."},{"text":"A hand-coded portfolio on Netlify now needs a simple contact form, with no back end of its own.","options":["Move to a dedicated server","Remove the form","Add a form service such as Netlify Forms or Formspree","Switch to a VPS"],"answer":2,"fb":"A form service collects submissions for a static site without the cost of a server."},{"text":"A St. Lucia craft shop on shared hosting slows down whenever other sites on the server are busy, and sales are growing.","options":["Upgrade to a VPS with guaranteed resources","Move to GitHub Pages","Stay on shared hosting","Delete the product photos"],"answer":0,"fb":"A VPS gives guaranteed resources, ending the \"noisy neighbour\" problem."},{"text":"Maya must upload hand-coded files to a traditional host that does not offer drag-and-drop.","options":["Use an SFTP client such as FileZilla","Email the files to the host","Print the files","Paste the code into a WhatsApp chat"],"answer":0,"fb":"SFTP (secure FTP) clients such as FileZilla transfer files to the server."},{"text":"A host advertises \"US$1 a month\" for the first year. What should the client check before signing up?","options":["Only the colour of the dashboard","Whether the host has a mobile app","The renewal price, uptime record, backups and free SSL","Nothing, it is a bargain"],"answer":2,"fb":"Introductory prices often rise sharply at renewal, and reliability and security features matter more than the first-year price."},{"text":"A regional bank needs full control of its server hardware and maximum performance, with expert staff to manage it.","options":["A dedicated server","Shared hosting","A website builder free plan","Free static host"],"answer":0,"fb":"A dedicated server gives one client the whole machine and full control, at a high cost."}],"win":"Strong decisions. You weighed traffic, features, cost over time and control, the factors a \"recommend and justify\" answer needs.","title":"Choose the right hosting (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Front end, back end, databases and CMS","html":"<p>A website has two sides.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <b>front end</b> (client side) is what runs in the visitor's browser: HTML, CSS and JavaScript. Everything you coded in Unit 6 is front end.</li>\n<li>The <b>back end</b> (server side) runs on the host's server. It processes form submissions, logs users in, saves bookings and builds pages from stored content. Common back-end languages include <b>PHP</b>, <b>Python</b> and <b>JavaScript (Node.js)</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A <b>static site</b> sends the same files to every visitor; it is fast and secure but needs manual edits to change content. A <b>dynamic site</b> builds pages on the server, often from a database, so content can change without editing HTML files.</p>\n<p><b>Databases</b> store structured data in <b>tables</b> of <b>records</b> (rows) and <b>fields</b> (columns). MySQL and PostgreSQL are common. Guavaberry Grove booking system might store:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>booking_id</th><th>guest_name</th><th>email</th><th>room</th><th>check_in</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1042</td><td>A. Brown</td><td>a.brown@example.com</td><td>Garden</td><td>2026-12-18</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The back end uses a query language (SQL) to add, find or update records. Because databases hold personal information, they must be protected, which is why data handling and security are studied together.</p>\n<p><b>Content management systems (CMS)</b> combine a back end, a database and an admin dashboard, so non-coders can publish. Section 2.1 of the course outline names <b>WordPress</b>, <b>Wix</b>, <b>Squarespace</b> and <b>Joomla</b>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Themes</b> control the design; <b>plugins</b> (WordPress) or <b>apps</b> (Wix) add features such as booking calendars, galleries and contact forms.</li>\n<li><b>User roles</b> control permissions: an <b>administrator</b> can change everything; an <b>editor</b> can publish and edit posts; an <b>author</b> or <b>contributor</b> can write but has limited rights.</li>\n<li><b>Self-hosted</b> WordPress (from WordPress.org, installed on your own host) gives full control but you handle updates. <b>Hosted</b> platforms such as WordPress.com, Wix and Squarespace look after the server for you.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A national news site publishes many articles a day; a CMS lets reporters post stories without touching code.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> To \"distinguish\" front end and back end, use the same feature: \"The front end runs in the visitor's browser and controls what they see, whereas the back end runs on the server and processes and stores data, such as a booking.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A website has a front end, which runs in the visitor's browser (HTML, CSS and JavaScript), and a back end, which runs on the server and saves things such as bookings. A static site shows the same files to everyone. A dynamic site builds pages from a database. A CMS such as WordPress lets a guest house owner update prices without code.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The static-versus-dynamic choice is a trade-off between <b>simplicity and capability</b>. Static sites are fast, cheap and have little to attack, but every content change means editing files. Dynamic sites can take bookings and let non-coders publish, but the database and CMS add maintenance and security duties. A database stores records in tables, and SQL queries read and change them, which is why unvalidated form input can become an injection attack. CMS user roles apply least privilege to content: an author can write but not delete the site. In an exam, distinguish front and back end on the same feature, then link the choice to the client's needs.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Static or dynamic?","q":"Guavaberry Grove wants guests to check room availability online, submit a booking request, and let the owner update prices herself each season without editing code. Which solution BEST meets all three needs?","options":[{"t":"A PDF price list emailed to guests who ask","fb":"This is not a website feature and gives no online availability or booking."},{"t":"A CMS such as WordPress on shared hosting, with a booking plugin that stores requests in a database","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. A CMS provides a back end and database for availability and bookings, plus a dashboard so the owner can edit prices without coding."},{"t":"Inline CSS on every page to highlight the prices","fb":"CSS only changes appearance. It cannot store data or process bookings."},{"t":"The hand-coded HTML site on GitHub Pages with prices typed into a table","fb":"A static site cannot check availability or save bookings, and the owner would need to edit HTML to change prices."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Three needs","p":"<p>Availability and bookings need stored data. Owner edits need a dashboard.</p>"},{"h":"Front end limits","p":"<p>HTML and CSS alone cannot save anything a visitor types.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Static or dynamic? (Foundation)","q":"Which kind of website can save bookings in a database and let the owner edit content from a dashboard?","options":[{"t":"A static HTML page","fb":"Static pages cannot save data."},{"t":"A CSS file","fb":"CSS only styles pages."},{"t":"A dynamic site built with a CMS","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. A CMS has a back end, database and dashboard."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A Barbados yoga studio wants: a fast, cheap site; a weekly class timetable the owner updates herself; and a contact form. It has no budget for a developer after launch. Which plan is BEST?","options":[{"t":"A hand-coded static site on GitHub Pages, with the timetable typed into an HTML table.","fb":"It is fast and free, but the owner would need to edit HTML every week, and there is no way to handle the form."},{"t":"A PDF timetable emailed to clients each week.","fb":"This is not a website and gives new clients no way to find the studio or contact it online."},{"t":"A dedicated server running custom back-end code.","fb":"Far too expensive and complex for a small studio with no developer."},{"t":"A hosted CMS or website builder with a timetable feature and a built-in contact form.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It gives a dashboard for weekly updates and a working form, with hosting and security managed for her."}],"title":"Static or dynamic? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Forms, data collection, privacy and permissions","html":"<p><b>Forms</b> are how websites collect data: contact messages, bookings, newsletter sign-ups, surveys. In HTML:</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;form action=\"https://formspree.io/f/your-id\" method=\"post\"&gt;\n  &lt;label for=\"email\"&gt;Email&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;input type=\"email\" id=\"email\" name=\"email\" required&gt;\n  &lt;label&gt;&lt;input type=\"checkbox\" name=\"consent\" required&gt;\n    I agree to Guavaberry Grove storing my details to reply to my enquiry.&lt;/label&gt;\n  &lt;button type=\"submit\"&gt;Send&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre>\n<ul>\n<li><b>action</b> is where the data is sent (a back-end script or a form service such as Formspree or Netlify Forms); <b>method=\"post\"</b> sends it in the request body rather than the URL.</li>\n<li>Each input has a <b>label</b> (for accessibility) and a <b>type</b> (email, tel, date, number) that brings up the right phone keyboard and allows basic <b>validation</b>. <b>required</b> stops empty submissions. Validation must also happen on the server, because browser checks can be bypassed.</li>\n<li>Google Forms and Microsoft Forms are quick alternatives that store responses in a spreadsheet.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Privacy principles</b> for any data you collect:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Data minimisation</b>: collect only what you need. A contact form needs a name and email, not a date of birth or passport number.</li>\n<li><b>Purpose and transparency</b>: say why you collect it in a clear <b>privacy policy</b> linked from every form.</li>\n<li><b>Consent</b>: users actively agree (an unticked box they tick themselves); separate consent for marketing emails.</li>\n<li><b>Secure storage</b>: encrypted connections (HTTPS), protected databases, and passwords stored only as <b>hashes</b>, never as plain text.</li>\n<li><b>Retention</b>: delete data you no longer need.</li>\n<li><b>Rights</b>: users may ask to see or delete their data.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Laws.</b> Many Caribbean territories have data protection laws, and Sint Maarten has its own personal data protection legislation; check the current law for your territory. The EU's <b>GDPR</b> can apply when a site targets visitors in the European Union, which matters for a guest house that welcomes Dutch and French guests.</p>\n<p><b>Permissions</b> follow the <b>principle of least privilege</b>: give each person only the access they need. The Guavaberry Grove receptionist needs to view bookings, not change the site theme or delete users. Remove accounts when staff leave. Apps and sites that ask for <b>location, camera or contact</b> permissions should explain why.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When evaluating a form in Paper 02, check four things: does it collect only necessary data, is consent clear, is the connection HTTPS, and is there a privacy policy link?</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Forms collect information such as bookings and sign-ups. Every input needs a label, and the right type, such as email or date. Collect only what you need, explain why in a privacy policy, ask for consent with a box the user ticks, use HTTPS, and delete data you no longer need. Give staff only the access they need.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Privacy principles turn into concrete design decisions. Data minimisation means removing fields, not just protecting them, because data you never collect cannot be leaked. Consent must be specific and active: bundling newsletter consent into a \"terms\" box, or pre-ticking it, is not genuine consent. Technical choices matter as well: method=\"get\" puts form data into the URL, where it can be saved in browser history and server logs, so personal data should use post over HTTPS. Browser validation improves usability but can be bypassed, so the server must validate too. For Caribbean businesses serving European guests, EU rules can also apply, so check the law for each audience.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the data-handling problems","intro":"<p>Keisha (marketing, Island Pulse Media) drafted a newsletter sign-up form and its settings for the Kralendijk Dive Shack. Some lines break privacy or security principles.</p>","q":"Select every line that is a privacy or security problem.","lines":["The form collects name and email address only.","The form also asks for date of birth and passport number \"for our records\".","The marketing consent box is already ticked when the page loads.","A link to the privacy policy sits next to the Submit button.","The form page loads over http:// with no SSL certificate.","Subscriber passwords are stored in the database as plain text.","Every staff member, including the summer intern, has an administrator account.","Old sign-ups are deleted after two years of no activity."],"correct":[1,2,4,5,6],"win":"Well spotted. You applied data minimisation, active consent, HTTPS, password hashing and least privilege.","miss":"Some problems remain. Check for unnecessary data, pre-ticked consent, a missing HTTPS connection, plain-text passwords and over-generous permissions.","tutor":[{"h":"Need to know","p":"<p>Does a newsletter really need every item it asks for?</p>"},{"h":"Active consent","p":"<p>Consent should be something the user does, not something already done for them.</p>"},{"h":"Who has the keys?","p":"<p>Should everyone have full control?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the data-handling problems (Foundation)","q":"Select the two problems.","lines":["The form asks only for name and email.","The consent box is pre-ticked.","The page uses http:// with no encryption.","A privacy policy is linked."],"correct":[1,2],"win":"Yes: consent must be active, and forms need HTTPS.","miss":"Look for pre-ticked consent and a page without HTTPS."},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A second, more polished form for Kralendijk Dive Shack. The problems are harder to spot.</p>","q":"Select every line that is a privacy or security problem.","lines":["The form posts over HTTPS to a reputable form service.","Newsletter consent is bundled into the single \"I accept the terms\" checkbox.","The form uses method=\"get\", so the email address appears in the page URL.","The only validation is the browser's required attribute; the server accepts anything it receives.","The privacy policy says enquiries are deleted after 12 months.","Subscriber passwords are stored as salted hashes.","The only backup is an unencrypted USB stick kept in the shop drawer.","The site asks for the visitor's location as soon as the home page opens, with no explanation.","The summer intern's account was deleted on her last day.","Staff accounts use 2FA through an authenticator app."],"correct":[1,2,3,6,7],"win":"Sharp judgement. Consent must be separate, personal data should not travel in the URL, the server must validate input, backups need encryption and an off-site copy, and permission requests need a reason.","miss":"Look again. The HTTPS form service, the retention policy, hashed passwords, the deleted intern account and authenticator-app 2FA are all good practice. Problems involve consent, URL exposure, validation, backups and permissions.","title":"Spot the data-handling problems (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Passwords, 2FA, HTTPS and backups","html":"<p>Security protects the <b>confidentiality</b>, <b>integrity</b> and <b>availability</b> of a site and its data (the CIA triad). Four everyday protections do most of the work.</p>\n<p><b>1. Strong passwords.</b> A strong password is <b>long</b> (at least 12 to 15 characters), <b>unique</b> to each account and hard to guess. A <b>passphrase</b> of several random words, such as <i>mango-hammock-turtle-91</i>, is long and memorable. Avoid names, birthdays, \"password123\" and anything linked to you, like \"Kingston2026\". Use a <b>password manager</b> (for example Bitwarden or the one built into your browser) to create and store unique passwords. Never reuse your email password on a website admin account.</p>\n<p><b>2. Two-factor authentication (2FA)</b> adds a second proof of identity. Factors are something you <b>know</b> (password), something you <b>have</b> (phone, security key) and something you <b>are</b> (fingerprint, face). With 2FA, a stolen password alone is not enough. Authenticator apps (Google Authenticator, Microsoft Authenticator) are safer than SMS codes, which can be intercepted through SIM-swap fraud. Turn on 2FA for the hosting account, the registrar, the CMS admin and the social media accounts linked to the site.</p>\n<p><b>3. HTTPS and SSL/TLS.</b> HTTPS encrypts data travelling between the browser and the server, so a booking form sent over hotel or airport Wi-Fi cannot be read in transit. It uses a <b>TLS certificate</b> (still commonly called an <b>SSL certificate</b>) issued by a certificate authority; Let's Encrypt provides them free, and most hosts install one automatically. Browsers mark plain http sites \"Not secure\", which scares off customers, and search engines favour HTTPS sites. Note: HTTPS protects data in transit; it does <b>not</b> prove the site owner is honest, because phishing sites can have certificates too.</p>\n<p><b>4. Backups.</b> Hurricane Irma in 2017 showed how quickly local equipment can be lost. Follow the <b>3-2-1 rule</b>: three copies of your data, on two different types of storage, with one copy <b>off-site</b> or in the cloud. Automate daily or weekly backups of files and the database, keep several older versions in case malware is copied into recent backups, and <b>test</b> that you can restore them.</p>\n<p>Other good habits: keep software updated, remove unused plugins and accounts, and change default admin usernames such as \"admin\".</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 01 trap: \"The padlock means the site is safe.\" Wrong. The padlock means the connection is encrypted. A Grade One answer distinguishes encryption in transit from trustworthiness.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Four habits protect a website. Use long, unique passwords or passphrases, kept in a password manager. Turn on two-factor authentication, so a stolen password is not enough. Use HTTPS, which locks data as it travels. Keep backups using the 3-2-1 rule: three copies, two types of storage, one off-site, as storms across the Caribbean have shown.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Each control protects a different part of the CIA triad: HTTPS protects <b>confidentiality</b> in transit, hashing and updates protect <b>integrity</b>, and backups protect <b>availability</b>. Not all 2FA is equal: SMS codes can be stolen through SIM-swap fraud, so authenticator apps or security keys are stronger. A common misconception is that the padlock proves a site is trustworthy; it proves only that the connection is encrypted, and phishing sites often have certificates. Backups need versioning and testing, because ransomware can sit unnoticed and be copied into recent backups, and an untested backup may not restore. Layered defences matter, because any single control can fail.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Threat and defence","intro":"<p>Zara is preparing a security checklist for Island Pulse Media's clients. Match each threat to the defence that best reduces it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"A fake \"electricity bill overdue\" email asking staff to log in on a lookalike page","r":"Staff training to check sender and URL, plus 2FA on accounts"},{"l":"Attackers trying thousands of common passwords on the admin login","r":"Long unique passwords, login limits and 2FA"},{"l":"A three-year-old WordPress plugin with a known security hole","r":"Update or remove plugins and keep the CMS current"},{"l":"Ransomware encrypts all the site files","r":"Recent tested backups stored off-site"},{"l":"Booking details read by someone on the same café Wi-Fi","r":"HTTPS with a valid TLS certificate"},{"l":"A former employee still logging in to the dashboard","r":"Remove old accounts and apply least privilege"}],"win":"Well matched. Each threat has a specific defence; layered defences give the best protection.","tutor":[{"h":"Name the weakness","p":"<p>Is the weakness people, passwords, old software, lost data, the connection or access rights?</p>"},{"h":"Recovery vs prevention","p":"<p>Some defences stop attacks; one helps you recover after an attack succeeds.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Threat and defence (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Phishing email","r":"Check the sender and URL before logging in"},{"l":"Weak password","r":"Long, unique password plus 2FA"},{"l":"Outdated plugin","r":"Update or remove it"},{"l":"Files destroyed","r":"Restore from a backup"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>More specific threats. Match each one to the defence that BEST reduces it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Criminals use passwords leaked from another site to log in to the admin dashboard (credential stuffing)","r":"A unique password for every account, plus 2FA"},{"l":"A fraudster ports the owner's phone number to a new SIM to receive her login codes","r":"Use an authenticator app or security key instead of SMS codes"},{"l":"Code typed into the search box lists every customer email from the database","r":"Server-side validation and an up-to-date CMS or reputable form tools"},{"l":"A flood of traffic takes the Carnival ticket site offline on launch day","r":"Hosting with DDoS protection or a content delivery network"},{"l":"A pirated premium theme quietly redirects visitors to a scam site","r":"Install themes and plugins only from trusted sources and run security scans"},{"l":"Ransomware is copied into last night's backup","r":"Keep several older backup versions and test restoring them"},{"l":"The office laptop holding the only copy of the site is damaged in a storm","r":"Follow the 3-2-1 backup rule with one copy off-site"},{"l":"Booking details are read by someone on the same airport Wi-Fi","r":"HTTPS with a valid TLS certificate"},{"l":"A staff member clicks a fake \"account suspended\" email and enters her password","r":"Phishing awareness training plus 2FA"}],"win":"Well matched. Specific threats need specific defences, and many need two layers, such as training plus 2FA.","title":"Threat and defence (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Common website security threats","html":"<p><b>Basic website security</b> starts here. Know these threats, how they work and how to reduce them.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Threat</th><th>How it works</th><th>Caribbean example</th><th>Defence</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Phishing</b></td><td>Fake emails, texts or sites trick people into giving passwords or payment details</td><td>A WhatsApp message claiming to be from a bank, linking to a lookalike login page</td><td>Training, check URLs, 2FA, never log in from a link</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Malware</b></td><td>Malicious software: viruses, trojans, spyware, <b>ransomware</b> (locks files and demands payment)</td><td>A hacked theme file injects code that redirects visitors to a scam site</td><td>Updates, antivirus, trusted downloads only, backups, security scans</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Weak or reused passwords</b></td><td>Attackers guess common passwords (<b>brute force</b>) or reuse passwords leaked from other sites (<b>credential stuffing</b>)</td><td>The admin login is \"admin\" with password \"island1\"</td><td>Long unique passwords, password manager, 2FA, login limits</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Outdated software and plugins</b></td><td>Known security holes in old CMS versions, themes or plugins are scanned for automatically</td><td>A guest house booking plugin not updated since 2022</td><td>Auto-updates, remove unused plugins, choose well-maintained ones</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Injection attacks</b></td><td>Harmful code typed into forms, such as SQL injection, to read or change a database</td><td>A search box that lets an attacker list all customer emails</td><td>Server-side validation, up-to-date CMS, reputable form tools</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>DDoS</b> (distributed denial of service)</td><td>Floods a site with traffic so real users cannot reach it</td><td>A ticket site taken offline on launch day</td><td>Hosting with DDoS protection or a content delivery network (CDN)</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Defacement</b></td><td>An attacker changes the site's content</td><td>A school site's home page replaced with a message</td><td>All of the above, plus backups to restore quickly</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Why it matters to businesses:</b> loss of customer trust, stolen data, legal consequences under data protection laws, lost income while the site is down, and search engines warning visitors away from infected sites.</p>\n<p><b>People are the weakest link.</b> Most successful attacks start with a person clicking a link or reusing a password, which is why staff training appears in every good security plan. Real Caribbean governments and businesses have reported cyber incidents in recent years; check reputable news sources for current cases rather than relying on rumours.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For a \"discuss\" question on website security, structure each paragraph as <b>threat, impact, defence</b>. Three well-developed threats score better than seven listed ones.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Common website threats include phishing, malware, weak passwords, out-of-date plugins, injection attacks, floods of traffic called DDoS, and defacement. Each has a defence, such as training, updates, strong passwords, server checks and backups. Most attacks start with a person clicking a fake link, so staff training matters as much as technology.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Attackers target small Caribbean businesses not because they are famous but because they are easy: automated tools scan the Internet for outdated plugins and weak logins. Thinking in terms of <b>attack surface</b> helps: every unused plugin, old account and default username is another way in, so removing things is often the best defence. The business impact is wider than the technical damage: lost bookings while the site is down, search engines warning visitors away, and possible legal duties if personal data leaks. In a \"discuss\" answer, develop each threat fully as threat, impact and defence, and judge which matters most for the client described.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Guavaberry Grove goes live","intro":"<p>Maya has finished the Guavaberry Grove site. The owner, Mrs. Richardson, now wants it online with a booking enquiry form, and later a WordPress blog. Make the infrastructure and security decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Mrs. Richardson asks which domain to register. Most guests come from the USA, the Netherlands and the wider Caribbean, and she wants a clear local identity. What does Maya recommend?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Register guavaberrygrove.vg (and guavaberrygrove.com if available) in the business's name, with auto-renew and WHOIS privacy turned on.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. The .sx shows local identity, owning the .com protects the brand, and auto-renew in the owner's name prevents losing the domain."},{"t":"Let Maya register the domain in her own personal name to save time.","to":"bad1","fb":"The business must own its domain. If the domain is in a contractor's name, the owner can lose control of it."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The site needs a booking enquiry form and a blog the owner can update herself. Which hosting does Maya choose?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A free static host only, with the form posting to nowhere.","to":"bad2","fb":"Static hosting cannot run WordPress, and a form with no back end or form service loses every enquiry."},{"t":"Reputable shared hosting with free SSL, automatic backups and WordPress support.","to":"n3","fb":"Correct. It is affordable, supports a CMS and database, and includes key security features."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Setting up WordPress, Maya must choose the admin login. The receptionist will also need access to read enquiries. What does Maya do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Username \"admin\", password \"guavaberry1\", shared by everyone so nobody gets locked out.","to":"bad3","fb":"A common username, a guessable password and a shared account are an open door to attackers."},{"t":"A unique username (not \"admin\"), a long passphrase stored in a password manager, 2FA, and an Editor-level account for the receptionist.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. Strong authentication plus least privilege."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Six months later, WordPress shows updates for three plugins, and one plugin has not been used since launch. A storm warning is issued for the island. What does Maya advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Back up the site, update WordPress and the plugins, delete the unused plugin, and confirm an off-site backup copy exists.","to":"good","fb":"Strong. Updates close known holes, removing unused plugins shrinks the attack surface, and off-site backups protect against storm damage."},{"t":"Ignore the updates because the site is working fine.","to":"bad4","fb":"Outdated plugins are one of the most common ways small sites are hacked."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Secure and stable.</b> The site is fast, encrypted with HTTPS, protected by strong logins and least privilege, kept up to date and backed up off-site. A Grade One answer would explain each layer and link it to the threat it prevents.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Lost control.</b> Years later the contractor moves abroad, the domain expires in her name and a stranger buys it. Try again: the business should own and auto-renew its domain.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Lost enquiries.</b> Guests fill in the form but nothing is saved or sent, and the owner cannot add blog posts. Try again: match hosting to the back-end features the site needs.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Hacked.</b> A brute-force attack guesses the password within hours and fills the site with spam links. Try again with strong unique credentials, 2FA and separate role-based accounts.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Infected.</b> Attackers exploit a known hole in an old plugin and redirect visitors to a scam page. Search engines flag the site as dangerous. Try again: update, remove unused plugins and back up.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Who owns it?","p":"<p>The domain, hosting and accounts should belong to the business, not the developer.</p>"},{"h":"Features drive hosting","p":"<p>Forms and blogs need something running on the server.</p>"},{"h":"Layers","p":"<p>Think passwords, 2FA, permissions, updates and backups together.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Guavaberry Grove goes live (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: Maya is putting the guest house site online with WordPress.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>How should Maya set up the admin login?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A long unique password with 2FA.","to":"n2","fb":"Yes. That resists guessing and stolen passwords."},{"t":"Username \"admin\" with a short password.","to":"bad","fb":"Easy to guess."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Plugin updates are waiting. What should she do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Back up, then update the plugins.","to":"good","fb":"Correct. Updates fix known security holes."},{"t":"Ignore them; the site still works.","to":"bad","fb":"Outdated plugins are a common way in for attackers."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> The site is protected by strong logins and updates.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Weak logins and old plugins invite attacks. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A harder launch. Mrs. Richardson's site will take booking enquiries from guests in the USA and the Netherlands, run a WordPress blog, and be managed by her and a part-time receptionist.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Who should own the domain, and how?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Register it in Maya's name now and transfer it later.","to":"bad1","fb":"Transfers are often forgotten; the business should own its domain from day one."},{"t":"Register it in the business's name, with auto-renew, WHOIS privacy and 2FA on the registrar account.","to":"n2","fb":"Excellent. Ownership, renewal, privacy and protection of the account that controls the domain."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The form will collect enquiries from Dutch guests. What must Maya consider about data?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Collect passport numbers now to save time at check-in.","to":"bad2","fb":"Unnecessary sensitive data greatly increases the harm of any breach."},{"t":"Collect only necessary fields, add a privacy policy and separate unticked marketing consent, and remember that EU rules can apply to guests targeted in the EU, as well as local law.","to":"n3","fb":"Correct. Minimisation, transparency and consent, with awareness of both local and EU rules."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Mrs. Richardson wants 2FA but asks whether text-message codes are enough.</p>","choices":[{"t":"SMS codes are fine for everyone.","to":"ok1","fb":"SMS 2FA is much better than none, but it is weaker than an authenticator app."},{"t":"Turn 2FA off; it is inconvenient.","to":"bad3","fb":"Without 2FA, a single stolen password gives full control."},{"t":"Recommend an authenticator app, because SMS codes can be intercepted through SIM-swap fraud.","to":"n4","fb":"Good. App-based codes are stronger than SMS."}]},"ok1":{"text":"<p>Months later, the receptionist's phone number is hijacked in a SIM-swap scam. What should Maya do now?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Do nothing, since nothing seems to have changed on the site.","to":"bad3","fb":"An unchecked compromise may still be active."},{"t":"Reset the passwords, review recent logins, and switch every account to an authenticator app.","to":"n4","fb":"Good recovery: contain the incident, then strengthen the weak control."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Hurricane season is approaching. How should backups be set up?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Automated daily backups of files and database, with several versions kept, one copy off-site in the cloud, and a test restore each quarter.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. The 3-2-1 rule, versioning and testing together protect availability."},{"t":"A single weekly backup saved on the same server as the site.","to":"bad4","fb":"If the server fails or is hacked, the backup is lost with it."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Secure, compliant and resilient.</b> The business owns its domain, collects only the data it needs with clear consent, protects logins with strong 2FA and can restore the site after any disaster. A Grade One answer links each control to the threat it reduces and the CIA principle it protects.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Lost control.</b> The transfer is forgotten, Maya changes jobs, and the domain expires. Try again: the business must own its domain.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>High-risk data.</b> A breach would expose passports, causing serious harm and legal trouble. Try again: collect only what is necessary.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Account taken over.</b> A phished password gives an attacker the dashboard. Try again with strong 2FA and a response plan.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>No recovery.</b> When the server is hit, the only backup goes with it. Try again with off-site, versioned, tested backups.</p>","end":"bad"}},"title":"Guavaberry Grove goes live (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Website builders vs hand-coded HTML","html":"<p>A useful activity is to build a multi-page site in HTML and then <b>repeat the build using a website builder</b>, for example <b>Weebly</b>, <b>Google Sites</b> or <b>Wix</b>. Website builders are hosted platforms with drag-and-drop editors, templates, hosting and SSL included.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Builder</th><th>Key features</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Google Sites</b></td><td>Free with a Google account; very simple; embeds Docs, Slides, Forms, YouTube and Maps easily; good for school portfolios and class sites</td><td>Limited design control and few advanced features; mainly suited to simple information sites</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Wix</b></td><td>Large template library; free-position drag-and-drop; apps for bookings, online stores and blogs</td><td>Free plan shows Wix branding and uses a Wix subdomain; changing templates later can be difficult; check current plan prices</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Weebly</b> (owned by Square)</td><td>Simple section-based editor; e-commerce tools linked to Square</td><td>Fewer recent updates than some rivals; check current features and plans</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Repeating the Guavaberry Grove build in Google Sites:</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Go to sites.google.com and start a blank site; name it.</li>\n<li>Choose a <b>theme</b> and set the brand colour using the same hex code as your CSS (#7a1f3d).</li>\n<li>Add pages in the <b>Pages</b> panel (Home, Rooms, Gallery, Experience, Contact); the navigation menu is built automatically.</li>\n<li>Use <b>Insert</b> to add text boxes, images (add alt text in the image options), a YouTube video and a Google Map.</li>\n<li>Preview on phone, tablet and laptop sizes, then <b>Publish</b> and choose the web address.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Hand-coded HTML and CSS</th><th>Website builder</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Skill needed</td><td>Coding knowledge</td><td>Little or none</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Speed</td><td>Slower to build</td><td>Very fast with templates</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Control and originality</td><td>Full control of every element</td><td>Limited to the platform's options</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cost</td><td>Code free; hosting free to low</td><td>Free plans with limits; paid plans for custom domain and no ads</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Performance</td><td>Lean code loads fast</td><td>Extra platform code can slow pages</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ownership and portability</td><td>Files are yours; move to any host</td><td>Hard or impossible to export; \"locked in\"</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Security and maintenance</td><td>You handle hosting choices</td><td>Platform handles hosting, SSL and updates</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Accessibility</td><td>You control semantics and alt text</td><td>Depends on the template; check headings and contrast</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Evaluate\" means weigh both sides and reach a <b>judgement</b> for a specific context: \"For a small guest house with no technical staff, a builder is better at first; for an agency portfolio that must stand out and load fast, hand-coding is better.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>You can build a website by writing HTML and CSS yourself, or with a website builder such as Google Sites, Wix or Weebly. Builders are fast and easy, with hosting included, but give less control and are hard to move later. Hand-coding takes more skill and time, but gives full control, fast pages and files you own.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The key evaluation idea is <b>lock-in</b>. A builder's convenience comes from its platform, which also means the site often cannot be exported, and prices or features can change. Hand-coded files are portable, but the owner depends on someone with coding skills for every change unless a CMS is added. Neither option is better in general; the right choice depends on the owner's skills, budget, how often content changes, and how distinctive and fast the site must be. Accessibility is not guaranteed by either: builder templates vary, and hand-coders must apply semantics themselves. A Grade One judgement names the context and explains why one option fits it.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Builder or hand-coded?","intro":"<p>After building Guavaberry Grove both ways, Maya lists what she noticed. Sort each observation by the approach it describes.</p>","main":"Is this an advantage of hand-coding, or an advantage of a website builder?","bins":["Advantage of hand-coded HTML and CSS","Advantage of a website builder"],"items":[{"t":"Building it proves coding skill for a portfolio or the SBA.","bin":0,"fb":"Hand-coding demonstrates technical skill that examiners and employers value."},{"t":"Pages are lean and load quickly on mobile data.","bin":0,"fb":"Hand-written code avoids extra platform scripts."},{"t":"The site files can be moved to any host at any time.","bin":0,"fb":"Hand-coded files are portable; builders often lock you in."},{"t":"A map, a video and a contact form were added with a few clicks.","bin":1,"fb":"Builders have ready-made widgets for common features."},{"t":"A professional-looking template was ready in under an hour.","bin":1,"fb":"Templates make builders very fast."},{"t":"Hosting, SSL and security updates are handled by the platform.","bin":1,"fb":"Hosted builders manage the infrastructure for you."},{"t":"Every element can be designed exactly as the mock-up shows.","bin":0,"fb":"Hand-coding gives full creative control, without template limits."},{"t":"The owner could add a new page herself in ten minutes with no training in code.","bin":1,"fb":"Drag-and-drop editors let non-coders update content."}],"win":"Well sorted. Hand-coding offers control, portability, speed and skill evidence; builders offer ease, speed of setup and managed hosting.","tutor":[{"h":"Control or convenience?","p":"<p>Hand-coding usually wins on control; builders usually win on convenience.</p>"},{"h":"Who maintains it?","p":"<p>Think about whether a non-coder owner could look after the site.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Builder or hand-coded? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Full control of the design","bin":0,"fb":"Hand-coding."},{"t":"No coding needed","bin":1,"fb":"Builder."},{"t":"Files can move to any host","bin":0,"fb":"Hand-coding."},{"t":"Hosting and SSL included","bin":1,"fb":"Builder."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","main":"Which approach better suits this client?","intro":"<p>Now judge by context: which approach BETTER suits each client?</p>","items":[{"t":"A design agency portfolio that must load fast and look unlike any template.","bin":0,"fb":"Full control and lean code make hand-coding the better fit."},{"t":"A church group in Grenada with no technical volunteers that needs a simple site this week.","bin":1,"fb":"A builder is fast and needs no coding."},{"t":"A client who wants to own every file and be free to move hosts at any time.","bin":0,"fb":"Hand-coded files are fully portable."},{"t":"A school club that wants to embed Google Forms, Slides and a calendar quickly.","bin":1,"fb":"Google Sites integrates these with a few clicks."},{"t":"A student who wants to show employers that she can write clean, semantic code.","bin":0,"fb":"Hand-coding is evidence of technical skill."},{"t":"A pop-up Carnival food stall needing an online menu for two weeks only.","bin":1,"fb":"For a short-lived, simple site, a builder's speed wins."},{"t":"A heritage site that must control every heading and landmark for screen-reader users.","bin":0,"fb":"Hand-coding gives full control over semantics and accessibility."},{"t":"A boutique owner who wants to update stock and photos herself every day.","bin":1,"fb":"A builder's editor lets a non-coder update content daily."}],"win":"Strong evaluation. Neither approach wins everywhere: the best choice depends on skills, time, control, ownership and how often content changes.","title":"Builder or hand-coded? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Advise on infrastructure and security","genre":"Paper 02 structured response: website infrastructure, data handling and security","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Bayside Lolo Grill in Grand Case wants to take online table reservations and send a monthly email newsletter. The owner, Mr. Hodge, knows nothing about websites. His nephew has suggested a free website builder, while Island Pulse Media has offered to hand-code a site and host it. Mr. Hodge is worried about \"getting hacked\" and about customer data.</p>","prompt":"<p>(a) Explain the difference between a domain name and web hosting, and recommend a suitable domain for the grill. <b>(3 marks)</b></p>\n<p>(b) Explain THREE measures Mr. Hodge should take to protect the reservation form and the customer data it collects. <b>(6 marks)</b></p>\n<p>(c) Evaluate whether a website builder or a hand-coded site is more suitable for the grill, and give a justified recommendation. <b>(6 marks)</b></p>","min":180,"max":420,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> A domain name is ... whereas web hosting is ... I recommend ... because ...<br><b>(b)</b> First, ... This protects ... Second, ... Third, ...<br><b>(c)</b> A website builder ... However, ... A hand-coded site ... On the other hand, ... Overall, I recommend ... because ...</p>","checklist":["I distinguished domain and hosting on the same feature, using \"whereas\".","I recommended a specific domain with a reason.","I explained three security or privacy measures, each with what it protects against.","I gave advantages and disadvantages of both a builder and a hand-coded site.","I reached a clear, justified recommendation for this grill."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Infrastructure knowledge","marks":3,"look":["address|name","host|server","store|files|live","\\.sx|\\.mf|\\.com|cctld","dns|ip"],"min":3,"strong":"You clearly separated the address (domain) from the place the files are stored (hosting).","weak":"Explain that a domain name is the address people type, whereas hosting is the server space that stores and delivers the files. Recommend a domain such as a .com or a local ccTLD and say why."},{"criterion":"Security and data handling","marks":6,"look":["https|ssl|tls|encrypt","password|passphrase","2fa|two.factor|authenticat","backup","update|plugin","consent|privacy polic","minim|only (the )?(data|information|details) (needed|necessary)|necessary","permission|least privilege|access"],"min":4,"strong":"You explained protections in depth and linked each to the threat it prevents.","weak":"Explain at least three measures, each with its purpose: HTTPS to encrypt form data, strong passwords and 2FA for the admin login, updates and backups, collecting only necessary data with clear consent and a privacy policy."},{"criterion":"Evaluation and recommendation","marks":6,"look":["builder|wix|google sites|weebly","hand.?cod|html","however|on the other hand|whereas|but","cost|free|price","control|custom|portab|lock","recommend|overall|therefore|best"],"min":4,"strong":"Your evaluation weighs both sides and ends with a judgement that fits the grill.","weak":"Give at least one advantage and one disadvantage of each approach (cost, ease of use, control, ownership, security, maintenance), then make a clear recommendation linked to Mr. Hodge's needs and skills."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> A domain name is the human-friendly address customers type to find the site, such as bayside-lolo.com, whereas web hosting is the rented space on a server that stores the site's files and sends them to visitors. DNS links the two by pointing the domain to the host's IP address. I recommend registering a .com, because many diners are visitors from the USA and Europe who expect it, in Mr. Hodge's own name with auto-renew turned on, and also registering the local name if available so a competitor cannot take it.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> First, the site must use <b>HTTPS</b> with a valid TLS (SSL) certificate. This encrypts names, phone numbers and emails as they travel from a customer's phone, so they cannot be read on public Wi-Fi in Grand Case, and it prevents the \"Not secure\" warning that would put customers off.</p>\n<p>Second, Mr. Hodge should protect every admin account with a <b>long, unique passphrase</b> stored in a password manager and turn on <b>two-factor authentication</b>. Even if a phishing email tricked him into revealing his password, an attacker would still need the code from his phone. Staff who only check reservations should get limited accounts, following the principle of least privilege.</p>\n<p>Third, the form should collect <b>only the data needed</b> (name, phone, email, date, party size), use an unticked box for newsletter <b>consent</b> and link to a short privacy policy explaining how the data is stored and when it is deleted. The site and its data should be <b>backed up</b> automatically to an off-site location, and any plugins kept <b>updated</b>, so that a hacked or storm-damaged site can be restored quickly.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> A website builder such as Wix would let Mr. Hodge's nephew create the site quickly from a template, with hosting, SSL and security updates handled by the platform and ready-made reservation and newsletter apps. However, the free plan shows platform branding and a subdomain, so a paid plan would be needed to look professional, and the site cannot easily be moved elsewhere later.</p>\n<p>A hand-coded site from Island Pulse Media would be fast, fully customised to the grill's brand and portable to any host. On the other hand, HTML and CSS alone cannot store reservations, so it would need a form service or back end, and Mr. Hodge would depend on the agency, and pay them, for every update.</p>\n<p>Overall, I recommend a <b>website builder on a paid plan with a custom domain</b>. Mr. Hodge has no technical skills and wants to avoid security problems, so a platform that manages hosting, SSL and updates, and lets him change the menu himself, best fits his needs and budget. If the business grows, he could later commission a custom site.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Part (b) marks usually come in pairs: one mark for naming a measure and one for explaining what it protects. \"Use HTTPS\" earns one; \"use HTTPS so that form data is encrypted in transit and cannot be read on public Wi-Fi\" earns both. In (c), an evaluation without a final judgement cannot reach full marks.</p>",
 "easy": {"bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: infrastructure and security basics (9 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part. Use the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>State</b> what a domain name is and what web hosting is. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Suggest</b> a domain name for the grill. <i>(1 mark)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> TWO ways to keep the reservation form and its data safe, and say what each one protects. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE advantage of a website builder for Mr. Hodge. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":70,"max":200,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> A domain name is ... Web hosting is ...<br><b>(b)</b> A good domain would be ...<br><b>(c)</b> First, ... This protects ... Second, ... This protects ...<br><b>(d)</b> One advantage of a website builder is ... because ...</p>","checklist":["I said a domain is the address and hosting is where the files are stored.","I suggested a domain such as a .com or a local ending.","I gave two safety measures.","I said what each measure protects.","I gave one builder advantage with a reason."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Domain and hosting","marks":2,"look":["address|name","store|server|files|live"],"min":2,"strong":"Both explained.","weak":"A domain is the address people type; hosting is the server space where the files are stored."},{"criterion":"Domain suggestion","marks":1,"look":["\\.com|\\.sx|\\.fr|\\.net"],"min":1,"strong":"Suitable domain.","weak":"Suggest a domain such as baysidelolo.com."},{"criterion":"Two safety measures","marks":4,"look":["https|ssl|encrypt|password|2fa|two.factor|backup|update|consent|privacy","protect|so that|stops|keeps|prevent"],"min":2,"strong":"Two measures with what they protect.","weak":"Name a measure such as HTTPS and say what it protects, e.g. it encrypts customer details as they are sent."},{"criterion":"Builder advantage","marks":2,"look":["easy|no cod|template|quick|fast|hosting|drag","because|so|which"],"min":2,"strong":"Advantage with a reason.","weak":"Give an advantage, such as no coding needed, and explain why it suits Mr. Hodge."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> A domain name is the address people type to find a website. Web hosting is the space on a server where the website's files are stored.</p><p><b>(b)</b> A good domain would be baysidelolo.com.</p><p><b>(c)</b> First, use HTTPS. This protects customers' names and phone numbers because it encrypts them as they are sent. Second, use a long password and two-factor authentication on the admin account. This protects the site from hackers who guess or steal the password.</p><p><b>(d)</b> One advantage of a website builder is that it is easy to use, because Mr. Hodge can change the menu himself with no coding.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>For part (c), each measure needs two things: what you do and what it protects. Write \"This protects ...\" after every measure so you collect both marks.</p>","title":"Advise on infrastructure and security (Foundation)"},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Paper 02 structured response: infrastructure, data protection and security, Grade One extension (20 marks)","prompt":"<p>Mr. Hodge now also wants a WordPress blog for weekly specials, and many of his customers are visitors from the USA and the Netherlands. Write a response that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Explains</b> how a visitor's browser finds the grill's website, using the terms domain, DNS, IP address and hosting, and <b>recommends</b> a domain strategy. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Recommends</b> a type of hosting for the new site and <b>justifies</b> it against TWO alternatives. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Discusses</b> THREE threats to the site and its customer data, giving the impact and a specific defence for each. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluates</b> the data-handling design of the reservation form against privacy principles. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluates</b> a website builder against a hand-coded site with a CMS, and gives a justified recommendation. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":420,"max":750,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> When a visitor types ..., the browser asks DNS ... which returns the IP address of ... I recommend ... because ...<br><b>(b)</b> I recommend ... rather than ... because ... A ... would ... but ...<br><b>(c)</b> The first threat is ... Its impact would be ... The defence is ... The second ... The third ...<br><b>(d)</b> The form should ... It meets / fails the principle of ... because ...<br><b>(e)</b> A builder ... whereas a hand-coded site with a CMS ... On balance, I recommend ...</p>","checklist":["I explained the path from domain through DNS to the host's IP address.","I recommended a domain strategy, such as .com plus a local ending, owned by the business.","I justified one hosting type against two alternatives.","Each of my three threats has an impact and a specific defence.","I evaluated the form using minimisation, consent, HTTPS and retention.","My final comparison ends with a justified recommendation."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"DNS chain and domain strategy","marks":4,"look":["dns","ip address","host|server","\\.com|\\.sx|\\.fr","renew|own|business's name|his name"],"min":4,"strong":"An accurate chain and a justified domain strategy.","weak":"Explain that DNS turns the domain into the host's IP address, then recommend domains owned by the business with auto-renew."},{"criterion":"Hosting justified against alternatives","marks":4,"look":["shared","vps|cloud|dedicated|static","wordpress|database|php|cms","because|whereas|rather than"],"min":4,"strong":"A clear recommendation weighed against two alternatives.","weak":"Recommend a host that supports WordPress and compare it with at least two other types on cost and features."},{"criterion":"Three threats with impact and defence","marks":6,"look":["phishing","plugin|outdated|update","password|brute|credential","malware|ransomware|injection|ddos","impact|would|lose|loss|damage","2fa|backup|https|training|validation"],"min":5,"strong":"Three developed threats, each with impact and defence.","weak":"For each threat, explain how it works, its impact on the grill and a specific defence."},{"criterion":"Form evaluated against privacy principles","marks":3,"look":["minimi|only the|necessary","consent|unticked|tick","https|encrypt","privacy policy|retention|delete"],"min":3,"strong":"The form is judged against several principles.","weak":"Judge the form on data minimisation, active consent, HTTPS and retention."},{"criterion":"Builder versus hand-coded with CMS","marks":3,"look":["builder|wix|squarespace|google sites","hand.?cod|wordpress|cms","recommend|on balance|overall|therefore"],"min":3,"strong":"A balanced comparison with a justified recommendation.","weak":"Compare the two on skills, cost, control and maintenance, then recommend one for Mr. Hodge."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> When a visitor types baysidelolo.com, the browser asks a DNS resolver for the domain's records. DNS works like a phone book: it returns the A record, the IP address of the server where the site is hosted. The browser then connects to that IP address over HTTPS and the host sends back the site's files. I recommend registering baysidelolo.com, because visitors from the USA and the Netherlands expect .com, and, because Grand Case is on the French side, the matching .fr name if the registration rules allow it, both in Mr. Hodge's business name with auto-renew and 2FA on the registrar account, so the domain can never lapse or be taken by a contractor.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> I recommend reputable shared hosting with WordPress support, free SSL and automatic backups. A free static host such as Netlify would be cheaper, but it cannot run WordPress or store reservations without extra services, so it does not meet the brief. A VPS would give more power and control, whereas it costs more and needs a technician to manage updates and security, which the grill does not have. Shared hosting is affordable and managed by the host, so it fits a small restaurant's traffic and budget.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> The first threat is <b>phishing</b>: a fake email persuades Mr. Hodge to enter his admin password on a lookalike page. The impact would be an attacker taking control of the site and customer data. The defence is training to check links and senders, plus 2FA with an authenticator app, so a stolen password alone is useless. The second threat is <b>outdated plugins</b>, which automated tools scan for known holes; a hacked plugin could redirect diners to a scam site and make Google flag the site as dangerous, losing bookings. The defence is to update WordPress and plugins promptly and remove unused ones. The third threat is <b>ransomware or malware</b> that locks or corrupts the site files, causing lost income while the site is down. The defence is automated, versioned backups with one copy off-site, tested regularly so the site can be restored quickly.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> The reservation form meets data minimisation if it asks only for name, phone, email, date and party size, and it meets the consent principle only if newsletter sign-up is a separate, unticked box. It must post over HTTPS so data is encrypted in transit, and the privacy policy should state a retention period, for example deleting reservation details after six months. If the form also asked for a date of birth with no stated purpose, it would fail minimisation.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> A website builder is quick to set up and handles hosting and security updates, but it gives less control and can lock the grill into one platform. A hand-coded site with WordPress gives a distinctive, portable design and an easy blog dashboard, whereas it requires the owner to keep plugins updated. On balance, I recommend WordPress on managed shared hosting with automatic updates turned on, because Mr. Hodge needs a weekly blog and wants to own his site, and managed updates reduce the security worry he raised.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>\"Discuss\" and \"evaluate\" need depth, not lists. For each threat, write three linked sentences: how it works, what it would do to this business, and the specific defence. Finish every evaluation with a judgement tied to the client's skills, budget and worries.</p>","title":"Advise on infrastructure and security (Challenge)"}}]},{"id":"M2U8","title":"Forms of digital publishing","objectives":["Describe the main forms of digital publishing: websites, blogs, social networking platforms, games, apps, podcasts, videos, e-books, newsletters and digital magazines.","Classify forms of digital publishing by purpose, audience, platform, content type and level of interactivity.","Define websites, blogs, social networking, games, apps, podcasts, videos and e-books, and evaluate them using accuracy, authority, usability, accessibility, satisfaction, purpose, and risk and security concerns, plus audience engagement and privacy.","Compare the Caribbean content available in each form of digital publishing.","Choose and justify the right form of digital publishing for a given audience and purpose."],"videos":[{"title":"Types of digital publishing","q":"types of digital publishing explained blogs podcasts ebooks websites"},{"title":"How to evaluate online sources","q":"how to evaluate websites accuracy authority currency CRAAP test"},{"title":"Choosing the right content format","q":"choosing the right content format for your audience content marketing"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Forms of digital publishing (1): websites, blogs, social, games and apps","html":"<p>Start with this definition. Learn it word for word:</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>What is web/online publishing?</b> Web publishing is the process of publishing original content on the Internet. The process includes building and uploading websites, updating the associated webpages, and posting content to these webpages online. Web publishing comprises personal, business and community websites in addition to e-books and blogs.</p>\n<p>More broadly, <b>digital publishing</b> is making content available to an audience in electronic form, over the Internet or on a device. There are eight forms: <b>websites, blogs, social networking, games, apps, podcasts, videos and e-books</b>. Two further forms are <b>newsletters</b> and <b>digital magazines</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Form</th><th>Definition</th><th>Caribbean example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Website</b></td><td>A collection of linked web pages under one domain name, built, uploaded and updated by its owner (personal, business or community). Can be static or dynamic.</td><td>A national newspaper's news site; a Negril villa rental site with a booking form.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Blog</b></td><td>A website, or part of one, made of regularly updated dated posts shown newest first, written in a personal or informal voice, usually open to reader comments.</td><td>A Grenada food blogger writing weekly posts on oil down and nutmeg recipes.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Social networking</b></td><td>Online platforms where users create profiles, connect with others and share posts, photos, stories and short video (Instagram, Facebook, TikTok, X, LinkedIn).</td><td>A Carnival troupe's Instagram showing costume reveals for Trinidad Carnival.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Game</b></td><td>Interactive entertainment with rules and goals, published digitally for browsers, consoles or phones, from casual puzzles to MMOGs (Unit 2.1).</td><td>A browser game in which players steer a sloop between St. Vincent and Bequia.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>App</b></td><td>A software application published through an app store (Google Play, App Store) and installed on a phone or tablet to carry out a task.</td><td>A bank's mobile app; a ride-hailing or food-delivery app in Trinidad and Tobago.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>The forms overlap. A national newspaper may publish a story on its <b>website</b>, share it on <b>social networks</b> and send it in a <b>newsletter</b>. This is <b>cross-platform publishing</b>: one piece of content, reshaped for several forms. At the Island Pulse Media, Maya builds client <b>websites</b>, Keisha writes the agency <b>blog</b> and runs its <b>social</b> accounts, Malik designs small <b>games</b>, and Jerome publishes <b>apps</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Static and dynamic.</b> A static website shows the same fixed pages to everyone (Unit 2.6). A dynamic website builds pages from a database, so news or prices change without rewriting the HTML. Most blogs are dynamic because they run on a CMS such as WordPress (Unit 2.9).</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 01 often tests the difference between a <b>website</b> and a <b>blog</b>. A blog is a <i>type</i> of website (or part of one) with dated posts in reverse chronological order, a personal voice and reader comments. \"All blogs are websites, but not all websites are blogs.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Web publishing means putting original content on the Internet by building, uploading and updating websites. There are eight main forms of digital publishing: websites, blogs, social networking, games, apps, podcasts, videos and e-books. A blog is a kind of website with dated posts, newest first. A Grenada food blogger posting weekly oil down recipes is publishing digitally.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The forms are best understood by their <b>defining features</b> rather than by famous examples, because platforms change but the features stay. A blog is defined by dated posts in reverse order and a personal voice, not by using WordPress; an app is defined by being installed from an app store. The forms also overlap through cross-platform publishing: one news story becomes a web article, a social post and a newsletter item, each shaped for its audience. Static versus dynamic is a separate question about how pages are produced, not a form in itself. In exam answers, give the definition, the distinguishing feature and a specific Caribbean example.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the form to the Caribbean example","intro":"<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media is building a slide for a client workshop. Match each form of digital publishing to the example that best shows it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Website","r":"A Bay Street jeweller's pages: Home, Collections, About, Contact, all under one domain"},{"l":"Blog","r":"Weekly dated posts by a Barbados surfer on the best breaks, with reader comments"},{"l":"Social networking platform","r":"A Negril beach bar shares Reels and Stories and replies to followers"},{"l":"Game","r":"A browser quiz-race where players collect guavaberries to unlock levels"},{"l":"App","r":"A Jamaican taxi service installed from Google Play with live driver tracking"},{"l":"Podcast","r":"A series of audio episodes on Caribbean history, released every Friday through an RSS feed"},{"l":"E-book","r":"A Dominica hiking guide sold through Kindle Direct Publishing as an EPUB file"}],"win":"Well matched. You recognised each form by its defining feature: pages under a domain, dated posts, social profiles and sharing, interactive play, installed software, episodic audio and book-length reflowable text.","tutor":[{"h":"Look for the defining feature","p":"<p>Dated posts with comments point to a blog. Installed from a store points to an app. Episodes plus RSS point to a podcast.</p>"},{"h":"Website vs social","p":"<p>Owning pages under your own domain is a website. Posting on someone else's network to followers is social networking.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the form (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Blog","r":"Dated posts written in a personal voice, newest first"},{"l":"App","r":"Installed on a phone from an app store"},{"l":"Podcast","r":"Audio episodes released in a series"},{"l":"E-book","r":"A book published as an EPUB file"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Ten forms now, including newsletters and digital magazines. Match each form to the Caribbean example that best shows its defining feature.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Website","r":"A Bridgetown law firm's linked pages, Services, Team and Contact, under one domain, with no dated posts"},{"l":"Blog","r":"A Barbados chef's weekly dated posts, newest first, with reader comments"},{"l":"Social networking","r":"A Carnival band's profile where followers share costume reveals and tag friends"},{"l":"Game","r":"A phone puzzle with levels and scores where players rebuild a Kalinago village"},{"l":"App","r":"A ferry-ticket tool installed from Google Play that works offline at the dock"},{"l":"Podcast","r":"Weekly episodes on Caribbean cricket that subscribers receive automatically through an RSS feed"},{"l":"Video","r":"A Trinidad comedy duo's 15-minute sketches uploaded to YouTube"},{"l":"E-book","r":"A Belize birdwatching guide sold as a reflowable EPUB on Kobo"},{"l":"Newsletter","r":"A Jamaican church's weekly email to members who signed up"},{"l":"Digital magazine","r":"A quarterly inflight edition with designed photo spreads and embedded video"}],"win":"Well matched. You identified each form by the feature that separates it from similar forms, such as dated posts, RSS delivery, opt-in email and issue-based design.","title":"Match the form to the Caribbean example (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Forms of digital publishing (2): podcasts, videos, e-books, newsletters and digital magazines","html":"<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Form</th><th>Definition</th><th>Caribbean example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Podcast</b></td><td>A series of digital audio (sometimes video) episodes, distributed through an <b>RSS feed</b> to apps such as Spotify and Apple Podcasts so listeners can subscribe and download or stream each new episode.</td><td>A St. Lucia podcast interviewing Jazz Festival musicians.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Video</b></td><td>Recorded or live moving-image content with sound, published online on YouTube, Vimeo, Facebook, TikTok or a website, in long-form or short-form (Reels, Shorts).</td><td>A Guyanese creator's YouTube cooking series on pepperpot.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>E-book</b></td><td>A book published in electronic form to be read on a screen or e-reader, usually as <b>EPUB</b> (reflowable text) or <b>PDF</b> (fixed layout), through Kindle Direct Publishing, Google Play Books, Apple Books or Kobo.</td><td>A Turks and Caicos teacher's illustrated children's e-book about the salt ponds.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Newsletter</b></td><td>A regular email (or platform post) sent to subscribers who opted in, through services such as Mailchimp, Substack or Beehiiv.</td><td>A weekly Kingstown events newsletter listing concerts, markets and ferry times.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Digital magazine</b></td><td>A magazine published electronically, often a designed, issue-based edition (flip-book or app edition) with images, video and links.</td><td>An inflight or tourism magazine with an online edition, such as Caribbean Beat (Caribbean Airlines' magazine).</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Newsletter or blog?</b> A blog waits for readers to visit (a <b>pull</b> channel). A newsletter lands in the subscriber's inbox (a <b>push</b> channel). Push channels usually get more reliable attention because they do not depend on a social media algorithm.</p>\n<p><b>Digital magazine or website?</b> A digital magazine is organised in <b>issues</b> with a designed layout, an editor and a regular schedule (monthly, quarterly). A news website is updated continuously, story by story.</p>\n<p><b>Podcast or audio file?</b> One MP3 on a website is just an audio file. It becomes a podcast when it is part of a <b>series</b> delivered by a feed that listeners can <b>subscribe</b> to.</p>\n<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media explains it to a client this way: \"The content is the story. The form is the container you put it in. The same Emancipation Day story could be a 3-minute video, a 20-minute podcast episode, a blog post with photos, or a chapter in an e-book.\"</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Learn one precise distinguishing feature for every form: RSS and subscription (podcast), reflowable EPUB (e-book), opt-in email (newsletter), issues and layout (digital magazine). Precise features earn the \"describe\" marks in Paper 02.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Podcasts are audio episodes that people subscribe to through an RSS feed. Videos are moving pictures with sound, long or short. E-books are books on screens, often as EPUB. Newsletters are regular emails sent to people who signed up. Digital magazines are designed issues, such as a tourism magazine's online edition. A weekly Kingstown events email is a newsletter.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The push-versus-pull distinction explains why newsletters have grown again: a blog or social post depends on readers visiting or on an algorithm showing it, while a newsletter arrives in an inbox the publisher <b>owns access to</b>. That ownership reduces platform dependency, though it brings duties around consent and unsubscribing. Similar care applies to the other boundaries: a single MP3 is not a podcast without a subscribable series, and a PDF archive of past issues is a digital magazine only if it keeps an issue-based, edited identity. Malik's point that \"the content is the story; the form is the container\" is the key to repurposing. Precise distinctions earn \"describe\" and \"distinguish\" marks.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Which form fits?","intro":"<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media is fielding calls from clients across the region. Choose the form of digital publishing each client is describing.</p>","items":[{"text":"A Curaçao dive shop wants a regular email that lands in customers' inboxes every Monday with dive conditions and offers.","options":["blog","podcast","newsletter"],"answer":2,"fb":"A regular opt-in email pushed to subscribers is a newsletter. A blog waits for readers to visit."},{"text":"A Trinidad author wants readers to change the font size on their phones and have the text reflow.","options":["EPUB e-book","website","PDF digital magazine"],"answer":0,"fb":"EPUB is reflowable, so text adjusts to screen and font size. PDF keeps a fixed layout."},{"text":"A Belize tourism body wants a quarterly, beautifully laid-out edition with photo spreads and embedded video.","options":["newsletter","digital magazine","social networking post"],"answer":1,"fb":"Issue-based, designed layout and a regular schedule are the marks of a digital magazine."},{"text":"A St. Kitts historian wants listeners to subscribe and receive each new 30-minute audio episode automatically.","options":["video","podcast","e-book"],"answer":1,"fb":"Episodes delivered through an RSS feed that listeners subscribe to make a podcast."},{"text":"A Speightstown bakery wants customers to order and pay from their phones, with push notifications when the bread is ready.","options":["app","digital magazine","blog"],"answer":0,"fb":"Ordering, payment and push notifications are app features. A blog or magazine cannot do this."},{"text":"A Roseau artist wants to share short clips, build followers and reply to comments every day.","options":["newsletter","e-book","social networking platform"],"answer":2,"fb":"Profiles, followers, sharing and comments define social networking platforms."}],"win":"Excellent. You chose each form by its defining feature: push email, reflowable text, issues and layout, RSS episodes, installed software and social sharing.","tutor":[{"h":"Push or pull?","p":"<p>Does the content arrive in the inbox, or does the reader go and find it?</p>"},{"h":"Function words","p":"<p>Order, pay and notifications point to software installed on the phone.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which form fits? (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A regular email sent to subscribers is a...","options":["blog","newsletter"],"answer":1,"fb":"Newsletters are pushed to subscribers' inboxes."},{"text":"Audio episodes you can subscribe to are a...","options":["e-book","podcast"],"answer":1,"fb":"A podcast is an episodic audio series delivered by a feed."},{"text":"Software installed from Google Play is an...","options":["website","app"],"answer":1,"fb":"Apps are installed from app stores."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder distinctions. Choose the BEST answer each time.</p>","items":[{"text":"A St. Kitts radio station puts one interview MP3 on its website. What would make it a podcast?","options":["Uploading it to Instagram","Adding a photo","Publishing it as part of a series delivered by an RSS feed that listeners can subscribe to","Making the file longer"],"answer":2,"fb":"A podcast is defined by an episodic series and subscription through a feed."},{"text":"A guesthouse \"News\" page was last changed in 2019 and has no dated posts or comments. Which form is it?","options":["A newsletter","A blog","A digital magazine","Part of a website"],"answer":3,"fb":"Without regular dated posts, a personal voice or comments, it is simply a website page, not a blog."},{"text":"A Port of Spain news outlet publishes one story as a web article, an Instagram carousel and a newsletter item. This is...","options":["cross-platform publishing","a digital magazine","web hosting","a game"],"answer":0,"fb":"Reshaping one piece of content for several forms is cross-platform publishing."},{"text":"Which feature MOST clearly separates a digital magazine from a news website?","options":["It is published in designed, issue-based editions on a schedule","It has a domain name","It contains text","It uses photographs"],"answer":0,"fb":"News sites update story by story; digital magazines publish edited issues with a designed layout."},{"text":"Why might a Dominica tour company choose a newsletter rather than relying only on Facebook posts?","options":["Facebook cannot show photos","Newsletters are always free to send","Newsletters never need consent","A newsletter is pushed to an inbox the company has opt-in access to, so it does not depend on an algorithm"],"answer":3,"fb":"Push delivery to an owned subscriber list reduces dependence on a platform's algorithm."},{"text":"A bank's tool for checking balances is available both as an installed app and as a mobile website. What separates the app?","options":["It shows text","It uses the Internet","It has a logo","It is installed from an app store onto the device"],"answer":3,"fb":"Installation from an app store is the defining feature of an app."}],"win":"Excellent. You separated forms by their defining features and explained why a publisher might choose one over another.","title":"Which form fits? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Classifying forms of digital publishing","html":"<p>You must be able to <b>classify</b> (group or categorise) forms of digital publishing using five bases. A classification is only useful if you state the <b>basis</b> you are grouping by.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Basis</th><th>Question to ask</th><th>Example groups</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Purpose</b></td><td>Why was it published?</td><td>Inform (news sites, electricity outage alerts), educate (e-books, tutorial videos), entertain (games, comedy Reels), persuade or sell (brand Instagram, e-commerce sites), build community (forums, groups).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Audience</b></td><td>Who is it for?</td><td>General public, a niche (Caribbean diaspora in New York), children, professionals, local residents, tourists.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Platform</b></td><td>Where and on what does it run?</td><td>Web browser, mobile app store, social platform, email inbox, e-reader, podcast app, console.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Content type</b></td><td>What media does it use?</td><td>Text-based (blogs, e-books, newsletters), audio (podcasts), visual (photo posts, digital magazines), video, interactive or multimedia (games, apps).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Level of interactivity</b></td><td>How much can the user do?</td><td><b>Low</b>: read or watch only (PDF e-book). <b>Medium</b>: comment, like, share, click links (blogs, social posts). <b>High</b>: user choices change what happens (games, apps, quizzes).</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p>One form can sit in different groups depending on the basis. A YouTube video is <i>video</i> by content type, <i>social platform</i> by platform, and may be <i>educate</i> or <i>entertain</i> by purpose. That is why you must always name the basis.</p>\n<p><b>A worked example.</b> Jerome at the Island Pulse Media is planning a hurricane-preparedness campaign before the season starts, remembering Hurricane Irma. He classifies his options:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>By <b>purpose</b>: all of them inform and educate.</li>\n<li>By <b>audience</b>: the app and newsletter target residents; the video targets residents and visitors.</li>\n<li>By <b>content type</b>: checklist e-book (text), shelter-map app (interactive), how-to video (video).</li>\n<li>By <b>interactivity</b>: the e-book is low, the video is medium (comments and shares), the app is high (the user enters a location and sees the nearest shelter).</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> If a question says \"classify\" or \"group\", set your answer out as a small table or a list headed by the basis: \"By level of interactivity: low ..., medium ..., high ...\". Examiners award marks for the correct basis <b>and</b> the correct placement.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>To classify forms of digital publishing means to group them. There are five bases: purpose (why it was made), audience (who it is for), platform (where it runs), content type (text, audio, video or interactive) and level of interactivity (low, medium or high). Always say which basis you are using. A hurricane app is high interactivity; a PDF checklist is low.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Classification is only meaningful with a stated basis, because the same item lands in different groups on different bases: a YouTube tutorial is video by content type, a social platform by platform, and educational by purpose. Classifying is also a planning tool. Grouping a campaign's options by interactivity, for example, shows whether the plan offers any way for the audience to respond, which may matter more than the number of channels. A subtle point is that purpose can be <b>mixed or hidden</b>: an \"informative\" post that is actually sponsored belongs in the persuade group too. Set classification answers out in a small table headed by each basis.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Which basis of classification?","intro":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is grouping a client's publications in different ways. For each grouping, decide which basis she is using.</p>","main":"Which basis of classification is being used?","bins":["Purpose","Audience","Content type","Level of interactivity"],"items":[{"t":"Making a separate children's version of a history e-book for primary schools.","bin":1,"fb":"Children are a target audience, so this is classification by audience."},{"t":"Placing blog posts with comments in the \"medium\" group, between e-books and apps.","bin":3,"fb":"Comments and shares give medium interactivity, which is the interactivity basis."},{"t":"Placing a PDF brochure in \"read only\" and a quiz game in \"user choices change the outcome\".","bin":3,"fb":"Grouping by how much the user can do is classification by level of interactivity."},{"t":"Listing a blog, an e-book and a newsletter together as text-based publications.","bin":2,"fb":"Text-based is a content type."},{"t":"Grouping a podcast and an audio walking tour together because both are audio.","bin":2,"fb":"Grouping by the media used (audio) is classification by content type."},{"t":"Separating content for cruise visitors at the Bridgetown cruise terminal from content for local residents.","bin":1,"fb":"Grouping by who the content is for is classification by audience."},{"t":"Grouping a brand Instagram account and an online store because both aim to sell.","bin":0,"fb":"Persuading and selling is a purpose."},{"t":"Putting an electricity outage alert and a national news site together because both inform people.","bin":0,"fb":"Grouping by why something was published (to inform) is classification by purpose."}],"win":"Well sorted. You identified the basis behind each grouping: why (purpose), who (audience), what media (content type) and how much the user can do (interactivity).","tutor":[{"h":"Four questions","p":"<p>Why was it made? Who is it for? What media does it use? What can the user do?</p>"},{"h":"Do not be fooled by examples","p":"<p>The same publication can appear in any bin. Focus on the reason given for the grouping.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which basis? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Grouping things that all aim to inform.","bin":0,"fb":"Inform is a purpose."},{"t":"Separating content for tourists from content for residents.","bin":1,"fb":"Tourists and residents are audiences."},{"t":"Grouping all audio publications together.","bin":2,"fb":"Audio is a content type."},{"t":"Grouping by how much the user can do.","bin":3,"fb":"That is the level of interactivity."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Five bases now, including platform. Decide which basis each grouping uses.</p>","bins":["Purpose","Audience","Platform","Content type","Level of interactivity"],"items":[{"t":"Separating content for cruise visitors from content for residents.","bin":1,"fb":"Who it is for is audience."},{"t":"Placing a PDF in \"read only\" and a quiz game in \"user choices change the outcome\".","bin":4,"fb":"How much the user can do is interactivity."},{"t":"Grouping an electricity outage alert and a news site together because both inform people.","bin":0,"fb":"Why it was published (to inform) is purpose."},{"t":"Listing everything that arrives in an email inbox in one group.","bin":2,"fb":"The email inbox is a platform."},{"t":"Grouping a podcast and an audio walking tour because both are audio.","bin":3,"fb":"The media used is content type."},{"t":"Making a version of a heritage e-book for primary-school children.","bin":1,"fb":"Children are a target audience."},{"t":"Putting the Kindle edition and the Kobo edition together as e-reader content.","bin":2,"fb":"Where and on what it runs is platform."},{"t":"Listing a blog, an e-book and a newsletter together as text-based.","bin":3,"fb":"Text-based is a content type."},{"t":"Grouping a brand Instagram account with an online store because both aim to sell.","bin":0,"fb":"Selling is a purpose."},{"t":"Ranking a blog with comments between a PDF and an app.","bin":4,"fb":"Medium interactivity sits between read-only and fully interactive."}],"win":"Well sorted. You identified the basis behind each grouping: why, who, where, what media and how much the user can do.","title":"Which basis of classification? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Evaluation criteria (1): accuracy, authority, usability and accessibility","html":"<p>To <b>evaluate</b> a form of digital publishing is to judge its quality and value against stated criteria, weighing strengths against weaknesses and reaching a conclusion. Task 2 uses seven criteria, in this order: <b>accuracy, authority, usability, accessibility, satisfaction, purpose, and risk and security concerns</b>. This screen covers the first four; the next covers the rest. You will also meet two extras: <b>audience engagement</b> and <b>privacy</b>.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Key questions</th><th>Warning signs</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>1. Accuracy</b></td><td>Are the facts correct, current and supported by evidence or sources? Is it dated?</td><td>No sources, no date, spelling errors, claims that cannot be checked, edited photos presented as real.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>2. Authority</b></td><td>Who published it? What are their qualifications or reputation? Is there an About page or contact details?</td><td>Anonymous author, no About page, a domain pretending to be an official body.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>3. Usability</b></td><td>Is it easy to navigate, fast to load, clear on a phone? Can users complete tasks quickly?</td><td>Broken links, cluttered pages, slow loading on mobile data, confusing menus.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>4. Accessibility</b></td><td>Can people with disabilities and limited connections use it? Are there captions, transcripts, alt text, good contrast, keyboard navigation (WCAG)?</td><td>Videos with no captions, images without alt text, low-contrast text, content locked behind large downloads.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Applying them in the Caribbean.</b> After Hurricane Irma in 2017, many unverified messages circulated on WhatsApp and Facebook in Sint Maarten. Official updates from government and emergency bodies scored high on <b>authority</b>, while forwarded voice notes from unknown senders scored low on authority and often on <b>accuracy</b>. A good evaluator checks <i>who</i> is speaking before sharing.</p>\n<p><b>Usability and accessibility are different.</b> Usability is how easy something is for users in general. Accessibility is whether people with disabilities, older devices or slow data can use it at all. A podcast may be highly usable for most people but inaccessible to a deaf user unless it has a transcript.</p>\n<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media uses a quick check on any client's site: open it on a mid-range phone on mobile data, try to find the contact number in under 10 seconds, and run it through the WAVE accessibility checker from WebAIM.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In an \"evaluate\" answer, never just define a criterion. Apply it: \"The blog scores low on <b>authority</b> because the author is anonymous and gives no qualifications, so readers cannot judge whether the medical advice is trustworthy.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>To evaluate a form of publishing means to judge how good it is using criteria. The first four are accuracy (are the facts right?), authority (who made it?), usability (is it easy to use?) and accessibility (can people with disabilities or slow data use it?). After Hurricane Irma, official updates scored higher on authority than forwarded voice notes from unknown senders.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Accuracy and authority are linked but not identical. A trusted source can still publish an outdated figure, and an unknown blogger can be right, so check both. Authority must also be verified, not assumed: a domain built to look like a government site borrows authority it does not have. Usability and accessibility differ in <b>who</b> they consider: usability asks whether typical users can complete a task, while accessibility asks whether anyone is excluded, including deaf users, screen-reader users and people on old phones. Strong evaluation names the criterion, gives specific evidence, explains the consequence for the audience, and avoids simply defining the term.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Spot the warning signs","intro":"<p>A client forwards Keisha this post from an unknown blog and asks whether they should share it on their business page.</p>","q":"Which lines show problems with ACCURACY or AUTHORITY? Select all that apply.","lines":["Posted by \"IslandTruth247\". No author name, no About page.","BREAKING: soursop tea cures diabetes, doctors are hiding it!","No date is shown on the post.","The post links to a national tourism board website for ferry times.","The web address imitates a real health ministry but is spelt slightly differently.","The page has clear headings and loads quickly on a phone.","No study, doctor or source is named to support the claim.","Images have alt text describing each bottle."],"correct":[0,1,2,4,6],"win":"Sharp eye. An anonymous author and a fake official-looking address (authority), plus a sensational claim with no date or sources (accuracy), mean the post should not be shared.","miss":"Look again. Ask: who wrote it, is the address genuine, can the claim be checked, and is it dated? Good layout and alt text are usability and accessibility strengths, not problems.","tutor":[{"h":"Two criteria only","p":"<p>This task is about accuracy and authority. Usability and accessibility features are a different matter.</p>"},{"h":"Who is really speaking?","p":"<p>Check the author, the About page and the exact spelling of the web address.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the warning signs (Foundation)","q":"Which lines are warning signs? Select all that apply.","lines":["No author name or About page.","A miracle cure claim with no sources.","The page loads quickly.","No date is shown on the post."],"correct":[0,1,3],"win":"Yes. Anonymous, unsupported and undated: weak authority and weak accuracy.","miss":"Fast loading is good. Look for missing authors, missing sources and missing dates."},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A client forwards a health article that looks professional. Evaluate it with Keisha.</p>","q":"Which lines show problems with ACCURACY, AUTHORITY or PURPOSE? Select all that apply.","lines":["The author is named as a nurse at a Castries clinic, with a link to the clinic's staff page.","The post was last updated in 2015 but describes \"current\" vaccination schedules.","Figures are introduced with \"studies show\", but no study is named.","The page sells the herbal tea it recommends, but never says so.","The article links to the World Health Organization fact sheet it quotes.","A \"miracle recovery\" photo turns up on a 2012 stock-photo site in a reverse image search.","The web address is gov-sx-health.com, designed to look like a government site.","Adverts on the page are clearly labelled \"Advertisement\".","The page loads slowly on mobile data."],"correct":[1,2,3,5,6],"win":"Sharp evaluation. Outdated \"current\" advice, unnamed studies and a misused stock photo damage accuracy; a hidden sales motive breaks purpose; and a lookalike government address fakes authority. The named nurse, the WHO link and labelled adverts are strengths.","miss":"Look again. Separate strengths (named author, linked source, labelled adverts) from problems. Slow loading is a usability issue, not accuracy, authority or purpose.","title":"Spot the warning signs (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Evaluation criteria (2): satisfaction, purpose, risk and security (plus extras)","html":"<p>These are criteria 5 to 7, followed by two <b>extras</b>. Use the seven first; add the extras to strengthen an answer.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Key questions</th><th>Evidence you could use</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>5. Satisfaction</b></td><td>Do users feel their need was met? Would they return or recommend it?</td><td>Ratings and reviews (app store stars), surveys, repeat visits, complaints.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>6. Purpose</b></td><td>Why does it exist: to inform, educate, sell, persuade, entertain? Is the purpose open or hidden? Does it achieve it?</td><td>Advertising disguised as news, undisclosed sponsorship, one-sided arguments (warning signs); a clear About page and labelled adverts (strengths).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>7. Risk and security concerns</b></td><td>What could go wrong for users or the publisher? Are users and their data protected from attack?</td><td>Risks: misinformation, cyberbullying, addictive design, copyright or defamation. Security: HTTPS padlock, secure payments, two-factor login, regular updates, apps from official stores only.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Extra: audience engagement</b></td><td>How actively do people interact with it?</td><td>Likes, comments, shares, saves, watch time, newsletter open and click rates.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Extra: privacy</b></td><td>What personal data is collected, and is it explained?</td><td>Privacy policy, cookie consent, app permissions (does a flashlight app need your contacts?), location tracking.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Satisfaction and engagement are not the same.</b> An electricity company's outage page may have low engagement (people visit, read, leave) but high satisfaction (they got the information fast). A controversial TikTok may have huge engagement (angry comments) but low satisfaction. Strong answers notice this difference.</p>\n<p><b>Privacy and security are linked but different.</b> Privacy is about <i>what</i> data is collected and <i>how</i> it is used. Security is about <i>protecting</i> it from theft or misuse. An app can be very secure yet still collect far more data than it needs.</p>\n<p><b>Risk differs by form.</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Social networking</b>: misinformation spreads fast; comments can turn abusive; content you post may be hard to delete.</li>\n<li><b>Games and apps</b>: in-app purchases, data collection, and for children, contact with strangers in chat.</li>\n<li><b>Websites and e-commerce</b>: phishing copies of real sites, insecure payment pages.</li>\n<li><b>Newsletters</b>: spam and phishing emails pretending to be a trusted sender.</li>\n<li><b>Podcasts and videos</b>: lower data risk, but copyright claims if music is used without permission.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Jerome at the Island Pulse Media reviews every client app's permissions before release: \"If the guesthouse booking app asks for microphone access, guests will ask why, and they will be right to.\"</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When a question asks you to evaluate one form, aim for at least two strengths and two weaknesses drawn from <b>different</b> criteria, then a one-sentence judgement: \"Overall, the app is suitable for ... because ..., provided that ...\".</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Three more criteria: satisfaction (did users get what they needed?), purpose (why does it exist, and is that honest?), and risk and security (could it harm users, and is their data safe?). Extras are engagement and privacy. Lots of likes is not the same as satisfaction: an electricity company's outage page may get few comments but still help everyone quickly.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The pairs that students confuse are the ones examiners test. <b>Engagement</b> measures activity, while <b>satisfaction</b> measures whether needs were met, so an angry viral post scores high on one and low on the other. <b>Privacy</b> concerns what data is collected and why, while <b>security</b> concerns protecting it, so a secure app can still be privacy-invasive if it collects contacts it does not need. Purpose evaluation includes transparency: disclosed sponsorship is acceptable, but disguised advertising undermines trust. Risk depends on the form and the audience: chat features in games carry special risks for children. Choose criteria that suit the purpose of the publication you are judging.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the evidence to the criterion","intro":"<p>The Nevis Nature Trails app is being reviewed. Match each piece of evidence to the evaluation criterion it best supports.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Accuracy","r":"Trail lengths are checked against park ranger measurements and dated"},{"l":"Authority","r":"Content is written by the island's national trust, with named authors"},{"l":"Accessibility","r":"Audio descriptions and large-text mode for visually impaired hikers"},{"l":"Satisfaction","r":"An average 4.6-star rating with reviews saying \"found the waterfall easily\""},{"l":"Purpose","r":"The About screen states the app exists to promote safe, eco-friendly hiking, and sponsors are clearly labelled"},{"l":"Risk and security","r":"Logins use two-factor authentication and data is sent over HTTPS"},{"l":"Engagement (extra)","r":"Users post 300 trail photos and comments in the first month"},{"l":"Privacy (extra)","r":"Location is used only while the trail map is open, as stated in the policy"}],"win":"Very good. You linked evidence to the precise criterion, which is what lets you write specific, mark-earning evaluation points.","tutor":[{"h":"Data use vs data protection","p":"<p>What data is collected and why is privacy. How it is protected from attack is risk and security.</p>"},{"h":"Feeling vs doing","p":"<p>Ratings and \"it met my need\" show satisfaction. Posting and commenting show engagement.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the evidence (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Authority","r":"Written by a named, qualified organisation"},{"l":"Accessibility","r":"Captions and audio descriptions"},{"l":"Satisfaction","r":"High star ratings and happy reviews"},{"l":"Risk and security","r":"HTTPS and two-factor login"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Trickier evidence about a trail app. Match each criterion to the evidence that BEST supports it, watching for engagement versus satisfaction and privacy versus security.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Accuracy","r":"Trail lengths match park ranger measurements and each page shows the date checked"},{"l":"Authority","r":"Written by the national trust's named staff, with an About screen"},{"l":"Usability","r":"Testers found the waterfall map in two taps on a budget phone"},{"l":"Accessibility","r":"Large-text mode and audio descriptions for visually impaired hikers"},{"l":"Satisfaction","r":"A 4.7-star average, with reviews saying \"found the trail easily\""},{"l":"Purpose","r":"Sponsored trails are clearly labelled and the aim of safe, eco-friendly hiking is stated"},{"l":"Risk and security","r":"Payments for guided hikes use HTTPS and accounts use two-factor login"},{"l":"Engagement (extra)","r":"Thousands of comments, many of them complaints about a broken map"},{"l":"Privacy (extra)","r":"The app asks for access to contacts, which a trail map does not need"}],"win":"Well analysed. You separated engagement (activity, even angry activity) from satisfaction, and privacy (what is collected) from security (how it is protected).","title":"Match the evidence to the criterion (Challenge)"}},{"type":"mcq","title":"Evaluating a form of publishing","q":"Antigua Sailing Week Live page on a social networking platform has 40,000 followers and thousands of comments on every post. However, a survey shows many sailors could not find the race start times, and several said the comments were full of arguments. Which conclusion is the BEST evaluation?","options":[{"t":"The page has a privacy problem because sailors cannot find start times.","fb":"Finding information is a usability issue. Privacy is about personal data."},{"t":"The page has low accuracy because there are many comments.","fb":"Comments say nothing about whether the facts are correct. The problem is finding information and the tone of comments."},{"t":"The page is excellent because 40,000 followers proves it is high quality.","fb":"Follower numbers measure reach, not quality. The survey shows users' needs were not met."},{"t":"High audience engagement but low user satisfaction and usability, so the page needs a clear schedule and better comment moderation.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. You separated engagement (lots of interaction) from satisfaction and usability (people could not complete their task) and gave a justified improvement."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Two different signals","p":"<p>Lots of comments is one criterion. \"Could not find the start times\" is another.</p>"},{"h":"Evaluate, then recommend","p":"<p>The best evaluation names the criteria and suggests what would improve the page.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Evaluating a form (Foundation)","q":"A page has thousands of comments, but users cannot find the information they need. This shows...","options":[{"t":"high accuracy","fb":"Comments do not prove accuracy."},{"t":"high engagement but low usability","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Interaction is high, but users cannot complete their task."},{"t":"strong security","fb":"Security is about protecting data."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","q":"During a storm, the government's hurricane-update website gets few comments or shares, but a survey shows 92% of users found their nearest shelter quickly. A popular community Facebook page gets ten times more shares but spreads an unverified rumour that a shelter is full. Which evaluation is BEST?","options":[{"t":"The website is poor because almost nobody comments on it.","fb":"A low comment rate is normal for an information service; the survey shows high satisfaction."},{"t":"Both are equally good because both are free.","fb":"Cost is not one of the criteria here, and the evidence shows clear differences in quality."},{"t":"The Facebook page is better because it has far higher engagement.","fb":"Engagement measures activity, not quality. Here the high engagement spread an inaccurate rumour."},{"t":"The website scores higher on accuracy, authority and satisfaction despite low engagement, while the Facebook page has high engagement but poor accuracy and higher risk; social posts should link to the official site.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. This separates engagement from satisfaction, applies accuracy, authority and risk, and recommends how the forms can work together."}],"title":"Evaluating a form of publishing (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Activity: forms of digital publishing","html":"<p>This is the class activity for this objective (Unit 2, Module 2, Objective 7: <i>evaluate forms of digital publishing</i>). Complete both tasks in your notebook or on Google Classroom.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Practical task 1: categorising forms of digital publishing.</b> Digital publishing comes in many different shapes and forms. These include websites, blogs, social networking, games, apps, podcasts, videos and e-books. <b>Define each of these terms.</b></p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Practical task 2: evaluation of forms of digital publishing.</b> <b>Discuss</b> how these forms of digital publishing are evaluated using each of the following: accuracy, authority, usability, accessibility, satisfaction, purpose, and risk and security concerns.</p>\n<p><b>How to tackle task 1.</b> Give each form a one-sentence definition with its <b>defining feature</b> (for example \"dated posts, newest first\" for a blog, \"episodes delivered by RSS\" for a podcast), then a Caribbean example. The definitions on the first two screens of this unit are the standard to aim for.</p>\n<p><b>How to tackle task 2: a worked example.</b> Here is one form evaluated against all seven criteria: <b>Sweet Pot Kitchen</b>, a Jamaican food blog about festival, bammy and sorrel recipes.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Evidence from the blog</th><th>Judgement</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Accuracy</b></td><td>Recipes are tested and dated; one post's history of sorrel gives no sources.</td><td>Mostly strong; history posts need references.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Authority</b></td><td>The About page names the writer, a trained cook from Port Antonio, with contact details.</td><td>Strong for recipes; weaker for history claims.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Usability</b></td><td>Clear menu by dish type, but long stories push the recipe card far down the page on phones.</td><td>Fair; add a \"jump to recipe\" button.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Accessibility</b></td><td>Photos have alt text; video clips have no captions; some light grey text on white.</td><td>Partly accessible; add captions, raise contrast.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Satisfaction</b></td><td>Comments say \"came out just like my grandmother's\"; readers return weekly.</td><td>High.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Purpose</b></td><td>To share Jamaican food heritage; sponsored posts for a Kingston grocery are labelled.</td><td>Clear and open purpose, achieved.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Risk and security</b></td><td>HTTPS is on; comment spam with suspicious links is not removed quickly.</td><td>Moderate risk; moderate comments, keep plugins updated.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Overall judgement:</b> Sweet Pot Kitchen is a trustworthy, satisfying blog for home cooks, provided it adds sources to history posts, captions to videos and faster comment moderation.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Discuss\" means explain both sides. For each criterion, give evidence, say whether it is a strength or a weakness, and end with an overall judgement. Repeat the method for at least two other forms, such as a podcast and an app, to complete task 2.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>This activity has two tasks. First, define each of the eight forms in one sentence with its key feature and a Caribbean example. Second, evaluate forms using all seven criteria: accuracy, authority, usability, accessibility, satisfaction, purpose, and risk and security. For each criterion give evidence, say if it is a strength or weakness, and end with an overall judgement.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The worked Sweet Pot Kitchen example shows the move from <b>description to judgement</b>. Each row gives evidence, then a verdict, and the overall judgement uses a condition (\"provided it adds sources...\"), which shows that the evaluation weighed weaknesses rather than ignoring them. Notice that criteria interact: weak accessibility (no captions) also lowers satisfaction for some users, and a strong About page supports both authority and purpose. Strong answers also weight criteria by the form's purpose, since accuracy matters more for a history post than for a recipe. When repeating the method for a podcast and an app, change the evidence, not just the names.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Task 1: match each form to its definition","intro":"<p>Complete task 1 the quick way. Match each of the eight forms of digital publishing to its definition.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Website","r":"Linked web pages under one domain, built, uploaded and updated by its owner"},{"l":"Blog","r":"Regularly updated dated posts, newest first, in a personal voice with comments"},{"l":"Social networking","r":"Platforms where users create profiles, connect and share posts with others"},{"l":"Game","r":"Interactive entertainment with rules and goals, played on a browser, console or phone"},{"l":"App","r":"Software installed from an app store onto a phone or tablet to carry out a task"},{"l":"Podcast","r":"A series of audio episodes delivered by an RSS feed that listeners subscribe to"},{"l":"Video","r":"Recorded or live moving images with sound, published online"},{"l":"E-book","r":"A book in electronic form, read on a screen or e-reader, often as EPUB"}],"win":"All eight defined. You can now answer task 1 using each form's defining feature: domain, dated posts, profiles, rules and goals, app store, RSS episodes, moving images and electronic book.","tutor":[{"h":"Find the key word","p":"<p>Each definition hides one defining feature. \"Subscribe\" and \"episodes\" point one way; \"app store\" points another.</p>"},{"h":"Website or blog?","p":"<p>Both live on the web. Which definition mentions dated posts in a personal voice?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the definition (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Website","r":"Linked web pages under one domain"},{"l":"Social networking","r":"Profiles, connections and shared posts"},{"l":"Game","r":"Interactive play with rules and goals"},{"l":"Video","r":"Moving images with sound, published online"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Go beyond definitions. Match each form to the feature that separates it from the form it is most often confused with.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Blog (not just a website)","r":"Dated posts in reverse chronological order, in a personal voice"},{"l":"Podcast (not just an audio file)","r":"An episodic series that listeners subscribe to through an RSS feed"},{"l":"Newsletter (not a blog)","r":"Pushed to the inboxes of people who opted in"},{"l":"Digital magazine (not a news website)","r":"Designed, edited issues released on a schedule"},{"l":"App (not a mobile website)","r":"Installed from an app store onto the device"},{"l":"E-book (not a web article)","r":"Book-length content packaged as EPUB or PDF for e-readers"},{"l":"Game (not an ordinary app)","r":"Rules and goals designed for play"},{"l":"Social networking (not an ordinary website)","r":"User profiles, connections and sharing between users"},{"l":"Website","r":"Linked pages under one domain, built and updated by the owner"}],"win":"Excellent. Knowing what separates similar forms is what earns full marks in \"distinguish\" questions.","title":"Task 1: match each form to its definition (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Comparing Caribbean content across the forms","html":"<p>The course outline asks you to <b>compare the Caribbean content available</b> in each form. To compare, you say what is <b>similar</b> and what is <b>different</b> across forms, using evidence. The table gives a general picture; check current examples yourself.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Form</th><th>Caribbean content: general picture</th><th>Strengths and gaps</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Websites</b></td><td>News sites (The Daily Herald, Jamaica Gleaner, Trinidad Express, Barbados Nation), government portals, tourism boards, hotels, banks.</td><td>Plenty of news and tourism content; some government and small-business sites are outdated or not mobile-friendly.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Blogs</b></td><td>Food, travel, culture, faith and personal finance blogs by Caribbean writers at home and in the diaspora.</td><td>Strong personal voice; quality and accuracy vary; many blogs stop updating.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Social networking</b></td><td>The most active form in the region: Carnival bands, musicians, small businesses, news outlets, community groups on Facebook, Instagram, TikTok and WhatsApp.</td><td>Huge reach and engagement; misinformation and platform dependency are serious risks.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Games</b></td><td>A small but growing number of Caribbean-made games and game jams; most games played are foreign.</td><td>Big gap in Caribbean stories and characters; opportunity for local developers.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Apps</b></td><td>Banking, telecom (TelEm, Digicel, Flow), delivery, ride-hailing, government service and news apps.</td><td>Mostly utility apps; fewer cultural or educational apps.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Podcasts and videos</b></td><td>Growing numbers of Caribbean talk, comedy, sports, faith and music podcasts; strong YouTube and TikTok creator scenes, especially in Jamaica and Trinidad and Tobago.</td><td>Authentic voices and Creole and Patois content; monetisation is harder for some territories.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>E-books</b></td><td>Caribbean literature from regional and specialist publishers (for example Peepal Tree Press), self-published authors on Kindle Direct Publishing, school texts.</td><td>Good literature range; fewer local-language and children's titles.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Newsletters and digital magazines</b></td><td>Business, tourism and inflight magazines with online editions (for example Caribbean Beat), event and church newsletters.</td><td>Steady audiences; many smaller titles still rely on PDF only.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Patterns to notice.</b> Caribbean content is <b>strongest</b> on social networking and video, where creating is cheap and the audience is already present. It is <b>thinnest</b> in games and in many educational apps, where costs and skills are higher. This links directly to the trend factors you will meet in Unit 10: affordability, commoditisation and empowerment.</p>\n<p>A Guyana example: a story about the Emancipation Day celebrations on 1 August may appear as a newspaper article (website), a photo carousel on Instagram (social), a live stream on Facebook (video), and perhaps a radio station's podcast. Each version reaches a different audience in a different way.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In a \"compare\" question, use comparative connectives: \"<b>Both</b> ... <b>however</b> ...\", \"<b>whereas</b>\", \"<b>in contrast</b>\". Always name a Caribbean example for each form you compare. Never present invented statistics; say \"in my observation\" or quote a named source.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>To compare Caribbean content across the forms, say what is similar and what is different. The region is strongest on social media and video, where making content is cheap and audiences are already there. It is weakest in games and many learning apps, which cost more to build. One Emancipation Day story can appear as a news article, an Instagram post and a live stream.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The pattern has an explanation, which is what lifts a comparison into analysis: content grows where <b>production costs are low and the audience is already present</b>. Social video needs only a phone; a game needs programmers, artists and months of work. The gaps are also opportunities, because a Caribbean-made game or educational app faces little local competition. Be careful with evidence in this topic: many claims about regional content are impressions, not data, so say \"in my observation\" or cite a named source and year. Use comparative connectives such as \"whereas\" and \"in contrast\", and name a real Caribbean example for each form you compare.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Choosing the right form for an audience and purpose","html":"<p>Choosing a form is a <b>justified decision</b>, not a guess. Island Pulse Media uses a five-question process that matches the classification bases you learned.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Purpose</b>: what must the content achieve? Inform quickly, teach in depth, entertain, sell, build community?</li>\n<li><b>Audience</b>: who are they, how old, where are they, what devices and data plans do they have, what languages do they use?</li>\n<li><b>Content</b>: what media suits the message: text, audio, images, video, interactive?</li>\n<li><b>Interactivity</b>: does the user need only to read, or to respond, choose and do things?</li>\n<li><b>Resources and risk</b>: budget, skills, time to maintain, privacy and security issues.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Need</th><th>Strong choice</th><th>Why</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Urgent alerts to residents (storm, water outage)</td><td>Social networking plus SMS or WhatsApp channel, with a website for detail</td><td>Fast push reach on phones people already use; the website is the authoritative record.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Teaching a skill step by step</td><td>Short video series, or e-book with images</td><td>Demonstration and replay; e-book works offline.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Deep discussion with experts</td><td>Podcast</td><td>Long-form, listened to while driving or working.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Loyal repeat customers</td><td>Newsletter</td><td>Owned list, arrives in the inbox, not controlled by an algorithm.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bookings, payments, frequent use</td><td>App or mobile-friendly website</td><td>High interactivity; an app only if people will use it often.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Engaging young people with heritage</td><td>Game or short-form video</td><td>High interactivity and entertainment value.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Avoid the \"app for everything\" trap.</b> Apps cost more to build and maintain, and many users will not install an app for something they use once a year. A mobile-friendly website often meets the same need more cheaply.</p>\n<p><b>Combine forms.</b> Most real strategies use a <b>main form</b> plus <b>supporting forms</b>: for example, a YouTube video as the main form, with short clips on TikTok and a newsletter linking to it. This also reduces the risk of depending on one platform.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Justify\" means give reasons that link your choice to the scenario. Use \"because\" and refer to the audience and purpose: \"A podcast is best because the target audience of taxi drivers can listen while working, and the purpose is in-depth discussion.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Choose a form by asking five questions: what is the purpose, who is the audience, what kind of content suits the message, how much interaction is needed, and what budget and risks are involved. Urgent storm alerts suit WhatsApp and social media, with a website for detail. Do not make an app for something people use once a year.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Good choices usually combine a <b>main form</b> with supporting forms, because each form has a weakness another can cover: a YouTube video gives depth, short clips spread it, and a newsletter brings loyal viewers back without relying on an algorithm. Combining forms also reduces platform dependency. The \"app for everything\" trap is a cost-benefit error: development, updates and app-store requirements cost money, and an app is valuable only if people use it often. Resources include time to maintain, since an abandoned blog or podcast damages credibility. A justified recommendation links each choice to a named audience, purpose and constraint, using \"because\".</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Choosing a form for a heritage project","intro":"<p>You are advising the Bonaire Heritage Youth Club, which wants to teach 13 to 18 year olds about the island's salt-pan history. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The club president says: \"Let's make a 200-page e-book. Everything in one place.\" Most of the audience use phones and spend their free time on TikTok and games. What do you advise first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Analyse the purpose and audience first: what must they learn, and how do they already consume content?","to":"n2","fb":"Good. The form should follow the purpose and audience, not the other way round."},{"t":"Agree to the 200-page e-book; it is the most complete option.","to":"bad1","fb":"Completeness does not equal engagement. A long text form ignores how this audience consumes content."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Your research shows the teens prefer short video and interactive content, and many have limited mobile data. Which main form do you recommend?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A 4K two-hour documentary to stream on YouTube.","to":"bad2","fb":"Too long for the audience and too heavy on data. It fails usability and accessibility."},{"t":"A custom native app with 3D graphics that must be installed.","to":"bad3","fb":"High cost, large download and low likelihood of installation. A mobile web game meets the need more cheaply."},{"t":"A short-form video series (60 to 90 seconds each) with captions, plus a lightweight quiz game on a mobile web page.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. Short video matches their habits; captions help accessibility; a light web game adds interactivity without a large download."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The game will ask players for a nickname. A parent asks about safety. What do you add?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Collect only a nickname (no email or location), state this in a simple privacy notice, use HTTPS and moderate any leaderboard names.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. You minimised data, explained it, secured it and reduced the risk of abusive names."},{"t":"Ask for full name, school and phone number so prizes can be sent.","to":"bad4","fb":"This collects far more personal data than needed from minors, creating privacy and safety risks."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> The series gets strong engagement and the quiz shows learning gains. A Grade One answer would explain why: the form matched the purpose (educate), the audience (teens on phones), the content type (short video, interactive game) and managed privacy and security.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Low uptake.</b> Few teenagers open a 200-page e-book. Try again and start with the audience's habits.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Viewers drop off.</b> Most stop watching in the first few minutes, and some cannot stream it at all. Try again and match the length and data load to the audience.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Budget gone.</b> The app takes months, costs most of the budget and is rarely installed. Try again with a lighter option.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Privacy complaint.</b> Parents object to the data collected about their children. Try again and collect only what you need.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Form follows audience","p":"<p>What do 13 to 18 year olds actually watch and play?</p>"},{"h":"Data and device limits","p":"<p>Light files and no install widen access.</p>"},{"h":"Minimise data","p":"<p>For young users, collect the least personal data possible.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Choosing a form (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: a youth club wants to teach teenagers island history.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The teens prefer short video and use phones with limited data. What do you recommend?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A 200-page e-book.","to":"bad","fb":"Few teenagers would read it."},{"t":"Short captioned videos plus a light quiz on a mobile web page.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. It fits their habits and their data."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You matched the form to the audience and purpose.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> The form did not suit the audience. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A harder brief. The Heritage Youth Club has US$800, two volunteer teachers, six months, and an audience of 13 to 18 year olds across the island who speak Papiamentu, Dutch, English and Spanish. It wants them to learn salt-pan history.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>The club wants \"an app, a magazine, a podcast and a game, all at once\". What do you advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Analyse purpose, audience and resources first, then choose one main form with one or two supporting forms that fit the budget.","to":"n2","fb":"Excellent. Resources are part of the decision; spreading US$800 across four forms would produce four weak products."},{"t":"Build all four, because more forms mean more reach.","to":"bad1","fb":"With this budget and team, quality and maintenance would collapse."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>Research shows the teens use TikTok and play browser games, and many have limited data. Which plan do you recommend?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Main form: a series of 60 to 90-second captioned videos. Support: a lightweight mobile web quiz game.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. It matches habits, stays light on data and adds interactivity cheaply."},{"t":"Main form: a quarterly digital magazine as a large PDF.","to":"ok1","fb":"A magazine suits depth, but a large PDF is heavy on data and does not match how this audience spends its time."},{"t":"Main form: a native app with 3D graphics.","to":"bad2","fb":"The budget and timeline cannot support it, and few teens would install it."}]},"ok1":{"text":"<p>The first issue is downloaded only 40 times. What now?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Turn the magazine's best stories into short captioned videos and link to a small web quiz.","to":"n3","fb":"Good recovery: repurpose the content into forms the audience actually uses."},{"t":"Print more copies.","to":"bad1","fb":"Printing does not solve the audience mismatch and costs more."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The audience speaks English, French and Spanish. How do you handle language?</p>","choices":[{"t":"English only, to save time.","to":"ok2","fb":"It is cheaper, but it shuts out many French-side and Spanish-speaking teenagers."},{"t":"Use subtitles in all three languages on the videos and a language choice on the quiz.","to":"n4","fb":"Correct. Subtitles are cheap and widen both reach and accessibility."}]},"ok2":{"text":"<p>French-side schools say their students cannot follow the videos. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Tell the schools to teach more English.","to":"bad1","fb":"This ignores the audience the brief named."},{"t":"Add French and Spanish subtitles to the existing videos.","to":"n4","fb":"Good recovery. Subtitles fix the problem without re-filming."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>The quiz will show a leaderboard. A parent asks about safety. What do you add?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Collect full names and schools so winners can be announced.","to":"bad3","fb":"This collects unnecessary personal data about minors and creates real risks."},{"t":"Nicknames only, no email or location, a simple privacy notice, HTTPS and moderation of leaderboard names.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Minimal data, transparency, security and moderation protect minors."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success within budget.</b> The videos reach teens on both sides of the island, the quiz shows learning gains, and parents trust the game. A Grade One answer justifies the main and supporting forms against purpose, audience, content, interactivity, resources and risk.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Resources wasted.</b> The plan does not match the audience or the budget. Try again: purpose, audience and resources first.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Over budget.</b> The app is unfinished after six months. Try again with a lighter form.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Privacy complaint.</b> Parents withdraw permission. Try again and collect only what you need.</p>","end":"bad"}},"title":"Choosing a form for a heritage project (Challenge)"}},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Steps to evaluate a form of digital publishing","intro":"<p>Put Keisha's evaluation method into the correct order. She uses it for every Paper 02-style evaluation.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Identify the form and state its purpose and target audience."},{"t":"Select the evaluation criteria that matter most for that purpose (for example accuracy and authority for news)."},{"t":"Gather evidence for each criterion: check sources, test on a phone, read the privacy policy, look at reviews and engagement."},{"t":"Identify strengths and weaknesses, with a specific example for each."},{"t":"Weigh the strengths against the weaknesses for this audience."},{"t":"Reach a justified judgement and recommend improvements."}],"win":"Correct order. An evaluation moves from context, to criteria, to evidence, to balanced judgement and recommendation.","tutor":[{"h":"Context first","p":"<p>You cannot judge quality until you know what the publication is for.</p>"},{"h":"Judgement last","p":"<p>The conclusion comes after you have weighed both sides.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Evaluation steps (Foundation)","intro":"Put the four steps in order.","blocks":[{"t":"State the purpose and audience."},{"t":"Choose the criteria."},{"t":"Find strengths and weaknesses with evidence."},{"t":"Give a justified judgement."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Now apply the method. Put the steps of Keisha's evaluation of the Sweet Pot Kitchen blog in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Sweet Pot Kitchen is a food blog whose purpose is to share Jamaican food heritage with home cooks."},{"t":"For this purpose, accuracy, authority, usability and satisfaction matter most."},{"t":"Evidence: recipes are tested and dated, but the sorrel history post names no sources."},{"t":"Strength: a named, trained author; weakness: recipe cards buried below long stories on phones."},{"t":"Weighed for home cooks, the trustworthy recipes outweigh the layout problem."},{"t":"Judgement: a trustworthy blog, provided it adds sources and a \"jump to recipe\" button."},{"t":"Re-check the blog after the changes to confirm the improvements worked."}],"win":"Correct. Context, criteria, evidence, strengths and weaknesses, weighing, a conditional judgement, then follow-up: the method applied to a real case.","title":"Steps to evaluate a form of digital publishing (Challenge)"}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Evaluate two forms of digital publishing","genre":"Structured response: evaluating forms of digital publishing","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">The Caroni Swamp Conservation Group in Trinidad wants to raise awareness of mangrove protection among residents and visitors. It is choosing between (1) a weekly podcast and (2) an Instagram account with short videos and photo posts.</p>","prompt":"<p>(a) Classify each option by <b>content type</b> and <b>level of interactivity</b>. (2 marks)</p><p>(b) <b>Evaluate</b> each option using at least THREE of the criteria: accuracy, authority, usability, accessibility, satisfaction, purpose, and risk and security concerns. You may add audience engagement or privacy as extras. (8 marks)</p><p>(c) Recommend ONE option for the group and <b>justify</b> your choice. (3 marks)</p>","min":220,"max":450,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> By content type, the podcast is ... whereas the Instagram account is ... By level of interactivity, ...<br><b>(b)</b> The podcast scores well on ... because ... However, ... In contrast, the Instagram account ... but a risk is ...<br><b>(c)</b> I recommend ... because the audience ... and the purpose ... To reduce the weakness, the group should ...</p>","checklist":["I classified both options by content type AND by interactivity.","I used at least three named evaluation criteria and applied each one.","I gave a strength and a weakness for each option.","I linked points to mangroves, the Caroni Swamp, residents or visitors.","My recommendation uses \"because\" and refers to audience and purpose."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Classification and knowledge of criteria","marks":4,"look":["audio","visual|video|photo|image","interactiv","low|medium|high","accura|authorit|usab|accessib"],"min":3,"strong":"You classified both forms correctly and used the criteria by name.","weak":"State the content type (audio vs visual/video) and the interactivity level (low, medium, high) of each option, and name the criteria you are using."},{"criterion":"Evaluation (strengths and weaknesses)","marks":6,"look":["engag|comment|share|like","caption|transcript|deaf|alt text","risk|misinform|abus|troll","privacy|security|data|location","however|whereas|in contrast|but","strength|weakness|advantage|limitation"],"min":3,"strong":"Your evaluation is balanced and applies each criterion to the options.","weak":"For each option give at least one strength and one weakness, using criteria such as engagement, accessibility (captions, transcripts), risks and privacy."},{"criterion":"Justified recommendation in context","marks":3,"look":["recommend|best|choose|should","because|since|therefore","mangrove|lagoon|caroni|swamp|resident|visitor|tourist"],"min":2,"strong":"Your recommendation is clear and tied to the group's audience and purpose.","weak":"Make one clear recommendation and justify it with \"because\", linking to the residents, visitors and the mangrove awareness purpose."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> By content type, the podcast is an <b>audio</b> form, whereas the Instagram account is <b>visual and video</b>. By level of interactivity, both are <b>medium</b>: podcast listeners can subscribe, rate and email questions, while Instagram users can like, comment, share and message, so Instagram offers more immediate two-way interaction.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> The podcast scores well on <b>authority</b> and <b>accuracy</b>, because 20-minute episodes allow marine biologists and fishermen from the Caroni area to explain mangrove ecology in depth, with sources named in the show notes. However, its <b>accessibility</b> is limited for deaf users unless transcripts are published, and <b>audience engagement</b> may be low among visitors who will not subscribe to a weekly show during a one-week stay.</p>\n<p>In contrast, the Instagram account offers high <b>audience engagement</b>: short Reels of herons in the lagoon can be shared quickly, and visitors already use the app. Its <b>usability</b> is high on phones. Captions and alt text can make it accessible. However, a <b>risk</b> is misinformation or abusive comments under posts about development on the lagoon, so comments must be moderated. There is also a <b>privacy and security</b> concern: geotagging the exact location of nesting birds could expose them to disturbance, and the account needs two-factor authentication to prevent hacking.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> I recommend the <b>Instagram account</b> as the main form, because the purpose is awareness among a broad audience of residents and short-stay visitors, and short captioned videos reach both groups on devices they already use. To overcome its weakness in depth, the group should link from Instagram to a short monthly podcast episode with a transcript, so interested residents can learn more from authoritative guests.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Evaluation marks go to <b>applied</b> criteria, not definitions. Name the criterion in bold or underline in your exam script, give evidence from the scenario, and always include at least one weakness for the option you recommend. A recommendation that admits a weakness and fixes it reads as a Grade One answer.</p>",
 "easy": {"bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: evaluating two forms (8 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part. Use the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>State</b> the content type of the podcast and of the Instagram account. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE strength of the podcast, naming the criterion. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE weakness of the Instagram account, naming the criterion. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Choose</b> one option and give ONE reason. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":70,"max":200,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> The podcast is ... The Instagram account is ...<br><b>(b)</b> A strength of the podcast is its ... because ...<br><b>(c)</b> A weakness of the Instagram account is ... because ...<br><b>(d)</b> I would choose ... because ...</p>","checklist":["I said the podcast is audio.","I said Instagram is visual or video.","I named a criterion for the strength.","I named a criterion for the weakness.","I chose one option and gave a reason linked to the audience."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Content types","marks":2,"look":["audio","visual|video|photo|image"],"min":2,"strong":"Both correct.","weak":"The podcast is audio; the Instagram account is visual or video."},{"criterion":"Podcast strength","marks":2,"look":["accura|authorit|usab|accessib|satisf|purpose|depth","because|so|as"],"min":2,"strong":"A named criterion with a reason.","weak":"Name a criterion such as authority and explain it, e.g. experts can explain mangroves in depth."},{"criterion":"Instagram weakness","marks":2,"look":["risk|misinform|abus|privacy|security|accura|accessib","because|so|as"],"min":2,"strong":"A named criterion with a reason.","weak":"Name a criterion such as risk and explain it, e.g. comments can spread misinformation."},{"criterion":"Choice and reason","marks":2,"look":["choose|recommend|pick|would use","resident|visitor|phone|audience|young|people"],"min":2,"strong":"Choice linked to the audience.","weak":"Choose one option and link your reason to the residents or visitors."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> The podcast is an audio form. The Instagram account is a visual and video form.</p><p><b>(b)</b> A strength of the podcast is its authority, because marine experts from Trinidad can explain mangroves in depth.</p><p><b>(c)</b> A weakness of the Instagram account is risk, because comments could spread misinformation about the lagoon.</p><p><b>(d)</b> I would choose Instagram because residents and visitors already use it on their phones, so more people will see the message.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Always name the criterion in your sentence, such as \"authority\" or \"risk\". Then add \"because\" and a fact from the scenario. That pattern earns both marks in each part.</p>","title":"Evaluate two forms of digital publishing (Foundation)"},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Structured response: evaluating forms of digital publishing, Grade One extension (20 marks)","prompt":"<p>The group may now fund TWO forms. Write a response that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Classifies</b> the podcast, the Instagram account and a third option, a monthly newsletter, by content type, platform and level of interactivity. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluates</b> the podcast and the Instagram account using at least FIVE criteria, including at least one extra (engagement or privacy), with a strength and a weakness for each option. <i>(9 marks)</i></li><li><b>Distinguishes</b> between engagement and satisfaction, using evidence the group could collect. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Recommends</b> a combination of TWO forms, justifying how they work together and how their weaknesses will be reduced. <i>(5 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":350,"max":700,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> By content type ... By platform ... By interactivity ...<br><b>(b)</b> The podcast scores well on ... However, ... The Instagram account ... In contrast, ...<br><b>(c)</b> Engagement means ... whereas satisfaction ... The group could measure ...<br><b>(d)</b> I recommend ... as the main form and ... as the supporting form because ... To reduce ..., the group should ...</p>","checklist":["I classified all three options on three bases.","I used at least five criteria, including an extra, with evidence from the scenario.","Each option has at least one strength and one weakness.","I distinguished engagement from satisfaction with measurable evidence.","My recommendation explains how the two forms support each other and how weaknesses are reduced."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Classification on three bases","marks":3,"look":["audio","visual|video|text","platform|app|inbox|email|instagram|podcast app","low|medium|high"],"min":4,"strong":"All three options classified accurately on each basis.","weak":"For each option give content type, platform and interactivity level."},{"criterion":"Evaluation using five criteria","marks":9,"look":["accura","authorit","usab","accessib","satisf|purpose","risk|security","engag|privacy","however|in contrast|whereas|but"],"min":6,"strong":"A balanced, evidence-based evaluation across at least five criteria.","weak":"Apply at least five named criteria to both options, giving a strength and a weakness for each with evidence."},{"criterion":"Engagement versus satisfaction","marks":3,"look":["engag","satisf","survey|rating|review|comment|share|like|repeat|downloads|open rate"],"min":3,"strong":"A precise distinction with measurable evidence.","weak":"Explain that engagement is activity, while satisfaction is whether needs were met, and name a way to measure each."},{"criterion":"Justified combination","marks":5,"look":["recommend","main|primary","support|link|combin|together","because","mangrove|lagoon|caroni|swamp|resident|visitor"],"min":4,"strong":"A convincing combination with weaknesses addressed.","weak":"Recommend two forms, explain how one supports the other, and show how each weakness is reduced for this audience."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> By content type, the podcast is <b>audio</b>, the Instagram account is <b>visual and video</b>, and the newsletter is mainly <b>text</b> with images. By platform, the podcast runs in podcast apps through an RSS feed, the Instagram account on a social networking app, and the newsletter in subscribers' email inboxes. By level of interactivity, all three are <b>medium</b>, but Instagram is the most interactive because users can comment, share and message instantly, whereas newsletter readers mainly click links.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> The podcast scores highly on <b>authority</b> and <b>accuracy</b>, because 20-minute episodes let named marine biologists and Caroni fishermen explain mangrove ecology with sources in the show notes. Its <b>purpose</b>, education, is clear. However, its <b>accessibility</b> is weak for deaf users unless transcripts are provided, and its <b>usability</b> for short-stay visitors is low, since few will subscribe during a one-week holiday.</p>\n<p>In contrast, the Instagram account has strong <b>usability</b> on phones and high <b>engagement</b>: captioned Reels of herons and crabs in the mangroves can be shared widely by visitors. Captions and alt text can make it accessible. However, its <b>accuracy</b> and <b>risk</b> are concerns, because comments under posts about lagoon development can spread misinformation or turn abusive, so moderation is essential. There is also a <b>privacy and security</b> issue: geotags could reveal nesting sites, and the account needs two-factor authentication to prevent hacking.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Engagement measures how actively people interact, such as likes, shares, comments and downloads, whereas satisfaction measures whether people's needs were met. A Reel might get thousands of shares yet leave viewers unsure how to help the mangroves. The group could measure engagement from Instagram insights and podcast downloads, and satisfaction through a short survey asking whether listeners learned how to protect the lagoon and whether they would recommend the content.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> I recommend Instagram as the <b>main</b> form and a monthly podcast episode as the <b>supporting</b> form. Instagram reaches both residents and visitors quickly, because they already use it, while the podcast adds the authoritative depth that short posts lack. The two work together: each Reel ends with \"Hear the full story\" and a link to the latest episode, and each episode is clipped into short audiograms for Instagram. To reduce the weaknesses, the group should publish transcripts for the podcast, moderate Instagram comments, remove geotags from nesting photos and turn on two-factor authentication.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Weight your time by the marks: part (b) is worth nearly half. Bold or underline each criterion name in your script, and make sure your recommendation in part (d) fixes the weaknesses you identified in part (b).</p>","title":"Evaluate two forms of digital publishing (Challenge)"}}]},{"id":"M2U9","title":"Online publishing platforms","objectives":["Distinguish between hosted free and paid online publishing platforms, and between hosted and self-hosted options, using WordPress.com, Blogger, Medium, self-hosted WordPress, Squarespace and Wix.","Use online platforms to publish content: blogs (frequency, SEO, community), Wikipedia (neutrality, verifiability, notability, collaborative authorship), YouTube (upload settings, thumbnails, metadata, monetisation) and live streaming.","Explain the setup and best practice for live streaming on Facebook Live, Instagram Live, Twitch and TikTok Live.","Evaluate the benefits and limitations of online platforms: cost, ease of use, reach, ownership, accessibility, analytics, monetisation, platform rules, privacy, security, copyright and content control.","Describe how content on online platforms is maintained over time."],"videos":[{"title":"WordPress.com vs WordPress.org","q":"wordpress.com vs wordpress.org difference explained for beginners"},{"title":"Uploading a YouTube video the right way","q":"how to upload a youtube video settings title description thumbnail tutorial"},{"title":"How to edit Wikipedia","q":"how to edit wikipedia for beginners neutral point of view citations"},{"title":"Live streaming with OBS Studio","q":"OBS Studio beginner tutorial live stream setup stream key"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"Hosted free, hosted paid and self-hosted platforms","html":"<p>An <b>online publishing platform</b> is a service that lets you create, store and publish content to an audience on the web without building all the technology yourself. You must be able to compare <b>hosted (free or paid)</b> platforms and the self-hosted option.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Hosted</b>: the platform company runs the servers, software, security and updates. You sign up and publish. Examples: <b>WordPress.com</b>, <b>Blogger</b> (Google), <b>Medium</b>, <b>Squarespace</b>, <b>Wix</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Self-hosted</b>: you download the free, open-source software from <b>WordPress.org</b> and install it on web hosting that you rent, with your own domain. You control everything, and you are responsible for everything.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Hosted free (WordPress.com free plan, Blogger, Medium)</th><th>Hosted paid (Squarespace, Wix premium, WordPress.com paid)</th><th>Self-hosted (WordPress.org on rented hosting)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Cost</td><td>Free to start</td><td>Monthly or yearly subscription</td><td>Hosting plus domain fees; free software; paid themes and plugins optional</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Web address</td><td>Subdomain, e.g. <i>yourname.blogspot.com</i> or <i>medium.com/@yourname</i></td><td>Custom domain included or easily added</td><td>Your own domain, e.g. <i>sxmeats.com</i></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ease of use</td><td>Very easy</td><td>Easy drag-and-drop templates</td><td>Steeper learning curve</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Control and features</td><td>Limited design and plugins; platform ads may appear</td><td>Good templates, built-in e-commerce, no platform ads</td><td>Full control: any theme, plugin, code or monetisation</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ownership and maintenance</td><td>Platform controls the rules and can change or remove features</td><td>Platform maintains it; moving away can be hard (lock-in)</td><td>You own the files and database; you handle updates, backups and security</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Medium</b> is different again: you write on Medium's own publication network, gaining access to its readers, but with very little design control. It suits writers who care about readers more than branding.</p>\n<p>The Island Pulse Media advises clients like this. A Georgetown teenager starting a poetry blog: <b>Blogger</b> or <b>Medium</b> (free, simple). A Holetown restaurant wanting a stylish menu site and online reservations: <b>Squarespace</b> or <b>Wix</b> (paid, easy, professional). A news start-up in Guyana that needs custom features and full ownership: <b>self-hosted WordPress</b>, with Maya managing updates.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A classic Paper 01 trap: <b>WordPress.com</b> is a <i>hosted</i> service; <b>WordPress.org</b> provides the software you <i>self-host</i>. Same software family, different ownership and responsibility.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>An online publishing platform lets you publish on the web without building the technology yourself. Hosted platforms, such as Blogger, Medium, Wix and Squarespace, run everything for you, free or for a fee. Self-hosted means installing WordPress.org on hosting you rent, so you control and maintain everything. A teenager's poetry blog in Georgetown could start free on Blogger.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The real trade-off is between <b>convenience and control</b>, and with control comes responsibility. Hosted platforms handle servers, security and updates, but they set the rules, can change features and make moving away difficult. Self-hosting gives full freedom over design, plugins and monetisation, but the owner must update software, make backups and fix security problems, which is why a self-hosted site without a maintainer is risky. Cost is more than the sign-up price: free plans may show adverts and use a subdomain that looks less professional. Recommend a platform by matching it to the client's skills, budget, need for features and plans for growth, and remember WordPress.com differs from WordPress.org.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Hosted free, hosted paid or self-hosted?","intro":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is sorting client notes. Decide which type of platform each note describes.</p>","main":"Which type of platform is described?","bins":["Hosted free","Hosted paid","Self-hosted"],"items":[{"t":"A Wix premium plan removes Wix ads and connects a custom domain.","bin":1,"fb":"Premium Wix plans are paid hosted services."},{"t":"The owner must arrange their own backups and security plugin after the server is hacked.","bin":2,"fb":"On a self-hosted site, security and backups are the owner's responsibility."},{"t":"A Willemstad boutique pays a monthly Squarespace plan for templates and online sales.","bin":1,"fb":"Squarespace is a hosted service that charges a subscription."},{"t":"A free WordPress.com site that shows the platform's ads and a wordpress.com address.","bin":0,"fb":"The free WordPress.com plan is hosted, free, and limited."},{"t":"A student blog at grenadaspice.blogspot.com with no fees at all.","bin":0,"fb":"Blogger is hosted by Google, free, and uses a blogspot subdomain."},{"t":"Full freedom to edit the theme's PHP code and add any plugin.","bin":2,"fb":"Full code and plugin control is the main benefit of self-hosting."},{"t":"The client installs WordPress.org software on rented hosting and must update plugins.","bin":2,"fb":"Installing the software yourself on your own hosting is self-hosting, with full responsibility for updates."},{"t":"A Jamaican essayist publishes on Medium to reach its readers, paying nothing.","bin":0,"fb":"Medium hosts the content; writing and publishing are free."}],"win":"Well sorted. You separated who runs the servers (hosted or self) and who pays (free or paid), and linked self-hosting to control and responsibility.","tutor":[{"h":"Who runs the servers?","p":"<p>If the platform company does, it is hosted. If the client rents hosting and installs the software, it is self-hosted.</p>"},{"h":"Who is responsible?","p":"<p>Updates, backups and security on the owner's shoulders point to self-hosting.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Hosted or self-hosted? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"A free Blogger blog.","bin":0,"fb":"Free and hosted by Google."},{"t":"A paid Squarespace plan.","bin":1,"fb":"Paid and hosted."},{"t":"WordPress.org installed on rented hosting.","bin":2,"fb":"That is self-hosted."},{"t":"Writing free on Medium.","bin":0,"fb":"Free and hosted."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","main":"Which type of platform should this client choose?","intro":"<p>Now recommend: which type of platform BEST suits each client?</p>","items":[{"t":"A Form 5 student wants to start a free poetry blog this weekend and has no budget.","bin":0,"fb":"A free hosted platform such as Blogger or Medium costs nothing and needs no technical skill."},{"t":"A Bonaire dive shop wants a polished site with online bookings and its own domain, and will pay monthly to avoid technical work.","bin":1,"fb":"A paid hosted platform includes templates, a custom domain and booking tools, and handles maintenance."},{"t":"A Guyanese news start-up needs custom features, full ownership of its data, and has a developer to maintain it.","bin":2,"fb":"Self-hosted WordPress gives full control, and the developer can handle updates and security."},{"t":"A writer cares most about reaching an existing community of readers, not about branding.","bin":0,"fb":"Medium's free publishing network offers built-in readers with little design control."},{"t":"A craft co-operative wants no platform adverts and a professional address, but has no technical staff.","bin":1,"fb":"A paid hosted plan removes platform adverts and adds a custom domain without needing technical skill."},{"t":"A developer wants to edit theme code and install any plugin she chooses.","bin":2,"fb":"Full code and plugin freedom requires self-hosting."},{"t":"A church youth group wants a simple free blog and does not mind a subdomain address.","bin":0,"fb":"A free hosted blog meets simple needs at no cost."},{"t":"A heritage archive must keep its database and files on servers it controls for long-term preservation.","bin":2,"fb":"Self-hosting gives full ownership of files and database."},{"t":"A restaurant wants an online menu and reservations set up in a week by a non-technical owner.","bin":1,"fb":"A paid hosted builder such as Squarespace or Wix is fast and easy, with reservations tools."}],"win":"Strong recommendations. You matched cost, control, responsibility and skills to each client, which is the core of a \"recommend a platform\" answer.","title":"Hosted free, hosted paid or self-hosted? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Publishing a blog: frequency, SEO and community","html":"<p><b>Publishing blogs</b> is a key use of online platforms. A blog succeeds when it is <b>consistent</b>, <b>findable</b> and <b>social</b>.</p>\n<p><b>1. Posting frequency and consistency</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Choose a schedule you can keep (for example one post a week, every Wednesday) and publish an <b>editorial calendar</b>. Consistency matters more than volume.</li>\n<li>Use <b>scheduling</b> in WordPress or Blogger to queue posts in advance, for example around Carnival or Heineken Regatta week.</li>\n<li>Mix <b>evergreen</b> posts (always useful: \"How to make johnny cakes\") with <b>timely</b> posts (\"Emancipation Day events this weekend\").</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>2. SEO (Search Engine Optimisation)</b> (see Unit 2.4)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Research a <b>focus keyword</b> people actually search, such as \"best beaches Tobago\", and use it in the title, the first paragraph, one heading and the URL slug (<i>/best-beaches-tobago</i>).</li>\n<li>Write a <b>meta description</b> of about 150 to 160 characters; add <b>alt text</b> to every image; compress images so pages load quickly on mobile data.</li>\n<li>Use <b>internal links</b> to your other posts and <b>external links</b> to authoritative sources. Plugins such as Yoast SEO or Rank Math give on-page checks in WordPress.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>3. Community building</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enable and <b>moderate comments</b>; reply within a day or two. Set a clear comment policy.</li>\n<li>Offer an <b>email subscription</b> so readers are notified of new posts (you own that list).</li>\n<li>Share posts on social platforms, collaborate with other Caribbean bloggers (guest posts), and invite readers to suggest topics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media runs the agency blog. After moving from random posting to a fixed Thursday schedule with keyword-focused titles, she watched which posts brought visitors in Google Search Console and wrote more on those topics.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For a Paper 02 blog question, organise your answer under three headings: <b>Frequency</b>, <b>SEO</b>, <b>Community</b>. Give one specific action under each (a day of the week, a keyword, a comment policy). Specific actions earn more marks than \"post often and use keywords\".</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A blog succeeds when it is consistent, easy to find and friendly. Post on a schedule you can keep, such as every Wednesday. Use a focus keyword in the title, first paragraph, a heading and the web address, write a meta description and add alt text. Build community by replying to comments, offering email sign-up and sharing posts on social media.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The three pillars reinforce each other. Consistency trains readers, and search engines, to expect new content; good SEO brings new readers; community turns them into returning readers and sharers. Evergreen posts keep earning traffic for years, while timely posts spike around events such as Carnival, so a strong calendar mixes both. An email list is the blog's <b>owned</b> audience, which protects it from social media algorithm changes. Data closes the loop: tools such as Google Search Console show which queries bring visitors, guiding the next topics. For a Paper 02 answer, give specific actions under each heading, such as a day, a keyword and a comment policy.</p>"},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Blog post SEO check","intro":"<p>A Barbados guesthouse owner sends Keisha the plan for her first blog post. The target keyword is <b>\"Bathsheba surfing guide\"</b>.</p>","q":"Which items are GOOD SEO practice? Select all that apply.","lines":["Title: \"Bathsheba Surfing Guide: Waves, Seasons and Safety\"","URL slug: /post-123456","The keyword appears naturally in the first paragraph.","Photos named IMG_0042.jpg with no alt text.","A 155-character meta description that includes the keyword.","The keyword repeated 40 times in one short post.","Links to the guesthouse's other posts about the east coast.","Images compressed so the page loads quickly on mobile data."],"correct":[0,2,4,6,7],"win":"Well spotted. Keyword in the title, opening and meta description, internal links and fast-loading images all help people find and stay on the post.","miss":"Check again. A meaningless URL, missing alt text and keyword stuffing all hurt SEO. Look for keyword placement, descriptions, links and speed.","tutor":[{"h":"Natural, not stuffed","p":"<p>Search engines reward a keyword used naturally in key places, not repeated over and over.</p>"},{"h":"Machines read text","p":"<p>Search engines cannot see a photo; they read its file name and alt text.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Blog SEO check (Foundation)","q":"Which are good SEO practice? Select all that apply.","lines":["Keyword in the post title.","Images with descriptive alt text.","Keyword repeated 40 times.","A clear meta description."],"correct":[0,1,3],"win":"Yes. Clear titles, alt text and descriptions help search engines.","miss":"Keyword stuffing hurts. Look again."},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>The guesthouse owner's second draft is more advanced, and some mistakes are subtler. The target keyword is <b>\"Bathsheba surfing guide\"</b>.</p>","q":"Which items are GOOD SEO practice? Select all that apply.","lines":["Title tag: \"Bathsheba Surfing Guide: Waves, Seasons and Safety\" (50 characters).","Title tag: \"Everything You Ever Wanted to Know About Our Lovely Little Guesthouse and the Bathsheba Surfing Guide\" (101 characters).","URL slug: /bathsheba-surfing-guide","The only H1 says \"Welcome!\" and the keyword appears only in an H3.","Alt text: \"Surfer riding a wave at the Soup Bowl, Bathsheba\".","Alt text on every photo: \"Bathsheba surfing guide Bathsheba surfing guide\".","A 210-character meta description that is cut off in search results.","An H2 answers a question people search for: \"When is the best surf season at Bathsheba?\"","A link to an authoritative external source on ocean safety.","The hero photo is uploaded as a 4 MB PNG."],"correct":[0,2,4,7,8],"win":"Well judged. A focused 50-character title, a clean slug, descriptive alt text, a question-based heading and an authoritative link all help. Long titles, a vague H1, stuffed alt text, an overlong description and heavy images hurt.","miss":"Look again. Check title length and keyword position, whether the H1 carries the topic, whether alt text describes rather than repeats keywords, the 150 to 160 character description limit, and image weight.","title":"Blog post SEO check (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Writing on Wikipedia","html":"<p><b>Wikipedia</b> is a free online encyclopaedia written <b>collaboratively</b> by volunteers using wiki software (MediaWiki). Anyone can edit, but every edit must follow the community's <b>editorial standards</b>. Writing on Wikipedia is another use of online platforms.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Standard</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Neutral point of view (NPOV)</b></td><td>Articles represent significant views fairly, without opinion, praise or promotion.</td><td>Write \"The festival attracts visitors from across the region\", not \"the best festival in the world\".</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Verifiability</b></td><td>Readers must be able to check every claim in a <b>reliable, published source</b>, cited inline.</td><td>Cite a national newspaper article or a published history book for the date a pier opened.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>No original research</b></td><td>Do not add your own theories, interviews or unpublished findings.</td><td>Your grandmother's story about Spanish Town is valuable, but it belongs on a blog or podcast, not Wikipedia.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Notability</b></td><td>A topic needs significant coverage in independent reliable sources to have its own article.</td><td>A small family restaurant with no independent coverage is not notable; a national festival covered by several newspapers is.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Conflict of interest</b></td><td>Do not write about yourself, your business or your client; declare any connection.</td><td>Island Pulse Media must not write a promotional article about its own clients.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Collaborative authorship.</b> No one owns an article. Every change is saved in the <b>View history</b>, so edits can be compared and reverted. Disagreements are discussed on the article's <b>Talk page</b> and resolved by consensus. Experienced editors patrol recent changes and remove vandalism quickly.</p>\n<p><b>How to contribute well.</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Create an account (it gives you a user page and makes your edits easier to track).</li>\n<li>Practise in your <b>sandbox</b> and read the Help pages.</li>\n<li>Start small: fix a typo, add a citation, update a fact with a source.</li>\n<li>Write an <b>edit summary</b> explaining every change.</li>\n<li>Use the Talk page before making large or disputed changes.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Many Caribbean topics are thin on Wikipedia: local musicians, historic buildings, Creole terms, island villages. Students can help close this gap, as long as they use published sources.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 01 likes this distinction: <b>verifiability</b> is about being able to check a claim in a published source; <b>notability</b> is about whether a topic deserves its own article at all. Do not mix them up.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Wikipedia is a free encyclopedia that volunteers write together. Anyone can edit, but edits must be neutral, backed by published sources, contain no personal research, cover notable topics and avoid conflicts of interest. Discuss changes on the Talk page; every edit is saved in View history. Your grandmother's Spanish Town story belongs on a blog, not Wikipedia.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Wikipedia's standards explain both its strengths and its limits as a source. Verifiability means articles report what reliable published sources say, not the truth as an editor knows it, so a correct but unpublished fact cannot be added. \"No original research\" also forbids <b>synthesis</b>: combining two sources to reach a conclusion neither states. These rules make Wikipedia a strong starting point for research but not a final citation, because content can change at any moment; follow its references to the original sources. The gap in Caribbean coverage is partly structural: topics need published independent coverage before they can be notable, so local journalism and publishing feed Wikipedia.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the Wikipedia standard","intro":"<p>Your class is editing the Wikipedia article on a Caribbean festival. Match each editing problem to the Wikipedia standard it breaks or the feature it uses.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Neutral point of view","r":"Removing \"the most amazing party in the Caribbean\" from the opening sentence"},{"l":"Verifiability","r":"Adding a citation to a published newspaper article for the founding year"},{"l":"No original research","r":"Deleting a paragraph based on a student's own unpublished survey"},{"l":"Notability","r":"Deciding a one-off school party with no news coverage should not have its own article"},{"l":"Conflict of interest","r":"A band manager declaring a connection before suggesting edits about the band"},{"l":"Talk page","r":"Where editors discuss and reach consensus on a disputed date"},{"l":"View history","r":"Comparing versions and reverting vandalism from last night"}],"win":"Excellent. You can explain how Wikipedia stays reliable: neutral wording, cited sources, no original research, notability, declared interests and transparent collaboration.","tutor":[{"h":"Checkable or deserving?","p":"<p>Can a reader check the fact? That is verifiability. Does the topic deserve an article? That is notability.</p>"},{"h":"Discussion vs record","p":"<p>Editors talk on one page and see every past version on another.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Wikipedia standards (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Neutral point of view","r":"No praise or opinion in the article"},{"l":"Verifiability","r":"Every claim cited to a published source"},{"l":"Notability","r":"Topic has independent coverage"},{"l":"Talk page","r":"Editors discuss changes"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder Wikipedia situations. Match each standard or feature to the action that applies it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Neutral point of view","r":"Rewording \"the island's greatest calypsonian\" to \"a calypsonian who won the national monarch title three times\""},{"l":"Verifiability","r":"Leaving a claim tagged \"citation needed\" until a published source is added"},{"l":"No original research","r":"Removing a paragraph that combines two sources to reach a conclusion neither source states"},{"l":"Notability","r":"Proposing deletion of a band article whose only coverage is the band's own social media"},{"l":"Conflict of interest","r":"A hotel's marketing officer suggesting changes on the Talk page instead of editing the hotel's article directly"},{"l":"Talk page","r":"Editors comparing two published dates for a fort's construction before agreeing which to use"},{"l":"View history","r":"Restoring the version from before a vandal replaced the text with jokes"},{"l":"Edit summary","r":"A short note explaining \"Added source for founding year\""},{"l":"Sandbox","r":"Practising a draft where it does not affect live articles"}],"win":"Excellent. You applied the standards to subtle cases, including synthesis as original research and the correct way to handle a conflict of interest.","title":"Match the Wikipedia standard (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Publishing video on YouTube","html":"<p>YouTube is the most widely used video platform. The steps below follow YouTube Studio; menu names can change, so check the current interface.</p>\n<p><b>Upload settings (Create, then Upload videos)</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>File</b>: export as MP4 (H.264 video, AAC audio). For a 1080p video use 1920 by 1080 at the frame rate you recorded (for example 30 fps). Name the file meaningfully: <i>tt-carnival-jouvert-2026.mp4</i>.</li>\n<li><b>Title</b>: clear, keyword first, under about 70 characters so it is not cut off: \"J'ouvert Morning in Port of Spain | Trinidad Carnival\".</li>\n<li><b>Description</b>: first two lines matter most (they show before \"more\"); add links, credits and <b>chapters</b> (timestamps such as 0:00 Intro, 1:15 The jump-up).</li>\n<li><b>Thumbnail</b>: upload a custom image, 1280 by 720 pixels (16:9), JPG or PNG, under the size limit. Use a clear face or subject, a few bold words and high contrast; it must honestly reflect the video (no clickbait).</li>\n<li><b>Audience</b>: you must state whether the video is <b>\"made for kids\"</b>. This affects comments and personalised ads.</li>\n<li><b>Tags, category, language, captions</b>: add relevant tags and the category; upload or check <b>captions</b> (auto-captions often mishear Caribbean names and Creole).</li>\n<li><b>Video elements</b>: <b>end screens</b> and <b>cards</b> link to other videos or playlists.</li>\n<li><b>Visibility</b>: <b>Public</b> (anyone), <b>Unlisted</b> (only people with the link), <b>Private</b> (only invited accounts), or <b>Schedule</b> a premiere time.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Metadata</b> is the title, description, tags, thumbnail, captions, category and chapters. It tells YouTube's search and recommendation systems what the video is about, so good metadata is YouTube's version of SEO.</p>\n<p><b>Monetisation.</b> Creators can earn through the <b>YouTube Partner Program</b> (ad revenue sharing, channel memberships, Super Chat, Super Thanks, Shopping). Entry requires meeting subscriber and watch-time (or Shorts views) thresholds and following the monetisation policies; check the current requirements and whether the programme is available in your territory. Sponsorships and affiliate links are other options, but must be disclosed.</p>\n<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media uploads client videos as <b>Unlisted</b> first so the client can approve them, then switches to Public at the agreed time.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Paper 02 may ask for a \"suitable title, description and thumbnail\" for a scenario. Write them out in full, and justify each in one line (keyword, clarity, 16:9 at 1280 by 720, honest image).</p>",
 "plain": "<p>To publish on YouTube, upload an MP4, write a clear title with the keyword first, a strong description with chapters, and a 1280 by 720 thumbnail that honestly shows the video. Say whether it is made for kids, check captions, and choose Public, Unlisted, Private or a scheduled premiere. An agency might upload a Carnival video as Unlisted so the client can approve it.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Metadata is YouTube's SEO, but it also affects <b>trust</b>: a clickbait thumbnail may win clicks yet lose viewers quickly, and the recommendation system notices short watch times. Settings carry legal and ethical weight too. \"Made for kids\" is a compliance decision that switches off comments and personalised ads. Paid promotions must be disclosed. Copyright works on two tracks: a Content ID claim may block or monetise a video without a strike, whereas a formal takedown request can bring a copyright strike, and repeated strikes can end a channel. Monetisation thresholds and availability vary by territory, so Caribbean creators often combine platform income with local sponsorship.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"YouTube upload decisions","intro":"<p>Malik at the Island Pulse Media is uploading a client's video about the Spice Island Emporium on Frederick Street in Port of Spain. Choose the best setting each time.</p>","items":[{"text":"The client must approve the video before anyone else sees it, but needs to watch it without logging into Malik's account.","options":["Unlisted","Made for kids","Public"],"answer":0,"fb":"Unlisted lets anyone with the link watch it, but it does not appear in search or on the channel."},{"text":"Which thumbnail size matches YouTube's recommended 16:9 format?","options":["1280 by 720","1080 by 1920","500 by 500"],"answer":0,"fb":"1280 by 720 is 16:9 landscape, YouTube's recommended thumbnail size. 1080 by 1920 is vertical 9:16."},{"text":"Viewers want to jump straight to the tasting section. What should Malik add?","options":["an end screen","more tags","chapters (timestamps in the description)"],"answer":2,"fb":"Timestamps in the description create chapters, so viewers can jump to sections."},{"text":"Auto-captions wrote \"shadow benny\" instead of \"chadon beni\". What should he do?","options":["edit the captions","turn off captions","leave them; auto-captions are always fine"],"answer":0,"fb":"Edited captions improve accuracy, accessibility and searchability. Turning them off excludes deaf viewers."},{"text":"The title, description, tags and thumbnail together are called the video's...","options":["metadata","bitrate","codec"],"answer":0,"fb":"Metadata is data about the video, used by search and recommendations."},{"text":"The client is paid by a rum brand to feature its product. What must they do?","options":["nothing","hide it in the tags","disclose the paid promotion"],"answer":2,"fb":"Platform rules and advertising standards require paid promotions to be disclosed."}],"win":"Great work. You chose visibility, thumbnail size, chapters, captions, metadata and disclosure correctly, which are the practical settings Paper 02 asks about.","tutor":[{"h":"Visibility options","p":"<p>Public, Unlisted, Private, Scheduled. Which one works with a shared link only?</p>"},{"h":"Landscape","p":"<p>16:9 is wider than it is tall.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"YouTube decisions (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"Only people with the link can watch. This is...","options":["Public","Unlisted"],"answer":1,"fb":"Unlisted videos need the link."},{"text":"A recommended thumbnail size is...","options":["1280 by 720","500 by 500"],"answer":0,"fb":"1280 by 720 is 16:9."},{"text":"Title, description and tags are...","options":["codecs","metadata"],"answer":1,"fb":"They are data about the video."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder upload decisions for client videos. Choose the BEST option.</p>","items":[{"text":"A title is 95 characters long, with the keyword \"pepper sauce\" at the very end. What is the BEST fix?","options":["Shorten it to under about 70 characters with the keyword near the front","Add more words","Move the keyword into the tags only","Write it in capitals"],"answer":0,"fb":"Long titles are cut off in results, so keep them short and front-load the keyword."},{"text":"A Grenada storyteller's channel is aimed at young children. What must be set, and what follows?","options":["Nothing needs to be set","Mark it \"made for kids\", which switches off comments and personalised ads","Set it to Private","Disable captions"],"answer":1,"fb":"Content for children must be marked \"made for kids\", which changes features such as comments and ad types."},{"text":"The client wants the video to launch at 7 p.m. on Friday with viewers chatting as it first plays.","options":["Keep it Unlisted forever","Post it as a Short","Upload it as Public now","Schedule it as a Premiere"],"answer":3,"fb":"A Premiere schedules the first showing with live chat."},{"text":"Chapters are not appearing. The description lists 1:15 The jump-up and 3:00 Tasting. What is missing?","options":["A longer title","A first timestamp at 0:00","A thumbnail","More tags"],"answer":1,"fb":"Chapters need a list that starts at 0:00, with several timestamps in order."},{"text":"A background song triggers a Content ID claim. How does this differ from a copyright strike?","options":["A claim lets the rights holder block or monetise the video without a strike; a strike follows a formal takedown request and repeated strikes can end the channel","A strike only affects the thumbnail","They are the same thing","A claim always deletes the channel"],"answer":0,"fb":"Claims and strikes are separate systems with different consequences."},{"text":"A rum brand pays for a product feature. Apart from saying so in the video, what should Malik do?","options":["Set the video to \"made for kids\"","Nothing else","Tick the paid promotion disclosure in YouTube Studio","Hide the brand name in the tags"],"answer":2,"fb":"YouTube has a paid promotion setting so viewers are told about the sponsorship."}],"win":"Excellent. You handled titles, audience settings, premieres, chapters, copyright and disclosure, the practical decisions behind professional publishing.","title":"YouTube upload decisions (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Live streaming: setup and best practice","html":"<p><b>Live streaming</b> is broadcasting video to an audience in real time over the Internet. This includes streaming on social media platforms and apps. Four platforms are compared below.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Platform</th><th>Best for</th><th>Setup notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Facebook Live</b></td><td>Community events, churches, news, local businesses; older audiences</td><td>Go live from the app, or use Live Producer with a <b>stream key</b> in streaming software. Can stream to a Page, profile or Group.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Instagram Live</b></td><td>Informal behind-the-scenes, Q&amp;A, product launches; younger audiences</td><td>Phone app, vertical 9:16; invite a guest to co-host; save the replay to the profile.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Twitch</b></td><td>Gaming, esports, creative and music streams; long sessions</td><td>Usually a computer with <b>OBS Studio</b> and a stream key; chat-heavy community.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>TikTok Live</b></td><td>Short-form audiences, creators, live selling</td><td>Phone app; TikTok sets a minimum age and an account requirement (such as follower numbers) to go live, so check the current rules.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Setup checklist</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Connection</b>: test the <b>upload</b> speed, not just download. Use wired or strong Wi-Fi; have mobile data as a backup.</li>\n<li><b>Equipment</b>: phone or camera on a <b>tripod</b>, an external microphone (audio problems lose viewers faster than video problems), light facing the speaker.</li>\n<li><b>Software</b> (for computers): OBS Studio, set up scenes (camera, slides, logo), paste the platform's <b>stream key</b>, choose a sensible resolution and bitrate for your upload speed (720p is often safer than 1080p on slower connections).</li>\n<li><b>Test</b>: do a private or unlisted test stream first.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Best practice</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Promote</b> the time in advance; start with a hook; repeat the topic for late joiners.</li>\n<li>Have a <b>moderator</b> manage the chat, remove abuse and pass questions to the host.</li>\n<li>Respect <b>privacy</b>: get consent before streaming people's faces, especially children.</li>\n<li>Avoid <b>copyrighted music</b> in the background; platforms can mute or cut the stream.</li>\n<li>End with a call to action, then <b>save and repurpose</b> the replay (highlights, Reels, a podcast episode).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>When the Island Pulse Media live-streamed a Crop Over concert on Facebook Live for a client, Keisha moderated comments while Malik monitored the stream health and audio levels.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Explain best practice\" needs a reason for every action: \"Use a moderator <b>because</b> live chat cannot be edited beforehand and abuse can appear in seconds.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Live streaming means broadcasting video in real time. Facebook Live suits community events, Instagram Live suits informal Q&amp;As, Twitch suits gaming, and TikTok Live suits short-form audiences. Test your upload speed, use a tripod and an external mic, run a private test, and have a moderator watch the chat. Avoid copyrighted background music, which can get a stream muted.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Live content removes the safety net of editing, so <b>planning replaces post-production</b>. Upload speed, not download speed, decides quality; streaming at 720p on a modest connection is better than a 1080p stream that keeps freezing. Sound matters more than picture, because viewers tolerate soft video but leave when they cannot hear. Moderation is a duty of care: abusive comments appear in seconds and are seen by everyone, including children. Consent matters too, especially when filming crowds at a Gros Islet street party. Finally, a stream's value continues after it ends: the replay can be cut into highlights, Reels or a podcast episode, extending reach.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Setting up a live stream","intro":"<p>Malik is preparing to live-stream the Gros Islet Friday night street party for a St. Lucia tourism client using OBS Studio. Put his steps in the correct order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Agree the purpose, platform and time with the client, and promote the stream in advance."},{"t":"Test the upload speed and set up the camera on a tripod with an external microphone."},{"t":"Build scenes in OBS Studio (camera, logo, captions) and paste the platform's stream key."},{"t":"Run a private test stream and check audio, video and chat."},{"t":"Go live, with Keisha moderating the chat and answering questions."},{"t":"End with a call to action, save the replay and cut highlight clips."},{"t":"Review the stream analytics (peak viewers, watch time) to plan the next one."}],"win":"Correct. Plan, set up, test, stream with moderation, repurpose, then review: the full live-streaming workflow.","tutor":[{"h":"Never go live untested","p":"<p>The private test comes before the public stream.</p>"},{"h":"After the stream","p":"<p>Repurposing comes before reviewing data for next time.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Live stream steps (Foundation)","intro":"Put the four steps in order.","blocks":[{"t":"Plan and promote the stream."},{"t":"Set up equipment and software."},{"t":"Run a test stream."},{"t":"Go live with a moderator."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>A fuller workflow. Put Malik's ten steps for the Gros Islet street party stream in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Agree the purpose, platform and time with the client, and ask the featured vendors for consent to be filmed."},{"t":"Promote the stream time in advance on the client's pages."},{"t":"Test the upload speed and set up the camera on a tripod with an external mic and a light."},{"t":"Build scenes in OBS Studio and paste the platform's stream key, choosing 720p for the connection."},{"t":"Run a private test stream and check audio, video and chat."},{"t":"Go live with a strong hook while Keisha moderates the chat."},{"t":"Halfway through, repeat the topic for viewers who joined late."},{"t":"End with a call to action and save the replay."},{"t":"Cut highlight clips from the replay for Reels."},{"t":"Review peak viewers and watch time to plan the next stream."}],"win":"Correct. Consent and promotion first, then set-up and testing, a moderated stream with a reminder for late joiners, then repurposing and review.","title":"Setting up a live stream (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Benefits and limitations of online platforms","html":"<p>These are the factors to weigh when you <b>evaluate the usefulness</b> of a platform: cost, ease of use, audience reach, ownership, accessibility, analytics, monetisation, platform rules, privacy, security, copyright and content control.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Benefits</th><th>Limitations</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Low cost</b>: free or cheap to start; no servers to buy.</td><td><b>Platform dependency</b>: if the platform closes, changes its terms or bans you, you can lose your audience overnight.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Ease of use</b>: templates, drag-and-drop, upload from a phone.</td><td><b>Algorithm changes</b>: a change to the feed can cut your reach sharply without warning.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Audience reach</b>: billions of existing users and built-in search and recommendations.</td><td><b>Content ownership and control</b>: terms of service grant the platform a licence to your content; you may be unable to export followers or comments.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Analytics</b>: free data on views, watch time, demographics, traffic sources.</td><td><b>Platform rules</b>: community guidelines can remove content or restrict accounts, sometimes by automated mistake.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Monetisation tools</b>: ads, memberships, tips, shops, subscriptions.</td><td><b>Copyright strikes</b>: on YouTube, a valid copyright takedown gives a strike; three active strikes can lead to the channel being terminated.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Community features</b>: comments, groups, live chat, shares.</td><td><b>Privacy and security</b>: data collection about you and your audience; hacked accounts; harassment.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Accessibility features</b>: auto-captions, alt text tools, screen-reader support.</td><td><b>Monetisation gaps</b>: some programmes have thresholds or are not available in every territory.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Owned vs rented media.</b> Island Pulse Media explains it to clients as \"renting land versus owning land\". A social media account is <b>rented</b>: convenient, but the landlord sets the rules. A self-hosted website and an email list are <b>owned</b>. A sensible strategy uses rented platforms for reach and sends people to owned channels for the long term.</p>\n<p><b>Caribbean angle.</b> Small Caribbean audiences can make monetisation thresholds hard to reach, and some payment services and creator programmes have been unevenly available across territories. Creators often combine platform income with local sponsorships from businesses such as hotels, restaurants and telecom providers.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Evaluate the usefulness\" questions need <b>both sides plus a judgement</b>. A strong structure: two benefits, two limitations, then \"Overall, the platform is useful for ... provided that ...\".</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Online platforms are cheap, easy to use, reach huge audiences, and give free analytics and ways to earn money. But they have limits: you depend on the platform, algorithms can cut your reach, you do not own your followers, rules can remove content, and copyright strikes can close a channel. Use social media for reach and send people to a website or email list you own.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>\"Renting versus owning land\" is a risk-management idea. Rented platforms give reach quickly, but the landlord can change the rules; owned channels grow more slowly but cannot be taken away. The strongest strategies <b>diversify</b>: two or more platforms for reach, plus an owned website and email list for the relationship. Some limitations are especially Caribbean: monetisation thresholds are harder to reach with small populations, and creator programmes or payment services may not be available in every territory, which pushes creators towards local sponsorship. In evaluation answers, weigh benefits against limitations for the specific client and finish with a conditional judgement using \"provided that\".</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Benefit or limitation?","intro":"<p>Keisha is preparing a report for the Grande Riviere Turtle Watch in Trinidad on using social media platforms. Sort each point.</p>","main":"Is this a benefit or a limitation of using an online platform?","bins":["Benefit","Limitation"],"items":[{"t":"A feed algorithm change halves the reach of the group's posts overnight.","bin":1,"fb":"Algorithm changes are outside your control: a limitation."},{"t":"Live comments let residents ask questions during a beach clean-up stream.","bin":0,"fb":"Community features are a benefit."},{"t":"The account is hacked because two-factor authentication was not turned on.","bin":1,"fb":"Security risks are a limitation that must be managed."},{"t":"A nesting-season post is shared by visitors in Canada and the Netherlands.","bin":0,"fb":"Global audience reach is a major benefit."},{"t":"The group cannot download a list of its followers' contact details.","bin":1,"fb":"You do not own the audience on a rented platform."},{"t":"A video is muted because it used a popular song without a licence.","bin":1,"fb":"Copyright rules and claims limit what you can publish."},{"t":"Free analytics show which turtle videos are watched longest.","bin":0,"fb":"Built-in analytics are a key benefit of platforms."},{"t":"Volunteers can post from their phones in seconds.","bin":0,"fb":"Ease of use is a benefit."}],"win":"Well sorted. You weighed reach, ease, analytics and community against algorithm changes, copyright, ownership and security, which is the core of evaluating usefulness.","tutor":[{"h":"Who is in control?","p":"<p>Points where the platform decides for you are usually limitations.</p>"},{"h":"Free tools","p":"<p>Things the platform gives you at no cost are usually benefits.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Benefit or limitation? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Free analytics on views.","bin":0,"fb":"Analytics are a benefit."},{"t":"An algorithm change cuts reach.","bin":1,"fb":"A limitation outside your control."},{"t":"Posts reach people worldwide.","bin":0,"fb":"Reach is a benefit."},{"t":"A copyright strike on the channel.","bin":1,"fb":"A limitation."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder points for Sea Turtle Watch's report. Decide whether each is a benefit or a limitation of relying on online platforms.</p>","items":[{"t":"Free auto-captions that volunteers can correct before publishing.","bin":0,"fb":"Built-in accessibility tools are a benefit."},{"t":"The platform's terms give it a licence to use the group's uploaded videos.","bin":1,"fb":"Content ownership and control are reduced on rented platforms."},{"t":"The creator monetisation programme is not available in the group's territory.","bin":1,"fb":"Uneven availability of monetisation is a real limitation in parts of the Caribbean."},{"t":"A built-in shop lets visitors buy \"adopt a nest\" certificates.","bin":0,"fb":"Monetisation tools are a benefit."},{"t":"Automated moderation removes a hatching video, mistaking it for graphic content.","bin":1,"fb":"Platform rules can remove content by automated mistake."},{"t":"Insights show that 60% of viewers are in the Netherlands.","bin":0,"fb":"Analytics reveal the audience, guiding content and timing."},{"t":"The group cannot export its comments and followers when it moves to another platform.","bin":1,"fb":"Lock-in: the audience is not owned and cannot be moved."},{"t":"A local hotel sponsors the series using the platform's branded-content tools.","bin":0,"fb":"Sponsorship tools support monetisation."},{"t":"A volunteer's reused password lets a hacker take over the page.","bin":1,"fb":"Account security is a risk that must be managed."},{"t":"A live stream from the beach reaches viewers on three continents.","bin":0,"fb":"Global reach is a major benefit."}],"win":"Strong evaluation. You weighed accessibility, analytics, monetisation and reach against ownership, availability, automated rules and security.","title":"Benefit or limitation? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"mcq","title":"Platform dependency","q":"Speightstown Kitchen sells fish cakes and bakes and takes all its orders through one social media account. The account is suspended by mistake for a week. Which action would MOST reduce this risk in future?","options":[{"t":"Turn off comments on all posts.","fb":"This reduces community engagement and does nothing about dependency."},{"t":"Buy followers to increase the account's size.","fb":"Bought followers breach platform rules and increase the risk of suspension."},{"t":"Build an owned channel, such as a simple website with an order form and an email or WhatsApp list, and link to it from social media.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Owned channels reduce platform dependency because the business controls them even if one platform suspends the account."},{"t":"Post more often on the same account.","fb":"More posts do not protect against suspension; the business still depends on one platform."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Rented vs owned","p":"<p>What does the business control that the platform cannot switch off?</p>"},{"h":"Spread the risk","p":"<p>One channel is a single point of failure.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Platform dependency (Foundation)","q":"A business relies on one social account that gets suspended. What best reduces this risk?","options":[{"t":"Post more often.","fb":"The business still depends on one platform."},{"t":"Also build an owned website and customer list.","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Owned channels cannot be switched off by the platform."},{"t":"Buy followers.","fb":"That breaks platform rules."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A Montserrat craft co-operative gets 90% of its sales from one Instagram account. It has a small budget and one volunteer with basic skills. Which strategy BEST reduces platform dependency while staying realistic?","options":[{"t":"Post three times as often on Instagram.","fb":"More posts increase dependency rather than reducing it."},{"t":"Build a custom app with its own social network.","fb":"Far beyond the budget and skills available."},{"t":"Keep Instagram for reach, add a low-cost hosted website with an order form and an email list, link to them in every post, and keep copies of all content.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It keeps the reach of the rented platform, builds owned channels the co-operative controls, and is realistic for one volunteer."},{"t":"Delete the Instagram account and use only a self-hosted website.","fb":"This removes the main source of reach and sales, and self-hosting is too demanding for one volunteer."}],"title":"Platform dependency (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Maintaining content on online platforms","html":"<p>Then comes <b>maintenance</b>: publishing is not the end of the job. Content on any platform must be kept <b>accurate, secure, working and relevant</b>. This links to content management techniques in Unit 10.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Task</th><th>What to do</th><th>Platform example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Update</b></td><td>Correct facts, prices, dates and opening hours; add an \"Updated on\" note.</td><td>Edit a blog post about ferry times to Anguilla when the timetable changes.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Fix</b></td><td>Check for broken links, missing images and embeds that no longer play.</td><td>Use a broken-link checker on a WordPress site every month.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Secure</b></td><td>Update the CMS, themes and plugins; use strong passwords and two-factor authentication; review who has admin access.</td><td>Remove a former intern's admin access to the client's Instagram.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Back up</b></td><td>Keep regular copies of the site and media off the platform.</td><td>Export WordPress content; keep original video files on cloud storage.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Moderate</b></td><td>Remove spam and abusive comments; answer questions.</td><td>Moderate YouTube comments and hold links for review.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Archive or remove</b></td><td>Unpublish or archive outdated content, or set old videos to Unlisted.</td><td>Archive last year's Carnival schedule so it does not confuse visitors.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Review analytics</b></td><td>Check what performs well and improve or repurpose it.</td><td>Refresh the five most-read posts with new photos and keywords.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>A maintenance schedule</b> keeps it manageable. Island Pulse Media gives every client one:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Weekly</b>: moderate comments, reply to messages, check that scheduled posts went out.</li>\n<li><b>Monthly</b>: update plugins, test forms, check broken links, read analytics.</li>\n<li><b>Quarterly</b>: content audit (update, merge or archive old posts), password and access review, test a backup restore.</li>\n<li><b>Before hurricane season</b>: confirm emergency contact pages are correct and that the site can handle a spike in visitors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Self-hosted platforms need more maintenance from the owner; hosted platforms handle software updates themselves, but you still maintain the <b>content</b>, access and backups.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Your SBA Progress and Process marks reward evidence of ongoing work. Screenshots of a maintenance log (date, task, result) are excellent evidence of incremental progress.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Publishing is not the end of the job. Keep content up to date, fix broken links, keep software secure, back up your work, moderate comments, archive old posts and check analytics. A schedule helps: weekly comment checks, monthly updates and link checks, and a bigger review every three months. Before hurricane season, check that emergency pages are correct.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Maintenance protects three things at once: <b>accuracy</b> (outdated ferry times mislead readers), <b>security</b> (old plugins and forgotten admin accounts are common entry points) and <b>reputation</b> (search engines and readers both reward fresh, working content). A content audit is a judgement exercise: update posts that still attract readers, merge thin posts on the same topic, and archive what is no longer useful, rather than deleting pages that other sites link to. Hosted platforms reduce the technical work but not the content work. A maintenance log, recording date, task and result, turns good intentions into a routine and provides evidence that the work was done.</p>"},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Choosing a platform for a local start-up","intro":"<p>You are advising Georgetown Youth Media Collective, a group of four students who want to publish Caribbean news explainers, short videos and occasional live Q&amp;As. They have a small budget and plan to grow. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>One member says: \"Let's just post everything on TikTok. It's free and everyone is there.\" What do you advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Agree: one free platform is enough.","to":"bad1","fb":"This creates platform dependency. One algorithm change or suspension could wipe out the audience."},{"t":"Use TikTok and YouTube for reach, but also create an owned home such as a website or newsletter.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. You get platform reach while reducing dependency on one platform."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>For the written explainers, which platform do you recommend to start with?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A hosted WordPress.com plan with a custom domain, upgrading to self-hosted WordPress when they need custom features.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. Easy and affordable now, with a clear path to more control later, and the content can be exported."},{"t":"Self-hosted WordPress with ten premium plugins straight away, though no one knows how to maintain it.","to":"bad2","fb":"Self-hosting without maintenance skills creates security and cost risks."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>They plan a live Q&amp;A with a local politician on Facebook Live. What do you add to the plan?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Just go live from a phone in the hallway and see what happens.","to":"bad3","fb":"Without testing or moderation, poor audio and abusive comments could damage the collective's reputation."},{"t":"A test stream, an external microphone, a chat moderator, clear comment rules and no copyrighted background music.","to":"n4","fb":"Excellent. You covered technical quality, moderation and copyright."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Six months later, how should they keep the platforms useful?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Set a maintenance schedule: update facts and plugins, check analytics, back up, and archive outdated explainers.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. Maintenance keeps content accurate, secure and relevant."},{"t":"Stop updating old posts; only new content matters.","to":"bad4","fb":"Outdated explainers spread wrong information and damage authority."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> The collective grows a loyal audience across platforms and owns its email list. A Grade One answer would explain why: you balanced reach and cost against dependency, ownership, security and maintenance.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Audience lost.</b> An algorithm change cuts their views sharply, and they have no other way to reach followers. Try again and add an owned channel.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Site hacked.</b> An outdated plugin is exploited and the site is defaced. Try again and match the platform to their skills.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Live stream trouble.</b> The audio is unclear and offensive comments go unchecked. Try again and plan the stream.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Credibility damaged.</b> Readers find old explainers with wrong figures and stop trusting the site. Try again and plan maintenance.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Reach vs ownership","p":"<p>Free social platforms give reach; owned channels give control.</p>"},{"h":"Skills matter","p":"<p>Choose a platform the team can actually maintain.</p>"},{"h":"Publishing is not the end","p":"<p>Think about what happens months after launch.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Choosing a platform (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: a student news group wants to publish explainers and videos.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Should they rely on one free social platform only?</p>","choices":[{"t":"No. Use social platforms for reach and also build an owned website or newsletter.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. That reduces platform dependency."},{"t":"Yes. One platform is enough.","to":"bad","fb":"One suspension or algorithm change could wipe out their audience."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> You balanced reach with ownership.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> Depending on one platform is risky. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A harder version. The Georgetown Youth Media Collective now has a sponsor, wants to monetise, and one member suggests writing a Wikipedia article about the collective to boost credibility.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>How should the collective spread its content?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Use TikTok and YouTube for reach, a hosted website for explainers, and an email list as the owned channel.","to":"n2","fb":"Excellent. Reach from rented platforms, plus channels the collective owns."},{"t":"Use TikTok only, since that is where the audience is.","to":"bad1","fb":"Total dependency on one platform risks losing the whole audience overnight."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>A member proposes writing a Wikipedia article about the collective. What do you advise?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Write it, citing the collective's own TikTok videos as sources.","to":"bad2","fb":"Self-published sources do not establish notability, and the article would break conflict-of-interest rules."},{"t":"Do not write it: it is a conflict of interest, and the collective is unlikely to be notable yet without independent coverage.","to":"n3","fb":"Correct. Writing about yourself breaks the conflict-of-interest guideline, and notability needs independent reliable sources."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The sponsor, a telecom company, pays for a video series. How should it be published?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Let the sponsor rewrite the scripts.","to":"ok1","fb":"The series is disclosed, but losing editorial control weakens the collective's authority as a news source."},{"t":"Hide the sponsorship so the videos seem independent.","to":"bad3","fb":"Hidden sponsorship breaks platform rules and destroys trust when discovered."},{"t":"Disclose the sponsorship in the video and tick the paid promotion setting, keeping editorial control of the explainers.","to":"n4","fb":"Good. Disclosure protects trust and meets platform rules; editorial independence protects authority."}]},"ok1":{"text":"<p>Viewers comment that the explainers sound like adverts. What do you do?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Agree with the sponsor that it may review facts but not editorial content, and label the series clearly.","to":"n4","fb":"Good recovery. Clear boundaries protect credibility."},{"t":"Delete the critical comments.","to":"bad3","fb":"Censoring criticism deepens the trust problem."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>One year on, a video uses an uncleared song and receives a copyright strike. Old explainers contain outdated figures. What is the best response?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Replace the music with licensed audio, learn the strike rules, and run a quarterly content audit to update or archive old explainers.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Fix the cause of the strike and build maintenance into the routine."},{"t":"Upload the same video again on a second channel.","to":"bad4","fb":"Re-uploading infringing content risks further strikes and can breach the platform's rules on getting around penalties."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Sustainable and trusted.</b> The collective grows across platforms, owns its email list, keeps its authority with disclosed sponsorship, and maintains accurate content. A Grade One answer weighs reach, ownership, credibility, rules and maintenance, and justifies every decision.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Audience lost.</b> An algorithm change cuts reach sharply. Try again with owned channels.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Article deleted.</b> Editors remove the article and flag the conflict of interest. Try again: build independent coverage first.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Trust broken.</b> Viewers discover the hidden or controlled sponsorship and unfollow. Try again with disclosure and independence.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Further penalties.</b> The second channel is flagged too. Try again: fix the cause and maintain content.</p>","end":"bad"}},"title":"Choosing a platform for a local start-up (Challenge)"}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Evaluate the usefulness of online publishing platforms","genre":"Structured response: evaluating online publishing platforms","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\">Guavaberry Grove, a small family business in Tortola, British Virgin Islands, that makes guavaberry jam and liqueur, wants to publish recipes, short videos and a monthly live tasting. The owner is choosing between (1) a free Blogger blog plus a YouTube channel and (2) a paid Squarespace website with an online shop, plus Instagram Live.</p>","prompt":"<p>(a) Distinguish between a <b>hosted free</b> and a <b>hosted paid</b> publishing platform, using the options above. (3 marks)</p><p>(b) Outline TWO settings or features the business should use when publishing its videos on YouTube. (2 marks)</p><p>(c) <b>Evaluate</b> the usefulness of online publishing platforms for this business, discussing at least TWO benefits and TWO limitations, and give a justified recommendation. (10 marks)</p>","min":250,"max":480,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> A hosted free platform such as Blogger ... whereas a hosted paid platform such as Squarespace ...<br><b>(b)</b> First, ... because ... Second, ... because ...<br><b>(c)</b> One benefit is ... For Guavaberry Grove this means ... A second benefit is ... However, a limitation is ... Another limitation is ... Overall, I recommend ... because ... provided that ...</p>","checklist":["I distinguished free and paid hosted platforms on the same features (cost, domain, features).","I named two specific YouTube settings, such as thumbnail, title, captions or visibility.","I gave two benefits and two limitations using syllabus terms (reach, analytics, dependency, ownership, copyright).","I linked my points to guavaberry products and the BVI.","I ended with a clear, justified recommendation."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Knowledge of platforms and YouTube publishing","marks":5,"look":["free|no cost","paid|subscription|monthly","domain|subdomain|blogspot","thumbnail|title|description|tag|metadata","caption|unlisted|public|visibility|chapter"],"min":3,"strong":"You distinguished the platform types clearly and named real YouTube settings.","weak":"Contrast free and paid hosting on cost, domain and features, and name specific YouTube settings such as a 1280 by 720 thumbnail, a keyword title or edited captions."},{"criterion":"Evaluation of benefits and limitations","marks":6,"look":["reach|audience|viewers","analytic|data|insight","monetis|shop|sell|revenue","depend|algorithm","own|control|terms","copyright|privacy|security|rules"],"min":3,"strong":"Your evaluation is balanced and uses precise syllabus terms.","weak":"Include at least two benefits (reach, analytics, monetisation, ease of use) and two limitations (platform dependency, algorithm changes, ownership, copyright, privacy)."},{"criterion":"Application and justified recommendation","marks":4,"look":["guavaberry|jam|liqueur|grove","tortola|bvi|virgin|tourist|visitor|customer","recommend|overall|therefore","because|provided|so that"],"min":3,"strong":"Your recommendation is justified and grounded in the business scenario.","weak":"Apply your points to Guavaberry Grove and finish with \"Overall, I recommend ... because ... provided that ...\"."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> A hosted free platform such as <b>Blogger</b> costs nothing and is run by the provider (Google), but the blog uses a subdomain such as <i>guavaberrygrove.blogspot.com</i> and offers limited design and e-commerce features. A hosted paid platform such as <b>Squarespace</b> charges a subscription, whereas it includes a custom domain (for example <i>guavaberrygrove.com</i>), professional templates and a built-in online shop, with no platform ads.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> First, the business should upload a custom <b>thumbnail</b> at 1280 by 720 pixels showing a bright jar of jam and a few bold words, because thumbnails strongly influence whether people click. Second, it should check and edit the <b>captions</b>, because auto-captions may mishear \"guavaberry\", and accurate captions improve accessibility and search.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> One benefit of online platforms is <b>audience reach</b>. YouTube and Instagram already have huge audiences, so a recipe video for guavaberry jam could be found by visitors planning a trip to the BVI as well as by diaspora customers abroad. A second benefit is <b>analytics and monetisation</b>: the business can see which recipes are watched longest and which countries viewers come from, and the Squarespace shop lets those viewers buy products directly.</p>\n<p>However, a key limitation is <b>platform dependency</b>. If Instagram changes its algorithm or suspends the account by mistake, the monthly live tasting could lose most of its audience. Another limitation is <b>ownership and platform rules</b>: the business does not own its followers, and if a video uses a popular song without a licence it may be muted or receive a <b>copyright strike</b>. Live streams also raise <b>privacy</b> concerns if customers are filmed without consent.</p>\n<p>Overall, I recommend option (2), the <b>Squarespace website with an online shop plus Instagram Live</b>, because Guavaberry Grove needs a professional, owned home that can take orders, while Instagram provides reach. This is useful provided that the owner also posts the recipe videos to YouTube with good metadata, collects an email list through the website, and follows a monthly maintenance schedule to update prices and stock.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>For \"evaluate the usefulness\", examiners look for <b>balance</b> and a <b>conditional judgement</b>. The phrase \"provided that\" shows you understand the limitations and how to manage them. Use the exact syllabus terms (platform dependency, ownership, analytics, monetisation) so the marker can tick them easily.</p>",
 "easy": {"bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: online publishing platforms (9 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part. Use the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Give</b> ONE difference between Blogger (hosted free) and Squarespace (hosted paid). <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> TWO settings to use when uploading the recipe videos to YouTube. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE benefit of using online platforms for the business. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> ONE limitation of using online platforms for the business. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Choose</b> option (1) or (2). <i>(1 mark)</i></li></ol>","min":70,"max":200,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Blogger is ... whereas Squarespace ...<br><b>(b)</b> I would use ... and ...<br><b>(c)</b> One benefit is ... because ...<br><b>(d)</b> One limitation is ... because ...<br><b>(e)</b> I choose option ...</p>","checklist":["I compared Blogger and Squarespace on cost, address or features.","I named two YouTube settings, such as a thumbnail and captions.","I gave one benefit with a reason.","I gave one limitation with a reason.","I chose an option."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Free versus paid","marks":2,"look":["free|no cost","paid|pay|subscription|monthly|domain|shop"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear difference.","weak":"Blogger is free but uses a blogspot address, whereas Squarespace is paid and includes a custom domain and an online shop."},{"criterion":"YouTube settings","marks":2,"look":["thumbnail|title|description|caption|unlisted|public|chapter|tag"],"min":1,"strong":"Settings named.","weak":"Name two settings, such as a 1280 by 720 thumbnail and edited captions."},{"criterion":"Benefit","marks":2,"look":["reach|audience|analytic|cheap|free|easy|sell|monetis","because|so|as"],"min":2,"strong":"Benefit with a reason.","weak":"Give a benefit, such as reaching tourists, and say why it helps."},{"criterion":"Limitation","marks":2,"look":["depend|algorithm|own|rules|copyright|privacy|hack","because|so|as|if"],"min":2,"strong":"Limitation with a reason.","weak":"Give a limitation, such as platform dependency, and say why it is a risk."},{"criterion":"Choice","marks":1,"look":["option|choose|recommend"],"min":1,"strong":"Choice made.","weak":"Say which option you choose."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Blogger is free and uses an address such as guavaberrygrove.blogspot.com, whereas Squarespace is paid and includes a custom domain and an online shop.</p><p><b>(b)</b> I would use a 1280 by 720 thumbnail and edited captions.</p><p><b>(c)</b> One benefit is audience reach, because tourists and people abroad can find the recipe videos.</p><p><b>(d)</b> One limitation is platform dependency, because if the account is suspended the business could lose its followers.</p><p><b>(e)</b> I choose option (2), because the online shop lets customers buy the jam.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Use the key terms from the unit, such as \"reach\" and \"platform dependency\", and add \"because\" to every benefit and limitation. One clear reason earns the second mark.</p>","title":"Evaluate the usefulness of online publishing platforms (Foundation)"},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Structured response: evaluating online publishing platforms, Grade One extension (20 marks)","prompt":"<p>The owner also considers a third option: self-hosted WordPress with WooCommerce. Write a response that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Compares</b> hosted free, hosted paid and self-hosted platforms for this business on cost, control, ownership and maintenance. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Writes</b> a YouTube title, the first two lines of a description and a thumbnail plan for a guavaberry jam recipe video, and <b>justifies</b> each. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explains</b> best practice for the monthly live tasting on Instagram Live. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluates</b> the usefulness of online platforms for Guavaberry Grove, discussing THREE benefits and THREE limitations, including one specific to the Caribbean. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li><b>Recommends</b> a strategy that balances rented and owned channels, with a maintenance plan. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":420,"max":750,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> A hosted free platform ... A hosted paid platform ... whereas self-hosted WordPress ...<br><b>(b)</b> Title: \"...\" because ... Description: \"...\" because ... Thumbnail: ... because ...<br><b>(c)</b> Before the stream, ... During it, ... Afterwards, ...<br><b>(d)</b> One benefit is ... A second ... A third ... However, ... In the Caribbean ...<br><b>(e)</b> I recommend ... Rented ... Owned ... Each month ...</p>","checklist":["I compared all three platform types on cost, control, ownership and maintenance.","My title is short with the keyword first, and my description lines and thumbnail plan are justified.","My live-stream advice covers testing, moderation, consent and copyright.","I evaluated three benefits and three limitations, one of them Caribbean-specific.","My strategy balances rented and owned channels and includes a maintenance plan."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Three platform types compared","marks":4,"look":["free","paid|subscription","self-hosted|wordpress\\.org|woocommerce","control|own|maint|update|security"],"min":4,"strong":"An accurate comparison on all four factors.","weak":"Compare free, paid and self-hosted options on cost, control, ownership and maintenance."},{"criterion":"YouTube metadata justified","marks":4,"look":["title","description","thumbnail","1280|16:9","keyword|search|because"],"min":4,"strong":"Specific, justified metadata.","weak":"Write a keyword-first title under 70 characters, two strong description lines and a 1280 by 720 thumbnail plan, each with a reason."},{"criterion":"Live-stream best practice","marks":3,"look":["test","moderat","consent|permission|licen|copyright|music"],"min":3,"strong":"Best practice with reasons.","weak":"Explain testing, moderation and consent or copyright, with a reason for each."},{"criterion":"Evaluation of benefits and limitations","marks":6,"look":["reach","analytic","monetis|shop|sell","depend|algorithm","own|terms|lock","caribbean|territor|threshold|small audience|availab"],"min":5,"strong":"A balanced evaluation with a Caribbean-specific point.","weak":"Give three benefits and three limitations, including one specific to Caribbean creators, each applied to the business."},{"criterion":"Balanced strategy with maintenance","marks":3,"look":["rented|social|instagram|youtube","owned|website|email list|newsletter","month|week|update|audit|backup"],"min":3,"strong":"A realistic strategy with a maintenance routine.","weak":"Use rented platforms for reach, owned channels for the relationship, and set a regular maintenance schedule."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> A hosted free platform such as Blogger costs nothing and needs no maintenance, but it offers limited design, no proper shop and a blogspot subdomain, and the platform controls the rules. A hosted paid platform such as Squarespace costs a monthly fee, whereas it includes a custom domain, a built-in shop and managed security, although moving away later is difficult. Self-hosted WordPress with WooCommerce gives full control and ownership of the files, database and customer list, but the owner pays for hosting and must handle updates, backups and security, or pay someone to do so.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> Title: \"How to Make Guavaberry Jam | Tortola Recipe\" (43 characters), because it puts the searched phrase first and stays under 70 characters so it is not cut off. Description, first two lines: \"Make real Tortola guavaberry jam in 30 minutes with Guavaberry Grove. Buy our jam and recipes at guavaberrygrove.com.\" These lines appear before \"more\", so they carry the keyword and the call to action. Thumbnail: 1280 by 720, a bright close-up of a jar beside fresh berries, with the words \"Guavaberry Jam\" in bold high-contrast text, because a clear subject and few words stand out in a busy feed and honestly show what the video contains.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Before the stream, the owner should promote the time, test the upload speed and run a short private test with an external microphone, because poor sound loses viewers fastest. During the tasting, a second person should moderate the chat, since abusive or spam comments appear instantly, and anyone shown on camera should have agreed to it. No copyrighted songs should play in the background, as the stream could be muted. Afterwards, the replay can be saved and cut into Reels.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> One benefit is reach: visitors planning trips to the BVI and diaspora customers abroad can find recipe videos. A second is analytics, which show which recipes keep viewers watching. A third is monetisation through the online shop, turning viewers into buyers. However, the business depends on platforms whose algorithms can cut reach overnight. It also does not own its followers, and platform rules or copyright strikes can remove content. In the Caribbean, small local audiences make creator monetisation thresholds hard to reach, and some programmes are not available in every territory, so platform income alone is unreliable.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> I recommend Squarespace as the owned home with the shop and an email sign-up, with YouTube and Instagram as rented channels for reach, every post linking back to the website. Each month the owner should update prices and stock, check analytics and broken links, and back up product photos and videos; every quarter she should review account access and archive outdated recipes.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Part (d) carries the most marks, so make each benefit and limitation specific to Guavaberry Grove. The Caribbean-specific limitation is often missed. Show your judgement in part (e) by explaining how the owned channels reduce the limitations you listed.</p>","title":"Evaluate the usefulness of online publishing platforms (Challenge)"}}]},{"id":"M2U10","title":"Content management and trends in web publishing","objectives":["Define content management (CM), explain why content is stored once in a repository for reuse, and explain the two core parts of a CMS: the Content Management Application (CMA) and the Content Delivery Application (CDA).","Distinguish among Enterprise Content Management (ECM), Document Management Software (DMS), Web Content Management (WCM), Mobile Content Management (MCM), headless CMS and Digital Asset Management (DAM), including the five stages of ECM.","Explain how a headless CMS decouples content from presentation and the role of an Application Programming Interface (API) in multi-channel content delivery.","Compare open source, proprietary and SaaS content management systems, partial and fully cloud CMS, and self-hosted and third-party hosted CMS.","Identify common CMS features and describe content management techniques: creating, editing, organising, tagging, scheduling, approving, publishing, archiving and updating.","Discuss the factors influencing trends in web publishing (commoditisation, pervasiveness of technology, affordability, accessibility, adoption, politics, empowerment) and their impact on creation, design, distribution, user experience and engagement.","Discuss current trends and developments in digital publishing and use research tools to discover and track them."],"videos":[{"title":"What is a CMS?","q":"what is a content management system CMS explained"},{"title":"Headless CMS explained","q":"headless CMS explained simply vs traditional CMS"},{"title":"What is an API?","q":"what is an API explained restaurant waiter analogy"},{"title":"How to use Google Trends","q":"how to use Google Trends for content research beginners"},{"title":"Progressive web apps explained","q":"progressive web apps PWA explained in 100 seconds"}],"screens":[{"type":"learn","title":"What is content management?","html":"<p>Start with one idea: <i>content is only valuable if the right people can find it, trust it and use it.</i> Content management is the discipline that makes that possible.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Content management (CM)</b> is the process for collection, delivery, retrieval, governance and overall management of information in any format. The term usually refers to the administration of the digital content lifecycle, from creation to permanent storage or deletion.</p>\n<p>The content may be text, images, video, audio and multimedia. Content management organises these pieces, together with their tagging schemes (such as XML and HTML), and <b>stores each item only once in a repository</b>. The stored content can then be used again and again (<b>content reuse</b>) and repurposed for <b>multichannel publishing</b>: a website, an app, a newsletter and social media.</p>\n<p><b>Why \"store once\" matters.</b> If a hurricane shelter list is typed separately into a website, an app and a PDF flyer, three copies must be updated every time a shelter opens or closes, and sooner or later one copy will be wrong. Stored once and pulled into each channel, one update corrects every channel at the same moment. This principle is <b>COPE: Create Once, Publish Everywhere</b>.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\">A <b>content management system (CMS)</b> is software that helps users create, manage and modify content on a website without the need for specialised technical knowledge. The CMS handles the basic infrastructure (pages, image storage and so on) so that you can focus on the content and the audience.</p>\n<p><b>The two core parts of a CMS</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Content Management Application (CMA)</b>: the part that lets you add and manage content. It is the dashboard or editor where authors type, upload, edit and approve.</li>\n<li><b>Content Delivery Application (CDA)</b>: the back-end, behind-the-scenes process that takes what you entered in the CMA, stores it properly, combines it with templates and makes it visible to visitors.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Content author (no coding) → CMA → database or repository → CDA → website visitor. A reporter at a national newspaper writes in the CMA while the CDA takes care of layout, menus, page addresses and publishing.</p>\n<p><b>The purpose of content management</b>: organising, storing, editing, publishing, updating and maintaining content so it stays accurate, findable and current. Because the CMS <b>separates content from design</b>, a designer can change the theme of the whole site without retyping a single story.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Distinguish between a CMA and a CDA\" is a common short-answer question. Use one sentence each and the word <b>whereas</b>: \"The CMA is where authors add and manage content, whereas the CDA is the back-end process that stores it and publishes it to visitors.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Content management is looking after content from the day it is made until it is stored or deleted. Each item is stored once in a repository, then reused on a website, an app and a newsletter. This is COPE: Create Once, Publish Everywhere. A CMS has two parts: the CMA, where authors add content, and the CDA, which publishes it for visitors.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>\"Store once\" is a <b>data-integrity</b> principle. Every duplicate copy is a chance for versions to disagree, which is dangerous for safety information such as shelter lists in hurricane season. The CMA and CDA split also explains why a CMS lowers the skill barrier: authors work in the CMA without touching templates, while the CDA applies the design and builds pages. A subtle point is that content management is about <b>governance</b>, not just software: who may publish, how content is approved and when it is retired. A CMS is a tool for that discipline. In exam answers, use \"whereas\" to distinguish CMA and CDA in one sentence.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Content management basics","intro":"<p>The Bonaire Dive Log website runs on a CMS. Match each term to the task or description it shows.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Content Management Application (CMA)","r":"The dashboard where the owner types a new reef post and uploads photos"},{"l":"Content Delivery Application (CDA)","r":"The back-end process that combines the post with the template and shows it to visitors"},{"l":"Repository","r":"The central store where each post and photo is kept only once"},{"l":"Content reuse","r":"Using the same reef description on the website, in the app and in the newsletter"},{"l":"Organising","r":"Putting every reef post into the category \"Dive sites\" with the tag \"beginner\""},{"l":"Separating content from design","r":"Switching to a new theme without retyping any posts"}],"win":"Well done. You can separate the CMA from the CDA, explain the repository and content reuse, and link content management purposes to real tasks.","tutor":[{"h":"Front or back?","p":"<p>Where the author works is one part. What happens behind the scenes to show the page is the other.</p>"},{"h":"Stored once","p":"<p>One central store; many uses of the same item.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Content management basics (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"CMA","r":"Where authors add and manage content"},{"l":"CDA","r":"Back-end process that publishes content to visitors"},{"l":"Repository","r":"Central store where content is kept once"},{"l":"Content reuse","r":"Using the same content in many publications"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>More precise terms. Match each term to what it looks like on the Bonaire Dive Log site.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Content Management Application (CMA)","r":"The editor where a guide types a new reef report and uploads photos"},{"l":"Content Delivery Application (CDA)","r":"The behind-the-scenes process that combines the report with the template and serves it to visitors"},{"l":"Repository","r":"The single store where the reef report and its photos are kept only once"},{"l":"COPE","r":"One update to the reef report changes the website, app and newsletter at the same moment"},{"l":"Multichannel publishing","r":"The same report appearing on the site, in the app, in the newsletter and on social media"},{"l":"Tagging scheme","r":"Labelling every report with dive site, depth and difficulty so it can be filtered"},{"l":"Separation of content and design","r":"A new theme is applied and every report keeps its text and photos"},{"l":"Content lifecycle","r":"A report moves from draft to published to updated, and finally to archive or deletion"}],"win":"Well matched. You can connect each content management term to what actually happens on a working site.","title":"Content management basics (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Types of digital content management and ECM","html":"<p>For just about every category of digital content there is a tool or process for managing it. Here are the main types you must know:</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Type</th><th>Manages</th><th>Typical users</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Enterprise Content Management (ECM)</b></td><td>All of an organisation's information across its whole lifecycle</td><td>Banks, utilities, government departments, hospitals</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Document Management Software (DMS)</b></td><td>Documents and records: storage, versions, approvals, compliance</td><td>Law offices, schools, accounts departments</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Web Content Management (WCM)</b></td><td>Content destined for websites</td><td>News sites, businesses, schools, tourism boards</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Mobile Content Management (MCM)</b></td><td>Secure access to organisational data on mobile devices</td><td>Field staff, sales teams, remote workers</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Headless CMS</b></td><td>Content delivered as data over an API to any output</td><td>Publishers with websites, apps and other channels</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Digital Asset Management (DAM)</b></td><td>Rich media files: photos, video, audio, logos</td><td>Media houses, marketing teams, photographers</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Enterprise Content Management (ECM)</b></p>\n<p>It is not enough simply to \"manage\" content. People must be able to access the <b>correct version</b> of a document or record, and content must help the organisation achieve its business goals.</p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>ECM</b> is the systematic collection and organisation of information that is to be used by a designated audience, such as business executives, staff or customers. ECM is neither a single technology nor a single method nor a single process. It is a dynamic combination of strategies, methods and tools used to <b>capture, manage, store, preserve and deliver</b> information that supports key organisational processes through its entire lifecycle.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Stage</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example at an electricity utility</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Capture</b></td><td>Entering content into the system.</td><td>Scanning a customer's signed connection form; importing meter photos.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Manage</b></td><td>Organising it so it can be found and used: tagging, indexing, routing for approval.</td><td>Tagging the form with the account number and sending it to a supervisor.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Store</b></td><td>Giving it an appropriate home in the organisation's infrastructure.</td><td>Saving it in the customer records repository.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Preserve</b></td><td>Long-term care or archiving, so it can be used as far into the future as needed.</td><td>Keeping records for the legally required years, with off-island backups.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Deliver</b></td><td>Putting information in the right people's hands exactly when they need it.</td><td>An agent sees the form instantly when the customer calls.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Update:</b> In industry, the label \"ECM\" is now often replaced by <b>content services platforms</b> (Gartner's term since about 2016) or <b>intelligent information management</b> (AIIM's term). The five stages and the exam definition above have not changed, so use ECM in CAPE answers.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A typical structured question asks you to explain each of the FIVE stages of ECM using a Caribbean organisation (10 marks). That is two marks per stage: say what the stage means, then give the example.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>There are several kinds of content management. ECM handles all of an organisation's information. A DMS manages documents and records. WCM manages website content. MCM gives staff safe access to data on phones and tablets. A headless CMS sends content to many outputs. DAM manages photos, video and audio. ECM has five stages: capture, manage, store, preserve and deliver.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>ECM is a <b>strategy</b>, not a product: a combination of methods and tools that supports how an organisation works. That is why exam definitions stress \"the right version to the right people at the right time\". The five stages also reveal priorities: preservation matters for legal records that must be kept for years, and off-island backups matter in a hurricane region. Each type answers a different question. DMS asks \"which version is official?\", DAM asks \"may we use this photo?\", MCM asks \"is this data safe on a lost tablet?\" Industry now often uses newer labels such as content services platforms, but the CAPE definitions and the five stages remain what examiners expect.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"The five stages of ECM","intro":"<p>The Leeward Islands Credit Union is handling a member's signed loan application. Put the five ECM stages in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Capture: the signed form is scanned at the St. John's branch and entered into the system."},{"t":"Manage: it is tagged with the member number and routed to a loans officer for approval."},{"t":"Store: the approved form is given a home in the member records repository."},{"t":"Preserve: it is archived and backed up off-island for the legally required number of years."},{"t":"Deliver: when the member calls from Jolly Harbour, the agent sees the correct version instantly."}],"win":"Correct. Capture, manage, store, preserve, deliver: the five stages of the ECM content lifecycle.","tutor":[{"h":"First and last","p":"<p>Content must enter the system first. It ends in the right person's hands.</p>"},{"h":"Short term, long term","p":"<p>Giving it a home comes before protecting it for years.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Stages of ECM (Foundation)","intro":"Put these four ECM stages in order.","blocks":[{"t":"Capture: enter content into the system."},{"t":"Manage and store: tag, route and give it a home."},{"t":"Preserve: archive and protect it long term."},{"t":"Deliver: right people, right time."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>The stage names are removed. A property deed is handled by a land registry. Put the five actions in ECM order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"The signed deed is scanned at the registry counter and entered into the system."},{"t":"It is indexed by parcel number and routed to a legal officer for checking."},{"t":"The checked deed is given a permanent home in the land records repository."},{"t":"It is archived for the long term, with an off-island backup copy."},{"t":"Years later, a notary retrieves the correct version instantly when the property is sold."}],"win":"Correct: capture, manage, store, preserve, deliver. You recognised each stage from what happens, not from its label.","title":"The five stages of ECM (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Types of CMS: DMS, WCM, MCM and DAM","html":"<p>The course outline lists Enterprise CMS, Web CMS, Document CMS, Mobile Content CMS and Headless CMS. The full names are below. Learn these definitions.</p>\n<p><b>Document Management Software (DMS)</b></p>\n<p class=\"passage\">A DMS is a system that helps capture, store and analyse a vast amount of information in a single platform. Its main purpose is to <b>control the lifecycle of documents and maintain regulatory compliance</b>. It acts as a digital filing cabinet, which is why it is sometimes called an <b>e-cabinet filing system</b>.</p>\n<p>Standard features: <b>workflow</b> (routing to the right people for approval), <b>indexing</b> (tagging with author, date, client, subject), <b>versioning</b> (keeping every version and restoring earlier ones) and <b>auditing</b> (recording who opened, changed or deleted a document, and when). A DMS manages documents as whole entities. It is best understood as a <b>sub-category</b> of content management software, which goes further: digital asset management, business process management, email management and more.</p>\n<p><b>Web Content Management (WCM)</b></p>\n<p class=\"passage\">WCM is used to create, manage, store and display content on web pages. A <b>Web Content Management System (WCMS)</b> is a program that helps in maintaining, controlling, changing and reassembling the content on a web page.</p>\n<p>Content is kept in a database and assembled using templates and languages such as XML and PHP. The three <b>key features of WCM</b>: (1) designing and organising websites for efficient access to relevant, up-to-date content; (2) controlling and preparing content for publication, including evaluation and approval; (3) <b>automating</b> key parts of the publishing process.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Update:</b> \".NET\" is often listed alongside XML and PHP. Strictly, .NET is a <b>framework</b>, not a language; WCM systems built on it are usually written in C#. You can write \"PHP or .NET-based systems\" to be precise.</p>\n<p><b>Mobile Content Management (MCM)</b></p>\n<p class=\"passage\">MCM is a set of technologies that provide <b>secure access to corporate data</b> on smartphones, tablets and other endpoint devices.</p>\n<p>Its main component is file storage and sharing. Some services are fully cloud-based; others connect existing repositories to a mobile front end. MCM should include <b>identity management</b>, so IT can control who sees which data and remove access if a device is lost. Example: a Grace Bay hotel's maintenance team opens floor plans on tablets; MCM keeps files current and can wipe a stolen tablet. Microsoft Intune with OneDrive or SharePoint, and Box, are widely used.</p>\n<p><b>Digital Asset Management (DAM)</b> stores, organises and distributes rich media (photos, video, audio, logos), recording metadata, usage rights and licence expiry. Examples: Adobe Experience Manager Assets, Bynder, Canto, Brandfolder.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Some websites use \"mobile content CMS\" to mean a CMS that formats content for apps. For CAPE, use this definition: MCM is about <b>secure access</b> to organisational data on mobile devices.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A DMS is a digital filing cabinet for documents, with workflow, indexing, versioning and auditing. WCM creates and manages the content on web pages. MCM gives staff secure access to company files on phones and tablets, and can wipe a lost device. DAM stores and organises photos, video and audio with details such as who owns them and when licences expire.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The four DMS features map onto <b>accountability</b>: workflow shows who approved a document, versioning shows what changed, auditing shows who opened it, and indexing makes it findable. That is why DMS suits regulated work such as law and banking. DAM's rights metadata prevents a costly error, reusing an image after its licence has expired. MCM is easy to misdefine: for CAPE it is about <b>secure mobile access</b> to organisational data, with identity management and remote wipe, not about formatting websites for phones. When choosing between types, ask what kind of content it is (documents, web pages, rich media or mobile data) and what risk must be controlled.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Which type of content management?","intro":"<p>Clients are asking the Island Pulse Media for advice. Choose the type of content management that best fits each need.</p>","items":[{"text":"A Barbados law firm needs versioning, auditing and regulatory compliance for thousands of contracts.","options":["DMS","MCM","WCM"],"answer":0,"fb":"Controlling the document lifecycle and compliance is the main purpose of a DMS, the \"e-cabinet\"."},{"text":"A Belize City restaurant wants to update its menu pages and blog through a browser.","options":["DAM","WCM","ECM"],"answer":1,"fb":"Content destined for the web is the job of WCM, such as WordPress."},{"text":"A regional telecom company must capture, manage, store, preserve and deliver all its information across departments and islands.","options":["DMS","MCM","ECM"],"answer":2,"fb":"Those are the five ECM stages covering all of an organisation's information."},{"text":"A Carnival committee wants one set of event listings to feed its website, app, airport screens and a smart speaker.","options":["Headless CMS","DMS","WCM"],"answer":0,"fb":"Content delivered as data over an API to many heads is a headless CMS."},{"text":"A cruise line's sales staff need secure access to price sheets on their tablets, with remote wipe if one is lost.","options":["WCM","ECM","MCM"],"answer":2,"fb":"Secure access to corporate data on mobile devices, with identity management, is MCM."},{"text":"A media house needs to tag 40,000 photos with photographer, location and licence expiry.","options":["WCM","DAM","MCM"],"answer":1,"fb":"Managing rich media files with rights metadata is Digital Asset Management."}],"win":"Excellent. You matched each need to ECM, DMS, WCM, MCM, headless or DAM by the kind of content and how it must be used.","tutor":[{"h":"Documents or web pages?","p":"<p>Contracts and compliance point one way; menus and blogs another.</p>"},{"h":"Mobile clue","p":"<p>Secure access on phones and tablets is not about building an app.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Which type? (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A system often called an e-cabinet filing system is...","options":["WCM","DMS"],"answer":1,"fb":"Document Management Software."},{"text":"Secure access to company data on smartphones is...","options":["MCM","DAM"],"answer":0,"fb":"Mobile Content Management."},{"text":"Managing content destined for web pages is...","options":["WCM","ECM"],"answer":0,"fb":"Web Content Management."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder cases with close distractors. Choose the BEST type of content management.</p>","items":[{"text":"A hospital must record who opened each patient consent form, and when, to meet regulations.","options":["DAM","Headless CMS","DMS with auditing","WCM"],"answer":2,"fb":"Auditing who opened or changed a document is a core DMS feature for compliance."},{"text":"A whole government ministry needs one strategy covering capture, storage, preservation and delivery of all its information.","options":["WCM","DMS","ECM","MCM"],"answer":2,"fb":"An organisation-wide lifecycle strategy is ECM; a DMS handles documents only."},{"text":"A tourism board's photographers need to know which images may still be used and when each licence expires.","options":["WCM","DMS","DAM","MCM"],"answer":2,"fb":"Rights and licence-expiry metadata for rich media is DAM."},{"text":"A field engineer's tablet is stolen, and IT must remove company files from it immediately.","options":["MCM","WCM","Headless CMS","ECM"],"answer":0,"fb":"Identity management and remote wipe on mobile devices are MCM features."},{"text":"A school wants teachers to update its website news pages through a browser.","options":["WCM","DMS","MCM","DAM"],"answer":0,"fb":"Creating and managing content for web pages is WCM."},{"text":"A festival wants one set of listings to feed its website, an app and screens at the venue.","options":["MCM","DMS","Headless CMS","Traditional WCM only"],"answer":2,"fb":"Delivering content as data over an API to many outputs is the headless approach."}],"win":"Excellent. You separated close types by the content involved and the problem being solved.","title":"Which type of content management? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Headless CMS: the head and the body","html":"<p class=\"passage\">A <b>headless CMS</b> is a content management system that provides a way to author content, but instead of tying that content to one particular output (such as rendering a web page), it provides the content <b>as data over an API</b>.</p>\n<p>Here is where the name comes from: the <b>\"head\"</b> is where your content ends up; the <b>\"body\"</b> is where your content is stored and authored. The point of a headless CMS is not that you do not want a head. It is that you get to <b>pick and choose which heads</b> (outputs) you send your content to: a website, a mobile app, a smart TV, an airport kiosk, a voice assistant or a newsletter.</p>\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 520 200\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:520px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Headless CMS sends content through an API to a website, a mobile app, airport screens and a voice assistant\">\n  <rect x=\"10\" y=\"70\" width=\"130\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"75\" y=\"95\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"currentColor\">BODY</text>\n  <text x=\"75\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"currentColor\">author + store</text>\n  <rect x=\"190\" y=\"80\" width=\"80\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"230\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"currentColor\">API</text>\n  <line x1=\"140\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"190\" y2=\"100\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <line x1=\"270\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"25\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <line x1=\"270\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"75\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <line x1=\"270\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"125\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <line x1=\"270\" y1=\"100\" x2=\"340\" y2=\"175\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <rect x=\"340\" y=\"10\" width=\"170\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n  <text x=\"425\" y=\"30\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"currentColor\">HEAD: website</text>\n  <rect x=\"340\" y=\"60\" width=\"170\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n  <text x=\"425\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"currentColor\">HEAD: mobile app</text>\n  <rect x=\"340\" y=\"110\" width=\"170\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n  <text x=\"425\" y=\"130\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"currentColor\">HEAD: airport screens</text>\n  <rect x=\"340\" y=\"160\" width=\"170\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\"/>\n  <text x=\"425\" y=\"180\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-size=\"12\" fill=\"currentColor\">HEAD: voice assistant</text>\n</svg>\n<p>In a <b>traditional (coupled)</b> CMS, the body and one head (the website theme) are joined, so content is locked to one output. A headless CMS <b>decouples</b> content from presentation.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Traditional (coupled) CMS</th><th>Headless CMS</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Front end</td><td>Built in; themes and templates included</td><td>None; developers build each front end separately</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Output</td><td>Mainly one website</td><td>Any channel that can call the API</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Skills needed</td><td>Low; ideal for non-technical users</td><td>Higher; needs developers for the front ends</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Flexibility</td><td>Limited by the theme</td><td>Very high; any design or framework</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Examples</td><td>WordPress, Joomla, Wix, Squarespace</td><td>Contentful, Sanity, Strapi; WordPress can also run headless</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Disadvantages</b> of going headless: every front end must be built by developers, editors lose a simple \"preview the page\" view, and it is often more complex and costly for a small site.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> A typical question asks you to compare a traditional CMS with a headless CMS under THREE headings (6 marks). Use these headings (front end, output, skills, flexibility) and give both sides for each: one mark per side.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A headless CMS stores content in a \"body\" and sends it as data through an API to any \"head\", meaning wherever the content appears, such as a website, an app, a smart TV or an airport screen. A traditional CMS joins the body to one head, the website theme. Headless is very flexible, but developers must build every front end.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Headless is a trade-off between <b>flexibility and simplicity</b>. Decoupling lets one story reach many channels and lets developers use any technology, but it removes the built-in theme and the easy page preview that non-technical editors rely on. For a small café with one website, headless adds cost without benefit; for a publisher with a website, app and newsletter, it removes retyping and keeps versions consistent. The line also blurs: WordPress can run as a headless back end. A Grade One answer compares the two on front end, output, skills and flexibility, gives both sides for each, and recommends one for the named client.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"APIs and the one-story, four-heads case","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>API</b> stands for <b>Application Programming Interface</b>, and it is the main participant in all of this interactivity. An API is like a messenger that takes our request to a system and returns a response back to us.</p>\n<p>We use APIs to fetch data for a web application, to connect to a remote server whose data keeps changing (such as weather or flight arrivals at Argyle International Airport) or to let two applications exchange data.</p>\n<p><b>The restaurant analogy.</b> You (the app) sit at a table. The kitchen (the content repository) has what you want, but you cannot walk in. The waiter (the API) takes your order in an agreed format, carries it to the kitchen and brings back your meal. A headless CMS kitchen can serve the website table, the app table and the smart TV table from the same pot.</p>\n<p>A request to a headless CMS API might return the content as data like this (JSON format):</p>\n<pre><code>{\n  \"headline\": \"Hurricane advisory: tropical storm watch\",\n  \"date\": \"2026-09-10\",\n  \"location\": \"Kingstown\",\n  \"summary\": \"Shelters open at 4 p.m.\"\n}</code></pre>\n<p>Each head then decides how to display it: a large banner on the website, a short push notification in the app, a paragraph in the newsletter.</p>\n<p><b>Case study: Island Pulse Media and the newsroom problem</b></p>\n<p>Island Pulse Media has won a contract with a client, the <b>Windward Islands Weekly</b>, a small news publisher in Kingstown, St. Vincent. Its website is a set of hand-coded HTML pages. Every story is emailed to one web developer, who copies it into a page and uploads it. When he is ill, nothing is published. Photos are on three laptops, nobody knows which article version is final, and the app shows different headlines from the website.</p>\n<p>Zara says: \"They do not need a prettier website. They need a system.\" <b>Malik</b> recommends a headless set-up. Now, when a reporter publishes a hurricane advisory in the CMS, the API sends it at once to the website, the app's push notification, the morning email newsletter and a lobby screen in the office. \"Before, the developer pasted it four times,\" Keisha writes. \"Now the reporter publishes once.\"</p>\n<p>Jerome moves 40,000 photos into a <b>DAM</b>, tagged with location (Kingstown, Bequia, Union Island), event, photographer and licence. An editor can now search \"Carnival, J'ouvert, landscape, staff photographer\" and find usable images in seconds, without reusing a freelancer's photo whose licence has expired.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In any headless answer, use three key words: <b>decoupled</b> (content separate from presentation), <b>API</b> (how content is delivered) and <b>multi-channel</b> (why it is useful). Question 3(c) asks for FOUR heads for a Caribbean tourism board: website, app, airport or cruise-port kiosk, smart TV, voice assistant, newsletter.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>An API is like a waiter. An app asks for something, the API carries the request to the system and brings back the answer. A headless CMS uses an API to send one story to a website, an app and a newsletter. A Kingstown newspaper now publishes a hurricane advisory once, and it appears on every channel at the same moment.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The case study shows that the newsroom's real problem was a <b>single point of failure</b>, not design: one developer pasting stories by hand meant illness stopped publishing and versions drifted apart. The API returns structured data (for example JSON fields for headline, date and summary), and each head decides how to display it, which is why structured content matters as much as the CMS itself. Moving photos into a DAM solved a legal risk as well as a search problem, because licence data prevents reuse of expired images. In exam answers, use the three key words: decoupled, API and multichannel, and name four realistic heads.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Why go headless?","q":"St. Lucia Jazz and Arts Festival publishes its programme on a WordPress website. It now wants the same programme to appear in a new mobile app, on digital screens at the venue and in a smart speaker skill, without staff retyping it. Which solution BEST meets this need?","options":[{"t":"A new WordPress theme with bigger fonts.","fb":"A theme only changes how one website looks; it does not deliver content to other channels."},{"t":"Three separate websites, one for each channel.","fb":"This multiplies work and staff would have to retype content, which is the problem to solve."},{"t":"Document Management Software with versioning for the programme PDF.","fb":"A DMS controls document lifecycles, but a PDF does not adapt to an app, screens and a voice assistant."},{"t":"A headless CMS that stores the programme once and delivers it through an API to each head.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Decoupling content from presentation lets one body feed many heads through an API: COPE."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Key phrase","p":"<p>\"The same programme ... many channels ... without retyping.\"</p>"},{"h":"Body and heads","p":"<p>Which CMS delivers content as data so you can pick the heads?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Why go headless? (Foundation)","q":"In a headless CMS, content is provided to outputs through a(n):","options":[{"t":"FTP","fb":"FTP uploads files to a server; it does not serve content to many channels on request."},{"t":"API","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. The API is the messenger that carries content to each head."},{"t":"PDF","fb":"A PDF is a fixed document format, not a delivery messenger."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","q":"A one-person bakery in Grenville, Grenada, wants a simple website with its menu, opening hours and a contact form. The owner has no technical skills and no plans for an app. A consultant recommends a headless CMS \"because it is the modern approach\". Which response BEST evaluates this advice?","options":[{"t":"Agree: headless is always better because it is more flexible.","fb":"Flexibility only matters if it is needed. Here it adds cost and complexity with no benefit."},{"t":"Reject it: a traditional CMS or website builder suits one website and a non-technical owner, whereas headless needs developers to build the front end and suits multichannel publishing.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. The evaluation weighs headless against the client's actual needs, skills and budget."},{"t":"Build three separate websites to be safe.","fb":"This multiplies work and does not address the client's simple need."},{"t":"Choose a DMS instead, because it has versioning.","fb":"A DMS manages document lifecycles; it is not a website solution for a menu and contact form."}],"title":"Why go headless? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Traditional or headless?","intro":"<p>Malik is preparing a comparison for Windward Islands Weekly. Sort each statement using the comparison table.</p>","main":"Does this describe a traditional (coupled) CMS or a headless CMS?","bins":["Traditional (coupled) CMS","Headless CMS"],"items":[{"t":"Very high flexibility: any design or framework.","bin":1,"fb":"Because the front end is separate, developers can use any technology."},{"t":"Themes and templates are built in.","bin":0,"fb":"A coupled CMS includes its own front end (the head) through themes."},{"t":"Output is mainly one website.","bin":0,"fb":"The body and one head are joined, so content is locked to one output."},{"t":"Low skills needed; ideal for non-technical users.","bin":0,"fb":"A traditional CMS lets non-technical users manage a site without developers."},{"t":"Content can go to any channel that can call the API.","bin":1,"fb":"Delivering content as data over an API is what makes headless multi-channel."},{"t":"Contentful, Sanity and Strapi.","bin":1,"fb":"These are headless CMS products that deliver content via API."},{"t":"Wix, Squarespace and Joomla.","bin":0,"fb":"These are coupled systems with built-in themes."},{"t":"Developers build each front end separately.","bin":1,"fb":"A headless CMS has no built-in front end, so each head is built by developers."}],"win":"Well sorted. You can compare traditional and headless CMS under front end, output, skills, flexibility and examples, exactly as Question 3(b) requires.","tutor":[{"h":"Is there a theme?","p":"<p>If the look comes built in, the head is attached.</p>"},{"h":"Who builds the front?","p":"<p>Needing developers for each output points to the decoupled option.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Traditional or headless? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Built-in themes","bin":0,"fb":"Traditional."},{"t":"Content as data over an API","bin":1,"fb":"Headless."},{"t":"Mainly one website","bin":0,"fb":"Traditional."},{"t":"Many heads from one body","bin":1,"fb":"Headless."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder statements, including advantages and disadvantages. Decide whether each describes a traditional or a headless CMS.</p>","items":[{"t":"Editors lose a simple \"preview the page\" view.","bin":1,"fb":"Without a built-in front end, previewing is harder in a headless set-up."},{"t":"Content is locked to one output, the website theme.","bin":0,"fb":"In a coupled CMS, the body and one head are joined."},{"t":"WordPress stores the content while a separate app front end requests it through an API.","bin":1,"fb":"WordPress running as a back end only is a headless set-up."},{"t":"Switching the theme redesigns the whole site in minutes.","bin":0,"fb":"Built-in themes are a strength of traditional CMS."},{"t":"One API request can feed the website, the app and a kiosk.","bin":1,"fb":"Multichannel delivery over an API is the purpose of headless."},{"t":"Ideal for a one-person café with one website.","bin":0,"fb":"For a single site run by a non-technical owner, a traditional CMS is simpler and cheaper."},{"t":"The front end can be rebuilt in any framework without touching the content.","bin":1,"fb":"Decoupling lets front ends change freely."},{"t":"Plugins add features to the built-in front end.","bin":0,"fb":"Plugins extending the theme are typical of coupled systems such as WordPress used traditionally."},{"t":"Higher cost, because each head must be developed.","bin":1,"fb":"Every head needs developer work, a key disadvantage of headless."},{"t":"Non-technical staff can run the whole site alone.","bin":0,"fb":"Low skill requirements are a strength of traditional CMS."}],"win":"Well analysed. You can argue both sides: headless wins on multichannel flexibility, traditional wins on simplicity, preview and cost.","title":"Traditional or headless? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Classifying CMS: open source, proprietary and SaaS","html":"<p class=\"passage\">Content management systems fall into <b>three major types</b>: <b>Open Source CMS</b>, <b>Proprietary CMS</b> and <b>SaaS (Software as a Service) CMS</b>.</p>\n<p><b>Open source CMS</b></p>\n<p>The most popular open source CMS run on <b>PHP</b>, a server-side scripting language. <b>WordPress, Drupal and Joomla</b> are the major examples. They can be used by anyone for any purpose without buying a licence, and customised without special permission.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Lower cost</b>: no contracts, no long-term commitments, no upgrade or licence fees (though hosting, premium themes and developer time still cost money).</li>\n<li><b>Huge ecosystems</b>: numerous free plugins, modules, themes and templates.</li>\n<li><b>SEO-friendly</b>: plugins such as Yoast SEO or Rank Math help sites rank well.</li>\n<li><b>Considerations</b>: the owner is responsible for updates, backups and security; poorly maintained plugins are a common route for hacking.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>WordPress powers more than four in ten of all websites by most industry estimates (check the latest W3Techs figure), which is why it appears so often on Caribbean business, school and media sites.</p>\n<p><b>Proprietary CMS</b></p>\n<p>The owner controls the application; others must purchase a licence or permission. Even licence holders usually cannot change or duplicate the CMS unless they buy a developer's licence. Most run properly only when hosted by the CMS owner.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Disadvantages</b>: licence costs; limited hosting options; <b>lack of portability</b> (vendor lock-in), the main reason many small businesses avoid proprietary CMS.</li>\n<li><b>Advantages</b>: professional support, vendor-managed security, features tailored to large organisations.</li>\n<li><b>Examples</b>: Microsoft SharePoint, Kentico, Sitecore. Shopify is also proprietary, delivered as SaaS.</li>\n</ul>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Update:</b> It is often said that many web hosts do not support proprietary systems. Today most large proprietary CMS (for example Sitecore and Kentico) are sold mainly as cloud services run by the vendor itself, so the limit is less about finding a host and more that you cannot choose your host at all.</p>\n<p><b>SaaS CMS</b> bundles WCM software, web hosting and technical support in a cloud subscription, priced per site or per user. <b>Wix, Squarespace, Shopify and Webflow</b> are popular examples (more on the next screens).</p>\n<p>The Island Pulse Media recommends Drupal for a regional government portal needing strict permissions and pages in English, Dutch, French and Papiamentu, but Wix for a Bequia café that just needs a menu and a booking button.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Do not confuse <b>open source</b> with <b>free of cost</b>. Open source means the <i>code</i> is open. You may still pay for hosting, themes or a developer.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>There are three kinds of CMS. Open source CMS, such as WordPress, Drupal and Joomla, are free to use and change. Proprietary CMS, such as SharePoint, need a paid licence and the owner controls the code. SaaS CMS, such as Wix and Squarespace, are cloud subscriptions with hosting and support included. A Bequia café might choose Wix for its menu.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The classification is about <b>licence and control</b>, which drive cost patterns and risks. Open source shifts costs from licences to people: hosting, developer time and the owner's responsibility for updates and security, which is why badly maintained plugins are a common hacking route. Proprietary systems bring vendor support and enterprise features but also lock-in; today many are sold as vendor-run cloud services, so you cannot choose your host at all. SaaS trades control for convenience and predictable billing. Note the product-family trap: WordPress.org is open source software, whereas WordPress.com is a SaaS service. Recommend by matching skills, budget and control needs to the client.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Open source, proprietary or SaaS?","intro":"<p>Jerome is preparing notes for a client in Antigua. Sort each statement into the three CMS classifications.</p>","main":"Which classification does this describe?","bins":["Open source CMS","Proprietary CMS","SaaS CMS"],"items":[{"t":"Microsoft SharePoint, used under a paid licence.","bin":1,"fb":"SharePoint is owned by Microsoft and used under licence: proprietary."},{"t":"Drupal, which a developer can download and customise without special permission.","bin":0,"fb":"Open source CMS can be used and customised by anyone without a licence."},{"t":"Squarespace: the supplier handles maintenance and upgrades in real time.","bin":2,"fb":"With SaaS, the supplier handles maintenance, upgrades and technical issues."},{"t":"Webflow: priced per site, hosted in the cloud.","bin":2,"fb":"Subscription priced per site or user, hosted in the cloud, is SaaS."},{"t":"Wix: a monthly subscription that includes hosting and support.","bin":2,"fb":"Software, hosting and support bundled in a cloud subscription is SaaS."},{"t":"Even licence holders cannot change the code without a developer's licence.","bin":1,"fb":"The owner controls a proprietary application."},{"t":"Sitecore, with professional vendor support for large enterprises.","bin":1,"fb":"Vendor support and enterprise features are advantages of proprietary CMS."},{"t":"Thousands of free plugins and themes written by a worldwide community.","bin":0,"fb":"Because anyone can develop for open source CMS, the ecosystem is huge."},{"t":"Joomla, which runs on PHP.","bin":0,"fb":"The most popular open source CMS (WordPress, Drupal, Joomla) run on PHP."}],"win":"Well sorted. Open source means free licence and open code; proprietary means a paid licence and owner control; SaaS means a cloud subscription with hosting and support bundled.","tutor":[{"h":"Licence or subscription?","p":"<p>A paid licence points one way; a monthly plan with hosting included points another.</p>"},{"h":"Can you change it?","p":"<p>Customising without permission is the mark of open code.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Open source, proprietary or SaaS? (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"WordPress","bin":0,"fb":"Open source."},{"t":"Microsoft SharePoint","bin":1,"fb":"Proprietary."},{"t":"Wix","bin":2,"fb":"SaaS."},{"t":"Joomla","bin":0,"fb":"Open source."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Trickier products and descriptions. Sort by licence and delivery model.</p>","items":[{"t":"WordPress.org software downloaded and installed on your own hosting.","bin":0,"fb":"The WordPress software itself is open source."},{"t":"A WordPress.com Business plan, hosted and supported by the provider for a monthly fee.","bin":2,"fb":"WordPress.com is a hosted service, so this is SaaS."},{"t":"Shopify, a cloud subscription for online stores.","bin":2,"fb":"Shopify is proprietary software delivered as SaaS."},{"t":"No licence fee, but you pay for hosting and a developer's time.","bin":0,"fb":"Open source is free to license, not free to run."},{"t":"A paid enterprise licence, with the vendor deciding where it can be hosted.","bin":1,"fb":"Owner control and licence fees mark a proprietary CMS."},{"t":"Kentico, licensed to a large company with vendor support.","bin":1,"fb":"Kentico is a proprietary CMS."},{"t":"Strapi, a headless CMS whose code anyone can download and modify.","bin":0,"fb":"Strapi's code is open source."},{"t":"Webflow, priced per site and hosted in the cloud.","bin":2,"fb":"Per-site subscription with cloud hosting is SaaS."},{"t":"Drupal, chosen for a multilingual government portal with strict permissions.","bin":0,"fb":"Drupal is open source."},{"t":"Microsoft SharePoint, used under licence for an internal school portal.","bin":1,"fb":"SharePoint is proprietary."}],"win":"Well sorted. You looked past the product name to the licence and the delivery model, including the WordPress.org and WordPress.com split.","title":"Open source, proprietary or SaaS? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"SaaS CMS and hosting choices","html":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>SaaS (Software as a Service) CMS</b> solutions usually bundle WCM software, web hosting and technical support. They are virtual solutions based on a <b>subscription model</b> and hosted in the cloud, usually priced per site or per user. The price generally includes storage, data transfer and ongoing support.</p>\n<p><b>Two kinds of cloud CMS</b></p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Partial cloud CMS</b>: sits on <b>your own cloud web server</b>. It offers greater flexibility because you can change its functionality by altering the source code or adding modules.</li>\n<li><b>Fully cloud CMS</b>: comes as part of a service or package. These are proprietary systems under the supplier's control, so it is not always possible to customise them to your requirements.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Benefits of cloud CMS for small and medium-sized businesses</b></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Costs are usually low; a small set-up fee generally covers basic implementation.</li>\n<li>The supplier handles maintenance, upgrades and technical issues.</li>\n<li>The software is accessible from any laptop, computer or mobile device with internet.</li>\n<li>New features and upgrades are available in real time.</li>\n<li>Packages are easily scalable: add sites or users as needs change.</li>\n<li>Content is stored off-island in data centres, supporting continuity during hurricanes and power outages, a lesson many Caribbean businesses learned after Hurricane Irma.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Self-hosted compared with third-party hosted</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th></th><th>Self-hosted</th><th>Third-party hosted</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Meaning</td><td>You install the CMS on a server you rent or own.</td><td>The provider runs the software and hosting for you.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Control</td><td>Full control of files, database, plugins and data.</td><td>Limited to what the provider allows.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Responsibility</td><td>You manage updates, backups, security, uptime.</td><td>The provider manages them.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cost pattern</td><td>Hosting fee plus your or a developer's time.</td><td>Predictable subscription.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Example</td><td>WordPress.org on a hosting account</td><td>WordPress.com, Wix, Squarespace</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Comparing the three types</b></p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Open source</th><th>Proprietary</th><th>SaaS</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Licence cost</td><td>Free</td><td>Paid licence</td><td>Monthly or annual subscription</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hosting</td><td>Self-hosted or any host</td><td>Usually the vendor's host</td><td>Included, in the cloud</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Customisation</td><td>Full access to code</td><td>Limited without developer licence</td><td>Limited to platform options</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Maintenance and security</td><td>Owner's responsibility</td><td>Vendor</td><td>Vendor</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Portability</td><td>High</td><td>Low</td><td>Low to medium</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Organisations with some technical skill wanting control</td><td>Large enterprises with budgets and support needs</td><td>Small businesses wanting speed and simplicity</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Question 2(c) asks you to discuss FIVE benefits of cloud CMS for a small Caribbean business (5 marks). Pick five from the list above and tie each to the business, for example \"off-island storage keeps a Bay Street boutique's online shop running after a storm.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A SaaS CMS is a cloud subscription that includes the software, hosting and support. A partial cloud CMS sits on your own cloud server, so you can change its code. A fully cloud CMS comes as a package that the supplier controls. Small businesses like cloud CMS because costs are low, the supplier handles updates, and content is stored safely off-island.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The partial-versus-fully cloud distinction is about <b>who controls the code</b>. Partial cloud keeps flexibility for custom features, such as a berth-booking module, but brings back some maintenance work; fully cloud removes that work but limits customisation to what the supplier allows. Self-hosting versus third-party hosting is the same trade-off at a different level: control and responsibility versus convenience and predictable cost. Off-island data centres are a genuine Caribbean advantage, because they keep a shop online when local power and networks fail after a storm, but they depend on Internet access and on the supplier staying in business. Tie each benefit to the named business.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"Partial or fully cloud?","q":"Jolly Harbour Yacht Services wants a cloud CMS installed on its own cloud web server so that its developer can alter the source code and add a custom berth-booking module. Which type of cloud CMS is this?","options":[{"t":"A headless CMS","fb":"Headless describes how content is delivered (over an API), not whether a cloud CMS is on your own server."},{"t":"Document Management Software","fb":"A DMS controls document lifecycles; it is not a type of cloud CMS for a booking site."},{"t":"A partial cloud CMS","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. A partial cloud CMS sits on your own cloud web server and can be changed by altering the code or adding modules."},{"t":"A fully cloud CMS","fb":"A fully cloud CMS comes as a package under the supplier's control, so custom code is not usually possible."}],"tutor":[{"h":"Whose server?","p":"<p>\"Its own cloud web server\" is the key phrase.</p>"},{"h":"Can it be changed?","p":"<p>One kind of cloud CMS lets you alter the source code; the other is under the supplier's control.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Partial or fully cloud? (Foundation)","q":"A cloud CMS that comes as a package under the supplier's control is a:","options":[{"t":"Open source CMS","fb":"Open source CMS can be customised freely, the opposite of supplier control."},{"t":"Fully cloud CMS","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Fully cloud CMS are proprietary packages the supplier controls."},{"t":"Partial cloud CMS","fb":"A partial cloud CMS sits on your own server and can be modified."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","q":"Client A, a yacht services company, wants a cloud CMS on its own cloud server so its developer can add a custom berth-booking module. Client B, a Redcliffe Quay boutique, wants zero maintenance, a predictable monthly fee and no custom code. Which pairing is correct?","options":[{"t":"A: partial cloud CMS. B: fully cloud CMS.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Partial cloud allows code changes on your own server; fully cloud is a supplier-controlled package with maintenance included."},{"t":"Both should use a fully cloud CMS.","fb":"Client A's custom module needs access to the code, which fully cloud packages usually do not allow."},{"t":"Both should use a headless CMS.","fb":"Headless describes how content is delivered, not where the CMS sits or who maintains it."},{"t":"A: fully cloud CMS. B: partial cloud CMS.","fb":"Reversed. A fully cloud CMS rarely allows custom code, and partial cloud would give the boutique maintenance work it does not want."}],"title":"Partial or fully cloud? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Common CMS features and popular systems","html":"<p>Activity 2: <i>CMSs have common features. Provide a list of these and expound on their usefulness.</i> Here is the list.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Feature</th><th>Usefulness</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>WYSIWYG / block editor</b></td><td>Authors format content visually (\"What You See Is What You Get\") without writing HTML.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Templates and themes</b></td><td>Separate design from content, so the site keeps a consistent look and can be redesigned without retyping.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>User roles and permissions</b></td><td>Control who can write, edit, approve or publish (contributor, author, editor, administrator).</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Workflow and approval</b></td><td>Route content through review before it goes live, preventing errors and unauthorised posts.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Version control and revisions</b></td><td>Track changes and restore earlier versions after mistakes.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Media library</b></td><td>Store, resize and reuse images, audio and video.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Search and metadata</b></td><td>Categories, tags and custom fields make content findable for users and search engines.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>SEO tools</b></td><td>Edit page titles, meta descriptions, friendly URLs and sitemaps.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Scheduling</b></td><td>Publish or unpublish automatically at a set date and time.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Plugins / extensions</b></td><td>Add forms, e-commerce, event calendars and analytics.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Multilingual support</b></td><td>Manage translations for English, Dutch, Spanish, French and Papiamentu audiences.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Responsive output</b></td><td>Content displays correctly on phones, tablets and desktops.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Security, backups and updates</b></td><td>Protect the site and allow recovery after failures or attacks.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Analytics integration</b></td><td>Measure what audiences read, for how long and from where.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Popular content management systems</b> (Activity 1 asks you to research 5 to 10 of these):</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>CMS</th><th>Type</th><th>Particular use</th></tr>\n<tr><td>WordPress</td><td>Open source (also SaaS via WordPress.com)</td><td>Blogs, business, school and news sites</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Drupal</td><td>Open source</td><td>Government and large institutional sites</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Joomla</td><td>Open source</td><td>Community and organisation portals (built-in multilingual)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wix / Squarespace</td><td>SaaS</td><td>Small businesses, portfolios, restaurants, boutique hotels</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Shopify</td><td>SaaS (proprietary)</td><td>Online stores</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Webflow</td><td>SaaS</td><td>Designers and agencies</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Ghost</td><td>Open source and hosted</td><td>Independent publishers: built-in newsletters and paid memberships</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Contentful / Sanity / Strapi</td><td>Headless</td><td>Multichannel publishers and app developers</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Microsoft SharePoint</td><td>Proprietary</td><td>Internal company and school portals</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For \"identify FOUR common CMS features and explain the usefulness of each\", name the feature, then say what problem it solves. A free WordPress.com, Wix or Ghost trial gives you real screenshots for Activity 2 and your SBA.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Most CMSs share common features: a visual editor, templates, user roles, approval workflow, version history, a media library, tags and search, SEO tools, scheduling, plugins, multilingual support, phone-friendly output, backups and analytics. Each one solves a problem. Scheduling, for example, lets an Emancipation Day feature go live at 6 a.m. on 1 July while staff are asleep.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Features are best explained as <b>problem and solution</b> pairs, because that is what \"explain the usefulness\" means. Several features work together for quality control: roles limit who can publish, workflow routes drafts for checking, and versioning allows recovery when mistakes slip through. Choosing a CMS means weighting the features that matter for the client: a multilingual government portal needs strong permissions and translations, while an independent writer may value built-in newsletters and memberships, as in Ghost. Plugins extend features but add risk, since each one must be maintained. When researching popular systems, record each system's type and its particular strength for a named use.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the CMS feature","intro":"<p>Maya at the Island Pulse Media is training the Windward Islands Weekly staff. Match each problem to the CMS feature that solves it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"WYSIWYG / block editor","r":"Reporters cannot write HTML but need to add headings and photos"},{"l":"User roles and permissions","r":"A trainee must be able to write stories but not publish them"},{"l":"Version control and revisions","r":"An editor deleted a paragraph by mistake and needs yesterday's version"},{"l":"Scheduling","r":"The Emancipation Day feature must go live at 6 a.m. on 1 July"},{"l":"Multilingual support","r":"Readers want stories in English, Dutch and Spanish"},{"l":"SEO tools","r":"Stories need better page titles and meta descriptions for search engines"},{"l":"Security, backups and updates","r":"The site must be recoverable after an attack or a storm"}],"win":"Well matched. You can identify common CMS features and explain the usefulness of each, as in a short-answer question.","tutor":[{"h":"Who may do what?","p":"<p>Limiting what a person can do is about roles.</p>"},{"h":"Time and history","p":"<p>Going back in time and going live in the future are two different features.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the CMS feature (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"WYSIWYG editor","r":"Format content without writing HTML"},{"l":"Scheduling","r":"Publish automatically at a set time"},{"l":"Media library","r":"Store and reuse images and video"},{"l":"Version control","r":"Restore an earlier version"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Subtler problems at the Windward Islands Weekly. Match each problem to the feature that solves it.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Workflow and approval","r":"Every draft must pass a sub-editor before it can go live"},{"l":"User roles and permissions","r":"A trainee may write stories but must never be able to publish them"},{"l":"Scheduling","r":"Carnival road-closure notices must unpublish automatically at midnight"},{"l":"Media library","r":"One uploaded photo must be reused at different sizes on the site and in the newsletter"},{"l":"Search and metadata","r":"Readers cannot find all the stories about Bequia"},{"l":"Plugins and extensions","r":"The paper wants an events calendar without writing code"},{"l":"Responsive output","r":"Stories look broken on readers' phones"},{"l":"Analytics integration","r":"The editor wants to know which stories readers finish"},{"l":"Version control and revisions","r":"Last week's home page must be restored after a bad edit"}],"win":"Well matched. You separated features that sound alike, such as roles versus workflow and scheduling to unpublish as well as publish.","title":"Match the CMS feature (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Content management techniques and the content workflow","html":"<p>There are nine <b>content management techniques</b>: <b>creating, editing, organising, tagging, scheduling, approving, publishing, archiving and updating</b>. Together they form a <b>content workflow</b>: the defined path content follows from draft to publication and beyond.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Creating</b>: drafting text and producing media in the CMS editor, using <b>structured content</b> (defined fields: headline, summary, body, image, author, location) so each field can be reused.</li>\n<li><b>Editing</b>: revising for accuracy, clarity, style and SEO; adding alt text and captions. A written <b>style guide</b> keeps voice consistent.</li>\n<li><b>Organising</b>: placing content in a consistent <b>taxonomy</b>, for example News &gt; Caribbean &gt; St. Vincent, which improves navigation, search and related links.</li>\n<li><b>Tagging</b>: adding tags and <b>metadata</b> (data about data: author, date, keywords, licence). Categories are broad and planned; tags are specific and flexible.</li>\n<li><b>Approving</b>: an editor reviews and signs off. In WordPress a Contributor submits \"for review\"; an Editor approves.</li>\n<li><b>Scheduling</b>: setting a date and time for automatic publishing, guided by a <b>content calendar</b> (what, when, which channel).</li>\n<li><b>Publishing</b>: making content live on one or more channels.</li>\n<li><b>Updating</b>: revising live content when facts change, noting the update date; version control keeps a record.</li>\n<li><b>Archiving</b>: removing outdated content from the main view while preserving a record. A regular <b>content audit</b> checks pages for accuracy, accessibility and relevance.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>There are also four supporting techniques: <b>separation of content and presentation</b> (what makes redesigns and multichannel publishing possible), <b>SEO and accessibility</b> (descriptive headings, alt text, captions, readable URLs), <b>version control</b>, and <b>backup and disaster recovery</b> (automatic off-site backups, essential in a hurricane region).</p>\n<p><b>Island Pulse Media case study: Keisha's workflow map.</b> For Windward Islands Weekly, a reporter (Author role) drafts a story with structured fields; the sub-editor (Editor role) checks facts and style; the editor-in-chief approves; the story is scheduled for 6 a.m. and published at once to the website, app and newsletter. Each version is saved, and stories older than two years move automatically to the archive. \"No single person can stop the paper any more,\" Zara says.</p>\n<p>The workflow states are <b>Draft, In Review, Approved, Scheduled, Published, Archived</b>. Updating happens while content is live, before it is finally archived.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> When asked to \"describe\" a technique, say what it is <b>and</b> give a CMS example: \"Scheduling is setting a future date and time for a post to publish automatically, for example the Carnival parade route at 6 a.m. on the opening day.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>A content workflow is the path content follows: create, edit, organise, tag, approve, schedule, publish, update and archive. Structured fields, such as headline, summary and image, let content be reused. Categories are broad; tags are specific. At the Windward Islands Weekly, a reporter drafts, a sub-editor checks, the editor approves, and the story publishes at 6 a.m.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A defined workflow turns quality from a personal habit into a <b>system</b>, which is why \"no single person can stop the paper any more\". Each stage has a purpose that is easy to skip under pressure: editing catches errors, approval assigns responsibility, and updating with a dated note keeps trust when facts change. Archiving is not deleting; it preserves a record while removing outdated content from view, and a regular content audit decides what to update, merge or archive. Supporting techniques sit underneath: separation of content and presentation, accessibility, version control and off-site backups. Describe each technique with a concrete CMS example.</p>"},{"type":"order","game":true,"title":"Put the content workflow in order","intro":"<p>The Island Pulse Media is producing a \"Hurricane season checklist\" post for a client in The Bahamas. Put the workflow steps in a sensible order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"Create: Keisha drafts the checklist in structured fields and adds photos in the CMS editor."},{"t":"Edit: Zara checks facts against official guidance, fixes style and adds alt text."},{"t":"Organise and tag: placed under Safety > The Bahamas and tagged \"hurricane\", \"checklist\"."},{"t":"Approve: the client reviews and signs off the post."},{"t":"Schedule: set to publish at 7 a.m. on 1 June, the start of the Atlantic hurricane season."},{"t":"Publish: the post goes live on the website, app and newsletter at once."},{"t":"Update: shelter details are revised when the government announces changes."},{"t":"Archive: at the end of the season the post is archived and a new version is planned."}],"win":"Correct. Create, edit, organise and tag, approve, schedule, publish, update, archive: a complete content workflow with checks before anything goes live.","tutor":[{"h":"Check before live","p":"<p>Editing and approval happen before the content is scheduled.</p>"},{"h":"Life after publishing","p":"<p>Content is updated while it is live and archived at the end.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Content workflow (Foundation)","intro":"Put the four workflow states in order.","blocks":[{"t":"Draft"},{"t":"In Review and Approved"},{"t":"Scheduled and Published"},{"t":"Archived"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>A fuller workflow with roles. Put the nine steps for a ferry-timetable story in order.</p>","blocks":[{"t":"The reporter, in the Author role, drafts the story in structured fields."},{"t":"The sub-editor checks facts against the ferry company and adds alt text."},{"t":"The story is placed under News > Caribbean > Bequia and tagged \"ferry\"."},{"t":"The editor-in-chief approves it."},{"t":"It is scheduled to publish at 6 a.m."},{"t":"The API publishes it to the website, app and newsletter at once."},{"t":"When the timetable changes, the story is updated with a dated correction note."},{"t":"Two years later, a content audit flags it as outdated."},{"t":"It is archived: removed from the main view but preserved as a record."}],"win":"Correct. Draft, edit, organise and tag, approve, schedule, publish, update, audit, archive: each role and step adds a check.","title":"Put the content workflow in order (Challenge)"}},{"type":"pick","game":true,"title":"Chapter review: true or false?","intro":"<p>These statements come from Section B of the chapter review. Pick only the <b>TRUE</b> statements.</p>","q":"Which statements are TRUE?","lines":["A content management system requires users to write all website code by hand.","The CDA stores content and makes it visible to visitors.","A DMS can be considered a sub-category of content management software.","ECM is a single piece of software.","A headless CMS cannot be used to build a website.","Open source CMS can be customised without special permission.","SaaS CMS suppliers usually handle maintenance and upgrades.","Most proprietary CMS can be hosted on any web host.","Metadata helps users and search engines find content."],"correct":[1,2,5,6,8],"win":"Well done. You avoided the traps: a CMS needs no hand coding, ECM is a combination of strategies, methods and tools, a website is one of a headless CMS's heads, and most proprietary CMS run properly only on the owner's host.","miss":"Not quite. Check the false ones again: a CMS removes the need to code everything; ECM is not one product; a website is just one possible head; proprietary CMS have limited hosting options.","tutor":[{"h":"Single or combination?","p":"<p>ECM is \"neither a single technology nor a single method nor a single process\".</p>"},{"h":"Heads","p":"<p>Is a website one of the heads a headless CMS can feed?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"True or false? (Foundation)","q":"Which statements are TRUE?","lines":["The CDA stores content and makes it visible to visitors.","ECM is a single piece of software.","SaaS CMS suppliers usually handle maintenance and upgrades.","A CMS requires users to write all website code by hand."],"correct":[0,2],"win":"Correct. The CDA publishes content, and SaaS suppliers handle maintenance.","miss":"Not quite. ECM is a combination of tools and methods, and a CMS removes the need to hand-code."},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder chapter-review statements. Some contain subtle errors.</p>","q":"Which statements are TRUE?","lines":["The CMA is the back-end process that publishes content to visitors.","A DMS manages documents as whole entities.","The five stages of ECM are capture, manage, store, preserve and deliver.","For CAPE, MCM means formatting web content so it fits phone screens.","In a headless CMS, the \"head\" is where content is stored and authored.","Open source CMS are always completely free to run.","A partial cloud CMS sits on your own cloud server and can be customised.","A fully cloud CMS is usually proprietary and under the supplier's control.","Categories are broad and planned, whereas tags are specific and flexible.","COPE stands for Create Once, Publish Everywhere.","Updating content happens after it has been archived."],"correct":[1,2,6,7,8,9],"win":"Excellent. You caught every trap: the CDA (not the CMA) delivers content, MCM is secure mobile access to data, the head is where content ends up, open source still has running costs, and updating happens while content is live.","miss":"Look again at the false ones: CMA versus CDA, the CAPE meaning of MCM, which part is the head, the hidden costs of open source, and when updating happens in the workflow.","title":"Chapter review: true or false? (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Factors influencing trends in web publishing","html":"<p>Section 2.10 of the course outline asks what drives change in how we publish and consume content online. You must be able to <b>discuss</b> these factors, including <b>politics</b>. Learn the seven factors with a Caribbean example for each.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Factor</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Effect on web publishing</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Commoditisation</b></td><td>Tools and services that were once specialist and expensive become standard, interchangeable and cheap.</td><td>Website builders, templates, hosting and stock media are now commodities, so anyone can publish a professional-looking site. Competition shifts to content quality.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Pervasiveness of technology</b></td><td>Technology is everywhere and always on: phones, Wi-Fi, 4G and 5G, smart devices.</td><td>Most people browse on phones, so publishers design mobile-first and publish short, frequent updates.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Affordability</b></td><td>The cost of devices, data and tools falls relative to income.</td><td>Free platforms and low-cost SaaS let students and small businesses publish; data costs still limit heavy video in some places.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Accessibility</b></td><td>How easily people, including those with disabilities or in remote areas, can reach and use content.</td><td>Captions, alt text, WCAG guidelines, lightweight pages and offline options widen the audience.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Adoption</b></td><td>How quickly and widely people take up a technology or platform.</td><td>When an audience moves (for example to WhatsApp Channels or TikTok), publishers must follow. Low adoption kills a format.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Politics</b></td><td>Government policy, laws, regulation and political events.</td><td>Data protection laws, accessibility laws, telecom regulation, platform bans and election misinformation rules shape what can be published and how.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Empowerment</b></td><td>Technology gives individuals and communities the power to create, speak and earn.</td><td>Citizen journalists, creators and small newsrooms reach global audiences without a gatekeeper.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Caribbean examples.</b> Commoditisation and affordability mean a doubles stand in San Fernando can run a free Instagram and a cheap Wix site. Pervasiveness means Digicel, Flow and other networks keep people online almost everywhere. After Hurricane Irma, many residents turned to Facebook groups and WhatsApp for updates, a sign of <b>adoption</b> and <b>empowerment</b>. Politics appears in regional debates about data protection and in governments moving services online.</p>\n<p>The factors <b>interact</b>. Cheap smartphones (affordability) plus constant connection (pervasiveness) lead to mass use of short video (adoption), which lets young creators earn (empowerment), which governments may then regulate (politics).</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> \"Discuss\" questions reward <b>linked</b> points. For each factor: define it, give an example, explain its effect on web publishing, and if possible show how it connects to another factor.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Seven factors drive change in web publishing: commoditisation (tools become cheap and standard), pervasiveness (technology everywhere), affordability (devices and data cost less), accessibility (more people can use content), adoption (people take up new platforms), politics (laws and government) and empowerment (anyone can publish). A doubles stand with a cheap Wix site shows commoditisation and affordability.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>The factors are most powerful when <b>linked into chains</b>. Cheaper phones (affordability) and constant coverage (pervasiveness) produce mass use of short video (adoption), which lets individuals build audiences (empowerment), which then attracts regulation (politics). Watch the close pairs: commoditisation is about specialist tools becoming standard and interchangeable, whereas affordability is about cost relative to income; pervasiveness is about technology being present everywhere, whereas adoption is about people choosing to use it. Each factor can also have negative effects, such as commoditisation lowering pay for content. In \"discuss\" answers, define, exemplify, explain the effect and link to a second factor.</p>"},{"type":"sort","game":true,"title":"Name the factor","intro":"<p>Zara is preparing a talk on why web publishing in the Caribbean has changed. Sort each example by the main factor it shows.</p>","main":"Which factor is MAINLY shown?","bins":["Commoditisation and affordability","Pervasiveness and adoption","Accessibility","Politics and empowerment"],"items":[{"t":"Stock photos and fonts that once cost a lot are now free on Canva.","bin":0,"fb":"Specialist resources becoming cheap and standard is commoditisation."},{"t":"A new data protection law requires sites to explain how they use visitors' data.","bin":3,"fb":"Laws and regulation are the politics factor."},{"t":"A government portal offers a low-data text version for users with weak connections.","bin":2,"fb":"Making content reachable for people with weak connections is accessibility."},{"t":"A Jamaican teenager builds a large YouTube audience with no TV station behind her.","bin":3,"fb":"Individuals publishing without gatekeepers is empowerment."},{"t":"Almost every customer at a Rodney Bay café browses on a phone with 4G.","bin":1,"fb":"Always-on mobile technology everywhere is pervasiveness."},{"t":"A Grenada spice farmer builds a professional site from a cheap template in an afternoon.","bin":0,"fb":"Templates and site builders have become cheap standard products: commoditisation and affordability."},{"t":"A news site adds captions and screen-reader-friendly headings for visually impaired readers.","bin":2,"fb":"Making content usable by people with disabilities is accessibility."},{"t":"Local businesses move their promotions to WhatsApp Channels after customers start using them daily.","bin":1,"fb":"Following where users have moved is adoption."}],"win":"Well sorted. You linked real changes to the trend factors, which is the evidence a \"discuss\" answer needs.","tutor":[{"h":"Who can use it?","p":"<p>Disabilities and weak connections point to accessibility.</p>"},{"h":"Laws or people power","p":"<p>Regulation is politics. Ordinary people gaining a voice is empowerment.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Name the factor (Foundation)","items":[{"t":"Website templates become cheap and standard.","bin":0,"fb":"Commoditisation."},{"t":"Everyone has a phone online all day.","bin":1,"fb":"Pervasiveness."},{"t":"Captions added for deaf users.","bin":2,"fb":"Accessibility."},{"t":"A new online safety law.","bin":3,"fb":"Politics."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Seven separate factors now, with close pairs such as commoditisation and affordability. Sort each example by the factor it MAINLY shows.</p>","main":"Which factor is MAINLY shown?","bins":["Commoditisation","Pervasiveness","Affordability","Accessibility","Adoption","Politics","Empowerment"],"items":[{"t":"A news site adds a low-data text version and captions on every video.","bin":3,"fb":"Making content usable by people with disabilities or weak connections is accessibility."},{"t":"A Belize teenager earns from her cooking channel with no TV station behind her.","bin":6,"fb":"Individuals publishing and earning without gatekeepers is empowerment."},{"t":"Local businesses move their updates to WhatsApp Channels because customers already did.","bin":4,"fb":"Following where users have moved is adoption."},{"t":"Data protection rules force sites to add cookie consent.","bin":5,"fb":"Laws and regulation are politics."},{"t":"Stock music, once costly and specialist, is now a generic feature of every video app.","bin":0,"fb":"Turning a specialist resource into a standard feature is commoditisation."},{"t":"Cheaper prepaid data bundles let more families stream video.","bin":2,"fb":"Lower data costs are affordability."},{"t":"A new video app fails because very few people install it.","bin":4,"fb":"Low adoption kills a format."},{"t":"Smart TVs and speakers are now connected in most homes.","bin":1,"fb":"Connected devices throughout daily life show pervasiveness."},{"t":"4G and Wi-Fi reach almost every beach and bus stop on the island.","bin":1,"fb":"Technology present everywhere and always on is pervasiveness."},{"t":"Smartphone prices fall so far that most secondary students own one.","bin":2,"fb":"Costs falling relative to income is affordability."},{"t":"Professional website templates are now standard and interchangeable across many builders.","bin":0,"fb":"Specialist products becoming standard and interchangeable is commoditisation."}],"win":"Well analysed. You separated the close pairs: standard tools (commoditisation) from lower costs (affordability), and technology being everywhere (pervasiveness) from people choosing to use it (adoption).","title":"Name the factor (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Impact on web publishing","html":"<p>You also need to explain <b>how</b> these factors influence six areas of web publishing. This is where \"discuss\" answers gain depth.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Area</th><th>Impact of the factors</th><th>Example</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Content creation</b></td><td>Cheap phones and free apps (affordability, commoditisation) mean more people create; AI tools speed up drafting; content becomes more visual and frequent.</td><td>A Dominica tour guide films, edits and posts a Reel from one phone.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Design</b></td><td>Pervasive phones push <b>mobile-first</b>, responsive layouts; templates make good design common, so brands must work harder to stand out.</td><td>Island Pulse Media designs every site for a phone screen first.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Distribution</b></td><td>Content travels through social feeds, search, messaging apps and newsletters; algorithms decide reach; politics can block or regulate platforms.</td><td>News outlets share stories to WhatsApp Channels as well as websites.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>User experience</b></td><td>Users expect fast, simple, personalised experiences; slow sites lose visitors, especially on costly mobile data.</td><td>A villa site that loads in 2 seconds keeps more visitors than one that takes 10.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Accessibility</b></td><td>Laws, standards (WCAG) and social expectation push captions, alt text, contrast and keyboard support.</td><td>Government sites adding text alternatives for older and disabled citizens.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Audience engagement</b></td><td>Empowerment and adoption turn audiences into participants: comments, duets, user-generated content, live chat.</td><td>A Carnival troupe invites fans to post costume videos with a hashtag.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Positive and negative impacts.</b> A balanced discussion includes both:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Positive</b>: more Caribbean voices online, lower costs, new jobs for creators and developers, better accessibility.</li>\n<li><b>Negative</b>: information overload and misinformation, dependence on foreign platforms and their algorithms, the <b>digital divide</b> for people who cannot afford data, privacy concerns, and lower pay for content as it becomes a commodity.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Zara at the Island Pulse Media sums it up: \"Anyone can publish now. So the advantage goes to those who publish <b>well</b>: accurate, accessible, mobile-first and genuinely useful.\"</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Use a chain in your answers: <b>factor</b> leads to <b>change</b> leads to <b>impact</b>. \"Because smartphones are pervasive, most visitors read on small screens, so publishers design mobile-first, which improves user experience for the majority.\"</p>",
 "plain": "<p>The seven factors change six areas of web publishing: content creation, design, distribution, user experience, accessibility and audience engagement. For example, cheap phones mean more people create content, and mobile-first design is now normal. There are good effects, such as more Caribbean voices, and bad effects, such as misinformation and depending on foreign platforms.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>A strong \"discuss\" answer uses the chain <b>factor → change → impact</b> and then weighs both sides. Lower barriers increase the volume of content, which makes quality, accuracy and accessibility the new competitive advantage, as Zara puts it. Some impacts are double-edged: algorithmic distribution gives small Caribbean creators global reach, yet also makes them dependent on foreign platforms' rules. The digital divide is a reminder that pervasiveness is uneven, since people without affordable data are left out of mobile-first services. Pair every impact with a specific regional example, and finish with a judgement about whether the overall effect is positive for the audience you are discussing.</p>"},{"type":"learn","title":"Emerging trends in web publishing","html":"<p>The course outline names several emerging trends. <b>Digital publishing</b> is the creation and distribution of content (news, books, magazines, academic work, newsletters, audio and video) through digital channels. Learn the <b>idea</b> behind each trend and check its current state before an exam.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>AI in the publishing workflow</b>: generative AI tools now assist with drafting, summarising, translating, transcribing interviews, writing headlines and alt text, generating images and tagging archives; many CMS platforms have AI assistants built into the editor. Responsible publishers adopt clear <b>AI policies</b>: human editors check facts, AI-assisted content is disclosed where appropriate, and original reporting remains the core product. Risks: factual errors, bias, copyright disputes and loss of audience trust.</li>\n<li><b>Voice search</b>: people ask spoken questions to phones and smart speakers. Publishers write in natural, question-style language (\"Where can I buy sorrel drink in Port of Spain?\") and use structured data.</li>\n<li><b>Progressive web apps (PWAs)</b>: websites that behave like apps: added to the home screen, work offline (using a <b>service worker</b>), load fast and send notifications, with no app store download.</li>\n<li><b>Web3</b>: the idea of a decentralised web built on blockchain, with user-owned data and digital tokens. Supporters see creator ownership; critics point to scams, energy use and limited adoption. Treat it as an evolving, debated trend.</li>\n<li><b>Short-form vs long-form content</b>: short vertical video (TikTok, Reels, Shorts) suits short attention and algorithms that favour quick engagement; long-form (podcasts, documentaries, in-depth articles) builds trust and loyal audiences. Most publishers use <b>both</b>: short to attract, long to keep.</li>\n<li><b>Subscriptions, memberships and the creator economy</b>: <b>hard paywalls</b>, <b>metered access</b> (a few free articles a month) and voluntary memberships provide reader revenue less dependent on advertising. <b>Newsletters</b> on Substack, Beehiiv and Ghost let writers and small newsrooms charge subscribers directly. Independent creators build audiences on YouTube, TikTok, Instagram and Patreon, sometimes rivalling traditional outlets.</li>\n<li><b>Video, audio and visual storytelling</b>: podcasts, audio articles (text read aloud by human or synthetic voices) and visual explainers now sit alongside text, so CMS and DAM systems must handle large files, captions and transcripts.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Keisha at the Island Pulse Media tells clients: \"Use short videos to get discovered, a newsletter to stay connected, and your website as home. And if AI helps you draft, a human checks every fact.\"</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> For each trend, have three things ready: <b>what it is</b>, <b>one benefit</b>, <b>one concern</b>. A typical question asks you to discuss FIVE trends for 10 marks: two marks each.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Emerging trends include AI tools that help with drafting and transcripts, voice search, progressive web apps that work offline, Web3, short and long-form content, subscriptions and newsletters, and more audio and video storytelling. Use short videos to get discovered, a newsletter to stay connected, and your website as home. If AI helps you draft, a person checks every fact.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Each trend should be judged by its <b>benefit and its risk</b>, and many have both. AI speeds up transcription and tagging but can introduce errors and bias, so responsible publishers keep a human editor in the loop and disclose AI assistance. Progressive web apps give app-like experiences without app-store costs, which suits low-budget Caribbean publishers. Web3 remains contested, with promises of creator ownership set against scams and low adoption. Paywalls differ: hard paywalls protect revenue but limit reach, metered access samples content first, and voluntary memberships rely on loyalty. Trends change quickly, so check their current state, and in answers give what it is, one benefit and one concern.</p>"},{"type":"multi","game":true,"title":"Spot the emerging trend","intro":"<p>Clients of the Island Pulse Media describe what they have noticed. Name the trend each time.</p>","items":[{"text":"A hotel's website can be added to a guest's home screen and still shows the map without Wi-Fi.","options":["voice search","Web3","progressive web app"],"answer":2,"fb":"Home-screen install and offline use through a service worker define a PWA."},{"text":"Customers ask their phones, \"Where can I eat doubles near Maracas?\"","options":["personalisation","newsletter","voice search"],"answer":2,"fb":"Spoken, question-style searches are voice search."},{"text":"A news site lets readers see three free articles a month, then asks them to subscribe.","options":["hard paywall","voluntary membership","metered paywall"],"answer":2,"fb":"A few free articles before payment is metered access."},{"text":"A writer moves her readers from social media to a paid email publication on Substack.","options":["short-form video","newsletters and subscriptions","PWA"],"answer":1,"fb":"Direct-to-subscriber email, sometimes paid, is the newsletter and subscription trend."},{"text":"A newsroom uses a tool to transcribe interviews and draft alt text, then an editor checks everything.","options":["voice search","Web3","AI in the publishing workflow"],"answer":2,"fb":"AI assisting the workflow, with human checking, is the responsible approach."},{"text":"A Trinidad podcast also publishes each episode as an audio article and a captioned vertical clip.","options":["commoditisation","video, audio and visual storytelling","DMS"],"answer":1,"fb":"Publishing in audio, video and visual formats alongside text is this trend."}],"win":"Excellent. You recognised PWAs, voice search, paywalls, newsletters, AI in the workflow and multimedia storytelling from real-world clues.","tutor":[{"h":"Offline clue","p":"<p>A website that works offline and installs to the home screen is a special kind of web app.</p>"},{"h":"Free, then pay","p":"<p>Count how many free articles the reader gets before paying.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Spot the trend (Foundation)","items":[{"text":"A website installed on the home screen that works offline is a...","options":["PWA","blog"],"answer":0,"fb":"Progressive web app."},{"text":"Asking a phone a spoken question is...","options":["voice search","Web3"],"answer":0,"fb":"Voice search."},{"text":"A few free articles a month before paying is a...","options":["hard paywall","metered paywall"],"answer":1,"fb":"Metered paywall."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>Harder clues, including trends from the next screen. Name the trend each time.</p>","items":[{"text":"Search engines answer \"Castries market days\" directly, and the publisher's visits fall.","options":["Voice search","Web3","Zero-click search","Personalisation"],"answer":2,"fb":"Answers shown directly on the results page reduce click-through: zero-click search."},{"text":"A newsroom attaches tamper-evident information to its photos showing how they were captured and edited.","options":["PWA","Commoditisation","Metered paywall","Content Credentials (provenance)"],"answer":3,"fb":"Content Credentials, based on the C2PA standard, show the origin and edits of media."},{"text":"A publisher links a headless CMS, a DAM, a search service and a newsletter tool through APIs.","options":["Composable, multichannel publishing","Voice search","Hard paywall","Short-form video"],"answer":0,"fb":"Connecting specialised services through APIs is a composable stack."},{"text":"A homepage shows each reader stories based on the topics she chose when signing up for the newsletter.","options":["Zero-click search","Personalisation using first-party data","Accessibility","Web3"],"answer":1,"fb":"Using data readers share directly to tailor content is personalisation with first-party data."},{"text":"A Curaçao e-book publisher must meet accessibility rules to sell to customers in the Netherlands.","options":["Voice search","Accessibility as a legal requirement","Commoditisation","Metered paywall"],"answer":1,"fb":"The European Accessibility Act makes accessibility a legal requirement for many digital products sold in the EU."},{"text":"Every article is locked unless the reader pays.","options":["Hard paywall","Newsletter","Voluntary membership","Metered paywall"],"answer":0,"fb":"Locking all content behind payment is a hard paywall."}],"win":"Excellent. You identified subtler trends: zero-click search, provenance, composable stacks, personalisation, legal accessibility and paywall types.","title":"Spot the emerging trend (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"More trends and developments in digital publishing","html":"<p><b>AI search and the \"zero-click\" challenge.</b> Search engines and chatbots increasingly answer questions directly with AI-generated summaries, so users get information without clicking through. This <b>zero-click search</b> reduces referral traffic and advertising income. Publishers respond by building <b>direct relationships</b> (newsletters, apps, memberships), negotiating licensing deals with AI companies and focusing on content AI cannot easily replace: local reporting, investigation, analysis and community voices.</p>\n<p><b>Headless, composable and multichannel publishing.</b> Publishers are moving from all-in-one CMS platforms to <b>composable stacks</b>: a headless CMS for content, a DAM for media, a search service, a newsletter tool and analytics, all connected by APIs. The aim is COPE, from websites and apps to smart TVs, in-car systems and voice assistants.</p>\n<p><b>Personalisation and data.</b> Algorithms tailor homepages, recommendations and newsletters to individual readers. Publishers rely more on <b>first-party data</b> (information readers share directly, such as newsletter sign-ups). Personalisation must be balanced against privacy, consent and the risk of <b>filter bubbles</b>.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Update:</b> Third-party tracking cookies are declining. That is still the direction, since Safari and Firefox block them by default and privacy laws are tightening, but in 2024 and 2025 Google dropped its plan to remove them from Chrome. So first-party data matters, while third-party cookies have not disappeared.</p>\n<p><b>Trust, authenticity and Content Credentials.</b> Deepfakes and AI images make it harder to know what is real. The <b>C2PA</b> standard and <b>Content Credentials</b> attach tamper-evident information to images and video showing how they were captured and edited. Fact-checking, transparent corrections and clear labelling of sponsored and AI-assisted content signal a trustworthy publisher.</p>\n<p><b>Accessibility and open formats.</b> Accessibility is moving from good practice to legal requirement, for example the <b>European Accessibility Act</b>, which affects e-books and digital services sold in the EU, including the Netherlands and France, so it matters to Caribbean businesses selling to European customers, including those in the Dutch and French Caribbean. Publishers adopt <b>EPUB 3</b>, alt text, captions, transcripts and <b>WCAG</b>. Open access and Creative Commons licensing keep growing.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Update:</b> The European Accessibility Act has applied since 28 June 2025, so for EU markets it is now a legal requirement, not just a trend.</p>\n<p><b>Mobile-first and low-bandwidth publishing.</b> Most Caribbean audiences read news on phones, often via WhatsApp shares and social feeds. Publishers compress images, use fast formats and distribute through WhatsApp Channels and Telegram. Light pages matter after storms, when connections are slow.</p>\n<p><b>Opportunities for Caribbean publishers</b>: low-cost SaaS and open source tools; diaspora audiences in North America and Europe reached through memberships, newsletters and e-books; multilingual publishing (English, Dutch, Spanish, French, Papiamentu, Kreyòl); and local culture and community reporting, exactly what global AI summaries cannot replace.</p>\n<p><b>Future focus</b>: readers may ask AI assistants for news instead of visiting sites, so content must be structured and well tagged; licensing archives to AI companies is a new revenue stream; AR overlays at heritage sites may join stories; and provenance by default may make verification as routine as a byline.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Question 4(b) asks how ONE trend creates an opportunity for a small Caribbean publisher. Choose a trend, explain the opportunity, and name the tool (Ghost, Substack, WhatsApp Channels).</p>",
 "plain": "<p>More trends: AI search answers questions directly, so fewer people click through to websites. Publishers link many tools through APIs, personalise content using data readers share, and use Content Credentials to show photos are real. Accessibility is becoming a legal duty in the EU. Caribbean publishers can grow by reaching the diaspora, publishing in local languages and reporting local stories.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Zero-click search shifts power from publishers to platforms, so the strategic response is to build <b>direct relationships</b> (newsletters, apps, memberships) and to publish what summaries cannot replace: local reporting, investigation and community voices. Personalisation carries a privacy trade-off and a risk of filter bubbles, and the decline of third-party cookies is real but uneven, so first-party data matters more. Note the updates: Chrome did not remove third-party cookies, and the European Accessibility Act has applied since June 2025. For Caribbean businesses selling to EU customers, legal accessibility is a present requirement, not a future trend. Opportunity answers should name a trend, the opportunity and a tool.</p>"},{"type":"mcq","title":"The zero-click challenge","q":"The Grenville Gazette in Grenada notices that many readers now get answers such as \"St. George's market days\" straight from AI search summaries and never visit its website. Which response is MOST likely to protect its income?","options":[{"t":"Publish more short generic articles to appear in more search results.","fb":"Generic content is exactly what AI summaries answer without a click, so this does not solve the problem."},{"t":"Remove the website and post only on one social platform.","fb":"This increases platform dependency and gives up the publisher's own home and data."},{"t":"Build a direct relationship with readers through a newsletter and membership, and focus on local reporting AI cannot easily replace.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. Direct relationships and unique local content are the main responses to zero-click search."},{"t":"Switch from WordPress to a proprietary CMS.","fb":"The CMS classification does not change how search engines show answers."}],"tutor":[{"h":"What is lost?","p":"<p>Zero-click search mainly reduces referral traffic. How can a publisher reach readers without search?</p>"},{"h":"Hard to replace","p":"<p>Which content can a global summary not copy easily?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Zero-click (Foundation)","q":"\"Zero-click\" search mainly threatens publishers by reducing:","options":[{"t":"referral traffic","ok":true,"fb":"Yes. Users get answers without clicking through to the site."},{"t":"server storage","fb":"Storage is not the issue; visitors are."},{"t":"printing costs","fb":"Printing is not affected by how search results are shown."}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","q":"The Grenville Gazette has lost 40% of its search traffic to AI summaries. It has a small team, a loyal local readership and a 20-year photo and story archive. Which plan is BEST, and why?","options":[{"t":"Block every search engine so readers must visit directly.","fb":"Blocking search makes the paper invisible to new readers and does not build loyalty."},{"t":"Publish 50 generic AI-written articles a day to win back search positions.","fb":"Generic content is exactly what AI summaries replace, and unchecked AI text risks errors and lost trust."},{"t":"Move all content to one social platform.","fb":"This trades one dependency for another and gives up the paper's own home and data."},{"t":"Grow a newsletter and membership, focus on local reporting that summaries cannot replace, and consider licensing the archive as a new revenue stream.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. It builds direct relationships, plays to the paper's unique strength and turns the archive into income."}],"title":"The zero-click challenge (Challenge)"}},{"type":"learn","title":"Research tools for discovering and tracking trends","html":"<p>You need to know <b>research tools</b> for discovering and tracking trends. The general types are search engines, analytics tools, trend reports, social media platforms, newsletters, industry publications, forums and digital media blogs. The table names specific tools.</p>\n<div class=\"tablewrap\"><table>\n<tr><th>Tool</th><th>Type</th><th>What it tells you</th></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Google Trends</b></td><td>Search trend tool (free)</td><td>How interest in a search term changes over time and by country; compares terms; shows rising queries. Example: compare \"Crop Over\" and \"Antigua Carnival\" over five years.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>SEMrush</b></td><td>SEO and competitor analysis (paid, limited free use)</td><td>Keyword volumes, which keywords competitor sites rank for, backlinks, site audits.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>BuzzSumo</b></td><td>Content research (paid, limited free use)</td><td>Which articles and topics get the most shares and engagement; identifies influencers.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Feedly</b></td><td>RSS news reader and aggregator</td><td>Collects new posts from chosen sites, blogs and newsletters in one place, so you can follow industry news daily.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>HubSpot Blog</b></td><td>Industry publication or digital marketing blog</td><td>Articles, guides and survey-based reports on marketing, content and social media trends.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>Analytics</b> (Google Analytics, platform insights)</td><td>Analytics tool</td><td>Your own audience data: visitors, devices, traffic sources, popular content, location.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><b>The Web Almanac</b> (HTTP Archive)</td><td>Trend report (free)</td><td>An annual, data-based report on the state of the web: page weight, performance, accessibility, CMS use, mobile. The 2025 edition is a useful resource.</td></tr>\n</table></div>\n<p><b>Using the tools together.</b> Keisha at the Island Pulse Media researches a client campaign this way:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Google Trends: is interest in \"Caribbean food tours\" rising, and when does it peak?</li>\n<li>SEMrush: which keywords do competitor tour sites rank for?</li>\n<li>BuzzSumo: which food-tour articles are shared most?</li>\n<li>Feedly and the HubSpot Blog: what are marketers saying about short video, newsletters and zero-click search this year?</li>\n<li>Client analytics: what devices and countries do current visitors come from?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><b>Activity 5 (Trend Watch)</b> uses these skills: choose one trend, find a recent news article about it, summarise it in your own words in 150 words and explain how it could affect a Caribbean newspaper, author or content creator.</p>\n<p><b>Limitations</b>: small Caribbean markets may show little data in some tools; paid tools are expensive; trend data shows <b>what</b> is happening but not always <b>why</b>; and vendor blogs may promote their own products. Always triangulate and note the date of your data.</p>\n<p class=\"tip\"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In the SBA proposal, a Google Trends screenshot or analytics chart, correctly dated and explained in one sentence, is strong evidence for why your product idea is relevant.</p>",
 "plain": "<p>Research tools help you discover and track trends. Google Trends shows how search interest changes. SEMrush shows competitors' keywords. BuzzSumo shows which articles are shared most. Feedly collects news from many sites. Analytics show your own audience. The Web Almanac reports yearly data on the state of the web. Compare \"Crop Over\" and \"Antigua Carnival\" in Google Trends.</p>",
 "deeper": "<p>Each tool answers a different question, so good research <b>triangulates</b>: Google Trends shows interest, SEMrush shows competition, analytics shows your own audience, and industry publications explain what the data might mean. Every tool has limits. Small Caribbean markets may produce too little data in some tools; vendor blogs may promote their own products; and trend data shows what is happening but not why. Always date your data, because trends move quickly, and prefer your own analytics for decisions about your own audience. A strong answer pairs a tool with a specific research question and explains one limitation of the result.</p>"},{"type":"match","game":true,"title":"Match the research tool","intro":"<p>Match each research question to the tool that answers it best.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Google Trends","r":"Has interest in \"Bahamas hurricane preparedness\" risen each June over five years?"},{"l":"SEMrush","r":"Which keywords does a rival Curaçao tour site rank for on Google?"},{"l":"BuzzSumo","r":"Which articles about Caribbean travel were shared most last month?"},{"l":"Feedly","r":"How can I read new posts from 20 design blogs in one place each morning?"},{"l":"Google Analytics","r":"What share of visitors to our own website use phones?"},{"l":"The Web Almanac","r":"What does yearly data say about page weight and CMS use across the web?"}],"win":"Very good. You chose each tool by the question it answers: search interest, competitor keywords, shared content, news feeds, your own audience and the state of the web.","tutor":[{"h":"Your data or everyone's?","p":"<p>Your own visitors come from analytics. Web-wide data comes from reports.</p>"},{"h":"Shares vs searches","p":"<p>One tool shows what people search for; another shows what they share.</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Match the research tool (Foundation)","pairs":[{"l":"Google Trends","r":"How search interest changes over time"},{"l":"BuzzSumo","r":"Which articles are shared most"},{"l":"Feedly","r":"Reading many blogs in one feed"},{"l":"Analytics","r":"Data about your own visitors"}]},
 "up": {"bloom":"Apply","intro":"<p>Harder research questions. Match each tool to the question it answers BEST.</p>","pairs":[{"l":"Google Trends","r":"In which months do searches for \"Caribbean food tours\" peak in Canada?"},{"l":"SEMrush","r":"Which pages bring a rival Aruba blog the most search traffic, and which sites link to it?"},{"l":"BuzzSumo","r":"Who shared the most-shared Carnival articles of the past year?"},{"l":"Feedly","r":"How can I follow 30 publishing-industry blogs and newsletters in one reading list?"},{"l":"Google Analytics","r":"Which countries and devices do visitors to our own website use?"},{"l":"Instagram Insights","r":"At what times are our Instagram followers most active?"},{"l":"The Web Almanac","r":"What does yearly data on millions of sites say about accessibility and page weight?"},{"l":"HubSpot Blog","r":"What do recent survey-based marketing guides say about short video and newsletters?"}],"win":"Very good. You matched each tool to a precise question, separating your own data (analytics and insights) from market and industry data.","title":"Match the research tool (Challenge)"}},{"type":"scenario","game":true,"title":"Fixing the newsroom problem","intro":"<p>You are working with Zara's team at the Island Pulse Media on a case study: <b>Windward Islands Weekly</b> in Kingstown, St. Vincent. One developer pastes every story by hand, photos are on three laptops, and the app shows different headlines from the website. Make the best decisions.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Reporters email stories to the developer, and last week a story with a wrong date went live. What do you set up first?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A CMS with user roles and a workflow: reporters draft as Authors, an Editor checks, the editor-in-chief approves before scheduling.","to":"n2","fb":"Good. Roles and an approval workflow remove the single point of failure and catch errors."},{"t":"Make every reporter an Administrator so they can fix mistakes faster.","to":"bad1","fb":"More admin rights increase security risk and remove checks; errors still reach the public."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The paper wants the same stories on its website, app, morning newsletter and a lobby screen, without retyping. What do you recommend?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A headless set-up: the body stores each story once and the API sends it to all four heads.","to":"n3","fb":"Strong. Decoupled content over an API gives COPE: create once, publish everywhere."},{"t":"Ask staff to copy and paste each story into the app, newsletter and screen software.","to":"bad2","fb":"Retyping wastes time and creates inconsistent versions, the original problem."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Jerome must sort 40,000 photos from three laptops. What does he use?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A DAM, tagging each photo with location, event, photographer and licence expiry.","to":"n4","fb":"Correct. Digital Asset Management with rights metadata makes photos findable and safe to reuse."},{"t":"One big shared folder named \"Photos\" with no tags.","to":"bad3","fb":"Without metadata nobody can find images or check licences."},{"t":"An MCM system so staff can open photos on tablets.","to":"bad3","fb":"MCM secures access on mobile devices; it does not organise media with rights metadata."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Search traffic is falling as AI summaries answer questions directly. How do you respond?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Copy whatever a large US site is doing this week.","to":"bad4","fb":"Without your own data you may follow a trend that does not suit a Caribbean audience."},{"t":"Check analytics and Google Trends, then grow the newsletter and memberships and focus on local reporting.","to":"n5","fb":"Excellent. Evidence first, then direct relationships and content AI cannot easily replace."}]},"n5":{"text":"<p>A manager suggests publishing 50 AI-written stories a day with no checks. What do you say?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Agree; more content always means more readers.","to":"bad5","fb":"Unchecked AI content can contain errors that damage trust and the paper's reputation."},{"t":"Use AI only to assist with transcripts, alt text and drafts; a human checks every fact and AI use is disclosed under a clear AI policy.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. You gain speed while protecting accuracy and trust."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Success.</b> \"No single person can stop the paper any more,\" Zara says. A Grade One answer explains why: roles and workflow, a headless CMS with an API for multichannel delivery, a DAM for media, evidence-based responses to zero-click search and a responsible AI policy.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Security breach.</b> One reporter's weak password is stolen and the attacker, with admin rights, defaces the site. Try again and use roles and approval.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Mixed messages.</b> The app shows an old hurricane advisory while the website shows the new one. Try again and use one content source.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Lost and risky.</b> An editor cannot find the Carnival photos and reuses a freelancer's image whose licence has expired. Try again with a system built for media and metadata.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Wrong audience.</b> The changes suit US readers but not island residents. Try again and use your own data.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad5":{"text":"<p><b>Trust lost.</b> Several AI stories contain invented details and readers complain. Try again and keep a human in the loop.</p>","end":"bad"}},"tutor":[{"h":"Checks before live","p":"<p>Which CMS features stop errors before publishing?</p>"},{"h":"One body, many heads","p":"<p>Think about decoupling and APIs.</p>"},{"h":"Media needs metadata","p":"<p>Which system in the table manages photos, video and licences?</p>"}],"easy":{"title":"Fixing the newsroom (Foundation)","intro":"<p>A shorter version: you help Windward Islands Weekly.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>Stories go live with errors. What do you add?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Give everyone admin rights.","to":"bad","fb":"That removes checks and adds security risk."},{"t":"An approval workflow: an editor checks before publishing.","to":"n2","fb":"Yes. Approval catches errors."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The same stories must reach the website and the app. What do you use?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Retype each story in the app.","to":"bad","fb":"Retyping causes mismatched versions."},{"t":"A headless CMS with an API.","to":"n3","fb":"Yes. One body, many heads."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>Thousands of untagged photos need organising. What do you use?</p>","choices":[{"t":"A folder with no names.","to":"bad","fb":"Nothing can be found that way."},{"t":"A DAM with tags and licence details.","to":"good","fb":"Yes. DAM manages media files."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Well done.</b> Workflow, headless delivery and a DAM solve the newsroom problem.</p>","end":"good"},"bad":{"text":"<p><b>Not quite.</b> That choice brings back errors, mismatches or lost files. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"}}},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>A harder phase of the Windward Islands Weekly project. The CMS and headless set-up are live; now the paper faces trust, privacy, legal and continuity challenges.</p>","nodes":{"start":{"text":"<p>A doctored photo of storm damage, claiming to be from Kingstown, is spreading online. Readers ask the paper to confirm its own photos are genuine. What do you introduce?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Stop publishing photos until the rumours die down.","to":"bad1","fb":"This removes valuable reporting and does not help readers tell real from fake."},{"t":"Content Credentials on staff photos, a public corrections policy and clear labelling of any AI-assisted images.","to":"n2","fb":"Excellent. Provenance, transparency and labelling build trust when fakes circulate."}]},"n2":{"text":"<p>The marketing team wants to personalise the homepage. Which data should it use?</p>","choices":[{"t":"First-party data readers choose to share, such as newsletter topic preferences, with clear consent and a privacy notice.","to":"n3","fb":"Correct. First-party data with consent balances personalisation and privacy."},{"t":"Buy third-party tracking data about readers' browsing on other sites.","to":"bad2","fb":"This raises serious privacy and consent problems and relies on a declining technology."},{"t":"Personalise everything so readers see only topics they already like.","to":"ok1","fb":"Personalisation helps, but showing only familiar topics risks a filter bubble for a news publisher."}]},"ok1":{"text":"<p>Editors worry readers are missing important island news. What now?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Keep a fixed \"top stories\" section for everyone, and personalise only the rest.","to":"n3","fb":"Good recovery. Everyone still sees essential news."},{"t":"Remove all non-personalised stories.","to":"bad2","fb":"This deepens the filter bubble."}]},"n3":{"text":"<p>The paper sells an e-book of its best stories to readers in the Netherlands. What legal point applies?</p>","choices":[{"t":"No rules apply because the paper is based in St. Vincent.","to":"bad3","fb":"Selling into the EU brings its rules with it."},{"t":"The European Accessibility Act applies to e-books sold in the EU, so produce an accessible EPUB 3 with alt text and navigation.","to":"n4","fb":"Correct. For EU sales, accessibility is now a legal requirement."}]},"n4":{"text":"<p>Hurricane season is coming. How should the content system be protected?</p>","choices":[{"t":"Rely on the office server and restore from it after the storm.","to":"bad4","fb":"If the office is damaged, the only copy goes with it."},{"t":"Automatic off-site backups of the CMS and DAM, a tested restore plan, and a light, low-data emergency page.","to":"good","fb":"Excellent. Continuity and accessibility on slow connections both matter after a storm."}]},"good":{"text":"<p><b>Trusted and resilient.</b> Readers can verify photos, personalisation respects privacy, the e-book meets EU law and the paper keeps publishing through the storm. A Grade One answer links each decision to a trend (provenance, first-party data, legal accessibility, low-bandwidth publishing) and justifies it.</p>","end":"good"},"bad1":{"text":"<p><b>Trust not rebuilt.</b> Readers turn to unverified sources instead. Try again with provenance and transparency.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad2":{"text":"<p><b>Privacy complaint.</b> Readers object to being tracked or boxed in. Try again with consented first-party data.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad3":{"text":"<p><b>Sales blocked.</b> The e-book fails EU accessibility requirements. Try again.</p>","end":"bad"},"bad4":{"text":"<p><b>Offline for days.</b> Storm damage takes out the only server. Try again with off-site backups.</p>","end":"bad"}},"title":"Fixing the newsroom problem (Challenge)"}},{"type":"checkin","title":"Quick check-in"},{"type":"write","title":"Case study: Bim Eats guide","genre":"Structured response: case study (Section G)","stimulus":"<p class=\"passage\"><b>Island Pulse Media | Bim Eats guide.</b> A food blogger in Barbados runs Bim Eats, a restaurant guide built in a free website builder. She now has 600 reviews, 8,000 photos and 30,000 monthly readers, many from the US and Canada planning trips. Problems include:<br>1. photos are stored on her phone and laptop with no names or tags;<br>2. two freelance writers send drafts by WhatsApp, and old versions are sometimes published;<br>3. she wants to add an app and a weekly newsletter but would have to retype everything;<br>4. the site was offline for four days after a storm because her backups were on a local hard drive;<br>5. her traffic from search has fallen as search engines answer questions such as \"best fish fry in Oistins\" directly.</p>","prompt":"<p>(a) Recommend a suitable CMS classification for Bim Eats and justify your choice. (4 marks)</p><p>(b) Recommend tools or features to solve each of the first four problems. (8 marks)</p><p>(c) Explain whether a headless CMS would be appropriate for her plans. (4 marks)</p><p>(d) Suggest TWO strategies to reduce her dependence on search traffic. (4 marks)</p><p><i>Total: 20 marks. The e-course gives you feedback against three criteria.</i></p>","min":320,"max":600,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> I recommend a ... CMS such as ... because ... This suits Bim Eats because ...<br><b>(b)</b> Problem 1: ... Problem 2: ... Problem 3: ... Problem 4: ...<br><b>(c)</b> A headless CMS stores content in the body and delivers it through an API to ... This would / would not suit her because ... However, ...<br><b>(d)</b> First, ... Second, ...</p>","checklist":["I named ONE classification (open source, proprietary or SaaS), gave an example and justified it with at least two reasons linked to her situation.","I gave a specific tool or feature for each of problems 1 to 4 (for example DAM, workflow and versioning, COPE or headless, off-site cloud backup).","I explained headless using body, head, API and multi-channel, and gave a balanced judgement.","I gave two strategies for zero-click search, such as a newsletter, memberships or unique local content.","I linked my answer to Barbados details: Oistins, the fish fry, storms, visitors from the US and Canada."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"CMS classification and headless judgement (parts a and c)","marks":8,"look":["saas|open.?source|proprietary","wordpress|ghost|wix|squarespace|webflow|drupal","cost|subscription|licen","support|maintenance|upgrade|scal","headless|api","head|channel|app|newsletter","however|but|although|developer"],"min":4,"strong":"You recommended and justified a classification and judged headless with balance, using body, head and API.","weak":"Name one classification with an example (for example SaaS such as Squarespace, or open source WordPress on managed hosting), give two reasons linked to her, then explain headless (content as data over an API to many heads) and weigh it against the cost of developers."},{"criterion":"Tools and features for the four problems (part b)","marks":8,"look":["dam|digital asset|media library","tag|metadata","workflow|approv|role","version|revision","cope|create once|reuse|headless|api","backup|cloud|off.?site|off.?island"],"min":4,"strong":"You matched a precise tool or feature to each problem and explained how it solves it.","weak":"Take each problem in turn: a DAM with metadata for photos; roles, workflow and versioning for drafts; create once, publish everywhere for the app and newsletter; automatic off-site cloud backups for storms."},{"criterion":"Responding to zero-click search (part d)","marks":4,"look":["zero.?click|ai summar|search","newsletter|email|subscri","member|direct|app|social|whatsapp","local|unique|original|review|community"],"min":3,"strong":"You gave two developed strategies that build direct relationships or unique value.","weak":"Explain zero-click briefly, then suggest a weekly newsletter or membership (a direct relationship) and original local reviews or videos that AI summaries cannot easily replace."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> I recommend a <b>SaaS CMS</b>, such as <b>Squarespace</b> or hosted <b>Ghost</b>. First, a SaaS CMS bundles the software, hosting and technical support in a predictable subscription, so the blogger does not need to manage servers or security herself. Second, the supplier handles maintenance and upgrades in real time and the package is easily scalable as her readership grows beyond 30,000. Third, content is stored off-island in data centres, which supports continuity during storms. An open source CMS such as WordPress would give more control, but she would be responsible for updates, backups and plugin security, which she has already struggled with.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Problem 1:</b> she should move her 8,000 photos into a <b>DAM</b> or the CMS media library and tag each with <b>metadata</b> such as restaurant, location (Oistins, Bridgetown, Holetown), dish, date and photographer, so any image can be found in seconds. <b>Problem 2:</b> the freelancers should be given <b>user roles</b> (Contributor) and use a <b>workflow</b>: they draft in the CMS, she approves, and <b>version control</b> shows which version is current, so old drafts are never published. <b>Problem 3:</b> she should store each review once as <b>structured content</b> (name, rating, price, location, photos) and follow <b>COPE</b>, so the same content feeds the website, app and newsletter without retyping. <b>Problem 4:</b> she should use <b>automatic off-site cloud backups</b> and a disaster recovery plan, so a storm on the island cannot take the site offline for four days.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> A headless CMS stores content in the <b>body</b> and delivers it as data through an <b>API</b> to any <b>head</b>. Because she wants a website, an app and a newsletter from one source, headless would let a new fish-fry review be published once and appear on all three channels, which suits multi-channel growth. However, headless needs developers to build each front end, which adds cost and complexity for a one-person business. A balanced recommendation is to start with a SaaS CMS that has built-in newsletters (such as Ghost) and an API, then move to headless when the app is ready.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> First, she should build a <b>direct relationship</b> with readers through a weekly <b>newsletter</b> and a paid <b>membership</b> offering early access to new reviews, so visitors from the US and Canada come to her rather than through search. Second, she should focus on <b>unique local content</b> that AI summaries cannot easily replace: original photos, short videos of cooks at the Oistins fish fry and honest first-hand reviews, shared through WhatsApp Channels and Instagram, which turns her guide into a destination in its own right.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>This is the Section G case study. It uses \"recommend\", \"justify\", \"explain\" and \"suggest\". For (a), name ONE classification and give reasons tied to her problems. For (b), answer every problem separately: two marks each, one for the tool and one for how it solves the problem. For (c), a judgement with \"however\" earns full marks. The e-course feedback uses three criteria; your teacher marks the full 20.</p>",
 "easy": {"bloom":"Understand","genre":"Structured response: case study, Bim Eats (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer each part. Use the sentence starters.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Name</b> a CMS type for Bim Eats (open source, proprietary or SaaS) and give ONE reason. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> a tool or feature to fix problem 1 (untagged photos). <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> a tool or feature to fix problem 2 (old drafts published). <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Give</b> a way to fix problem 4 (backups lost in a storm). <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Suggest</b> ONE way to get readers who do not come from search. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":80,"max":220,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> I recommend a ... CMS because ...<br><b>(b)</b> For the photos, she should use ... so that ...<br><b>(c)</b> For the drafts, she should use ... so that ...<br><b>(d)</b> For backups, she should ... so that ...<br><b>(e)</b> She could start a ... so that ...</p>","checklist":["I named one CMS type with a reason.","I suggested a DAM or tags for the photos.","I suggested workflow, roles or versioning for the drafts.","I suggested cloud or off-site backups.","I suggested a newsletter, membership or similar."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"CMS type","marks":2,"look":["saas|open.?source|proprietary","because|so|as"],"min":2,"strong":"Type with a reason.","weak":"Name a type, such as SaaS, and give a reason, such as the supplier handles updates."},{"criterion":"Photos","marks":2,"look":["dam|digital asset|media library|tag|metadata","find|search|organis|so that"],"min":2,"strong":"Fix explained.","weak":"Use a DAM or media library and tag photos so they can be found."},{"criterion":"Drafts","marks":2,"look":["workflow|role|approv|version|revision","so that|so|because|current|latest"],"min":2,"strong":"Fix explained.","weak":"Use roles and an approval workflow with version control so only the latest version is published."},{"criterion":"Backups","marks":2,"look":["cloud|off.?site|off.?island|online","backup|back up|copy"],"min":2,"strong":"Fix explained.","weak":"Keep automatic backups in the cloud, off the island."},{"criterion":"Readers not from search","marks":2,"look":["newsletter|email|member|subscri|whatsapp|social|app","so that|so|because"],"min":2,"strong":"Strategy explained.","weak":"Start a weekly newsletter so readers come straight to her."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> I recommend a SaaS CMS such as Squarespace because the supplier handles hosting, updates and security for her.</p><p><b>(b)</b> For the photos, she should use a DAM or the CMS media library and tag each photo with the restaurant and place, so that she can find any photo quickly.</p><p><b>(c)</b> For the drafts, she should give the writers Contributor roles and use an approval workflow with version control, so that only the latest approved version is published.</p><p><b>(d)</b> For backups, she should keep automatic backups in the cloud, off the island, so that a storm cannot destroy them.</p><p><b>(e)</b> She could start a weekly newsletter so that readers in the US and Canada come straight to her guide.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Each part needs a tool AND what it does. Write \"so that ...\" after every tool, and you will collect both marks each time.</p>","title":"Case study: Bim Eats guide (Foundation)"},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Structured response: case study, Bim Eats, Grade One extension (25 marks)","prompt":"<p>The blogger has a budget for one part-time developer and plans to hire two more writers. Write a consultant's report that:</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Evaluates</b> open source, proprietary and SaaS CMS for Bim Eats and <b>recommends</b> one, with a named example. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li><b>Designs</b> a content workflow for the three writers, naming roles, states and the techniques used at each stage. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li><b>Explains</b> how a DAM and structured content would solve problems 1 and 3, using the terms metadata and COPE. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluates</b> whether to move to a headless CMS now or later, using the terms body, head and API. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Discusses</b> TWO trends that threaten or help Bim Eats, including zero-click search, and <b>recommends</b> a response to each. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Recommends</b> a research tool to track one of these trends and states one limitation of it. <i>(3 marks)</i></li></ol>","min":450,"max":850,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Open source such as ... However ... Proprietary ... SaaS ... I recommend ... because ...<br><b>(b)</b> Draft: ... In Review: ... Approved: ... Scheduled: ... Published: ... Archived: ...<br><b>(c)</b> A DAM ... metadata such as ... Structured content ... COPE means ...<br><b>(d)</b> A headless CMS stores content in the body and ... through an API ... to heads such as ... Now / later because ...<br><b>(e)</b> Zero-click search ... so she should ... A second trend ...<br><b>(f)</b> I recommend ... because ... One limitation is ...</p>","checklist":["I evaluated all three CMS classifications and recommended one with a named example.","My workflow names roles, states and techniques at each stage.","I used metadata and COPE correctly for the DAM and structured content.","My headless judgement uses body, head and API and considers timing and cost.","I discussed two trends with a response to each.","I recommended a research tool and stated a limitation."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"CMS classifications evaluated","marks":5,"look":["open.?source","proprietary","saas","wordpress|ghost|squarespace|wix|drupal|webflow","recommend","cost|licen|subscription|maint|lock"],"min":6,"strong":"All three evaluated with a justified recommendation.","weak":"Weigh each classification on cost, control and maintenance for Bim Eats, then recommend one named system."},{"criterion":"Content workflow with roles","marks":5,"look":["author|contributor","editor|admin","draft","review|approv","schedul","archiv|updat"],"min":5,"strong":"A complete workflow with roles, states and techniques.","weak":"Name roles (Contributor, Editor, Administrator) and the states Draft, In Review, Approved, Scheduled, Published, Archived, with techniques at each stage."},{"criterion":"DAM, metadata and COPE","marks":4,"look":["dam|digital asset","metadata|tag","cope|create once","structured|field"],"min":4,"strong":"Accurate use of all four ideas.","weak":"Explain how a DAM with metadata fixes the photos and how structured content and COPE feed every channel."},{"criterion":"Headless judgement","marks":4,"look":["body","head","api","now|later|when|cost|developer"],"min":4,"strong":"A balanced, timed judgement using the key terms.","weak":"Explain body, head and API, then decide whether headless suits her now or later, with reasons about cost and developers."},{"criterion":"Trends and responses","marks":4,"look":["zero.?click|ai summar","newsletter|member|subscri|direct","personali|first.?party|pwa|short.?form|video|accessib|credential","local|unique|original"],"min":3,"strong":"Two trends discussed with specific responses.","weak":"Explain zero-click search and one other trend, with a specific response to each."},{"criterion":"Research tool and limitation","marks":3,"look":["google trends|semrush|buzzsumo|analytics|feedly|web almanac","limitation|however|but|small market|little data|paid|not why"],"min":2,"strong":"A suitable tool with a genuine limitation.","weak":"Name a tool linked to the trend and explain one limitation of its data."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> An open source CMS such as WordPress has no licence fee and a huge plugin ecosystem, and Bim Eats could own all its data. However, the owner would be responsible for updates, backups and security, and the part-time developer would spend much of the budget on maintenance. A proprietary CMS such as Sitecore offers vendor support but carries licence costs and lock-in far beyond a blog's budget. A SaaS CMS bundles software, hosting and support in a predictable subscription and stores content off-island. I recommend a SaaS CMS such as hosted Ghost, because it includes built-in newsletters and memberships, an API for later growth, and maintenance handled by the supplier, which frees the developer for new features.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> Writers work in the Contributor role: they create reviews in structured fields and save them as Draft, adding tags and alt text. Submitting moves a review to In Review, where the owner, as Editor, edits for accuracy and style and checks prices with the restaurant. She then marks it Approved and schedules it, for example for 7 a.m. on Thursday, so it is Published to the site and newsletter together. When prices change, she updates the live review with a dated note, and a six-monthly content audit moves closed restaurants to Archived. Version control records every change, so an old draft can never overwrite the approved version.</p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> A DAM would hold all 8,000 photos once, each tagged with metadata such as restaurant, location (Oistins, Bridgetown), dish, photographer and date, so any image can be found in seconds and reused safely. Structured content means each review is stored in fields (name, rating, price, location, photos) rather than as one block of text. Following COPE (Create Once, Publish Everywhere), the same fields feed the website, the newsletter and later the app, so nothing is retyped and every channel shows the same version.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> A headless CMS keeps content in the body and delivers it as data through an API to any head, such as the website, an app or a newsletter. Moving now would let one review appear on every channel, but each head would need developer work, which the part-time budget cannot yet support. I recommend moving later: start with a SaaS CMS that offers an API, then build the app as a new head when funding allows, so the content does not need to move again.</p>\n<p><b>(e)</b> Zero-click search threatens Bim Eats, because search engines now answer \"best fish fry in Oistins\" directly, so fewer readers click through. Her response should be a weekly newsletter and a low-cost membership offering early reviews, building a direct relationship. Short-form video helps her: 30-second clips of cooks at the fish fry can be discovered on Instagram and TikTok by visitors planning trips, so she should post captioned clips that link back to full reviews.</p>\n<p><b>(f)</b> I recommend Google Trends to track interest in searches such as \"Oistins restaurants\" by month and by country, because it shows when US and Canadian visitors start planning. One limitation is that small Caribbean markets may produce too little search data for reliable trends, so she should compare it with her own analytics.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>This extension rewards judgement in every part. Evaluate all three CMS types before recommending one, time your headless decision (\"now or later, because ...\"), and finish trend answers with a specific response. Use the key terms (metadata, COPE, body, head, API) exactly, as examiners look for them.</p>","title":"Case study: Bim Eats guide (Challenge)"}}]}],"test":{"title":"Module 2 Test","pass":70,"items":[{"q":"Which statement BEST describes the ePub format?","options":["A content management system for blogs","A video format for online courses","A fixed image of printed pages that cannot change size","An open e-book format with reflowable text that adapts to the reader's screen"],"answer":3,"why":"ePub is an open standard whose text reflows to fit phones, tablets and e-readers. A fixed image of printed pages is tempting because some e-books look like print, but that describes a PDF or a fixed-layout scan, not reflowable ePub."},{"q":"A Jamaican author publishes the same novel as a paperback and as an ePub e-book. What is the main purpose of giving each format its own ISBN?","options":["To uniquely identify each edition and format for sellers, libraries and distributors","To compress the e-book file","To improve the book's search engine ranking","To protect the novel automatically under copyright law"],"answer":0,"why":"An ISBN is an identifier that lets the book trade track one specific edition and format. Copyright protection is tempting because both relate to books, but copyright exists automatically when the work is created; an ISBN gives no legal protection."},{"q":"The network funded by the US Department of Defense in the late 1960s, which became the forerunner of the Internet, was","options":["the World Wide Web","Ethernet","ARPANET","Web 2.0"],"answer":2,"why":"ARPANET linked research computers from 1969 and introduced packet switching that the Internet still uses. The World Wide Web is tempting, but it came about twenty years later (Tim Berners-Lee, around 1989 to 1991) and runs on top of the Internet."},{"q":"An online game in which thousands of players from Barbados, Guyana and around the world share one persistent virtual world at the same time is a","options":["MMOG","ePub","PWA","CMS"],"answer":0,"why":"MMOG means massively multiplayer online game: a very large, shared, persistent world. A PWA is tempting because games can run in browsers, but a PWA is simply a web app that installs like a native app; it says nothing about large numbers of shared players."},{"q":"A Barbadian photographer licenses her photos under Creative Commons BY-NC. A hotel wants to use one in a paid advertisement. What should the hotel do?","options":["Edit the photo so that it becomes a new work","Use it freely as long as it credits the photographer","Use it without credit because it is free online","Ask the photographer for permission, because NC does not allow commercial use"],"answer":3,"why":"NC (non-commercial) forbids commercial use without separate permission, and an advert is commercial. Crediting the photographer is tempting because BY does require attribution, but attribution alone does not cancel the non-commercial condition."},{"q":"A student copies three paragraphs from a national newspaper article into her blog post and publishes it under her own name with no credit. This is","options":["fair use","plagiarism","attribution","parody"],"answer":1,"why":"Presenting another writer's words as your own is plagiarism, and it may also infringe copyright. Fair use is tempting because the passage is short, but fair use requires a limited, transformative purpose such as criticism; copying without credit and claiming authorship is never fair use."},{"q":"Which writing technique is MOST suitable for a podcast script about the history of Anguilla's salt ponds?","options":["Conversational language, short sentences and spoken signposts such as 'next, we will hear'","Long paragraphs with footnotes and references","Tables and bullet points for each date","Hyperlinks placed inside every sentence"],"answer":0,"why":"Podcast scripts are written for the ear, so they must sound natural and guide the listener. Tables and bullet points are tempting because they organise facts clearly, but listeners cannot see them; they suit web pages, not audio."},{"q":"Maya sketches the layout of a tour company's home page on paper and asks five users where they would tap to book. This is a","options":["style guide","low-fidelity prototype","hierarchy map","high-fidelity prototype"],"answer":1,"why":"Paper sketches are low-fidelity prototypes: fast, cheap and ideal for early testing of layout and flow. A high-fidelity prototype is tempting because it is also tested with users, but it is a detailed digital model, often clickable, that looks close to the finished product."},{"q":"A diagram that lists every page of the Tamarind Heritage website and shows how the pages link together is a","options":["concept map","site map","mood board","storyboard"],"answer":1,"why":"A site map shows the full set of pages and their links, planning site structure. A concept map is tempting because it also shows relationships, but it links ideas and topics, not the actual pages of a website."},{"q":"Which of the following goes AGAINST the criteria for web-friendly content?","options":["Long, dense paragraphs filled with technical jargon","Short paragraphs and scannable headings","Plain language and clear calls to action","Addressing the reader directly as 'you'"],"answer":0,"why":"Web-friendly content uses short paragraphs and direct address, and avoids jargon and needless repetition. Addressing the reader as 'you' is tempting because it feels informal, but direct address is specifically recommended for web writing."},{"q":"Keisha adds a descriptive title tag, a meta description and relevant keywords to each page of a Grenada spice tour website. This MAINLY improves","options":["the security of the booking form","the site's search engine optimisation and machine indexability","the site's colour contrast","the loading speed of images"],"answer":1,"why":"Titles, meta descriptions and keywords help search engines understand and rank the page. Loading speed is tempting because it also affects SEO, but metadata does not make images load faster; compressing and resizing images does."},{"q":"What is the MAIN purpose of adding alt text to images on a web page?","options":["To describe the image for screen reader users and search engines","To stop people from copying the image","To make the image file smaller","To add a caption below the image for all visitors"],"answer":0,"why":"Alt text is read aloud by screen readers and indexed by search engines, supporting accessibility and SEO. A visible caption is tempting, but alt text is normally hidden and only shown if the image fails to load; captions are separate elements."},{"q":"A photographer shooting johnny cakes for a café website wants the food sharp and the background softly blurred. Which technique should she use?","options":["The rule of thirds with a fisheye lens","A deep depth of field with a small aperture","A shallow depth of field with a wide aperture","A high-angle shot with flash"],"answer":2,"why":"A shallow depth of field (wide aperture such as f/1.8, or portrait mode on a phone) keeps the subject sharp and blurs the background. Deep depth of field is tempting because it keeps things sharp, but it keeps the background sharp too, which makes it distracting."},{"q":"A podcast recorded in a Castries classroom has a constant air-conditioner hum. In Audacity, the BEST way to remove it is to","options":["convert the file to MP3","add a fade-in to the start of the recording","increase the volume of the whole track","select a few seconds of hum only, capture the noise profile, then apply Noise Reduction to the whole track"],"answer":3,"why":"Noise Reduction learns the hum from a sample (the noise profile) and removes it across the track. Converting to MP3 is tempting because it changes the file, but MP3 compression does not remove noise and actually loses some audio quality."},{"q":"On a tour website, the line 'Home > Tours > Booby Island' at the top of a page is an example of","options":["a hamburger menu","an anchor link","a breadcrumb trail","a footer"],"answer":2,"why":"Breadcrumbs show users their location in the site hierarchy and let them step back up a level. An anchor link is tempting because it also helps navigation, but it jumps to a section within the same page."},{"q":"Which HTML code opens an external booking site in a new browser tab?","options":["&lt;a href=\"https://book.example.com\" new&gt;Book&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;a src=\"https://book.example.com\" tab=\"new\"&gt;Book&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;a href=\"https://book.example.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"&gt;Book&lt;/a&gt;","&lt;link href=\"https://book.example.com\"&gt;Book&lt;/link&gt;"],"answer":2,"why":"The a element uses href for the address, and target=\"_blank\" opens a new tab (rel=\"noopener\" improves security). The link element is tempting because of its name, but it belongs in the head to connect files such as stylesheets; it does not create clickable links."},{"q":"In the CSS box model, what is the correct order of layers from the inside of an element outwards?","options":["Content, padding, border, margin","Padding, content, margin, border","Content, border, padding, margin","Margin, border, padding, content"],"answer":0,"why":"Content sits inside padding, which is surrounded by the border, with margin outside the border separating the box from others. Content, border, padding, margin is tempting because it starts correctly, but padding is always inside the border."},{"q":"Which elements does the CSS rule <code>.price { color: #b00020; }</code> style?","options":["Only elements named &lt;price&gt;","The single element with id=\"price\"","All elements with class=\"price\"","Every paragraph on the page"],"answer":2,"why":"A full stop before a name is a class selector, which can apply to many elements. The id option is tempting, but id selectors use a hash (#price) and should match only one element."},{"q":"A Grenada tourism page uses light grey text (#bbbbbb) on a white background. Which design principle does this MOST clearly break?","options":["The rule of thirds","Consistency of navigation","Responsive layout","Accessibility, because the colour contrast is too low (WCAG)"],"answer":3,"why":"WCAG requires sufficient contrast between text and background; pale grey on white is hard to read, especially outdoors or for users with low vision. Responsive layout is tempting because it is also a UI principle, but it concerns adapting to screen sizes, not colour."},{"q":"A student researching hurricane preparedness checks who wrote a blog post and whether the writer works for a national disaster management office. Which evaluation criterion is she applying?","options":["Satisfaction","Authority","Accessibility","Usability"],"answer":1,"why":"Authority asks who created the content and whether they are qualified and credible. Usability is tempting because it is a common evaluation criterion, but it measures how easy the content is to use and navigate, not who wrote it."},{"q":"A Dominican heritage group wants commuters to learn Caribbean history in 20-minute episodes while driving. Which form of digital publishing is MOST suitable?","options":["A podcast","An interactive e-book","A photo blog","An infographic"],"answer":0,"why":"Podcasts are audio and can be enjoyed safely while driving. An interactive e-book is tempting because it can include audio, but it is designed to be read and tapped, which is unsafe and impractical for drivers."},{"q":"A Grenadian bakery built its whole audience on one social platform. After an algorithm change, its posts now reach far fewer followers. This illustrates which limitation of online platforms?","options":["Community features","Platform dependency","Monetisation tools","Analytics tools"],"answer":1,"why":"Platform dependency means you do not control the rules or algorithms of the platform your audience lives on. Analytics tools are tempting because the bakery would notice the drop through analytics, but analytics are a benefit, not the cause of the problem."},{"q":"Which editorial standard is REQUIRED when writing a Wikipedia article about the Battle of Fort George?","options":["A neutral point of view supported by reliable, verifiable sources","A persuasive tone that promotes Belize tourism","The author's name and photograph at the top","Original research from the writer's own interviews"],"answer":0,"why":"Wikipedia requires neutrality and verifiability from published reliable sources. Original research is tempting because interviews sound rigorous, but Wikipedia does not allow unpublished original research; it must cite existing sources."},{"q":"Compared with free hosted WordPress.com, a self-hosted WordPress site gives Frigate Bay Surf School","options":["full control over plugins, design and data, but responsibility for hosting, backups and security","automatic security updates handled by the platform","a guaranteed higher Google ranking","no need to pay for hosting or a domain"],"answer":0,"why":"Self-hosting gives maximum control and ownership but the owner must pay for hosting and maintain security and backups. Automatic updates are tempting because many hosts offer them, but on a self-hosted site keeping the system updated is ultimately the owner's responsibility."},{"q":"A news organisation stores articles once and sends them through an API to its website, mobile app and smartwatch alerts, each with its own design. This is a","options":["headless CMS","static HTML site","document CMS","enterprise resource system"],"answer":0,"why":"A headless CMS separates content (the 'body') from presentation (the 'head'), delivering content to many channels. A document CMS is tempting because it stores documents, but it manages files such as PDFs and contracts inside an organisation, not multi-channel publishing."},{"q":"In a content workflow, which stage comes immediately after an editor reviews a draft blog post?","options":["Approval","Creation","Publishing","Archiving"],"answer":0,"why":"The workflow runs creation, review, approval, publishing, archiving. Publishing is tempting because it seems the natural next step, but content must be approved by someone with authority before it goes live."},{"q":"Which of the following is a proprietary, cloud-hosted CMS?","options":["WordPress.org software","Joomla","Wix","Drupal"],"answer":2,"why":"Wix is owned by a company and runs on its own servers. WordPress.org software is tempting because WordPress is very well known, but the WordPress.org software is open source and can be self-hosted."},{"q":"Which factor influencing trends in web publishing covers government regulation, censorship and data laws?","options":["Affordability","Politics","Accessibility","Commoditisation"],"answer":1,"why":"Politics covers government regulation, censorship and data laws, and sits alongside commoditisation, pervasiveness, affordability, accessibility, adoption and empowerment. Accessibility is tempting because it is very topical, but it is about whether people can reach and use content, not about government action."},{"q":"More people now ask smart speakers questions such as 'Where can I buy sorrel drink in Port of Spain?'. How should web publishers respond to this trend?","options":["Remove all text and use only images","Use longer pages full of repeated keywords","Publish only PDF documents","Write clear, conversational question-and-answer content with local details"],"answer":3,"why":"Voice search favours natural-language questions and direct answers, such as FAQ sections with local information. Repeating keywords is tempting because it once helped SEO, but keyword stuffing is now penalised and does not match how people speak."},{"q":"Which BEST describes a progressive web app (PWA)?","options":["A social media page for a business","A printed brochure converted to PDF","An app that is only available on the Apple App Store","A website that can be installed on the home screen and can work offline like an app"],"answer":3,"why":"A PWA is a website with app-like features such as installation, offline access and notifications. An App Store app is tempting because PWAs feel like apps, but PWAs are delivered through the web and do not need an app store."}]},"paper2":{"type":"write","title":"Paper 02 practice: planning and fixing a Caribbean tour website","genre":"Paper 02 structured response: site structure, HTML and CSS correction, SEO and CMS selection","stimulus":"<p><b>Read the scenario carefully and then answer the questions that follow.</b></p>\n<p class=\"passage\"><b>Booby Island Ferry Tours</b> runs boat trips from Frigate Bay, St. Kitts, to Booby Island. The owner, Ms. Richardson, wants a website that cruise visitors and residents can use on their phones to see tours, prices, a photo gallery, reviews and a way to book. Maya at Island Pulse Media inspects the first draft page, written by a relative. Part of the code is shown below.</p>\n<pre><code>&lt;h1&gt;Booby Island Ferry Tours&lt;/h1&gt;\n&lt;img src=\"ferry.jpg\"&gt;\n&lt;p style=\"color:#cccccc\"&gt;Daily trips from Frigate Bay to Booby Island.&lt;/p&gt;\n&lt;a href=\"https://book.example.com\"&gt;Book now&lt;/a&gt;\n\n/* style.css */\nimg { width: 1600px; }</code></pre>\n<p>Maya also notes that the page has no title tag or meta description, the photo is a 9 MB file, and the owner has no coding skills but wants to post new photos and prices herself every week.</p>","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Draw or describe a site map for the Booby Island Ferry Tours website with at least <b>FIVE</b> pages, showing the hierarchy. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Identify <b>THREE</b> errors or weaknesses in the code shown and write corrected HTML or CSS for each. <i>(5 marks)</i></li><li>Explain <b>TWO</b> ways to improve the page's search engine optimisation and machine indexability. <i>(3 marks)</i></li><li>Recommend a content management system or publishing platform for Ms. Richardson. Justify your choice with <b>TWO</b> benefits and state <b>ONE</b> limitation. <i>(4 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[15 marks]</b></p>","min":250,"max":450,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Home (top level) links to: ... Under Tours: ...<br><b>(b)</b> Error 1: ... Corrected code: ... Error 2: ... Corrected code: ... Error 3: ... Corrected code: ...<br><b>(c)</b> First, ... because ... Second, ... because ...<br><b>(d)</b> I recommend ... because (benefit 1) ... and (benefit 2) ... One limitation is ...</p>","checklist":["My site map has a Home page at the top and at least four child pages, with at least one second-level page.","I identified three separate code problems (missing alt, low contrast, fixed image width, link behaviour) and wrote correct code for each.","My corrected code uses correct syntax: quotation marks, closing tags and semicolons.","I gave two SEO improvements with reasons (title tag, meta description, keywords, alt text, headings, compressed images).","I recommended a named CMS with two benefits and one limitation linked to Ms. Richardson's needs.","I labelled my answers (a) to (d)."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Site map and hierarchy","marks":3,"look":["home","tour|price|trip","gallery|photo","contact|book|review|about|faq","child|sub|level|under|hierarch|branch|-&gt;|->|>"],"min":3,"strong":"Your site map has a clear home page, five or more pages and a visible hierarchy.","weak":"Show Home at the top, with Tours (and sub-pages such as Snorkel Trip and Sunset Cruise), Gallery, Reviews, About and Book/Contact beneath it."},{"criterion":"HTML and CSS corrections","marks":5,"look":["alt=","max-width|100%|height: ?auto|responsive","contrast|#333|#222|#000|dark","target=|_blank|noopener","compress|resiz|kb|file size|9 ?mb"],"min":3,"strong":"You found three real errors and wrote accurate corrected code.","weak":"Fix three problems with code: add alt=\"Ferry arriving at Booby Island\"; change img to max-width: 100%; height: auto; and use dark text such as color: #222222 for contrast."},{"criterion":"SEO and machine indexability","marks":3,"look":["title","meta|description","keyword|search|rank|google","alt|heading|h1|index"],"min":2,"strong":"You explained two SEO improvements and why they help search engines.","weak":"Give two improvements with reasons, e.g. a title tag 'Gasparee Island Ferry Tours | San Fernando, Trinidad and Tobago' and a meta description containing keywords visitors search for."},{"criterion":"CMS recommendation","marks":4,"look":["wordpress|wix|squarespace|google sites|weebly","no cod|easy|drag|dashboard|update|edit","template|theme|plugin|mobile|responsive|booking|seo","limit|however|cost|fee|depend|control|security|update"],"min":3,"strong":"Your CMS choice is justified with two relevant benefits and a realistic limitation.","weak":"Name a CMS and link it to her needs, e.g. Wix: no coding, drag-and-drop editing so she can update prices weekly; mobile-ready templates; limitation: monthly fees and less control over code and data."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Site map:<br><b>Home</b> (level 1)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&gt; <b>Tours and Prices</b> (level 2) &gt; Booby Island Day Trip, Snorkel Trip, Sunset Cruise (level 3)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&gt; <b>Photo Gallery</b> (level 2)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&gt; <b>Reviews</b> (level 2)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&gt; <b>About Us</b> (level 2)<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&gt; <b>Book and Contact</b> (level 2), with directions and a Google Map.<br>Every level 2 page is reachable from the main menu, and each tour page links to Book and Contact.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Error 1: the image has no alt text</b>, so screen reader users and search engines cannot tell what it shows. Corrected: <code>&lt;img src=\"ferry.jpg\" alt=\"Booby Island Ferry Tours boat arriving at Booby Island\"&gt;</code><br><b>Error 2: the CSS fixes the image at 1600px wide</b>, so on a phone it overflows the screen and forces sideways scrolling. Corrected: <code>img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }</code> The 9 MB photo should also be resized to about 1600px and compressed as a JPEG of under 300 KB so it loads quickly on mobile data.<br><b>Error 3: the paragraph colour #cccccc on a white background has very low contrast</b> and fails WCAG, especially in bright Caribbean sunlight. Corrected: <code>&lt;p style=\"color:#222222\"&gt;Daily trips from Frigate Bay to Booby Island.&lt;/p&gt;</code>, or better, move the colour into style.css: <code>p { color: #222222; }</code><br>The booking link goes to an external site, so it should open in a new tab to keep visitors on the tour site: <code>&lt;a href=\"https://book.example.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"&gt;Book now&lt;/a&gt;</code></p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> First, add a descriptive <b>title tag</b> such as <code>&lt;title&gt;Booby Island Ferry Tours | Frigate Bay, St. Kitts&lt;/title&gt;</code>, because search engines display it as the headline in results and use its keywords to rank the page. Second, add a <b>meta description</b> in the head, for example \"Daily ferry and snorkel trips to Booby Island from Frigate Bay, St. Kitts. Book online.\", because it gives search engines a summary to index and encourages visitors to click. Adding alt text and compressing the image also help, as Google favours accessible, fast-loading pages.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> I recommend <b>Wix</b>, a proprietary cloud-hosted CMS. <b>Benefit 1:</b> it has a drag-and-drop editor and dashboard, so Ms. Richardson can upload new photos and change prices every week without any coding. <b>Benefit 2:</b> its templates are mobile-responsive and it offers built-in SEO settings and booking tools, which suits cruise visitors searching on their phones. <b>Limitation:</b> it charges a monthly fee for a custom domain and removes adverts only on paid plans, and because it is proprietary she has less control over the code and cannot easily move the site to another host later.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>When Paper 02 shows you code, examiners want <b>the error named, the reason it matters and the corrected line</b>. Write code exactly: quotation marks around attribute values, closing tags, and a semicolon after each CSS declaration. If the question says \"write corrected code\", a description alone will lose marks. For CMS questions, link every benefit to the person in the scenario (here, an owner with no coding skills).</p>",
 "easy": {"bloom":"Apply","genre":"Paper 02 structured response: site map, code correction, SEO and CMS (10 marks)","prompt":"<p>Answer ALL parts. Use the sentence starters to help you.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li><b>Complete</b> a site map: put Home at the top and list FOUR pages underneath it. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li>Look at the code. <b>Identify</b> TWO errors and <b>write</b> the corrected code for each:<br>(i) the &lt;img&gt; line; (ii) the CSS rule for img. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> TWO things to add to the head of the page to help search engines. <i>(2 marks)</i></li><li><b>Name</b> a CMS for Ms. Richardson and give ONE reason why it suits her. <i>(2 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[10 marks]</b></p>","min":80,"max":250,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Home<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&gt; ... &gt; ... &gt; ... &gt; ...<br><b>(b)</b> (i) The image is missing ... Corrected: &lt;img src=\"ferry.jpg\" alt=\"...\"&gt;<br>(ii) A width of 1600px is too wide for a phone. Corrected: img { max-width: ...; height: ...; }<br><b>(c)</b> A title tag ... and a meta description ...<br><b>(d)</b> I would choose ... because ...</p>","checklist":["My site map has Home at the top and four pages under it.","I added alt text to the img tag, inside quotation marks.","I changed the img rule to max-width: 100%; height: auto;","I named a title tag and a meta description.","I named a CMS such as Wix and gave one reason linked to Ms. Richardson."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Site map","marks":2,"look":["home","tour|price|gallery|photo|review|book|contact|about"],"min":2,"strong":"A clear site map.","weak":"Put Home at the top, then pages such as Tours and Prices, Gallery, Reviews and Book."},{"criterion":"Code corrections","marks":4,"look":["alt=","max-width:\\s*100%","height:\\s*auto"],"min":2,"strong":"Both errors found and corrected.","weak":"Add alt=\"Ferry arriving at Booby Island\" to the img tag, and change the CSS to img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }."},{"criterion":"SEO in the head","marks":2,"look":["title","meta|description"],"min":2,"strong":"Two correct items.","weak":"Add a title tag and a meta description to the head."},{"criterion":"CMS choice","marks":2,"look":["wix|wordpress|squarespace|google sites|weebly","because|so|no cod|easy|update"],"min":2,"strong":"CMS with a reason.","weak":"Name a CMS such as Wix and say it lets her update prices without coding."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> Home<br>&nbsp;&nbsp;&gt; Tours and Prices &gt; Photo Gallery &gt; Reviews &gt; Book and Contact</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> (i) The image is missing alt text. Corrected: <code>&lt;img src=\"ferry.jpg\" alt=\"Ferry arriving at Booby Island\"&gt;</code><br>(ii) A width of 1600px is too wide for a phone screen. Corrected: <code>img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }</code></p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> A title tag, such as <code>&lt;title&gt;Booby Island Ferry Tours | Frigate Bay, St. Kitts&lt;/title&gt;</code>, and a meta description that tells search engines what the page is about.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> I would choose Wix because Ms. Richardson can change prices and add photos herself with no coding.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>When the question shows code, copy the line and fix it. Check your quotation marks and semicolons. In part (d), one clear reason linked to the owner, such as \"no coding\", earns the second mark.</p>","title":"Paper 02 practice: planning and fixing a Caribbean tour website (Foundation)"},
 "up": {"bloom":"Evaluate","genre":"Paper 02 structured response: site structure, responsive code, SEO and platform evaluation, Grade One extension (20 marks)","prompt":"<p>Ms. Richardson now also wants the site to work well on phones for cruise visitors and to take bookings securely. Answer ALL parts.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>Draw or describe a site map of at least <b>SIX</b> pages on <b>THREE</b> levels, and trace a user pathway for a cruise visitor booking a snorkel trip. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li>Identify <b>FOUR</b> errors or weaknesses in the code and the notes, and write corrected HTML or CSS for each, including one rule that makes the page responsive for phones. <i>(6 marks)</i></li><li>Write a title tag and a meta description for the home page, stating the character count of each, and explain <b>TWO</b> further ways to improve machine indexability. <i>(4 marks)</i></li><li><b>Evaluate</b> TWO publishing options for Ms. Richardson, a hosted website builder and self-hosted WordPress, and recommend one, including <b>ONE</b> security measure for the booking process. <i>(6 marks)</i></li></ol><p style=\"text-align:right\"><b>[20 marks]</b></p>","min":350,"max":700,"frame":"<p><b>(a)</b> Level 1: Home. Level 2: ... Level 3: ... Pathway: ... → ... → ... → ...<br><b>(b)</b> Error 1: ... Corrected: ... Error 2: ... Corrected: ... Error 3: ... Corrected: ... Error 4: ... Corrected: ...<br><b>(c)</b> Title tag (.. characters): ... Meta description (.. characters): ... First, ... Second, ...<br><b>(d)</b> A website builder such as ... However, ... Self-hosted WordPress ... whereas ... I recommend ... because ... For security, ...</p>","checklist":["My site map has at least six pages on three labelled levels.","My pathway runs from arrival to booking confirmation.","I corrected four separate problems with accurate code, including a responsive rule.","My title tag is 50 to 60 characters and my meta description is 150 to 160, with the counts stated.","I explained two more indexability improvements with reasons.","I evaluated both options on more than one factor and recommended one, with a security measure."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Site map on three levels and user pathway","marks":4,"look":["level 1|level one","level 2|level two","level 3|level three","snorkel|sunset|day trip","→|->|then"],"min":4,"strong":"A clear three-level structure and a complete booking pathway.","weak":"Label Levels 1 to 3 with at least six pages, then trace a cruise visitor from arrival to booking confirmation."},{"criterion":"Four corrections with accurate code","marks":6,"look":["alt=","max-width:\\s*100%","height:\\s*auto","color:\\s*#[0-9a-f]{3,6}","rel=.?noopener","compress|resiz|kb|webp|jpe?g","@media|viewport"],"min":5,"strong":"Four genuine problems corrected with accurate code, including a responsive rule.","weak":"Correct the missing alt text, the fixed 1600px width, the low-contrast colour and the external link or heavy image, and add a viewport tag or media query."},{"criterion":"Title, meta description and indexability","marks":4,"look":["<title|title tag","meta name=.?description|meta description","characters","h1|heading|alt|sitemap|internal link|real text|slug"],"min":4,"strong":"Correctly sized metadata with counts and two well-explained improvements.","weak":"Write a 50 to 60 character title and a 150 to 160 character meta description with counts, then explain two improvements such as an XML sitemap and descriptive headings."},{"criterion":"Evaluation, recommendation and security","marks":6,"look":["wix|squarespace|builder","wordpress","cost|fee|subscription|hosting","control|own|lock|maint|update|plugin","recommend","https|ssl|tls|2fa|two.factor|password"],"min":5,"strong":"A balanced evaluation with a justified recommendation and a relevant security measure.","weak":"Weigh both options on cost, ease, control and maintenance, recommend one for Ms. Richardson, and add a measure such as HTTPS or two-factor authentication."}],"model":"<p><b>(a)</b> <b>Level 1:</b> Home. <b>Level 2:</b> Tours and Prices, Photo Gallery, Reviews, About Us, Book and Contact. <b>Level 3:</b> Booby Island Day Trip, Snorkel Trip and Sunset Cruise, all under Tours and Prices. User pathway: a cruise visitor searches \"Booby Island ferry\" → lands on Home → taps Tours and Prices in the hamburger menu → opens Snorkel Trip → taps \"Book this trip\" → chooses date and seats → pays → sees a confirmation page with the meeting point at Frigate Bay. A \"Book this trip\" button on every tour page keeps the pathway short.</p>\n<p><b>(b)</b> <b>Error 1:</b> the image has no alt text, so screen readers and search engines cannot tell what it shows. Corrected: <code>&lt;img src=\"ferry.webp\" alt=\"Booby Island Ferry Tours boat arriving at Booby Island\"&gt;</code><br><b>Error 2:</b> the CSS fixes the image at 1600px, so it overflows a phone screen. Corrected: <code>img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }</code> together with the viewport tag in the head: <code>&lt;meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\"&gt;</code><br><b>Error 3:</b> #cccccc text on white has very low contrast and fails WCAG, especially in bright sunlight. Corrected in style.css: <code>p { color: #222222; }</code><br><b>Error 4:</b> the 9 MB photo is far too heavy for mobile data. It should be resized to about 1600px wide and exported as WebP or JPEG under about 300 KB. The external booking link should also open safely: <code>&lt;a href=\"https://book.example.com\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"&gt;Book now (opens in a new tab)&lt;/a&gt;</code><br>For layout on phones, the tour cards stack by default and sit side by side on wider screens: <code>@media (min-width: 768px) { .tours { display: flex; gap: 1rem; } }</code></p>\n<p><b>(c)</b> Title tag (49 characters): <code>&lt;title&gt;Booby Island Ferry Tours | Frigate Bay, St. Kitts&lt;/title&gt;</code>. Meta description (152 characters): <code>&lt;meta name=\"description\" content=\"Daily ferry and snorkel trips to Booby Island from Frigate Bay, St. Kitts. See tour times and prices, view photos and book your seats online in minutes.\"&gt;</code>. First, I would use one descriptive h1 such as \"Ferry and snorkel trips to Booby Island\", with h2 headings for each tour, because crawlers use headings to understand the page structure. Second, I would submit an XML sitemap through Google Search Console and add internal links from every tour page to Book and Contact, so crawlers can find and index every page, including new tours.</p>\n<p><b>(d)</b> A hosted website builder such as Wix is quick to set up, has mobile-ready templates and booking apps, and the platform handles hosting, SSL and updates, so Ms. Richardson can change prices and photos herself without coding. However, it charges a monthly fee for a custom domain and no adverts, and the site is hard to move elsewhere later. Self-hosted WordPress gives full control and ownership, with booking plugins and no platform lock-in, whereas she would have to pay for hosting and keep WordPress and its plugins updated, which is risky for an owner with no technical skills. I recommend the website builder on a paid plan, because ease of updating and managed security matter most for her. For security, the booking process must run over HTTPS, and her admin account should use a long passphrase with two-factor authentication through an authenticator app.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>At extension level, every code fix needs three parts: the error, why it matters and the corrected line. State character counts for metadata, because examiners check them. In part (d), compare both options on the same factors, then let the owner's skills decide the recommendation.</p>","title":"Paper 02 practice: planning and fixing a Caribbean tour website (Challenge)"}}});
